window.LD_CONTENT={"acts":[{"id":"prologue","label":"Prologue","title":"The Longest Age"},{"id":"roots","label":"Act I","title":"Roots"},{"id":"toolmakers","label":"Act II","title":"The First Toolmakers"},{"id":"many-humans","label":"Act III","title":"Many Ways of Being Human"},{"id":"one-species","label":"Act IV","title":"One Species, Every Continent"},{"id":"warming","label":"Act V","title":"After the Ice"},{"id":"transition","label":"Act VI","title":"Sowing and Herding"},{"id":"metal","label":"Act VII","title":"Toward Metal"}],"chapters":[{"id":"the-longest-age","order":0,"act":"prologue","title":"The Longest Age","subtitle":"What the Stone Age was, and how we know","era":"Stone Age: Palaeolithic, Mesolithic and Neolithic","start":3300000,"end":5000,"dateLabel":"3.3 million–5,000 years ago","regions":["africa","europe","asia","oceania","americas"],"focus":[-170,-50,180,72],"emblem":"globe","summary":"The Stone Age spans more than 99 percent of the human technological past, from the first knapped stones 3.3 million years ago to the spread of metal. This prologue explains where the name came from, why it undersells the period, and how dating methods, ancient DNA and ancient proteins let us read it.","paragraphs":[["On a dry hillside west of Lake Turkana in northern Kenya, archaeologists in 2011 found sharp-edged stones that someone had struck from lava cobbles 3.3 million years ago.","They are the oldest known tools made by breaking stone.","From that moment until metal arrived, only 5,000 to 7,000 years ago in the places that had it first, our ancestors and their relatives lived in what we call the Stone Age.","It covers more than 99 percent of the human technological past."],["The name was born in a museum.","In Copenhagen in the early 1800s, a merchant’s son named Christian Jürgensen Thomsen sorted Denmark’s antiquities into stone, bronze and iron, and his guidebook of 1836 turned a filing system into a history.","The label stuck, though it misleads.","Stone is simply what survives.","Wood, bone, hide, plant fibre and string did much of the work, and nearly all of it has rotted away."],["So how can we know anything about so distant a past?","We read clocks hidden in volcanic ash, cave crystals and grains of sand.","We recover DNA from bones, and even from the dirt of cave floors, and proteins from tooth enamel 2 million years old.","Ice cores and the mud of the deep ocean record the climates people lived through."],["This journey begins even earlier, with the first upright apes in Africa some 7 million years ago, and it ends as copper and bronze begin to change the world.","It crosses every inhabited continent.","Along the way we will meet many kinds of humans, and many arguments, because the evidence is still coming out of the ground.","Welcome to the longest age of all."]],"sentences":["On a dry hillside west of Lake Turkana in northern Kenya, archaeologists in 2011 found sharp-edged stones that someone had struck from lava cobbles 3.3 million years ago.","They are the oldest known tools made by breaking stone.","From that moment until metal arrived, only 5,000 to 7,000 years ago in the places that had it first, our ancestors and their relatives lived in what we call the Stone Age.","It covers more than 99 percent of the human technological past.","The name was born in a museum.","In Copenhagen in the early 1800s, a merchant’s son named Christian Jürgensen Thomsen sorted Denmark’s antiquities into stone, bronze and iron, and his guidebook of 1836 turned a filing system into a history.","The label stuck, though it misleads.","Stone is simply what survives.","Wood, bone, hide, plant fibre and string did much of the work, and nearly all of it has rotted away.","So how can we know anything about so distant a past?","We read clocks hidden in volcanic ash, cave crystals and grains of sand.","We recover DNA from bones, and even from the dirt of cave floors, and proteins from tooth enamel 2 million years old.","Ice cores and the mud of the deep ocean record the climates people lived through.","This journey begins even earlier, with the first upright apes in Africa some 7 million years ago, and it ends as copper and bronze begin to change the world.","It crosses every inhabited continent.","Along the way we will meet many kinds of humans, and many arguments, because the evidence is still coming out of the ground.","Welcome to the longest age of all."],"pronounce":{"Jürgensen":"Yur-gen-sen","Thomsen":"Tom-sen","Turkana":"Tur-kah-nah"},"body":[{"heading":"A museum’s filing system","paragraphs":["The Stone Age began as a way of arranging display cases. In 1816 Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, a Copenhagen merchant’s son and self-taught antiquarian, took charge of Denmark’s national collection of antiquities. He sorted the objects by the material of their cutting tools and by which things had been found together in graves and hoards: first stone, then bronze, then iron. In 1836 Thomsen set out the scheme in a guidebook, Ledetraad til Nordisk Oldkyndighed, translated into English in 1848 as A Guide to Northern Antiquities.","Later scholars divided the stone part. In Pre-historic Times (1865) the English banker and naturalist John Lubbock coined ‘Palaeolithic’ (old stone) for the age of flaked tools and extinct animals such as the mammoth, and ‘Neolithic’ (new stone) for the age of polished axes. Hodder Westropp proposed ‘Mesolithic’ for the hunter-gatherers in between, and ‘Chalcolithic’ came into use for the Copper Age. In 1929 John Goodwin and Clarence van Riet Lowe, finding the European terms a poor fit for southern Africa, divided the African record into an Earlier, Middle and Later Stone Age, a scheme still used across the continent.","Each region now has its own sequence: Lower, Middle and Upper Palaeolithic in Europe and West Asia, the pottery-rich Jōmon in Japan, Paleoindian and Archaic in the Americas. These words describe ways of making things, not stages of human worth. ‘Stone Age’ is a technological label, and the people in this story were as intelligent and inventive as anyone alive today."]},{"heading":"More than stones","paragraphs":["Stone dominates museum cases for one reason: it survives. Where conditions are exceptional, glimpses of the rest appear, such as the wooden spears of Schöningen in Germany or traces of birch-tar glue on tool edges. These perishable or ‘invisible’ technologies probably made up most of the toolkit for most of the Stone Age.","String is a good example. In 2020 a team led by Bruce Hardy reported a tiny fragment of three-ply cord, twisted from the inner bark of a conifer and stuck to a stone tool at Abri du Maras in southern France, made by Neanderthals roughly 41,000–52,000 years ago. In 2024 Nicholas Conard and Veerle Rots showed that a 20-centimetre baton of mammoth ivory with four spirally grooved holes, found in 2015 at Hohle Fels cave in southwest Germany and about 37,000 years old, was most likely a rope-making tool: with replicas, experimenters quickly twisted cattail fibres into several metres of strong rope. Cord implies nets, snares, bags and lashings, a technology almost never preserved.","The label also hides how long stone stayed useful: flint sickles remained common well into the Bronze Age, and in highland New Guinea ground stone axes were still quarried and traded in the twentieth century."]},{"heading":"Most of the human story","paragraphs":["The oldest known knapped stone tools, from Lomekwi 3 in Kenya, are about 3.3 million years old. The first smelted copper appears in southeast Europe and Iran around 7,000 years ago, and bronze became common in Mesopotamia and neighbouring regions around 5,000 years ago. On any reckoning the Stone Age covers more than 99 percent of the time that humans and their relatives have been making tools. Homo sapiens has existed for less than a tenth of it.","There was no single end date. Southwest Asia and southeast Europe moved into copper and bronze first; China used bronze on a large scale only from about 4,000 years ago; much of sub-Saharan Africa went straight from stone to iron within the last 3,000 years, without a Bronze Age. In the Americas people hammered native copper around the Great Lakes for thousands of years and Andean societies later smelted metals, but stone remained the everyday material. Australia and most Pacific islands used no metal until contact with Europeans. This history follows the story to the arrival of metal, about 5,000 years ago in the first places to have it; a late chapter traces how the change played out region by region."]},{"heading":"Clocks in rocks and bones","paragraphs":["Most dates in this app come from physics. Radiocarbon dating, developed by Willard Libby in the late 1940s, measures the decay of carbon-14 in once-living material such as charcoal, bone or seeds. Carbon-14 halves every 5,730 years, so after about 50,000 years too little is left to measure reliably. Raw radiocarbon ages must also be calibrated against tree rings and other records to become calendar years.","Older sites need other clocks. Potassium–argon and argon–argon dating measure the argon that accumulates in volcanic minerals after an eruption. East Africa’s Rift Valley is layered with volcanic ash, which is why it provides the backbone of dates for early human fossils: in 1961 potassium–argon dating showed that Bed I at Olduvai Gorge, with the skull nicknamed ‘Nutcracker Man’, was about 1.75 million years old, far older than most scholars had expected. Palaeomagnetism exploits the fact that Earth’s magnetic field has flipped many times; the last full reversal happened about 780,000 years ago, so a layer magnetised in the reverse direction must be older.","Other methods count accumulated damage. Luminescence dating measures energy stored in quartz and feldspar grains by natural radiation: optically stimulated luminescence (OSL) dates when sediment last saw sunlight, and thermoluminescence (TL) when burnt flint was last heated. Electron spin resonance (ESR) works similarly on tooth enamel. Uranium-series dating measures the build-up of thorium in cave crusts and stalagmites, back to roughly 500,000 years; uranium–lead dating reaches millions. Cosmogenic nuclide dating uses aluminium-26 and beryllium-10, created in quartz by cosmic rays at the surface, which decay at different rates once a rock is buried in a cave. No method is perfect, so good sites are dated by several, and dates in this app are given as ranges."]},{"heading":"Molecules that remember","paragraphs":["Since the 1990s, genetics has become a second archive. Svante Pääbo’s laboratory recovered Neanderthal mitochondrial DNA in 1997, published a draft Neanderthal genome in 2010 and, the same year, identified the Denisovans from the DNA of a finger bone, a human group first known almost entirely from its genes. Pääbo received the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 2022. The oldest human DNA sequenced so far, from the Sima de los Huesos in Spain, is about 430,000 years old. DNA can even be recovered without bones: in 2017 Viviane Slon and colleagues extracted Neanderthal and Denisovan DNA from cave sediments, and in 2022 environmental DNA from northern Greenland revealed an ecosystem about 2 million years old.","Proteins last longer than DNA, especially the tough proteins of tooth enamel. Palaeoproteomics, the study of ancient protein sequences, has placed Homo antecessor from Spain, about 800,000 years old, on the human family tree and identified a Denisovan jaw from the Tibetan Plateau. In 2025 a team led by Palesa Madupe read enamel proteins from four Paranthropus robustus teeth about 2 million years old from Swartkrans in South Africa and determined each individual’s sex, and a team led by Daniel Green recovered enamel protein fragments up to 18 million years old from mammal fossils in Kenya’s Turkana Basin.","Chemistry also tells us what people ate and where they grew up. Carbon isotopes in enamel distinguish diets based on trees and shrubs from diets rich in tropical grasses and sedges; nitrogen isotopes indicate how much meat was eaten; strontium isotopes match a tooth to the geology of the place where its owner spent childhood, revealing who moved."]},{"heading":"Archives of climate","paragraphs":["Deep-sea sediments record the climate swings people lived through: they contain the shells of foraminifera whose oxygen isotopes track global ice volume and ocean temperature. In 2005 Lorraine Lisiecki and Maureen Raymo combined 57 such records into the LR04 stack, which covers the last 5.3 million years and anchors the numbered marine isotope stages used throughout archaeology: odd numbers for warm stages, even numbers for cold ones.","Antarctic ice cores trap bubbles of ancient air. The European Project for Ice Coring in Antarctica recovered 800,000 years of carbon dioxide and temperature at Dome C, and in January 2025 its successor project reached bedrock at nearby Little Dome C, retrieving a 2,800-metre core with ice more than 1.2 million years old. It should illuminate the Mid-Pleistocene Transition, when the rhythm of ice ages lengthened from about 41,000 to about 100,000 years. Lake cores, stalagmites, pollen and fossil soils show how particular landscapes changed."]},{"heading":"How to read this journey","paragraphs":["For most of the story, dates are given as ‘years ago’ counted back from the year 2000. For roughly the last 12,000 years, where archaeologists commonly use calendar dates, chapters give years BCE (before the Common Era): 9600 BCE is about 11,600 years ago. Uncertain dates appear as ranges, such as 3.3–2.6 million years ago, and the dating method is named where it matters.","‘Hominin’ means humans and all extinct relatives more closely related to us than to chimpanzees and bonobos. Species names are working hypotheses, so each chapter sets out the main arguments and the evidence on each side, naming researchers and years. The story runs broadly in time order and moves between continents as the evidence does, beginning in Africa, where the earliest hominins, tools and members of our own species have been found."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Is “Stone Age” still a useful label?","views":[{"label":"Keep it, with care","text":"Most archaeologists still use the three-age terms and their subdivisions because they provide a shared vocabulary across two centuries of literature, and because stone really is the most continuous record of behaviour over 3.3 million years. Museums, textbooks and site reports worldwide are organised this way."},{"label":"Replace or regionalise it","text":"Critics note that the terms grew out of Scandinavian museum collections and a Victorian idea of progress, privilege the one material that survives, and fit Africa, Australia and the Americas poorly. John Shea (2013) proposed describing stone tools by technological modes labelled A to I rather than by named ages, building on the five modes Grahame Clark outlined in 1969, and African specialists debate whether the boundaries of the Earlier, Middle and Later Stone Age still mean anything."}],"status":"In 2026 the old names remain in everyday use, but most researchers treat them as loose labels for technological traditions rather than stages that all humans passed through, and increasingly describe what people actually did, such as knapping methods, hafting or ornament making."},{"question":"How far back can ancient molecules take us?","views":[{"label":"A deep molecular record","text":"Palaeoproteomics has moved from human enamel about 800,000 years old (Homo antecessor, 2020) to Paranthropus about 2 million years old and mammal enamel up to 18 million years old (both 2025). Researchers in the field expect proteins to help sort out relationships among early hominins that will never yield DNA."},{"label":"A thin signal","text":"Others stress that enamel preserves only a handful of proteins, so each fragment carries few informative differences; contamination, chemical damage and tiny sample sizes make family-tree placements tentative. Hominin DNA has not been recovered from anything older than about 430,000 years, and warm climates destroy it quickly."}],"status":"Proteins now reliably reveal sex and broad relationships for some species more than 2 million years old, but detailed family trees of early hominins still rest mainly on anatomy. More samples, especially from Africa, will show how far the molecular record can reach."},{"question":"When did the Stone Age begin?","views":[{"label":"3.3 million years ago","text":"Sonia Harmand and colleagues argue that the Lomekwi 3 artefacts were deliberately knapped and securely dated, marking the start of stone-tool making some 700,000 years before the Oldowan."},{"label":"About 2.6 million years ago or a little earlier","text":"Some archaeologists regard Lomekwi as a single, contested site and begin the continuous record of flaked tools with the Oldowan at Nyayanga, Ledi-Geraru and Gona, between about 3.0 and 2.6 million years ago."},{"label":"Earlier than we can see","text":"Chimpanzees and capuchin monkeys use stones as hammers, sometimes producing flakes by accident, so hominins probably pounded with stones long before they knapped them on purpose, leaving traces that are hard to recognise, such as the disputed 3.39-million-year-old cut marks at Dikika."}],"status":"The 3.3-million-year date is widely cited, and chapter 3 examines the evidence. Either way, the Stone Age did not begin with a single invention but with a gradual change in how our relatives used stone."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Span","value":"About 3.3 million to 5,000 years ago, more than 99 percent of the human technological past"},{"label":"Three-age system","value":"Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, Copenhagen, published 1836"},{"label":"Palaeolithic and Neolithic","value":"Terms coined by John Lubbock in Pre-historic Times, 1865"},{"label":"Radiocarbon limit","value":"About 50,000 years"},{"label":"Oldest hominin proteins","value":"About 2 million years, Paranthropus robustus, Swartkrans (2025)"},{"label":"Oldest deep ice core","value":"More than 1.2 million years, Little Dome C, Antarctica (2025)"}],"siteIds":["lomekwi-3","swartkrans","sima-de-los-huesos","denisova-cave","abri-du-maras","hohle-fels","lake-mungo","monte-verde"],"routes":[],"themes":["technology","species","genetics","climate","migration"],"sources":[{"cite":"Thomsen, C. J. (1836). Ledetraad til Nordisk Oldkyndighed. Copenhagen: Kongelige Nordiske Oldskrift-Selskab. English translation: A Guide to Northern Antiquities (1848)."},{"cite":"Lubbock, J. (1865). Pre-historic Times, as Illustrated by Ancient Remains, and the Manners and Customs of Modern Savages. London: Williams and Norgate."},{"cite":"Goodwin, A. J. H. & van Riet Lowe, C. (1929). The Stone Age Cultures of South Africa. Annals of the South African Museum 27, 1–289."},{"cite":"Harmand, S. et al. (2015). 3.3-million-year-old stone tools from Lomekwi 3, West Turkana, Kenya. Nature 521, 310–315.","doi":"10.1038/nature14464"},{"cite":"Hardy, B. L. et al. (2020). Direct evidence of Neanderthal fibre technology and its cognitive and behavioral implications. Scientific Reports 10, 4889.","doi":"10.1038/s41598-020-61839-w"},{"cite":"Conard, N. J. & Rots, V. (2024). Rope making in the Aurignacian of Central Europe more than 35,000 years ago. Science Advances 10, eadh5217.","doi":"10.1126/sciadv.adh5217"},{"cite":"Slon, V. et al. (2017). Neandertal and Denisovan DNA from Pleistocene sediments. Science 356, 605–608."},{"cite":"Madupe, P. P. et al. (2025). Enamel proteins reveal biological sex and genetic variability in southern African Paranthropus. Science 388, 969.","doi":"10.1126/science.adt9539"},{"cite":"Green, D. R. et al. (2025). Eighteen million years of diverse enamel proteomes from the East African Rift. Nature.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-025-09040-9"},{"cite":"Lisiecki, L. E. & Raymo, M. E. (2005). A Pliocene–Pleistocene stack of 57 globally distributed benthic δ18O records. Paleoceanography 20, PA1003."}]},{"id":"first-hominins","order":1,"act":"roots","title":"The First Upright Apes","subtitle":"Sahelanthropus, Orrorin, Ardipithecus and the origins of walking","era":"Late Miocene and Early Pliocene","start":7000000,"end":4400000,"dateLabel":"7–4.4 million years ago","regions":["africa","europe"],"focus":[5,-5,45,50],"emblem":"skull","summary":"The oldest proposed members of the human lineage, from Chad, Kenya and Ethiopia, mix ape and human traits. Whether they walked upright, what our last common ancestor with chimpanzees looked like, and why bipedalism evolved remain live debates.","paragraphs":[["On the 19th of July 2001, in the shifting dunes of the Djurab Desert in northern Chad, Ahounta Djimdoumalbaye spotted a dark, crushed skull lying in the sand.","His team, led by the French palaeontologist Michel Brunet, named it Sahelanthropus tchadensis.","Its nickname, Toumaï, means hope of life in the local Daza language.","It is about 7 million years old."],["Toumaï had a brain no bigger than a chimpanzee’s, but a shorter face and small canine teeth, worn flat at the tips instead of sharpened like an ape’s.","Did it walk on two legs?","A thigh bone found at the same spot has divided experts for twenty years, and a study published in early 2026 argues, once again, that it did."],["Other candidates followed.","In Kenya’s Tugen Hills, thigh bones about 6 million years old belong to a creature called Orrorin.","In Ethiopia, a partial skeleton of Ardipithecus ramidus, nicknamed Ardi, shows that 4.4 million years ago a hominin could walk upright on the ground yet still had a grasping big toe for climbing trees."],["These fossils overturned a comfortable story.","Our ancestors did not stride out of the forest onto the open grasslands of eastern Africa.","They began in wooded country across a wider stretch of the continent, as part-time walkers and part-time climbers.","Why they first stood up at all is still one of the great open questions."]],"sentences":["On the 19th of July 2001, in the shifting dunes of the Djurab Desert in northern Chad, Ahounta Djimdoumalbaye spotted a dark, crushed skull lying in the sand.","His team, led by the French palaeontologist Michel Brunet, named it Sahelanthropus tchadensis.","Its nickname, Toumaï, means hope of life in the local Daza language.","It is about 7 million years old.","Toumaï had a brain no bigger than a chimpanzee’s, but a shorter face and small canine teeth, worn flat at the tips instead of sharpened like an ape’s.","Did it walk on two legs?","A thigh bone found at the same spot has divided experts for twenty years, and a study published in early 2026 argues, once again, that it did.","Other candidates followed.","In Kenya’s Tugen Hills, thigh bones about 6 million years old belong to a creature called Orrorin.","In Ethiopia, a partial skeleton of Ardipithecus ramidus, nicknamed Ardi, shows that 4.4 million years ago a hominin could walk upright on the ground yet still had a grasping big toe for climbing trees.","These fossils overturned a comfortable story.","Our ancestors did not stride out of the forest onto the open grasslands of eastern Africa.","They began in wooded country across a wider stretch of the continent, as part-time walkers and part-time climbers.","Why they first stood up at all is still one of the great open questions."],"pronounce":{"Djurab":"Joo-rab","Ahounta Djimdoumalbaye":"Ah-hoon-tah Jim-doo-mal-bye","Sahelanthropus tchadensis":"Sah-hel-an-thro-pus chad-en-sis","Toumaï":"Too-my","Daza":"Dah-zah","Orrorin":"Or-ror-in","Ardipithecus ramidus":"Ar-dee-pith-eh-kus ram-ih-dus","Tugen":"Too-gen"},"body":[{"heading":"A skull from the Djurab","paragraphs":["On 19 July 2001 Ahounta Djimdoumalbaye, a member of the Franco-Chadian palaeoanthropological mission led by Michel Brunet, found a cranium at locality TM 266 in the Toros-Menalla area of the Djurab Desert, northern Chad. Published in Nature in 2002 as Sahelanthropus tchadensis and nicknamed Toumaï, ‘hope of life’ in the Daza language, it was badly crushed and had to be reconstructed digitally. The animals found with it point to 7–6 million years ago, and a cosmogenic method based on beryllium isotopes gave about 7.2–6.8 million years.","Toumaï combines a chimpanzee-sized brain of about 360–370 cubic centimetres with features seen later in hominins: a relatively short face, a heavy brow ridge, and small canines worn flat at the tips rather than honed against the lower premolar as in apes. Brunet’s team argued that the foramen magnum, where the spinal cord leaves the skull, sat further forward than in apes, a clue to upright posture. Critics including Milford Wolpoff, Brigitte Senut and Martin Pickford questioned this and suggested Toumaï might be an ape. Toumaï lived in a mosaic of lake shore, gallery forest and grassland beside a much larger ancient Lake Chad."]},{"heading":"The thigh bone that would not settle","paragraphs":["A partial femur found at TM 266 in 2001 was recognised as possibly hominin only in 2004, by Aude Bergeret, and stayed unpublished for years. In 2020 Roberto Macchiarelli and colleagues described it and concluded that its shaft lacked the features of a habitual biped. In 2022 Guillaume Daver, Franck Guy and colleagues from the original team published the femur together with two forearm bones (ulnae) in Nature and reached the opposite verdict: Sahelanthropus walked upright on the ground as a matter of routine but also climbed carefully in trees.","The argument did not end there. In a 2025 reply, Marine Cazenave and colleagues contended that neither the external shape nor the internal bone structure supports habitual bipedalism. In early 2026 a team led by Scott Williams of New York University reported in Science Advances a femoral tubercle, the attachment of the iliofemoral ligament that stabilises the hip when we stand, and found the femur long relative to the ulna, approaching the proportions of Australopithecus. One complication remains: nobody can be sure the limb bones belong to the same individual, or even the same species, as the skull."]},{"heading":"Millennium Man","paragraphs":["In late 2000 a team led by Brigitte Senut and Martin Pickford recovered fossils of at least five individuals from the Lukeino Formation in the Tugen Hills of Kenya’s Baringo County, about 6 million years old according to argon–argon dating and palaeomagnetism of the surrounding volcanic rocks. Published in 2001 as Orrorin tugenensis (‘original man’ in the Tugen language) and nicknamed Millennium Man, the find includes teeth, jaw fragments, an upper arm bone, a finger bone and three partial femora.","The femora carry the argument. The best preserved has a long femoral neck and a groove for the obturator externus muscle, traits linked to upright walking. In 2008 Brian Richmond and William Jungers found it most similar to the femora of Australopithecus and Paranthropus, supporting bipedalism, while a 2013 analysis led by Sergio Almécija saw a mosaic that also recalls Miocene apes. Senut and Pickford’s claim that Orrorin lay closer to our own line than the australopiths has won little support. Its curved finger bone suggests that Orrorin still climbed."]},{"heading":"Ardi and her kin","paragraphs":["In Ethiopia’s Middle Awash, Yohannes Haile-Selassie described Ardipithecus kadabba from teeth and bones 5.8–5.2 million years old, first as a subspecies in 2001 and as a species in 2004. Its canines still show some ape-like honing, but a toe bone about 5.2 million years old has a joint angled upward in a way that suggests pushing off from the toes while walking.","The younger Ardipithecus ramidus was named in 1994 from Aramis, where Tim White’s Middle Awash team had found its first fossil in 1992. In November 1994 the team found the first bones of a partial skeleton, ARA-VP-6/500, later nicknamed Ardi. The bones were so fragile and crushed that excavation, CT scanning and reconstruction took 15 years; in October 2009 the team described Ardi in 11 papers in Science. The skeleton lay between two volcanic ash layers dated to about 4.4 million years. Ardi was a female of about 50 kilograms and 1.2 metres, with a brain of 300–350 cubic centimetres.","Her anatomy is a mix no one predicted. The upper pelvis is short and broad like a biped’s, and the foot was stiffened for push-off, yet the big toe diverged and could grasp like an ape’s. The hands were flexible, and canines were small in both sexes. Owen Lovejoy and White argued that Ardi walked upright on the ground and clambered carefully in trees on her palms, and that she lacked the knuckle-walking specialisations of chimpanzees and gorillas. More Ar. ramidus fossils come from Gona, 4.5–4.3 million years old."]},{"heading":"Woodland, not savanna","paragraphs":["For decades human origins were tied to open grassland. In 1983 the French palaeontologist Yves Coppens popularised the ‘East Side Story’, building on an idea of Adriaan Kortlandt: the rifting of East Africa had cut off drier eastern landscapes, where apes became upright hominins, from western forests, where apes stayed apes. Toumaï, found about 2,500 kilometres west of the Rift Valley, and an australopith jaw from Koro Toro in Chad (chapter 2) undercut the scenario, and Coppens himself acknowledged it.","The earliest hominins also lived in more wooded places than the savanna idea implied. White’s team reconstructed Aramis as woodland with patches of forest, based on fossil plants, snails, monkeys, antelopes and the carbon isotopes of soils and teeth. In 2010 Thure Cerling and colleagues countered that the same evidence pointed to a tree or bush savanna with only 5–25 percent tree cover. Both sides agreed that Ardi did not live on open grassland, and Orrorin and Sahelanthropus also lived in mosaics with trees nearby."]},{"heading":"The ancestor we share with chimpanzees","paragraphs":["Genetics sets the time frame. Human and chimpanzee genomes differ at roughly 1.2 percent of their DNA letters, and turning that difference into time depends on the assumed mutation rate and generation length, so estimates of the split range from about 5 to 13 million years ago. Direct counts of new mutations in families, and longer generation times measured in wild apes by Kevin Langergraber and colleagues (2012), push the split earlier than older fossil-calibrated figures of 5–7 million years. A 2016 study led by Priya Moorjani estimated that the two genomes diverged on average around 12 million years ago; because the ancestral population already carried variation, the populations themselves separated somewhat later. A hominin in Chad at 7 million years is therefore genetically plausible.","What the ancestor looked like is disputed. For much of the twentieth century it was pictured as something like a chimpanzee, a knuckle-walking forest ape. The Ardipithecus team argued in 2009 and 2015 that chimpanzees and gorillas are themselves highly specialised and that the ancestor was a more generalised climber. In 2021 Thomas Prang and colleagues analysed Ardi’s hand and found it most similar to suspensory apes such as chimpanzees and bonobos, implying a more chimpanzee-like ancestor that hung and swung beneath branches."]},{"heading":"Apes in Europe","paragraphs":["Miocene Europe was home to many apes, and some researchers look there for our origins. In 2017 Jochen Fuss, Madelaine Böhme and colleagues argued that Graecopithecus freybergi, known from a lower jaw found in 1944 at Pyrgos Vassilissis near Athens and a premolar from Bulgaria, both about 7.2 million years old, was a hominin because of the partly fused roots of its premolars. Most specialists replied that one dental trait in so fragmentary a sample cannot identify a hominin.","In 2019 Böhme’s team described Danuvius guggenmosi, an 11.6-million-year-old ape from the Hammerschmiede clay pit in Bavaria, and proposed that it combined straight, weight-bearing legs with hanging arms, a mode they called extended-limb clambering, which could have preceded bipedalism. Scott Williams and colleagues replied in Nature in 2020 that the bones did not demonstrate the claimed hip, knee and lower-back features. Most palaeoanthropologists still see Africa as the cradle of the lineage."]},{"heading":"Why stand up?","paragraphs":["Bipedalism is the earliest defining trait of hominins, and explanations abound. Raymond Dart and others linked it to moving into open savanna. Peter Wheeler argued in the 1980s and 1990s that standing upright reduces the skin area struck by the midday sun and lifts the body into cooler breezes. Owen Lovejoy proposed in 1981 that males who carried food to mates gained a reproductive advantage, a scenario he later connected to Ardi’s small canines. In 2007 Michael Sockol, David Raichlen and Herman Pontzer measured chimpanzees on treadmills and found that human walking uses about 75 percent less energy than chimpanzee knuckle-walking.","Other ideas begin in the trees. Kevin Hunt noted in 1994 that chimpanzees stand upright mainly to feed from small trees, and in 2007 Susannah Thorpe, Roger Holder and Robin Crompton showed that orangutans walk upright on thin, flexible branches with straightened legs, suggesting that bipedalism began as a way of moving through the canopy. The earliest hominins, walkers and climbers in wooded settings, fit a gradual shift better than a sudden move onto grassland."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Did Sahelanthropus walk upright?","views":[{"label":"A habitual biped","text":"Michel Brunet’s team, Guillaume Daver and Franck Guy (2022) and Scott Williams’s group (2026) point to the forward position of the foramen magnum, a femur they read as adapted to upright walking, and a femoral tubercle for the hip ligament that bipeds rely on. They see an animal that walked upright on the ground and climbed in trees."},{"label":"Not demonstrated","text":"Roberto Macchiarelli, Marine Cazenave and colleagues argue that the femur’s shape and internal structure match apes that are not habitual bipeds, that the skull’s posture clues are ambiguous, and that the limb bones cannot be securely tied to the skull. Some critics consider Sahelanthropus an ape close to the common ancestor rather than a hominin."}],"status":"Unresolved in 2026. The bones are fragmentary and may come from different individuals. A pelvis, a foot or a more complete skeleton from Toros-Menalla would settle the question."},{"question":"Was our last common ancestor with chimpanzees chimp-like?","views":[{"label":"No, a generalised climber","text":"Tim White, Owen Lovejoy and Gen Suwa argue from Ardi that chimpanzees and gorillas evolved knuckle-walking, large canines and other specialisations independently, and that the ancestor was a more generalised ape that clambered in trees on its palms."},{"label":"Broadly, yes","text":"Thomas Prang, Scott Williams and others argue that Ardi’s hand and features shared by the African apes point to an ancestor with suspensory adaptations like those of chimpanzees and bonobos, so the chimpanzee remains a reasonable, if imperfect, model."}],"status":"The dispute turns on how a few crushed fossils are read, and on whether the similarities of chimpanzees and gorillas are inherited or evolved in parallel. African ape fossils from 10–7 million years ago, still extremely rare, are needed."},{"question":"Did the human lineage begin outside Africa?","views":[{"label":"A European contribution","text":"Madelaine Böhme, Jochen Fuss and colleagues argue that Graecopithecus had hominin-like premolar roots about 7.2 million years ago and that Danuvius shows a way of moving that could precede bipedalism. Other researchers have proposed that 5.7-million-year-old footprints at Trachilos on Crete were made by a hominin."},{"label":"An African origin","text":"Most researchers answer that these claims rest on isolated teeth, fragmentary bones or ambiguous traces, whereas all securely identified early hominins, and both living African apes, are African."}],"status":"An African origin remains the consensus. European Miocene apes matter for understanding ape evolution as a whole, and better-preserved fossils could still change parts of the picture."},{"question":"Why did bipedalism evolve?","views":[{"label":"Advantages on the ground","text":"Reduced heat load (Peter Wheeler), cheaper walking between scattered food patches (Sockol, Raichlen and Pontzer, 2007) and carrying food or infants (Owen Lovejoy) all favour upright walking on the ground."},{"label":"Origins in the trees","text":"Kevin Hunt’s postural-feeding model and the arboreal-bipedalism model of Susannah Thorpe and Robin Crompton suggest that upright posture arose among branches and was later used on the ground."}],"status":"The earliest fossils come from wooded habitats and combine walking and climbing traits, which weakens a pure savanna explanation. Most researchers think several advantages combined, and that the first hominins were not yet the efficient long-distance walkers seen later."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Oldest proposed hominin","value":"Sahelanthropus tchadensis (Toumaï), about 7 million years, Chad"},{"label":"Oldest hominin femora","value":"Orrorin tugenensis, about 6 million years, Tugen Hills, Kenya"},{"label":"Ardi","value":"Partial skeleton of Ardipithecus ramidus, 4.4 million years, Aramis, Ethiopia; published 2009"},{"label":"Brain size","value":"About 300–370 cubic centimetres, similar to a chimpanzee"},{"label":"Human–chimpanzee split","value":"Genetic estimates range from about 5 to 13 million years ago"}],"siteIds":["toros-menalla","kapsomin","middle-awash","aramis","gona","pyrgos-vassilissis","hammerschmiede"],"routes":[],"themes":["species","climate"],"sources":[{"cite":"Brunet, M. et al. (2002). A new hominid from the Upper Miocene of Chad, Central Africa. Nature 418, 145–151."},{"cite":"Daver, G. et al. (2022). Postcranial evidence of late Miocene hominin bipedalism in Chad. Nature 609, 94–100.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-022-04901-z"},{"cite":"Cazenave, M. et al. (2025). Postcranial evidence does not support habitual bipedalism in Sahelanthropus tchadensis: a reply to Daver et al. (2022). Journal of Human Evolution 198, 103557."},{"cite":"Williams, S. A. et al. (2026). Earliest evidence of hominin bipedalism in Sahelanthropus tchadensis. Science Advances 12, eadv0130.","doi":"10.1126/sciadv.adv0130"},{"cite":"Richmond, B. G. & Jungers, W. L. (2008). Orrorin tugenensis femoral morphology and the evolution of hominin bipedalism. Science 319, 1662–1665.","doi":"10.1126/science.1154197"},{"cite":"White, T. D. et al. (2009). Ardipithecus ramidus and the paleobiology of early hominids. Science 326, 75–86."},{"cite":"Prang, T. C. et al. (2021). Ardipithecus hand provides evidence that humans and chimpanzees evolved from an ancestor with suspensory adaptations. Science Advances 7, eabf2474.","doi":"10.1126/sciadv.abf2474"},{"cite":"Moorjani, P. et al. (2016). Variation in the molecular clock of primates. PNAS 113, 10607–10612.","doi":"10.1073/pnas.1600374113"},{"cite":"Böhme, M. et al. (2019). A new Miocene ape and locomotion in the ancestor of great apes and humans. Nature 575, 489–493.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-019-1731-0"},{"cite":"Williams, S. A. et al. (2020). Reevaluating bipedalism in Danuvius. Nature 586.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-020-2736-4"}]},{"id":"australopiths","order":2,"act":"roots","title":"The Southern Apes","subtitle":"Australopithecus, Kenyanthropus and Paranthropus","era":"Pliocene and Early Pleistocene","start":4200000,"end":1000000,"dateLabel":"4.2–1 million years ago","regions":["africa"],"focus":[12,-30,45,18],"emblem":"footprints","summary":"For more than three million years Africa was home to several kinds of upright-walking apes at once, from Lucy’s species to the big-jawed Paranthropus. Fossils, footprints, isotopes and ancient proteins reveal a crowded, varied family rather than a single line leading to us.","paragraphs":[["About 3.66 million years ago, a volcano called Sadiman dusted the plains of northern Tanzania with fine ash.","Rain turned it into a kind of wet cement, and across it walked guinea fowl, hares, antelope and at least two upright apes.","Mary Leakey’s team uncovered their trail at Laetoli in 1978.","The prints show an arched foot and a big toe in line with the others, much like ours."],["They were probably made by Australopithecus afarensis, the species of Lucy, whose skeleton was found in Ethiopia in 1974.","Lucy stood little more than a metre tall and had a brain not much larger than a chimpanzee’s, yet she walked upright.","And she was not alone.","A second kind of footprint at Laetoli, a foot from Burtele with a grasping big toe, and a flat-faced skull from Kenya show that several kinds of hominin shared eastern Africa."],["Later came the robust species, Paranthropus, with huge molars and a crest of bone on top of the skull to anchor enormous chewing muscles.","Chemical traces in their teeth show that one of them ate mostly grasses and sedges.","Proteins recovered from enamel 2 million years old can now tell males from females, and a fossil hand from Kenya shows that they could grip with surprising dexterity."],["For some three million years these southern apes were the most successful hominins on Earth.","Our own genus would emerge among them, not after them."]],"sentences":["About 3.66 million years ago, a volcano called Sadiman dusted the plains of northern Tanzania with fine ash.","Rain turned it into a kind of wet cement, and across it walked guinea fowl, hares, antelope and at least two upright apes.","Mary Leakey’s team uncovered their trail at Laetoli in 1978.","The prints show an arched foot and a big toe in line with the others, much like ours.","They were probably made by Australopithecus afarensis, the species of Lucy, whose skeleton was found in Ethiopia in 1974.","Lucy stood little more than a metre tall and had a brain not much larger than a chimpanzee’s, yet she walked upright.","And she was not alone.","A second kind of footprint at Laetoli, a foot from Burtele with a grasping big toe, and a flat-faced skull from Kenya show that several kinds of hominin shared eastern Africa.","Later came the robust species, Paranthropus, with huge molars and a crest of bone on top of the skull to anchor enormous chewing muscles.","Chemical traces in their teeth show that one of them ate mostly grasses and sedges.","Proteins recovered from enamel 2 million years old can now tell males from females, and a fossil hand from Kenya shows that they could grip with surprising dexterity.","For some three million years these southern apes were the most successful hominins on Earth.","Our own genus would emerge among them, not after them."],"pronounce":{"Sadiman":"Sah-dee-man","Laetoli":"Lay-toe-lee","Australopithecus afarensis":"Oss-tra-lo-pith-eh-kus ah-far-en-sis","Burtele":"Bur-teh-leh","Paranthropus":"Par-an-thro-pus"},"body":[{"heading":"From anamensis to afarensis","paragraphs":["The genus Australopithecus, ‘southern ape’, appears about 4.2 million years ago. Its oldest species, A. anamensis, was named in 1995 by Meave Leakey and colleagues from Kanapoi and Allia Bay near Lake Turkana, Kenya; a shin bone from Kanapoi, with a knee built to carry the body’s weight directly over the ankle, shows that it walked upright. Then in 2016 a member of Yohannes Haile-Selassie’s team, Ali Bereino, found an almost complete cranium at Miro Dora in the Woranso-Mille area of Ethiopia. Published in 2019, the 3.8-million-year-old MRD cranium has a small braincase and a strongly projecting face.","MRD complicated a tidy story in which anamensis gradually turned into A. afarensis. Because a 3.9-million-year-old frontal bone from Belohdelie had been assigned to afarensis, Haile-Selassie’s team argued that the two species overlapped for at least 100,000 years, so afarensis may have budded off from part of the anamensis population. Not everyone accepts the attribution of the Belohdelie fossil, and the question remains open."]},{"heading":"Lucy’s world","paragraphs":["On 24 November 1974 Donald Johanson and Tom Gray spotted an arm bone in a gully at Hadar, in Ethiopia’s Afar region. The team recovered about 40 percent of one skeleton, AL 288-1, nicknamed Lucy after a Beatles song played in camp; in Ethiopia she is Dinkinesh, ‘you are marvellous’. Volcanic ash layers dated by argon–argon place her at about 3.2 million years. She stood about 1.1 metres tall, had long arms and curved fingers, but a pelvis and knee built for walking upright. Her species had brains of roughly 380–550 cubic centimetres.","A. afarensis, named in 1978, lived from about 3.9 to 2.9 million years ago. At site AL 333 in 1975 the Hadar team found more than 200 fossils from at least 13 individuals, the ‘First Family’. At Dikika, across the Awash River, Zeresenay Alemseged’s team found in 2000 the skeleton of a child, nicknamed Selam, who died at about three years old some 3.3 million years ago. Her shoulder blades resemble those of young gorillas, suggesting that she still climbed, and a 2020 study of afarensis braincases led by Philipp Gunz found an ape-like brain organisation combined with slow, prolonged brain growth, hinting at a long childhood.","How did Lucy die? In 2016 John Kappelman and colleagues argued from CT scans of fractures in her arm and other bones that she fell from a tall tree. Johanson and Tim White countered that such breaks are common among Hadar fossils and happened long after death, during burial and fossilisation."]},{"heading":"Footprints and feet","paragraphs":["Laetoli in northern Tanzania preserves the oldest known hominin footprints. Around 3.66 million years ago, ash from the Sadiman volcano settled on the landscape, was dampened by rain and hardened, recording the tracks of birds, hares, antelopes and hominins. In 1978 Mary Leakey’s team found Site G, a trail of about 70 prints left by two or three individuals walking with a human-like heel strike, arched foot and big toe aligned with the others. Prints found at Site S in 2015 include those of an individual perhaps 1.65 metres tall, evidence of large size differences within the species.","Prints at Site A, found in 1976, had been set aside as possibly made by a bear walking on its hind legs. Ellison McNutt, Jeremy DeSilva and colleagues re-excavated them in 2019 and showed in 2021 that a hominin made them, with a short, wide foot and a cross-stepping gait unlike that of the Site G walkers. Two kinds of bipedal hominin apparently lived at Laetoli.","Another foot comes from Burtele in the Woranso-Mille area: eight bones found in 2009, 3.4 million years old, with a big toe that could still grasp. In 2015 Haile-Selassie’s team named Australopithecus deyiremeda from jaws of the same age nearby, and in November 2025 the team reported in Nature new fossils linking the foot to that species. Carbon isotopes show that deyiremeda ate mostly foods from trees and shrubs, while afarensis in the same region had a more mixed diet."]},{"heading":"A crowded Pliocene","paragraphs":["In 1995 Michel Brunet’s team found a jaw, nicknamed Abel, at Koro Toro in Chad, about 3.5 million years old; named A. bahrelghazali, it placed australopiths 2,500 kilometres west of the Rift Valley. In 1999 Justus Erus found a flat-faced cranium, KNM-WT 40000, at Lomekwi west of Lake Turkana; Meave Leakey’s team named it Kenyanthropus platyops in 2001, about 3.5 million years old. Tim White argued that its flat face might partly reflect cracking and distortion. At Bouri in the Middle Awash, A. garhi, named in 1999, lived about 2.5 million years ago close to animal bones bearing early cut marks (chapter 3)."]},{"heading":"Caves of the south","paragraphs":["In 1924 quarry workers at Buxton Limeworks near Taung found a small skull with a natural cast of its brain. Raymond Dart described it in Nature in February 1925 as Australopithecus africanus, the ‘southern ape of Africa’, and argued that it was an upright human relative. Many British scholars, still misled by the Piltdown forgery, dismissed it for two decades. The Taung Child died aged about three; in 2006 Lee Berger showed that damage in its eye sockets matches the talon marks eagles leave on monkey prey.","At Sterkfontein, Robert Broom and John Robinson blasted out an adult africanus cranium, Sts 5, in April 1947, nicknamed Mrs Ples, although it may be male. In 1994 Ronald Clarke recognised hominin foot bones in a box of fossils from the cave, and in 1997 his assistants Stephen Motsumi and Nkwane Molefe found the matching broken bone in the cave wall. Twenty years of painstaking excavation produced Little Foot, StW 573, the most complete australopith skeleton known, unveiled in 2017.","Dating these caves is notoriously hard. In 2015 Darryl Granger and colleagues used the decay of aluminium-26 and beryllium-10 in buried quartz to date Little Foot to about 3.67 million years, whereas uranium–lead dates on flowstones had suggested an age nearer 2.2 million. In 2022 the same method dated the deposit containing Mrs Ples and most Sterkfontein australopiths to about 3.4–3.7 million years, a million years older than thought and contemporary with Lucy. Little Foot’s species is disputed too: Clarke assigns it to A. prometheus, while a 2025 study led by Jesse Martin argued that it fits neither prometheus nor africanus and may be a new species.","The youngest australopith is A. sediba from Malapa, about 15 kilometres from Sterkfontein. In August 2008 nine-year-old Matthew Berger, son of Lee Berger, found a bone that led to two partial skeletons, a boy and an adult female, about 1.98 million years old. Sediba combined a small brain with a strikingly human-like hand and pelvis. Berger proposed it as a possible ancestor of Homo, but since Homo fossils are older, most researchers see it as a late relative that evolved some Homo-like traits independently."]},{"heading":"The robust ones","paragraphs":["By about 2.7 million years ago a very different branch had appeared: Paranthropus, with massive jaws, huge flat molars, flaring cheekbones and, in males, a bony crest on top of the skull to anchor enormous chewing muscles. P. aethiopicus is best known from the ‘Black Skull’ (KNM-WT 17000), found west of Lake Turkana in 1985 and about 2.5 million years old. In July 1959 Mary Leakey found the cranium OH 5 at Olduvai Gorge; Louis Leakey named it Zinjanthropus boisei, and as P. boisei it earned the nickname Nutcracker Man. In South Africa, Robert Broom named P. robustus in 1938 from Kromdraai; it is also well known from Swartkrans and Drimolen.","Isotopes overturned the nutcracker image. In 2011 Thure Cerling and colleagues showed that P. boisei enamel records a diet of roughly three-quarters C4 plants, probably grasses and sedges, more like a grazing animal than any living ape, and its teeth lack the micro-pitting produced by cracking hard nuts. P. robustus ate a more mixed diet. For the older australopiths of Sterkfontein, a 2025 study of nitrogen isotopes in enamel led by Tina Lüdecke found values typical of plant eaters, suggesting that meat was at most a small part of their diet.","Molecules now add detail. In 2025 Palesa Madupe and colleagues read enamel proteins from four P. robustus teeth from Swartkrans, about 2 million years old. Peptides of the Y-linked form of amelogenin identified two males, and the other two were inferred to be female, results that did not always match the sex suggested by tooth size. One individual differed from the others at the protein level, hinting at more diversity within Paranthropus than anatomy has recognised.","In October 2025 Carrie Mongle and colleagues described a partial skeleton from Koobi Fora in Kenya, about 1.52 million years old, whose hand bones were found with unmistakable P. boisei teeth and jaw. Its thumb-to-finger proportions are human-like enough to allow tool use, while other features resemble gorilla hands and suggest a powerful grip. In 2024 Kevin Hatala’s team reported a 1.5-million-year-old lakeshore surface at Koobi Fora where Paranthropus and Homo erectus left footprints, probably within hours of each other, each with a distinctive gait. P. boisei vanished about 1.3 million years ago and P. robustus perhaps around 1 million years ago."]}],"debates":[{"question":"How old is Little Foot, and what was it?","views":[{"label":"About 3.67 million years","text":"Darryl Granger, Ronald Clarke and colleagues argue that cosmogenic burial dating of the rock around the skeleton gives 3.67 million years, that the flowstones used for younger dates formed later in voids, and that a 2024 study of the associated animal fossils also points to about 3.6 million years."},{"label":"Younger","text":"Researchers including Robyn Pickering, Jan Kramers and Andy Herries have argued from uranium–lead dates, palaeomagnetism and cave stratigraphy for younger ages, and a 2022 analysis of monkey fossils from Sterkfontein’s Member 4 suggested roughly 2.6–2.0 million years for that deposit."}],"status":"Recent work leans towards an age of about 3.6 million years, but Sterkfontein’s cave fills are complex and agreement is incomplete. The species question, A. prometheus, A. africanus or something new, is separately unresolved."},{"question":"How many hominin species shared the Pliocene?","views":[{"label":"Many","text":"Yohannes Haile-Selassie, Meave Leakey, Ellison McNutt and others read A. deyiremeda, Kenyanthropus platyops, A. bahrelghazali and the Laetoli Site A prints as evidence that several hominins lived at the same time, with different diets and ways of moving."},{"label":"Fewer","text":"Tim White and others have argued that some of these species rest on distorted or fragmentary fossils that fall within the variation of A. afarensis, and that naming species from single jaws or skulls inflates diversity."}],"status":"The 2025 fossils linking the Burtele foot to A. deyiremeda, with isotopic evidence of a distinct diet, strengthened the case for at least two species in the Afar around 3.4 million years ago. Kenyanthropus remains contested until better-preserved specimens are found."},{"question":"Did Lucy die falling from a tree?","views":[{"label":"A fatal fall","text":"John Kappelman and colleagues (2016) argued that compressive fractures in Lucy’s shoulder, arm, knee and other bones formed at death and match those of people who fall from a height and land feet first."},{"label":"Damage after death","text":"Donald Johanson and Tim White replied that similar breaks are common in Hadar fossils of many animals and arise from burial, sediment pressure and weathering long after death."}],"status":"Unresolved. Without soft tissue or a clear way to tell fresh from fossil breaks at this age, the fall remains a plausible but unproven hypothesis."},{"question":"How different were australopith males and females?","views":[{"label":"Strongly dimorphic","text":"Henry McHenry and later Adam Gordon and colleagues (2008) estimated that A. afarensis males were far larger than females, as in gorillas or orangutans, which would suggest intense competition between males."},{"label":"Modest, human-like","text":"Philip Reno and Owen Lovejoy (2003, 2015) used Lucy as a template to estimate the sizes of the AL 333 individuals and concluded that dimorphism was close to that of modern humans, consistent with reduced male competition."}],"status":"Estimates depend on whether more than one species is mixed in the sample and on how sex is assigned. Enamel proteins, which in 2025 gave reliable sex for Paranthropus, might eventually test the estimates directly if recovered from older fossils."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Lucy","value":"AL 288-1, A. afarensis, about 3.2 million years, Hadar (Ethiopia), found 1974"},{"label":"Oldest hominin footprints","value":"Laetoli, Tanzania, about 3.66 million years"},{"label":"Most complete australopith","value":"Little Foot (StW 573), Sterkfontein, probably about 3.67 million years"},{"label":"Neighbours","value":"A. afarensis and A. deyiremeda in the Afar, about 3.4 million years ago"},{"label":"Paranthropus boisei diet","value":"About three-quarters C4 plants such as grasses and sedges"},{"label":"Last robust australopith","value":"P. robustus, around 1 million years ago in South Africa"}],"siteIds":["kanapoi","woranso-mille","hadar","dikika","laetoli","koro-toro","taung","sterkfontein","malapa","olduvai-gorge","swartkrans","koobi-fora"],"routes":[],"themes":["species","food","climate"],"sources":[{"cite":"Haile-Selassie, Y. et al. (2019). A 3.8-million-year-old hominin cranium from Woranso-Mille, Ethiopia. Nature 573, 214–219."},{"cite":"Kappelman, J. et al. (2016). Perimortem fractures in Lucy suggest mortality from fall out of tall tree. Nature 537, 503–507."},{"cite":"McNutt, E. J. et al. (2021). Footprint evidence of early hominin locomotor diversity at Laetoli, Tanzania. Nature 600, 468–471.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-021-04187-7"},{"cite":"Haile-Selassie, Y. et al. (2025). New finds shed light on diet and locomotion in Australopithecus deyiremeda. Nature.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-025-09714-4"},{"cite":"Granger, D. E. et al. (2022). Cosmogenic nuclide dating of Australopithecus at Sterkfontein, South Africa. PNAS 119, e2123516119.","doi":"10.1073/pnas.2123516119"},{"cite":"Martin, J. M. et al. (2025). The StW 573 Little Foot fossil should not be attributed to Australopithecus prometheus. American Journal of Biological Anthropology.","doi":"10.1002/ajpa.70177"},{"cite":"Cerling, T. E. et al. (2011). Diet of Paranthropus boisei in the early Pleistocene of East Africa. PNAS 108, 9337–9341."},{"cite":"Madupe, P. P. et al. (2025). Enamel proteins reveal biological sex and genetic variability in southern African Paranthropus. Science 388, 969.","doi":"10.1126/science.adt9539"},{"cite":"Mongle, C. S. et al. (2025). New fossils reveal the hand of Paranthropus boisei. Nature 647.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-025-09594-8"},{"cite":"Hatala, K. G. et al. (2024). Footprint evidence for locomotor diversity and shared habitats among early Pleistocene hominins. Science 386, 1004–1010.","doi":"10.1126/science.ado5275"}]},{"id":"first-tools","order":3,"act":"toolmakers","title":"The First Toolmakers","subtitle":"Lomekwi, Nyayanga and the Oldowan","era":"Early Stone Age (Lomekwian and Oldowan)","start":3300000,"end":1700000,"dateLabel":"3.3–1.7 million years ago","regions":["africa"],"focus":[0,-5,45,38],"emblem":"chopper","summary":"More than 3 million years ago, hominins in eastern Africa began breaking stones on purpose to make sharp edges. From Lomekwi’s heavy cores to the Oldowan flakes of Nyayanga, Gona and Olduvai, the first technology spread across Africa, and it may not have been invented by our own genus.","paragraphs":[["Strike one cobble of lava against another at just the right angle, and a sharp flake peels away, with an edge keener than any tooth or claw.","Around 3.3 million years ago, west of Lake Turkana in Kenya, someone was doing exactly that.","At a site called Lomekwi 3, archaeologists found heavy cores, flakes and stone anvils, the oldest known knapped tools, older than any fossil of our own genus."],["By about 2.6 million years ago a more controlled way of flaking, the Oldowan, appears in Ethiopia and Kenya.","At Nyayanga, near the shore of Lake Victoria, toolmakers used simple flakes to butcher hippopotamuses and to pound plants, sometime between 3 million and 2.6 million years ago.","The only hominin fossils found there are teeth of Paranthropus, the robust cousin of our ancestors."],["The Oldowan spread across Africa, from Algeria to Tanzania, and for more than half a million years it barely changed.","Capuchin monkeys in Brazil produce similar flakes by accident when they bang stones together, and chimpanzees in West Africa leave behind their own nut-cracking stones.","So archaeologists must ask a hard question: was the Oldowan a tradition, taught and learned, or an easy trick that any clever primate could reinvent?"],["What is clear is that the first toolmakers did not need big brains.","The old idea that stone tools define our genus, and that Homo invented them, no longer fits the evidence."]],"sentences":["Strike one cobble of lava against another at just the right angle, and a sharp flake peels away, with an edge keener than any tooth or claw.","Around 3.3 million years ago, west of Lake Turkana in Kenya, someone was doing exactly that.","At a site called Lomekwi 3, archaeologists found heavy cores, flakes and stone anvils, the oldest known knapped tools, older than any fossil of our own genus.","By about 2.6 million years ago a more controlled way of flaking, the Oldowan, appears in Ethiopia and Kenya.","At Nyayanga, near the shore of Lake Victoria, toolmakers used simple flakes to butcher hippopotamuses and to pound plants, sometime between 3 million and 2.6 million years ago.","The only hominin fossils found there are teeth of Paranthropus, the robust cousin of our ancestors.","The Oldowan spread across Africa, from Algeria to Tanzania, and for more than half a million years it barely changed.","Capuchin monkeys in Brazil produce similar flakes by accident when they bang stones together, and chimpanzees in West Africa leave behind their own nut-cracking stones.","So archaeologists must ask a hard question: was the Oldowan a tradition, taught and learned, or an easy trick that any clever primate could reinvent?","What is clear is that the first toolmakers did not need big brains.","The old idea that stone tools define our genus, and that Homo invented them, no longer fits the evidence."],"pronounce":{"Lomekwi":"Lo-meh-kwee","Nyayanga":"Nyah-yahn-gah","Oldowan":"Ol-doh-wan","Paranthropus":"Par-an-thro-pus","Turkana":"Tur-kah-nah"},"body":[{"heading":"How to break a stone","paragraphs":["Knapping relies on conchoidal fracture. Fine-grained, brittle rocks such as flint, chert, obsidian, fine basalt and quartzite break like thick glass: a blow near the edge of a cobble, struck at an angle of less than 90 degrees to a flat ‘striking platform’, sends a cone of force through the stone and detaches a flake with a smooth, shell-like surface. The flake keeps the marks of the blow, a bulb of percussion and ripple lines, and the core keeps a matching scar. Series of scars struck from the same platform, and flakes that refit onto their core, distinguish deliberate knapping from breakage by rivers, rockfalls or trampling animals.","The skill lies in choosing the right stone, angle and force, and in planning each blow from the scars left by the last. Brain-imaging studies by Dietrich Stout and colleagues show that even simple Oldowan knapping recruits networks for visual and motor coordination. Three techniques appear early: freehand percussion with a hammerstone; bipolar percussion, with the core resting on an anvil; and passive hammer, in which the core is swung against a stationary anvil."]},{"heading":"Lomekwi 3: older than Homo","paragraphs":["In July 2011 a team led by Sonia Harmand and Jason Lewis, surveying west of Lake Turkana, took a wrong turn and found stone artefacts on a hillside. Excavations at Lomekwi 3 recovered 149 artefacts, including cores, flakes, anvils and hammerstones, some weighing several kilograms. Published in Nature in 2015, they were dated to about 3.3 million years by argon–argon dating of nearby volcanic ash layers and by palaeomagnetism, some 700,000 years before the oldest known Oldowan tools.","Harmand’s team named the industry the Lomekwian. Its makers relied mainly on anvil-based techniques and produced large, irregular flakes, with less control than later Oldowan knappers. In 2016 Manuel Domínguez-Rodrigo and Luis Alcalá questioned whether the artefacts really came from the 3.3-million-year-old layer, since many were collected on the surface and the in situ finds might have come from younger sediments. The excavators answered with detailed geological mapping. No second site of the same age has yet been found."]},{"heading":"Cut marks at Dikika","paragraphs":["Stone use may be older still. In 2010 Shannon McPherron, Zeresenay Alemseged and colleagues reported two animal bones from Dikika, Ethiopia, a rib of a cow-sized animal and a thigh bone of a goat-sized one, with marks they interpreted as cutting flesh and hammering for marrow, dated to about 3.39 million years ago. No stone tools were found with them. Domínguez-Rodrigo, Travis Pickering and Henry Bunn argued that the marks were made when bones were trampled in coarse sand. If the marks are genuine, they were probably made by Australopithecus afarensis, the only hominin known in the area, perhaps using naturally sharp stones."]},{"heading":"The Oldowan appears","paragraphs":["The Oldowan, named by Mary Leakey after Olduvai Gorge, where she classified its tools in a 1971 monograph, consists of cores, sharp flakes, hammerstones and battered pieces. Its knappers struck flakes with controlled freehand blows, often turning a core to find new platforms. For decades the oldest examples came from Gona in Ethiopia’s Afar, where Sileshi Semaw and colleagues reported tools 2.6–2.5 million years old in 1997. In 2019 David Braun and colleagues described slightly older tools from Bokol Dora 1 at Ledi-Geraru, 2.61–2.58 million years old, in the same research area as the oldest known jaw of Homo (chapter 4). They differ subtly from the Gona tools, hinting at more than one early tradition.","In 2023 a team led by Thomas Plummer extended the early Oldowan far to the south-west. At Nyayanga on the Homa Peninsula, by Lake Victoria in Kenya, they excavated 330 artefacts dated to between about 3.0 and 2.6 million years ago by argon–argon, palaeomagnetism and associated fossils. Cut marks and fractures on hippopotamus bones show butchery, and microscopic wear on the tools shows that they were used on animal tissue and on plants, including starchy parts. The only hominin remains are two Paranthropus molars, among the oldest fossils of the genus.","A 2025 study in Nature Communications, led by David Braun, followed the Oldowan at Namorotukunan in Kenya’s Turkana Basin through about 300,000 years, from roughly 2.75 to 2.44 million years ago. Despite environmental upheavals, including droughts and fires recorded in the same sequence, the knappers kept selecting good stone and making tools the same way, suggesting a stable, well-learned skill."]},{"heading":"Across Africa","paragraphs":["By 2 million years ago Oldowan toolmakers were widespread. At Kanjera South, also on the Homa Peninsula, Plummer’s excavations show that hominins carried good stone more than 10 kilometres and repeatedly butchered small antelopes, apparently whole, and the heads of larger ones, evidence for regular meat eating. At Ain Boucherit in northeastern Algeria, a team led by Mohamed Sahnouni reported in 2018 Oldowan tools and cut-marked bones dated by palaeomagnetism, electron spin resonance and animal fossils to about 2.4 and 1.9 million years ago. If the older date holds, toolmaking either spread across the continent quickly or began in more than one place; some researchers suspect the layers are younger.","At Olduvai Gorge, sites in Bed I from about 1.9 million years ago preserve dense concentrations of tools and animal bones that shaped debates about early human behaviour (chapter 4). The Oldowan continued in many places long after the first handaxes appeared about 1.75 million years ago, and it was the technology carried into Asia by the first hominins to leave Africa (chapter 6)."]},{"heading":"Other primates, other stones","paragraphs":["Stone tool use is not unique to hominins, which both helps and complicates the search for the first tools. West African chimpanzees crack nuts with stone hammers, and in 2007 Julio Mercader and colleagues excavated chimpanzee nut-cracking stones about 4,300 years old at Noulo in Côte d’Ivoire’s Taï Forest. In 2016 Tomos Proffitt and colleagues showed that wild bearded capuchins in Brazil’s Serra da Capivara National Park, pounding quartzite cobbles, unintentionally detach flakes with conchoidal fractures that resemble some early hominin flakes; capuchins there have used stones for at least 3,000 years. Long-tailed macaques in Thailand produce similar debris when cracking nuts and shellfish.","Percussion with stones is therefore probably ancient among primates, and sharp flakes can arise without intent. What marks the hominin record is the repeated, deliberate production of flakes, their transport across the landscape and their use on meat and plants. Lomekwi 3 may have emerged from a long, nearly invisible phase of pounding and cracking."]},{"heading":"Learning to knap","paragraphs":["Oldowan tools look simple, but making them well takes practice. In 2015 Thomas Morgan and colleagues taught volunteers Oldowan knapping in chains of learners, each passing the skill on by a different means: letting the next person study the products, demonstrating, gesturing or talking. Learners taught with gestures and especially with speech produced more usable flakes, and the authors suggested that toolmaking and teaching may have co-evolved, favouring early forms of language. Other experiments, by William Snyder, Claudio Tennie and colleagues in 2022, found that people who had never seen knapping could work out how to detach sharp flakes on their own, supporting the view that Oldowan flaking lay within reach of individual invention."]},{"heading":"Who made them?","paragraphs":["For decades the answer seemed obvious: Homo habilis, the ‘handy man’, named in 1964 partly because of the Oldowan tools at Olduvai. The evidence now points to a wider cast. Lomekwi 3 is older than any known Homo; the nearest hominin fossils in time and place are Kenyanthropus platyops and Australopithecus afarensis. Cut-marked bones at Bouri lie near fossils of A. garhi. At Nyayanga, Paranthropus is the only hominin found. Early Homo is present in the Ledi-Geraru area near the oldest Oldowan tools, but not at the tool site itself.","Anatomy supports a broad view. In 2015 Matthew Skinner and colleagues found that the internal bone structure of A. africanus hand bones reflects forceful, human-like grips, and in 2025 Paranthropus boisei hand bones from Koobi Fora showed human-like thumb and finger proportions (chapter 2). Several kinds of hominin, with brains little larger than a chimpanzee’s, probably made and used stone tools."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Are the Lomekwi 3 artefacts the oldest stone tools?","views":[{"label":"Yes, 3.3 million years old","text":"Sonia Harmand, Jason Lewis and colleagues argue that artefacts excavated in place, with clear knapping scars and refits, lie in sediments securely dated by volcanic ash and palaeomagnetism to 3.3 million years."},{"label":"Not yet proven","text":"Manuel Domínguez-Rodrigo and Luis Alcalá (2016) and other sceptics note that many pieces were surface finds, question the link between the excavated artefacts and the dated layer, and call for a second site of similar age before rewriting the story."}],"status":"Lomekwi 3 is widely cited as the oldest evidence of knapping, but it remains a single site. The discovery of comparable artefacts elsewhere, or new work at Lomekwi, would settle the question."},{"question":"Were the Dikika bones butchered 3.39 million years ago?","views":[{"label":"Butchery marks","text":"Shannon McPherron, Zeresenay Alemseged and colleagues argue that the marks’ shapes, positions and internal features match stone-tool cuts and percussion rather than trampling."},{"label":"Trampling","text":"Manuel Domínguez-Rodrigo, Travis Pickering and Henry Bunn argue that the marks match experimental damage from bones trampled in abrasive sand, and note that no tools were found."}],"status":"Still debated in 2026. Methods that separate cut marks from trampling statistically have improved, but two bones are a small sample; more marked bones from the same period would be decisive."},{"question":"Who made the first stone tools?","views":[{"label":"Early Homo","text":"The traditional view links tools to Homo, whose earliest fossil, LD 350-1 at 2.8 million years, comes from the same area as the oldest Oldowan tools, and whose smaller teeth may reflect food processed with tools."},{"label":"Other hominins too","text":"Sonia Harmand, Thomas Plummer and others point out that Lomekwi 3 predates known Homo, that Paranthropus is the only hominin at Nyayanga, that A. garhi lived near cut-marked bones at Bouri, and that australopith and Paranthropus hands could make tools."}],"status":"Most researchers now accept that tool use was not unique to Homo and that several hominins probably made tools. Direct proof, such as a toolmaker’s hand found with tools, is still lacking."},{"question":"Was early knapping a cultural tradition?","views":[{"label":"Taught and learned","text":"Dietrich Stout, Thomas Morgan and others argue that controlled Oldowan flaking takes practice and benefits from teaching, and that consistent, long-lived traditions such as those at Namorotukunan imply social transmission."},{"label":"Reinvented by individuals","text":"Claudio Tennie, William Snyder and colleagues argue that basic flaking is a ‘latent solution’ that individuals can rediscover, as capuchins and naive human volunteers show, so its stability need not imply culture."}],"status":"Both processes probably played a part. Regional differences between early sites, if confirmed, would support learned traditions; better experiments on how quickly naive knappers reach Oldowan standards would help."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Oldest knapped tools","value":"Lomekwi 3, Kenya, about 3.3 million years (published 2015)"},{"label":"Oldest Oldowan","value":"Nyayanga (3.0–2.6 million years) and Bokol Dora 1, Ledi-Geraru (2.61–2.58 million years)"},{"label":"Oldest claimed cut marks","value":"Dikika, Ethiopia, about 3.39 million years (disputed)"},{"label":"Early Oldowan in North Africa","value":"Ain Boucherit, Algeria, about 2.4 million years (debated)"},{"label":"Early big-game butchery","value":"Hippopotamus bones at Nyayanga"},{"label":"Non-human stone archaeology","value":"Chimpanzee nut-cracking stones 4,300 years old, Côte d’Ivoire"}],"siteIds":["lomekwi-3","dikika","nyayanga","ledi-geraru","gona","bouri","ain-boucherit","kanjera-south","olduvai-gorge"],"routes":[],"themes":["technology","food","animals","species"],"sources":[{"cite":"Harmand, S. et al. (2015). 3.3-million-year-old stone tools from Lomekwi 3, West Turkana, Kenya. Nature 521, 310–315.","doi":"10.1038/nature14464"},{"cite":"McPherron, S. P. et al. (2010). Evidence for stone-tool-assisted consumption of animal tissues before 3.39 million years ago at Dikika, Ethiopia. Nature 466, 857–860."},{"cite":"Domínguez-Rodrigo, M. & Alcalá, L. (2016). 3.3-million-year-old stone tools and butchery traces? More evidence needed. PaleoAnthropology 2016, 46–53."},{"cite":"Semaw, S. et al. (1997). 2.5-million-year-old stone tools from Gona, Ethiopia. Nature 385, 333–336."},{"cite":"Braun, D. R. et al. (2019). Earliest known Oldowan artifacts at >2.58 Ma from Ledi-Geraru, Ethiopia, highlight early technological diversity. PNAS 116, 11712–11717."},{"cite":"Plummer, T. W. et al. (2023). Expanded geographic distribution and dietary strategies of the earliest Oldowan hominins and Paranthropus. Science 379, 561–566."},{"cite":"Braun, D. R. et al. (2025). Early Oldowan technology thrived during Pliocene environmental change in the Turkana Basin, Kenya. Nature Communications 16."},{"cite":"Sahnouni, M. et al. (2018). 1.9-million- and 2.4-million-year-old artifacts and stone tool-cutmarked bones from Ain Boucherit, Algeria. Science 362, 1297–1301."},{"cite":"Proffitt, T. et al. (2016). Wild monkeys flake stone tools. Nature 539, 85–88."},{"cite":"Mercader, J. et al. (2007). 4,300-year-old chimpanzee sites and the origins of percussive stone technology. PNAS 104, 3043–3048."},{"cite":"Morgan, T. J. H. et al. (2015). Experimental evidence for the co-evolution of hominin tool-making teaching and language. Nature Communications 6, 6029."}]},{"id":"genus-homo","order":4,"act":"toolmakers","title":"The Genus Homo","subtitle":"Early Homo, bigger brains and the meat question","era":"Early Stone Age (Oldowan)","start":2800000,"end":1400000,"dateLabel":"2.8–1.4 million years ago","regions":["africa"],"focus":[26,-12,45,14],"emblem":"hand","summary":"Our genus appears in eastern Africa about 2.8 million years ago, in landscapes shared with other hominins. Homo habilis and H. rudolfensis, butchered carcasses at Olduvai and the first systematic bone tools raise two old questions: what counts as Homo, and did meat make us human?","paragraphs":[["In January 2013, a graduate student named Chalachew Seyoum was climbing a slope at Ledi-Geraru, in Ethiopia’s Afar region, when he saw a tooth poking out of the ground.","It belonged to half of a lower jaw, and that jaw, about 2.8 million years old, is the oldest fossil yet assigned to our own genus, Homo."],["Its owner lived only a few hundred thousand years after Lucy’s kind, and not far away.","But the first members of our genus did not simply replace the southern apes.","For more than a million years, eastern Africa was home to several kinds of hominin at once: the handy man, Homo habilis, named from Olduvai Gorge in 1964; the flat-faced Homo rudolfensis; the big-jawed Paranthropus; and, from about 2 million years ago, Homo erectus.","At Ileret in Kenya, fossils show that habilis and erectus lived side by side for half a million years."],["These hominins carried stone flakes to carcasses, sliced off meat and smashed bones for marrow.","At a place called FLK Zinj, thousands of animal bones and stone tools lie together.","Were the butchers bold hunters or cautious scavengers, and did the extra fat and protein fuel the growth of bigger brains?","A study in 2022 warned that the evidence for rising meat eating partly reflects where archaeologists have chosen to dig."],["One discovery is new and clear.","By 1.5 million years ago at Olduvai, someone was knapping tools not only from stone, but from the leg bones of elephants and hippos."]],"sentences":["In January 2013, a graduate student named Chalachew Seyoum was climbing a slope at Ledi-Geraru, in Ethiopia’s Afar region, when he saw a tooth poking out of the ground.","It belonged to half of a lower jaw, and that jaw, about 2.8 million years old, is the oldest fossil yet assigned to our own genus, Homo.","Its owner lived only a few hundred thousand years after Lucy’s kind, and not far away.","But the first members of our genus did not simply replace the southern apes.","For more than a million years, eastern Africa was home to several kinds of hominin at once: the handy man, Homo habilis, named from Olduvai Gorge in 1964; the flat-faced Homo rudolfensis; the big-jawed Paranthropus; and, from about 2 million years ago, Homo erectus.","At Ileret in Kenya, fossils show that habilis and erectus lived side by side for half a million years.","These hominins carried stone flakes to carcasses, sliced off meat and smashed bones for marrow.","At a place called FLK Zinj, thousands of animal bones and stone tools lie together.","Were the butchers bold hunters or cautious scavengers, and did the extra fat and protein fuel the growth of bigger brains?","A study in 2022 warned that the evidence for rising meat eating partly reflects where archaeologists have chosen to dig.","One discovery is new and clear.","By 1.5 million years ago at Olduvai, someone was knapping tools not only from stone, but from the leg bones of elephants and hippos."],"pronounce":{"Chalachew Seyoum":"Cha-la-chew Seh-yoom","Ledi-Geraru":"Leh-dee Geh-rah-roo","Olduvai":"Ol-doo-vye","rudolfensis":"roo-dolf-en-sis","Ileret":"Ee-leh-ret","Paranthropus":"Par-an-thro-pus"},"body":[{"heading":"The jaw from Ledi-Geraru","paragraphs":["On 29 January 2013 Chalachew Seyoum, a graduate student at Arizona State University, found the left half of a lower jaw with several teeth at Ledi-Geraru in the Afar. Published in Science in 2015 by Brian Villmoare, William Kimbel and colleagues, LD 350-1 lies between volcanic ashes dated by argon–argon to 2.80–2.75 million years. It combines a primitive, receding chin region like that of Australopithecus with slim molars and symmetrical premolars like those of later Homo. A companion study led by Erin DiMaggio showed that the landscape had become more open, with grassland and trees along rivers.","In 2025 the Ledi-Geraru team reported in Nature new teeth dated between about 2.8 and 2.6 million years ago. Some belong to early Homo; others belong to an Australopithecus that matches neither A. afarensis, which had disappeared by about 2.95 million years ago, nor A. garhi. The Afar therefore held at least two hominin lineages when Homo appeared, and the australopith may be a species still without a name. Other early Homo fossils include a 2.33-million-year-old upper jaw from Hadar (A.L. 666-1), found with Oldowan tools, and a jaw from Uraha in Malawi, roughly 2.4 million years old."]},{"heading":"Handy man","paragraphs":["The genus Homo was once defined by a big brain. In 1960, at FLK NN in Olduvai Gorge, Jonathan Leakey, eldest son of Louis and Mary Leakey, found the jaw, skull bones and hand bones of a juvenile, OH 7, about 1.8 million years old. In 1964 Louis Leakey, Phillip Tobias and John Napier named it Homo habilis, ‘handy man’, a name suggested by Raymond Dart, and redefined Homo to admit brains below the 750-cubic-centimetre ‘cerebral Rubicon’ proposed by Arthur Keith. They argued that habilis, not the Paranthropus found nearby, made the Oldowan tools.","Further finds filled out the species: the crushed skull OH 24, nicknamed ‘Twiggy’; the skull KNM-ER 1813 from Koobi Fora, about 1.9 million years old, with a brain of only about 510 cubic centimetres; and OH 62, a fragmentary partial skeleton found in 1986 that suggests long arms and short legs. Brain sizes in habilis range from roughly 500 to 700 cubic centimetres. In 2015 Fred Spoor and colleagues reconstructed the distorted OH 7 jaw digitally and found it surprisingly primitive, with a long, narrow dental arcade, while estimating its brain at roughly 730–820 cubic centimetres. They concluded that the Homo lineage had split from Australopithecus well before 2.3 million years ago, consistent with LD 350-1."]},{"heading":"The flat-faced one","paragraphs":["In 1972 Bernard Ngeneo, a member of Richard Leakey’s team at Koobi Fora east of Lake Turkana, found fragments of a skull that became KNM-ER 1470. Reconstructed from many pieces, it had a brain of about 750 cubic centimetres and a long, flat face. First thought to be nearly 3 million years old, it was redated to about 1.9 million years after a long controversy over the age of a volcanic ash, the KBS Tuff. Its differences from habilis led Valerii Alexeev in 1986 to name a second species, Homo rudolfensis, after Lake Rudolf, the old name for Lake Turkana.","For decades 1470 stood almost alone. In 2012 Meave Leakey, Fred Spoor and colleagues described three new Koobi Fora fossils dated to 1.95–1.78 million years, including the face of a juvenile (KNM-ER 62000) and an almost complete lower jaw (KNM-ER 60000). The face matched 1470 and the jaw fitted it, strengthening the case that two species of early Homo besides H. erectus lived around Lake Turkana at the same time."]},{"heading":"What counts as Homo?","paragraphs":["In 1999 Bernard Wood and Mark Collard argued in Science that habilis and rudolfensis resemble australopiths more than later humans in body size and shape, limb proportions, jaws, teeth and growth, and should be moved to Australopithecus. On this view Homo begins with H. erectus, the first hominin with a humanlike body. Susan Antón, Richard Potts and Leslie Aiello argued in 2014 for keeping a diverse early Homo, in which traits of the ‘human package’, such as larger brains, smaller teeth, longer legs and wider ranging, appeared piecemeal in different species.","Part of the problem is that most early Homo fossils are isolated jaws, teeth or skulls, and few can be linked to a skeleton. It is hard to decide whether LD 350-1, A.L. 666-1 or the Uraha jaw belong to habilis, rudolfensis or something else, and many researchers simply speak of ‘early Homo’."]},{"heading":"Bones at FLK Zinj","paragraphs":["In 1959, the season Mary Leakey found the Paranthropus boisei cranium OH 5, the Leakeys excavated the surrounding level at FLK in Bed I of Olduvai Gorge. FLK Zinj, about 1.84 million years old, produced thousands of stone artefacts and bone fragments from dozens of large animals, mostly antelopes, concentrated in a few hundred square metres. It became the test case for how early hominins obtained meat.","Lewis Binford argued in 1981 that hominins were marginal scavengers, picking at scraps left by carnivores. Robert Blumenschine’s experiments in the 1980s and 1990s, comparing tooth marks with hammerstone marks, suggested that carnivores often reached the bones first and hominins then broke them for marrow. Henry Bunn and Ellen Kroll, and later Manuel Domínguez-Rodrigo and colleagues, countered that cut marks cluster on the meaty mid-shafts of limb bones, which predators strip quickly, so hominins must often have had early access to fleshed carcasses, by hunting or by driving predators away."]},{"heading":"Did meat make us human?","paragraphs":["Brains are costly: the human brain is about 2 percent of body mass but uses around 20 percent of resting energy. In 1995 Leslie Aiello and Peter Wheeler proposed the expensive-tissue hypothesis: the brain could only grow if another costly organ, the gut, shrank, which required a richer, more digestible diet such as meat and marrow. Butchery marks from about 2.6 million years ago onward, and the larger brains of early Homo and especially H. erectus, seemed to fit.","In 2022 a team led by Andrew Barr tested the pattern with the zooarchaeological record of eastern Africa between 2.6 and 1.2 million years ago. They found that the apparent jump in evidence for carnivory after H. erectus appeared tracks a jump in research effort: more sites and more bones have been studied from the later period. Once sampling was taken into account, there was no sustained increase. Meat was clearly eaten, but its role as the driver of brain growth needs other support. Alternatives include underground plant foods, cooking and help from grandmothers in feeding children."]},{"heading":"Tools of bone","paragraphs":["In 2025 a team led by Ignacio de la Torre reported in Nature 27 bone tools from the T69 Complex at Olduvai Gorge, about 1.5 million years old. Most were made from limb bones of elephants and hippopotamuses and knapped with a hammerstone, like stone cores, to create long cutting or heavy-duty edges. Hominins at Swartkrans and Drimolen in South Africa had used bone fragments to dig into termite mounds, but the Olduvai tools are the oldest evidence of systematic bone knapping, roughly a million years before the practice became common. Their makers could have been H. erectus or Paranthropus boisei, both present nearby."]},{"heading":"Living side by side","paragraphs":["The idea of a single ladder from habilis to erectus was undermined in 2007, when Fred Spoor, Meave Leakey and colleagues described two fossils from Ileret, east of Lake Turkana: an upper jaw of H. habilis (KNM-ER 42703) about 1.44 million years old, and a small braincase of H. erectus (KNM-ER 42700) about 1.55 million years old. The two species evidently shared the basin for roughly half a million years, presumably in different ecological niches, alongside Paranthropus boisei. Around 1.9 million years ago the Turkana Basin may have held four hominin species at once."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Where does the genus Homo begin?","views":[{"label":"About 2.8 million years ago","text":"Brian Villmoare, William Kimbel and colleagues assign LD 350-1 to Homo on the basis of its slim molars and premolar shape, and the 2025 Ledi-Geraru teeth add further early Homo specimens alongside a separate australopith."},{"label":"With Homo erectus","text":"Bernard Wood and Mark Collard (1999) argued that habilis and rudolfensis lack the body, growth pattern and diet that define our genus, so Homo should begin with erectus about 2 million years ago; others note that a single jaw cannot establish a genus."}],"status":"Most researchers accept a Homo lineage by about 2.8 million years ago, but where to draw the genus boundary depends on definitions. Associated skulls and skeletons of the earliest Homo are the missing evidence."},{"question":"One species of early Homo or two?","views":[{"label":"Two species","text":"Meave Leakey, Fred Spoor and colleagues (2012) argue that the flat, long face of 1470 and KNM-ER 62000 and the distinctive jaw of KNM-ER 60000 separate H. rudolfensis from H. habilis."},{"label":"One variable species","text":"Phillip Tobias and later researchers who stress the size differences between males and females in living apes have treated 1470 and the habilis fossils as one variable species."}],"status":"The 2012 finds persuaded many that two species existed, but the boundaries of each remain debated, and some fossils fit neither comfortably."},{"question":"Hunters or scavengers at FLK Zinj?","views":[{"label":"Early access","text":"Henry Bunn, Ellen Kroll and Manuel Domínguez-Rodrigo argue that cut marks on meat-bearing bone shafts show that hominins butchered fleshed carcasses, obtained by hunting or by chasing predators away."},{"label":"Scavenging","text":"Lewis Binford and Robert Blumenschine argued that carnivores often fed first, leaving hominins scraps and marrow, as tooth marks beneath percussion marks suggest."}],"status":"Many zooarchaeologists now favour early access to at least some carcasses, but marks can form in several ways, and the balance of hunting and scavenging probably varied between sites and species."},{"question":"Did meat drive brain expansion?","views":[{"label":"Meat made the difference","text":"Following Leslie Aiello and Peter Wheeler (1995), many researchers argue that energy-dense animal foods allowed a smaller gut and a larger brain, and that butchery evidence tracks the rise of Homo."},{"label":"The evidence is biased","text":"Andrew Barr and colleagues (2022) showed that the apparent rise in butchery after about 2 million years ago mirrors research effort; others emphasise plant foods, cooking or cooperative childcare."}],"status":"Meat eating by early Homo is certain; its causal role in brain growth is open. Standardised sampling across sites and new dietary tracers such as nitrogen and zinc isotopes in enamel may help."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Oldest fossil of Homo","value":"LD 350-1 jaw, 2.80–2.75 million years, Ledi-Geraru (Ethiopia)"},{"label":"Homo habilis named","value":"1964, from OH 7 at Olduvai Gorge"},{"label":"Brain size of early Homo","value":"About 500–800 cubic centimetres"},{"label":"Famous butchery site","value":"FLK Zinj, Olduvai, about 1.84 million years"},{"label":"Oldest systematic bone tools","value":"T69 Complex, Olduvai, about 1.5 million years (2025)"},{"label":"Side by side","value":"H. erectus at 1.55 and H. habilis at 1.44 million years, Ileret (Kenya)"}],"siteIds":["ledi-geraru","hadar","uraha","koobi-fora","flk-zinj","olduvai-gorge","ileret","swartkrans"],"routes":[],"themes":["species","food","technology","animals"],"sources":[{"cite":"Villmoare, B. et al. (2015). Early Homo at 2.8 Ma from Ledi-Geraru, Afar, Ethiopia. Science 347, 1352–1355."},{"cite":"Villmoare, B. et al. (2025). New discoveries of Australopithecus and Homo from Ledi-Geraru, Ethiopia. Nature."},{"cite":"Leakey, L. S. B., Tobias, P. V. & Napier, J. R. (1964). A new species of the genus Homo from Olduvai Gorge. Nature 202, 7–9."},{"cite":"Wood, B. & Collard, M. (1999). The human genus. Science 284, 65–71."},{"cite":"Leakey, M. G. et al. (2012). New fossils from Koobi Fora in northern Kenya confirm taxonomic diversity in early Homo. Nature 488, 201–204."},{"cite":"Spoor, F. et al. (2007). Implications of new early Homo fossils from Ileret, east of Lake Turkana, Kenya. Nature 448, 688–691."},{"cite":"Bunn, H. T. & Kroll, E. M. (1986). Systematic butchery by Plio/Pleistocene hominids at Olduvai Gorge, Tanzania. Current Anthropology 27, 431–452."},{"cite":"Aiello, L. C. & Wheeler, P. (1995). The expensive-tissue hypothesis: the brain and the digestive system in human and primate evolution. Current Anthropology 36, 199–221."},{"cite":"Barr, W. A. et al. (2022). No sustained increase in zooarchaeological evidence for carnivory after the appearance of Homo erectus. PNAS 119, e2115540119."},{"cite":"de la Torre, I. et al. (2025). Systematic bone tool production at 1.5 million years ago. Nature."}]},{"id":"erectus-acheulean","order":5,"act":"toolmakers","title":"Walkers and Handaxe Makers","subtitle":"Homo erectus and the Acheulean in Africa","era":"Early Stone Age (Acheulean)","start":2000000,"end":200000,"dateLabel":"2 million–200,000 years ago","regions":["africa"],"focus":[15,-30,45,15],"emblem":"handaxe","summary":"Around 2 million years ago a new kind of human appeared in Africa: taller, longer-legged and built for long days on the move. Homo erectus created the Acheulean handaxe, a design that lasted more than 1.5 million years, and may have been the first hominin to use fire.","paragraphs":[["In August 1984, Kamoya Kimeu, the most celebrated fossil hunter of his generation, noticed a small piece of skull on a slope near the Nariokotome River, west of Lake Turkana.","Over the following years the team uncovered almost an entire skeleton: a boy of about eight who died some 1.5 million years ago.","He is known as the Turkana Boy."],["He belonged to Homo erectus, and his body tells a new story.","Long legs, narrow hips and a tall, slender build suit long-distance walking in the heat, probably with plenty of sweating to keep cool.","Footprints at Ileret, 1.5 million years old, show feet with arches like ours.","Some researchers think these people could run animals to exhaustion under the midday sun, though others doubt it."],["Homo erectus also made something new.","From about 1.75 million years ago, at Kokiselei in Kenya and Konso in Ethiopia, knappers began shaping large flakes on both faces into teardrop-shaped handaxes.","The Acheulean, as this tradition is called, lasted for more than 1.5 million years, one of the longest-lived designs ever made.","Its best makers achieved a symmetry that still puzzles archaeologists, who debate whether it served use, teaching or display."],["There are first hints of fire too: reddened earth at Koobi Fora, and ash deep inside Wonderwerk Cave in South Africa about a million years ago.","And through it all, the climate of eastern Africa swung between wet and dry, testing these travellers again and again."]],"sentences":["In August 1984, Kamoya Kimeu, the most celebrated fossil hunter of his generation, noticed a small piece of skull on a slope near the Nariokotome River, west of Lake Turkana.","Over the following years the team uncovered almost an entire skeleton: a boy of about eight who died some 1.5 million years ago.","He is known as the Turkana Boy.","He belonged to Homo erectus, and his body tells a new story.","Long legs, narrow hips and a tall, slender build suit long-distance walking in the heat, probably with plenty of sweating to keep cool.","Footprints at Ileret, 1.5 million years old, show feet with arches like ours.","Some researchers think these people could run animals to exhaustion under the midday sun, though others doubt it.","Homo erectus also made something new.","From about 1.75 million years ago, at Kokiselei in Kenya and Konso in Ethiopia, knappers began shaping large flakes on both faces into teardrop-shaped handaxes.","The Acheulean, as this tradition is called, lasted for more than 1.5 million years, one of the longest-lived designs ever made.","Its best makers achieved a symmetry that still puzzles archaeologists, who debate whether it served use, teaching or display.","There are first hints of fire too: reddened earth at Koobi Fora, and ash deep inside Wonderwerk Cave in South Africa about a million years ago.","And through it all, the climate of eastern Africa swung between wet and dry, testing these travellers again and again."],"pronounce":{"Kamoya Kimeu":"Ka-moy-ah Kee-may-oo","Nariokotome":"Nar-ee-oh-ko-toh-meh","Ileret":"Ee-leh-ret","Kokiselei":"Ko-kee-seh-lay","Konso":"Kon-so","Acheulean":"Ah-shoo-lee-an","Wonderwerk":"Von-der-verk","Koobi Fora":"Koo-bee For-ah"},"body":[{"heading":"A new kind of human","paragraphs":["Homo erectus was named from Java, where Eugène Dubois found a skullcap in 1891, but its roots are African. The earliest securely dated specimen is DNH 134, the braincase of a child of two or three from Drimolen in South Africa, dated to 2.04–1.95 million years ago and published in 2020 by Andy Herries, Stephanie Baker and colleagues. At Koobi Fora in Kenya, a skull fragment (KNM-ER 2598) may be about 1.9 million years old, and the well-preserved skull KNM-ER 3733 is about 1.6 million years old. Some researchers call the early African fossils Homo ergaster, a name given in 1975 to a Koobi Fora jaw, and reserve H. erectus for Asian fossils; others see one widespread, variable species.","Compared with earlier hominins, H. erectus had brains of about 600 to 900 cubic centimetres at first and more than 1,000 later, smaller teeth and jaws, a taller, more linear body and modern proportions of arms to legs. Its teeth grew faster than ours, suggesting a childhood shorter than a modern human’s but longer than an ape’s."]},{"heading":"The Turkana Boy","paragraphs":["In August 1984 Kamoya Kimeu, leader of Richard Leakey’s fossil-hunting team, spotted a skull fragment near the Nariokotome River west of Lake Turkana. Excavations led by Leakey and Alan Walker over several seasons recovered the skull, jaw, spine, ribs, pelvis and limbs of one individual, KNM-WT 15000, about 1.5 million years old: the most complete early human skeleton known. Growth lines in his teeth indicate that he died at about eight years old, already around 1.6 metres tall. Early estimates that he would have reached 1.85 metres as an adult have since been revised downward.","The skeleton shows long legs, a barrel-shaped rib cage and narrow hips, a build that loses heat efficiently in hot, open country. Earlier suggestions that he suffered from a skeletal disorder have been reconsidered; a 2014 re-examination attributed the asymmetry of his spine to a slipped disc in the lower back."]},{"heading":"Built to walk, and perhaps to run","paragraphs":["Footprints confirm the anatomy. In 2009 Matthew Bennett and colleagues described prints at Ileret, east of Lake Turkana, about 1.5 million years old, with a pronounced arch, a big toe in line with the others and a human-like transfer of weight. Later excavations reported by Kevin Hatala in 2016 revealed nearly 100 prints of at least 20 individuals, whose sizes suggest groups that included several adult males moving along the lake edge.","H. erectus probably also lost most of its body hair and relied on sweating to shed heat. In 2004 Dennis Bramble and Daniel Lieberman proposed that the genus evolved for endurance running, pointing to the nuchal ligament that steadies the head, an enlarged gluteus maximus, long Achilles tendons, short toes and large balance canals in the inner ear. Running could have helped scavengers reach carcasses first, or allowed hunters to chase animals in the heat until they collapsed.","Critics such as Travis Pickering and Henry Bunn argued in 2007 that such persistence hunting demands tracking skills and open terrain that early Homo may have lacked, and that the anatomy is equally well explained by long-distance walking. In 2024 Eugène Morin and Bruce Winterhalder compiled some 400 historical and ethnographic accounts of running hunts from around the world and argued that such hunts can be energetically efficient, reviving the hypothesis without settling when the behaviour began."]},{"heading":"The first handaxes","paragraphs":["About 1.75 million years ago, toolmakers in eastern Africa began striking large flakes, often more than 10 centimetres long, from boulders and shaping them on both faces into handaxes, cleavers and picks. This is the Acheulean, named after Saint-Acheul in northern France, where such tools were collected in the nineteenth century. The oldest securely dated examples come from Kokiselei 4 near Lake Turkana, about 1.76 million years old (Christopher Lepre and colleagues, 2011), and from Konso in southern Ethiopia, about 1.75 million years old (Yonas Beyene and colleagues, 2013). FLK West at Olduvai Gorge has handaxes about 1.7 million years old.","Later Acheulean tools became thinner and more symmetrical, though not in a steady progression. At Konso, refined handaxes appear by about 850,000 years ago, and a handaxe knapped from a hippopotamus thigh bone about 1.4 million years old was described in 2020. In 2023 a team led by Margherita Mussi reported that H. erectus lived at Melka Kunture in the Ethiopian highlands, about 2,000 metres above sea level, close to 2 million years ago, making Oldowan tools and soon afterward some of the earliest Acheulean ones. At Simbiro III, part of the same site complex, knappers about 1.2 million years ago mass-produced handaxes from obsidian. Oldowan-style toolkits persisted alongside the Acheulean, and in Africa the Acheulean gave way to the Middle Stone Age between about 300,000 and 200,000 years ago."]},{"heading":"Why so symmetrical?","paragraphs":["Handaxes are effective tools. Experiments show that they butcher large animals well, with long, durable edges that can be resharpened and enough weight for heavy cutting, and many carry wear traces from cutting meat, wood or plants. Yet many are more symmetrical than the work strictly requires, which has prompted other explanations.","One links handaxes to teaching and language. Studies by Dietrich Stout and colleagues show that learning to make a good handaxe takes many hours of practice, often hundreds, and that it engages regions of the inferior frontal cortex that overlap with those used in language; demanding skills may have favoured teaching and communication. Another, the ‘sexy handaxe’ hypothesis of Marek Kohn and Steven Mithen (1999), proposed that fine symmetrical handaxes advertised the maker’s skill, planning and health to potential mates, which would explain why some were made in large numbers and apparently little used. Critics reply that symmetry emerges naturally when knappers maintain an even edge, and that the display idea is hard to test."]},{"heading":"Olorgesailie and a changing climate","paragraphs":["Olorgesailie, in the southern Kenya Rift, is strewn with thousands of Acheulean handaxes. Mary and Louis Leakey began work there in 1942, and Rick Potts of the Smithsonian Institution has led research since 1985. Its sediments record Acheulean activity from about 1.2 million to about 500,000 years ago as lakes rose and dried and faults and volcanoes reshaped the basin; a small H. erectus braincase about 900,000 years old was found there in 2003.","From such records Potts developed the variability selection hypothesis (1996, 1998): key human traits, such as larger brains, flexible behaviour and wide-ranging movement, evolved not because Africa steadily dried into savanna but because environments swung ever more widely between wet and dry. Drill cores from the Olorgesailie area, analysed in 2020, show a marked increase in environmental variability after about 400,000 years ago, when the Acheulean there was replaced by new technologies (chapter 11). Other researchers stress long-term drying and the spread of grasslands, as in Peter deMenocal’s dust records from ocean cores, or short pulses of extreme variability marked by deep, short-lived lakes, as proposed by Mark Maslin and Martin Trauth."]},{"heading":"First sparks","paragraphs":["Chapter 9 examines fire in depth, but the earliest claims involve H. erectus in Africa. At Koobi Fora site FxJj20, about 1.5 million years old, reddened patches of sediment and heated stone artefacts, most recently studied by Sarah Hlubik and colleagues, suggest fire close to where hominins worked, although natural fires are hard to rule out. At Swartkrans, C. K. Brain and Andrew Sillen reported burnt bones in 1988 in deposits about 1 million years old or older. The strongest early case is Wonderwerk Cave in South Africa, where Francesco Berna and colleagues (2012) found microscopic ash of burnt grasses and leaves, and burnt bone, in Acheulean layers about 1 million years old, some 30 metres inside the cave.","Richard Wrangham argues that cooking began with H. erectus about 1.9 million years ago, explaining its smaller teeth and gut and larger brain. The archaeological record does not yet show regular fire use that early; habitual use becomes clear only within the last 400,000 years or so. Either cooking began earlier than the evidence shows, or the anatomical changes had other causes, such as better tools and more meat."]},{"heading":"A long run","paragraphs":["H. erectus was one of the longest-lived human species. In Africa, the Daka skullcap from Bouri in Ethiopia and the Buia skull from Eritrea, both about 1 million years old, show it still flourishing, and African populations probably gave rise to later forms of Homo (chapter 10). Beyond Africa, where it spread early (chapter 6), H. erectus survived on Java until about 117,000–108,000 years ago."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Were early Homo built to run?","views":[{"label":"Endurance runners","text":"Dennis Bramble and Daniel Lieberman (2004) argue that a suite of features absent in australopiths, from the nuchal ligament to the Achilles tendon, evolved for running, enabling scavenging races and persistence hunting; Eugène Morin and Bruce Winterhalder (2024) found hundreds of historical records of running hunts."},{"label":"Walkers first","text":"Travis Pickering and Henry Bunn (2007) and others argue that the same traits serve long-distance walking and carrying, that persistence hunting needs tracking skills and terrain early Homo may not have had, and that butchery evidence better fits ambush or scavenging."}],"status":"Few doubt that H. erectus was an efficient long-distance walker. Whether running shaped its evolution remains contested, since fossils cannot show how often it ran."},{"question":"Why were handaxes symmetrical?","views":[{"label":"Function","text":"Many experimental archaeologists argue that symmetry produces balanced, durable edges that are easy to resharpen, so it follows from use in butchery and woodworking."},{"label":"Teaching and language","text":"Dietrich Stout and colleagues argue that making standardised handaxes required long apprenticeships and shared goals, favouring teaching and possibly aspects of language."},{"label":"Display","text":"Marek Kohn and Steven Mithen (1999) proposed that fine handaxes signalled the maker’s quality to potential mates, explaining apparently unused and oversized examples."}],"status":"These explanations are not mutually exclusive. Use-wear studies, brain imaging of knappers and large statistical studies of handaxe shape continue to test them; the display hypothesis has the least direct support."},{"question":"Did Homo erectus cook?","views":[{"label":"Cooking from about 1.9 million years ago","text":"Richard Wrangham argues that smaller teeth, a reduced gut and a larger brain in H. erectus imply cooked food, and that early fire traces at Koobi Fora and Wonderwerk support this."},{"label":"Fire came later","text":"Wil Roebroeks, Paola Villa and others argue that convincing evidence of habitual fire appears only in the last few hundred thousand years, and that early traces may reflect occasional use of natural fire."}],"status":"Occasional fire use by about 1 million years ago is now widely accepted for Wonderwerk; regular cooking at 1.9 million years ago lacks archaeological support. Chapter 9 returns to the question."},{"question":"Did climate variability drive human evolution?","views":[{"label":"Variability selection","text":"Rick Potts argues that increasingly unstable environments favoured flexible, wide-ranging, innovative hominins, citing the Olorgesailie basin and its drill cores."},{"label":"Drying and pulses","text":"Peter deMenocal emphasised long-term aridification and grassland expansion, while Mark Maslin and Martin Trauth proposed that brief phases of extreme variability, with deep lakes appearing and vanishing, drove bursts of speciation and dispersal."}],"status":"Drill-core programmes across eastern Africa now allow these ideas to be tested against detailed records. Most researchers see climate as important but its effects as varying from basin to basin."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Oldest Homo erectus","value":"DNH 134, Drimolen (South Africa), 2.04–1.95 million years"},{"label":"Turkana Boy","value":"KNM-WT 15000, about 1.5 million years, found 1984 by Kamoya Kimeu"},{"label":"Oldest handaxes","value":"Kokiselei 4, Kenya (1.76 million years) and Konso, Ethiopia (1.75 million years)"},{"label":"Acheulean in Africa","value":"About 1.75 million to 200,000 years ago"},{"label":"Early fire","value":"Wonderwerk Cave, about 1 million years; debated traces at Koobi Fora about 1.5 million"},{"label":"Modern feet","value":"Ileret footprints, Kenya, about 1.5 million years"}],"siteIds":["drimolen","koobi-fora","nariokotome","ileret","kokiselei","konso","olduvai-gorge","melka-kunture","olorgesailie","bouri","swartkrans","wonderwerk-cave"],"routes":[],"themes":["species","technology","fire","climate","food"],"sources":[{"cite":"Herries, A. I. R. et al. (2020). Contemporaneity of Australopithecus, Paranthropus, and early Homo erectus in South Africa. Science 368, eaaw7293."},{"cite":"Bennett, M. R. et al. (2009). Early hominin foot morphology based on 1.5-million-year-old footprints from Ileret, Kenya. Science 323, 1197–1201."},{"cite":"Bramble, D. M. & Lieberman, D. E. (2004). Endurance running and the evolution of Homo. Nature 432, 345–352."},{"cite":"Morin, E. & Winterhalder, B. (2024). Ethnography and ethnohistory support the efficiency of hunting through endurance running in humans. Nature Human Behaviour."},{"cite":"Lepre, C. J. et al. (2011). An earlier origin for the Acheulian. Nature 477, 82–85."},{"cite":"Beyene, Y. et al. (2013). The characteristics and chronology of the earliest Acheulean at Konso, Ethiopia. PNAS 110, 1584–1591."},{"cite":"Mussi, M. et al. (2023). Early Homo erectus lived at high altitudes and produced both Oldowan and Acheulean tools. Science 382, 713–718."},{"cite":"Kohn, M. & Mithen, S. (1999). Handaxes: products of sexual selection? Antiquity 73, 518–526."},{"cite":"Potts, R. (1998). Variability selection in hominid evolution. Evolutionary Anthropology 7, 81–96."},{"cite":"Berna, F. et al. (2012). Microstratigraphic evidence of in situ fire in the Acheulean strata of Wonderwerk Cave, Northern Cape province, South Africa. PNAS 109, E1215–E1220."}]},{"id":"out-of-africa-first","order":6,"act":"toolmakers","title":"Out of Africa, the First Time","subtitle":"Shangchen, Dmanisi, Java, Zhoukoudian and the Movius Line","era":"Early and Middle Pleistocene; Lower Palaeolithic","start":2100000,"end":100000,"dateLabel":"about 2.1 million–100,000 years ago","regions":["asia"],"focus":[25,-12,125,48],"emblem":"globe","summary":"Hominins reached the Caucasus, China and Java far earlier than once thought: stone tools on the Chinese Loess Plateau are 2.1 million years old, and Homo erectus survived on Java until about 110,000 years ago.","paragraphs":[["About 2.1 million years ago, on the edge of a vast plateau of wind-blown dust in central China, someone struck sharp flakes from lumps of quartz and left them behind.","Chinese geologists found those flakes at a place called Shangchen, sealed in layers of ancient soil.","They are older than any fossil of Homo erectus found outside Africa, and nobody knows who made them."],["The earliest travellers whose bones we do have lived at Dmanisi, in the foothills of the Caucasus, about 1.8 million years ago.","Beneath the ruins of a medieval town, excavators found five skulls.","The brains were small and the bodies short, yet their legs were built for long walks.","One elderly individual had lost nearly every tooth years before death, and some researchers think others must have helped that person eat."],["From the Caucasus and the Levant, the trail runs across Asia.","On Java, a Dutch army doctor named Eugène Dubois dug a skullcap from a riverbank in 1891 and gave the world its first Homo erectus.","Near Beijing, the bones of Peking Man came out of a limestone cave, and in 1941, as war spread across the Pacific, the original fossils vanished and have never been found."],["This was not one great migration but many small drifts of hunters and foragers following game, water and stone.","On Java their descendants lasted astonishingly long.","The last known Homo erectus lived beside the Solo River until about 110,000 years ago, more than one and a half million years after the species first appeared."]],"sentences":["About 2.1 million years ago, on the edge of a vast plateau of wind-blown dust in central China, someone struck sharp flakes from lumps of quartz and left them behind.","Chinese geologists found those flakes at a place called Shangchen, sealed in layers of ancient soil.","They are older than any fossil of Homo erectus found outside Africa, and nobody knows who made them.","The earliest travellers whose bones we do have lived at Dmanisi, in the foothills of the Caucasus, about 1.8 million years ago.","Beneath the ruins of a medieval town, excavators found five skulls.","The brains were small and the bodies short, yet their legs were built for long walks.","One elderly individual had lost nearly every tooth years before death, and some researchers think others must have helped that person eat.","From the Caucasus and the Levant, the trail runs across Asia.","On Java, a Dutch army doctor named Eugène Dubois dug a skullcap from a riverbank in 1891 and gave the world its first Homo erectus.","Near Beijing, the bones of Peking Man came out of a limestone cave, and in 1941, as war spread across the Pacific, the original fossils vanished and have never been found.","This was not one great migration but many small drifts of hunters and foragers following game, water and stone.","On Java their descendants lasted astonishingly long.","The last known Homo erectus lived beside the Solo River until about 110,000 years ago, more than one and a half million years after the species first appeared."],"pronounce":{"Shangchen":"Shahng-chun","Dmanisi":"Dmah-nee-see","Eugène Dubois":"Oo-zhen Doo-bwah","Solo":"Soh-loh"},"body":[{"heading":"Tools older than the species","paragraphs":["For most of the twentieth century, the human story outside Africa began with Homo erectus leaving the continent around 1 million years ago. That date has been pushed back again and again. The most dramatic push came in 2018, when a team led by Zhu Zhaoyu of the Chinese Academy of Sciences, working with the British archaeologist Robin Dennell, reported 96 stone artefacts from Shangchen, on the southern edge of the Chinese Loess Plateau near Lantian in Shaanxi province.","The Loess Plateau is a gigantic archive of dust. In cold, dry glacial phases wind laid down loess; in warmer, wetter phases the surface weathered into soil. By tying this layer cake to reversals of Earth’s magnetic field, the team placed the artefacts in 17 separate layers spanning about 2.12 to 1.26 million years ago. The tools are simple flakes, cores, notched and pointed pieces, mostly of quartz and quartzite from the nearby Qinling Mountains. Their age is what matters: they are about 270,000 years older than Dmanisi and older than any Homo erectus fossil found outside Africa. No bones of the toolmakers have been found."]},{"heading":"Dmanisi: small brains at the gates of Asia","paragraphs":["Dmanisi sits on a basalt promontory in southern Georgia where the Mashavera and Pinezauri rivers meet, beneath the ruins of a medieval town. Archaeologists digging out medieval grain pits noticed fossil animal bones in the 1980s, and in 1991 a hominin jaw appeared. Since then the site has yielded five skulls, several jaws, parts of skeletons and thousands of simple Oldowan-style stone tools. In 2011 Reid Ferring and colleagues used volcanic ash, argon dating and magnetic reversals to show that people first arrived shortly after 1.85 million years ago and returned repeatedly until about 1.78 million years ago; most fossils are about 1.77 million years old.","The Dmanisi people were short, roughly 1.5 metres tall, with brains of about 550 to 730 cubic centimetres, barely more than Homo habilis. Yet their legs and feet were built for long-distance walking. They shared the landscape with sabre-toothed cats, giant hyenas and wolves, and cut marks on animal bones show they butchered carcasses. The most complete adult skull of any early Homo, Skull 5, combines the smallest brain of the group, 546 cubic centimetres, with a huge projecting face; its cranium turned up in 2005, five years after its matching jaw. In 2013 David Lordkipanidze and colleagues argued in Science that all five skulls fall within the variation of a single population.","Skull 4 belonged to an elderly individual who had lost all but one tooth several years before death: the bony sockets had fully healed over. Chewing raw meat or tough plants would have been hard, and the excavators suggested that companions helped, perhaps by sharing soft food. Sceptics note that marrow and brain could be eaten without teeth, so care is a plausible reading rather than a proven one."]},{"heading":"The Levantine corridor and ʿUbeidiya","paragraphs":["Any land route out of Africa passes through the Levant. At ʿUbeidiya, about three kilometres south of the Sea of Galilee in Israel’s Jordan Valley, tilted beds of ancient lake and river sediment contain handaxes, picks and choppers among the bones of African and Eurasian animals, plus a handful of hominin fragments. For decades the site was dated by its fauna to roughly 1.6–1.2 million years ago.","In 2026 a team led by Ari Matmon, Omry Barzilai and Miriam Belmaker combined cosmogenic nuclide dating, palaeomagnetism and uranium–lead dating of Melanopsis snail shells and concluded that the deposits are at least 1.9 million years old. If that holds, ʿUbeidiya is as old as Dmanisi, and makers of Acheulean handaxes left Africa almost as early as makers of simple Oldowan tools. Because it would put ʿUbeidiya’s handaxes as early as the oldest Acheulean in Africa itself, the result will be tested hard."]},{"heading":"Java: from Dubois to the drowned plains","paragraphs":["Java produced the first Homo erectus ever found. The Dutch army doctor Eugène Dubois went to the Dutch East Indies expressly to find a ‘missing link’, and in 1891 his workers uncovered a skullcap on the bank of the Solo River at Trinil; a thighbone followed in 1892. He named the creature Pithecanthropus erectus. From the 1930s Ralph von Koenigswald and later Indonesian researchers made Sangiran, an eroded dome of sediments north of Surakarta, the richest Homo erectus locality in Asia and now a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","When Homo erectus reached Java has swung back and forth. In 1994 Carl Swisher and colleagues argued from argon dating for about 1.8–1.6 million years. In 2020 Shuji Matsu’ura and colleagues dated zircon crystals from the Sangiran volcanic layers with fission-track and uranium–lead methods, two independent clocks that agreed, and concluded that the oldest hominins there are younger than 1.5 million years and most likely about 1.3 million. Some researchers still defend older ages, but the younger chronology is now widely used.","Trinil keeps surprising. In 2014 Josephine Joordens and colleagues re-examined freshwater mussel shells Dubois had collected. One bears a zigzag scratched with a hard point; others have a hole punched exactly where the muscle that closes the shell attaches, a neat way of opening it. Argon and luminescence dating of sediment inside the shells gave 540,000–430,000 years, making the zigzag the oldest known deliberate geometric engraving.","The species lasted on Java for an extraordinarily long time. Between 1931 and 1933 Dutch surveyors dug twelve skullcaps and two shinbones from a bone bed 20 metres above the Solo River at Ngandong. Estimates of their age ranged from about 550,000 to 27,000 years until 2020, when Yan Rizal, Kira Westaway and colleagues combined 52 dates from several methods to show that a flood washed the bones in between 117,000 and 108,000 years ago: the last known appearance of Homo erectus anywhere. In 2025 researchers described two Homo erectus skull fragments, about 140,000 years old, dredged from the floor of the Madura Strait, showing that these populations also roamed the lowland plains of Sundaland now under the sea."]},{"heading":"China: Peking Man and the northern frontier","paragraphs":["The oldest well-dated hominin fossil in China is the Gongwangling cranium, found in 1964 a few kilometres from Shangchen. A 2015 magnetostratigraphic study pushed its age from about 1.15 to about 1.63 million years; it has a thick vault and a small brain. Far to the northeast, in the Nihewan Basin west of Beijing at about 40 degrees north, the Majuangou sites contain stone tools and cut-marked bones dated by palaeomagnetism to about 1.66 million years, an age a 2024 burial-dating study broadly confirmed. Hominins were enduring hard northern winters well over a million years ago.","The most famous Chinese site is Locality 1 at Zhoukoudian, a collapsed limestone cave about 50 kilometres southwest of Beijing. Excavations between the 1920s and 1937, directed at various times by Davidson Black, Pei Wenzhong and Franz Weidenreich, produced remains of roughly forty individuals, including several skullcaps. Black named them Sinanthropus pekinensis in 1927; they are now classed as Homo erectus. In late 1941 the original fossils were packed for shipment to the United States to keep them from the Japanese army, and disappeared. Weidenreich’s casts and descriptions survive.","In 2009 Shen Guanjun and colleagues dated quartz grains from the lower layers by measuring aluminium-26 and beryllium-10, isotopes created by cosmic rays that decay at known rates once sediment is buried. The answer, about 770,000 years, was roughly 300,000 years older than usually assumed. Zhoukoudian was also long celebrated as the first home of fire, but in 1998 Steve Weiner and colleagues found no intact hearths or ash in the classic layers. Excavations since 2009 have reported burnt bones and heated sediment in an upper layer, which Gao Xing and colleagues interpret as fire maintained by hominins; the wider debate is taken up in the chapter on fire and wood."]},{"heading":"India and the line that Movius drew","paragraphs":["South Asia lies on every plausible route east, yet its early record is thin and fossils are almost absent. The key site is Attirampakkam, near Chennai, where Shanti Pappu and colleagues excavated more than 3,500 artefacts, including handaxes and cleavers, from a trench over 9 metres deep. In 2011 they dated the quartzite tools themselves with cosmogenic aluminium and beryllium: the lowest Acheulean levels are about 1.5 million years old. Handaxe makers were in southern India within a few hundred thousand years of the Acheulean’s first appearance in Africa.","In 1944 the Harvard archaeologist Hallam Movius drew a line through northern India. West of it, handaxes were common; east of it, in East and Southeast Asia, he saw only ‘chopper and chopping tool’ industries, which he read as a sign of a culturally backward region. The Movius Line became a textbook fixture. Its prejudice has long been discarded, and its facts have eroded. In 1978 an American soldier, Greg Bowen, found handaxes at Jeongok-ri in South Korea. In 2000 Hou Yamei and colleagues reported Acheulean-like handaxes from the Bose Basin in southern China, in layers containing tektites, glassy debris from a meteorite impact, dated by argon to about 803,000 years ago. Handaxes are now known from central China too, though they remain rarer in the east."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Did hominins older than Homo erectus leave Africa?","views":[{"label":"Yes, the first travellers predate erectus","text":"Zhu Zhaoyu, Robin Dennell and others point out that the Shangchen tools, at about 2.12 million years, are as old as the earliest Homo erectus fossils in Africa, about 2 million years old at Drimolen in South Africa. The small brains and primitive build of the Dmanisi people, and the ape-like wrist and foot of Homo floresiensis on Flores, fit a scenario in which a pre-erectus hominin, something like Homo habilis, left Africa first. Contested older claims, such as river-gravel tools at Xihoudu in northern China dated in 2020 to about 2.43 million years, would push this further if they held."},{"label":"No, erectus was first","text":"Others note that no hominin fossil outside Africa is older than Dmanisi, and that the Dmanisi skulls, despite their small brains, share key features with Homo erectus. Erectus-like fossils in Africa approach 2 million years, so an early erectus dispersal soon after the species arose could still explain Shangchen. Claims such as Xihoudu are widely doubted because rivers can knock natural flakes off stones."}],"status":"Open. Most specialists accept Shangchen’s age, but without fossils the toolmaker’s identity is unknown. A skeleton or datable teeth from Asia older than 2 million years, ideally with ancient proteins, would settle it."},{"question":"Were the Dmanisi fossils one species or several?","views":[{"label":"One variable population","text":"David Lordkipanidze, Christoph Zollikofer, Marcia Ponce de León and colleagues argued in 2013 that the five skulls, all from a short span of time, differ from each other no more than individuals of living chimpanzees or humans do. By that yardstick, some African fossils assigned to Homo habilis, Homo rudolfensis and Homo erectus might belong to one evolving lineage, with Dmanisi as an early form of Homo erectus."},{"label":"Two or more species","text":"Jeffrey Schwartz, Ian Tattersall and others replied that the differences, especially Skull 5’s enormous face and jaw, exceed normal variation. A 2025 study of the crown areas of the back teeth by Victor Nery, Walter Neves and colleagues found that Skull 5’s jaw groups with australopiths while other Dmanisi jaws group with Homo, and supported two coexisting species: Homo georgicus for Skull 5 and Homo caucasi for the others."}],"status":"Most researchers still treat Dmanisi as one early Homo erectus population, because the fossils come from the same deposits and a short time span, and tooth size alone is a weak guide to species. The 2025 study reopened the question without overturning the consensus."},{"question":"What does the Movius Line really mean?","views":[{"label":"A real difference in frequency","text":"Stephen Lycett, Christopher Norton and Christopher Bae accept that handaxes are far rarer east of India and argue that this needs explaining. Possible reasons: the first migrants left Africa before the Acheulean existed; small, scattered populations lost complex skills; and in the tropics bamboo and poor-quality stone served instead, an idea proposed by Geoffrey Pope. Norton and colleagues suggested keeping a ‘Movius Line in the broad sense’ to describe these differences."},{"label":"Largely an artefact of research history","text":"Others stress that handaxes from Bose, Danjiangkou, Luonan and Jeongok-ri show Acheulean-style tools were made in East Asia, and a 2012 morphometric comparison by Wang Wei, Stephen Lycett and colleagues found the Bose handaxes similar in shape to western Acheulean ones. For them the line mostly reflects uneven research and an old assumption of Asian backwardness."}],"status":"Nobody now reads the line as evidence of lesser abilities. Researchers agree that handaxes are scarcer in East Asia and disagree on whether ancestry, ecology or population size explains it. Better dating of Chinese handaxe sites is the key test."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Oldest tools outside Africa","value":"about 2.12 million years, Shangchen (China)"},{"label":"Oldest fossils outside Africa","value":"about 1.85–1.77 million years, Dmanisi (Georgia)"},{"label":"Earliest Acheulean in India","value":"about 1.5 million years, Attirampakkam"},{"label":"Peking Man, lower layers","value":"about 770,000 years (cosmogenic burial dating)"},{"label":"Oldest geometric engraving","value":"540,000–430,000 years, shell from Trinil (Java)"},{"label":"Last known Homo erectus","value":"117,000–108,000 years ago, Ngandong (Java)"}],"siteIds":["shangchen","dmanisi","ubeidiya","gongwangling","majuangou","zhoukoudian","sangiran","trinil","ngandong","bose-basin","attirampakkam"],"routes":[{"id":"levantine-corridor-early","label":"Through the Levant to the Caucasus","start":2100000,"end":1800000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[36,3.5],[33.5,22],[34.5,29.5],[35.56,32.69],[38.5,37],[44.34,41.34]]},{"id":"across-asia-early","label":"Across Asia to the Loess Plateau","start":2200000,"end":2100000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[35.56,32.69],[48,35.5],[60,37.5],[75,39.5],[95,38],[109.49,34.22]]},{"id":"southern-asia-to-java","label":"Through southern Asia to Java","start":1800000,"end":1300000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[43.3,12.6],[52,17],[62,25.5],[72,22],[79.89,13.23],[90,23],[100,15],[104,2],[110.84,-7.46]]}],"themes":["species","migration","technology","climate","art"],"sources":[{"cite":"Zhu, Z. et al. (2018). Hominin occupation of the Chinese Loess Plateau since about 2.1 million years ago. Nature 559, 608–612."},{"cite":"Ferring, R. et al. (2011). Earliest human occupations at Dmanisi (Georgian Caucasus) dated to 1.85–1.78 Ma. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 108.","doi":"10.1073/pnas.1106638108"},{"cite":"Lordkipanidze, D. et al. (2013). A complete skull from Dmanisi, Georgia, and the evolutionary biology of early Homo. Science 342, 326–331.","doi":"10.1126/science.1238484"},{"cite":"Nery, V. et al. (2025). Testing the taxonomy of Dmanisi hominin fossils through dental crown area. PLOS One.","doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0336484"},{"cite":"Matsu’ura, S. et al. (2020). Age control of the first appearance datum for Javanese Homo erectus in the Sangiran area. Science 367, 210–214.","doi":"10.1126/science.aau8556"},{"cite":"Joordens, J. C. A. et al. (2015). Homo erectus at Trinil on Java used shells for tool production and engraving. Nature 518, 228–231.","doi":"10.1038/nature13962"},{"cite":"Rizal, Y. et al. (2020). Last appearance of Homo erectus at Ngandong, Java, 117,000–108,000 years ago. Nature 577, 381–385.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-019-1863-2"},{"cite":"Shen, G. et al. (2009). Age of Zhoukoudian Homo erectus determined with 26Al/10Be burial dating. Nature 458, 198–200.","doi":"10.1038/nature07741"},{"cite":"Pappu, S. et al. (2011). Early Pleistocene presence of Acheulian hominins in South India. Science 331, 1596–1599.","doi":"10.1126/science.1200183"},{"cite":"Lycett, S. J. & Bae, C. J. (2010). The Movius Line controversy: the state of the debate. World Archaeology 42, 521–544.","doi":"10.1080/00438243.2010.517667"}]},{"id":"first-europeans","order":7,"act":"toolmakers","title":"The First Europeans","subtitle":"Korolevo, Orce, Atapuerca, Happisburgh, Mauer and Boxgrove","era":"Early and Middle Pleistocene; Lower Palaeolithic","start":1420000,"end":400000,"dateLabel":"about 1.4 million–400,000 years ago","regions":["europe"],"focus":[-10,34,45,56],"emblem":"footprints","summary":"People reached Europe by about 1.4 million years ago, first with simple flake tools and later with handaxes. Their story includes the oldest faces in western Europe, cannibalism at Gran Dolina, footprints on an English estuary and, probably, repeated retreats before the cold.","paragraphs":[["Europe was a late and difficult destination.","Seen from Africa, it is a cold western peninsula of Asia, with long winters and short growing seasons.","The oldest firm evidence of people there comes from Korolevo, in western Ukraine, where stone tools lie in sediments dated to about 1.4 million years ago."],["At the other end of the continent, in southern Spain, a child’s milk tooth from Barranco León is almost as old.","In the Atapuerca hills near Burgos, a broken piece of face nicknamed Pink shows that people related to Homo erectus had reached the far west between 1.4 and 1.1 million years ago."],["About 850,000 years ago, in the cave of Gran Dolina, the bodies of people now called Homo antecessor were butchered and eaten.","Their bones carry the same cut marks as the deer and bison beside them, and many belonged to children.","From the same era come footprints on an estuary at Happisburgh, in eastern England: adults and children walking across the mud, preserved for close to a million years, then erased by the sea within weeks of their discovery in 2013."],["Europe may have been settled and abandoned several times as ice ages came and went.","By half a million years ago, though, handaxe makers were butchering horses on the English coast at Boxgrove, and heavily built people, known from a jaw found at Mauer in Germany, had made the continent their home."]],"sentences":["Europe was a late and difficult destination.","Seen from Africa, it is a cold western peninsula of Asia, with long winters and short growing seasons.","The oldest firm evidence of people there comes from Korolevo, in western Ukraine, where stone tools lie in sediments dated to about 1.4 million years ago.","At the other end of the continent, in southern Spain, a child’s milk tooth from Barranco León is almost as old.","In the Atapuerca hills near Burgos, a broken piece of face nicknamed Pink shows that people related to Homo erectus had reached the far west between 1.4 and 1.1 million years ago.","About 850,000 years ago, in the cave of Gran Dolina, the bodies of people now called Homo antecessor were butchered and eaten.","Their bones carry the same cut marks as the deer and bison beside them, and many belonged to children.","From the same era come footprints on an estuary at Happisburgh, in eastern England: adults and children walking across the mud, preserved for close to a million years, then erased by the sea within weeks of their discovery in 2013.","Europe may have been settled and abandoned several times as ice ages came and went.","By half a million years ago, though, handaxe makers were butchering horses on the English coast at Boxgrove, and heavily built people, known from a jaw found at Mauer in Germany, had made the continent their home."],"pronounce":{"Korolevo":"Koh-roh-leh-voh","Barranco León":"Bah-rahn-koh Leh-on","Atapuerca":"Ah-tah-pwer-kah","Burgos":"Boor-gohs","Gran Dolina":"Grahn Doh-lee-nah","Happisburgh":"Haze-bruh","Mauer":"Mow-er"},"body":[{"heading":"A cold peninsula of Asia","paragraphs":["From the Caucasus, Europe is a peninsula at the western end of Asia, reaching into latitudes with shorter winter days, colder winters and more seasonal food than the lands where hominins evolved. Dmanisi shows that people stood at its southeastern edge by 1.8 million years ago, yet firm evidence for Europe proper appears only some 400,000 years later. The early record is thin: a few dozen sites, most with stone tools only, and a handful of fossils. They lie beyond the reach of radiocarbon and often of luminescence, so researchers combine palaeomagnetism, cosmogenic nuclides, electron spin resonance and the evolutionary stages of small mammals such as voles.","The climate was changing too. During the Mid-Pleistocene Transition, roughly 1.25 to 0.7 million years ago, ice-age cycles lengthened from about 41,000 to about 100,000 years, and glacial phases became colder and longer. For hominins without tailored clothing and perhaps without routine fire, a severe glacial could make much of the continent uninhabitable."]},{"heading":"Korolevo and the eastern gateway","paragraphs":["The oldest securely dated evidence now comes from Korolevo, in the Transcarpathian region of western Ukraine, where a quarry on an andesite hill exposes a deep sequence of loess and ancient soils. Excavations led by Vladislav Gladilin from the 1970s recovered thousands of artefacts from many layers. In 2024 a team led by Roman Garba of the Czech Academy of Sciences and John Jansen dated the lowest artefact layer by measuring cosmogenic aluminium-26 and beryllium-10 in quartz, and obtained about 1.42 million years. The tools are simple cores and flakes of local volcanic stone. Combining the date with climate and habitat modelling, the team argued that hominins spread into Europe from the east and southeast during warm interglacial phases.","Other early claims are scattered along the northern Mediterranean. Pirro Nord in southern Italy has Oldowan-type tools in a fissure filling whose fauna suggests roughly 1.6–1.3 million years. Kozarnika Cave in Bulgaria and Lézignan-la-Cèbe in southern France have claims of similar or greater age that many specialists regard as uncertain."]},{"heading":"Orce and the Iberian south","paragraphs":["The Orce region of Andalusia, an ancient lake basin northeast of Granada, has been at the centre of debate for four decades. In the early 1980s a skull fragment from Venta Micena was announced as ‘Orce Man’ and then fiercely disputed; most experts concluded it could not be shown to be human. The case for early people at Orce now rests on firmer ground. At Barranco León excavators found a hominin milk molar in 2002, published in 2013 with an age of about 1.4 million years based on palaeomagnetism, electron spin resonance and fauna, together with Oldowan cores and flakes.","At nearby Fuente Nueva 3, dated to about 1.2 million years, stone tools lie around the remains of an extinct mammoth, Mammuthus meridionalis, together with fossil dung of the giant hyena Pachycrocuta. The excavators read this as a scene of competition: hominins and hyenas going after the same carcass."]},{"heading":"Atapuerca: the oldest faces in western Europe","paragraphs":["The Sierra de Atapuerca near Burgos in northern Spain is a limestone hill cut by a railway trench dug around 1900, which sliced through caves packed with sediment and bone. At Sima del Elefante, the team led by Eudald Carbonell, José María Bermúdez de Castro and Juan Luis Arsuaga found a fragment of a lower jaw in 2007, reported the next year as about 1.2–1.1 million years old.","In 2022, deeper in the same deposit, they found pieces of the left side of a face: part of the upper jaw and cheekbone. The fossil, ATE7-1, was nicknamed ‘Pink’, a nod to Pink Floyd and to the palaeontologist Rosa Huguet, who led its 2025 description in Nature. Dated to between 1.4 and 1.1 million years ago, it is the oldest human face known from western Europe. It lacks the modern-looking midface of the later Homo antecessor and resembles Homo erectus in several features, so the team cautiously assigned it to Homo affinis erectus, meaning ‘related to erectus’."]},{"heading":"Gran Dolina and Homo antecessor","paragraphs":["In 1994, in level TD6 of the Gran Dolina cave a short walk away, the excavators found remains of roughly a dozen people, dated by palaeomagnetism and later by electron spin resonance to about 850,000 years ago. In 1997 they named a new species, Homo antecessor, ‘the pioneer’. The most striking specimen is part of the face of a boy about ten or eleven years old. His cheek region is hollowed and set back, surprisingly like our own, although his brow and teeth are archaic.","Many of the human bones carry cut marks, percussion damage and even human tooth marks, identical to those on the deer, bison and horse bones in the same layer. Bodies were skinned, dismembered, defleshed and their bones broken for marrow, and most of the dead were children and adolescents. It is the oldest well-documented case of cannibalism. The excavators have suggested regular cannibalism, perhaps of outsiders killed in conflicts between groups; others see occasional hunger.","In 2020 a team led by Frido Welker and Enrico Cappellini recovered proteins from the enamel of a TD6 molar. The sequences placed Homo antecessor as a close sister lineage of the common ancestor of Neanderthals, Denisovans and modern humans, rather than as that ancestor itself."]},{"heading":"The far north: Pakefield and Happisburgh","paragraphs":["Britain was then a peninsula joined to the continent across what is now the southern North Sea. In 2005 Simon Parfitt and colleagues reported 32 flint artefacts from river deposits at Pakefield in Suffolk, about 700,000 years old, laid down in a warm interglacial with Mediterranean-like summers. In 2010 the same group reported flint flakes from Happisburgh in Norfolk, in estuarine deposits older than 780,000 years and perhaps close to 900,000, formed when pine and spruce forest covered the region and winters were colder than today.","In May 2013 storms stripped the sand from the Happisburgh foreshore and exposed a surface of compacted silt pocked with elongated hollows. Nick Ashton, Sarah Duffy and colleagues recorded them with photogrammetry, in rain and between tides, before the sea destroyed them within about two weeks. They were footprints of a group of at least five people, adults and children, whose estimated heights ranged from about 0.9 to 1.7 metres. They are the oldest known hominin footprints outside Africa. Homo antecessor, the only European species known from that time, is the usual candidate."]},{"heading":"Handaxes and heavier bodies: Mauer, Boxgrove and Arago","paragraphs":["Handaxes reached Europe long after they appeared in Africa. Among the earliest are large cutting tools found with butchered mammoth bones at Barranc de la Boella, near Tarragona in Catalonia, in deposits roughly 1 million to 780,000 years old, and handaxes at La Noira in central France dated to about 700,000 years. By 500,000 years ago the Acheulean was widespread.","On 21 October 1907 a sand-pit worker, Daniel Hartmann, found a massive, chinless lower jaw at Mauer near Heidelberg. Otto Schoetensack named it Homo heidelbergensis the following year. In 2010 Günther Wagner and colleagues dated the sands with electron spin resonance, uranium-series and luminescence methods to about 609,000 years.","At Boxgrove, near the south coast of England, excavations led by Mark Roberts uncovered an ancient beach and grassland about 480,000–500,000 years old. A robust shinbone points to a tall, heavily built person, and two incisors carry scratches from stone tools used to cut meat gripped in the teeth. Around the bones of a single horse lie several separate scatters of flint debris, where people sat and made handaxes on the spot to butcher it; a horse shoulder blade has a round hole that may be a spear wound. Antler and bone served as soft hammers for shaping flint.","In the Caune de l’Arago cave at Tautavel in the French Pyrenees, excavations led by Henry de Lumley since 1964 have yielded more than a hundred human remains, including the Arago 21 face, about 450,000 years old. These heavier-bodied Europeans lead into the tangled story of the Middle Pleistocene and the roots of the Neanderthals."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Was Europe settled continuously, or colonised and emptied again and again?","views":[{"label":"Repeated colonisation and local extinction","text":"Robin Dennell, María Martinón-Torres and José María Bermúdez de Castro proposed in 2011 that Europe acted as a population ‘sink’, colonised from western Asia in mild phases and depopulated in harsh glacials, with survivors only in southern refuges. In 2023 a team led by Vasiliki Margari, with Chris Stringer and Axel Timmermann, analysed a marine sediment core off Portugal and found an abrupt, extreme cooling about 1.12 million years ago, comparable to the harshest later glacials. Their climate modelling suggested even southern Europe became too cold for hominins, which could explain a gap in the record until about 900,000 years ago, when better-adapted groups arrived."},{"label":"More continuity than the gaps suggest","text":"Other researchers argue that the scarcity of sites between about 1.1 and 0.9 million years reflects poor preservation and imprecise dating rather than true absence, and that sheltered parts of Iberia and Italy may have supported small populations through cold spells. Sites such as Sima del Elefante and Vallparadís in Catalonia sit close to the supposed gap, so their exact ages matter."}],"status":"Most specialists accept that Europe’s early populations were small and vulnerable to climate, so regional extinctions are likely. How often the continent was emptied, and whether Homo antecessor descended from the first colonists or from a later wave, remains open."},{"question":"Where does Homo antecessor fit in the human family tree?","views":[{"label":"Ancestor of later humans","text":"When they named the species in 1997, Bermúdez de Castro, Arsuaga, Carbonell and colleagues proposed that it might be the last common ancestor of Neanderthals and modern humans, pointing to the modern-looking midface of the boy from TD6."},{"label":"A close relative on a side branch","text":"Critics argued that a child’s face does not reliably predict adult shape and that the teeth resemble other early Eurasian fossils. The 2020 enamel-protein study led by Frido Welker placed antecessor as a sister lineage to, rather than the ancestor of, the group that produced Neanderthals, Denisovans and modern humans."}],"status":"The protein result is widely treated as the best current evidence: Homo antecessor was a close relative of our ancestors, probably not the ancestor itself. One implication is that the modern-looking face may be an old feature that our own lineage retained."},{"question":"Which way did the first Europeans come?","views":[{"label":"From the east, by land","text":"Most researchers, including the 2024 Korolevo team, argue that hominins came through the Caucasus, Anatolia or the Balkans, the only land routes, and spread westward during warm phases. The sequence of dates, Dmanisi about 1.8 million years, Korolevo about 1.4 million and the Iberian sites about 1.4–1.2 million, fits this."},{"label":"Across the Strait of Gibraltar","text":"Josep Gibert, who led early work at Orce, and some other researchers argued that hominins crossed from North Africa, where Oldowan tools at Aïn Boucherit in Algeria are about 2.4–1.9 million years old. The strait narrowed when sea level fell but never closed, so this route would require a water crossing."}],"status":"The eastern route remains the default because it needs no sea crossing, but the near-identical ages of the oldest Spanish and Ukrainian sites keep the question alive. A clearly dated Early Pleistocene site near Gibraltar or in Sicily would change the picture."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Oldest dated evidence in Europe","value":"about 1.42 million years, stone tools at Korolevo (Ukraine)"},{"label":"Oldest face in western Europe","value":"ATE7-1 (‘Pink’), 1.4–1.1 million years, Sima del Elefante"},{"label":"Homo antecessor","value":"about 850,000 years, Gran Dolina TD6, with evidence of cannibalism"},{"label":"Oldest footprints outside Africa","value":"Happisburgh (England), more than 780,000 years old"},{"label":"Homo heidelbergensis type fossil","value":"Mauer 1 jaw, about 609,000 years"},{"label":"Boxgrove","value":"about 480,000–500,000 years; horse butchery with handaxes"}],"siteIds":["korolevo","barranco-leon","fuente-nueva-3","sima-del-elefante","gran-dolina","happisburgh","pakefield","mauer","boxgrove","arago","barranc-de-la-boella","la-noira"],"routes":[{"id":"europe-east-to-west","label":"Into Europe from the east","start":1500000,"end":1400000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[44.34,41.34],[36,40.5],[29,41],[26,44],[23.13,48.15]]},{"id":"europe-mediterranean-west","label":"Westward along the northern Mediterranean","start":1400000,"end":1200000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[23.13,48.15],[19,45],[15.4,41.7],[11,44],[5,43.5],[1,41.5],[-2.48,37.72]]},{"id":"gibraltar-crossing","label":"Proposed crossing of the Strait of Gibraltar","start":1500000,"end":1400000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[-6,34],[-5.6,35.8],[-5.4,36.2],[-4,37],[-2.48,37.72]]}],"themes":["species","migration","climate","technology","food","violence"],"sources":[{"cite":"Garba, R. et al. (2024). East-to-west human dispersal into Europe 1.4 million years ago. Nature 627."},{"cite":"Huguet, R. et al. (2025). The earliest human face of Western Europe. Nature."},{"cite":"Carbonell, E. et al. (2008). The first hominin of Europe. Nature 452, 465–469."},{"cite":"Bermúdez de Castro, J. M. et al. (1997). A hominid from the Lower Pleistocene of Atapuerca, Spain: possible ancestor to Neandertals and modern humans. Science 276, 1392–1395."},{"cite":"Welker, F. et al. (2020). The dental proteome of Homo antecessor. Nature 580, 235–238."},{"cite":"Parfitt, S. A. et al. (2010). Early Pleistocene human occupation at the edge of the boreal zone in northwest Europe. Nature 466, 229–233."},{"cite":"Ashton, N. et al. (2014). Hominin footprints from Early Pleistocene deposits at Happisburgh, UK. PLoS ONE 9(2): e88329."},{"cite":"Wagner, G. A. et al. (2010). Radiometric dating of the type-site for Homo heidelbergensis at Mauer, Germany. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 107, 19726–19730."},{"cite":"Roberts, M. B. & Parfitt, S. A. (1999). Boxgrove: A Middle Pleistocene Hominid Site at Eartham Quarry, Boxgrove, West Sussex. London: English Heritage."},{"cite":"Margari, V. et al. (2023). Extreme glacial cooling likely led to hominin depopulation of Europe in the Early Pleistocene. Science 381, 693–699."}]},{"id":"island-hominins","order":8,"act":"toolmakers","title":"Islanders","subtitle":"Flores, Luzon, Sulawesi and the first sea crossings","era":"Early to Late Pleistocene; Lower and Middle Palaeolithic","start":1500000,"end":50000,"dateLabel":"about 1.5 million–50,000 years ago","regions":["asia","oceania","europe"],"focus":[20,-12,128,40],"emblem":"boat","summary":"Hominins crossed the sea to Sulawesi and Flores more than a million years ago and later reached Luzon. Isolated on islands, some lineages became tiny: Homo floresiensis and Homo luzonensis.","paragraphs":[["While Europe’s first people battled the cold, hominins in Southeast Asia faced a different barrier: the sea.","East of Bali runs one of the sharpest boundaries in biology, the Wallace Line, where deep straits stayed open even when ice ages lowered the oceans.","Yet on the island of Flores, beyond that line, stone tools lie in volcanic sediments about a million years old."],["Someone had crossed.","Their descendants stayed, and shrank.","In 2003, in a limestone cave called Liang Bua, an Indonesian and Australian team uncovered the skeleton of an adult, probably a woman, barely more than a metre tall, with a brain the size of a chimpanzee’s.","They named her species Homo floresiensis.","She shared the island with dwarf elephant relatives, giant rats, storks taller than she was, and Komodo dragons."],["Flores was not unique.","On Luzon, in the Philippines, people butchered a rhinoceros about 709,000 years ago, and a cave there later yielded another small species, Homo luzonensis.","On Sulawesi, simple stone flakes are more than a million years old."],["Nobody knows how these first islanders made the journey.","Perhaps they drifted on tangles of vegetation torn loose by tsunamis or floods.","Perhaps some of them meant to go.","Either way, the sea had been crossed again and again, long before our own species existed."]],"sentences":["While Europe’s first people battled the cold, hominins in Southeast Asia faced a different barrier: the sea.","East of Bali runs one of the sharpest boundaries in biology, the Wallace Line, where deep straits stayed open even when ice ages lowered the oceans.","Yet on the island of Flores, beyond that line, stone tools lie in volcanic sediments about a million years old.","Someone had crossed.","Their descendants stayed, and shrank.","In 2003, in a limestone cave called Liang Bua, an Indonesian and Australian team uncovered the skeleton of an adult, probably a woman, barely more than a metre tall, with a brain the size of a chimpanzee’s.","They named her species Homo floresiensis.","She shared the island with dwarf elephant relatives, giant rats, storks taller than she was, and Komodo dragons.","Flores was not unique.","On Luzon, in the Philippines, people butchered a rhinoceros about 709,000 years ago, and a cave there later yielded another small species, Homo luzonensis.","On Sulawesi, simple stone flakes are more than a million years old.","Nobody knows how these first islanders made the journey.","Perhaps they drifted on tangles of vegetation torn loose by tsunamis or floods.","Perhaps some of them meant to go.","Either way, the sea had been crossed again and again, long before our own species existed."],"pronounce":{"Liang Bua":"Lee-ahng Boo-ah","Homo floresiensis":"Hoh-moh flor-ess-ee-en-sis","Homo luzonensis":"Hoh-moh loo-zon-en-sis","Sulawesi":"Soo-lah-weh-see","Luzon":"Loo-zon"},"body":[{"heading":"Beyond the Wallace Line","paragraphs":["In the late 1850s the naturalist Alfred Russel Wallace noticed that the animals of Bali and Lombok, separated by a strait a few dozen kilometres wide, belong to different worlds: tigers, monkeys and woodpeckers to the west, cockatoos, marsupials and honeyeaters farther east. The reason is geology. In glacial periods, when sea level fell by up to about 120 metres, Java, Sumatra, Borneo and Bali joined mainland Asia in a single landmass, Sundaland. The islands to the east, a zone biologists now call Wallacea that includes Lombok, Sulawesi, Flores and Timor, stayed islands, cut off by deep water. Luzon, in the Philippines, was likewise never joined to the mainland.","Any hominin reaching Flores, Sulawesi or Luzon therefore crossed open water. These islands became natural experiments: tiny founding populations isolated for hundreds of thousands of years among strange animals."]},{"heading":"Flores: a million years of toolmakers","paragraphs":["Flores has the longest record. In the 1990s Mike Morwood and Indonesian and Dutch colleagues found stone tools with fossils of Stegodon, an extinct elephant relative, in the Soa Basin of central Flores. In 2010 Adam Brumm and colleagues dated tools at Wolo Sege, sealed beneath volcanic ash, to about 1.02 million years using argon dating. At Mata Menge, about 700,000 years old, the team found in 2014 a fragment of an adult lower jaw and several teeth. Published in 2016, they came from hominins as small as or smaller than the later Liang Bua individuals, with teeth resembling those of early Javanese Homo erectus.","In 2024 Yousuke Kaifu, Gert van den Bergh, Adam Brumm and colleagues described a fragment of an upper arm bone from Mata Menge. It points to an adult only about a metre tall, slightly shorter than the famous Liang Bua skeleton. Tiny bodies had evolved on Flores within roughly 300,000 years of the first arrivals, and then stayed that way for more than half a million years."]},{"heading":"The hobbit of Liang Bua","paragraphs":["Liang Bua, ‘cool cave’ in the local Manggarai language, is a large limestone cave in western Flores. In September 2003 an Indonesian and Australian team led by Mike Morwood and Raden Soejono found a partial skeleton, LB1, about six metres below the floor. Described in Nature in 2004 by Peter Brown and colleagues, it belonged to an adult, probably female, about 1.06 metres tall, with a brain of roughly 400 cubic centimetres, within the chimpanzee range. Its wrist bones were ape-like, its feet long and flat, its jaw chinless. The team named a new species, Homo floresiensis, quickly nicknamed ‘the hobbit’. Further digging recovered bones of several more individuals.","The first dates put the youngest remains at about 18,000 years, which would have meant hobbits survived long after modern humans reached the region. A decade of further excavation by Thomas Sutikna, Matthew Tocheri and colleagues showed that the layers were more complicated than they looked: an ancient erosion surface separated old deposits from much younger ones. Their 2016 revision put the skeletal remains at about 100,000 to 60,000 years old, and the stone tools attributed to the species at about 190,000 to 50,000 years.","The hobbits shared Flores with an island menagerie: a dwarf Stegodon, the Flores giant rat (which still survives), Komodo dragons, vultures and an extinct giant marabou stork about 1.8 metres tall. The Stegodon bones in the cave are mostly from juveniles, some with cut marks, which suggests the hominins hunted or scavenged the young."]},{"heading":"Disease or new species?","paragraphs":["The discovery set off one of the fiercest disputes in modern palaeoanthropology. The Indonesian anthropologist Teuku Jacob and others argued that LB1 was a modern human with microcephaly, a condition that produces a very small brain. Later proposals blamed Laron syndrome, iodine-deficiency cretinism and, in 2014, Down syndrome. Each was answered in detail. Studies of the brain’s shape from the inside of the skull by Dean Falk and colleagues did not match microcephaly; the other individuals from the cave were equally small; and the wrist, foot and shoulder resemble early hominins, not any modern condition. The far older and equally small Mata Menge fossils settled the matter for most researchers.","Genetics answered a related question. Near Liang Bua lives the Rampasasa community, whose members are short. A 2018 genomic study led by Serena Tucci found no trace of Homo floresiensis ancestry in them; their stature arose independently from variation already present in modern humans, a reminder that island settings repeatedly favour small bodies."]},{"heading":"Luzon: a butchered rhino and a new species","paragraphs":["On Luzon, the largest island of the Philippines, stone tools had long been reported with extinct animals in the Cagayan Valley, but without secure dates. In 2018 Thomas Ingicco and colleagues published an excavation at Kalinga: 57 stone tools and a nearly complete skeleton of an extinct rhinoceros, Rhinoceros philippinensis, with 13 cut marks and both upper arm bones smashed open for marrow. Electron spin resonance dating of the rhino’s tooth enamel gave about 709,000 years.","Clues to the toolmakers came from Callao Cave in northern Luzon. A foot bone found there in 2007 was followed by more fossils from later excavations: teeth, finger and toe bones and part of a thighbone from at least three individuals. In 2019 Florent Détroit, Armand Mijares and colleagues named Homo luzonensis. Its teeth are small, with some primitive traits such as multi-rooted premolars, and its finger and toe bones are curved like those of australopiths. Uranium-series dating gives minimum ages of about 50,000 and 67,000 years."]},{"heading":"Sulawesi and the oldest island tools","paragraphs":["Sulawesi, the sprawling island between Borneo and the Moluccas, has no hominin fossils yet, but its Walanae Basin preserves tools. In 2016 Gerrit van den Bergh and colleagues reported stone artefacts at Talepu at least 118,000 years old. In 2025 a team led by Budianto Hakim and Adam Brumm published a handful of small stone flakes from Calio, in the same basin, dated by palaeomagnetism and by dating of associated animal fossils to between about 1.48 and 1.04 million years ago. Sulawesi was therefore reached at least as early as Flores, which raises the possibility that the Flores hominins came from the north rather than directly from Java."]},{"heading":"The Mediterranean and the island rule","paragraphs":["Early sea crossings are argued for in the Mediterranean too. In 2008 and 2009 Thomas Strasser, Eleni Panagopoulou and colleagues found quartz handaxes, cleavers and picks near Plakias on the south coast of Crete, an island separated from the mainland for about five million years. From the uplifted marine terraces and soils around the tools, they estimated an age of at least 130,000 years. At Stelida on Naxos, Tristan Carter and colleagues reported in 2019 artefacts at least 200,000 years old; at low sea level Naxos was part of a larger Cycladic landmass, and whether reaching it required a sea crossing is debated.","Islands reshape their inhabitants. The pattern known as the island rule, described by J. Bristol Foster in 1964, holds that large mammals tend to shrink and small ones to grow when isolated, where predators are few and food is limited. The Mediterranean had dwarf elephants about a metre tall on Sicily and Malta and a dwarf mammoth on Crete; Flores had dwarf Stegodon and giant rats. Homo floresiensis and Homo luzonensis suggest that hominins obeyed the same rule."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Where did Homo floresiensis come from?","views":[{"label":"Dwarfed Homo erectus","text":"Gert van den Bergh, Yousuke Kaifu, Adam Brumm and colleagues argue that Homo erectus from Java, or a close relative, reached Flores about a million years ago and shrank there. The Mata Menge jaw and teeth, about 700,000 years old, look like miniature early Javanese Homo erectus, and the 2024 arm bone shows that dwarfing happened fast. Kaifu’s studies of the LB1 skull also found erectus-like features. Dwarfing of this scale is known in other island mammals."},{"label":"Descendant of a more primitive ancestor","text":"Debbie Argue, Mike Lee and colleagues found in a 2017 phylogenetic analysis that Homo floresiensis branches off near Homo habilis, before Homo erectus. They point to its ape-like wrist, long feet, shoulder and limb proportions, which would have had to re-evolve if it descended from long-legged erectus. That would imply an otherwise unknown early dispersal of small-bodied hominins out of Africa."}],"status":"The dwarfed-erectus view gained ground after the Mata Menge finds and is now favoured by many, but the primitive limbs and wrist remain hard to explain. Fossils from Sulawesi, or ancient proteins from Flores teeth, could settle it."},{"question":"How did hominins cross the sea?","views":[{"label":"Accidental drift","text":"Robin Dennell and colleagues argued in 2014 that the islands were colonised rarely and by accident, by individuals swept offshore on mats of vegetation or uprooted trees during tsunamis, cyclones or floods, perhaps from Sulawesi to Flores on prevailing currents. Long gaps between arrivals and tiny founding populations fit chance events."},{"label":"Deliberate voyages","text":"Mike Morwood and the rock-art researcher Robert Bednarik argued that repeated crossings of several straits implied planned trips on rafts, and with them cooperation and perhaps some form of language. Bednarik built bamboo rafts using stone tools to show such voyages were possible."}],"status":"No raft or boat survives from this period, and a small group drifting ashore could found a population. Most researchers favour occasional accidental crossings for the earliest arrivals, but the growing number of islands reached keeps the question open."},{"question":"Did hominins cross the Mediterranean before modern humans?","views":[{"label":"Yes, Crete shows it","text":"Thomas Strasser, Curtis Runnels and colleagues argue that the Plakias handaxes show seafaring by pre-modern hominins at least 130,000 years ago, and possibly much earlier given the Acheulean style of the tools."},{"label":"Not proven","text":"Sceptics note that the artefacts were not dated directly, that some came from surfaces, and that tools of Acheulean appearance can be made in later periods. Naxos, and Lesbos in the eastern Aegean, were probably reachable by land or short hops at low sea level."}],"status":"Crete remains an intriguing but contested case. Direct dating of artefact-bearing sediments on the island would test it."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Oldest tools on Flores","value":"about 1.02 million years, Wolo Sege"},{"label":"Oldest tools on Sulawesi","value":"about 1.48–1.04 million years, Calio (2025)"},{"label":"Homo floresiensis (LB1)","value":"about 1.06 m tall, brain about 400 cm³"},{"label":"Liang Bua ages","value":"skeletons 100,000–60,000 years; tools 190,000–50,000 years"},{"label":"Butchered rhino on Luzon","value":"about 709,000 years, Kalinga"},{"label":"Homo luzonensis","value":"at least 50,000–67,000 years, Callao Cave"}],"siteIds":["wolo-sege","mata-menge","liang-bua","kalinga-luzon","callao-cave","calio","talepu","plakias","stelida"],"routes":[{"id":"sundaland-to-flores","label":"Island-hopping from Java to Flores","start":1100000,"end":1000000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[110.84,-7.46],[114.5,-8.3],[116.1,-8.6],[117.4,-8.6],[119.3,-8.5],[121.08,-8.66]]},{"id":"borneo-to-sulawesi","label":"Across the Makassar Strait to Sulawesi","start":1500000,"end":1100000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[115,-1],[116.8,-1.5],[119.3,-3.4],[119.95,-4.4]]},{"id":"sulawesi-to-flores","label":"Possible drift from Sulawesi to Flores","start":1100000,"end":1000000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[119.95,-4.4],[120.4,-6.2],[120.8,-8.4]]},{"id":"to-luzon","label":"Towards Luzon","start":800000,"end":710000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[116.5,6],[117.5,8.8],[119.5,10.8],[121,13.3],[121.6,17.5]]},{"id":"aegean-to-crete","label":"Possible crossing to Crete","start":200000,"end":130000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[22.5,36.8],[23,36.2],[23.6,35.6],[24.39,35.19]]}],"themes":["species","seafaring","migration","animals"],"sources":[{"cite":"Brown, P. et al. (2004). A new small-bodied hominin from the Late Pleistocene of Flores, Indonesia. Nature 431, 1055–1061."},{"cite":"Sutikna, T. et al. (2016). Revised stratigraphy and chronology for Homo floresiensis at Liang Bua in Indonesia. Nature 532, 366–369."},{"cite":"Brumm, A. et al. (2010). Hominins on Flores, Indonesia, by one million years ago. Nature 464, 748–752."},{"cite":"van den Bergh, G. D. et al. (2016). Homo floresiensis-like fossils from the early Middle Pleistocene of Flores. Nature 534, 245–248."},{"cite":"Kaifu, Y. et al. (2024). Early evolution of small body size in Homo floresiensis. Nature Communications 15."},{"cite":"Argue, D. et al. (2017). The affinities of Homo floresiensis based on phylogenetic analyses of cranial, dental, and postcranial characters. Journal of Human Evolution 107, 107–133."},{"cite":"Ingicco, T. et al. (2018). Earliest known hominin activity in the Philippines by 709 thousand years ago. Nature 557, 233–237."},{"cite":"Détroit, F. et al. (2019). A new species of Homo from the Late Pleistocene of the Philippines. Nature 568, 181–186."},{"cite":"Hakim, B. et al. (2025). Hominins on Sulawesi during the Early Pleistocene. Nature."},{"cite":"Strasser, T. F. et al. (2010). Stone Age seafaring in the Mediterranean: evidence from the Plakias region for Lower Palaeolithic and Mesolithic habitation of Crete. Hesperia 79, 145–190."}]},{"id":"fire-and-wood","order":9,"act":"toolmakers","title":"Fire and Wood","subtitle":"The first hearths, the first fire-making and the oldest wooden tools","era":"Early and Middle Pleistocene; Lower and Middle Palaeolithic","start":1500000,"end":120000,"dateLabel":"about 1.5 million–120,000 years ago","regions":["africa","asia","europe"],"focus":[-12,-30,108,56],"emblem":"fire","summary":"Traces of fire go back about 1.5 million years, cooked fish to 780,000 years and the deliberate making of fire, on current evidence, to about 400,000 years. Rare waterlogged sites show that wood, from spears to a notched log structure, was just as important as stone.","paragraphs":[["Fire leaves frustratingly little behind.","Ash blows away, charcoal crumbles, and lightning can burn a landscape without any human help.","So the search for the first hearths is a search for faint traces: a patch of reddened earth, a flint cracked by heat, a scatter of burnt bone."],["Some of those traces are very old.","Deep inside Wonderwerk Cave in South Africa, where no wildfire could reach, ash and burnt bone lie in layers about a million years old.","On the shore of an ancient lake in Israel, at Gesher Benot Yaʿaqov, people cooked fish 780,000 years ago."],["Using fire is not the same as making it.","In 2025 researchers working at Barnham, in eastern England, reported a patch of earth heated again and again, and two small pieces of pyrite, a mineral that throws sparks when struck with flint.","Someone had carried them there about 400,000 years ago.","It is the oldest evidence that people could light a fire whenever they chose."],["Fire had a partner that rots even faster: wood.","At Kalambo Falls in Zambia, two logs joined by a notch cut about 476,000 years ago form the oldest known wooden structure.","At Schöningen in Germany, hunters left spears longer than a tall man, carefully shaped, beside the bones of butchered horses.","Around fires like these, anthropologists suspect, the long evenings became a time for stories."]],"sentences":["Fire leaves frustratingly little behind.","Ash blows away, charcoal crumbles, and lightning can burn a landscape without any human help.","So the search for the first hearths is a search for faint traces: a patch of reddened earth, a flint cracked by heat, a scatter of burnt bone.","Some of those traces are very old.","Deep inside Wonderwerk Cave in South Africa, where no wildfire could reach, ash and burnt bone lie in layers about a million years old.","On the shore of an ancient lake in Israel, at Gesher Benot Yaʿaqov, people cooked fish 780,000 years ago.","Using fire is not the same as making it.","In 2025 researchers working at Barnham, in eastern England, reported a patch of earth heated again and again, and two small pieces of pyrite, a mineral that throws sparks when struck with flint.","Someone had carried them there about 400,000 years ago.","It is the oldest evidence that people could light a fire whenever they chose.","Fire had a partner that rots even faster: wood.","At Kalambo Falls in Zambia, two logs joined by a notch cut about 476,000 years ago form the oldest known wooden structure.","At Schöningen in Germany, hunters left spears longer than a tall man, carefully shaped, beside the bones of butchered horses.","Around fires like these, anthropologists suspect, the long evenings became a time for stories."],"pronounce":{"Wonderwerk":"Von-der-verk","Gesher Benot Yaʿaqov":"Geh-sher Beh-note Yah-ah-kove","Barnham":"Bar-num","Kalambo":"Kah-lahm-boh","Schöningen":"Shuh-ning-en"},"body":[{"heading":"How to find an ancient fire","paragraphs":["Fire is the hardest early technology to prove. Ash is blown or washed away, charcoal fragments, and wildfires burn soils, bones and stones with no hominin involved. Archaeologists therefore look for several clues together: sediment heated in place, detected by its magnetic properties or by mineral changes seen under the microscope in intact blocks of soil; burnt bone, identified by colour and infrared spectroscopy; flints cracked, reddened or pitted by heat; and a spatial pattern, such as burnt material clustered in one spot. Fire deep inside a cave is stronger evidence than fire in the open, because lightning rarely reaches there.","Researchers also distinguish three stages that the record tends to blur: opportunistic use of natural fire, habitual tending of fires kept alight, and the ability to make fire at will."]},{"heading":"Early African embers","paragraphs":["The oldest claims come from East Africa. At FxJj 20 Main, in the Koobi Fora region east of Lake Turkana, Randy Bellomo described in the 1990s reddened patches of sediment among stone tools about 1.5 million years old, with magnetic signatures suggesting heating like that of a campfire. A 2019 study led by Sarah Hlubik reported burnt bone and heated stone artefacts from the same area. At Swartkrans in South Africa, Bob Brain and Andrew Sillen reported in 1988 about 270 burnt bones among many thousands in Member 3, roughly a million years old or more, heated to a range of temperatures they compared to experimental campfires. At both sites natural fires cannot be completely excluded.","The case is stronger at Wonderwerk Cave in South Africa’s Northern Cape. In 2012 Francesco Berna, Michael Chazan and colleagues studied intact blocks of Acheulean deposits about 1 million years old, some 30 metres inside the cave. Under the microscope they found ashed plant remains and burnt bone fragments that had burned where they lay, too deep in the cave for wildfire to explain."]},{"heading":"Lakeshore fires in the Levant","paragraphs":["At Gesher Benot Yaʿaqov, on the Jordan River north of the Sea of Galilee, waterlogged layers about 780,000 years old preserve wood, seeds and fruit. Naama Goren-Inbar and colleagues reported burnt seeds, wood and flint in 2004, and Nira Alperson-Afil mapped tiny burnt flint fragments concentrated in distinct patches, ‘phantom hearths’ that recur through many layers. In 2022 Irit Zohar and colleagues examined fish teeth, mostly from large carp. X-ray diffraction showed that the enamel crystals had been heated to moderate temperatures, below about 500 °C, consistent with cooking rather than burning, and the teeth clustered in the hearth areas. It is the oldest known evidence of cooking.","At Evron Quarry, an open-air site in western Galilee about 1 million to 800,000 years old, no fire was visible to the eye. In 2022 Zane Stepka, Filipe Natalio and colleagues trained an artificial-intelligence model on the spectroscopic signatures of flint heated to known temperatures, then scanned artefacts from the site. Some flints and a fragment of tusk had been heated to several hundred degrees, suggesting fire that ordinary methods had missed."]},{"heading":"From keeping fire to making it","paragraphs":["After about 400,000 years ago, traces of fire become far more common. At Beeches Pit in Suffolk, England, burnt flint and hearth features date to about 400,000 years ago. At Qesem Cave in Israel, Ruth Shahack-Gross and colleagues identified in 2014 a large central hearth used repeatedly around 300,000 years ago, ringed by burnt bone and flint. In 2011 Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa surveyed the European record and argued that fire became habitual only from about 400,000–300,000 years ago; earlier Europeans, at Happisburgh or Gran Dolina, left no sign of it, even in cold climates.","Whether these people could light fires on demand is a separate question. The earliest direct evidence long came from Neanderthal handaxes about 50,000 years old with wear from striking pyrite, reported by Andrew Sorensen and colleagues in 2018. In December 2025 a team led by Rob Davis and Nick Ashton of the British Museum published evidence from Barnham, Suffolk, about 400,000 years old: a patch of clayey silt heated repeatedly to high temperatures, heat-shattered flint handaxes, and two fragments of iron pyrite, a mineral rare in the local geology that throws sparks when struck with flint. They argue the pyrite was brought in to make fire, pushing fire-making back by some 350,000 years. The makers were probably early Neanderthals, like the people represented by the Swanscombe skull from Kent."]},{"heading":"Cooking and the human body","paragraphs":["In his 2009 book Catching Fire, the primatologist Richard Wrangham argued that cooking made us human. Heat makes starch and protein easier to digest and kills pathogens, so cooked food yields more energy for less chewing and a smaller gut. He linked the smaller teeth and jaws, reduced gut and larger body of Homo erectus about 1.9 million years ago to the start of cooking. Critics reply that clear evidence of regular fire lags far behind that date. In 2016 Katherine Zink and Daniel Lieberman showed that simply slicing meat and pounding tubers with stone tools reduces chewing effort substantially, offering another route to smaller teeth. Many researchers now picture a stepwise process: meat eating and food processing first, occasional fire and cooking later, habitual cooking by the Middle Pleistocene."]},{"heading":"Around the campfire","paragraphs":["Fire also changed the shape of the day, extending light into the evening and creating a place to gather. In 2014 the anthropologist Polly Wiessner analysed conversations she had recorded in the 1970s among the Ju/’hoansi of the Kalahari in Namibia and Botswana. Daytime talk dealt mostly with practical matters, complaints and gossip; by firelight, most long conversations were stories about people near and far, the spirit world and the past. She argued that firelight talk helped build the wider networks and shared understandings on which human societies depend. How far back this goes cannot be measured, but a hearth like Qesem’s, used again and again, was certainly a place where people sat together."]},{"heading":"The age of wood","paragraphs":["Stone survives; wood almost never does, yet it was probably the most used material through most of prehistory. At Kalambo Falls on the Zambia–Tanzania border, where a high water table has kept deposits waterlogged, a team led by Larry Barham found two logs joined at right angles through a notch cut deliberately into the upper one, with marks of chopping and scraping. Published in 2023 and dated by luminescence to at least 476,000 years ago, it is the oldest known wooden structure, perhaps part of a platform or foundation by the river. The same deposits held a wedge, a digging stick and other worked wood.","In the 1990s Hartmut Thieme uncovered at Schöningen, an opencast lignite mine in northern Germany, around ten spears of spruce and pine, 1.8 to 2.5 metres long, thickest towards the front like modern javelins, among the butchered remains of many horses. A 2024 study of the whole wooden assemblage by Dirk Leder and colleagues documented bark removal, splitting, shaping and seasoning of the wood. The spears were long dated to about 300,000 years; a 2025 study argued that the spear layer is younger, about 200,000 years, and the age is still being discussed.","Other finds fill in the picture: a yew spear tip from Clacton-on-Sea in England, about 400,000 years old; a yew spear about 2.4 metres long found in 1948 among the bones of a straight-tusked elephant at Lehringen in Germany, about 120,000 years old; boxwood digging sticks shaped with the help of fire at Poggetti Vecchi in Tuscany, about 171,000 years old; and a yew digging stick at Aranbaltza in the Basque Country, about 90,000 years old. In 2025 Chinese researchers reported dozens of wooden tools from Gantangqing in Yunnan, roughly 300,000 years old, including digging sticks probably used to extract roots and tubers on an ancient lakeshore: the first such evidence from East Asia."]}],"debates":[{"question":"When did fire become a habitual part of life?","views":[{"label":"Early, with Homo erectus","text":"Richard Wrangham, John Gowlett and others argue that the anatomy of Homo erectus implies cooked food from about 1.9 million years ago, and that Koobi Fora, Swartkrans and Wonderwerk show fire in use by 1.5 to 1 million years. For them, the thin early record reflects poor preservation of ash and charcoal."},{"label":"Late, from about 400,000 years ago","text":"Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa argue that fire traces become regular only after about 400,000 years ago, and that early Europeans lived in cold places without leaving any sign of fire. The oldest cases are few, scattered and hard to separate from natural fires."}],"status":"Fire use before 1 million years ago is supported at a handful of sites, but fire appears routinely across many sites only in the later Middle Pleistocene. The gap between the anatomical argument and the archaeological evidence is still unresolved."},{"question":"Could Neanderthals and their ancestors make fire?","views":[{"label":"Dependent on natural fire","text":"Dennis Sandgathe, Harold Dibble and colleagues found in the French caves of Pech de l’Azé IV and Roc de Marsal that Neanderthals used fire often in warmer phases but rarely in the coldest, when they would have needed it most. They proposed in 2011 that Neanderthals gathered fire from wildfires rather than making it."},{"label":"Fire-makers","text":"Wear traces on Neanderthal bifaces reported by Andrew Sorensen and colleagues in 2018, and the 2025 Barnham study of imported pyrite about 400,000 years old, indicate that fire could be struck with flint and pyrite. Less fire in cold phases might reflect scarce fuel or different uses of sites."}],"status":"Barnham has shifted opinion towards early fire-making, though some researchers want more sites before accepting that the pyrite was used for sparks. The skill may have been known to some groups and not to others."},{"question":"Were the earliest wooden spears thrown or thrust?","views":[{"label":"Thrown weapons","text":"Hartmut Thieme argued that the forward-balanced Schöningen spears were javelins. In 2019 Annemieke Milks and colleagues had trained javelin athletes throw replicas and found they could hit targets at up to about 20 metres."},{"label":"Thrusting or multipurpose tools","text":"Others think the spears were used mainly at close range, as the Lehringen elephant find might suggest, or for several jobs including digging and warding off carnivores."}],"status":"The experiments show that throwing was feasible, and Schöningen also yielded shorter double-pointed throwing sticks, so most researchers now think these hunters used both thrown and thrusting weapons."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Oldest widely cited fire traces","value":"about 1.5 million years, Koobi Fora (Kenya)"},{"label":"Fire deep inside a cave","value":"about 1 million years, Wonderwerk (South Africa)"},{"label":"Oldest evidence of cooking","value":"fish, about 780,000 years, Gesher Benot Yaʿaqov (Israel)"},{"label":"Earliest evidence of making fire","value":"about 400,000 years, Barnham (England), published 2025"},{"label":"Oldest wooden structure","value":"at least 476,000 years, Kalambo Falls (Zambia)"},{"label":"Oldest complete spears","value":"Schöningen (Germany), about 300,000 or 200,000 years (debated)"}],"siteIds":["koobi-fora-fxjj20","swartkrans","wonderwerk-cave","gesher-benot-yaaqov","evron-quarry","qesem-cave","barnham","kalambo-falls","schoningen","clacton-on-sea","poggetti-vecchi","gantangqing"],"routes":[],"themes":["fire","technology","food","society"],"sources":[{"cite":"Davis, R. et al. (2025). Earliest evidence of making fire. Nature."},{"cite":"Berna, F. et al. (2012). Microstratigraphic evidence of in situ fire in the Acheulean strata of Wonderwerk Cave, Northern Cape province, South Africa. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 109, E1215–E1220."},{"cite":"Goren-Inbar, N. et al. (2004). Evidence of hominin control of fire at Gesher Benot Ya’aqov, Israel. Science 304, 725–727."},{"cite":"Zohar, I. et al. (2022). Evidence for the cooking of fish 780,000 years ago at Gesher Benot Ya’aqov, Israel. Nature Ecology & Evolution 6, 2016–2028."},{"cite":"Stepka, Z. et al. (2022). Hidden signatures of early fire at Evron Quarry (1.0 to 0.8 Mya). Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 119."},{"cite":"Roebroeks, W. & Villa, P. (2011). On the earliest evidence for habitual use of fire in Europe. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 108, 5209–5214."},{"cite":"Barham, L. et al. (2023). Evidence for the earliest structural use of wood at least 476,000 years ago. Nature 622, 107–111."},{"cite":"Thieme, H. (1997). Lower Palaeolithic hunting spears from Germany. Nature 385, 807–810."},{"cite":"Aranguren, B. et al. (2018). Wooden tools and fire technology in the early Neanderthal site of Poggetti Vecchi (Italy). Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 115, 2054–2059."},{"cite":"Wiessner, P. W. (2014). Embers of society: Firelight talk among the Ju/’hoansi Bushmen. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 111, 14027–14035."}]},{"id":"middle-pleistocene","order":10,"act":"many-humans","title":"The Muddle in the Middle","subtitle":"Bodo, Kabwe, Sima de los Huesos, Homo naledi and the Chinese crania","era":"Middle Pleistocene","start":800000,"end":200000,"dateLabel":"about 800,000–200,000 years ago","regions":["africa","europe","asia"],"focus":[-12,-30,130,50],"emblem":"skull","summary":"Between about 800,000 and 200,000 years ago, several human lineages evolved in parallel across Africa, Europe and Asia. Their fossils resist neat classification, but ancient DNA from Sima de los Huesos and genetic clocks outline the branching that produced Neanderthals, Denisovans and our own species.","paragraphs":[["In northern Spain, at the bottom of a thirteen-metre shaft in the Atapuerca hills, lie the bones of at least 28 people.","They are about 430,000 years old, and in 2016 they yielded the oldest human nuclear DNA yet recovered.","It showed that these people were early relatives of the Neanderthals."],["The long stretch of time after the first Europeans is a puzzle that palaeoanthropologists call the muddle in the middle.","Across Africa, Europe and Asia, large-brained people with heavy brows left skulls that fit no tidy scheme: at Bodo in Ethiopia, where someone scraped the flesh from a face with a stone tool, at Kabwe in Zambia, and at Dali, Jinniushan and Harbin in China."],["Stranger still, a small-brained species, Homo naledi, lived in South Africa as recently as about 250,000 years ago, at the same time as the earliest members of our own species.","Its bones lie deep in a cave system reached only through narrow chutes, and whether it placed its dead there on purpose is fiercely argued."],["Genetics sketches the outline that the bones cannot.","Somewhere between about 765,000 and 550,000 years ago, the ancestors of modern humans parted from the line that would produce the Neanderthals in the west and the Denisovans in the east.","The fossils of this middle period are the scattered traces of that branching, still being sorted into their places."]],"sentences":["In northern Spain, at the bottom of a thirteen-metre shaft in the Atapuerca hills, lie the bones of at least 28 people.","They are about 430,000 years old, and in 2016 they yielded the oldest human nuclear DNA yet recovered.","It showed that these people were early relatives of the Neanderthals.","The long stretch of time after the first Europeans is a puzzle that palaeoanthropologists call the muddle in the middle.","Across Africa, Europe and Asia, large-brained people with heavy brows left skulls that fit no tidy scheme: at Bodo in Ethiopia, where someone scraped the flesh from a face with a stone tool, at Kabwe in Zambia, and at Dali, Jinniushan and Harbin in China.","Stranger still, a small-brained species, Homo naledi, lived in South Africa as recently as about 250,000 years ago, at the same time as the earliest members of our own species.","Its bones lie deep in a cave system reached only through narrow chutes, and whether it placed its dead there on purpose is fiercely argued.","Genetics sketches the outline that the bones cannot.","Somewhere between about 765,000 and 550,000 years ago, the ancestors of modern humans parted from the line that would produce the Neanderthals in the west and the Denisovans in the east.","The fossils of this middle period are the scattered traces of that branching, still being sorted into their places."],"pronounce":{"Homo naledi":"Hoh-moh nah-leh-dee","Bodo":"Boh-doh","Kabwe":"Kahb-weh","Jinniushan":"Jin-nyoh-shahn","Atapuerca":"Ah-tah-pwer-kah"},"body":[{"heading":"A tangle of names","paragraphs":["Between roughly 800,000 and 200,000 years ago the human family tree is at its most confusing. Fossils from this part of the Middle Pleistocene combine large brains, often 1,100 to 1,300 cubic centimetres, with heavy brow ridges, long low skulls and big faces. They come from Africa, Europe and Asia, are often incomplete and poorly dated, and differ in ways that are hard to sort. Palaeoanthropologists call the problem the ‘muddle in the middle’.","The default name was long Homo heidelbergensis, after the Mauer jaw of about 609,000 years, applied to large-brained fossils in Africa and Europe and often cast as the common ancestor of Neanderthals and modern humans. But many European fossils now look like early Neanderthals, and Mauer itself may sit on the Neanderthal line. African fossils such as Kabwe were sometimes called Homo rhodesiensis, a name coined by Arthur Smith Woodward in 1921. In 2021 Mirjana Roksandic, Predrag Radović, Xiu-Jie Wu and Christopher Bae proposed Homo bodoensis, based on the Bodo cranium, for most African fossils of the period ancestral to our species; they rejected rhodesiensis partly because it evokes the colonialist Cecil Rhodes."]},{"heading":"Africa: Bodo and Kabwe","paragraphs":["The Bodo cranium was found in 1976 in the Middle Awash of Ethiopia and is about 600,000 years old by argon dating. It has a huge face and a brain of about 1,250 cubic centimetres. In 1986 Tim White described fine cut marks around the eye sockets and on the cheek: someone had removed the flesh with a stone tool. Whether this was mortuary ritual, cannibalism or something else is unknown, but it is one of the oldest signs of deliberate processing of a human body after death.","Kabwe 1, or Broken Hill 1, was found in 1921 by miners at a lead and zinc mine in what was then Northern Rhodesia, now Zambia. The heavy-browed skull has some of the worst tooth decay known in a fossil human, with cavities in most upper teeth and abscesses. The cave was destroyed by mining, so its age was long unknown. In 2020 Rainer Grün, Chris Stringer and colleagues dated the skull directly by uranium-series and electron spin resonance to about 299,000 years, give or take 25,000. Kabwe was thus contemporary with the earliest Homo sapiens at Jebel Irhoud in Morocco and with Homo naledi. The skull is held by the Natural History Museum in London, and Zambia has asked for its return."]},{"heading":"The pit of bones","paragraphs":["The richest Middle Pleistocene site anywhere is Sima de los Huesos, the ‘pit of bones’, reached by crawling through the Cueva Mayor cave system in the Sierra de Atapuerca and descending a 13-metre shaft. Since the 1980s the team led by Juan Luis Arsuaga has recovered thousands of human fossils from at least 28 individuals, mostly teenagers and young adults, together with cave bears and other carnivores but almost no herbivores. In 2014 Arsuaga and colleagues dated the deposit to about 430,000 years ago and showed that the skulls combine Neanderthal-like faces and teeth with more primitive braincases: Neanderthal features evolved piece by piece, the face first.","Among the bones lay a single stone tool, a handaxe of red quartzite found in 1998 and nicknamed ‘Excalibur’. Cranium 17 has two nearly identical fractures above the left eye, made by the same object in two separate blows; Nohemi Sala and colleagues argued in 2015 that it is the oldest known case of lethal violence between people."]},{"heading":"DNA from 430,000 years ago","paragraphs":["The cave’s constant cool temperature preserved something extraordinary. In 2013 Matthias Meyer, Svante Pääbo and colleagues reconstructed a nearly complete mitochondrial genome from a Sima de los Huesos thighbone. To general surprise it was closer to that of the Denisovans, the Asian archaic group first identified in Siberia, than to Neanderthals. In 2016 the team recovered nuclear DNA from two individuals, which showed clearly that the Sima people belonged to the Neanderthal lineage after its split from the Denisovans.","The mitochondrial puzzle has a likely solution, set out by Cosimo Posth and colleagues in 2017: early Neanderthals carried Denisovan-like mitochondria, which were later replaced by a type introduced through interbreeding with an early African population related to Homo sapiens. The Sima results also show that Neanderthals and Denisovans had already diverged by 430,000 years ago."]},{"heading":"Homo naledi: a small brain in a late world","paragraphs":["In 2013 the cavers Steven Tucker and Rick Hunter squeezed through an 18-centimetre-wide chute in the Rising Star cave system, in South Africa’s Cradle of Humankind, and found bones in a chamber later named Dinaledi. Lee Berger recruited slender scientists able to fit through; by 2015 they had recovered more than 1,500 specimens from at least 15 individuals and named Homo naledi. It stood about 1.4 metres tall, with a brain of 465 to 610 cubic centimetres, human-like hands and feet, curved fingers suited to climbing, and primitive shoulders and hips. The 2017 dating was a shock: between 335,000 and 236,000 years ago, from uranium-series and electron spin resonance dating of teeth and flowstones.","In 2023 Berger and colleagues went further, arguing in preprints, a popular book and a documentary that Homo naledi buried its dead in pits dug into the cave floor, engraved cross-hatched patterns on the walls, and used fire to light its way. The journal eLife’s reviewers judged the evidence incomplete, citing a lack of sediment analyses showing that pits had been dug and of dates for the engravings. In 2024 a group led by María Martinón-Torres published a critique concluding that there was no scientific evidence for burial or rock art. As of 2026 most specialists regard these claims as unproven."]},{"heading":"East Asia’s big-headed crania","paragraphs":["China holds a rich series of crania that do not fit Homo erectus. The two Yunxian skulls from Hubei, found in 1989 and 1990 and crushed by sediment, are about 1 million years old. In 2025 a team led by Xiaobo Feng and Xijun Ni, with Chris Stringer, published in Science a digital reconstruction of one of them and an analysis placing it near the root of the lineage that includes the Harbin skull and, by implication, the Denisovans. They concluded that the lineages leading to Homo sapiens, Neanderthals and this Asian group had separated more than 1 million years ago, much earlier than most genetic estimates.","Younger crania include Dali in Shaanxi, found in 1978 and about 260,000 years old; Jinniushan in Liaoning, a partial skeleton of a large-bodied woman with a brain of about 1,300 cubic centimetres; and Hualongdong in Anhui, about 300,000 years old, whose teenage face and jaw mix archaic and modern-looking features. In 2024 Christopher Bae and Xiu-Jie Wu proposed the name Homo juluensis, ‘big head’, for very large-brained fossils from Xujiayao and Xuchang, suggesting Denisovans might belong to it; many colleagues doubt such fragmentary material can support a new species. The Harbin skull, hidden in a well after its discovery in 1933 and named Homo longi in 2021, was identified in 2025 from proteins and DNA as Denisovan, a story told in the Denisovan chapter. In India, the Narmada skull found at Hathnora in 1982 remains the best-known Middle Pleistocene hominin, with a poorly fixed age."]},{"heading":"The Levant and the genetic clock","paragraphs":["In 2021 Israel Hershkovitz, Yossi Zaidner and colleagues described part of a skull and a jaw from Nesher Ramla, found with Levallois tools and dated to about 140,000–120,000 years ago. They combine archaic and Neanderthal-like traits, and the team proposed a long-lived Levantine population that contributed to the Neanderthals of Europe. Others see simply early Neanderthals or a local archaic group.","Genetics supplies the framework. Comparisons of Neanderthal, Denisovan and modern genomes put the split between the lineage leading to modern humans and that leading to Neanderthals and Denisovans at roughly 765,000–550,000 years ago, and the Neanderthal–Denisovan split at roughly 470,000–380,000 years ago, although the numbers depend on assumed mutation rates. Denisovan genomes also carry traces of an older ‘superarchaic’ population, separated perhaps for more than a million years. The fossils of the middle period are what survives of these branching populations."]}],"debates":[{"question":"What should these fossils be called?","views":[{"label":"Keep the existing names","text":"Chris Stringer, Eric Delson and others argue that Homo heidelbergensis and Homo rhodesiensis, used carefully, are adequate. They note that the rules of zoological nomenclature do not allow names to be dropped for their associations, and that Homo bodoensis would be a junior synonym of rhodesiensis if Bodo and Kabwe belong to the same species."},{"label":"Adopt Homo bodoensis","text":"Mirjana Roksandic, Predrag Radović, Xiu-Jie Wu and Christopher Bae argue that heidelbergensis has become a catch-all, that many European fossils belong on the Neanderthal line, and that a clean name for the African ancestors of Homo sapiens reduces confusion while avoiding one that recalls Cecil Rhodes."}],"status":"Homo bodoensis has not been widely adopted. Many researchers use heidelbergensis loosely or avoid species names, describing fossils by site and lineage instead. Better-dated African fossils and ancient proteins will be needed for a consensus."},{"question":"Did the Sima de los Huesos people place their dead in the pit?","views":[{"label":"A deliberate accumulation","text":"Juan Luis Arsuaga, Eudald Carbonell and colleagues argue that the absence of herbivores and of living debris, the youthful age profile and the lone handaxe point to bodies deliberately dropped into the shaft, perhaps the oldest funerary behaviour known. Some have suggested Excalibur was an offering."},{"label":"Natural or accidental causes","text":"Peter Andrews, Yolanda Fernández-Jalvo and others proposed that bodies could have been washed or dragged in by natural processes or carnivores, or that people fell. A single handaxe could have arrived with a body."}],"status":"The site is widely seen as the strongest candidate for early mortuary behaviour, but intent cannot be demonstrated from bones alone, and there is no consensus."},{"question":"Did Homo naledi bury its dead and make engravings?","views":[{"label":"Yes","text":"Lee Berger, John Hawks, Agustín Fuentes and colleagues argue that oval pits holding articulated skeletons cut through the cave layers and were refilled, that cross-hatched marks were engraved on nearby walls, and that the remoteness of the chambers implies intentional transport of the dead by a small-brained species."},{"label":"Not demonstrated","text":"Critics, including eLife’s reviewers and a 2024 team led by María Martinón-Torres, say the work lacks the sediment analyses, dating and documentation needed to show that pits were dug, and that the engravings are undated and could be much younger. Some also object that the claims were promoted in a book and documentary before peer review."}],"status":"Unresolved and widely doubted in 2026. Most researchers accept that Homo naledi remains ended up in remote chambers, but not that they were buried or that the species made engravings."},{"question":"How deep are the splits between human lineages?","views":[{"label":"Deep, over a million years","text":"Xiaobo Feng, Xijun Ni, Chris Stringer and colleagues argue from the reconstructed Yunxian skull and a Bayesian analysis of anatomy that the lineages leading to Homo sapiens, Neanderthals and the Asian group including Harbin had diverged more than 1 million years ago."},{"label":"Shallower, as genomes suggest","text":"Most genetic estimates put the split between the sapiens lineage and the Neanderthal–Denisovan lineage at roughly 765,000–550,000 years ago, with Neanderthals and Denisovans as each other’s closest relatives. Critics add that reconstructing a crushed skull involves many choices and that anatomical trees are sensitive to convergent features and to continued interbreeding."}],"status":"Open. Ancient proteins from Yunxian or other fossils near a million years old, and better-calibrated mutation rates, could reconcile the two approaches or decide between them."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Sima de los Huesos","value":"about 430,000 years; at least 28 individuals; oldest hominin nuclear DNA"},{"label":"Bodo cranium","value":"about 600,000 years; cut marks from defleshing"},{"label":"Kabwe 1","value":"about 299,000 years (Zambia), dated directly in 2020"},{"label":"Homo naledi","value":"335,000–236,000 years, Rising Star Cave (South Africa)"},{"label":"Sapiens versus Neanderthal–Denisovan split","value":"roughly 765,000–550,000 years ago (genetic estimates)"},{"label":"Yunxian crania","value":"about 1 million years; 2025 reconstruction argues for deep lineage splits"}],"siteIds":["bodo","kabwe","sima-de-los-huesos","rising-star-cave","yunxian","dali","jinniushan","hualongdong","xujiayao","harbin","hathnora","nesher-ramla"],"routes":[],"themes":["species","genetics","burial","violence"],"sources":[{"cite":"Roksandic, M. et al. (2022). Resolving the “muddle in the middle”: the case for Homo bodoensis sp. nov. Evolutionary Anthropology 31, 20–29."},{"cite":"Grün, R. et al. (2020). Dating the skull from Broken Hill, Zambia, and its position in human evolution. Nature 580, 372–375."},{"cite":"Arsuaga, J. L. et al. (2014). Neandertal roots: cranial and chronological evidence from Sima de los Huesos. Science 344, 1358–1363."},{"cite":"Meyer, M. et al. (2014). A mitochondrial genome sequence of a hominin from Sima de los Huesos. Nature 505, 403–406."},{"cite":"Meyer, M. et al. (2016). Nuclear DNA sequences from the Middle Pleistocene Sima de los Huesos hominins. Nature 531, 504–507."},{"cite":"Dirks, P. H. G. M. et al. (2017). The age of Homo naledi and associated sediments in the Rising Star Cave, South Africa. eLife 6, e24231."},{"cite":"Martinón-Torres, M. et al. (2024). No scientific evidence that Homo naledi buried their dead and produced rock art. Journal of Human Evolution."},{"cite":"Feng, X. et al. (2025). The phylogenetic position of the Yunxian cranium elucidates the origin of Dragon Man and the Denisovans. Science."},{"cite":"Bae, C. J. & Wu, X. (2024). Making sense of eastern Asian Late Quaternary hominin variability. Nature Communications 15."},{"cite":"Hershkovitz, I. et al. (2021). A Middle Pleistocene Homo from Nesher Ramla, Israel. Science 372, 1424–1428."}]},{"id":"levallois-msa","order":11,"act":"many-humans","title":"Prepared Cores and Hafted Points","subtitle":"Levallois, the Middle Stone Age and the Middle Palaeolithic","era":"Middle Pleistocene; late Acheulean, early Middle Stone Age and Middle Palaeolithic","start":500000,"end":200000,"dateLabel":"about 500,000–200,000 years ago","regions":["africa","europe","asia"],"focus":[-12,-30,110,50],"emblem":"point","summary":"Between about 500,000 and 200,000 years ago, toolmakers across Africa and Eurasia moved from handaxes to prepared cores, hafted points and glue, while some began carrying stone over long distances and collecting pigments. Whether these changes spread with people or were invented many times is still debated.","paragraphs":[["For more than a million years, the handaxe was the signature tool of our ancestors.","Then, between about 500,000 and 200,000 years ago, toolmakers in many regions began doing something subtler.","Instead of shaping one stone into one tool, they shaped a core so that a single well-aimed blow would release a flake of a size and form decided in advance.","Archaeologists call it the Levallois method, after a suburb of Paris where such flakes were first described."],["At Kathu Pan in South Africa, stone points about 500,000 years old carry damage suggesting they were bound to wooden shafts as spear tips.","At Olorgesailie, in the Kenyan rift, people living around 320,000 years ago carried black obsidian from sources up to 95 kilometres away and kept lumps of red and black pigment.","The handaxes that had filled the same basin for hundreds of thousands of years were gone."],["The new ways of working stone appear far beyond Africa: in Armenia about 330,000 years ago, in southern India by roughly 385,000 years ago, and later in southwest China.","Some archaeologists see the spread of one population’s know-how.","Others see the same good idea arising again and again from older handaxe traditions."],["Around the same time, toolmakers in Italy fixed stone to wood with tar cooked from birch bark.","The age of composite tools, built from several parts and several materials, had begun."]],"sentences":["For more than a million years, the handaxe was the signature tool of our ancestors.","Then, between about 500,000 and 200,000 years ago, toolmakers in many regions began doing something subtler.","Instead of shaping one stone into one tool, they shaped a core so that a single well-aimed blow would release a flake of a size and form decided in advance.","Archaeologists call it the Levallois method, after a suburb of Paris where such flakes were first described.","At Kathu Pan in South Africa, stone points about 500,000 years old carry damage suggesting they were bound to wooden shafts as spear tips.","At Olorgesailie, in the Kenyan rift, people living around 320,000 years ago carried black obsidian from sources up to 95 kilometres away and kept lumps of red and black pigment.","The handaxes that had filled the same basin for hundreds of thousands of years were gone.","The new ways of working stone appear far beyond Africa: in Armenia about 330,000 years ago, in southern India by roughly 385,000 years ago, and later in southwest China.","Some archaeologists see the spread of one population’s know-how.","Others see the same good idea arising again and again from older handaxe traditions.","Around the same time, toolmakers in Italy fixed stone to wood with tar cooked from birch bark.","The age of composite tools, built from several parts and several materials, had begun."],"pronounce":{"Levallois":"Leh-val-wah","Kathu Pan":"Kah-too Pahn","Olorgesailie":"Oh-lor-geh-sigh-lee"},"body":[{"heading":"Planning in stone","paragraphs":["The Levallois method takes its name from Levallois-Perret, a suburb of Paris, where nineteenth-century collectors found distinctive flakes in gravel pits. The knapper first trims the edges of a nodule and then shapes one face into a gently domed surface, like a tortoise shell, with ridges placed to guide the fracture. A striking platform is prepared at one end. A single blow then removes a flake, point or blade whose shape was fixed by the preparation, and the core can be reshaped and struck again. Archaeologists see a longer chain of planned steps than in making a handaxe, and a way to get many thin, standardised blanks from one stone, ideal for fitting to handles.","The idea had roots. Late Acheulean knappers already prepared large cores to strike big flakes for handaxes and cleavers. In South Africa, so-called Victoria West cores, shaped to yield one large flake, appear at Canteen Kopje about 1 million years ago, according to a 2017 study by Hao Li, Kathleen Kuman and colleagues. Levallois can be seen as a miniaturised, more flexible version of these older habits."]},{"heading":"Kathu Pan and the first spear points","paragraphs":["At Kathu Pan 1, a sinkhole in South Africa’s Northern Cape, a layer dated by luminescence and by uranium-series and electron spin resonance dating of an animal tooth to about 500,000 years contains points and blades made from prepared cores. In 2012 Jayne Wilkins, Benjamin Schoville, Kyle Brown and Michael Chazan reported that some points show fractures typical of impact and thinning at the base consistent with hafting; replicas mounted on spears and thrust into carcasses produced similar damage. They concluded that these were hafted spear tips, some 200,000 years older than the previously accepted evidence.","Not everyone agreed. In 2014 Veerle Rots and Hugues Plisson argued that similar fractures can come from trampling, knapping accidents or other uses, and that hafting had not been demonstrated. The Kathu Pan team replied with further analyses. The points remain among the strongest early candidates for hafted weapons, but the case is not closed."]},{"heading":"Olorgesailie: the Middle Stone Age begins","paragraphs":["The Olorgesailie basin in southern Kenya holds one of Africa’s longest archaeological records, famous for dense scatters of Acheulean handaxes. In 2018 three papers in Science by Rick Potts, Alison Brooks, Alan Deino and colleagues described what came next. The last handaxes there are about 500,000 years old. After a gap in the sediment record, by about 320,000 years ago the toolkit had changed completely: smaller, carefully prepared points and flakes typical of the Middle Stone Age, and no handaxes.","The new toolmakers used obsidian from several sources 25 to 95 kilometres away, far beyond the few kilometres typical for Acheulean stone, which suggests wider ranges or exchange between groups. They also collected black manganese-rich rocks and red ochre, some of it ground or scraped, probably for pigment. The researchers linked these changes to a time of environmental instability, when earth movements reshaped the basin, climate swung between wet and dry, and much of the large-mammal community was replaced. The Olorgesailie Middle Stone Age is about as old as the earliest Homo sapiens fossils from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco, though no human bones were found with the tools."]},{"heading":"Blades and colour: Kapthurin and Twin Rivers","paragraphs":["Some innovations appear surprisingly early. In the Kapthurin Formation near Lake Baringo in Kenya, Sally McBrearty and colleagues found that blades, long narrow flakes usually associated with the much later Upper Palaeolithic, were made systematically about 545,000–509,000 years ago. Younger layers at Kapthurin, about 285,000 years old, contain Levallois flakes, grindstones and pieces of red ochre.","At Twin Rivers, a hilltop cave near Lusaka in Zambia, Lawrence Barham found hundreds of pieces of pigment, including red and yellow ochre, specularite and purple varieties, some ground or scraped, in deposits roughly 400,000 to 260,000 years old. Barham argued in 2002 that the systematic collection of a range of colours points to early symbolic use, perhaps body decoration. Others note that ochre has practical uses, as an ingredient in adhesives, as sun protection or in treating hides. The debate over colour and symbolism continues with the younger Middle Stone Age finds of later chapters."]},{"heading":"One invention or many?","paragraphs":["Prepared-core technology turns up across Africa and Eurasia within a few hundred thousand years, and where it came from is contested. At Nor Geghi 1 in Armenia’s Hrazdan valley, sandwiched between lava flows, Daniel Adler and colleagues reported in 2014 an assemblage about 335,000–325,000 years old in which Levallois cores and flakes occur alongside bifaces made with the same technical logic, using obsidian from several sources. Adler argued that Levallois here grew out of local handaxe-making rather than arriving with a new population from Africa.","At Attirampakkam in southern India, where Acheulean levels go back about 1.5 million years, Kumar Akhilesh, Shanti Pappu and colleagues reported in 2018 a Middle Palaeolithic toolkit with Levallois flakes and points, dated by luminescence to between about 385,000 and 172,000 years ago, as handaxes faded. This is earlier than most estimates for Homo sapiens outside Africa, undercutting the idea that such tools in India had to be brought by our species. In southwest China, where Levallois was long thought absent, Hu Yue, Ben Marwick and colleagues reported in 2019 Levallois cores and flakes from Guanyindong cave in Guizhou, dated by luminescence to about 170,000–80,000 years ago.","In the Levant, the Acheulo-Yabrudian complex of about 420,000–200,000 years ago, best known from Qesem Cave near Tel Aviv, combined late handaxes, thick scrapers and a precocious blade industry called the Amudian, before Levallois-based industries took over."]},{"heading":"Handles and glue","paragraphs":["Levallois points and flakes were well suited to hafting, and hafting changed what tools could do. A composite tool, stone fixed to wood with binding or glue, gives more leverage, reach and force than a hand-held stone, and it requires making and assembling separate parts. The oldest known adhesive comes from Campitello Quarry in Tuscany, where Paul Mazza and colleagues reported in 2006 two flint flakes partly coated in birch-bark tar, in deposits about 200,000 years old with remains of straight-tusked elephants. Birch tar does not occur naturally; it forms only when bark is heated with little oxygen.","How demanding that was is itself debated. Experiments by Paul Kozowyk and colleagues suggested that making useful amounts takes skill and planning; in 2019 Patrick Schmidt and colleagues showed that tar can condense on a stone surface next to burning birch bark, a much simpler route. Either way, the Campitello flakes show early Neanderthals combining fire, chemistry and stone."]},{"heading":"Why the handaxe faded, and what to call it","paragraphs":["The Acheulean did not end everywhere at once. Handaxes disappear at Olorgesailie by 320,000 years ago, persist in parts of Arabia and Europe until about 200,000 years ago or later, and Neanderthals were still making them 60,000 years ago. Suggested reasons for the shift include the advantages of smaller, standardised, hafted tools; larger and better-connected populations able to maintain complex skills; changes in planning abilities; and unstable environments that rewarded flexible toolkits. None is tied to a single species: the ancestors of both Neanderthals and Homo sapiens made the change.","To organise this variety, the Cambridge archaeologist Grahame Clark proposed in 1969 five technological modes: Mode 1, simple cores and flakes (Oldowan); Mode 2, bifaces (Acheulean); Mode 3, prepared cores (Levallois, the Middle Palaeolithic and Middle Stone Age); Mode 4, long blades from prismatic cores (Upper Palaeolithic); and Mode 5, microliths. The scheme is still widely used, but critics such as John Shea note that it implies a single ladder of progress, while blades at Kapthurin half a million years ago and African microliths older than 60,000 years break the sequence. In 2013 Shea proposed nine modes, A to I, describing techniques rather than stages. The labels Middle Stone Age, coined for South Africa by John Goodwin and Clarence van Riet Lowe in 1929, and Middle Palaeolithic reflect the history of research more than real boundaries."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Did Levallois spread with one population, or was it invented many times?","views":[{"label":"Spread by dispersal","text":"Robert Foley and Marta Mirazón Lahr argued in 1997 that prepared-core technologies were carried out of Africa by a population ancestral to both Neanderthals and modern humans, so their spread traces a dispersal. Some researchers still link particular regional appearances, in the Levant or India, to movements of early Homo sapiens or related groups."},{"label":"Invented independently","text":"Daniel Adler and colleagues, working at Nor Geghi, argue that Levallois emerged from local Acheulean traditions in several regions, because the logic of preparing a core was already present in late handaxe-making. Guanyindong in China and Attirampakkam in India show prepared-core technology without clear evidence of new populations arriving."}],"status":"Most researchers now accept that Levallois arose more than once, but the balance between invention and dispersal is argued region by region, and some appearances may still mark population movements."},{"question":"Were the Kathu Pan points hafted spear tips?","views":[{"label":"Yes","text":"Jayne Wilkins, Benjamin Schoville, Kyle Brown and Michael Chazan point to impact fractures, basal thinning suited to hafting, and experiments in which spear-mounted replicas acquired the same damage, making these the oldest known hafted weapons, about 500,000 years old."},{"label":"Not demonstrated","text":"Veerle Rots and Hugues Plisson argue that the fracture types can form through trampling, manufacture or other uses, and that without direct traces of hafting, such as adhesive residues or binding wear, the interpretation goes beyond the evidence."}],"status":"Still debated. The points are widely cited as the earliest likely hafted weapons; residue analyses or further well-dated finds of similar age would settle the question."},{"question":"Do stone-tool ‘modes’ and period names help or mislead?","views":[{"label":"Useful shorthand","text":"Grahame Clark’s five modes and the terms Middle Stone Age and Middle Palaeolithic give a shared vocabulary for comparing regions across millions of years, and many researchers still find them indispensable for broad synthesis."},{"label":"Misleading ladders","text":"John Shea and others argue that these schemes imply a single progression and a tight link between technology and species that the evidence contradicts: blades, prepared cores and microliths appear, vanish and reappear independently. Shea’s Modes A to I describe what was done rather than a stage reached."}],"status":"Both systems are in use. Researchers increasingly describe specific techniques and their dates, treating the old period names as convenient labels rather than stages of development."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Earliest likely hafted points","value":"about 500,000 years, Kathu Pan 1 (South Africa)"},{"label":"Early systematic blades","value":"about 545,000–509,000 years, Kapthurin Formation (Kenya)"},{"label":"Early Middle Stone Age","value":"about 320,000 years, Olorgesailie (Kenya); obsidian from 25–95 km away"},{"label":"Early Levallois in the Caucasus","value":"about 335,000–325,000 years, Nor Geghi 1 (Armenia)"},{"label":"Middle Palaeolithic in India","value":"from about 385,000 years, Attirampakkam"},{"label":"Oldest known adhesive","value":"birch-bark tar, about 200,000 years, Campitello (Italy)"}],"siteIds":["canteen-kopje","kathu-pan-1","kapthurin","olorgesailie","twin-rivers","nor-geghi-1","qesem-cave","attirampakkam","guanyindong","campitello"],"routes":[],"themes":["technology","art","society","migration"],"sources":[{"cite":"Wilkins, J., Schoville, B. J., Brown, K. S. & Chazan, M. (2012). Evidence for early hafted hunting technology. Science 338, 942–946."},{"cite":"Rots, V. & Plisson, H. (2014). Projectiles and the abuse of the use-wear method in a search for impact. Journal of Archaeological Science 48, 154–165."},{"cite":"Brooks, A. S. et al. (2018). Long-distance stone transport and pigment use in the earliest Middle Stone Age. Science 360, 90–94."},{"cite":"Deino, A. L. et al. (2018). Chronology of the Acheulean to Middle Stone Age transition in eastern Africa. Science 360, 95–98."},{"cite":"Barham, L. S. (2002). Systematic pigment use in the Middle Pleistocene of south-central Africa. Current Anthropology 43, 181–190."},{"cite":"Adler, D. S. et al. (2014). Early Levallois technology and the Lower to Middle Paleolithic transition in the Southern Caucasus. Science 345, 1609–1613."},{"cite":"Akhilesh, K. et al. (2018). Early Middle Palaeolithic culture in India around 385–172 ka reframes Out of Africa models. Nature 554, 97–101."},{"cite":"Hu, Y. et al. (2019). Late Middle Pleistocene Levallois stone-tool technology in southwest China. Nature 565, 82–85."},{"cite":"Mazza, P. P. A. et al. (2006). A new Palaeolithic discovery: tar-hafted stone tools in a European Mid-Pleistocene bone-bearing bed. Journal of Archaeological Science 33, 1310–1318."},{"cite":"Shea, J. J. (2013). Lithic Modes A–I: a new framework for describing global-scale variation in stone tool technology illustrated with evidence from the East Mediterranean Levant. Journal of Archaeological Method and Theory 20, 151–186."}]},{"id":"neanderthals","order":12,"act":"many-humans","title":"The Neanderthals","subtitle":"Hunters, craftspeople and carers from Iberia to the Altai","era":"Middle Palaeolithic","start":430000,"end":40000,"dateLabel":"about 430,000–40,000 years ago","regions":["europe","asia"],"focus":[-10,29,90,57],"emblem":"hand","summary":"For more than 350,000 years Neanderthals lived across western Eurasia, hunting elephants, rendering bone grease, making tar, cord and fire, marking caves and shells, burying some of their dead and caring for the sick. Their genomes reveal small, scattered communities and very old contacts with our own ancestors.","paragraphs":[["About 125,000 years ago, beside a lake in what is now eastern Germany, a group of Neanderthals butchered a straight-tusked elephant bull that weighed around 13 tonnes, about twice as much as a large African elephant today.","Cut marks cover almost every bone.","At a neighbouring lake they smashed the bones of more than 170 deer, horses and wild cattle and simmered them in water to skim off the fat."],["These were the heirs of the prepared-core toolmakers of the last chapter.","For more than 350,000 years their lineage lived from the Atlantic coast of Iberia to the Altai Mountains of Siberia.","They made glue from birch bark, twisted plant fibres into cord and struck sparks from pyrite.","Deep inside Bruniquel Cave in France, 176,000 years ago, they broke off hundreds of stalagmites and arranged them in rings, working by firelight more than 300 metres from daylight."],["They also looked after one another.","A man at Shanidar in Iraq lived for years with a withered arm, a damaged eye and failing hearing.","A Neanderthal child in Spain, born with the inner-ear malformation of Down syndrome, survived to at least six."],["Their genomes, read from bones and teeth, describe small communities: a father and his teenage daughter in a Siberian cave, and whole lineages that kept apart for tens of thousands of years.","They were not a rough draft of us, but a people with a long history of their own."]],"sentences":["About 125,000 years ago, beside a lake in what is now eastern Germany, a group of Neanderthals butchered a straight-tusked elephant bull that weighed around 13 tonnes, about twice as much as a large African elephant today.","Cut marks cover almost every bone.","At a neighbouring lake they smashed the bones of more than 170 deer, horses and wild cattle and simmered them in water to skim off the fat.","These were the heirs of the prepared-core toolmakers of the last chapter.","For more than 350,000 years their lineage lived from the Atlantic coast of Iberia to the Altai Mountains of Siberia.","They made glue from birch bark, twisted plant fibres into cord and struck sparks from pyrite.","Deep inside Bruniquel Cave in France, 176,000 years ago, they broke off hundreds of stalagmites and arranged them in rings, working by firelight more than 300 metres from daylight.","They also looked after one another.","A man at Shanidar in Iraq lived for years with a withered arm, a damaged eye and failing hearing.","A Neanderthal child in Spain, born with the inner-ear malformation of Down syndrome, survived to at least six.","Their genomes, read from bones and teeth, describe small communities: a father and his teenage daughter in a Siberian cave, and whole lineages that kept apart for tens of thousands of years.","They were not a rough draft of us, but a people with a long history of their own."],"pronounce":{"Bruniquel":"Brew-nee-kell","Shanidar":"Shah-nee-dar","Altai":"Al-tie"},"body":[{"heading":"A lineage 400,000 years in the making","paragraphs":["At the Sima de los Huesos in Spain’s Atapuerca hills, the bones of at least 28 people from about 430,000 years ago already show Neanderthal features in the face and teeth but more primitive braincases, and ancient DNA places them on the Neanderthal branch. The full set of traits accumulated piece by piece and was largely in place by about 150,000–130,000 years ago.","Neanderthals had long, low skulls with a bulge at the back, a brain averaging about 1,400 cubic centimetres (slightly larger than ours), a projecting midface with a wide nasal opening, and stocky bodies with broad chests and short forearms and shins. A study of a juvenile skeleton from El Sidrón in Spain, led by Antonio Rosas in 2017, found that they grew at a pace close to ours, although the brain was still growing at an age when a modern child’s is almost complete. They ranged from Portugal to the Altai Mountains and from Wales to the Levant and the Zagros Mountains of Iraq, through ice ages and through interglacials as warm as today."]},{"heading":"Elephants, grease and seafood","paragraphs":["At Neumark-Nord, ancient lake basins exposed by lignite mining in eastern Germany, Neanderthals of the last interglacial, about 125,000 years ago, killed and dismembered straight-tusked elephants. A 2023 study led by Sabine Gaudzinski-Windheuser and Lutz Kindler examined the remains of more than 70 animals, mostly adult males of up to 13 tonnes, with cut marks on nearly every bone. The preference for big males points to hunting rather than scavenging, and one bull could have fed dozens of people for weeks, suggesting groups larger or more settled than usually assumed.","In 2025 the same group described a “fat factory” at the neighbouring site of Neumark-Nord 2. There Neanderthals smashed the bones of at least 172 deer, horses and aurochs into tens of thousands of fragments and heated them in water to render bone grease, a labour-intensive technique previously credited only to much later modern humans.","Plants mattered too. Starch grains trapped in dental calculus from Shanidar in Iraq and Spy in Belgium include cooked grass seeds, legumes and date palm (Amanda Henry and colleagues, 2011). DNA in calculus from El Sidrón records mushrooms and pine nuts, and one individual with a dental abscess carried traces of poplar, a natural source of salicylic acid, and of Penicillium mould (Laura Weyrich and colleagues, 2017). At Gruta da Figueira Brava on the Portuguese coast, between about 106,000 and 86,000 years ago, Neanderthals gathered mussels, limpets and crabs and ate fish, seabirds and seals (João Zilhão and colleagues, 2020)."]},{"heading":"Tar, string and sparks","paragraphs":["Neanderthal stoneworking is called Mousterian, after Le Moustier in the Dordogne. It combined the Levallois method with other systems, such as thick Quina scrapers, and the Micoquian of central and eastern Europe added asymmetric bifacial knives that could be resharpened repeatedly.","They made glue. Birch tar forms only when bark is heated with little oxygen, and two lumps from Königsaue in Germany, one bearing the imprint of a stone tool and of a fingertip, show Neanderthals producing it. Experiments by Patrick Schmidt in 2019 showed that small amounts of tar can form simply by burning bark next to a stone, but a 2023 chemical study concluded that the Königsaue tar was made by a more demanding method.","At Abri du Maras in the Ardèche, a stone flake preserved a fragment of three-ply cord twisted from inner-bark fibres, about 52,000–41,000 years old (Bruce Hardy and colleagues, 2020); cord implies bags, nets or snares that rarely survive. In 2018 Andrew Sorensen, Émilie Claud and Marie Soressi showed that microscopic wear on some 20 handaxes from French sites about 50,000 years old matches the traces left by striking pyrite to make sparks, evidence that Neanderthals could make fire at will."]},{"heading":"Rings, shells, talons and lines","paragraphs":["The most startling structure lies 336 metres inside Bruniquel Cave in southwestern France. Jacques Jaubert’s team documented about 400 pieces of broken stalagmite, some 112 metres in total length, arranged in two rings and several piles, with traces of fire. Uranium-series dating of calcite that grew back over the broken pieces gave an age of about 176,500 years (2016). Their purpose is unknown; building them required light, planning and cooperation.","At Cueva de los Aviones near Cartagena in Spain, perforated seashells bearing red and yellow pigment were dated to about 115,000–120,000 years ago (Dirk Hoffmann, João Zilhão and colleagues, 2018). At Krapina in Croatia, eight white-tailed eagle talons from about 130,000 years ago carry cut marks and polish consistent with being worn.","Cave painting is more contested. In 2018 Hoffmann’s team dated mineral crusts over a red ladder-like sign at La Pasiega, a hand stencil at Maltravieso and red-stained stalagmites at Ardales in Spain to more than 64,000 years ago, long before Homo sapiens reached Iberia. Critics replied that uranium leaching can make such crusts look too old; a 2021 study confirmed that the Ardales pigment was deliberately applied, but the ages are still debated.","Clearer marks come from La Roche-Cotard in the Loire valley, where finger flutings on a soft chalk wall were sealed in by sediment about 57,000 years ago, in a cave containing only Mousterian tools (Jean-Claude Marquet and colleagues, 2023). A giant deer toe bone from Einhornhöhle in Germany was engraved about 51,000 years ago with stacked chevrons (Dirk Leder and colleagues, 2021), and in 2025 Francesco d’Errico’s team described pieces of ochre shaped into crayons and resharpened at Micoquian sites in Crimea."]},{"heading":"Graves and a flower story","paragraphs":["In 1908 the Bouyssonie brothers found an elderly man in a pit at La Chapelle-aux-Saints in France. Sceptics have questioned whether such early finds were true burials, but new fieldwork led by William Rendu (2014) showed that the pit was dug by people. At La Ferrassie in the Dordogne, seven or more individuals, including infants, were found; a 2020 restudy of the two-year-old La Ferrassie 8 concluded that the child was buried intentionally about 41,000 years ago.","At Shanidar Cave in Iraqi Kurdistan, Ralph Solecki excavated the remains of ten Neanderthals between 1951 and 1960. Clumps of pollen near Shanidar 4 inspired the idea of a “flower burial”, but a 2023 study led by Chris Hunt argued that burrowing bees more likely brought the pollen in. In 2018 renewed excavations led by Graeme Barker found Shanidar Z, a woman about 75,000 years old, in a cluster of bodies which suggests that the spot was used repeatedly for the dead."]},{"heading":"Care and speech","paragraphs":["Shanidar 1, a man who lived into his forties, had a withered right arm, a crushing blow to the left side of his face that probably affected his eye, an injured leg and probably severe hearing loss, yet he survived for years (Erik Trinkaus and Sébastien Villotte, 2017). In 2024 Mercedes Conde-Valverde’s team showed that an inner-ear fragment from Cova Negra near Valencia belonged to a child of at least six with a malformation found only in Down syndrome, which would have caused deafness and disabling vertigo. They argue that the child’s survival required care from more people than the mother alone.","Could Neanderthals speak? The Kebara 2 skeleton from Israel, about 60,000 years old, preserves a hyoid bone almost identical to ours. Neanderthals carried the modern human version of FOXP2, a gene involved in the fine motor control of speech. In 2021 Conde-Valverde and colleagues modelled the outer and middle ears of Neanderthal skulls and found hearing tuned much like ours, including the frequencies important for consonants. Most researchers now accept that Neanderthals spoke; what their languages were like cannot be known."]},{"heading":"Small communities, long isolations","paragraphs":["A toe bone from Denisova Cave, sequenced in 2014, came from a woman whose parents were as closely related as half-siblings, and the Chagyrskaya 8 genome (2020) points to groups of fewer than about 60 people. In 2022 Laurits Skov and colleagues sequenced 13 Neanderthals from Chagyrskaya and Okladnikov caves in the Altai, about 59,000–51,000 years old, among them a father and his teenage daughter. Y chromosomes were far less diverse than mitochondrial DNA, suggesting that women usually moved between communities of perhaps 10–20 people.","“Thorin”, a man from Grotte Mandrin in the Rhône valley who lived about 50,000–42,000 years ago, belonged to a lineage that had split from other late Neanderthals about 105,000 years ago and stayed genetically isolated for some 50,000 years (Ludovic Slimak and colleagues, 2024). In 2026 a study led by Cosimo Posth argued that most late European Neanderthals descend from a population that survived a collapse about 75,000 years ago in a refuge in southwestern France, while a 2026 study of 27 late Neanderthals from Belgium and France found well-connected groups rather than dwindling, inbred ones. Their disappearance is the subject of chapter 20.","Genes also flowed into Neanderthals from our side long before the main encounter. Their mitochondrial DNA and Y chromosomes were replaced by lineages from early relatives of Homo sapiens, and a 2024 study by Liming Li and Joshua Akey estimated that 2.5–3.7 percent of late Neanderthal genomes came from modern human ancestors, partly from contact about 250,000–200,000 years ago. The reverse flow, into our own ancestors, is the story of chapter 18."]}],"debates":[{"question":"How symbolic were Neanderthals?","views":[{"label":"A symbolic culture like ours","text":"João Zilhão, Francesco d’Errico, Dirk Hoffmann and others argue that pigment-stained shells at Cueva de los Aviones, eagle talons at Krapina, the Bruniquel rings, the Einhornhöhle engraving, the La Roche-Cotard flutings and the Crimean ochre crayons add up to a symbolic culture comparable to that of contemporary Homo sapiens in Africa, and that Iberian cave art older than 64,000 years can only be Neanderthal."},{"label":"Rare and ambiguous","text":"Sceptics, including Randall White and colleagues, who challenged the 2018 cave-art dates, argue that uranium-thorium ages on thin crusts can be too old, that many marks could be natural or practical, and that 300,000 years of Neanderthal history produced far fewer and less standardised symbolic objects than Homo sapiens made after 50,000 years ago."}],"status":"Most researchers now accept that Neanderthals used pigments, wore ornaments and made deliberate marks. The disagreement is over how often, whether this reflects the same capacity as ours, and whether any Iberian cave painting is truly Neanderthal. Direct dating of the pigment itself would settle the art question."},{"question":"Were Neanderthal bodies and faces shaped by the cold?","views":[{"label":"Cold adaptation","text":"Short limbs and broad trunks conserve heat, as Bergmann’s and Allen’s rules predict for cold-climate mammals, and computer simulations of airflow published in 2017 and 2018 suggest that the large Neanderthal nasal cavity warmed and moistened cold, dry air efficiently and supported high energy demands."},{"label":"Genetic drift","text":"Timothy Weaver, Charles Roseman and Chris Stringer showed in 2007 that most skull differences between Neanderthals and modern humans are consistent with random genetic drift in small, isolated populations. Others note that Neanderthal traits were already forming in warm periods and that Neanderthals thrived in interglacials."}],"status":"Many researchers now see a mixture: drift in small populations shaped much of the skull, while body proportions and perhaps the nose reflect climate and high energy use."},{"question":"Did Neanderthals bury their dead?","views":[{"label":"Deliberate burial","text":"Re-excavations at La Chapelle-aux-Saints (William Rendu, 2014), the restudy of La Ferrassie 8 (Antoine Balzeau and colleagues, 2020) and the new finds at Shanidar show pits dug by people, complete articulated bodies and places used repeatedly for the dead."},{"label":"Natural or unproven","text":"Harold Dibble, Dennis Sandgathe and earlier Robert Gargett argued that many skeletons excavated in the early twentieth century were poorly recorded and could have been covered by natural processes, and that claimed grave goods such as the Shanidar flowers have natural explanations."}],"status":"The case for intentional burial is now strong at a handful of sites, but there is no convincing evidence for grave goods, and burial seems to have been occasional rather than a general custom."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Time span","value":"Neanderthal lineage from about 430,000 (Sima de los Huesos) to about 40,000 years ago"},{"label":"Range","value":"From Portugal and Gibraltar to the Altai Mountains of Siberia"},{"label":"Largest prey","value":"Straight-tusked elephants of up to 13 tonnes, Neumark-Nord, about 125,000 years ago"},{"label":"Oldest construction","value":"Stalagmite rings in Bruniquel Cave, about 176,500 years old"},{"label":"First family","value":"A father and his teenage daughter, Chagyrskaya Cave (2022)"},{"label":"Modern human DNA in Neanderthals","value":"2.5–3.7 percent of late Neanderthal genomes (2024 estimate)"}],"siteIds":["neumark-nord","figueira-brava","abri-du-maras","bruniquel-cave","cueva-de-los-aviones","krapina","la-roche-cotard","la-chapelle-aux-saints","shanidar-cave","cova-negra","kebara-cave","chagyrskaya-cave"],"routes":[],"themes":["species","technology","food","fire","art","ornament","burial","genetics","language","society"],"sources":[{"cite":"Gaudzinski-Windheuser, S. et al. (2023). Hunting and processing of straight-tusked elephants 125.000 years ago: Implications for Neanderthal behavior. Science Advances 9, eadd8186.","doi":"10.1126/sciadv.add8186"},{"cite":"Kindler, L. et al. (2025). Large-scale processing of within-bone nutrients by Neanderthals, 125,000 years ago. Science Advances 11, eadv1257.","doi":"10.1126/sciadv.adv1257"},{"cite":"Jaubert, J. et al. (2016). Early Neanderthal constructions deep in Bruniquel Cave in southwestern France. Nature 534, 111–114.","doi":"10.1038/nature18291"},{"cite":"Hoffmann, D. L. et al. (2018). U-Th dating of carbonate crusts reveals Neandertal origin of Iberian cave art. Science 359, 912–915.","doi":"10.1126/science.aap7778"},{"cite":"Marquet, J.-C. et al. (2023). The earliest unambiguous Neanderthal engravings on cave walls: La Roche-Cotard, Loire Valley, France. PLOS ONE 18, e0286568.","doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0286568"},{"cite":"Leder, D. et al. (2021). A 51,000-year-old engraved bone reveals Neanderthals’ capacity for symbolic behaviour. Nature Ecology & Evolution 5.","doi":"10.1038/s41559-021-01487-z"},{"cite":"Conde-Valverde, M. et al. (2021). Neanderthals and Homo sapiens had similar auditory and speech capacities. Nature Ecology & Evolution 5.","doi":"10.1038/s41559-021-01391-6"},{"cite":"Skov, L. et al. (2022). Genetic insights into the social organization of Neanderthals. Nature 610, 519–525.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-022-05283-y"},{"cite":"Slimak, L. et al. (2024). Long genetic and social isolation in Neanderthals before their extinction. Cell Genomics 4."},{"cite":"Li, L., Comi, T. J., Bierman, R. F. & Akey, J. M. (2024). Recurrent gene flow between Neanderthals and modern humans over the past 200,000 years. Science 385, eadi1768.","doi":"10.1126/science.adi1768"}]},{"id":"denisovans","order":13,"act":"many-humans","title":"The Denisovans","subtitle":"A people found in DNA first, and only later in bone","era":"Middle and Late Pleistocene","start":300000,"end":30000,"dateLabel":"about 300,000–30,000 years ago","regions":["asia","oceania"],"focus":[70,-12,150,56],"emblem":"dna","summary":"Discovered in 2010 through the genome of a finger bone from a Siberian cave, the Denisovans have since been traced by proteins and DNA to the Tibetan Plateau, Laos, the Taiwan Strait and northeast China, where the Harbin skull finally gave them a face. Their DNA survives in people across Asia and Oceania.","paragraphs":[["In 2008, archaeologists in Denisova Cave, in the Altai Mountains of Siberia, found a fragment of a child’s little finger.","Two years later, geneticists in Leipzig read its DNA and discovered a kind of human no one had known existed.","They named them after the cave: the Denisovans."],["For a decade, the Denisovans were a genome without a face.","Then fossils began to surface: a jaw kept by a Buddhist monk on the Tibetan Plateau, a child’s tooth from a cave in Laos, a jaw dredged up by fishermen from the Taiwan Strait.","Each was identified not by its shape but by the ancient proteins inside it.","In 2025 a massive skull from Harbin in northeast China, hidden in a well for decades after its discovery, was linked to the Denisovans by its proteins and by DNA from the hardened plaque on its teeth."],["These people lived from the cold Altai to the tropics.","At a cave 3,280 metres above sea level on the Tibetan Plateau they hunted blue sheep and wild yak, and they were still there perhaps 40,000 years ago."],["They live on in us.","People in New Guinea and Australia carry several percent Denisovan DNA, and the Ayta Magbukon of the Philippines carry the most of all.","Many Tibetans owe their ease at high altitude to a gene inherited from Denisovans."]],"sentences":["In 2008, archaeologists in Denisova Cave, in the Altai Mountains of Siberia, found a fragment of a child’s little finger.","Two years later, geneticists in Leipzig read its DNA and discovered a kind of human no one had known existed.","They named them after the cave: the Denisovans.","For a decade, the Denisovans were a genome without a face.","Then fossils began to surface: a jaw kept by a Buddhist monk on the Tibetan Plateau, a child’s tooth from a cave in Laos, a jaw dredged up by fishermen from the Taiwan Strait.","Each was identified not by its shape but by the ancient proteins inside it.","In 2025 a massive skull from Harbin in northeast China, hidden in a well for decades after its discovery, was linked to the Denisovans by its proteins and by DNA from the hardened plaque on its teeth.","These people lived from the cold Altai to the tropics.","At a cave 3,280 metres above sea level on the Tibetan Plateau they hunted blue sheep and wild yak, and they were still there perhaps 40,000 years ago.","They live on in us.","People in New Guinea and Australia carry several percent Denisovan DNA, and the Ayta Magbukon of the Philippines carry the most of all.","Many Tibetans owe their ease at high altitude to a gene inherited from Denisovans."],"pronounce":{"Denisova":"Deh-nee-soh-va","Denisovans":"Deh-nee-soh-vans","Ayta Magbukon":"Eye-tah Mag-boo-kon","Altai":"Al-tie"},"body":[{"heading":"A genome before a face","paragraphs":["Denisova Cave lies in the Anui valley of the Russian Altai. In 2008 excavators from Novosibirsk, led by Anatoly Derevianko and Michael Shunkov, recovered a fragment of a girl’s little finger. When Svante Pääbo’s group in Leipzig sequenced its mitochondrial DNA in 2010, it matched neither modern humans nor Neanderthals. The nuclear genome, published by David Reich, Pääbo and colleagues later that year, showed that the girl belonged to a sister group of the Neanderthals, named Denisovans after the cave. The girl, Denisova 3, lived roughly 76,000–52,000 years ago.","The cave has since produced several very large Denisovan molars and a bone fragment, Denisova 11, first recognised as human by collagen fingerprinting. Its genome, reported in 2018 by Viviane Slon and colleagues, came from a girl of at least 13, who lived about 90,000 years ago, whose mother was a Neanderthal and whose father was a Denisovan. DNA recovered from the cave’s sediments shows Denisovans present from roughly 250,000 years ago, with Neanderthals at other times. In 2025 the Leipzig group released a preprint describing a high-coverage genome from Denisova 25, a molar about 200,000 years old, the oldest human genome of such quality; it indicates that people in Oceania and South Asia inherited DNA from a deeply divergent Denisovan population."]},{"heading":"On the roof of the world","paragraphs":["The first Denisovan fossil found outside Siberia came from Baishiya Karst Cave, a Buddhist sacred site 3,280 metres above sea level on the northeastern Tibetan Plateau in Gansu, China. A monk found a half mandible there in 1980. In 2019 Fahu Chen, Frido Welker, Jean-Jacques Hublin and colleagues extracted ancient proteins from one of its molars, identified it as Denisovan, and dated a carbonate crust on the bone to at least 160,000 years ago. The jaw is robust, with very large molars.","Sediment DNA published in 2020 showed Denisovans in the cave around 100,000 and 60,000 years ago. In 2024 a Nature study led by Huan Xia and Dongju Zhang identified about 2,500 bone fragments by their proteins and anatomy. Among them was a Denisovan rib from layers dated to about 48,000–32,000 years ago. Cut marks and impact scars show that Denisovans butchered blue sheep, wild yak, horses, woolly rhinoceros and spotted hyena, took marmots and birds, and used bones as tools. They lived at high altitude through cold and warm periods for more than 100,000 years."]},{"heading":"Into the tropics and across the strait","paragraphs":["In 2022 Fabrice Demeter, Clément Zanolli and colleagues described a child’s molar from Tam Ngu Hao 2 (Cobra Cave) in the Annamite mountains of northern Laos, dated to about 164,000–131,000 years ago. Its shape closely matches the Baishiya molars, and proteins suggest the child was probably a girl. It placed Denisovans, or a close relative, in the forests of Southeast Asia.","The Penghu 1 jaw was dredged up by fishermen from the Penghu Channel between Taiwan and the Penghu islands, seafloor that was dry land when glacial sea levels were low, and bought from an antique shop in Tainan. Described in 2015 as archaic Homo, it probably dates to one of the low sea-level phases between about 190,000 and 10,000 years ago. In 2025 Takumi Tsutaya, Frido Welker and colleagues recovered more than 4,000 amino acids from it, including two Denisovan-specific variants and a Y-chromosome form of the enamel protein amelogenin, which shows that it came from a male.","In September 2026 two papers in Nature reported new Denisovan fossils from Bianfu Cave, in an abandoned quarry in Yunnan in southwestern China: four teeth, two skull fragments and part of an arm bone, from layers the team estimates at roughly 170,000–130,000 years old, alongside more than 1,300 stone and bone tools and butchered animal bones. The finds take the number of known Denisovan fossils past 20."]},{"heading":"The Harbin skull and a Denisovan face","paragraphs":["According to his family, a labourer found a nearly complete skull in 1933 while a bridge was being built over the Songhua River at Harbin in northeast China, and hid it in a well. It reached scientists in 2018. In 2021 Qiang Ji, Xijun Ni and colleagues named it Homo longi, “Dragon Man”, and uranium-series dating gave a minimum age of about 146,000 years. The skull is huge, with a brain of about 1,420 cubic centimetres, a massive brow, broad square eye sockets, a wide nose and an enormous molar.","In June 2025 teams led by Qiaomei Fu at the Institute of Vertebrate Paleontology and Paleoanthropology in Beijing published two studies. Ancient proteins from the skull included Denisovan-specific variants, and mitochondrial DNA recovered from the dental calculus fell within the Denisovan range, close to early Denisovans from the Siberian cave. Harbin became the first Denisovan with a face.","Before any skull was known, David Gokhman and Liran Carmel had tried in 2019 to predict Denisovan anatomy from DNA methylation, chemical marks that switch genes off, in the Denisova 3 genome. They predicted traits such as a wide skull and a long dental arch. The Baishiya jaw and the Harbin skull broadly fit the direction of these predictions, but the method rests on a single finger bone and on gene effects inferred from modern skeletal disorders, so it could never say how large any difference was."]},{"heading":"What to call them","paragraphs":["“Denisovan” is a population label, not a species name. Now that Harbin is linked to them, some researchers, including Xijun Ni and Chris Stringer, argue that Denisovans should be called Homo longi. In 2024 Christopher Bae and Xiujie Wu proposed Homo juluensis, “big head”, for fossils from Xujiayao and Xuchang in northern China, a group they suggested might also include the Denisovans; under the rules of zoological naming, the older name would take precedence if both describe the same species. Many geneticists prefer to keep “Denisovans” until more fossils with DNA or proteins are known, and that usage is kept here."]},{"heading":"Denisovan DNA in living people","paragraphs":["Papuans, Aboriginal Australians and other Oceanians carry the most Denisovan DNA, several percent of their genomes (estimates range from about 3 to 6 percent). In 2021 Maximilian Larena and Mattias Jakobsson found the highest level of all in the Ayta Magbukon, an Indigenous people of the Bataan peninsula on Luzon in the Philippines, with roughly 30–40 percent more Denisovan ancestry than Papuans. East and South Asians carry much smaller amounts.","These segments came from more than one Denisovan population. Sharon Browning and colleagues (2018) found two distinct pulses in East Asians, one from a group close to the Altai Denisovans. Guy Jacobs, Murray Cox and colleagues (2019) found in Papuans segments from two Denisovan groups that had separated from the Siberian ones hundreds of thousands of years earlier, and suggested that one of them may have interbred with modern humans in or near New Guinea. If so, Denisovans lived east of the Wallace Line and must have crossed the sea.","Some inherited variants proved useful. In 2014 Emilia Huerta-Sánchez, Rasmus Nielsen and colleagues showed that the version of EPAS1 that helps Tibetans live at high altitude without overproducing red blood cells came from Denisovans, a fitting legacy for a people who lived at Baishiya. Other introgressed variants in Asian and Oceanian populations affect immunity.","The Denisovans had deep roots of their own. Their genome carries a small share of DNA from an older, unidentified “superarchaic” population that may have separated from other human lineages up to about 2 million years ago (Alan Rogers and colleagues, 2020), perhaps descendants of an early Homo erectus dispersal into Asia."]}],"debates":[{"question":"What species were the Denisovans?","views":[{"label":"Homo longi","text":"Qiang Ji, Xijun Ni, Chris Stringer and colleagues named Homo longi in 2021 from the Harbin skull. Now that proteins and mitochondrial DNA tie Harbin to the Denisovans, they argue that this is the correct species name, with priority over later proposals."},{"label":"Homo juluensis","text":"Christopher Bae and Xiujie Wu (2024) grouped large-brained, wide-faced fossils from Xujiayao, Xuchang and possibly Baishiya and Penghu into Homo juluensis, and suggested that Denisovans belong there. Critics note that the defining fossils have no molecular data."},{"label":"Keep an informal label","text":"Many geneticists and some palaeoanthropologists argue that with a handful of fossils and uncertain boundaries between Denisovans, Neanderthals and other East Asian groups, a population label is more honest than a formal species."}],"status":"Since 2025 the name Homo longi has gained support, but no consensus exists. A Denisovan skull with nuclear DNA, or proteins from more Chinese fossils, would clarify how many groups there were."},{"question":"Where did Denisovans meet our ancestors?","views":[{"label":"Mainland Asia","text":"The known fossils all come from mainland Asia and the continental shelf, and the Denisovan segments in East Asians point to contact with northern Denisovans. Some geneticists think most mixing took place as modern humans spread through mainland Southeast Asia."},{"label":"Island Southeast Asia and New Guinea","text":"The very high Denisovan ancestry in Papuans and the Ayta Magbukon, and the deep divergence of the Denisovan groups involved, lead Murray Cox, João Teixeira and others to argue for encounters in Wallacea or New Guinea, implying that Denisovans crossed water."}],"status":"Both are probably right: there were several encounters with different Denisovan groups. A Denisovan fossil east of the Wallace Line would settle the second question."},{"question":"Which other Asian fossils are Denisovan?","views":[{"label":"Many of them","text":"Qiaomei Fu’s team and others suggest that big-faced Middle Pleistocene skulls from Dali and Jinniushan, and the Xujiayao remains, may also belong to the Denisovan lineage, given their resemblances to Harbin and to Denisovan teeth."},{"label":"Only with molecules","text":"Others warn that East Asian fossils of this period vary widely, and that only specimens identified by DNA or proteins should be called Denisovan, lest the category swallow every archaic East Asian."}],"status":"Protein analysis is now being applied to Chinese collections, so the list of confirmed Denisovans should grow quickly."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Identified","value":"2010, from the genome of a finger bone from Denisova Cave"},{"label":"Fossils","value":"More than 20 fragments by 2026, from Siberia, Tibet, Laos, the Taiwan Strait and China"},{"label":"First skull","value":"Harbin, at least 146,000 years old, linked by proteins and DNA in 2025"},{"label":"Highest site","value":"Baishiya Karst Cave, 3,280 metres, used from at least 160,000 to about 40,000 years ago"},{"label":"Living legacy","value":"Several percent of Oceanian genomes; highest in the Ayta Magbukon of the Philippines"},{"label":"High-altitude gene","value":"EPAS1 variant in Tibetans, inherited from Denisovans"}],"siteIds":["denisova-cave","baishiya-karst-cave","tam-ngu-hao-2","penghu-channel","harbin","xujiayao","jinniushan"],"routes":[],"themes":["species","genetics","food","animals","climate","migration"],"sources":[{"cite":"Reich, D. et al. (2010). Genetic history of an archaic hominin group from Denisova Cave in Siberia. Nature 468, 1053–1060."},{"cite":"Slon, V. et al. (2018). The genome of the offspring of a Neanderthal mother and a Denisovan father. Nature 561, 113–116."},{"cite":"Chen, F. et al. (2019). A late Middle Pleistocene Denisovan mandible from the Tibetan Plateau. Nature 569, 409–412."},{"cite":"Xia, H. et al. (2024). Middle and Late Pleistocene Denisovan subsistence at Baishiya Karst Cave. Nature."},{"cite":"Tsutaya, T. et al. (2025). A male Denisovan mandible from Pleistocene Taiwan. Science.","doi":"10.1126/science.ads3888"},{"cite":"Fu, Q. et al. (2025). The proteome of the late Middle Pleistocene Harbin individual. Science."},{"cite":"Fu, Q. et al. (2025). Denisovan mitochondrial DNA from dental calculus of the >146,000-year-old Harbin cranium. Cell."},{"cite":"Huerta-Sánchez, E. et al. (2014). Altitude adaptation in Tibetans caused by introgression of Denisovan-like DNA. Nature 512, 194–197."},{"cite":"Jacobs, G. S. et al. (2019). Multiple deeply divergent Denisovan ancestries in Papuans. Cell 177, 1010–1021."},{"cite":"Larena, M. et al. (2021). Philippine Ayta possess the highest level of Denisovan ancestry in the world. Current Biology 31, 4219–4230."}]},{"id":"origin-of-sapiens","order":14,"act":"many-humans","title":"Becoming Homo sapiens","subtitle":"Jebel Irhoud, Omo and the African roots of our species","era":"Middle Stone Age","start":350000,"end":150000,"dateLabel":"about 350,000–150,000 years ago","regions":["africa"],"focus":[-18,-36,52,37],"emblem":"skull","summary":"Fossils from Morocco, South Africa and Ethiopia show that the Homo sapiens lineage existed across Africa by about 300,000 years ago, with a modern face long before a modern braincase. Genetic models of living Africans point to deep, connected populations rather than one small cradle.","paragraphs":[["In 1961, miners digging for barite at a hill called Jebel Irhoud in Morocco broke into a cave packed with sediment and found a human skull.","More than half a century later, flints that had been burnt in ancient fires in the same deposits were dated to about 315,000 years ago."],["The Jebel Irhoud people had faces that would not look out of place today: short, and tucked beneath the forehead.","But their braincases were long and low, unlike our rounded heads.","Our species, it seems, was not born complete.","Its features arrived one by one."],["Other early members of our lineage lived at Florisbad in South Africa about 259,000 years ago, and beside the Omo River in Ethiopia at least 233,000 years ago, a date fixed by a layer of volcanic ash.","At Herto, also in Ethiopia, people handled the skulls of their dead 160,000 years ago.","Across the same continent lived other kinds of humans, among them Homo naledi."],["Geneticists read a similar story in the DNA of living Africans.","Their models disagree on the details: a web of loosely connected populations lasting a million years, or two ancient groups that separated and merged again about 300,000 years ago.","Either way, Homo sapiens did not arise from one small group in one place.","It was assembled across Africa, over a very long time."]],"sentences":["In 1961, miners digging for barite at a hill called Jebel Irhoud in Morocco broke into a cave packed with sediment and found a human skull.","More than half a century later, flints that had been burnt in ancient fires in the same deposits were dated to about 315,000 years ago.","The Jebel Irhoud people had faces that would not look out of place today: short, and tucked beneath the forehead.","But their braincases were long and low, unlike our rounded heads.","Our species, it seems, was not born complete.","Its features arrived one by one.","Other early members of our lineage lived at Florisbad in South Africa about 259,000 years ago, and beside the Omo River in Ethiopia at least 233,000 years ago, a date fixed by a layer of volcanic ash.","At Herto, also in Ethiopia, people handled the skulls of their dead 160,000 years ago.","Across the same continent lived other kinds of humans, among them Homo naledi.","Geneticists read a similar story in the DNA of living Africans.","Their models disagree on the details: a web of loosely connected populations lasting a million years, or two ancient groups that separated and merged again about 300,000 years ago.","Either way, Homo sapiens did not arise from one small group in one place.","It was assembled across Africa, over a very long time."],"pronounce":{"Jebel Irhoud":"Jeh-bel Ear-hood","Florisbad":"Floh-ris-bat","Omo":"Oh-moh","Herto":"Her-toh","naledi":"na-leh-dee"},"body":[{"heading":"Jebel Irhoud: an old face that looks young","paragraphs":["In 1961 miners digging for barite at Jebel Irhoud, west of Marrakesh, broke into a sediment-filled cave and found a human skull. More fossils followed, and for decades the finds were thought to be around 40,000 and later 160,000 years old, perhaps a North African Neanderthal or a late archaic human. Jean-Jacques Hublin’s team reopened the site in 2004 and recovered remains of at least five individuals, including a face (Irhoud 10) and a complete adult lower jaw (Irhoud 11).","In 2017 Daniel Richter and colleagues dated flint tools that had been heated in ancient fires to 315,000 ± 34,000 years by thermoluminescence, and electron spin resonance dating of a tooth gave 286,000 ± 32,000 years. The faces are short and tucked beneath the braincase, like ours, and the teeth resemble ours more than those of Neanderthals; the braincases, however, are long and low. Hublin and colleagues concluded that the Irhoud people belong to the earliest known stage of the Homo sapiens lineage, and that our face took shape before our brain shape did. They made Middle Stone Age tools with Levallois cores, used fire and hunted gazelles."]},{"heading":"Fossils across a continent","paragraphs":["At Florisbad, a spring site in South Africa’s Free State, Thomas Dreyer found a partial skull in 1932. Electron spin resonance dating of a tooth by Rainer Grün and colleagues (1996) gave about 259,000 years. Its broad face mixes modern and archaic traits.","In 1967 Richard Leakey’s team found two skulls in the Kibish Formation beside Ethiopia’s Omo River. Omo I looks broadly modern, Omo II more archaic. Argon dating in 2005 suggested about 195,000 years; in 2022 Céline Vidal, Clive Oppenheimer and colleagues matched the thick ash layer above Omo I to an eruption of the Shala volcano about 233,000 years ago, so Omo I is at least that old.","At Herto in the Middle Awash of Ethiopia, Tim White’s team found three skulls about 160,000 years old, two adults and a child, with cut marks and polish suggesting that the heads were handled after death. White named them Homo sapiens idaltu, a subspecies few now use. Partial skulls from Kenya and Tanzania, such as Guomde, Eliye Springs and Ngaloba (Laetoli Hominid 18), may belong to the same period but are poorly dated.","Our ancestors were not alone. The Kabwe skull from Zambia, usually assigned to Homo heidelbergensis or Homo rhodesiensis, has been dated to about 299,000 years, and Homo naledi lived in South Africa’s Rising Star cave system between about 335,000 and 236,000 years ago. Several human lineages shared Africa as Homo sapiens emerged."]},{"heading":"A face first, a rounder brain later","paragraphs":["Anatomists recognise Homo sapiens by a tall, rounded braincase, a small face tucked beneath it, a chin and reduced brow ridges. These traits did not arrive together. In 2018 Simon Neubauer, Jean-Jacques Hublin and Philipp Gunz compared virtual casts of the inside of fossil braincases from Jebel Irhoud onwards. Brain size was already within the modern range 300,000 years ago, but globularity, the rounded shape associated with expansion of the parietal region and the cerebellum, developed gradually and reached the present-day range only between about 100,000 and 35,000 years ago. What this meant for thought is unknown, but it shows that ‘modern’ anatomy was assembled over hundreds of thousands of years."]},{"heading":"Reading origins in living genomes","paragraphs":["Africa holds more human genetic diversity than the rest of the world combined, because people outside Africa descend from a subset who left later (chapter 18). The deepest splits among living populations separate the ancestors of the Khoe-San peoples of southern Africa from everyone else. In 2017 Carina Schlebusch, Mattias Jakobsson and colleagues sequenced the genome of a boy buried at Ballito Bay about 2,000 years ago, before later migrations reached the region, and estimated that split at 350,000–260,000 years ago.","Modellers disagree about what came before. In 2023 Aaron Ragsdale, Brenna Henn, Simon Gravel and colleagues fitted demographic models to genomes from southern, eastern and western Africa. The best fit was a “weakly structured stem”: two or more populations that stayed connected by migration for more than a million years, so that no single group was ever the one ancestor. In their model, the first lasting split among living populations happens only about 120,000–135,000 years ago, and no mixing with a distinct archaic species is required.","In 2025 Trevor Cousins, Aylwyn Scally and Richard Durbin, applying a new method to present-day genomes, proposed that all living humans descend from two ancestral populations that separated about 1.5 million years ago and merged again about 300,000 years ago, contributing roughly 80 and 20 percent of our ancestry. The larger one went through a bottleneck after the split and appears to be the population from which Neanderthals and Denisovans also descend; genes inherited from the smaller one may have been linked to brain function.","A third line of work finds ghost ancestry. Arun Durvasula and Sriram Sankararaman (2020) estimated that 2–19 percent of the genomes of West African Yoruba and Mende people comes from an unknown archaic population that split off before the ancestors of Neanderthals and modern humans parted. All these models fit the data reasonably well, and ancient DNA from Africa does not yet reach back more than about 20,000 years, too recent to test them directly."]},{"heading":"One cradle or many?","paragraphs":["The recent African origin model, argued from fossils by Chris Stringer and Peter Andrews in 1988 and from mitochondrial DNA by Rebecca Cann, Mark Stoneking and Allan Wilson in 1987, placed our origins in Africa, with East Africa usually named as the likeliest home. Southern Africa has its advocates too, citing the deep Khoe-San split, the rich southern record of early behaviour (chapter 15) and a 2019 study by Eva Chan and Vanessa Hayes that located a homeland in the Makgadikgadi–Okavango wetlands of Botswana; critics replied that the distribution of mitochondrial lineages today cannot pinpoint where they arose.","In 2018 Eleanor Scerri and colleagues argued instead for ‘African multiregionalism’: Homo sapiens emerged from populations scattered across the continent, separated by deserts and forests, reconnected when climate opened green corridors, and exchanging genes and ideas over hundreds of thousands of years. The spread of fossils with some modern traits from Morocco to South Africa, the appearance of Middle Stone Age technology in many regions by about 300,000 years ago, and the genetic models of a structured stem all fit this view.","The lineage itself is older still. Genetic estimates place the separation of our ancestors from those of Neanderthals and Denisovans roughly 550,000–765,000 years ago, and a 2025 study of the Yunxian skull from China, led by Xiaobo Feng and Xijun Ni with Chris Stringer among the authors, argued that the split could be more than a million years old. The Homo sapiens of 300,000 years ago were the newest branch of a long and tangled history."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Where in Africa did Homo sapiens originate?","views":[{"label":"East Africa","text":"The oldest well-dated fossils with fully modern-looking braincases come from Omo Kibish and Herto in Ethiopia, and many genetic studies place the earliest diversification in eastern Africa, the traditional heartland of the recent African origin model."},{"label":"Southern Africa","text":"Advocates point to the deepest genetic split, that of Khoe-San ancestors, the Florisbad skull, the rich record of early symbolic behaviour on the southern coast, and the 2019 mitochondrial study by Eva Chan and Vanessa Hayes, which critics consider methodologically flawed."},{"label":"Across the continent","text":"Eleanor Scerri, Jean-Jacques Hublin and others argue that no single region was the cradle. Jebel Irhoud in the far northwest, Florisbad in the south and Omo in the east show the lineage spread across Africa early, with populations repeatedly separating and reconnecting."}],"status":"The pan-African view has become the most widely held since 2017, but it is hard to test: the fossil record is sparse, especially in West and Central Africa, and ancient DNA does not survive long in the tropics."},{"question":"What does the genetic signal of deep structure mean?","views":[{"label":"A weakly structured stem","text":"Aaron Ragsdale and colleagues (2023) model several long-lived, interconnected African populations exchanging migrants, with no need for admixture from a separate archaic species."},{"label":"Two populations that merged","text":"Trevor Cousins, Aylwyn Scally and Richard Durbin (2025) infer two populations that separated about 1.5 million years ago and rejoined about 300,000 years ago in an 80 to 20 ratio."},{"label":"Ghost archaic admixture","text":"Arun Durvasula and Sriram Sankararaman (2020) and others detect segments in West Africans best explained by interbreeding with an unknown archaic group, comparable to the Neanderthal ancestry of Eurasians."}],"status":"The models are not fully exclusive and all fit current data. Distinguishing them needs more diverse African genomes, better methods and, ideally, ancient proteins or DNA from African fossils."},{"question":"Were the Jebel Irhoud people Homo sapiens?","views":[{"label":"Earliest Homo sapiens","text":"Jean-Jacques Hublin, Philipp Gunz and colleagues argue that the face, jaw and teeth place Jebel Irhoud firmly on the Homo sapiens lineage, and that a species can be defined by features that appear first, with the globular braincase evolving later within it."},{"label":"Close to, but not yet, sapiens","text":"Some palaeoanthropologists, stressing the elongated braincase and large teeth, prefer to call the Irhoud people early members of the sapiens lineage or ‘pre-sapiens’, reserving the species name for fossils with the full modern package, such as Omo I and Herto."}],"status":"The disagreement is partly about definitions. Everyone agrees that Jebel Irhoud belongs close to our ancestry and shows that modern features appeared piecemeal."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Oldest fossils of our lineage","value":"Jebel Irhoud, Morocco, about 315,000 years (thermoluminescence, 2017)"},{"label":"Omo I","value":"At least about 233,000 years, dated by the Shala volcanic ash (2022)"},{"label":"Herto","value":"About 160,000 years, Middle Awash, Ethiopia"},{"label":"Rounded braincase","value":"Reached the modern range only between about 100,000 and 35,000 years ago"},{"label":"Deepest living split","value":"Ancestors of the Khoe-San, estimated at 350,000–260,000 years ago (2017)"},{"label":"2025 genetic model","value":"Two populations, separated about 1.5 million years ago, merged about 300,000 years ago"}],"siteIds":["jebel-irhoud","florisbad","omo-kibish","herto","kabwe","olorgesailie","ballito-bay"],"routes":[],"themes":["species","genetics","migration","climate","technology"],"sources":[{"cite":"Hublin, J.-J. et al. (2017). New fossils from Jebel Irhoud, Morocco and the pan-African origin of Homo sapiens. Nature 546, 289–292."},{"cite":"Richter, D. et al. (2017). The age of the hominin fossils from Jebel Irhoud, Morocco, and the origins of the Middle Stone Age. Nature 546, 293–296."},{"cite":"Vidal, C. M. et al. (2022). Age of the oldest known Homo sapiens from eastern Africa. Nature 601, 579–583."},{"cite":"White, T. D. et al. (2003). Pleistocene Homo sapiens from Middle Awash, Ethiopia. Nature 423, 742–747."},{"cite":"Neubauer, S., Hublin, J.-J. & Gunz, P. (2018). The evolution of modern human brain shape. Science Advances 4, eaao5961."},{"cite":"Scerri, E. M. L. et al. (2018). Did our species evolve in subdivided populations across Africa, and why does it matter? Trends in Ecology & Evolution 33, 582–594."},{"cite":"Schlebusch, C. M. et al. (2017). Southern African ancient genomes estimate modern human divergence to 350,000 to 260,000 years ago. Science 358, 652–655."},{"cite":"Ragsdale, A. P. et al. (2023). A weakly structured stem for human origins in Africa. Nature 617, 755–763."},{"cite":"Cousins, T., Scally, A. & Durbin, R. (2025). A structured coalescent model reveals deep ancestral structure shared by all modern humans. Nature Genetics."},{"cite":"Durvasula, A. & Sankararaman, S. (2020). Recovering signals of ghost archaic introgression in African populations. Science Advances 6, eaax5097."}]},{"id":"msa-symbolism","order":15,"act":"many-humans","title":"Shells, Ochre and Lines","subtitle":"Symbols, ornaments and new technologies in Middle Stone Age Africa","era":"Middle Stone Age","start":200000,"end":50000,"dateLabel":"about 200,000–50,000 years ago","regions":["africa"],"focus":[-18,-36,45,37],"emblem":"engraving","summary":"Long before the painted caves of Europe, people in Africa were collecting shellfish, heat-treating stone, making paint, drilling shell beads, engraving patterns, sleeping on insect-repellent bedding, hunting with arrows and burying a child. These innovations appeared piecemeal, and some came and went.","paragraphs":[["About 100,000 years ago, in a cave above the Indian Ocean near the southern tip of Africa, someone ground red ochre to powder, mixed it with crushed bone, charcoal and fat, and stirred the paste inside two abalone shells.","Archaeologists at Blombos Cave found the shells where they had been left, with the grinding stones beside them."],["Blombos holds more.","A flake of stone bears a crosshatched pattern drawn with an ochre crayon 73,000 years ago.","Tiny snail shells, pierced and worn smooth by string, were once a necklace or a bracelet.","Along the same coast at Pinnacle Point, people were gathering mussels 164,000 years ago and heating stone so that it would flake more cleanly.","Further east, at Sibudu, they slept on beds of sedge topped with leaves that repel mosquitoes, glued stone tips to shafts and hunted with bows and arrows."],["In Morocco, perforated shell beads are at least 142,000 years old.","In Kenya, 78,000 years ago, a small child was laid in a grave with its knees drawn up and its head perhaps resting on a pillow."],["For a long time archaeologists believed that art and ornament appeared suddenly in Europe, about 40,000 years ago.","Africa tells a slower and more uneven story, in which new ideas flared, faded and returned over more than 100,000 years."]],"sentences":["About 100,000 years ago, in a cave above the Indian Ocean near the southern tip of Africa, someone ground red ochre to powder, mixed it with crushed bone, charcoal and fat, and stirred the paste inside two abalone shells.","Archaeologists at Blombos Cave found the shells where they had been left, with the grinding stones beside them.","Blombos holds more.","A flake of stone bears a crosshatched pattern drawn with an ochre crayon 73,000 years ago.","Tiny snail shells, pierced and worn smooth by string, were once a necklace or a bracelet.","Along the same coast at Pinnacle Point, people were gathering mussels 164,000 years ago and heating stone so that it would flake more cleanly.","Further east, at Sibudu, they slept on beds of sedge topped with leaves that repel mosquitoes, glued stone tips to shafts and hunted with bows and arrows.","In Morocco, perforated shell beads are at least 142,000 years old.","In Kenya, 78,000 years ago, a small child was laid in a grave with its knees drawn up and its head perhaps resting on a pillow.","For a long time archaeologists believed that art and ornament appeared suddenly in Europe, about 40,000 years ago.","Africa tells a slower and more uneven story, in which new ideas flared, faded and returned over more than 100,000 years."],"pronounce":{"Blombos":"Blom-boss","Sibudu":"See-boo-doo"},"body":[{"heading":"Looking for the moment we became modern","paragraphs":["For much of the twentieth century, the Upper Palaeolithic caves of Europe defined what archaeologists called “behavioural modernity”: art, ornaments, standardised bone tools and long-distance exchange, all appearing together around 40,000 years ago. Richard Klein proposed a genetic change in the brain about 50,000 years ago as the trigger. In 2000 Sally McBrearty and Alison Brooks published “The revolution that wasn’t”, arguing that in Africa these behaviours had appeared piecemeal across the Middle Stone Age, beginning more than 200,000 years ago. Discoveries since then have largely borne them out, while revealing a record that flickers on and off."]},{"heading":"Pinnacle Point: living from the sea","paragraphs":["Pinnacle Point is a cluster of caves in the cliffs near Mossel Bay on South Africa’s south coast. In 2007 Curtis Marean’s team reported that people in cave PP13B were collecting brown mussels, limpets and other shellfish about 164,000 years ago, during a cold, dry glacial period, and grinding pieces of red ochre. The same levels contain small bladelets. Kyle Brown and colleagues showed in 2009 that the silcrete used for tools had been deliberately heated to make it flake more predictably, a process that requires controlled fire and hours of planning.","At the nearby rock shelter PP5-6, microliths (small blades blunted along one edge) appear about 71,000 years ago and continue for some 11,000 years (Brown and colleagues, 2012). Microliths are usually hafted in rows or as the tips of light projectiles, which implies composite weapons. Marean proposed a coastal adaptation hypothesis: predictable intertidal shellfish, harvested at low spring tides, allowed small populations to survive harsh glacial times on the southern coast and may have encouraged territorial cooperation.","Not every early innovation was coastal. At Ga-Mohana Hill in the Kalahari, hundreds of kilometres from the sea, Jayne Wilkins and colleagues (2021) found white calcite crystals and fragments of ostrich eggshell, apparently collected on purpose, about 105,000 years ago, when the region was wetter than today. Inland sites such as Border Cave and Sibudu, discussed below, add bedding, cooking, glues and arrows to the picture."]},{"heading":"Blombos Cave: paint, lines and beads","paragraphs":["Blombos Cave, in a limestone cliff overlooking the Indian Ocean some 300 kilometres east of Cape Town, has been excavated by Christopher Henshilwood since 1991. In levels about 100,000 years old his team found two abalone shells used as containers for a red paste of ground ochre, crushed bone, charcoal and fat, with grinding stones beside them: a paint-making kit (2011).","Pieces of ochre from Blombos carry engraved crosshatched and parallel lines, the most elaborate about 75,000–77,000 years old. In 2018 the team reported a silcrete flake about 73,000 years old with a crosshatched pattern of nine red lines drawn with an ochre crayon, described as the oldest known drawing. Dozens of tiny Nassarius kraussianus snail shells, perforated and worn from being strung, some stained with ochre, date to about 75,000 years ago. The same Still Bay levels contain finely made bifacial spear points, some finished by pressure flaking, as well as bone awls and points.","At Diepkloof Rock Shelter on the west coast, Pierre-Jean Texier and colleagues (2010) reported 270 fragments of engraved ostrich eggshell, many from water containers, about 60,000 years old. The motifs, hatched bands and parallel lines, follow a limited set of rules that changed over time, which the authors read as a shared graphic tradition passed down the generations."]},{"heading":"Beds, glue and poisoned arrows","paragraphs":["At Border Cave, on the border between South Africa and Eswatini, Lyn Wadley’s team found that by about 200,000 years ago people laid grass bedding, sometimes over layers of ash, which deter crawling insects (2020). The same cave preserved charred rhizomes of Hypoxis, a starchy plant, from about 170,000 years ago, evidence of cooking.","At Sibudu Cave in KwaZulu-Natal, bedding from about 77,000 years ago was built from sedges and rushes topped with leaves of the river wild-quince (Cryptocarya woodii), which contain chemicals that kill mosquitoes, and old bedding was periodically burnt (Wadley and colleagues, 2011). Sibudu’s toolmakers mixed plant gum with ochre to make compound glues for hafting stone tips, about 70,000–60,000 years ago, a recipe that requires adjusting ingredients and drying over a fire.","Sibudu also holds some of the earliest evidence for bows and arrows: quartz segments with impact fractures and residues from about 64,000 years ago (Marlize Lombard and Laurel Phillipson, 2010), and a bone point about 61,000 years old resembling arrowheads used by San hunters in recent times. A study published in 2026 reported traces of toxic alkaloids from Boophone disticha, a bulb used for arrow poison in southern Africa, on backed quartz tools about 60,000 years old from Umhlatuzana Rock Shelter, which would make them the oldest known poisoned arrows."]},{"heading":"Beads in North Africa","paragraphs":["The oldest known shell beads come from Morocco. At Bizmoune Cave near Essaouira, El Mehdi Sehasseh, Steven Kuhn, Abdeljalil Bouzouggar and colleagues found 33 perforated shells of Tritia gibbosula, a small sea snail, in layers at least 142,000 years old (2021). At Grotte des Pigeons near Taforalt, similar beads, some coated with ochre, date to about 82,000 years ago. People carried such shells far inland, and they appear at several other Moroccan and Algerian sites, suggesting a long-lived tradition of personal ornament used, perhaps, to signal identity or alliances."]},{"heading":"A child laid to rest","paragraphs":["In 2021 María Martinón-Torres, Nicole Boivin, Michael Petraglia and colleagues described the burial of a child of about two and a half to three years at Panga ya Saidi, a cave in the hills behind the Kenyan coast, dated by luminescence to about 78,000 years ago. The child, nicknamed Mtoto (“child” in Swahili), lay on its right side in a pit with the legs drawn up. The tightly flexed position suggests the body was wrapped, and the head may have rested on a perishable support. It is the oldest known deliberate burial in Africa. An infant from Border Cave, buried with a perforated cone shell, may be of similar age."]},{"heading":"Innovations that came and went","paragraphs":["Middle Stone Age innovations did not accumulate in a straight line. Barbed bone points from Katanda on the Semliki River in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, dated to about 90,000 years ago by several methods, look like harpoons of much later periods; some researchers doubt the dates because nothing similar appears again for tens of thousands of years.","In southern Africa, the Still Bay, with its bifacial points, and the Howiesons Poort, with its backed microliths and engraved eggshell, were dated by Zenobia Jacobs and colleagues (2008) to short windows around 72,000–71,000 and 65,000–60,000 years ago. Later work at Diepkloof and other sites suggests longer and more varied spans, but both industries still appear, flourish and vanish. Explanations include population size (larger, better-connected groups keep skills alive), climate change and shifting social networks."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Was there a sudden “human revolution”?","views":[{"label":"A late revolution","text":"Richard Klein argued that a neural change about 50,000 years ago unlocked fully modern behaviour, pointing to the burst of art, ornaments and new tools that followed the dispersal from Africa. Paul Mellars and others saw a real threshold around 60,000–40,000 years ago, whatever its cause."},{"label":"Gradual accumulation","text":"Sally McBrearty and Alison Brooks (2000), followed by Christopher Henshilwood, Francesco d’Errico and many others, argue that the components of modern behaviour appeared piecemeal in Africa over more than 150,000 years, and that the European Upper Palaeolithic reflects demography and preservation rather than a new kind of mind."}],"status":"The gradualist view now dominates. Few researchers still seek a single genetic switch, but many agree that the density of symbolic evidence rose sharply after about 50,000 years ago, and debate why."},{"question":"Why did the Still Bay and Howiesons Poort appear and disappear?","views":[{"label":"Population size","text":"Adam Powell, Stephen Shennan and Mark Thomas (2009) modelled cultural transmission and argued that complex skills are maintained only when populations are dense and well connected; when groups shrink or fragment, techniques are lost."},{"label":"Environment","text":"Others link the industries to shifts in climate and resources recorded in marine and cave records, arguing that people changed toolkits to suit new hunting conditions."},{"label":"Social networks and choice","text":"Some researchers stress that the industries begin and end at different times at different sites, suggesting regional social networks and cultural choices rather than a single driver."}],"status":"The better dated the sites become, the less synchronous these industries look, which favours local and social explanations combined with demography. More precise dating at many sites is the key."},{"question":"Did coastal living make people modern?","views":[{"label":"Coastal adaptation","text":"Curtis Marean argues that dense, predictable shellfish on the southern coast supported larger, more territorial groups, which favoured cooperation, planning and symbolic signalling, as seen at Pinnacle Point and Blombos."},{"label":"Inland evidence too","text":"Lyn Wadley and others point to bedding and cooking at Border Cave, glues and arrows at Sibudu, and crystals and ostrich eggshell collected about 105,000 years ago at Ga-Mohana Hill in the Kalahari, showing innovations far from the sea."}],"status":"The coast was clearly one important setting, but not the only one. Most researchers now see innovation spread across many environments, with the coast especially well preserved and well studied."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Oldest shell beads","value":"Bizmoune Cave, Morocco, at least 142,000 years"},{"label":"Bedding","value":"Border Cave, about 200,000 years"},{"label":"Paint workshop","value":"Blombos Cave, about 100,000 years"},{"label":"Oldest drawing","value":"Blombos Cave, about 73,000 years"},{"label":"Oldest burial in Africa","value":"Mtoto, Panga ya Saidi, Kenya, about 78,000 years"},{"label":"Early arrows","value":"Sibudu, about 64,000 years; poisoned tips reported from Umhlatuzana, about 60,000 years"}],"siteIds":["pinnacle-point","blombos-cave","diepkloof","sibudu-cave","umhlatuzana","border-cave","bizmoune-cave","grotte-des-pigeons","panga-ya-saidi"],"routes":[],"themes":["art","ornament","technology","food","burial","fire","society","climate"],"sources":[{"cite":"McBrearty, S. & Brooks, A. S. (2000). The revolution that wasn’t: a new interpretation of the origin of modern human behavior. Journal of Human Evolution 39, 453–563."},{"cite":"Marean, C. W. et al. (2007). Early human use of marine resources and pigment in South Africa during the Middle Pleistocene. Nature 449, 905–908."},{"cite":"Brown, K. S. et al. (2012). An early and enduring advanced technology originating 71,000 years ago in South Africa. Nature 491, 590–593."},{"cite":"Henshilwood, C. S. et al. (2011). A 100,000-year-old ochre-processing workshop at Blombos Cave, South Africa. Science 334, 219–222."},{"cite":"Henshilwood, C. S. et al. (2018). An abstract drawing from the 73,000-year-old levels at Blombos Cave, South Africa. Nature 562, 115–118."},{"cite":"Texier, P.-J. et al. (2010). A Howiesons Poort tradition of engraving ostrich eggshell containers dated to 60,000 years ago at Diepkloof Rock Shelter, South Africa. PNAS 107, 6180–6185."},{"cite":"Wadley, L. et al. (2011). Middle Stone Age bedding construction and settlement patterns at Sibudu, South Africa. Science 334, 1388–1391."},{"cite":"Wadley, L. et al. (2020). Fire and grass-bedding construction 200 thousand years ago at Border Cave, South Africa. Science 369, 863–866."},{"cite":"Sehasseh, E. M. et al. (2021). Early Middle Stone Age personal ornaments from Bizmoune Cave, Essaouira, Morocco. Science Advances 7."},{"cite":"Martinón-Torres, M. et al. (2021). Earliest known human burial in Africa. Nature 593, 95–100."}]},{"id":"early-dispersals","order":16,"act":"many-humans","title":"Early Journeys out of Africa","subtitle":"Homo sapiens in Greece, the Levant, Arabia and Asia before the great dispersal","era":"Middle Palaeolithic and Middle Stone Age","start":210000,"end":50000,"dateLabel":"about 210,000–50,000 years ago","regions":["europe","asia"],"focus":[0,-5,110,47],"emblem":"footprints","summary":"Fossils, footprints and genes show that Homo sapiens left Africa repeatedly long before the main dispersal of about 60,000–50,000 years ago, reaching the Levant, Arabia, Southeast Asia and perhaps Greece and France. Most of these groups left few or no descendants among living people, but they mixed with Neanderthals.","paragraphs":[["About 120,000 years ago, on the muddy shore of a lake in what is now the Nefud Desert of Saudi Arabia, a few people walked among elephants, camels and wild horses.","Their footprints hardened, were buried, and came to light again in 2017, when erosion stripped away the sediments above them."],["These travellers were Homo sapiens, and they were not the first of their kind to leave Africa.","A jaw from Misliya Cave in Israel is between 194,000 and 177,000 years old.","A broken skull from Apidima in Greece may be older still, though not everyone agrees.","At Skhul and Qafzeh, near the eastern Mediterranean coast, people buried their dead with deer antlers, red ochre and seashells more than 90,000 years ago."],["Some went much further.","Teeth from a cave in Sumatra are about 70,000 years old, and a skull from Tam Pà Ling in Laos is of similar age.","In a rock shelter above the Rhône in France, a single child’s tooth hints that modern humans visited Europe 54,000 years ago, some ten thousand years before they settled there for good."],["Yet most people living outside Africa today descend from a later wave.","The earlier pioneers seem to have faded away when climate closed the green corridors they had followed, or to have been absorbed by others.","Their clearest trace is in the genomes of the Neanderthals they met."]],"sentences":["About 120,000 years ago, on the muddy shore of a lake in what is now the Nefud Desert of Saudi Arabia, a few people walked among elephants, camels and wild horses.","Their footprints hardened, were buried, and came to light again in 2017, when erosion stripped away the sediments above them.","These travellers were Homo sapiens, and they were not the first of their kind to leave Africa.","A jaw from Misliya Cave in Israel is between 194,000 and 177,000 years old.","A broken skull from Apidima in Greece may be older still, though not everyone agrees.","At Skhul and Qafzeh, near the eastern Mediterranean coast, people buried their dead with deer antlers, red ochre and seashells more than 90,000 years ago.","Some went much further.","Teeth from a cave in Sumatra are about 70,000 years old, and a skull from Tam Pà Ling in Laos is of similar age.","In a rock shelter above the Rhône in France, a single child’s tooth hints that modern humans visited Europe 54,000 years ago, some ten thousand years before they settled there for good.","Yet most people living outside Africa today descend from a later wave.","The earlier pioneers seem to have faded away when climate closed the green corridors they had followed, or to have been absorbed by others.","Their clearest trace is in the genomes of the Neanderthals they met."],"pronounce":{"Nefud":"Neh-food","Misliya":"Mis-lee-yah","Apidima":"Ah-pee-dee-mah","Skhul":"Skool","Qafzeh":"Kaf-zeh","Tam Pà Ling":"Tam Pah Ling"},"body":[{"heading":"Leaving early","paragraphs":["Most people outside Africa today descend from a dispersal about 60,000–50,000 years ago (chapter 18). Yet fossils, footprints and stone tools show Homo sapiens outside Africa much earlier. These early movements seem to have followed humid phases, when rains turned the Sahara, Sinai and Arabia into grassland with lakes and rivers. How many of the pioneers survived, and what they left behind, is one of the liveliest questions in the field."]},{"heading":"Apidima and Misliya","paragraphs":["Two broken skulls embedded in a block of cemented cave sediment were recovered from Apidima Cave on the Mani peninsula of southern Greece in 1978. In 2019 Katerina Harvati and colleagues reconstructed them from CT scans. Apidima 2, a face, is Neanderthal-like and was dated by uranium-series to at least 170,000 years ago. Apidima 1, the back of a braincase, is rounded and lacks the Neanderthal bun; the team identified it as an early Homo sapiens at least 210,000 years old, the oldest outside Africa. Critics note that the fragment is small and distorted, and that one region of the skull is a thin basis for naming a species.","The earliest widely accepted Homo sapiens outside Africa is a left upper jaw with teeth from Misliya Cave on Mount Carmel, Israel, published in 2018 by Israel Hershkovitz and colleagues. Three dating methods place it between about 194,000 and 177,000 years ago, with Levallois-based tools of the Early Middle Palaeolithic."]},{"heading":"Skhul and Qafzeh: burials in the Levant","paragraphs":["In the early 1930s Dorothy Garrod’s excavations on Mount Carmel uncovered skeletons at es-Skhul, and René Neuville found more at Qafzeh near Nazareth, where Bernard Vandermeersch resumed work in 1965. The Skhul and Qafzeh people, dated mostly to about 120,000–90,000 years ago by thermoluminescence and electron spin resonance, look largely modern with some archaic traits. They buried their dead: at Qafzeh an adolescent was laid with deer antlers on the chest, and a young woman was buried with a small child. Qafzeh also has lumps of red ochre and seashells carried from the coast some 35 kilometres away, and perforated Nassarius shells from Skhul are among the oldest possible beads anywhere.","In 2025 a team including Anne Dambricourt Malassé and Israel Hershkovitz re-examined the skull of Skhul I, a child of about three to five years, and argued that it combines a modern braincase with a Neanderthal-like lower jaw, making it possibly the oldest known individual of mixed ancestry. The claim rests on anatomy alone, since no DNA survives.","The Levant was a shared zone. Neanderthals are known from Tabun, Kebara and Amud, the latter two mainly between about 70,000 and 50,000 years ago, and in 2021 Hershkovitz’s team described a different archaic population at Nesher Ramla, dated to about 140,000–120,000 years ago. All of these groups made Levallois tools, so stone tools alone rarely reveal who occupied a cave."]},{"heading":"Green Arabia","paragraphs":["Today the interior of the Arabian Peninsula is desert, but during humid phases monsoon rains reached far north, filling lakes and rivers. Huw Groucutt, Michael Petraglia and colleagues have mapped thousands of these palaeolakes. At Khall Amayshan 4 in the Nefud Desert they found occupation phases at about 400,000, 300,000, 200,000, 130,000–75,000 and 55,000 years ago, each coinciding with a humid window and each with a different toolkit (2021).","At Alathar, another ancient lake bed in the Nefud, Mathew Stewart and colleagues (2020) identified seven human footprints among hundreds left by elephants, camels, horses and other animals, dated by luminescence to about 120,000 years ago. The prints were probably made by Homo sapiens, since Neanderthals are not known in the region at that time. At nearby Al Wusta, a human finger bone dated directly by uranium-series to about 88,000 years is the oldest directly dated Homo sapiens fossil outside Africa and the Levant (Groucutt and colleagues, 2018).","At Jebel Faya in the United Arab Emirates, Simon Armitage and colleagues (2011) dated an assemblage of handaxes, scrapers and other tools to about 125,000 years ago and argued that it resembled East African Middle Stone Age toolkits, suggesting a crossing of the southern Red Sea when sea levels were low. Others see these tools as a local Arabian development or link them to the Levant."]},{"heading":"Southern and eastern Asia","paragraphs":["In the Annamite mountains of northern Laos, Tam Pà Ling cave has yielded a skull, jaws, a frontal bone and other remains since 2009. Luminescence and uranium-series dating of the sediments and bones places them between about 86,000 and 68,000 years ago (Sarah Freidline, Kira Westaway, Fabrice Demeter and colleagues, 2023). At Lida Ajer in Sumatra, teeth collected by Eugène Dubois in the late 1880s were dated in 2017 to about 73,000–63,000 years ago, showing modern humans living in tropical rainforest.","Claims from southern China go further back. In 2015 Wu Liu, María Martinón-Torres and colleagues reported 47 modern human teeth from Fuyan Cave in Hunan, dated by overlying flowstone to 120,000–80,000 years ago, and a partial jaw from Zhiren Cave in Guangxi had been dated to about 100,000 years. In 2021, however, a study led by Xue-feng Sun combined new dating of sediments and teeth with ancient DNA at Fuyan and several other southern Chinese caves, and concluded that the human remains there are far younger than claimed, supporting a late arrival of Homo sapiens in the region. The species of the Zhiren jaw is itself debated."]},{"heading":"Grotte Mandrin","paragraphs":["At Grotte Mandrin in the Rhône valley of France, Ludovic Slimak’s team excavated a layer, called E, sandwiched between Neanderthal occupations. It contains a distinctive industry of tiny points and blades, which they call the Neronian and compare with the Initial Upper Palaeolithic of the Levant. In 2022 they reported that a child’s milk tooth from this layer belongs to Homo sapiens, and dated the layer to about 56,800–51,700 years ago using luminescence and a count of soot films on the cave wall. If correct, modern humans briefly reached western Europe about 54,000 years ago, some 10,000 years before they are otherwise known there, and then disappeared. Critics question whether a single milk tooth can be securely assigned to a species, and whether the Neronian requires a migration to explain it."]},{"heading":"Traces in Neanderthal genomes","paragraphs":["Some early dispersals left their mark not in us but in Neanderthals. In 2016 Martin Kuhlwilm and colleagues found DNA from early modern humans in the Altai Neanderthal genome, from contact more than 100,000 years ago. Neanderthal mitochondrial DNA and Y chromosomes were replaced by versions from an African population related to Homo sapiens (Cosimo Posth and colleagues, 2017; Martin Petr and colleagues, 2020), and a 2024 analysis by Liming Li and Joshua Akey detected gene flow from modern humans into Neanderthals around 250,000–200,000 and again around 120,000–100,000 years ago, times that match the fossils from Misliya, Apidima, Skhul and Qafzeh.","Living non-Africans, however, show little or no sign of these early groups. Luca Pagani and colleagues (2016) reported that about 2 percent of Papuan genomes might come from an earlier dispersal, but other studies found no such trace. That is why many researchers speak of “failed dispersals”: groups that spread when climate opened green corridors and then shrank, retreated or were absorbed when it closed. Others dislike the term, since these people lived outside Africa for thousands of years and their genes survive in Neanderthal genomes."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Was Apidima 1 an early Homo sapiens?","views":[{"label":"Yes, at least 210,000 years ago","text":"Katerina Harvati and colleagues (2019) argue that the rounded back of the Apidima 1 braincase, without an occipital bun, matches Homo sapiens and not Neanderthals, and that uranium-series dating of the bone shows it is older than the Neanderthal-like Apidima 2 in the same block."},{"label":"Not proven","text":"Critics argue that the fragment preserves too little anatomy, is distorted by the surrounding sediment, and that the dating of fossils embedded in cemented breccia is uncertain; some think both skulls could belong to one variable archaic population."}],"status":"The claim is taken seriously and fits genetic evidence for early gene flow into Neanderthals, but it is not universally accepted. A better-preserved fossil of similar age in southeastern Europe would be decisive."},{"question":"Were modern humans in southern China before 80,000 years ago?","views":[{"label":"An early presence","text":"Wu Liu, María Martinón-Torres and colleagues argued from the Fuyan teeth, sealed below flowstone dated to more than 80,000 years ago, and from the Zhiren and Luna cave finds, that Homo sapiens lived in southern China by 120,000–80,000 years ago."},{"label":"A late arrival","text":"Xue-feng Sun and colleagues (2021) re-dated the sediments and remains at several of these caves and concluded that the human teeth are much younger than the flowstones, having moved down through the deposits, so the evidence for an early presence evaporates."}],"status":"Opinion has shifted towards a late arrival in China, around or after 50,000 years ago, though the original teams dispute the redating. Direct dating of the Fuyan teeth themselves remains the crucial test."},{"question":"Was Grotte Mandrin layer E made by Homo sapiens?","views":[{"label":"A modern human incursion","text":"Ludovic Slimak and colleagues (2022) argue that the milk tooth is modern human, that the Neronian tools resemble the Levantine Initial Upper Palaeolithic, and that the layer, dated to about 54,000 years ago, sits between Neanderthal layers, recording a short-lived visit."},{"label":"Unproven","text":"Sceptics note that identifications from isolated milk teeth are difficult, that the tool comparisons can be read differently, and that the dating combines methods with large uncertainties."}],"status":"The finding is widely discussed and plausible given the 2024 evidence for modern humans at Ranis in Germany by about 47,000–45,000 years ago, but ancient DNA from layer E would settle it."},{"question":"Did the early dispersals fail?","views":[{"label":"Dead ends","text":"Genomes of living non-Africans trace almost entirely to one dispersal about 60,000–50,000 years ago; many geneticists therefore see earlier groups as having died out or retreated to Africa without descendants."},{"label":"Partial legacies","text":"Michael Petraglia, Huw Groucutt and others argue for multiple dispersals, some of which may have lasted in South Asia or Arabia, and point to the traces in Neanderthal genomes and the possible early signal in Papuans."}],"status":"Both views agree that pre-60,000 dispersals contributed little or nothing to most living people; they disagree about how long the early groups persisted and where."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Oldest claimed outside Africa","value":"Apidima 1, Greece, at least 210,000 years (contested)"},{"label":"Oldest widely accepted","value":"Misliya maxilla, Israel, 194,000–177,000 years"},{"label":"Arabian footprints","value":"Alathar, Nefud Desert, about 120,000 years"},{"label":"Oldest directly dated fossil in Arabia","value":"Al Wusta finger bone, about 88,000 years"},{"label":"Southeast Asia","value":"Tam Pà Ling, Laos, 86,000–68,000 years"},{"label":"Western Europe","value":"Grotte Mandrin layer E, about 54,000 years (debated)"}],"siteIds":["apidima-cave","misliya-cave","es-skhul","qafzeh-cave","jebel-faya","tam-pa-ling","lida-ajer","fuyan-cave","grotte-mandrin"],"routes":[{"id":"sinai-early","label":"Early crossings into the Levant","start":200000,"end":90000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[31.25,30.05],[32.3,30.6],[33.8,31.1],[34.6,31.8],[34.97,32.7]]},{"id":"bab-el-mandeb-early","label":"Southern Red Sea crossing into Arabia","start":130000,"end":75000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[42.6,11.6],[43.35,12.6],[44.5,14.5],[48,16.5],[53,18.5],[56.5,21.5],[55.85,25.1]]},{"id":"levant-nefud-early","label":"Into green Arabia from the Levant","start":130000,"end":75000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[35.5,32],[36.8,30.5],[38,29.3],[39,28.2]]},{"id":"arabia-asia-early","label":"Early spread into South and Southeast Asia","start":100000,"end":65000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[56.3,25.3],[57.5,26.8],[61.5,25.3],[66.5,25],[72.5,22.5],[78.5,21],[87.5,23],[95,21.5],[103.4,20.2]]},{"id":"sunda-early","label":"South to Sumatra","start":80000,"end":65000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[95,21.5],[98.5,15],[100.5,7],[101,2.5],[100.65,-0.3]]},{"id":"levant-to-rhone","label":"Possible route to Grotte Mandrin","start":56000,"end":52000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[35.6,33.9],[36,36.5],[32,37.5],[27,39.5],[23.5,41.5],[19.5,44],[14.5,45.8],[9.5,45.2],[6.5,44.8],[4.75,44.49]]}],"themes":["species","migration","burial","ornament","genetics","climate","technology"],"sources":[{"cite":"Harvati, K. et al. (2019). Apidima Cave fossils provide earliest evidence of Homo sapiens in Eurasia. Nature 571, 500–504."},{"cite":"Hershkovitz, I. et al. (2018). The earliest modern humans outside Africa. Science 359, 456–459."},{"cite":"Groucutt, H. S. et al. (2018). Homo sapiens in Arabia by 85,000 years ago. Nature Ecology & Evolution 2, 800–809."},{"cite":"Stewart, M. et al. (2020). Human footprints provide snapshot of last interglacial ecology in the Arabian interior. Science Advances 6."},{"cite":"Groucutt, H. S. et al. (2021). Multiple hominin dispersals into Southwest Asia over the past 400,000 years. Nature 597, 376–380."},{"cite":"Armitage, S. J. et al. (2011). The southern route “out of Africa”: evidence for an early expansion of modern humans into Arabia. Science 331, 453–456."},{"cite":"Freidline, S. E. et al. (2023). Early presence of Homo sapiens in Southeast Asia by 86–68 kyr at Tam Pà Ling, Northern Laos. Nature Communications 14."},{"cite":"Westaway, K. E. et al. (2017). An early modern human presence in Sumatra 73,000–63,000 years ago. Nature 548, 322–325."},{"cite":"Sun, X.-F. et al. (2021). Ancient DNA and multimethod dating confirm the late arrival of anatomically modern humans in southern China. PNAS 118."},{"cite":"Slimak, L. et al. (2022). Modern human incursion into Neanderthal territories 54,000 years ago at Mandrin, France. Science Advances 8."}]},{"id":"catastrophes","order":17,"act":"many-humans","title":"Bottlenecks and Catastrophes","subtitle":"A disputed population crash, a super-eruption, megadroughts and a failing magnetic field","era":"Middle and Late Pleistocene","start":930000,"end":40000,"dateLabel":"about 930,000–40,000 years ago","regions":["africa","asia","europe","oceania"],"focus":[-20,-42,178,55],"emblem":"volcano","summary":"Genetic claims of a near-extinction 900,000 years ago, the Toba super-eruption, African megadroughts and the Laschamp magnetic excursion have all been proposed as turning points in human evolution. Tested against archaeology and genomes, most turn out to be stories of regional hardship and resilience rather than global catastrophe.","paragraphs":[["About 74,000 years ago, a volcano in northern Sumatra tore itself apart.","The eruption of Toba left a crater now filled by a lake about 100 kilometres long, and its ash fell across India.","Microscopic shards of its glass have been found on the coast of South Africa, almost 9,000 kilometres away."],["For years, many scientists believed that this eruption brought a volcanic winter that nearly wiped out our ancestors.","So archaeologists went looking.","In India, people were making the same kinds of tools just above the ash as just below it.","At Pinnacle Point in South Africa, life went on and even flourished.","In the lowlands of Ethiopia, people coped with longer dry seasons by catching fish in shrinking pools."],["Other crises are harder to read.","A genetic study published in 2023 argued that between 930,000 and 813,000 years ago our ancestors dwindled to about 1,300 breeding adults, but other geneticists find no trace of such a crash.","Between about 135,000 and 75,000 years ago, great droughts shrank Lake Malawi to a fraction of its size.","And about 41,000 years ago the Earth’s magnetic field weakened, letting more cosmic radiation into the atmosphere."],["Each of these events has been cast as a turning point in human history.","The evidence so far tells a quieter story, of people who moved, changed their diets and adjusted, again and again."]],"sentences":["About 74,000 years ago, a volcano in northern Sumatra tore itself apart.","The eruption of Toba left a crater now filled by a lake about 100 kilometres long, and its ash fell across India.","Microscopic shards of its glass have been found on the coast of South Africa, almost 9,000 kilometres away.","For years, many scientists believed that this eruption brought a volcanic winter that nearly wiped out our ancestors.","So archaeologists went looking.","In India, people were making the same kinds of tools just above the ash as just below it.","At Pinnacle Point in South Africa, life went on and even flourished.","In the lowlands of Ethiopia, people coped with longer dry seasons by catching fish in shrinking pools.","Other crises are harder to read.","A genetic study published in 2023 argued that between 930,000 and 813,000 years ago our ancestors dwindled to about 1,300 breeding adults, but other geneticists find no trace of such a crash.","Between about 135,000 and 75,000 years ago, great droughts shrank Lake Malawi to a fraction of its size.","And about 41,000 years ago the Earth’s magnetic field weakened, letting more cosmic radiation into the atmosphere.","Each of these events has been cast as a turning point in human history.","The evidence so far tells a quieter story, of people who moved, changed their diets and adjusted, again and again."],"pronounce":{"Toba":"Toh-bah","Malawi":"Ma-lah-wee"},"body":[{"heading":"A world of swinging climates","paragraphs":["Between about 1.25 million and 700,000 years ago, the rhythm of the ice ages changed. During what climatologists call the Mid-Pleistocene Transition, glacial cycles lengthened from about 41,000 years to about 100,000 years, and the cold phases grew deeper, with larger ice sheets and lower sea levels. In Africa the swings showed up less as ice than as alternating wet and dry phases, driven partly by the precession of Earth’s orbit, which controls the strength of the monsoons.","In the 1990s Rick Potts of the Smithsonian proposed that such instability, rather than any single environment, favoured hominins able to cope with change: the variability selection hypothesis. A drill core from the Olorgesailie basin in Kenya, published in 2020, showed that from about 400,000 years ago the landscape swung more often between wet and dry conditions, just as handaxes gave way to Middle Stone Age toolkits and long-distance obsidian exchange. Climate simulations by Axel Timmermann’s group (2022) likewise linked the habitats of different hominin species to shifting climate, and a 2023 study led by Vasiliki Margari suggested that an extreme cold spell about 1.12 million years ago temporarily emptied Europe of people."]},{"heading":"A bottleneck 900,000 years ago?","paragraphs":["In 2023 Wangjie Hu, Haipeng Li, Yi-Hsuan Pan and colleagues published in Science a method called FitCoal, which infers past population size from the frequencies of genetic variants in living people. Applied to 3,154 modern genomes, it indicated that between about 930,000 and 813,000 years ago the ancestors of modern humans fell to about 1,280 breeding individuals and stayed near that level for about 117,000 years, a loss of some 98.7 percent. The authors linked the crash to the Mid-Pleistocene Transition and to a gap in the African and Eurasian fossil record, and suggested that it may have helped set apart the lineage leading to Homo sapiens, Neanderthals and Denisovans.","Critics responded quickly. Other widely used methods applied to the same genomes show no such bottleneck. Critiques published in 2024 argued that FitCoal can produce a spurious bottleneck from data simulated without one, and that ancient population structure, with separate groups exchanging migrants, can mimic changes in size (see the models in chapter 14). Palaeoanthropologists noted that the supposed fossil gap contains sites such as Gran Dolina in Spain. The authors defend their result, but most population geneticists regard it as unproven."]},{"heading":"Toba","paragraphs":["About 74,000 years ago the Toba volcano in northern Sumatra erupted, leaving a caldera now filled by Lake Toba, about 100 kilometres long. The eruption, one of the largest of the past two million years, ejected thousands of cubic kilometres of rock and ash. The Youngest Toba Tuff blankets much of northern Sumatra, and ash fell across the Bay of Bengal and the Indian subcontinent.","In 1998 Stanley Ambrose combined this event with genetic evidence for a small ancestral population and proposed that a volcanic winter lasting several years, followed by a millennium of intensified glacial cold, reduced our ancestors to a few thousand breeding adults and accelerated the genetic differentiation of human populations. The idea became popular as the “Toba catastrophe”."]},{"heading":"Life went on","paragraphs":["Archaeologists have since tested the idea directly. At Jwalapuram in southern India, Michael Petraglia’s team (2007) found Middle Palaeolithic stone tools immediately below and above the Toba ash, with similar technology before and after. At Dhaba in the Middle Son valley of northern India, Chris Clarkson and colleagues (2020) reported occupation from about 80,000 to 65,000 years ago that continued across the eruption. Whether these toolmakers were Homo sapiens or another hominin is still debated (chapter 16).","In South Africa, almost 9,000 kilometres from Sumatra, a team led by Eugene Smith and Curtis Marean (2018) identified microscopic glass shards of Toba ash, known as cryptotephra, at the Pinnacle Point 5-6 rock shelter and at an open-air site at Vleesbaai. At Pinnacle Point, occupation continued without a break and even intensified after the eruption. Lake Malawi sediment cores studied by Christine Lane, Ben Chorn and Thomas Johnson (2013) contain Toba ash with no sign of a sharp cooling in East Africa.","In 2024 John Kappelman and colleagues reported Toba cryptotephra at Shinfa-Metema 1 in the lowlands of northwestern Ethiopia. People there hunted and fished along a seasonal river, and after the eruption, as dry seasons apparently lengthened, they relied more on fish trapped in shrinking pools. The authors suggest that such seasonal rivers could have served as “blue highways” for dispersal, and interpret small triangular points as possible arrowheads. Genomes show no human population crash dated to 74,000 years ago. Toba is now widely seen as a regional disaster, not a near-extinction."]},{"heading":"Megadroughts","paragraphs":["Drilling in Lake Malawi in 2005 revealed that between about 135,000 and 75,000 years ago tropical Africa went through several megadroughts, far more severe than those of the Last Glacial Maximum. Christopher Scholz, Andrew Cohen and colleagues (2007) estimated that Lake Malawi, today almost 600 kilometres long and more than 700 metres deep, shrank at times to a small, saline lake, losing at least 95 percent of its water. Cores from Lake Tanganyika and from Lake Bosumtwi in Ghana record dry phases too.","After about 70,000 years ago the climate became wetter and more stable. Scholz and Cohen suggested that the end of the droughts allowed human populations to grow, which may have set the stage for the dispersal out of Africa after 60,000 years ago. Others emphasise that dry phases in some regions coincided with wet phases elsewhere, such as the humid windows that opened corridors through the Sahara and Arabia, so that local crisis and opportunity existed side by side."]},{"heading":"When the magnetic shield weakened","paragraphs":["About 41,000 years ago, Earth’s magnetic field weakened drastically and its poles briefly swung, an event first recognised in lava flows at Laschamps in France’s Massif Central. For several centuries the field fell to a small fraction, perhaps a tenth or less, of its present strength, letting more cosmic rays into the atmosphere and raising the production of radiocarbon and beryllium-10.","In 2021 Alan Cooper, Chris Turney and colleagues used radiocarbon in an ancient kauri tree preserved in a swamp at Ngāwhā in New Zealand to date these changes precisely. They proposed that the transition around 42,000 years ago, which they called the “Adams Event” after the author Douglas Adams, caused ozone loss, more ultraviolet radiation and shifts in atmospheric circulation, and they linked it to megafaunal extinctions in Australia, the end of the Neanderthals and a rise in cave use and red ochre handprints. Critics pointed out that ice cores and many climate records show no marked change at that time, and that the archaeological events do not line up closely.","In 2025 Agnit Mukhopadhyay, Raven Garvey and colleagues at the University of Michigan modelled how auroras and space radiation would have spread to lower latitudes over Europe and North Africa during the excursion. They speculated that tailored clothing, ochre used as sunscreen and cave shelters might have given Homo sapiens an advantage over Neanderthals. The idea is untested; the Neanderthal decline is examined in chapter 20."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Did our ancestors nearly die out 930,000–813,000 years ago?","views":[{"label":"A severe bottleneck","text":"Wangjie Hu, Haipeng Li and colleagues (2023) argue that FitCoal detects a drop to about 1,280 breeding individuals lasting some 117,000 years, which fits the Mid-Pleistocene climate shift and a sparse fossil record, and could have helped separate our lineage."},{"label":"An artefact of the method","text":"Other population geneticists find no such signal with established methods, show that FitCoal can invent bottlenecks in simulations and that population structure can mimic them, and question the claimed fossil gap."}],"status":"Unresolved, but most specialists doubt the bottleneck. Better methods for structured populations and more ancient proteins from fossils of that period are needed."},{"question":"Did the Toba eruption nearly wipe out humans?","views":[{"label":"Volcanic winter and bottleneck","text":"Stanley Ambrose (1998), building on Michael Rampino and Stephen Self’s volcanic-winter models, argued that years of cold and darkness shrank human populations to a few thousand adults and shaped modern genetic diversity."},{"label":"Resilience","text":"Michael Petraglia, Chris Clarkson, Curtis Marean, Christine Lane, John Kappelman and others point to continuous occupation in India, South Africa and Ethiopia, no cooling signal in Lake Malawi and no genetic bottleneck at that date."}],"status":"The catastrophe hypothesis has largely been abandoned. The eruption probably caused severe regional effects, especially in South and Southeast Asia, but the global human population came through."},{"question":"Did the Laschamp excursion change human history?","views":[{"label":"A global crisis","text":"Alan Cooper, Chris Turney and colleagues (2021) argue that the weakened field caused ozone loss and climate shifts that contributed to megafaunal extinctions, Neanderthal decline and new behaviours such as cave use and ochre handprints; a 2025 modelling study added a possible advantage for well-clothed Homo sapiens."},{"label":"Coincidence","text":"Palaeoclimatologists note that Greenland ice cores and cave records show no distinct climate event at the Laschamp minimum, and archaeologists point out that Neanderthal decline, Australian extinctions and cave art do not cluster at 42,000–41,000 years ago."}],"status":"Most researchers remain unconvinced that the excursion had major effects on humans. Precise dating of archaeological sites against the radiocarbon record of the excursion is the test."},{"question":"Did climate variability drive human evolution?","views":[{"label":"Variability selection","text":"Rick Potts argues that repeated swings between wet and dry favoured flexible behaviour, larger brains and new technologies, supported by the Olorgesailie drill core and the timing of the Middle Stone Age."},{"label":"Many causes","text":"Others argue that correlations between climate records and evolutionary events are loose, that local environments differed greatly, and that social and demographic factors, such as population size and networks, were at least as important."}],"status":"Scientific drilling projects in East African lake basins now allow climate and archaeology to be compared at the same place, and they show that climate mattered, but rarely as a single trigger."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Claimed bottleneck","value":"About 1,280 breeding individuals, 930,000–813,000 years ago (2023; disputed)"},{"label":"Toba eruption","value":"About 74,000 years ago, northern Sumatra"},{"label":"Farthest Toba ash","value":"Glass shards at Pinnacle Point, South Africa, almost 9,000 kilometres away"},{"label":"African megadroughts","value":"About 135,000–75,000 years ago; Lake Malawi lost at least 95 percent of its water"},{"label":"Laschamp excursion","value":"About 41,000 years ago; the magnetic field fell to a small fraction of today’s strength"},{"label":"Mid-Pleistocene Transition","value":"Glacial cycles lengthened from about 41,000 to 100,000 years between about 1.25 million and 700,000 years ago"}],"siteIds":["toba-caldera","jwalapuram","pinnacle-point","shinfa-metema-1","olorgesailie","ngawha","laschamps"],"routes":[],"themes":["climate","genetics","species","migration","technology","food"],"sources":[{"cite":"Hu, W. et al. (2023). Genomic inference of a severe human bottleneck during the Early to Middle Pleistocene transition. Science 381, 979–984."},{"cite":"Ambrose, S. H. (1998). Late Pleistocene human population bottlenecks, volcanic winter, and differentiation of modern humans. Journal of Human Evolution 34, 623–651."},{"cite":"Petraglia, M. et al. (2007). Middle Paleolithic assemblages from the Indian subcontinent before and after the Toba super-eruption. Science 317, 114–116."},{"cite":"Clarkson, C. et al. (2020). Human occupation of northern India spans the Toba super-eruption ~74,000 years ago. Nature Communications 11."},{"cite":"Smith, E. I. et al. (2018). Humans thrived in South Africa through the Toba eruption about 74,000 years ago. Nature 555, 511–515."},{"cite":"Lane, C. S., Chorn, B. T. & Johnson, T. C. (2013). Ash from the Toba supereruption in Lake Malawi shows no volcanic winter in East Africa at 75 ka. PNAS 110, 8025–8029."},{"cite":"Kappelman, J. et al. (2024). Adaptive foraging behaviours in the Horn of Africa during Toba supereruption. Nature 628."},{"cite":"Scholz, C. A. et al. (2007). East African megadroughts between 135 and 75 thousand years ago and bearing on early-modern human origins. PNAS 104, 16416–16421."},{"cite":"Cooper, A. et al. (2021). A global environmental crisis 42,000 years ago. Science 371, 811–818."},{"cite":"Potts, R. et al. (2020). Increased ecological resource variability during a critical transition in hominin evolution. Science Advances 6."}]},{"id":"great-dispersal","order":18,"act":"one-species","title":"The Great Dispersal","subtitle":"Out of Africa for good, and the Neanderthal inheritance","era":"Late Middle Stone Age; Middle to Upper Palaeolithic transition","start":70000,"end":40000,"dateLabel":"70,000–40,000 years ago","regions":["africa","asia","europe"],"focus":[5,-5,120,60],"emblem":"globe","summary":"Between about 60,000 and 45,000 years ago one African population spread across Eurasia, mixing with Neanderthals for several thousand years on the way. Everyone whose ancestry lies outside Africa descends from it and still carries its genetic souvenirs.","paragraphs":[["The survivors of those hard millennia did not stay put.","Sometime between 60,000 and 50,000 years ago, a population from eastern Africa, perhaps only a few thousand people, moved into Asia.","Earlier groups had made the same journey and faded away.","This one did not.","Every person whose family history lies outside Africa descends from it."],["We know this because their descendants’ genomes tell a single story.","Almost everyone outside Africa carries one to two per cent Neanderthal DNA, and in 2024 two teams dated when it arrived.","Leonardo Iasi and colleagues, comparing ancient and living genomes, found one long episode of mixing, centred on about 47,000 years ago and lasting perhaps 7,000 years.","Arev Sümer’s team read the oldest modern human genomes yet recovered, from Ranis in Germany and Zlatý kůň in Czechia, and reached almost the same date."],["The first Europeans met Neanderthals face to face.","At Bacho Kiro Cave in Bulgaria, people who lived about 45,000 years ago had Neanderthal ancestors only five to seven generations back.","A man from Peștera cu Oase in Romania had one just four to six generations back.","Yet these pioneers left few or no descendants among later Europeans."],["What lasted was the inheritance.","Neanderthal and Denisovan genes still shape our immune defences and our skin, and one stretch of Neanderthal DNA raised the risk of severe Covid for millions of people in 2020."]],"sentences":["The survivors of those hard millennia did not stay put.","Sometime between 60,000 and 50,000 years ago, a population from eastern Africa, perhaps only a few thousand people, moved into Asia.","Earlier groups had made the same journey and faded away.","This one did not.","Every person whose family history lies outside Africa descends from it.","We know this because their descendants’ genomes tell a single story.","Almost everyone outside Africa carries one to two per cent Neanderthal DNA, and in 2024 two teams dated when it arrived.","Leonardo Iasi and colleagues, comparing ancient and living genomes, found one long episode of mixing, centred on about 47,000 years ago and lasting perhaps 7,000 years.","Arev Sümer’s team read the oldest modern human genomes yet recovered, from Ranis in Germany and Zlatý kůň in Czechia, and reached almost the same date.","The first Europeans met Neanderthals face to face.","At Bacho Kiro Cave in Bulgaria, people who lived about 45,000 years ago had Neanderthal ancestors only five to seven generations back.","A man from Peștera cu Oase in Romania had one just four to six generations back.","Yet these pioneers left few or no descendants among later Europeans.","What lasted was the inheritance.","Neanderthal and Denisovan genes still shape our immune defences and our skin, and one stretch of Neanderthal DNA raised the risk of severe Covid for millions of people in 2020."],"pronounce":{"Zlatý kůň":"Zlah-tee koon","Bacho Kiro":"Bah-cho Kee-ro","Peștera cu Oase":"Pesh-teh-rah koo Wah-seh","Sümer":"Sue-mer","Ranis":"Rah-nis"},"body":[{"heading":"One exodus that counted","paragraphs":["Homo sapiens had left Africa before. A jaw from Misliya, a skull from Apidima, teeth from southern China and a layer at Grotte Mandrin in France record earlier forays (chapter 16). But the genomes of living people point to one later event. Non-Africans share the signature of a founding bottleneck; their mitochondrial lineages all fall within two branches, M and N, of a single African lineage called L3; and all of them carry Neanderthal DNA from the same episode of mixing. Geneticists therefore speak of a main out-of-Africa dispersal, usually placed between about 60,000 and 50,000 years ago, with earlier migrants contributing little or nothing that survives.","Why this dispersal succeeded where others failed is still debated. A 2025 Nature study led by Emily Hallett modelled the environments of dated African sites over the past 120,000 years and found that from about 70,000 years ago people began to occupy a much wider range of habitats, from forests to arid lands. On this view the emigrants were already ecological generalists, equipped for whatever Eurasia offered."]},{"heading":"Which way out, and where to wait","paragraphs":["Two gateways were open. A northern route ran along the Nile or the Red Sea hills and across Sinai into the Levant. A southern route crossed the Bab-el-Mandeb strait, narrowed by low sea levels, into southern Arabia, then followed the coast or wetter inland corridors during humid phases. Both are plausible, and the evidence is circumstantial: stone tools rarely identify their makers, and genetics records populations, not footpaths.","A 2024 study in Nature Communications led by Leonardo Vallini and Luca Pagani offered a way around the problem. Combining ancient and present-day genomes with palaeoclimate models, it argued that after leaving Africa the ancestors of all non-Africans lived for thousands of years in a “hub”, most likely the Persian Plateau, whose mountains and valleys could support more people than the rest of Southwest Asia. People in Iran and neighbouring regions today carry an ancestry component that best matches this hub population, from which waves spread west and east over a period the authors place between about 70,000 and 45,000 years ago.","Genetics also hints at a lineage that stayed behind. The so-called Basal Eurasians branched off before the main Neanderthal mixing and carried little or no Neanderthal DNA. No skeleton has been assigned to them, but their ancestry appears in early hunter-gatherers and farmers of the Near East, diluting their Neanderthal share. Where they lived, perhaps in Arabia, the now-drowned Gulf basin or the Levant, is unknown."]},{"heading":"A genetic clock for the mixing","paragraphs":["Neanderthal DNA works as a clock. Every generation, recombination cuts inherited chunks into shorter pieces, so the length of Neanderthal segments reveals how long ago mixing took place. In 2014 Qiaomei Fu and colleagues sequenced the femur of a man who died about 45,000 years ago at Ust’-Ishim in western Siberia. His Neanderthal segments were long, placing the mixture only some 7,000–13,000 years before his lifetime.","In December 2024 two studies sharpened the picture. Leonardo Iasi, Benjamin Peter, Priya Moorjani and colleagues analysed 58 ancient genomes from Europe and Western and Central Asia together with 275 present-day genomes. Almost all the Neanderthal ancestry in living non-Africans, they concluded in Science, came from a single extended period of gene flow between about 50,500 and 43,500 years ago, centred on 47,000 years ago and lasting roughly 7,000 years. They also found that “Neanderthal deserts”, long stretches of our genome with no Neanderthal DNA, already existed in the oldest individuals, so selection removed harmful variants within a few thousand years.","The second study, led by Arev Sümer and Johannes Krause in Nature, sequenced six individuals from the Ilsenhöhle at Ranis in Germany and the woman from Zlatý kůň in Czechia. They belonged to one small population, and the Zlatý kůň woman was a fifth- or sixth-degree relative of two Ranis individuals. Their Neanderthal segments came from the same shared admixture, dated to about 49,000–45,000 years ago. The implication is striking: until roughly 47,000 years ago the ancestors of all non-Africans still formed one population, and the split between the ancestors of Europeans and East Asians came later."]},{"heading":"Pioneers in Europe","paragraphs":["At Ranis, bone fragments once too scrappy to identify were shown in 2024, by protein analysis and mitochondrial DNA, to belong to Homo sapiens. A team led by Dorothea Mylopotamitaki, Marcel Weiss and Jean-Jacques Hublin dated them to as early as about 47,500 years ago and linked them to the Lincombian–Ranisian–Jerzmanowician (LRJ), a leaf-point industry of northern Europe long attributed to Neanderthals. Isotopes from animal teeth showed that these people lived on a cold steppe comparable to present-day Siberia. Their genomes suggest dark skin, hair and eyes, and represent the deepest known branch of the non-African family: a group that left no known descendants.","At Bacho Kiro Cave in Bulgaria, Homo sapiens remains dated to about 46,000–42,000 years ago lie with Initial Upper Palaeolithic blades, cave-bear tooth pendants and bone tools. Genomes published by Mateja Hajdinjak and colleagues in 2021 showed that three individuals had Neanderthal ancestors only five to seven generations back, and that they were more closely related to later East Asians and Native Americans than to later Europeans. A jaw from Peștera cu Oase in Romania, about 40,000 years old, carried 6–9% Neanderthal DNA and a Neanderthal ancestor four to six generations back, as Fu and colleagues reported in 2015. Neither group contributed much to living Europeans."]},{"heading":"The Initial Upper Palaeolithic trail","paragraphs":["Archaeologists track the migrants through a distinctive toolkit, the Initial Upper Palaeolithic (IUP): long, pointed blades struck from flat-faced cores, often accompanied by bone tools and beads. Its best-dated early appearance is at Boker Tachtit in Israel’s Negev, where Elisabetta Boaretto and colleagues in 2021 dated the earliest phase to 50,000–49,000 years ago. Similar assemblages appear at Bacho Kiro, at Kara-Bom in the Russian Altai and at Tolbor-16 in Mongolia by about 45,000 years ago, and at Shuidonggou in northern China a few thousand years later.","Many researchers, including Nicolas Zwyns, read this chain of sites as the trail of a northern dispersal of Homo sapiens across the steppe, and the Bacho Kiro genomes, with their East Asian links, fit the idea. Others caution that a technique can spread by contact and that most IUP sites have no human fossils. Europe’s other early industries, such as the Uluzzian and the Châtelperronian, raise similar questions of authorship (chapter 20)."]},{"heading":"What the mixing left in us","paragraphs":["Neanderthal DNA makes up roughly 1–2% of every non-African genome, and people of East Asian ancestry carry slightly more than Europeans. Denisovans added more in Oceania and traces across Asia (chapter 13). Archaic DNA is especially scarce on the X chromosome. A 2026 Science study by Alexander Platt, Daniel Harris and Sarah Tishkoff argued that this reflects who mated with whom, mainly Neanderthal men with sapiens women, rather than genetic incompatibility, because Neanderthal X chromosomes carry plenty of sapiens DNA from earlier contacts.","Some inherited variants proved useful. Neanderthal versions of Toll-like receptor genes and the OAS gene cluster shaped immune responses; others, such as variants near BNC2, affect skin and hair. The Denisovan version of EPAS1 helps Tibetans live at high altitude. Not every souvenir is welcome: in 2020 Hugo Zeberg and Svante Pääbo showed that the strongest genetic risk factor for severe Covid-19, a stretch of chromosome 3, came from Neanderthals and is carried by about half of South Asians and a sixth of Europeans. A protective Neanderthal variant on chromosome 12 was identified the following year.","Neanderthal ancestry even reached Africa. A 2020 study in Cell led by Lu Chen and Joshua Akey found more Neanderthal-like DNA in African genomes than expected. They explained it partly by later migrations back into Africa of people who carried Neanderthal ancestry, and partly by much earlier gene flow from modern humans into Neanderthals."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Did the main dispersal leave Africa by the northern or the southern route?","views":[{"label":"Southern route","text":"Supporters point to the short crossing of the Bab-el-Mandeb at times of low sea level, to stone tools showing that people lived in Arabia during humid ‘Green Arabia’ phases (a theme of work by Michael Petraglia and colleagues), and to the early arrival of people in South Asia and Sahul, which a route along the Indian Ocean rim would favour."},{"label":"Northern route","text":"Others favour an exit through Sinai. A 2015 genetic comparison by Luca Pagani and colleagues found non-Africans slightly closer to Egyptians than to Ethiopians; the earliest Initial Upper Palaeolithic is in the Levant at Boker Tachtit; and the Neanderthal mixing most likely took place in Southwest Asia, where Neanderthals lived."}],"status":"Unresolved. Later migrations within northeast Africa and Arabia blur the genetic signal, and both gates may have been used at different times. The hub model shifts the question: whichever route was taken, the ancestors of non-Africans seem to have gathered in Southwest Asia, probably on the Persian Plateau, before spreading. Dated human fossils from Arabia or Iran between 60,000 and 45,000 years ago would help most."},{"question":"When did the ancestors of today’s non-Africans leave Africa?","views":[{"label":"About 60,000–50,000 years ago","text":"The 2024 admixture dates from Iasi and colleagues and from Sümer and colleagues require one non-African population still together around 47,000 years ago. With the earliest securely dated IUP (about 50,000 years at Boker Tachtit) and the ages of Ranis, Bacho Kiro and Ust’-Ishim, this favours a departure not much before 60,000 years ago."},{"label":"Earlier, before 60,000 years ago","text":"Archaeologists who accept an arrival in Australia by 65,000 years ago at Madjedbebe, and the 67,800-year-old hand stencil on Muna in Sulawesi reported in 2026, argue that Homo sapiens was already deep into Asia earlier. Some reconcile this with the genetics through a long stay in the hub, or through earlier waves that left little DNA."}],"status":"The genetic clock and some archaeological dates are in tension. Admixture dates depend on assumed generation times and recombination maps; luminescence and uranium-series dates have their own uncertainties. Most geneticists favour a main dispersal around 60,000–50,000 years ago. The Sahul evidence is discussed in the next chapter."},{"question":"Was mixing with Neanderthals one long episode or several?","views":[{"label":"One extended pulse","text":"Iasi and colleagues and Sümer and colleagues find that almost all Neanderthal ancestry in living people traces to a single period of roughly 7,000 years, shared by all non-Africans before they diverged."},{"label":"Several pulses","text":"Earlier studies, such as Benjamin Vernot and Joshua Akey (2015) and Fernando Villanea and Joshua Schraiber (2019), explained East Asians’ slightly higher Neanderthal share by an extra pulse into their ancestors. Bacho Kiro and Oase prove that local mixing also happened later."}],"status":"Both are partly right. Later local encounters happened, but mostly in pioneer groups that died out, so they barely register today. The East Asian excess is now often explained by the dilution of West Eurasian ancestry with Basal Eurasians, who carried no Neanderthal DNA, and by differences in selection, rather than by a second major pulse."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Neanderthal DNA in non-Africans","value":"About 1–2% of the genome"},{"label":"Main mixing period","value":"About 50,500–43,500 years ago (Iasi et al. 2024)"},{"label":"Oldest modern human genomes","value":"Ranis and Zlatý kůň, about 45,000 years"},{"label":"Earliest Initial Upper Palaeolithic","value":"Boker Tachtit, Israel, 50,000–49,000 years ago"},{"label":"Most recent Neanderthal ancestor found","value":"Oase 1, four to six generations back"}],"siteIds":["boker-tachtit","bacho-kiro-cave","ranis-ilsenhohle","zlaty-kun","pestera-cu-oase","ust-ishim"],"routes":[{"id":"ooa-main-east-africa","label":"Main dispersal from eastern Africa","start":70000,"end":60000,"certainty":"established","path":[[35.5,-2],[37.5,4.5],[39,11],[39.8,15]]},{"id":"ooa-northern-route","label":"Northern route via Sinai and the Levant","start":65000,"end":50000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[39.8,15],[37.2,19.6],[33.5,27],[33.8,30.5],[35,32],[38,35.5],[44.4,33.3],[51.4,34.3]]},{"id":"ooa-southern-route","label":"Southern route via Bab-el-Mandeb and Arabia","start":65000,"end":50000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[39.8,15],[43.3,12.6],[45,13.5],[49,15],[54,17],[57.5,22.5],[56.3,26.5],[52.5,29.5],[51.4,32.5]]},{"id":"ooa-into-europe","label":"Spread into Europe","start":48000,"end":45000,"certainty":"established","path":[[51.4,34.3],[44,38.5],[35,39],[29,41],[25.4,42.9],[21.8,45],[16.6,48.9],[14.1,49.9],[11.6,50.7]]},{"id":"ooa-into-east-asia","label":"Spread into Central and East Asia","start":50000,"end":40000,"certainty":"established","path":[[51.4,34.3],[58,37.5],[66,40],[76,45],[85.5,50.8],[96,49.5],[103,49.2],[106.5,38.3],[115.9,39.7]]}],"themes":["migration","genetics","species","technology"],"sources":[{"cite":"Iasi, L. N. M. et al. (2024). Neandertal ancestry through time: insights from genomes of ancient and present-day humans. Science 386, eadq3010.","doi":"10.1126/science.adq3010"},{"cite":"Sümer, A. P. et al. (2024). Earliest modern human genomes constrain timing of Neanderthal admixture. Nature (published online December 2024).","doi":"10.1038/s41586-024-08420-x"},{"cite":"Mylopotamitaki, D. et al. (2024). Homo sapiens reached the higher latitudes of Europe by 45,000 years ago. Nature 626.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-023-06923-7"},{"cite":"Vallini, L. et al. (2024). The Persian plateau served as hub for Homo sapiens after the main out of Africa dispersal. Nature Communications 15.","doi":"10.1038/s41467-024-46161-7"},{"cite":"Hajdinjak, M. et al. (2021). Initial Upper Palaeolithic humans in Europe had recent Neanderthal ancestry. Nature 592, 253–257.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-021-03335-3"},{"cite":"Boaretto, E. et al. (2021). The absolute chronology of Boker Tachtit (Israel) and implications for the Middle to Upper Paleolithic transition in the Levant. PNAS 118 (25), e2014657118.","doi":"10.1073/pnas.2014657118"},{"cite":"Fu, Q. et al. (2014). Genome sequence of a 45,000-year-old modern human from western Siberia. Nature 514, 445–449.","doi":"10.1038/nature13810"},{"cite":"Zeberg, H. & Pääbo, S. (2020). The major genetic risk factor for severe COVID-19 is inherited from Neanderthals. Nature 587, 610–612.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-020-2818-3"},{"cite":"Chen, L., Wolf, A. B., Fu, W., Li, L. & Akey, J. M. (2020). Identifying and interpreting apparent Neanderthal ancestry in African individuals. Cell 180, 677–687."},{"cite":"Platt, A., Harris, D. N. & Tishkoff, S. A. (2026). Interbreeding between Neanderthals and modern humans was strongly sex biased. Science.","doi":"10.1126/science.aea6774"}]},{"id":"sahul","order":19,"act":"one-species","title":"Across Wallacea to Sahul","subtitle":"The first ocean voyages, the oldest rock art and a continent of giants","era":"Late Pleistocene","start":70000,"end":40000,"dateLabel":"70,000–40,000 years ago","regions":["oceania"],"focus":[100,-45,158,8],"emblem":"hand","summary":"To reach Australia and New Guinea, people had to cross open sea again and again through the islands of Wallacea. When they first arrived is one of archaeology’s sharpest disputes, sharpened again in 2026 by a hand stencil at least 67,800 years old.","paragraphs":[["On the island of Muna, off southeastern Sulawesi, someone pressed a hand against a limestone wall and sprayed red pigment around it.","The outline of the fingers was then narrowed, turning the hand into something like a claw.","A thin mineral crust later grew over the paint, and in 2026 that crust gave a minimum age of 67,800 years.","It is the oldest dated rock art in the world."],["The painters were on their way somewhere.","East of Borneo and Bali lies Wallacea, a scatter of islands that no land bridge ever joined to Asia or to Sahul, the single continent that Australia, New Guinea and Tasmania formed when sea levels were low.","To reach Sahul, people had to cross open water again and again, at least once over a distance approaching 100 kilometres.","Models suggest that the founding population numbered well over a thousand.","These were planned voyages, not accidents."],["When the first voyagers landed is fiercely debated.","Stone tools at Madjedbebe in Arnhem Land have been dated to about 65,000 years ago, while some archaeologists and geneticists argue for about 50,000.","Either way, people spread quickly.","They caught tuna off Timor, gathered pandanus nuts in the New Guinea highlands and buried their dead with ceremony at Lake Mungo 40,000 years ago.","And Sahul’s giant animals, from the rhinoceros-sized Diprotodon to the towering flightless bird Genyornis, disappeared."]],"sentences":["On the island of Muna, off southeastern Sulawesi, someone pressed a hand against a limestone wall and sprayed red pigment around it.","The outline of the fingers was then narrowed, turning the hand into something like a claw.","A thin mineral crust later grew over the paint, and in 2026 that crust gave a minimum age of 67,800 years.","It is the oldest dated rock art in the world.","The painters were on their way somewhere.","East of Borneo and Bali lies Wallacea, a scatter of islands that no land bridge ever joined to Asia or to Sahul, the single continent that Australia, New Guinea and Tasmania formed when sea levels were low.","To reach Sahul, people had to cross open water again and again, at least once over a distance approaching 100 kilometres.","Models suggest that the founding population numbered well over a thousand.","These were planned voyages, not accidents.","When the first voyagers landed is fiercely debated.","Stone tools at Madjedbebe in Arnhem Land have been dated to about 65,000 years ago, while some archaeologists and geneticists argue for about 50,000.","Either way, people spread quickly.","They caught tuna off Timor, gathered pandanus nuts in the New Guinea highlands and buried their dead with ceremony at Lake Mungo 40,000 years ago.","And Sahul’s giant animals, from the rhinoceros-sized Diprotodon to the towering flightless bird Genyornis, disappeared."],"pronounce":{"Madjedbebe":"Mudge-ed-beh-beh","Wallacea":"Wol-ay-see-ah","Sahul":"Sah-hool","Diprotodon":"Dye-pro-toe-don","Genyornis":"Jen-ee-or-nis"},"body":[{"heading":"Sunda, Sahul and the lines between","paragraphs":["Through much of the last glacial period, sea level stood 50–120 metres below today’s. Borneo, Sumatra, Java and Bali joined mainland Southeast Asia as the continent of Sunda, while Australia, New Guinea and Tasmania formed Sahul. Between them lay Wallacea: Sulawesi, Lombok, Flores, Timor, the Moluccas and hundreds of smaller islands, separated by deep channels that never dried.","Alfred Russel Wallace mapped the boundary in the 1850s. West of a line between Bali and Lombok and between Borneo and Sulawesi lived Asian animals such as tigers and monkeys; to the east, marsupials and cockatoos increasingly took over. For humans the Wallace Line marked where walking stopped. Earlier hominins had crossed into Wallacea, reaching Flores and Sulawesi more than a million years ago (chapter 8), but only Homo sapiens is known to have gone on to Sahul.","Two broad routes are proposed. A northern one ran from Borneo to Sulawesi and on through the Sula islands or the Moluccas to the Bird’s Head of New Guinea; a southern one led along the Lesser Sunda chain through Flores and Timor to the exposed shelf off northwest Australia. A 2018 modelling study by Shimona Kealy, Julien Louys and Sue O’Connor, based on which islands were visible from one another, favoured the northern route."]},{"heading":"Voyagers, not castaways","paragraphs":["No Pleistocene boat survives; the craft may have been bamboo rafts or bark canoes. What can be estimated is scale. In 2019 Corey Bradshaw, Sean Ulm and colleagues modelled births, deaths and extinction risk and concluded that a viable colony needed some 1,300–1,550 founders, arriving at once or in repeated voyages of at least 130 people over roughly 700–900 years. At least one crossing approached 100 kilometres. Sahul was not settled by a raft blown off course but by planned, repeated expeditions.","People soon mastered the sea. At Jerimalai cave in East Timor, Sue O’Connor’s team found about 38,000 fish bones from more than 2,800 individual fish, including tuna and other fast open-water species, in layers dated to about 42,000 years ago. A fishhook cut from shell, dated to between 23,000 and 16,000 years ago, is among the oldest known. Catching tuna implies boats, lines and knowledge of seasons and currents."]},{"heading":"How old? Madjedbebe and the long chronology","paragraphs":["Madjedbebe, a sandstone rock shelter in Mirarr country in Arnhem Land, is at the heart of the debate. Excavations by Chris Clarkson’s team in partnership with the Gundjeihmi Aboriginal Corporation, published in Nature in 2017, dated the lowest dense layer of artefacts to about 65,000 years ago using optically stimulated luminescence (OSL) of individual sand grains. The finds include grinding stones, ground ochre mixed with mica to make a reflective pigment, and fragments of the world’s oldest known edge-ground axes. Charred plant remains studied by Anna Florin and colleagues in 2020 show that the first occupants ate fruits, nuts, roots and palm stems, some of which needed processing.","Critics, notably Jim O’Connell and Jim Allen, argue that artefacts can sink through loose sand, moved by termites or trampling, so the deepest tools may be younger than the grains around them. Most other early sites in Sahul, they note, date between about 54,000 and 43,000 years ago. In 2025 they added a genetic argument: Aboriginal Australians and Papuans carry the same Neanderthal admixture as other non-Africans, dated to about 50,500–43,500 years ago, so their ancestors cannot have reached Sahul by 65,000 years ago.","The long chronology has since gained support. A 2025 study in Science Advances by Francesca Gandini, Martin Richards, Pedro Soares and colleagues analysed some 2,500 mitochondrial genomes from Australia, New Guinea and the western Pacific, corrected for the way mutation rates appear to change over time, and dated the founding lineages to about 60,000 years ago, arriving along a main northern and a minor southern route. The Muna stencil, if made by Homo sapiens, puts our species in Wallacea by 67,800 years ago. How these dates fit the genomic admixture clock remains unresolved."]},{"heading":"Painting on the way: Sulawesi","paragraphs":["The limestone karst of Maros-Pangkep in South Sulawesi, and the islands nearby, hold some of the world’s oldest paintings. They are dated by uranium-series analysis of thin calcite crusts that formed over the pigment, so each date is a minimum. In 2021 Adam Brumm, Maxime Aubert and colleagues reported a life-size painting of a Sulawesi warty pig at Leang Tedongnge at least 45,500 years old. In 2024 a team co-led by Adhi Agus Oktaviana used a laser-ablation version of the method to date a scene at Leang Karampuang to at least 51,200 years ago: a pig 92 centimetres long with at least three human-like figures around it, the oldest known narrative image.","In January 2026 the same Indonesian and Australian team reported hand stencils in Liang Metanduno on Muna Island, one at least 67,800 years old. Its fingers appear to have been deliberately narrowed after stencilling, a stylistic choice the authors see as a mark of Homo sapiens, although other kinds of humans had lived in the region earlier. It is the oldest dated rock art known anywhere."]},{"heading":"Across a continent","paragraphs":["Once in Sahul, people spread into every environment. At Boodie Cave on Barrow Island, then a hill on the coastal plain of northwest Australia, Peter Veth’s team found shellfish and other marine foods from about 51,000 years ago. Warratyi rock shelter in the Flinders Ranges was located by Adnyamathanha elder Clifford Coulthard and archaeologist Giles Hamm; its layers from 49,000–46,000 years ago contain Australia’s earliest known use of ochre and gypsum pigment, a bone point, and bones of Diprotodon and eggshell of a giant bird. At Devil’s Lair in the far southwest, people were present by about 48,000 years ago.","In the mountains of New Guinea, Glenn Summerhayes and colleagues excavated sites in the Ivane Valley, about 2,000 metres above sea level, dated to 49,000–44,000 years ago. People there gathered pandanus nuts, ate yams, burned forest to open it up and made heavy ‘waisted’ axes notched for hafting: some of the earliest evidence anywhere of Homo sapiens living in high tropical mountains."]},{"heading":"Lake Mungo and the living ancestors","paragraphs":["The Willandra Lakes of western New South Wales, now dry, held water and fish 40,000 years ago. In 1968 geologist Jim Bowler found burned bones eroding from a dune at Lake Mungo: a young woman whose body had been cremated, her remaining bones broken and burned again, then buried. In 1974 he found a man laid on his back with hands clasped, sprinkled with red ochre. Both are dated to about 40,000 years ago, among the oldest known cremation and ochre burial anywhere. Nearby, hundreds of footprints of adults and children were pressed into a clay pan between about 23,000 and 19,000 years ago.","For the Paakantyi (Barkindji), Mutthi Mutthi and Ngiyampaa peoples, these are ancestors, not specimens. Mungo Lady was returned from the Australian National University in 1992 and Mungo Man in 2017. Since 2022 the Traditional Owners have pursued the reburial of both, and of more than a hundred other ancestral remains, at undisclosed places in the Willandra Lakes, a process marked by disagreement among some elders about how and where it should be done. Genetic work supports deep continuity: in 2017 Ray Tobler, Alan Cooper and colleagues found that Aboriginal mitochondrial lineages have stayed in the same regions for up to about 50,000 years."]},{"heading":"The vanished giants","paragraphs":["Sahul’s Pleistocene animals included Diprotodon optatum, a wombat relative the size of a rhinoceros weighing up to about 2.7 tonnes; Genyornis newtoni, a flightless bird around two metres tall; the marsupial lion Thylacoleo; giant short-faced kangaroos; and a giant monitor lizard. Most had vanished by about 40,000 years ago.","The case against humans rests on timing. At Lynch’s Crater in Queensland, Susan Rule, Chris Johnson and colleagues found in 2012 that spores of dung fungi, a proxy for large herbivores, collapsed around 41,000 years ago, before fire and vegetation changed. A 2016 analysis by Frédérik Saltré and colleagues found no link between extinctions and climate shifts. Gifford Miller’s team argued in 2016 that burned Genyornis eggshells showed people cooking the eggs about 47,000 years ago. Others said the shells came from a large megapode, but a 2022 protein study led by Beatrice Demarchi confirmed they were Genyornis; whether the burning was human remains debated.","Others stress climate. Sahul grew drier and more variable, and at South Walker Creek in Queensland, Scott Hocknull and colleagues (2020) documented a diverse megafauna that disappeared about 40,000 years ago amid environmental decline, with no sign of people. At Cuddie Springs in New South Wales, Judith Field and colleagues report stone tools among bones of Diprotodon and Genyornis dated to roughly 36,000–30,000 years ago, suggesting long coexistence, although the integrity of the layers has been challenged. A 2019 study of southeastern Australia by Saltré and colleagues concluded that extinctions followed the combination of human presence and climatic stress."]}],"debates":[{"question":"When did people first reach Sahul?","views":[{"label":"Long chronology, about 65,000 years","text":"Chris Clarkson, Zenobia Jacobs, Richard ‘Bert’ Roberts and colleagues defend the Madjedbebe luminescence dates, citing consistent single-grain ages and distinctive artefacts in the lowest layer. The 2025 mitochondrial estimate of about 60,000 years (Gandini and colleagues) and the 67,800-year-old Muna stencil add support."},{"label":"Short chronology, about 50,000 years","text":"Jim O’Connell, Jim Allen and others argue that artefacts moved downward in Madjedbebe’s sands, that the other early sites cluster between about 54,000 and 43,000 years ago, and that the Neanderthal admixture shared by all non-Africans, dated to about 50,500–43,500 years ago, rules out an earlier arrival of the ancestors of today’s Aboriginal Australians and Papuans."}],"status":"Unresolved in 2026. One suggested reconciliation, an earlier population that was later absorbed or replaced, has no direct evidence. More sites older than 55,000 years, independent dating of Madjedbebe’s deepest layers, or ancient genomes from Pleistocene Sahul or Wallacea would decide it."},{"question":"Which way did the first voyagers go through Wallacea?","views":[{"label":"Northern route","text":"Least-cost and intervisibility models (Kealy and colleagues, 2018), Sulawesi’s very early rock art and the 2025 mitochondrial study, which traced most founding lineages through the northern islands to New Guinea, favour a path via Sulawesi and the Moluccas."},{"label":"Southern route","text":"The chain through Flores and Timor led directly to the broad exposed shelf of northwest Australia. Early sites on Timor such as Jerimalai, and a minor southern genetic signal, show that this route was used too."}],"status":"Probably both, perhaps at different times. What is clear is that the crossings were repeated and deliberate. Early sites on the Moluccas or the now-drowned Sahul shelf would sharpen the picture."},{"question":"What wiped out Sahul’s megafauna?","views":[{"label":"Humans","text":"Richard Roberts, Tim Flannery, Chris Johnson, Corey Bradshaw and Frédérik Saltré point to extinctions following human arrival, the dung-spore collapse at Lynch’s Crater and the lack of a climate trigger. Megafauna had survived earlier arid phases."},{"label":"Climate","text":"Stephen Wroe, Judith Field, Scott Hocknull and others emphasise long-term drying, sites like South Walker Creek where megafauna vanished without people, and possible coexistence at Cuddie Springs."},{"label":"Both","text":"Regional studies, such as Saltré and colleagues in 2019, find that extinction came where hunting and landscape burning acted on populations already stressed by climate."}],"status":"Most researchers now see human impact as the main driver, acting with climate, though the balance varies by region and species. Better last-appearance dates for each species are the main need; collagen fingerprinting and ancient proteins are starting to supply them."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Oldest dated rock art","value":"Hand stencil, Liang Metanduno (Muna), at least 67,800 years"},{"label":"Earliest occupation claim","value":"Madjedbebe, about 65,000 years (OSL); disputed"},{"label":"Oldest narrative scene","value":"Leang Karampuang, at least 51,200 years"},{"label":"Minimum founding population","value":"About 1,300–1,550 people (Bradshaw et al. 2019)"},{"label":"Earliest deep-sea fishing","value":"Jerimalai, Timor, about 42,000 years ago"},{"label":"Mungo burials","value":"About 40,000 years old; returned to country (Mungo Man in 2017)"}],"siteIds":["madjedbebe","liang-metanduno","maros-pangkep-karst","jerimalai","ivane-valley","boodie-cave","warratyi","devils-lair","lake-mungo"],"routes":[{"id":"sahul-northern-route","label":"Northern route via Sulawesi and the Moluccas","start":70000,"end":50000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[116.8,0.8],[119.6,-3],[121.5,-1.6],[124.8,-1.9],[127.6,-1.5],[130,-1.9],[132.5,-1.3],[136.5,-4],[141,-5.5],[147.2,-8.4]]},{"id":"sahul-southern-route","label":"Southern route via Flores and Timor","start":70000,"end":50000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[114.6,-8.3],[116.2,-8.6],[118.5,-8.5],[121,-8.6],[124.3,-8.4],[127.2,-8.5],[128,-10.8],[130.2,-12.5],[132.9,-12.5]]},{"id":"sahul-interior-southeast","label":"Into the interior and the southeast","start":55000,"end":42000,"certainty":"established","path":[[132.9,-12.5],[133.9,-23.7],[138.9,-30.3],[143.1,-33.7]]},{"id":"sahul-western-coasts","label":"Along the western coasts","start":55000,"end":46000,"certainty":"established","path":[[132.9,-12.5],[126,-15.5],[121,-19],[115.4,-20.8],[114,-26],[115.1,-34.1]]}],"themes":["seafaring","migration","art","animals","burial","food","climate"],"sources":[{"cite":"Oktaviana, A. A., Joannes-Boyau, R., Hakim, B. et al. (2026). Rock art from at least 67,800 years ago in Sulawesi. Nature.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-025-09968-y"},{"cite":"Oktaviana, A. A. et al. (2024). Narrative cave art in Indonesia by 51,200 years ago. Nature 631.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-024-07541-7"},{"cite":"Clarkson, C. et al. (2017). Human occupation of northern Australia by 65,000 years ago. Nature 547, 306–310.","doi":"10.1038/nature22968"},{"cite":"Allen, J. & O’Connell, J. F. (2025). Recent DNA studies question a 65 kya arrival of humans in Sahul. Archaeology in Oceania.","doi":"10.1002/arco.70002"},{"cite":"Gandini, F., Almeida, M. et al. (2025). Genomic evidence supports the “long chronology” for the peopling of Sahul. Science Advances 11 (48)."},{"cite":"Bradshaw, C. J. A. et al. (2019). Minimum founding populations for the first peopling of Sahul. Nature Ecology & Evolution 3, 1057–1063.","doi":"10.1038/s41559-019-0902-6"},{"cite":"Summerhayes, G. R. et al. (2010). Human adaptation and plant use in highland New Guinea 49,000 to 44,000 years ago. Science 330, 78–81.","doi":"10.1126/science.1193130"},{"cite":"O’Connor, S., Ono, R. & Clarkson, C. (2011). Pelagic fishing at 42,000 years before the present and the maritime skills of modern humans. Science 334, 1117–1121."},{"cite":"Demarchi, B. et al. (2022). Ancient proteins resolve controversy over the identity of Genyornis eggshell. PNAS 119, e2109326119.","doi":"10.1073/pnas.2109326119"},{"cite":"Bowler, J. M. et al. (2003). New ages for human occupation and climatic change at Lake Mungo, Australia. Nature 421, 837–840."}]},{"id":"neanderthal-end","order":20,"act":"one-species","title":"The Last Neanderthals","subtitle":"Dating a disappearance, and the arguments over why","era":"Middle to Upper Palaeolithic transition","start":50000,"end":39000,"dateLabel":"50,000–39,000 years ago","regions":["europe","asia"],"focus":[-12,33,45,56],"emblem":"skull","summary":"Between about 45,000 and 40,000 years ago the Neanderthals vanished as Homo sapiens spread through Europe. Better radiocarbon dates, ancient genomes and population models have turned the question from what killed them into how several pressures combined.","paragraphs":[["While the first Australians were learning their new continent, the Neanderthals of Europe were running out of time.","Their last securely dated traces cluster between about 41,000 and 39,000 years ago.","After that, across a range that once stretched from Wales to the Altai Mountains, there is nothing."],["For decades the story seemed simple: clever newcomers replaced slow-witted natives.","The evidence now tells a slower and stranger tale.","Modern humans reached Germany and Bulgaria by about 47,000 to 45,000 years ago, so the two populations shared parts of Europe for thousands of years, in a patchwork of territories rather than along a front line.","At Grotte Mandrin in the Rhône valley, a Neanderthal nicknamed Thorin belonged to a lineage that had been cut off from other Neanderthals for around 50,000 years.","His people were few, isolated and inbred."],["So what ended them?","Some researchers point to competition with larger, better connected groups of Homo sapiens.","Others stress violent swings of climate, a colossal eruption near Naples about 39,850 years ago, or diseases passed between the two species.","Many now think the Neanderthals were already so thinly spread that bad luck alone could have finished them, and that part of them was absorbed rather than erased.","Their DNA still runs in billions of people today."]],"sentences":["While the first Australians were learning their new continent, the Neanderthals of Europe were running out of time.","Their last securely dated traces cluster between about 41,000 and 39,000 years ago.","After that, across a range that once stretched from Wales to the Altai Mountains, there is nothing.","For decades the story seemed simple: clever newcomers replaced slow-witted natives.","The evidence now tells a slower and stranger tale.","Modern humans reached Germany and Bulgaria by about 47,000 to 45,000 years ago, so the two populations shared parts of Europe for thousands of years, in a patchwork of territories rather than along a front line.","At Grotte Mandrin in the Rhône valley, a Neanderthal nicknamed Thorin belonged to a lineage that had been cut off from other Neanderthals for around 50,000 years.","His people were few, isolated and inbred.","So what ended them?","Some researchers point to competition with larger, better connected groups of Homo sapiens.","Others stress violent swings of climate, a colossal eruption near Naples about 39,850 years ago, or diseases passed between the two species.","Many now think the Neanderthals were already so thinly spread that bad luck alone could have finished them, and that part of them was absorbed rather than erased.","Their DNA still runs in billions of people today."],"pronounce":{"Mandrin":"Mahn-dran","Châtelperronian":"Shah-tel-peh-roh-nee-an","Uluzzian":"Oo-loot-see-an"},"body":[{"heading":"Dating a disappearance","paragraphs":["Radiocarbon dating reaches back about 50,000 years, and near that limit a trace of modern carbon contamination makes an old bone look thousands of years younger. Many ‘late’ Neanderthal dates of the 1990s and 2000s turned out to be contamination. Improved chemistry, such as ultrafiltration of collagen and the dating of a single amino acid, hydroxyproline, pushed dates back. Neanderthal bones from Vindija Cave in Croatia, once dated to around 32,000–28,000 years, proved older than about 40,000 years in a 2017 study led by Thibaut Devièse; in 2021 his team redated the Neanderthals of Spy in Belgium to about 44,200–40,600 years ago.","In 2014 Tom Higham and colleagues published nearly 200 new dates from about 40 sites between Spain and Russia. They concluded that the Mousterian, the Neanderthals’ stone-tool tradition, ended between about 41,000 and 39,000 years ago, and that Neanderthals and Homo sapiens overlapped in Europe for some 2,600–5,400 years, region by region rather than along a single advancing front. A 2022 statistical study for France and northern Spain by Igor Djakovic, Alastair Key and Marie Soressi placed the last Neanderthals there at about 40,900–39,800 years ago, with 1,400–2,900 years of overlap.","Other finds have extended the encounter. Homo sapiens was at Ranis in Germany by about 47,500 years ago and at Bacho Kiro in Bulgaria by about 45,000 (chapter 18). At Grotte Mandrin, Ludovic Slimak’s team argues that sapiens briefly occupied the cave about 54,000 years ago, between Neanderthal layers. The meeting was long and patchy, not a sudden conquest."]},{"heading":"Who made the ‘transitional’ industries?","paragraphs":["Between about 45,000 and 40,000 years ago several regional industries mixed old and new elements: blades, bone tools and ornaments. In France and northern Spain, the Châtelperronian combined curved-backed knives with personal ornaments. At the Grotte du Renne at Arcy-sur-Cure, its layers held pierced and grooved animal teeth, bone awls and Neanderthal remains, and in 2016 Frido Welker and colleagues used ancient proteins to confirm that bone fragments from those layers were Neanderthal. At Saint-Césaire in Charente-Maritime, a partial Neanderthal skeleton was found in 1979 in a Châtelperronian level.","Whether Neanderthals made the Châtelperronian is still argued. Ofer Bar-Yosef and Jean-Guillaume Bordes proposed in 2010 that the Grotte du Renne layers were mixed, and a 2018 re-examination led by Brad Gravina questioned whether the Saint-Césaire skeleton truly belonged with the Châtelperronian tools. If Neanderthals were the makers, a further question follows: did they invent ornaments independently, as Francesco d’Errico and João Zilhão argue, or copy sapiens neighbours?","In Italy and Greece the Uluzzian, with small crescent-shaped tools, shell beads and bone points, began about 45,000 years ago. In 2011 Stefano Benazzi and colleagues identified two milk teeth from Grotta del Cavallo in Apulia as Homo sapiens, making the Uluzzian one of Europe’s earliest sapiens industries. Zilhão and colleagues questioned the teeth’s position in the sequence, though not their modern shape. In 2019 Katsuhiro Sano and colleagues reported impact damage on Uluzzian backed pieces consistent with mechanically propelled weapons, perhaps spearthrowers or bows. And the northern European leaf-point industry once attributed to Neanderthals, the LRJ, is now linked to sapiens at Ranis."]},{"heading":"A last refuge in Iberia?","paragraphs":["For years southern Iberia looked like the Neanderthals’ final stronghold. At Gorham’s Cave in Gibraltar, Clive Finlayson and colleagues reported in 2006 Mousterian hearths with radiocarbon ages of about 28,000 years, some even younger. João Zilhão proposed an ‘Ebro frontier’: for several thousand years after sapiens settled northern Spain, Neanderthals held the lands south of the Ebro River, and in 2017 his team reported Mousterian levels at Cueva Antón in Murcia dated to about 37,000 years ago.","Redating has undermined much of this. In 2013 Rachel Wood, Tom Higham and colleagues re-dated eleven southern Iberian sites with ultrafiltration and found most Mousterian layers far older than previously thought, many beyond the reach of radiocarbon. In 2020 Jonathan Haws and colleagues dated Aurignacian tools at Lapa do Picareiro in central Portugal to about 41,000–38,000 years ago, showing that sapiens reached Iberia’s Atlantic side about as early as the rest of western Europe. The Gorham’s Cave dates, on charcoal that may have moved between layers, have not been confirmed independently."]},{"heading":"Few, isolated and inbred","paragraphs":["Neanderthal genomes reveal how small their populations were. High-quality genomes from the Altai and Croatia contain long runs of identical DNA, signs of parents who were related, and point to a far smaller effective population than that of contemporary sapiens. A 2022 study of Chagyrskaya and Okladnikov caves in Siberia led by Laurits Skov identified 13 individuals, including a father and his teenage daughter, and suggested that communities numbered perhaps 10 to 20 people, with women moving between groups.","In 2024 a team led by Ludovic Slimak and Martin Sikora published the genome of ‘Thorin’, a Neanderthal found at Grotte Mandrin from 2015 onwards and dated by his archaeological context to roughly 50,000–42,000 years ago. His lineage had split from other late European Neanderthals about 105,000 years earlier and apparently exchanged no genes with them for some 50,000 years, even though they lived not far apart. The authors saw evidence of tiny, closed communities.","In 2019 Krist Vaesen and colleagues modelled such populations. Inbreeding, Allee effects (the difficulty of finding mates and cooperating when numbers are low) and random fluctuations in births and deaths could, they found, drive Neanderthals extinct within about 10,000 years with no help from Homo sapiens. Competition would only have hastened the end."]},{"heading":"Cold, ash and pathogens","paragraphs":["The Neanderthals’ last millennia were climatically violent. Greenland ice cores and European cave records show rapid swings between milder and harsher phases. In 2018 Michael Staubwasser and colleagues, using stalagmites from Romanian caves, linked cold, dry spells around 44,000 and 40,800 years ago to gaps in Neanderthal occupation along the Danube. Heinrich Stadial 4, when armadas of icebergs chilled the North Atlantic around 40,000 years ago, followed.","About 39,850 years ago the Campanian Ignimbrite eruption of the Phlegraean Fields near Naples, the largest in Europe for some 200,000 years, spread ash from Italy to Russia. It was once blamed for finishing the Neanderthals. But in 2012 John Lowe and colleagues traced microscopic ash from the eruption in cave and lake sequences and found that the Neanderthal decline and the arrival of sapiens in many regions came before it. The eruption and the cold that followed may have struck populations already in trouble.","Diseases may also have mattered. In 2019 Gili Greenbaum and colleagues modelled two species that each carried pathogens to which the other lacked immunity. The model explains why the contact zone could stall for thousands of years before sapiens, perhaps helped by immune genes gained through interbreeding, finally advanced."]},{"heading":"Absorbed, outcompeted or unlucky?","paragraphs":["Some Neanderthals did not so much vanish as merge. Every non-African today carries 1–2% Neanderthal DNA, mostly from mixing about 50,000–43,500 years ago. Fred Smith’s assimilation model, and later demographic models, describe a small population steadily absorbed into a larger one, sometimes called extinction by hybridisation. A 2026 study by Alexander Platt, Daniel Harris and Sarah Tishkoff argued that the mixing mainly involved Neanderthal men and sapiens women, which implies that many hybrid children grew up among sapiens.","The competition view stresses differences that could have favoured sapiens: larger and far-reaching social networks, visible in shells and stone moved hundreds of kilometres; a broader diet including small game, birds and fish; projectile weapons and, later, eyed needles for tailored clothing; and perhaps higher fertility. Critics such as Paola Villa and Wil Roebroeks (2014) argue that the archaeological record does not show Neanderthals to have been cognitively or technologically inferior, and that numbers, not intelligence, made the difference.","Most researchers now favour a combination: small, fragmented Neanderthal populations, stressed by climate swings, meeting a steady flow of more numerous newcomers with whom they competed and interbred. The genomes from Ranis and Bacho Kiro are a reminder that many early sapiens groups died out as well."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Who made the Châtelperronian and the Uluzzian?","views":[{"label":"Neanderthals and sapiens respectively","text":"Jean-Jacques Hublin, Frido Welker, Francesco d’Errico and João Zilhão point to Neanderthal bones and proteins in Châtelperronian layers at the Grotte du Renne and the Saint-Césaire skeleton. Stefano Benazzi and colleagues identify the Grotta del Cavallo teeth as Homo sapiens, making the Uluzzian a sapiens industry."},{"label":"Associations unproven","text":"Ofer Bar-Yosef, Jean-Guillaume Bordes and Brad Gravina argue that disturbed layers could have mixed Neanderthal bones with later tools. Zilhão and colleagues argue the Cavallo teeth may be intrusive, leaving the Uluzzian’s makers open; some suggest Neanderthals made it."}],"status":"Most researchers accept a Neanderthal association for the Châtelperronian and a sapiens one for the Uluzzian, but neither is beyond doubt. Ancient DNA recovered from the sediments of these layers, as achieved at other caves, could settle it."},{"question":"Did Neanderthals hold out in southern Iberia long after 40,000 years ago?","views":[{"label":"Late survival","text":"Clive Finlayson’s dates from Gorham’s Cave (about 28,000 radiocarbon years) and João Zilhão’s ‘Ebro frontier’, supported by Mousterian dates of about 37,000 years at Cueva Antón, suggest a southern refuge that outlasted Neanderthals elsewhere by several thousand years."},{"label":"No reliable late dates","text":"Rachel Wood, Tom Higham and colleagues found that ultrafiltered dates from southern Iberian Mousterian sites are much older; early Aurignacian dates at Lapa do Picareiro in Portugal show sapiens there by about 41,000 years ago."}],"status":"The weight of evidence has shifted against survival much later than 40,000 years ago, but the Murcia dates keep the question open. New dating of the Gorham’s Cave layers and sediment DNA from late Mousterian sites would help."},{"question":"What mainly caused the Neanderthals’ extinction?","views":[{"label":"Competition with Homo sapiens","text":"Larger sapiens populations, wider networks and broader diets let them outcompete Neanderthals for territory and prey, as argued by Paul Mellars and many others."},{"label":"Demographic fragility","text":"Genomes show tiny, inbred Neanderthal populations; Vaesen and colleagues’ models show such populations could collapse on their own within about 10,000 years."},{"label":"Climate and catastrophe","text":"Abrupt cold phases, Heinrich Stadial 4 and the Campanian Ignimbrite eruption stressed already small groups, as argued by Staubwasser and others."},{"label":"Assimilation","text":"Fred Smith and others see a gradual absorption through interbreeding, supported by the Neanderthal DNA in living people."}],"status":"Few researchers now defend a single cause. The emerging consensus is that small, fragmented Neanderthal populations, hit by climatic stress, were overwhelmed demographically by a larger sapiens population with which they competed and interbred."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"End of the Mousterian","value":"About 41,000–39,000 years ago (Higham et al. 2014)"},{"label":"Overlap with Homo sapiens","value":"About 2,600–5,400 years across Europe, region by region"},{"label":"Campanian Ignimbrite eruption","value":"About 39,850 years ago, Phlegraean Fields, Italy"},{"label":"Thorin’s lineage","value":"Isolated for about 50,000 years (Slimak et al. 2024)"},{"label":"Neanderthal DNA today","value":"About 1–2% of non-African genomes"}],"siteIds":["grotte-mandrin","grotte-du-renne","saint-cesaire","grotta-del-cavallo","gorhams-cave","cueva-anton","campi-flegrei","spy-cave","vindija-cave"],"routes":[],"themes":["species","genetics","climate","technology","ornament","society"],"sources":[{"cite":"Higham, T. et al. (2014). The timing and spatiotemporal patterning of Neanderthal disappearance. Nature 512, 306–309."},{"cite":"Slimak, L. et al. (2024). Long genetic and social isolation in Neanderthals before their extinction. Cell Genomics 4."},{"cite":"Djakovic, I., Key, A. & Soressi, M. (2022). Optimal linear estimation models predict 1400–2900 years of overlap between Homo sapiens and Neandertals prior to their disappearance from France and northern Spain. Scientific Reports 12."},{"cite":"Welker, F. et al. (2016). Palaeoproteomic evidence identifies archaic hominins associated with the Châtelperronian at the Grotte du Renne. PNAS 113."},{"cite":"Benazzi, S. et al. (2011). Early dispersal of modern humans in Europe and implications for Neanderthal behaviour. Nature 479, 525–528."},{"cite":"Wood, R. E. et al. (2013). Radiocarbon dating casts doubt on the late chronology of the Middle to Upper Palaeolithic transition in southern Iberia. PNAS 110."},{"cite":"Vaesen, K., Scherjon, F., Hemingway, L. & Verpoorte, A. (2019). Inbreeding, Allee effects and stochasticity might be sufficient to account for Neanderthal extinction. PLOS ONE 14."},{"cite":"Lowe, J. et al. (2012). Volcanic ash layers illuminate the resilience of Neanderthals and early modern humans to natural hazards. PNAS 109."},{"cite":"Greenbaum, G. et al. (2019). Disease transmission and introgression can explain the long-lasting contact zone of modern humans and Neanderthals. Nature Communications 10."},{"cite":"Villa, P. & Roebroeks, W. (2014). Neandertal demise: an archaeological analysis of the modern human superiority complex. PLOS ONE 9."}]},{"id":"aurignacian-art","order":21,"act":"one-species","title":"Ivory, Flutes and Painted Lions","subtitle":"The Aurignacian and Europe’s first figurative art","era":"Early Upper Palaeolithic (Aurignacian)","start":43000,"end":30000,"dateLabel":"43,000–30,000 years ago","regions":["europe"],"focus":[-10,36,45,56],"emblem":"lionman","summary":"In the Swabian Jura and at Chauvet, Europe’s early Homo sapiens carved ivory animals and a lion-headed figure, played bone and ivory flutes and painted lions and rhinos by firelight. Discoveries in Sulawesi now show that such art was not a European invention.","paragraphs":[["In September 2008, in the Hohle Fels cave in southwest Germany, Nicholas Conard’s team lifted six fragments of mammoth ivory from the lowest Upper Palaeolithic layer.","Fitted together, they formed a woman six centimetres tall, with huge breasts, a carefully carved pubic triangle and, instead of a head, a small ring for hanging on a cord.","She is at least 40,000 years old."],["As the Neanderthals faded, the newcomers to Europe made art of a kind never seen there before.","In the valleys of the Swabian Jura they carved horses, mammoths, cave lions and a human figure with a lion’s head, and they played flutes made from the wing bones of vultures and swans and from hollowed ivory, the oldest known musical instruments.","Archaeologists call their culture the Aurignacian, after a cave at Aurignac in the French Pyrenees foothills."],["A few thousand years later, on the walls of Chauvet Cave in southern France, artists drew prides of lions, charging rhinoceroses and herds of horses with a confidence that astonished everyone when the cave was found in 1994.","For a century such art was taken as proof of a creative explosion unique to Europe.","Painted animals in Sulawesi, older still, have ended that idea.","What remains open is what the images meant to the people who made them."]],"sentences":["In September 2008, in the Hohle Fels cave in southwest Germany, Nicholas Conard’s team lifted six fragments of mammoth ivory from the lowest Upper Palaeolithic layer.","Fitted together, they formed a woman six centimetres tall, with huge breasts, a carefully carved pubic triangle and, instead of a head, a small ring for hanging on a cord.","She is at least 40,000 years old.","As the Neanderthals faded, the newcomers to Europe made art of a kind never seen there before.","In the valleys of the Swabian Jura they carved horses, mammoths, cave lions and a human figure with a lion’s head, and they played flutes made from the wing bones of vultures and swans and from hollowed ivory, the oldest known musical instruments.","Archaeologists call their culture the Aurignacian, after a cave at Aurignac in the French Pyrenees foothills.","A few thousand years later, on the walls of Chauvet Cave in southern France, artists drew prides of lions, charging rhinoceroses and herds of horses with a confidence that astonished everyone when the cave was found in 1994.","For a century such art was taken as proof of a creative explosion unique to Europe.","Painted animals in Sulawesi, older still, have ended that idea.","What remains open is what the images meant to the people who made them."],"pronounce":{"Hohle Fels":"Hoh-leh Fels","Aurignacian":"Or-in-yay-shun","Aurignac":"Or-in-yak","Chauvet":"Show-vay"},"body":[{"heading":"The Swabian Jura: the oldest figurines","paragraphs":["Caves in two small valleys of southwest Germany, the Ach and the Lone, have produced several dozen ivory figurines from Aurignacian layers dated by radiocarbon to between about 43,000 and 35,000 years ago. In 2017 six of these caves were inscribed together as the UNESCO World Heritage Site “Caves and Ice Age Art in the Swabian Jura”.","At Vogelherd, excavated by Gustav Riek in 1931, the finds included a small horse, mammoths and lions; in 2006 Nicholas Conard’s team, sieving the old spoil heaps, found a complete little mammoth. At Hohlenstein-Stadel in 1939, Robert Wetzel and Otto Völzing recovered hundreds of ivory fragments that were recognised as a figure only three decades later. Reassembled by Joachim Hahn in 1969 and again in 2012–2013 with newly excavated pieces, the Lion Man (Löwenmensch) stands 31.1 centimetres tall: an upright body with a cave lion’s head, carved from a mammoth tusk about 40,000 years ago. Experimental carving by Wulf Hein suggests it took hundreds of hours of skilled work.","The Hohle Fels figure, published by Conard in Nature in 2009, is the oldest undisputed depiction of a human body. The first report gave a minimum age of 35,000 years; later radiocarbon work on the basal Aurignacian there places it at about 40,000 years or more."]},{"heading":"The first music","paragraphs":["In the same layer at Hohle Fels, excavators found a nearly complete flute made from the wing bone of a griffon vulture, 21.8 centimetres long with five finger holes, along with fragments of ivory flutes. Geißenklösterle produced flutes of swan bone and of mammoth ivory; making an ivory flute meant splitting a curved piece of tusk, hollowing both halves and fitting them back together airtight. Radiocarbon dating by Tom Higham, Nicholas Conard and colleagues in 2012 placed the start of the Aurignacian at Geißenklösterle at about 43,000–42,000 years ago. Replicas play a range of notes, and music, presumably with song and dance, was part of life from the beginning of the Upper Palaeolithic."]},{"heading":"Chauvet","paragraphs":["On 18 December 1994 three cavers, Jean-Marie Chauvet, Éliette Brunel and Christian Hillaire, entered a sealed cave above the Ardèche gorge near Vallon-Pont-d’Arc. Inside were hundreds of animal images, among them horses, aurochs, bison, reindeer and cave bears, but above all animals rarely painted elsewhere: cave lions, woolly rhinoceroses and mammoths. The artists shaded with charcoal, scraped walls white before drawing, used bulges in the rock to give bodies volume and repeated outlines to suggest movement. A hanging rock bears the lower body of a woman merged with a bison.","The first radiocarbon dates, on charcoal from the drawings themselves, were far older than style suggested. Christian Züchner, and Paul Pettitt with Paul Bahn, argued that such mastery belonged later, in the Gravettian or Solutrean, and that the samples might be contaminated. The answer came from sheer weight of evidence. In 2016 Anita Quiles, Jean-Michel Geneste and colleagues combined 259 radiocarbon dates on charcoal, bones and drawings into a model with two phases of activity, about 37,000–33,500 and 31,000–28,000 years ago, while cosmogenic chlorine-36 dating by Benjamin Sadier and colleagues (2012) showed that rockfalls had closed the entrance by about 21,000 years ago. Chauvet was inscribed on the UNESCO list in 2014; visitors see a full-scale replica."]},{"heading":"Fumane and El Castillo","paragraphs":["At Grotta di Fumane in the Lessini hills north of Verona, stones that fell from the cave roof into Aurignacian layers of around 40,000 years ago carry red ochre paintings, including an animal and a figure with horn-like projections sometimes called the ‘shaman’. The same layers hold hundreds of perforated marine shells carried from the coast. Fumane’s older Neanderthal layers contain bird bones cut to remove large feathers, a hint that Neanderthals too decorated their bodies.","In northern Spain, Alistair Pike and colleagues in 2012 dated calcite crusts over paintings in eleven caves by the uranium-series method. A red disk in El Castillo came out at a minimum of 40,800 years and a hand stencil there at least 37,300. That is at or before the arrival of Homo sapiens in Cantabria, so the authors raised the possibility of Neanderthal painters. In 2018 Dirk Hoffmann and colleagues reported minimum ages above 64,000 years for a ladder-like red sign at La Pasiega, a hand stencil at Maltravieso and red-painted flowstone at Ardales, which would make them Neanderthal work."]},{"heading":"Beads, points and early genomes","paragraphs":["The Aurignacian toolkit, with blades, thick ‘carinated’ pieces that served as cores for tiny bladelets, and split-based points of antler, spread across Europe between about 43,000 and 35,000 years ago. Its makers wore ornaments in quantity: pierced teeth, shells and ivory beads. Randall White has shown that basket-shaped ivory beads from southwest France were made in standardised stages, and shells travelled hundreds of kilometres inland. In 2006 Marian Vanhaeren and Francesco d’Errico mapped regional bead styles that may reflect distinct social or linguistic groups.","Genomes show who these people were. Kostenki 14 from the Don in Russia, about 37,000 years old, already belonged to the western Eurasian branch, separate from East Asians (Seguin-Orlando and colleagues, 2014). An individual from Goyet in Belgium, about 35,000 years old, carries ancestry that resurfaced in western Europe after the Ice Age, and a woman from Peștera Muierii in Romania, of similar age, shows high genetic diversity. The earlier pioneers of Bacho Kiro and Oase contributed little to any of them."]},{"heading":"What was the art for?","paragraphs":["Interpretations have followed the intellectual fashions of their times. Around 1900 Salomon Reinach and the Abbé Henri Breuil read cave art as hunting magic: painting an animal, sometimes pierced by spears, would ensure success in the hunt. Yet in many caves the animals painted are not those most eaten, and at Chauvet predators dominate.","In the 1960s André Leroi-Gourhan and Annette Laming-Emperaire proposed a structuralist reading: decorated caves were organised spaces in which horses and bison, linked to male and female principles, formed the central pairing, with other animals at the margins. The scheme was influential but rested on Leroi-Gourhan’s own classifications and fits poorly with later, fully recorded caves such as Chauvet.","From the 1980s David Lewis-Williams, later joined by Jean Clottes, argued that much cave imagery drew on altered states of consciousness reached through dance, rhythm or sensory deprivation: geometric signs resemble the patterns people see in early trance, and composite figures such as the Lion Man reflect experiences of transformation in shamanic ritual. Critics including Paul Bahn and Patricia Helvenston object that ‘shamanism’ is defined too loosely, that the neuropsychological model is hard to test, and that it transplants southern African ethnography onto Ice Age Europe.","Other approaches look at context. Iegor Reznikoff and later acoustic surveys found that images cluster where caves resonate or echo. Hand stencils and finger flutings show that children, some very young, took part. Many researchers see the images as ways of storing and passing on knowledge of animals, seasons and territories; a 2023 proposal by Bennett Bacon and colleagues that dots and lines beside animals record a seasonal calendar remains contested. Most accept that no single explanation fits 30,000 years of art across a continent."]},{"heading":"Not a European miracle","paragraphs":["For much of the twentieth century Aurignacian art was treated as evidence of a sudden ‘creative explosion’ in Europe about 40,000 years ago. Indonesia changed that. In 2014 Maxime Aubert and colleagues dated a hand stencil in Maros, Sulawesi, to at least 39,900 years and a painted babirusa to at least 35,400; later work in Borneo (at least 40,000 years) and Sulawesi pushed painted animals back to at least 45,500 years, a narrative scene to 51,200 and a hand stencil on Muna to 67,800 (chapter 19). Together with engraved ochre and shell beads in Africa more than 70,000 years old, these finds suggest that the capacity for art travelled with Homo sapiens out of Africa, and that where it survives depends largely on where rock and climate preserved it."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Did Neanderthals paint in the caves of Spain?","views":[{"label":"Yes","text":"Dirk Hoffmann, Alistair Pike, João Zilhão and colleagues argue that uranium-series minimum ages of more than 64,000 years at La Pasiega, Maltravieso and Ardales, long before Homo sapiens reached Iberia, can only mean Neanderthal artists, consistent with Neanderthal use of pigments, shells and feathers."},{"label":"Not proven","text":"Maxime Aubert, Adam Brumm and Jillian Huntley (2018), and separately Randall White and colleagues (2020), argue that uranium can leach from thin crusts, making them look too old; that some samples may not overlie deliberate marks; and that the ‘art’ is non-figurative red pigment whose human origin is uncertain in places."}],"status":"Open. The Ardales pigments are widely accepted as deliberate, but whether the dates are reliable and whether all the marks are Neanderthal work is still argued. Repeat sampling by independent labs and new dating methods such as laser-ablation uranium-series mapping may resolve it."},{"question":"Was Chauvet really painted more than 30,000 years ago?","views":[{"label":"Aurignacian and Gravettian phases","text":"Jean Clottes, Jean-Michel Geneste, Anita Quiles and colleagues point to hundreds of consistent radiocarbon dates on drawings, torch marks and bones, and to rockfalls that sealed the cave by about 21,000 years ago."},{"label":"Later, on stylistic grounds","text":"Christian Züchner, Paul Pettitt and Paul Bahn argued in the late 1990s and 2000s that the refined style matched later art and that samples could have been contaminated or that artists used old charcoal."}],"status":"Resolved for most researchers since the 2016 chronological model: the first phase is about 37,000–33,500 years old. The lesson is that ‘primitive’ style is not a guide to age."},{"question":"What did Palaeolithic art mean?","views":[{"label":"Hunting magic","text":"Salomon Reinach and Henri Breuil saw images as spells to secure prey; animals marked with spear-like lines seemed to support this."},{"label":"Structural order","text":"André Leroi-Gourhan saw a mythic system of paired animals and signs arranged by cave topography."},{"label":"Shamanism and altered states","text":"David Lewis-Williams and Jean Clottes relate signs and composite beings to trance experience and ritual journeys into the underworld."},{"label":"Social information and experience","text":"Others emphasise communication of knowledge, group identity, acoustics and the participation of children and whole communities."}],"status":"No single theory dominates. Most researchers now study particular caves and objects in context, and accept that meanings varied across tens of thousands of years and hundreds of communities."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Oldest figurative carvings","value":"Swabian Jura ivories, about 40,000 years or more"},{"label":"Oldest musical instruments","value":"Bird-bone and ivory flutes, about 43,000–40,000 years"},{"label":"Lion Man","value":"31.1 cm, mammoth ivory, about 40,000 years"},{"label":"Chauvet’s phases","value":"About 37,000–33,500 and 31,000–28,000 years ago"},{"label":"El Castillo red disk","value":"At least 40,800 years (uranium-series)"},{"label":"World Heritage","value":"Chauvet 2014; Swabian Jura caves 2017"}],"siteIds":["hohle-fels","hohlenstein-stadel","vogelherd","geissenklosterle","chauvet-cave","grotta-di-fumane","el-castillo","kostenki","goyet-caves","pestera-muierii"],"routes":[],"themes":["art","music","ornament","technology","genetics","society"],"sources":[{"cite":"Conard, N. J. (2009). A female figurine from the basal Aurignacian of Hohle Fels Cave in southwestern Germany. Nature 459, 248–252."},{"cite":"Conard, N. J., Malina, M. & Münzel, S. C. (2009). New flutes document the earliest musical tradition in southwestern Germany. Nature 460, 737–740."},{"cite":"Higham, T. et al. (2012). Testing models for the beginnings of the Aurignacian and the advent of figurative art and music: the radiocarbon chronology of Geißenklösterle. Journal of Human Evolution 62."},{"cite":"Quiles, A. et al. (2016). A high-precision chronological model for the decorated Upper Paleolithic cave of Chauvet-Pont d’Arc, Ardèche, France. PNAS 113."},{"cite":"Pike, A. W. G. et al. (2012). U-series dating of Paleolithic art in 11 caves in Spain. Science 336, 1409–1413."},{"cite":"Hoffmann, D. L. et al. (2018). U-Th dating of carbonate crusts reveals Neandertal origin of Iberian cave art. Science 359, 912–915."},{"cite":"White, R. et al. (2020). Still no archaeological evidence that Neanderthals created Iberian cave art. Journal of Human Evolution 144."},{"cite":"Aubert, M. et al. (2014). Pleistocene cave art from Sulawesi, Indonesia. Nature 514, 223–227."},{"cite":"Seguin-Orlando, A. et al. (2014). Genomic structure in Europeans dating back at least 36,200 years. Science 346, 1113–1118."},{"cite":"Lewis-Williams, D. (2002). The Mind in the Cave: Consciousness and the Origins of Art. London: Thames & Hudson."}]},{"id":"north-asia","order":22,"act":"one-species","title":"Into North and East Asia","subtitle":"Siberia, Mongolia, China, Tibet, Japan and the Ryukyus","era":"Upper Palaeolithic (Initial Upper Palaeolithic to microblade traditions)","start":45000,"end":15000,"dateLabel":"45,000–15,000 years ago","regions":["asia"],"focus":[70,18,150,73],"emblem":"needle","summary":"Homo sapiens reached the Siberian Arctic, the Tibetan Plateau and the islands off East Asia long before the coldest phase of the last Ice Age. Genomes from Yana, Mal’ta and Tianyuan reveal lost populations whose descendants include Native Americans.","paragraphs":[["While ivory lions were being carved in Germany, other descendants of the great dispersal were pushing into the coldest and highest places people had ever lived.","By about 32,000 years ago, hunters were butchering mammoths and woolly rhinoceroses on the Yana River in Siberia, far above the Arctic Circle, where the sun does not rise for weeks in winter.","Two milk teeth they left behind have yielded the genomes of a people geneticists now call the Ancient North Siberians."],["Farther south, near Lake Baikal, a small boy buried at Mal’ta about 24,000 years ago carried ancestry that survives today in both Europeans and Native Americans.","Near Beijing, a man from Tianyuan Cave, 40,000 years old, belonged to the ancestral line of East Asians, yet shared an unexpected genetic link with a few peoples of the Amazon."],["Others climbed.","At Nwya Devu, 4,600 metres up on the Tibetan Plateau, people were making stone blades between 40,000 and 30,000 years ago.","And others went to sea.","Obsidian from Kōzushima, an island south of Tokyo, reached the Japanese mainland about 38,000 years ago, and by about 35,000 years ago people had crossed to Okinawa.","In 2019 a Japanese team paddled a dugout canoe, hollowed out with replica stone axes, 225 kilometres from Taiwan to Yonaguni, to show how such a voyage could have been done."]],"sentences":["While ivory lions were being carved in Germany, other descendants of the great dispersal were pushing into the coldest and highest places people had ever lived.","By about 32,000 years ago, hunters were butchering mammoths and woolly rhinoceroses on the Yana River in Siberia, far above the Arctic Circle, where the sun does not rise for weeks in winter.","Two milk teeth they left behind have yielded the genomes of a people geneticists now call the Ancient North Siberians.","Farther south, near Lake Baikal, a small boy buried at Mal’ta about 24,000 years ago carried ancestry that survives today in both Europeans and Native Americans.","Near Beijing, a man from Tianyuan Cave, 40,000 years old, belonged to the ancestral line of East Asians, yet shared an unexpected genetic link with a few peoples of the Amazon.","Others climbed.","At Nwya Devu, 4,600 metres up on the Tibetan Plateau, people were making stone blades between 40,000 and 30,000 years ago.","And others went to sea.","Obsidian from Kōzushima, an island south of Tokyo, reached the Japanese mainland about 38,000 years ago, and by about 35,000 years ago people had crossed to Okinawa.","In 2019 a Japanese team paddled a dugout canoe, hollowed out with replica stone axes, 225 kilometres from Taiwan to Yonaguni, to show how such a voyage could have been done."],"pronounce":{"Mal’ta":"Mahl-tah","Tianyuan":"Tyen-yoo-en","Nwya Devu":"Nwee-yah Deh-voo","Kōzushima":"Koh-zoo-shee-mah","Yonaguni":"Yoh-nah-goo-nee","Yana":"Yah-nah"},"body":[{"heading":"The northern route: Altai and Mongolia","paragraphs":["In the mountains and steppes of southern Siberia and Mongolia, the Initial Upper Palaeolithic (IUP) appears abruptly: long blades struck from flat-faced cores, bone tools and ornaments. Kara-Bom in the Russian Altai, an open-air site excavated since the 1980s, is a reference sequence, with IUP layers dated to more than 40,000 years ago. At Tolbor-16 in northern Mongolia, Nicolas Zwyns and colleagues in 2019 described a blade industry dated to about 45,000 years ago, linking the Altai with northern China and supporting a “northern route” of Homo sapiens across the steppe. The nearby site of Tolbor-21 has yielded some of the earliest ornaments in the region.","Denisova Cave in the Altai, famous for Denisovan and Neanderthal remains (chapter 13), also holds Upper Palaeolithic layers. A 2019 chronology by Katerina Douka, Tom Higham and colleagues dated the earliest pendants of pierced animal teeth and bone points there to about 49,000–43,000 years ago. Who made them is unknown: no Homo sapiens bone of that age has been identified in the cave, and some researchers suggest Denisovans. Bone needles from the Upper Palaeolithic levels are among the oldest known."]},{"heading":"Above the Arctic Circle","paragraphs":["Yana RHS, the Rhinoceros Horn Site, lies on the lower Yana River at about 71° North. Found by Vladimir Pitulko in 2001, it has produced stone tools, beads and weapon foreshafts carved from woolly rhinoceros horn and mammoth ivory, dated to about 32,000 years ago. Life there, well before the Last Glacial Maximum, required tailored clothing, reliable shelter and hunting skills fit for Arctic winters.","In 2019 Martin Sikora, Eske Willerslev and colleagues sequenced two milk teeth from Yana. The children belonged to a previously unknown population, the Ancient North Siberians, an early offshoot of the western Eurasian branch that split away soon after western and eastern Eurasians separated. Over the following millennia, related people mixed with groups of East Asian ancestry, forming populations that contributed to the ancestors of both later Siberians and Native Americans."]},{"heading":"Mal’ta and the Ancient North Eurasians","paragraphs":["At Mal’ta, on the Belaya River near Lake Baikal, Mikhail Gerasimov excavated from 1928 a settlement with dwelling traces, about 30 ivory figurines of women, and carved ivory birds in flight, probably swans or geese. A boy aged about four had been buried with an ivory diadem, a bead necklace and a bird-shaped pendant about 24,000 years ago.","In 2014 Maanasa Raghavan, Eske Willerslev and colleagues sequenced his genome. MA-1, as he is known, belonged to a population now called the Ancient North Eurasians, closely related to western Eurasians yet contributing an estimated 14–38% of the ancestry of Native Americans. Ancient North Eurasian ancestry later reached Europe too, largely through Bronze Age steppe populations, so many Europeans carry some. A genome from Afontova Gora near Krasnoyarsk, about 17,000 years old, showed that this population persisted in south-central Siberia through the Last Glacial Maximum."]},{"heading":"China: Tianyuan, Zhoukoudian and Xiamabei","paragraphs":["Tianyuan Cave, a few kilometres from the famous Zhoukoudian fossil hill near Beijing, yielded in 2003 a partial skeleton of a man who lived about 40,000 years ago; isotopes in his bones show he ate much freshwater fish. Genetic studies led by Qiaomei Fu and Melinda Yang placed him on the lineage leading to present-day East Asians and Native Americans, but also found that he shares extra ancestry with Goyet Q116-1 in Belgium and with some Amazonian groups such as the Suruí and Karitiana, a sign of complex population structure in Pleistocene Asia.","The Upper Cave at Zhoukoudian, excavated in 1933–1934, held the remains of several people with ornaments of pierced animal teeth, shells and fish bones, and a bone needle, dated to roughly 35,000–30,000 years ago. The original fossils vanished in 1941 along with the Peking Man collection.","At Shuidonggou in Ningxia, blades like those of the Altai and Mongolia appear about 41,000 years ago; later layers contain ostrich-eggshell beads. At Xiamabei in the Nihewan Basin of Hebei, however, a 2022 Nature study led by Fa-Gang Wang, Shixia Yang, Francesco d’Errico and Michael Petraglia described a different toolkit about 40,000 years old: miniature blades and flakes, some bearing residues from hafting, and an area where two kinds of ochre had been processed on a limestone slab, the earliest known ochre processing in East Asia. China’s Upper Palaeolithic was a patchwork of traditions, not a single wave."]},{"heading":"The roof of the world","paragraphs":["In 2018 Xiaoling Zhang and colleagues reported Nwya Devu, 4,600 metres above sea level in the interior of the Tibetan Plateau, with a blade industry dated by optically stimulated luminescence to between 40,000 and 30,000 years ago. It is by far the highest Palaeolithic site of its age. Whether people lived there all year or visited in summer is unclear. Denisovans had reached the plateau’s edge much earlier at Baishiya Karst Cave, and a Denisovan version of the EPAS1 gene later helped Tibetans cope with thin air.","An older and more speculative claim comes from Chusang, a travertine hot spring at about 4,270 metres. In 2021 David Zhang, Matthew Bennett and colleagues reported five handprints and five footprints pressed into once-soft travertine, dated by uranium-series to 226,000–169,000 years ago, and argued that their deliberate arrangement, probably by children, makes them the oldest known ‘parietal art’. Others see playful or incidental marks; the makers may have been Denisovans."]},{"heading":"Japan and the Ryukyus","paragraphs":["The Japanese archipelago has one of the densest Palaeolithic records in the world, with thousands of sites that begin abruptly about 38,000 years ago. Early assemblages include edge-ground stone axes, among the oldest outside Australia, and on Honshu hunters dug rows of pit traps across animal trails, as at Hatsunegahara in Shizuoka. Obsidian from Kōzushima, one of the Izu Islands, reached sites around Tokyo from about 38,000 years ago: proof of repeated return voyages across open sea, since the island was never joined to the mainland.","The Ryukyu Islands, too, were always islands. People reached Okinawa by about 35,000 years ago. At Sakitari Cave, Masaki Fujita and colleagues found fishhooks made from sea-snail shell about 23,000 years old, among the oldest known, along with evidence of seasonal meals of freshwater crabs. At Shiraho-Saonetabaru on Ishigaki, discovered during airport construction, excavators recovered the remains of many individuals, including one of the oldest near-complete skeletons in East Asia, about 27,000 years old, laid in a tightly flexed position.","How did they get there? From 2016 Yousuke Kaifu and colleagues tested Palaeolithic-style craft. Rafts of reeds and of bamboo proved too slow or fragile to cross the Kuroshio, one of the world’s strongest currents. In July 2019 a crew of five paddled a dugout canoe, hollowed from a cedar log with replica stone axes, 225 kilometres from Taiwan to Yonaguni in about 45 hours, navigating without instruments. The results, published in 2025, show that the crossing was feasible with a dugout and skilled paddlers, although no Palaeolithic boat has ever been found."]},{"heading":"Microblades and the coldest millennia","paragraphs":["As the Last Glacial Maximum approached, a new technology spread across North and East Asia: microblades, tiny standardised blades pressed from carefully shaped wedge-shaped cores and set in rows into slotted points of bone or antler. They appear in northern China by roughly 28,000–26,000 years ago on current dating, and then across Siberia, Mongolia, Korea and Hokkaido. Light, replaceable and economical with scarce stone, they suited highly mobile hunters in cold, open landscapes, and the tradition later travelled through Beringia into Alaska (chapter 27)."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Does the Initial Upper Palaeolithic mark a dispersal of Homo sapiens?","views":[{"label":"A population on the move","text":"Nicolas Zwyns, Steven Kuhn and others see the same blade-making method appearing in quick succession from the Levant to Bulgaria, the Altai, Mongolia and northern China, matching the ages of early sapiens such as Ust’-Ishim and the Bacho Kiro genomes with their East Asian links."},{"label":"Ideas travelling, not only people","text":"Others point out that most IUP sites lack human remains, that similar methods can arise by convergence or spread through contact, and that Denisovans or Neanderthals may have adopted or even contributed to some assemblages, as the uncertain makers of Denisova Cave’s ornaments show."}],"status":"Most researchers accept that the IUP broadly tracks early sapiens, but its spread was probably uneven, with regional variants and some local involvement. Human remains or sediment DNA from IUP layers in the Altai, Mongolia and China would be decisive."},{"question":"Who made the early Upper Palaeolithic ornaments at Denisova Cave?","views":[{"label":"Homo sapiens","text":"The ornaments’ age, about 49,000–43,000 years, overlaps the spread of sapiens through northern Asia, and similar objects elsewhere are associated with sapiens."},{"label":"Denisovans","text":"Only Denisovan and Neanderthal remains are known from the cave, and some researchers, including members of the Russian excavation team, have argued that Denisovans made the ornaments and bone tools."}],"status":"Open. The makers could be identified by human fossils from the relevant layers or by more detailed DNA analysis of the sediments."},{"question":"How did people reach the Ryukyu Islands?","views":[{"label":"Dugout canoes","text":"Yousuke Kaifu’s team argues that only a dugout, paddled by an experienced crew who understood the Kuroshio, could reliably make the crossing from Taiwan, as their 2019 voyage demonstrated."},{"label":"Other craft or other routes","text":"Sceptics note that an experiment proves possibility, not practice; that no Palaeolithic boats survive; and that people may also have island-hopped south from Kyushu, where some crossings are shorter."}],"status":"The Ryukyus were certainly reached by deliberate voyages about 35,000 years ago. The type of boat and the direction of arrival remain uncertain; ancient genomes from Shiraho-Saonetabaru may show where the islanders came from."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Arctic occupation","value":"Yana RHS, about 32,000 years ago, at about 71° North"},{"label":"Ancient North Eurasians","value":"Mal’ta boy MA-1, about 24,000 years"},{"label":"High-altitude site","value":"Nwya Devu, 4,600 m, 40,000–30,000 years ago"},{"label":"Early ochre processing","value":"Xiamabei, China, about 40,000 years"},{"label":"Among the oldest fishhooks","value":"Sakitari Cave, Okinawa, about 23,000 years"},{"label":"Dugout test voyage","value":"Taiwan to Yonaguni, 225 km in about 45 hours (2019)"}],"siteIds":["denisova-cave","tolbor-16","yana-rhs","malta","afontova-gora","tianyuan-cave","zhoukoudian-upper-cave","shuidonggou","xiamabei","kozushima","sakitari-cave","shiraho-saonetabaru"],"routes":[{"id":"ryukyu-crossing","label":"Crossing from Taiwan into the Ryukyus","start":36000,"end":30000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[121.2,22.8],[123,24.47],[124.2,24.4],[125.3,24.8],[127.7,26.2]]},{"id":"kozushima-voyages","label":"Obsidian voyages to Kōzushima","start":38000,"end":20000,"certainty":"established","path":[[138.95,34.75],[139.14,34.21]]}],"themes":["migration","genetics","seafaring","technology","ornament","burial","climate"],"sources":[{"cite":"Zwyns, N. et al. (2019). The Northern Route for human dispersal in Central and Northeast Asia: new evidence from the site of Tolbor-16, Mongolia. Scientific Reports 9, 11759.","doi":"10.1038/s41598-019-47972-1"},{"cite":"Douka, K. et al. (2019). Age estimates for hominin fossils and the onset of the Upper Palaeolithic at Denisova Cave. Nature 565, 640–644."},{"cite":"Pitulko, V. V. et al. (2004). The Yana RHS site: humans in the Arctic before the Last Glacial Maximum. Science 303, 52–56."},{"cite":"Sikora, M. et al. (2019). The population history of northeastern Siberia since the Pleistocene. Nature 570, 182–188."},{"cite":"Raghavan, M. et al. (2014). Upper Palaeolithic Siberian genome reveals dual ancestry of Native Americans. Nature 505, 87–91."},{"cite":"Yang, M. A. et al. (2017). 40,000-year-old individual from Asia provides insight into early population structure in Eurasia. Current Biology 27."},{"cite":"Wang, F.-G. et al. (2022). Innovative ochre processing and tool use in China 40,000 years ago. Nature 603, 284–289."},{"cite":"Zhang, X. L. et al. (2018). The earliest human occupation of the high-altitude Tibetan Plateau 40 thousand to 30 thousand years ago. Science 362, 1049–1051."},{"cite":"Fujita, M. et al. (2016). Advanced maritime adaptation in the western Pacific coastal region extends back to 35,000–30,000 years before present. PNAS 113."}]},{"id":"gravettian-lgm","order":23,"act":"one-species","title":"The Gravettian and the Ice Maximum","subtitle":"Mammoth hunters, “Venus” figurines and the coldest millennia","era":"Mid Upper Palaeolithic (Gravettian, Solutrean, Epigravettian)","start":34000,"end":19000,"dateLabel":"34,000–19,000 years ago","regions":["europe"],"focus":[-10,36,45,58],"emblem":"venus","summary":"From Portugal to the Russian Plain, Gravettian hunters shared weapons, figurines and lavish burial customs while belonging to genetically distinct populations. Then the Last Glacial Maximum drove Europe’s people into southern refuges, where the Solutreans made their laurel-leaf points.","paragraphs":[["About 30,000 years ago, in the Pavlov Hills of Moravia, someone modelled a small woman from wet loess and set her in a fire.","She came out hard, the oldest known figure of fired clay.","Around her camp lay thousands of fragments of clay animals, many cracked and burst by the heat, perhaps on purpose."],["Archaeologists call this culture the Gravettian.","Its makers spread a shared set of weapons and a shared fashion in art from the Atlantic to the River Don.","They carved female figures in ivory and stone, from Willendorf in Austria to Kostenki in Russia.","They buried some of their dead with astonishing wealth: at Sungir, east of Moscow, two children lay head to head beneath some 10,000 ivory beads, each one cut and drilled by hand.","Yet ancient genomes show that beneath this common culture lived at least two distinct populations."],["Then the cold deepened.","From about 26,000 years ago, ice sheets spread across Scandinavia and the British Isles, sea level fell by more than 120 metres, and northern Europe emptied of people.","Human life drew back into southern refuges in Iberia, Italy, the Balkans and the plains north of the Black Sea.","In France and Spain, the Solutreans flaked stone points as thin as laurel leaves and sewed with fine eyed needles, and so came through the coldest millennia of the Ice Age."]],"sentences":["About 30,000 years ago, in the Pavlov Hills of Moravia, someone modelled a small woman from wet loess and set her in a fire.","She came out hard, the oldest known figure of fired clay.","Around her camp lay thousands of fragments of clay animals, many cracked and burst by the heat, perhaps on purpose.","Archaeologists call this culture the Gravettian.","Its makers spread a shared set of weapons and a shared fashion in art from the Atlantic to the River Don.","They carved female figures in ivory and stone, from Willendorf in Austria to Kostenki in Russia.","They buried some of their dead with astonishing wealth: at Sungir, east of Moscow, two children lay head to head beneath some 10,000 ivory beads, each one cut and drilled by hand.","Yet ancient genomes show that beneath this common culture lived at least two distinct populations.","Then the cold deepened.","From about 26,000 years ago, ice sheets spread across Scandinavia and the British Isles, sea level fell by more than 120 metres, and northern Europe emptied of people.","Human life drew back into southern refuges in Iberia, Italy, the Balkans and the plains north of the Black Sea.","In France and Spain, the Solutreans flaked stone points as thin as laurel leaves and sewed with fine eyed needles, and so came through the coldest millennia of the Ice Age."],"pronounce":{"Gravettian":"Grav-et-ee-an","Sungir":"Soon-gheer","Solutreans":"So-loo-tree-anz","Kostenki":"Kos-tyen-kee","Willendorf":"Vil-en-dorf"},"body":[{"heading":"One culture, several peoples","paragraphs":["The Gravettian, named after La Gravette in the Dordogne, flourished from about 33,000 to 26,000 years ago. Its hallmark is the Gravette point, a narrow blade with one edge blunted by steep retouch, probably used as a projectile tip; in central and eastern Europe shouldered points were common. Gravettian people hunted mammoth, reindeer and horse, trapped hares and foxes for fur, made cordage and nets, and gathered in large seasonal camps on river terraces and loess plains.","In 2023 a Nature study led by Cosimo Posth, He Yu and Johannes Krause, analysing genomes of 356 hunter-gatherers (116 of them new), showed that the Gravettian was not one people. Individuals from France and Iberia formed a ‘Fournol’ cluster; those from Moravia and Italy a ‘Věstonice’ cluster. A shared culture had spread across genetically distinct populations. After the glacial maximum, Fournol-related ancestry persisted in the southwest, while Věstonice ancestry disappeared, even from Italy, once assumed to have sheltered it."]},{"heading":"Dolní Věstonice, Pavlov and Předmostí","paragraphs":["Beneath the Pavlov Hills in south Moravia, excavations since the 1920s by Karel Absolon, Bohuslav Klíma, Jiří Svoboda and others uncovered camps with hearths, huts and heaps of mammoth bone. The Věstonice Venus, 11 centimetres tall and found in 1925, is made of fired loess, and the sites have yielded thousands of fragments of fired clay figures of animals and people. Pamela Vandiver, Olga Soffer and colleagues argued in 1989 that many were put into the fire wet so that they would crack or explode, perhaps in some kind of performance or divination. These people made ceramics but not pots.","Clay fragments from Pavlov I and Dolní Věstonice also carry impressions of cordage, netting and plain-woven textiles, identified by Soffer, James Adovasio and David Hyland. Clothes, bags and nets of plant fibre, rarely preserved, were part of Gravettian life.","In 1986 excavators uncovered a triple burial at Dolní Věstonice: three young people side by side, their heads covered in red ochre, the middle one with a malformed skeleton. That individual was long interpreted as a woman; genetic sexing published in 2016 showed all three were male. At Předmostí near Přerov, Karel Maška excavated in 1894 a mass grave of about 20 people amid the bones of more than 1,000 mammoths; most of the human remains were destroyed by fire at Mikulov Castle in 1945. Large canid skulls from Předmostí feature in the debate on the first dogs (chapter 24)."]},{"heading":"Figures of women","paragraphs":["Gravettian ‘Venus’ figurines, a nineteenth-century name now used with caution, occur from the Pyrenees to the Don. The Venus of Willendorf, 11 centimetres of limestone once coloured with red ochre, was found in 1908 in the Wachau valley of Austria and is about 30,000 years old. In 2022 a micro-CT study led by Gerhard Weber compared its oolitic stone with samples across Europe and found the closest match near Lake Garda in northern Italy, implying that the figure or its stone travelled hundreds of kilometres across or around the Alps.","Other famous examples are the ivory figure from Lespugue in the French Pyrenees, with swelling breasts and buttocks; the relief of a woman holding a horn at Laussel in the Dordogne; the tiny ivory head with a grid-patterned hood or hairstyle from Brassempouy; and series of ivory and marl figures from Kostenki on the Don and Avdeevo near Kursk. Not all are obese or pregnant: they range from slender young women to heavy mature bodies, and several wear caps, belts or bands that Soffer and colleagues identified in 2000 as depictions of woven clothing.","What they meant is debated. Many writers saw fertility charms or a Great Mother goddess, a view Marija Gimbutas extended to later prehistory. In 1996 LeRoy McDermott argued that their distortions match a woman’s view of her own body, making them self-representations. A 2020 study led by physician Richard Johnson linked the most obese figures to times and places closest to the ice sheets, suggesting that fat bodies symbolised survival. Others see idealised images of health and maturity, or objects with many uses; their variety makes a single meaning unlikely."]},{"heading":"Burials rich and strange","paragraphs":["At Sungir, on the Klyazma River near Vladimir in Russia, Otto Bader’s excavations from 1955 revealed an adult man and, in a separate grave, two children laid head to head, all about 34,000 years old and belonging to an early Upper Palaeolithic culture just before the Gravettian. The man wore about 3,000 mammoth-ivory beads, probably sewn onto clothing, and some 25 ivory bracelets; the children had around 10,000 beads between them, carved ivory animals and spears of straightened mammoth tusk more than 2 metres long. Experiments by Randall White suggest that each bead took most of an hour to make, so the beads alone represent thousands of hours of work.","A 2017 study in Science led by Martin Sikora and Eske Willerslev sequenced four Sungir individuals and found that they were not close kin: the two children were not siblings. Their genetic diversity implied that small bands belonged to wider mating networks that avoided inbreeding, much as among recent hunter-gatherers.","In Liguria, the cave of Arene Candide held ‘Il Principe’, a youth of about 15 buried around 28,000 years ago with a cap of hundreds of perforated shells, red ochre, four ‘bâtons percés’ of elk antler and a long flint blade in his hand; injuries to his jaw and shoulder may have killed him. At Krems-Wachtberg in Austria, two newborns about 30,000 years old were buried under a mammoth shoulder blade with ivory beads; in 2020 ancient DNA showed they were identical twins, the oldest known, one dying at birth and the other at about seven weeks."]},{"heading":"The Last Glacial Maximum","paragraphs":["From about 26,500 to 19,000 years ago the world’s ice sheets reached their greatest extent. The Fennoscandian ice sheet covered Scandinavia, the Baltic and parts of Germany, Poland and Russia, and met the British–Irish ice sheet in the northern North Sea; the Alps and Pyrenees carried ice caps. Global sea level fell by about 120–130 metres, exposing Doggerland and the northern Adriatic. Much of the land between the ice and the Mediterranean was polar desert or dry steppe-tundra, with permafrost reaching deep into western and central Europe.","People withdrew from the north for thousands of years; Britain and the north European plain appear empty. Populations survived in refugia: southwest France and northern Spain, the rest of Iberia, Italy, the Balkans and parts of the Ukrainian and Russian plains, where Epigravettian groups kept hunting mammoth. Genetic data show a fall in population and the loss of some lineages, including the Věstonice cluster."]},{"heading":"The Solutrean","paragraphs":["In France and Iberia the Solutrean, named after the Roche de Solutré in Burgundy, flourished from about 25,000 to 20,000 years ago. Its masterpieces are bifacial ‘laurel-leaf’ points, some so large and thin, like those in a cache found at Volgu, that they can hardly have been practical. Solutrean knappers heated flint to make it flake more predictably and refined pressure flaking. Eyed bone needles for close-fitting clothing appear in western Europe at this time, and the cave of Parpalló near Valencia yielded thousands of engraved and painted stone plaquettes.","In 2012 Dennis Stanford and Bruce Bradley set out the ‘Solutrean hypothesis’: that Solutrean people followed the edge of the North Atlantic sea ice, hunting seals, and founded the Clovis culture of North America. Most researchers reject it. Clovis appears about 13,000 years ago, several thousand years after the Solutrean ended; shared features of bifacial flaking arose independently in many places; no Solutrean artefacts or art have been found in America; and ancient genomes from the Americas, beginning with the Clovis-associated Anzick child in 2014, show descent from East Asian and Ancient North Eurasian ancestors with no trace of an Ice Age European source."]},{"heading":"Houses of mammoth bone","paragraphs":["On the plains of Russia and Ukraine, where trees were scarce, people built with mammoth bone. At Kostenki 11 on the Don, Alexander Pryor and colleagues in 2020 described a circular structure about 12.5 metres across made from the bones of more than 60 mammoths, dated to about 25,000 years ago. Charcoal shows that conifer wood was burned, and charred plant remains hint at plant foods; the structure may have served for storage or gatherings rather than simply as a dwelling. Better-known mammoth-bone houses at Mezhyrich and Mezin in Ukraine are later, around 15,000 years old."]}],"debates":[{"question":"What were the ‘Venus’ figurines for?","views":[{"label":"Fertility and goddess","text":"Early scholars and later Marija Gimbutas saw the emphasis on breasts, bellies and hips as celebrating fertility or a mother goddess."},{"label":"Self-representation","text":"LeRoy McDermott (1996) argued that the proportions match what a woman sees looking down at her own body, so women may have made them."},{"label":"Survival in the cold","text":"Richard Johnson and colleagues (2020) found the most obese figures closest to the ice sheets and argued that they idealised the body fat needed to survive hard times."},{"label":"Many meanings","text":"Olga Soffer, James Adovasio, Sarah Nelson and others stress the range of ages, bodies and clothing and see social roles, status or group identity rather than a single symbol."}],"status":"No consensus. The figurines span thousands of kilometres and years, and most researchers now avoid a single explanation. Careful study of context, wear and manufacture, as with Willendorf’s stone, is adding more than new theories."},{"question":"Did Solutreans cross the Atlantic to America?","views":[{"label":"Yes, along the ice edge","text":"Dennis Stanford and Bruce Bradley argued that similarities between Solutrean and Clovis bifaces, and a few early east-coast American finds, reflect Solutrean seafarers who followed the sea-ice edge."},{"label":"No","text":"Lawrence Straus, David Meltzer and Ted Goebel (2005) and later Michael O’Brien and colleagues point to a gap of several thousand years, the lack of Solutrean art or tools in America, convergent technology, and genomes that trace Native Americans to Asia."}],"status":"Rejected by nearly all specialists. Ancient DNA from the Americas has made the hypothesis very hard to sustain."},{"question":"Why were the Sungir children buried with such wealth?","views":[{"label":"Inherited status","text":"Because young children could hardly have earned such honours, some scholars see the beads and spears as signs of status passed down within families, an early form of social ranking."},{"label":"Special individuals and ritual","text":"Vincenzo Formicola (2007) noted that many rich Upper Palaeolithic burials, including Sungir’s, involved people with unusual bodies or conditions; Erik Trinkaus and Alexandra Buzhilova (2018) described abnormalities in one of the children and suggested special treatment of exceptional individuals."}],"status":"Open. Burials this rich are rare, which argues against routine hereditary ranking but does not exclude it. Genomes and isotopes from more burials will help show whether wealth followed kinship."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Gravettian","value":"About 33,000–26,000 years ago, from Portugal to the Don"},{"label":"Oldest fired-clay figure","value":"Věstonice Venus, about 30,000 years, 11 cm"},{"label":"Sungir beads","value":"About 13,000 ivory beads in two graves, about 34,000 years ago"},{"label":"Last Glacial Maximum","value":"About 26,500–19,000 years ago; sea level about 120 m lower"},{"label":"Kostenki 11 ring","value":"About 12.5 m across, bones of more than 60 mammoths, about 25,000 years"},{"label":"Gravettian genomes","value":"Fournol and Věstonice clusters (Posth et al. 2023)"}],"siteIds":["dolni-vestonice","predmosti","willendorf","krems-wachtberg","sungir","kostenki","arene-candide","brassempouy","lespugue","laussel","solutre"],"routes":[],"themes":["art","ornament","burial","genetics","climate","settlement","technology","society"],"sources":[{"cite":"Posth, C. et al. (2023). Palaeogenomics of Upper Palaeolithic to Neolithic European hunter-gatherers. Nature 615, 117–126."},{"cite":"Sikora, M. et al. (2017). Ancient genomes show social and reproductive behavior of early Upper Paleolithic foragers. Science 358, 659–662."},{"cite":"Weber, G. W. et al. (2022). The microstructure and the origin of the Venus from Willendorf. Scientific Reports 12."},{"cite":"Pryor, A. J. E. et al. (2020). The chronology and function of a new circular mammoth-bone structure at Kostenki 11. Antiquity 94."},{"cite":"Teschler-Nicola, M. et al. (2020). Ancient DNA reveals monozygotic newborn twins from the Upper Palaeolithic. Communications Biology 3."},{"cite":"Soffer, O., Adovasio, J. M. & Hyland, D. C. (2000). The “Venus” figurines: textiles, basketry, gender, and status in the Upper Paleolithic. Current Anthropology 41."},{"cite":"Vandiver, P. B., Soffer, O., Klíma, B. & Svoboda, J. (1989). The origins of ceramic technology at Dolní Věstonice, Czechoslovakia. Science 246, 1002–1008."},{"cite":"Straus, L. G., Meltzer, D. J. & Goebel, T. (2005). Ice Age Atlantis? Exploring the Solutrean–Clovis ‘connection’. World Archaeology 37."},{"cite":"Johnson, R. J. et al. (2021). Upper Paleolithic figurines showing women with obesity may represent survival symbols of climatic change. Obesity 29."},{"cite":"Clark, P. U. et al. (2009). The Last Glacial Maximum. Science 325, 710–714."}]},{"id":"dogs","order":24,"act":"one-species","title":"Wolf into Dog","subtitle":"The first domestic animal: timing, place and debates","era":"Upper Palaeolithic to Early Holocene","start":35000,"end":10000,"dateLabel":"35,000–10,000 years ago","regions":["europe","asia","americas"],"focus":[-15,25,160,78],"emblem":"dog","summary":"Dogs are the only animal domesticated by hunter-gatherers. Ancient genomes, burials and a 2026 record from Anatolia show how Ice Age wolves became dogs, while where it happened, and why, are still argued over.","paragraphs":[["About 14,200 years ago, on a hillside above the Rhine, a group of hunters buried two of their own: a man of about forty and a young woman.","Beside them they laid a dog.","It was only about seven months old, and it had been desperately ill.","Its teeth record a bout of canine distemper that normally kills within three weeks.","This puppy lived on for weeks more, which means someone kept it warm, clean and fed, long after it could be of any use."],["Dogs are the only animal that people domesticated while they still lived entirely by hunting and gathering.","Every other domestic animal came with farming, thousands of years later.","Genomes show that all dogs descend from a population of Ice Age wolves that has since vanished, and that the ancestors of dogs split from other wolves somewhere between about 40,000 and 20,000 years ago.","Where the taming happened is still argued over.","Siberia, during the coldest millennia of the last glaciation, is a leading candidate, and the genes point broadly to eastern Eurasia, with western wolves adding their share later."],["In 2026, ancient DNA pushed the oldest confirmed dog back to 15,800 years ago: a pup from Pınarbaşı, a hunters’ rock shelter in central Anatolia.","By 14,000 years ago, closely related dogs lived from Anatolia to southern England.","Candidates twice as old, from caves in Belgium and Moravia, remain disputed."],["How a rival predator became a companion is the deepest puzzle.","Perhaps the boldest wolves tamed themselves at the edge of human camps.","Perhaps people raised pups on winter meat they could not eat themselves.","Quite possibly, both."]],"sentences":["About 14,200 years ago, on a hillside above the Rhine, a group of hunters buried two of their own: a man of about forty and a young woman.","Beside them they laid a dog.","It was only about seven months old, and it had been desperately ill.","Its teeth record a bout of canine distemper that normally kills within three weeks.","This puppy lived on for weeks more, which means someone kept it warm, clean and fed, long after it could be of any use.","Dogs are the only animal that people domesticated while they still lived entirely by hunting and gathering.","Every other domestic animal came with farming, thousands of years later.","Genomes show that all dogs descend from a population of Ice Age wolves that has since vanished, and that the ancestors of dogs split from other wolves somewhere between about 40,000 and 20,000 years ago.","Where the taming happened is still argued over.","Siberia, during the coldest millennia of the last glaciation, is a leading candidate, and the genes point broadly to eastern Eurasia, with western wolves adding their share later.","In 2026, ancient DNA pushed the oldest confirmed dog back to 15,800 years ago: a pup from Pınarbaşı, a hunters’ rock shelter in central Anatolia.","By 14,000 years ago, closely related dogs lived from Anatolia to southern England.","Candidates twice as old, from caves in Belgium and Moravia, remain disputed.","How a rival predator became a companion is the deepest puzzle.","Perhaps the boldest wolves tamed themselves at the edge of human camps.","Perhaps people raised pups on winter meat they could not eat themselves.","Quite possibly, both."],"pronounce":{"Pınarbaşı":"Puh-nar-bah-shuh","Moravia":"Mo-ray-vee-uh"},"body":[{"heading":"A predator at the hearth","paragraphs":["Every farm animal was domesticated by farmers or herders within roughly the last 11,000 years. The dog is the exception. It was domesticated by hunter-gatherers during the last Ice Age, thousands of years before anyone planted a crop, and it remains the only large carnivore people have ever domesticated. That makes it a double puzzle: grey wolves hunted the same prey as people and were dangerous rivals, yet some of them became partners in hunting, hauling, guarding and companionship.","Genetics has reframed the question. All dogs share an ancestry distinct from every living wolf population. Dogs and modern wolves are sister branches of Late Pleistocene wolf populations, and the particular wolves that gave rise to dogs seem to have died out. Finding where and when domestication happened therefore means sequencing ancient wolves as well as ancient dogs."]},{"heading":"Clocks in the genome","paragraphs":["Early genetic estimates, based on an assumed mutation rate, placed the split between dogs and wolves at around 16,000–11,000 years ago. In 2015 Pontus Skoglund, Love Dalén and colleagues sequenced a wolf rib from the Taimyr Peninsula in Arctic Siberia, radiocarbon dated to about 35,000 years ago. The Taimyr wolf belonged to a population that branched off very close to the moment when the lines leading to dogs and to modern wolves separated, so it acted as a calibration point: the ancestors of dogs had split from other wolves by 27,000–40,000 years ago. A population split is not the same as domestication, though. The lineage leading to dogs may have been a distinct wolf population for thousands of years before it had anything to do with people.","In 2020 Anders Bergström, Pontus Skoglund, Greger Larson and colleagues analysed 27 ancient dogs and found that by about 11,000 years ago at least five distinct lineages existed: in the Levant, in Karelia in northeastern Europe, around Lake Baikal, in the Americas, and one leading to the New Guinea singing dog and the dingo. After that, little wolf DNA entered dogs.","A 2022 follow-up in Nature by the same group sequenced 72 ancient wolves spanning 100,000 years. Dogs proved closer to ancient wolves from eastern Eurasia than to western ones, but early dogs in the Near East and Africa carried up to half of their ancestry from a separate source related to southwest Eurasian wolves. None of the ancient wolves sampled matched either source directly: the progenitor populations are still missing."]},{"heading":"Siberia, and other candidates","paragraphs":["In 2021 the zooarchaeologist Angela Perri, with Greger Larson, David Meltzer and others, combined dog genetics, human genetics and archaeology into a specific scenario. During the Last Glacial Maximum, about 26,000–19,000 years ago, people and wolves in Siberia were squeezed into the same pockets of habitable land. The authors proposed that dogs were domesticated there by about 23,000 years ago, and that dogs later travelled with the people who crossed Beringia into the Americas.","The weakness of the hypothesis is that no dog bones of that age have been found in Siberia; the case rests on genetic dates. Others have favoured Europe, the Middle East, Central Asia or southern East Asia, and a 2016 study led by Laurent Frantz argued for two domestications, one in the west and one in the east. The wolf genomes of 2022 favour one main eastern origin with later mixing, and they explain why the answer has been so slippery: Late Pleistocene wolves formed a single, well-connected population across the northern hemisphere, so regional genetic differences are small."]},{"heading":"Palaeolithic dogs? Goyet and Předmostí","paragraphs":["Bones offer older candidates. In 2009 Mietje Germonpré and colleagues identified a canid skull from Goyet Cave in Belgium, about 36,000 years old, as a “Palaeolithic dog” because of its short, broad snout. In 2012 they described similar skulls from Předmostí in Moravia, a Gravettian mammoth-hunters’ site roughly 31,000 years old; one had a fragment of bone wedged between its front teeth, which they read as placed there after death. A 33,000-year-old skull from Razboinichya Cave in the Altai was proposed in 2011 as an “incipient dog”.","Most specialists remain unconvinced. Pleistocene wolves varied more than modern ones, and a 2016 critique by Angela Perri, titled “A wolf in dog’s clothing”, argued that without a proper picture of that variation such skulls cannot be classed as dogs. The Goyet animal’s mitochondrial DNA falls outside all modern dog lineages, and no genome has yet confirmed a dog older than about 16,000 years. A 2020 dental microwear study at Předmostí found that the two skull types had eaten differently, which fits the idea that one group lived off human leftovers but does not prove domestication. Many now see them as unusual wolves, or “protodogs” that left no descendants."]},{"heading":"The first confirmed dogs","paragraphs":["In March 2026 two papers in Nature transformed the record. A team led by William Marsh and Lachie Scarsbrook, with Laurent Frantz and Greger Larson, sequenced canids from Pınarbaşı, a hunter-gatherer rock shelter in central Anatolia, and from Gough’s Cave in southern England. A Pınarbaşı pup dated to about 15,800 years ago is the oldest genetically confirmed dog, roughly 5,000 years older than the previous record. The dogs there were buried and apparently fed fish. The Gough’s Cave dog, about 14,300 years old, was strikingly similar genetically, as was a dog from Kesslerloch in Switzerland.","The second team, led by Anders Bergström, analysed 216 dogs and wolves from Europe and neighbouring regions and confirmed the Kesslerloch animal, dated to about 14,200 years ago, as a dog. Europe’s Palaeolithic dogs came from the same eastern wolf source as all others, with small additions from western wolves, so they do not represent a separate domestication. Dogs that lived in Europe before farming still account for roughly half the ancestry of many popular European breeds.","Burials tell the same story from another angle. At Bonn-Oberkassel in Germany, quarry workers in 1914 found the grave of a man of about 40 and a woman of about 25, with red ochre, carved bone and antler objects and two dogs, dated to about 14,200 years ago. A 2018 re-study led by Luc Janssens showed that the younger dog died at about 28 weeks, after severe distemper between about 19 and 23 weeks of age. Without nursing, such a dog dies within about three weeks. In the Levant, at the Natufian village of ʿAin Mallaha, a woman buried about 12,000 years ago lay with her hand on a puppy three to five months old (chapter 29 describes the Natufians).","Skulls agree. A 2025 study in Science led by Allowen Evin compared hundreds of canid skulls spanning 50,000 years and found clearly domestic forms by about 11,000 years ago at Veretye in northern Russia, with variety in size and shape growing through the Holocene."]},{"heading":"Dogs on the move","paragraphs":["Dogs spread with people. On Zhokhov Island in the Siberian Arctic, a camp about 9,500 years old has yielded parts of sledges and many dog bones. In 2020 Mikkel Sinding and colleagues sequenced a Zhokhov dog: modern Siberian huskies, Alaskan malamutes and Greenland sled dogs share most of their ancestry with it, along with traces from Pleistocene Siberian wolves. A 2025 study of 73 ancient dog genomes from eastern Eurasia found that dogs moved with hunter-gatherers, farmers and later pastoralists.","In the Americas, the oldest directly dated dogs come from Koster and Stilwell II in Illinois, about 10,000 years old and buried individually, as Angela Perri and colleagues reported in 2019. Older ties are hinted at in Alaska: a canid from Swan Point, dated to between about 12,900 and 11,300 years ago, had eaten salmon, which local wolves rarely caught, suggesting that people fed it. It matches no known dog lineage and may have been a tamed wolf (François Lanoë and colleagues, 2024). After 1492 the original American dogs were almost entirely replaced by European breeds; a 2018 study found that their closest surviving genetic relative is a transmissible cancer that still passes between dogs worldwide."]},{"heading":"How a wolf becomes a dog","paragraphs":["The self-domestication model, developed by Raymond and Lorna Coppinger and popularised by Brian Hare and Vanessa Woods as “survival of the friendliest”, holds that the least fearful wolves began to exploit human camps, and that natural selection for tolerance of people did most of the work before humans took over breeding. Selection on behaviour did leave marks in the genome: in 2017 Bridgett vonHoldt and colleagues linked dogs’ hypersociability to variants in genes that, in humans, are involved in Williams–Beuren syndrome. Critics object that mobile Ice Age hunters left little refuse; a 2025 computer model by David Elzinga and colleagues found that self-domestication could nevertheless have run fast enough.","Other scenarios put people in charge from the start, with hunters raising pups taken from dens. A 2021 hypothesis by Maria Lahtinen and colleagues adds an ecological twist. Humans cannot live on lean meat alone, because the liver can safely process only limited protein; in a northern winter, when fat and plant foods were scarce, hunters would have had surplus lean meat. Feeding it to captive wolves cost nothing. The ideas are not exclusive: wolves may have come close on their own terms before people began to raise and select them."]}],"debates":[{"question":"When were dogs domesticated?","views":[{"label":"Early, before the glacial maximum","text":"Mietje Germonpré and colleagues argue that skulls from Goyet (about 36,000 years), Předmostí (about 31,000) and Razboinichya Cave (about 33,000) belong to early dogs, pointing to their short snouts and crowded teeth. Genetic estimates that place the split between dog ancestors and other wolves at 27,000–40,000 years ago leave room for such an early start."},{"label":"During or after the glacial maximum","text":"Angela Perri, Greger Larson and many geneticists argue that the population split need not mark domestication. Their Siberian scenario puts it around 23,000 years ago, and the oldest genetically confirmed dogs (Pınarbaşı, about 15,800 years; Gough’s Cave and Kesslerloch, about 14,300–14,200) are much younger than the disputed skulls."}],"status":"In 2026 the firm lower limit is about 15,800 years ago, and the close similarity of dogs from Anatolia to England shows that domestication must have begun well before that. Genomes from dogs older than 20,000 years, or from the missing progenitor wolves, would settle it; the Goyet and Předmostí skulls are widely regarded as unusual wolves or failed early relationships."},{"question":"Where were dogs domesticated, and how many times?","views":[{"label":"One eastern origin","text":"The 2022 ancient-wolf study by Anders Bergström and colleagues found dogs closer to ancient eastern Eurasian wolves, and the 2021 synthesis by Angela Perri placed the process in Siberia. The 2026 European genomes show that even the oldest European dogs came from this eastern source."},{"label":"Two origins or major western input","text":"Laurent Frantz and colleagues argued in 2016 for separate domestications in western and eastern Eurasia. The 2022 study found that early dogs in the Near East and Africa carry up to half of their ancestry from southwest Eurasian wolves, which could mean an independent domestication there or heavy mixing with local wolves."}],"status":"Most researchers now favour one main domestication in the east with later wolf admixture in the west, but because the source wolf populations have not been sampled, a second, partly independent domestication cannot be ruled out."},{"question":"Did wolves domesticate themselves?","views":[{"label":"Self-domestication","text":"Raymond and Lorna Coppinger, and later Brian Hare and Vanessa Woods, argue that bold wolves scavenging around camps were naturally selected for friendliness. A 2025 agent-based model by David Elzinga and colleagues found the process could have been fast enough."},{"label":"Human-driven taming","text":"Others argue that hunters deliberately raised wolf pups. Maria Lahtinen and colleagues (2021) proposed that surplus lean meat in harsh winters let people feed young wolves at no cost to themselves."}],"status":"The two ideas are not mutually exclusive and are hard to test with bones. Most researchers expect a gradual process in which a commensal phase was followed by active human control."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Oldest genetically confirmed dog","value":"Pınarbaşı, Türkiye, about 15,800 years ago (reported 2026)"},{"label":"Split of dog ancestors from other wolves","value":"By about 27,000–40,000 years ago (Taimyr wolf, 2015)"},{"label":"Dog lineages by 11,000 years ago","value":"At least five (Bergström and colleagues, 2020)"},{"label":"Oldest dog in a human grave","value":"Bonn-Oberkassel, Germany, about 14,200 years ago"},{"label":"Oldest sled dogs","value":"Zhokhov Island, Siberian Arctic, about 9,500 years ago"}],"siteIds":["goyet-caves","predmosti","pinarbasi","goughs-cave","kesslerloch","bonn-oberkassel","ain-mallaha","swan-point","koster","zhokhov-island"],"routes":[],"themes":["animals","genetics","burial","society","migration"],"sources":[{"cite":"Skoglund, P. et al. (2015). Ancient wolf genome reveals an early divergence of domestic dog ancestors and admixture into high-latitude breeds. Current Biology 25, 1515–1519."},{"cite":"Bergström, A. et al. (2020). Origins and genetic legacy of prehistoric dogs. Science 370, 557–564.","doi":"10.1126/science.aba9572"},{"cite":"Bergström, A. et al. (2022). Grey wolf genomic history reveals a dual ancestry of dogs. Nature 607, 313–320.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-022-04824-9"},{"cite":"Perri, A.R. et al. (2021). Dog domestication and the dual dispersal of people and dogs into the Americas. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 118."},{"cite":"Janssens, L. et al. (2018). A new look at an old dog: Bonn-Oberkassel reconsidered. Journal of Archaeological Science 92."},{"cite":"Lahtinen, M. et al. (2021). Excess protein enabled dog domestication during severe Ice Age winters. Scientific Reports 11, 7.","doi":"10.1038/s41598-020-78214-4"},{"cite":"Lanoë, F.B. et al. (2024). Late Pleistocene onset of mutualistic human/canid (Canis spp.) relationships in subarctic Alaska. Science Advances.","doi":"10.1126/sciadv.ads1335"},{"cite":"Evin, A. et al. (2025). The emergence and diversification of dog morphology. Science.","doi":"10.1126/science.adt0995"},{"cite":"Marsh, W.A., Scarsbrook, L. et al. (2026). Dogs were widely distributed across western Eurasia during the Palaeolithic. Nature 651, 995–1003.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-026-10170-x"},{"cite":"Bergström, A., Furtwängler, A., Johnston, S. et al. (2026). Genomic history of early dogs in Europe. Nature 651, 986–994.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-026-10112-7"}]},{"id":"magdalenian","order":25,"act":"one-species","title":"The Magdalenians","subtitle":"Lascaux, Altamira and the resettling of northern Europe","era":"Upper Palaeolithic (Magdalenian)","start":21000,"end":14000,"dateLabel":"21,000–14,000 years ago","regions":["europe"],"focus":[-10,36,25,56],"emblem":"bison","summary":"As the ice began to retreat, Magdalenian hunters painted Lascaux and Altamira, carved spear-throwers and harpoons, and pushed back into northern Europe, until a warming climate and a new population transformed the continent around 14,000 years ago.","paragraphs":[["On the twelfth of September 1940, four teenagers wriggled down a hole on a wooded hill above the Vézère river in southwestern France.","By lamplight they found themselves among painted animals: black aurochs more than 5 metres long, red cows, galloping horses, stags with branching antlers.","They had found Lascaux."],["Its painters belonged to the Magdalenian culture, which flourished across western Europe between roughly 21,000 and 14,000 years ago, as the great ice sheets began to shrink.","They hunted reindeer and horses, made harpoons, eyed needles and spear-throwers carved with animals, and returned to the same deep caves for generations.","At Altamira in northern Spain, a girl named María spotted painted bison on a cave ceiling in 1879.","The experts called them a fake, and it took more than twenty years for the leading sceptic to apologise in print."],["Magdalenian life had a darker side as well.","In Gough’s Cave in southern England, about 14,700 years ago, people cleaned human skulls and trimmed them into cups, and cut marks show that bodies were eaten, probably as part of how the dead were mourned."],["Then the climate lurched.","About 14,700 years ago, Greenland warmed dramatically within a few decades.","Forests crept north, and the reindeer herds followed the retreating tundra.","Ancient DNA shows that new people, with roots in Italy and the Balkans, then spread across the continent and largely replaced the Magdalenians.","Their descendants would become the hunters, fishers and gatherers of Europe’s Mesolithic."]],"sentences":["On the twelfth of September 1940, four teenagers wriggled down a hole on a wooded hill above the Vézère river in southwestern France.","By lamplight they found themselves among painted animals: black aurochs more than 5 metres long, red cows, galloping horses, stags with branching antlers.","They had found Lascaux.","Its painters belonged to the Magdalenian culture, which flourished across western Europe between roughly 21,000 and 14,000 years ago, as the great ice sheets began to shrink.","They hunted reindeer and horses, made harpoons, eyed needles and spear-throwers carved with animals, and returned to the same deep caves for generations.","At Altamira in northern Spain, a girl named María spotted painted bison on a cave ceiling in 1879.","The experts called them a fake, and it took more than twenty years for the leading sceptic to apologise in print.","Magdalenian life had a darker side as well.","In Gough’s Cave in southern England, about 14,700 years ago, people cleaned human skulls and trimmed them into cups, and cut marks show that bodies were eaten, probably as part of how the dead were mourned.","Then the climate lurched.","About 14,700 years ago, Greenland warmed dramatically within a few decades.","Forests crept north, and the reindeer herds followed the retreating tundra.","Ancient DNA shows that new people, with roots in Italy and the Balkans, then spread across the continent and largely replaced the Magdalenians.","Their descendants would become the hunters, fishers and gatherers of Europe’s Mesolithic."],"pronounce":{"Vézère":"Vay-zair","Lascaux":"Lass-coh","Altamira":"Al-tah-mee-rah","Magdalenian":"Mag-duh-lee-nee-un"},"body":[{"heading":"Heirs of the glacial refuges","paragraphs":["The Magdalenian is named after La Madeleine, a rock shelter in the Vézère valley of the Dordogne, which Gabriel de Mortillet used in 1869 to define the last great culture of the Upper Palaeolithic. It took shape in southwestern France and northern Spain, where people had sheltered through the coldest part of the Last Glacial Maximum (chapter 23), and by about 16,000 years ago it stretched from Portugal to southern Poland. An early phase called the Badegoulian is sometimes counted as its first stage and sometimes as a separate culture.","Magdalenian groups hunted reindeer, horse, red deer, ibex, saiga antelope and bison, snared birds and hares and caught salmon. Many followed seasonal rounds and gathered at favoured places; in 1980 Margaret Conkey proposed that Altamira was one such aggregation site. Ancient DNA identifies who they were: a 2023 study led by Cosimo Posth found that Magdalenian individuals belong to a genetic cluster named after a skeleton from Goyet in Belgium (“GoyetQ2”), descended from western European Gravettian people who had survived the glacial maximum in Iberia and southwestern France before spreading north and east."]},{"heading":"Lascaux","paragraphs":["On 12 September 1940 Marcel Ravidat, aged 18, returned with three younger friends, Jacques Marsal, Georges Agnel and Simon Coencas, to a hole on a hill above Montignac. Inside was a cave decorated with some 600 paintings and drawings and around 1,500 engravings. In the Hall of the Bulls, huge black aurochs, the largest more than 5 metres long, stride among red cows, horses and stags beside a strange spotted beast nicknamed the “unicorn”. At the bottom of a shaft lies the most enigmatic scene: a stick-like man with a bird’s head falls backwards before a bison whose entrails spill from a wound, a bird perches on a staff beside him, and a woolly rhinoceros walks away. It has been read as a hunting accident, a shaman’s trance and a myth.","Dating the paintings is hard, because Lascaux’s blacks are mostly manganese oxides that contain no carbon. Radiocarbon dates on charcoal and on an antler point from the floor have long suggested about 18,600–17,000 years ago. In 2019 Sylvain Ducasse and Mathieu Langlais re-dated bone and antler objects from the cave and argued that the main visits were earlier, in the Badegoulian.","Lascaux opened to the public in 1948. Visitors’ breath, lights and humidity brought carbon dioxide, algae and calcite growth, and in 1963 the culture minister André Malraux closed it. The public now visits replicas, Lascaux II (1983) and Lascaux IV at Montignac (2016), while the original, hit by fungal outbreaks since 2001, is closely monitored."]},{"heading":"Altamira and the sceptics","paragraphs":["Altamira, in Cantabria, was found by a local hunter, Modesto Cubillas, around 1868. The landowner, Marcelino Sanz de Sautuola, an amateur prehistorian, dug in its entrance, and in 1879 his daughter María, then about eight, looked up in a low side chamber and saw painted animals on the ceiling. Sautuola published them in 1880 as prehistoric, but the International Congress of prehistorians meeting in Lisbon that year dismissed them as too fresh and too accomplished; some suspected forgery. Sautuola died in 1888 without vindication. Only after painted and engraved caves were accepted in France, at La Mouthe, Les Combarelles and Font-de-Gaume, did the leading sceptic, Émile Cartailhac, publish his “Mea culpa d’un sceptique” in 1902.","The Polychrome Ceiling holds some two dozen bison in red and black, several curled over natural bulges of rock that give them volume. Radiocarbon dates on charcoal in the paint place them in the Magdalenian, roughly 17,000 years ago. Uranium-series dating of calcite crusts, published in 2012 by Alistair Pike and colleagues, showed that a red sign on the same ceiling is at least 35,600 years old, so the chamber was decorated over some 20,000 years. The cave now admits only a handful of visitors a week; most people see a replica opened in 2001."]},{"heading":"Deep caves and open rocks","paragraphs":["Much of the finest Magdalenian art lies in the Pyrenees and the Dordogne. At Niaux in the Ariège, the Salon Noir, far inside the mountain, holds bison, horses and ibex drawn in bold black outline. Font-de-Gaume, near Les Eyzies, has dozens of polychrome bison and is one of the few original painted caves still open to small groups. In 1912 and 1914 the three sons of Count Henri Bégouën explored the Volp caves: in Le Tuc d’Audoubert they found two clay bison, each about 60 centimetres long, modelled against a rock deep underground, and in Les Trois-Frères the “Sorcerer”, an antlered figure with human legs, known mainly from a drawing by Henri Breuil that critics say tidied it up.","Art was not confined to caves. In the Côa Valley of northeastern Portugal, archaeologists in the early 1990s recorded thousands of animals engraved on schist outcrops along the river, spanning the Gravettian, the Magdalenian and later periods. A dam under construction would have drowned them; after a national campaign it was abandoned in 1995, and the valley became a World Heritage Site in 1998, joined in 2010 by Siega Verde across the border in Spain. Such open-air galleries hint at how much art has weathered away elsewhere. Britain’s only known Ice Age cave art, engraved figures in Church Hole at Creswell Crags, was recognised in 2003; flowstone over them gives a minimum age of about 12,800 years."]},{"heading":"Weapons, needles and engraved stones","paragraphs":["Magdalenian toolkits were built around bladelets, tiny blades glued into grooves in antler and bone points. Spear-throwers let hunters hurl darts harder and farther, and many were carved into sculptures: one from Le Mas d’Azil shows a young ibex looking back at a lump of dung on which birds perch. About 13,000 years ago, a carver at Montastruc near Bruniquel turned the tip of a mammoth tusk into two swimming reindeer, now in the British Museum. Late in the period, barbed antler harpoons appear, and eyed bone needles made close-fitting clothing routine.","At Gönnersdorf in the Rhineland, excavated from 1968 by Gerhard Bosinski, the floors of a camp roughly 16,000–15,000 years old were paved with slate slabs engraved with mammoths, horses, birds and many stylised female figures seen in profile. Such plaques show that images belonged to everyday life as well as to the deep caves."]},{"heading":"Counting the moons?","paragraphs":["Rows of dots and lines accompany many Ice Age animals. In 2023 a team led by Bennett Bacon, an independent researcher from London, with the Durham archaeologist Paul Pettitt and the psychologist Robert Kentridge, argued that sequences of up to 13 marks count lunar months from the start of spring, and that a Y-shaped sign means giving birth. In their reading, the counts beside horses, bison or deer match the months when those animals mated or gave birth, making the marks a phenological calendar and a form of proto-writing. Sceptics reply that the fit depends on assumed breeding seasons for Ice Age animals, on where the year is taken to begin and on which sequences are included, and that numbers from 1 to 13 can be made to fit many schemes."]},{"heading":"Skull cups and the treatment of the dead","paragraphs":["At Gough’s Cave in Cheddar Gorge, human bones about 14,700 years old carry cut marks, percussion damage and human tooth marks. In 2011 Silvia Bello, Simon Parfitt and Chris Stringer showed that three skulls, of two adults and a young child, had been carefully cleaned and trimmed into cups, and in 2017 Bello’s team described a human forearm bone engraved with a zigzag pattern. They interpret the evidence as funerary cannibalism, a ritual rather than a response to starvation. In 2023 William Marsh and Bello surveyed Magdalenian sites across Europe and concluded that cannibalism was a recurrent Magdalenian way of treating the dead, which vanished as people of different ancestry, who buried their dead, spread across Europe. A 2025 study of bones from Maszycka Cave in Poland, about 18,000 years old, argued that some Magdalenian cannibalism instead followed violent conflict."]},{"heading":"Warming and a new population","paragraphs":["Magdalenian groups had already pushed into land freed by the ice: Britain was resettled around 15,000 years ago, and reindeer hunters of the Hamburgian culture reached the North European Plain. About 14,700 years ago the Bølling–Allerød warming began (chapter 28). Greenland warmed by around 10 °C within decades, birch and pine spread north, and red deer, elk and aurochs replaced reindeer in much of the west. The Late Magdalenian gave way to the Azilian, named after Le Mas d’Azil and known for pebbles painted with red dots and stripes, and to the Federmesser groups of the north.","It was also a change of people. In 2016 Qiaomei Fu, David Reich and colleagues found that from about 14,000 years ago a new ancestry, named after a burial at Villabruna in the Italian Alps, spread across Europe; it was more closely related to Near Eastern populations than earlier Europeans had been. Posth’s 2023 study showed that people with this ancestry, linked to the Epigravettian of Italy and the Balkans, largely replaced the Magdalenian population in western and central Europe. The couple buried with dogs at Bonn-Oberkassel (chapter 24) already carried it, and their descendants became Europe’s Mesolithic hunter-gatherers (chapter 31)."]}],"debates":[{"question":"How old are the Lascaux paintings?","views":[{"label":"Classic Magdalenian","text":"The long-standing view, supported by researchers such as Norbert Aujoulat, rests on radiocarbon dates from charcoal and an antler point found on the cave floor, around 18,600–17,000 years ago, and on stylistic parallels with Magdalenian portable art."},{"label":"Earlier, Badegoulian","text":"In 2019 Sylvain Ducasse and Mathieu Langlais re-dated bone and antler objects from the cave and argued that the main human activity belongs to the Badegoulian, before the classic Magdalenian, so that the art may be older than usually thought."}],"status":"The mineral pigments cannot be radiocarbon dated, and objects on the floor may not date the paintings themselves. The question remains open; new methods for dating manganese pigments or calcite films over the images could settle it."},{"question":"Do dots and lines beside Ice Age animals record a lunar calendar?","views":[{"label":"A phenological calendar","text":"Bennett Bacon, Paul Pettitt, Robert Kentridge and colleagues (2023) argue that counts of up to 13 marks record lunar months since spring, and a Y sign births, matching the breeding cycles of the animals depicted."},{"label":"Unproven pattern-matching","text":"Critics among Palaeolithic art specialists note that the breeding seasons of Ice Age animals are assumed, that the starting point of the year is flexible, and that small numbers can be fitted to many schemes, so the correlation may be coincidental."}],"status":"The idea has been widely discussed but not widely accepted. An independent test on sequences not used to build the model would be the next step."},{"question":"Why did Magdalenians consume their dead?","views":[{"label":"Funerary ritual","text":"Silvia Bello, William Marsh and colleagues argue from the careful shaping of skull cups, the engraved bone and the absence of food stress at Gough’s Cave that cannibalism was a mortuary custom, repeated across Magdalenian sites."},{"label":"Violence or hunger","text":"Other researchers point to evidence of violent death at some sites, such as the 2025 reading of Maszycka Cave in Poland as the aftermath of conflict, and note that survival cannibalism is hard to exclude elsewhere."}],"status":"At Gough’s Cave the ritual interpretation is well supported. Across the Magdalenian as a whole, both practices may have occurred, and each site has to be judged on its own evidence."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Lascaux","value":"Found 12 September 1940; about 600 paintings and 1,500 engravings; closed to the public in 1963"},{"label":"Altamira","value":"Paintings spotted by María Sanz de Sautuola in 1879; accepted as genuine only in 1902"},{"label":"Oldest dated sign at Altamira","value":"At least 35,600 years old (uranium-series, 2012)"},{"label":"Gough’s Cave skull cups","value":"About 14,700 years old"},{"label":"Genetic turnover","value":"Villabruna ancestry spreads across Europe from about 14,000 years ago"}],"siteIds":["la-madeleine","lascaux","altamira","font-de-gaume","niaux","les-trois-freres","tuc-daudoubert","coa-valley","gonnersdorf","mas-dazil","goughs-cave","creswell-crags"],"routes":[],"themes":["art","technology","burial","genetics","climate","society"],"sources":[{"cite":"Fu, Q. et al. (2016). The genetic history of Ice Age Europe. Nature 534, 200–205."},{"cite":"Posth, C. et al. (2023). Palaeogenomics of Upper Palaeolithic to Neolithic European hunter-gatherers. Nature 615, 117–126."},{"cite":"Pike, A.W.G. et al. (2012). U-series dating of Paleolithic art in 11 caves in Spain. Science 336, 1409–1413."},{"cite":"Bello, S.M., Parfitt, S.A., Stringer, C.B. (2011). Earliest directly-dated human skull-cups. PLoS ONE 6(2), e17026."},{"cite":"Marsh, W.A., Bello, S. (2023). Cannibalism and burial in the late Upper Palaeolithic: combining archaeological and genetic evidence. Quaternary Science Reviews 319."},{"cite":"Bacon, B. et al. (2023). An Upper Palaeolithic proto-writing system and phenological calendar. Cambridge Archaeological Journal 33."},{"cite":"Ducasse, S., Langlais, M. (2019). Twenty years on, a new date with Lascaux: reassessing the chronology of the cave’s Paleolithic occupations through new 14C AMS dating. Paléo 30."},{"cite":"Cartailhac, É. (1902). La grotte d’Altamira, Espagne. Mea culpa d’un sceptique. L’Anthropologie 13."}]},{"id":"african-lsa","order":26,"act":"one-species","title":"Africa’s Later Stone Age","subtitle":"Beads, bows and notched bones from Border Cave to the Nile","era":"Later Stone Age; Aterian and Iberomaurusian","start":70000,"end":10000,"dateLabel":"70,000–10,000 years ago","regions":["africa"],"focus":[-18,-35,52,37],"emblem":"bow","summary":"Across Africa, tiny hafted blades, bows and poison, ostrich eggshell beads and painted stones mark the Later Stone Age, which began at different times in different regions. In the north, Iberomaurusian foragers ate enough acorns to rot their teeth, and on the Nile, Jebel Sahaba records recurring violence.","paragraphs":[["High on a cliff of the Lebombo Mountains, on today’s border between South Africa and Eswatini, lies Border Cave.","In layers between about 44,000 and 40,000 years old, archaeologists found a lump of beeswax mixed with plant resin, bone points for arrows, beads cut from ostrich eggshell and a baboon bone marked with a row of notches.","Many of these things would have been familiar to San hunters recorded in the nineteenth century, tens of thousands of years later."],["This is Africa’s Later Stone Age: a world of tiny stone blades set into composite tools, of bows and poisoned arrows, and of beads exchanged over hundreds of kilometres.","It did not begin everywhere at once.","In East Africa its hallmarks appear around 50,000 years ago and in southern Africa a little later, while in parts of West Africa older ways of making tools lasted until about 12,000 years ago."],["In Namibia, around 30,000 years ago, someone painted animals on stone slabs in a cave now called Apollo 11.","In Morocco, people who buried their dead at Taforalt ate so many acorns and pine nuts that about half of their teeth decayed.","And beside the Nile, at Jebel Sahaba, a cemetery of 61 people records wounds from arrows and spears, some healed and some fatal: the scars of conflict that returned again and again."],["Ancient genomes now show how these peoples were related, and how, after about 20,000 years ago, their worlds grew more local."]],"sentences":["High on a cliff of the Lebombo Mountains, on today’s border between South Africa and Eswatini, lies Border Cave.","In layers between about 44,000 and 40,000 years old, archaeologists found a lump of beeswax mixed with plant resin, bone points for arrows, beads cut from ostrich eggshell and a baboon bone marked with a row of notches.","Many of these things would have been familiar to San hunters recorded in the nineteenth century, tens of thousands of years later.","This is Africa’s Later Stone Age: a world of tiny stone blades set into composite tools, of bows and poisoned arrows, and of beads exchanged over hundreds of kilometres.","It did not begin everywhere at once.","In East Africa its hallmarks appear around 50,000 years ago and in southern Africa a little later, while in parts of West Africa older ways of making tools lasted until about 12,000 years ago.","In Namibia, around 30,000 years ago, someone painted animals on stone slabs in a cave now called Apollo 11.","In Morocco, people who buried their dead at Taforalt ate so many acorns and pine nuts that about half of their teeth decayed.","And beside the Nile, at Jebel Sahaba, a cemetery of 61 people records wounds from arrows and spears, some healed and some fatal: the scars of conflict that returned again and again.","Ancient genomes now show how these peoples were related, and how, after about 20,000 years ago, their worlds grew more local."],"pronounce":{"Lebombo":"Leh-bom-bo","Eswatini":"Ess-wah-tee-nee","Taforalt":"Tah-for-alt","Jebel Sahaba":"Jeh-bel Sah-hah-bah"},"body":[{"heading":"A patchwork transition","paragraphs":["Archaeologists recognise the Later Stone Age (LSA) by a package of traits that replaced the flake and point technologies of the Middle Stone Age (MSA): bladelets and backed microliths hafted into composite tools, bored stones, bone points and awls, and ostrich eggshell beads. The package did not arrive at once. At Mumba rock shelter in Tanzania, Enkapune ya Muto in Kenya’s Central Rift and Lukenya Hill near Nairobi, early LSA assemblages date to roughly 50,000–40,000 years ago. At Border Cave in South Africa the change had happened by about 44,000 years ago, while elsewhere in southern Africa MSA technology continued for thousands of years.","In West Africa, dates from sites such as Laminia in Senegal and Iho Eleru in Nigeria show MSA-style toolmaking persisting until about 13,000–11,000 years ago, as Eleanor Scerri and colleagues emphasised in 2021. Many supposed LSA features, including bladelets, beads and bone tools, had already come and gone within the MSA (chapter 15), so most researchers now see a mosaic of regional changes rather than a single revolution."]},{"heading":"Border Cave’s toolkit","paragraphs":["In 2012 two papers in PNAS, led by Paola Villa and Francesco d’Errico with Lucinda Backwell and others, reported organic finds from Border Cave layers dated to about 44,000–42,000 years ago and from younger levels. They included fragments of wooden digging sticks, bone awls, bone points of the kind used to tip arrows (one decorated with a spiral groove filled with red ochre), notched bones, beads of ostrich eggshell and marine shell, and a lump of beeswax mixed with the resin of a toxic Euphorbia, about 40,000 years old and probably a hafting glue. A thin wooden stick carried residues of ricinoleic acid, a compound from castor beans, and was interpreted as a poison applicator.","The authors noted that almost every item has a counterpart in the equipment of San hunter-gatherers recorded in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries, and suggested that elements of San material culture are very old. Others caution that useful tools can be reinvented many times and that objects do not carry an ethnic identity across 40,000 years. San communities today are contemporary peoples with long and changing histories of their own."]},{"heading":"Beads, networks and notched bones","paragraphs":["Ostrich eggshell beads, made by breaking shell into pieces, drilling them and grinding them into discs, are the LSA’s signature ornaments. Stanley Ambrose’s excavations at Enkapune ya Muto produced some of the oldest, roughly 50,000–40,000 years old depending on which dates are accepted, and early beads also come from Mumba. In 2022 Jennifer Miller and Yiming Wang compared the sizes and styles of beads from sites across eastern and southern Africa over 50,000 years. They concluded that from about 50,000 to 33,000 years ago the two regions shared bead styles, pointing to social ties across some 3,000 kilometres, which later broke down. Among the Ju|’hoansi of the Kalahari, the anthropologist Polly Wiessner documented a gift-exchange system called hxaro, in which beadwork maintains friendships that can be called on in hard times; many archaeologists see ancient beads as signs of comparable networks.","Other objects hint at record-keeping. The Lebombo bone from Border Cave, a baboon fibula with 29 notches, is roughly 43,000 years old. The Ishango bone, a bone handle found by Jean de Heinzelin in 1950 where the Semliki River leaves Lake Edward in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, and dated to about 25,000–20,000 years ago, bears three columns of notches; one column is grouped as 11, 13, 17 and 19, the prime numbers between 10 and 20. De Heinzelin proposed an arithmetic game, and Alexander Marshack a lunar calendar."]},{"heading":"Painted stones and the rock art of the San","paragraphs":["In July 1969 Wolfgang Erich Wendt was excavating a rock shelter in the Huns Mountains of southern Namibia when news came of the Moon landing, and he named the site Apollo 11 Cave. Among his finds were slabs of stone painted with animals in charcoal, ochre and white, including a rhinoceros and a cat-like creature that may have human hind legs. Dates on charcoal from the same layer put them at around 30,000 years ago, the oldest dated figurative art in Africa, though the exact figure depends on the method. In southern Africa the Robberg industry, named after the peninsula where Nelson Bay Cave lies, produced standardised bladelets between about 22,000 and 12,000 years ago, through the coldest part of the last glaciation.","Southern Africa holds tens of thousands of rock-art sites, from the Drakensberg to the Brandberg and Tsodilo. From the 1970s David Lewis-Williams argued, using nineteenth-century testimonies of |xam people recorded by Wilhelm Bleek and Lucy Lloyd and later Ju|’hoan ethnography, that many paintings show the experiences of healers in trance: bleeding noses, bent-forward postures, dying eland and figures part human and part animal. Most surviving San paintings are probably no more than a few thousand years old, far younger than the Apollo 11 slabs."]},{"heading":"North Africa: tanged points and missing teeth","paragraphs":["North Africa followed its own path. The Aterian, named after Bir el-Ater in Algeria, is known for stone points with a tang for hafting. It is now dated from more than 100,000 years ago to perhaps 30,000 years ago and counted as a regional Middle Stone Age, associated with early Homo sapiens at Moroccan sites such as Dar es-Soltane and Contrebandiers. After a poorly understood gap, the Iberomaurusian appears across the Maghreb from roughly 25,000–22,000 years ago, with backed bladelets, grinding stones and large cemeteries. Many Iberomaurusian men and women had their upper central incisors removed in youth, as skulls from Taforalt and Afalou-Bou-Rhummel show.","At the Grotte des Pigeons near Taforalt in eastern Morocco, a large cemetery dates to about 15,000–13,700 years ago. In 2014 Louise Humphrey and colleagues found decay in about half of all adult teeth, a rate usually seen only among farmers, and linked it to heavy use of sweet acorns and pine nuts. A 2024 isotope study led by Zineb Moubtahij confirmed a diet rich in plants and suggested that infants were weaned onto plant foods. In 2018 Marieke van de Loosdrecht, Choongwon Jeong, Johannes Krause and colleagues sequenced seven Taforalt individuals: about two-thirds of their ancestry was shared with the Natufians of the Levant (chapter 29), and one-third was African. A 2025 study of two women buried about 7,000 years ago at Takarkori in the Libyan Sahara found a deep, long-isolated North African lineage closely related to that African part of Taforalt ancestry (chapter 36)."]},{"heading":"The Nile: tubers and wounds","paragraphs":["Along the Nile, Late Palaeolithic foragers lived by the river’s seasonal floods. At Wadi Kubbaniya near Aswan, excavated by Fred Wendorf and Romuald Schild, camps about 19,000–17,000 years old preserved charred tubers of nut-grass, which had to be ground and cooked to be eaten, together with the bones of catfish caught as the floodwaters fell. Barley grains once claimed as the world’s earliest cereals turned out, when dated directly by radiocarbon, to be much later intrusions.","Farther south, near Wadi Halfa in Sudan, Wendorf’s team excavated the cemetery of Jebel Sahaba in 1965–66, during the international campaign to rescue Nubian sites before the Aswan High Dam flooded them. It held 61 men, women and children, many with stone projectile fragments in or against their bones, and it became famous as the earliest evidence of warfare. A 2021 re-analysis led by Isabelle Crevecoeur, examining the skeletons now in the British Museum under the microscope, found many previously unnoticed injuries, including numerous healed ones. That points to repeated episodes of violence during people’s lives rather than a single massacre. New radiocarbon dates put the cemetery at more than 13,400 years old, and the authors linked the conflict to competition for resources in a time of climatic upheaval."]},{"heading":"Genomes of African foragers","paragraphs":["Ancient DNA survives poorly in the tropics, but it is beginning to fill in the picture. In 2022 Mark Lipson, Elizabeth Sawchuk, Mary Prendergast and colleagues sequenced six individuals from Malawi, Tanzania and Zambia, spanning about 18,000 to 400 years ago, and re-analysed 28 others. They found deep genetic structure among eastern, southern and central African forager populations, evidence that people once found partners over long distances, and a shift after about 20,000 years ago towards more local mating networks. The regionalisation matches what archaeologists see in stone tools and bead styles as the Later Stone Age went on."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Were the Lebombo and Ishango bones mathematical or calendrical records?","views":[{"label":"Counting and calendars","text":"Jean de Heinzelin saw arithmetic in the Ishango groupings, including the primes 11, 13, 17 and 19; Alexander Marshack read Ishango as a lunar calendar, and the 29 notches of the Lebombo bone are often compared with a lunar month."},{"label":"Tallies or practical marks","text":"Sceptics note that the Lebombo bone is broken, so the count of 29 is incomplete, that primes can appear by chance in small numbers, and that notches can be tallies of anything or grip marks. Microscope studies by Francesco d’Errico and others show that some notched bones were marked in separate sessions with different tools, which supports record-keeping but not a specific meaning."}],"status":"Most researchers accept that at least some notched bones are deliberate records, but the claims for prime numbers or lunar calendars are widely considered over-interpretations that cannot be tested."},{"question":"What happened at Jebel Sahaba?","views":[{"label":"A single deadly conflict","text":"Fred Wendorf’s excavation and many later textbooks treated the cemetery as the record of an intense episode of warfare, often described as the earliest known war."},{"label":"Recurrent violence","text":"Isabelle Crevecoeur and colleagues (2021) found many healed injuries alongside fatal ones, showing that individuals survived earlier attacks and that violence recurred over time."}],"status":"The 2021 re-analysis is now widely accepted: Jebel Sahaba documents repeated, probably small-scale raids and clashes rather than one battle. Its causes remain debated, with environmental stress along the Nile the leading hypothesis."},{"question":"Does Border Cave show San culture 44,000 years ago?","views":[{"label":"Deep continuity","text":"Francesco d’Errico, Lucinda Backwell and colleagues argue that the close match between Border Cave objects and ethnographic San equipment shows that key elements of San material culture go back more than 40,000 years."},{"label":"Convergence and caution","text":"Critics argue that digging sticks, bone points and beads are generic solutions that can be reinvented, that tens of thousands of years of change separate the two, and that equating ancient artefacts with a living people risks freezing that people in time."}],"status":"The finds themselves are not in doubt. Most researchers accept a long regional tradition while avoiding claims that a specific modern identity existed 44,000 years ago."},{"question":"Do San paintings depict trance?","views":[{"label":"Trance and healing","text":"David Lewis-Williams and Thomas Dowson argued from nineteenth-century |xam testimony and Kalahari ethnography that much of the art records healers’ experiences in altered states."},{"label":"Many meanings","text":"Researchers such as Anne Solomon argue that the model is too uniform, and that myth, the dead, rain-making, initiation and gender relations are also represented."}],"status":"The trance model remains influential for southern African art, but most specialists now accept that paintings made over thousands of years served several purposes, and they caution against applying it to Ice Age art elsewhere."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Earliest Later Stone Age","value":"About 50,000–40,000 years ago in East Africa; about 44,000 at Border Cave"},{"label":"Oldest dated painted art in Africa","value":"Apollo 11 Cave slabs, Namibia, around 30,000 years old"},{"label":"Ishango bone","value":"About 25,000–20,000 years old, Lake Edward, DR Congo"},{"label":"Taforalt","value":"Decay in about half of adult teeth, about 15,000 years ago"},{"label":"Jebel Sahaba","value":"61 people, over 13,400 years old, many with healed and unhealed injuries"}],"siteIds":["border-cave","enkapune-ya-muto","mumba-rockshelter","ishango","apollo-11-cave","nelson-bay-cave","grotte-des-pigeons","wadi-kubbaniya","jebel-sahaba"],"routes":[],"themes":["technology","ornament","art","food","violence","genetics","burial"],"sources":[{"cite":"Villa, P. et al. (2012). Border Cave and the beginning of the Later Stone Age in South Africa. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 109."},{"cite":"d’Errico, F. et al. (2012). Early evidence of San material culture represented by organic artifacts from Border Cave, South Africa. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 109."},{"cite":"Miller, J.M., Wang, Y.V. (2022). Ostrich eggshell beads reveal 50,000-year-old social network in Africa. Nature 601."},{"cite":"Humphrey, L.T. et al. (2014). Earliest evidence for caries and exploitation of starchy plant foods in Pleistocene hunter-gatherers from Morocco. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 111."},{"cite":"van de Loosdrecht, M. et al. (2018). Pleistocene North African genomes link Near Eastern and sub-Saharan African human populations. Science 360."},{"cite":"Crevecoeur, I. et al. (2021). New insights on interpersonal violence in the Late Pleistocene based on the Nile valley cemetery of Jebel Sahaba. Scientific Reports 11."},{"cite":"Lipson, M. et al. (2022). Ancient DNA and deep population structure in sub-Saharan African foragers. Nature 603."},{"cite":"Scerri, E.M.L. et al. (2021). Continuity of the Middle Stone Age into the Holocene. Scientific Reports 11."},{"cite":"Brooks, A.S., Smith, C.C. (1987). Ishango revisited: new age determinations and cultural interpretations. African Archaeological Review 5."},{"cite":"Lewis-Williams, J.D. (1981). Believing and Seeing: Symbolic Meanings in Southern San Rock Paintings. London: Academic Press."}]},{"id":"americas-peopling","order":27,"act":"one-species","title":"Into the Americas","subtitle":"Beringia, White Sands, Monte Verde and Clovis","era":"Late Pleistocene (Palaeoindian)","start":30000,"end":11000,"dateLabel":"30,000–11,000 years ago","regions":["americas","asia"],"focus":[-180,-45,-35,72],"emblem":"point","summary":"People from northeast Asia reached the Americas across Beringia and spread to southern Chile by 14,500 years ago, long before Clovis. Footprints at White Sands may push arrival back to 23,000 years ago, and the fate of the Ice Age giants is still debated.","paragraphs":[["About 23,000 years ago, on the muddy shore of a lake in what is now New Mexico, teenagers and young children walked and played along the water’s edge.","Their footprints filled with sediment and hardened.","Today the lake is the white gypsum flat of White Sands, and those prints are among the most debated evidence in American archaeology."],["For most of the twentieth century the story seemed settled.","Hunters crossed a land bridge from Siberia to Alaska, walked south through a gap between the ice sheets around 13,000 years ago, and spread across two continents with their fluted Clovis spear points.","That model has collapsed.","At Monte Verde in southern Chile, people were living in timber-framed shelters and eating seaweed 14,500 years ago, and sites in Idaho, Texas, Florida and Oregon are as old or older."],["Genomes tell a parallel story.","The ancestors of Native Americans formed as a distinct population in northeast Asia or Beringia between about 25,000 and 20,000 years ago, from a mixture of East Asian and Ancient North Eurasian ancestry.","Some of their descendants stayed in the north.","Others moved south, probably along a Pacific coast rich in kelp, fish and seals, because the inland corridor between the ice sheets could not support travellers until about 13,000 years ago."],["Then, as the Ice Age ended, mammoths, mastodons, giant ground sloths and American horses vanished.","Whether people, climate or both were to blame is one of the oldest arguments in the field."]],"sentences":["About 23,000 years ago, on the muddy shore of a lake in what is now New Mexico, teenagers and young children walked and played along the water’s edge.","Their footprints filled with sediment and hardened.","Today the lake is the white gypsum flat of White Sands, and those prints are among the most debated evidence in American archaeology.","For most of the twentieth century the story seemed settled.","Hunters crossed a land bridge from Siberia to Alaska, walked south through a gap between the ice sheets around 13,000 years ago, and spread across two continents with their fluted Clovis spear points.","That model has collapsed.","At Monte Verde in southern Chile, people were living in timber-framed shelters and eating seaweed 14,500 years ago, and sites in Idaho, Texas, Florida and Oregon are as old or older.","Genomes tell a parallel story.","The ancestors of Native Americans formed as a distinct population in northeast Asia or Beringia between about 25,000 and 20,000 years ago, from a mixture of East Asian and Ancient North Eurasian ancestry.","Some of their descendants stayed in the north.","Others moved south, probably along a Pacific coast rich in kelp, fish and seals, because the inland corridor between the ice sheets could not support travellers until about 13,000 years ago.","Then, as the Ice Age ended, mammoths, mastodons, giant ground sloths and American horses vanished.","Whether people, climate or both were to blame is one of the oldest arguments in the field."],"pronounce":{"Monte Verde":"Mon-teh Vair-deh","Beringia":"Beh-rin-jee-uh"},"body":[{"heading":"Beringia and the standstill","paragraphs":["When sea level stood about 120 metres lower, a plain up to 1,000 kilometres wide from north to south joined Siberia and Alaska. Beringia was cold, dry grassland, and people were already in the Siberian Arctic by about 32,000 years ago (chapter 22). Genomes show that the ancestors of Native Americans formed as a distinct population between roughly 25,000 and 20,000 years ago, when a group related to East Asians mixed with Ancient North Eurasians, known from the 24,000-year-old boy of Mal’ta near Lake Baikal. In 2007 Erika Tamm and colleagues used mitochondrial DNA to propose a “Beringian standstill”: several thousand years of isolation during which distinctive lineages accumulated. Where it happened is debated. The name implies Beringia itself, but some genomic studies place the isolated population in northeast Asia.","In 2013 archaeologists at the Upward Sun River site in central Alaska found the burial of an infant girl about 11,500 years old, whom the Healy Lake Village community named Xach’itee’aanenh T’eede Gaay, “sunrise girl-child”. Her genome, published in 2018 by José Víctor Moreno-Mayar, Ben Potter, Eske Willerslev and colleagues, revealed a previously unknown population, the Ancient Beringians, who had split from other Native American ancestors about 22,000–18,000 years ago and stayed in the north. The rest divided around 17,500–14,600 years ago into northern and southern branches; the southern branch gave rise to most Indigenous peoples of Central and South America.","A further puzzle came in 2015, when Pontus Skoglund, David Reich and colleagues found that some Amazonian peoples, such as the Suruí and Karitiana, share slightly more genetic variants with Indigenous Australians, New Guineans and Andaman Islanders than other Native Americans do. The signal, attributed to an unsampled “Population Y”, was later found in a roughly 10,400-year-old individual from Lagoa Santa in Brazil. Most geneticists think it reflects structure within the founding population in northeast Asia or Beringia rather than a voyage across the Pacific."]},{"heading":"Footprints at White Sands","paragraphs":["In the Tularosa Basin of New Mexico, the dried bed of Ice Age Lake Otero preserves the tracks of mammoths, giant ground sloths, camels and dire wolves, and of people. In 2021 Matthew Bennett, Kathleen Springer, Jeffrey Pigati and colleagues reported human footprints, mostly of teenagers and children, sealed between layers containing seeds of the aquatic plant Ruppia cirrhosa radiocarbon dated to about 23,000–21,000 years ago, in the depths of the Last Glacial Maximum. A group led by David Madsen objected that aquatic plants absorb old carbon dissolved in lake water, which could make the dates thousands of years too old.","In 2023 the team answered with radiocarbon dates on pollen from land plants, mainly conifers, and with optically stimulated luminescence dates on quartz grains; both gave similar ages. Many archaeologists were persuaded. Sceptics still ask why no stone tools or camps of that age have been found nearby, and whether the pollen could have been washed in from older deposits."]},{"heading":"Older claims","paragraphs":["Other sites claim still earlier arrivals, and each is disputed. At Chiquihuite Cave, high in the mountains of Zacatecas in Mexico, Ciprian Ardelean and colleagues reported in 2020 nearly 2,000 limestone artefacts in layers beginning about 33,000 years ago; critics argue that many could be naturally broken rock and note the absence of hearths or human DNA. At Bluefish Caves in the Yukon, a 2017 re-study led by Lauriane Bourgeon identified cut marks on a horse jaw about 24,000 years old, though carnivores and trampling can leave similar marks. At Santa Elina rock shelter in central Brazil, researchers reported in 2023 bony skin plates of a giant ground sloth, about 27,000 years old, that appear to have been drilled and polished into ornaments. None has convinced most specialists on its own; together with White Sands, they keep the possibility of a much earlier arrival open."]},{"heading":"Before Clovis","paragraphs":["The case for people before Clovis rests on sites that most archaeologists now accept. At Monte Verde in southern Chile, excavated by Tom Dillehay from 1977, waterlogged peat preserved the timber foundations of a long, apparently hide-covered structure, wooden stakes, pieces of meat, wild potatoes and medicinal plants, dated to about 14,500 years ago. In 2008 Dillehay’s team identified several species of seaweed brought from the coast, eaten and probably used as medicine. After a panel of visiting sceptics accepted the site in 1997, the Clovis-first model collapsed. Dillehay has also reported stone tools about 18,500 years old from deeper layers, a claim that remains contested.","In North America, Paisley Caves in Oregon yielded human coprolites containing human DNA, about 14,300 years old, alongside stemmed points. At Page-Ladson, a sinkhole in Florida’s Aucilla River, divers recovered a stone knife and a mastodon tusk with cut marks, about 14,550 years old. Beside Buttermilk Creek in Texas, the Friedkin site produced thousands of artefacts in layers dated by luminescence to about 15,500–13,200 years ago. At Cooper’s Ferry in Idaho, Loren Davis and colleagues dated pits and stemmed points to about 16,000 years ago, and have compared the points with those of Late Palaeolithic Japan, a hint of a Pacific Rim connection."]},{"heading":"Clovis and Folsom","paragraphs":["Even the existence of Ice Age Americans was once controversial. In 1908 a flood exposed large bones in Wild Horse Arroyo near Folsom, New Mexico, and George McJunkin, a ranch foreman born into slavery, recognised that they were unusual. Excavations by the Colorado Museum of Natural History in 1926–27 found fluted points among the bones of an extinct bison, and in 1927 a point was left in place between two ribs so that visiting scientists could see it: proof that people had hunted Ice Age animals in the Americas.","In the 1930s, at Blackwater Draw near Clovis, New Mexico, larger fluted points were found with mammoth bones, beneath the Folsom layers. Clovis points, thinned by long flakes struck from the base, spread across much of North America; a 2020 re-dating by Michael Waters and colleagues places the Clovis complex at about 13,050–12,750 years ago. Many researchers now see Clovis as a technology that spread quickly among people already present, rather than the arrival of the first Americans. The only known Clovis burial, the Anzick child from Montana, belongs genetically to the southern branch of Native American ancestry."]},{"heading":"Coast or corridor?","paragraphs":["How people got south of the ice is still argued. The Laurentide and Cordilleran ice sheets met along the eastern flank of the Rocky Mountains during the glacial maximum. When they parted, an ice-free corridor opened between them, but a 2016 study led by Mikkel Winther Pedersen, using ancient DNA from lake sediments, found that it lacked the plants and animals needed to support travellers until about 12,600 years ago, and bison fossils suggest the corridor was not open end to end until roughly 13,000 years ago. That is too late for Monte Verde or Paisley Caves.","The alternative is the Pacific coast, where outer islands and headlands were free of ice by about 17,000–16,000 years ago. In 2007 Jon Erlandson and colleagues proposed a “kelp highway”: a near-continuous belt of kelp forests from Japan to Baja California, rich in fish, shellfish, sea mammals and seabirds, which people with boats could follow. Its weakness is evidence: the Ice Age shoreline now lies tens of metres underwater, so the route must largely be inferred."]},{"heading":"The great die-off","paragraphs":["By about 11,000 years ago North America had lost most of its large mammals, including mammoths, mastodons, giant ground sloths, horses, camels, sabre-toothed cats, short-faced bears and dire wolves, and South America lost glyptodonts and giant sloths. In 1967 Paul Martin proposed prehistoric overkill, later sharpened into a “blitzkrieg” in which skilled hunters wiped out naive prey within centuries. Supporters point to the timing and to a worldwide pattern of extinctions following human arrival. Critics such as Donald Grayson and David Meltzer note that only a handful of Clovis sites show mammoths or mastodons killed or butchered, and that some species were declining as climate swung from the Bølling–Allerød warmth into the Younger Dryas cold (chapter 28). Many researchers now think hunting and habitat change acted together.","In 2007 Richard Firestone, Allen West, James Kennett and colleagues proposed a more dramatic cause: a comet impact or airburst about 12,800 years ago that triggered the Younger Dryas, wildfires, the extinctions and a collapse of Clovis populations. The idea has been promoted vigorously, but most researchers reject it, for the reasons summarised in the debates below."]}],"debates":[{"question":"When did people first reach the Americas?","views":[{"label":"During or before the glacial maximum","text":"The White Sands team (Bennett, Springer, Pigati and colleagues) argue that three independent dating methods place footprints at 23,000–21,000 years ago. Ciprian Ardelean (Chiquihuite Cave) and the Santa Elina researchers argue for even earlier occupation, perhaps by groups that left few descendants."},{"label":"Not much before 16,000 years ago","text":"Sceptics note that the oldest widely accepted sites with tools and camps, such as Cooper’s Ferry, are about 16,000 years old, that genetic models place the formation of the ancestral Native American population around 25,000–20,000 years ago in Asia or Beringia, and that the White Sands dates rest on indirect material."}],"status":"By 2026 many archaeologists accept that people were at White Sands by about 21,000 years ago, but a pre-glacial arrival is not established. If it is right, those first arrivals may have contributed little to later Native American ancestry. Stone tools or human DNA of similar age would settle it."},{"question":"Did the first Americans travel down the coast or through an inland corridor?","views":[{"label":"Pacific coast","text":"Jon Erlandson, Todd Braje, Loren Davis and others argue that coastal areas were free of ice by about 17,000–16,000 years ago and offered a rich kelp-forest economy, which fits the ages of Monte Verde, Paisley Caves and Cooper’s Ferry."},{"label":"Ice-free corridor","text":"The traditional route between the ice sheets suited the Clovis-first model. Some researchers still see it as important for later movements, including bison and people travelling north and south after about 13,000 years ago."}],"status":"Sediment DNA and bison fossils show that the corridor was not viable before about 13,000–12,600 years ago, so most researchers now favour the coast for the first arrivals. Direct proof lies on drowned shorelines, which are only beginning to be explored."},{"question":"What killed the American megafauna?","views":[{"label":"Human hunting","text":"Following Paul Martin, supporters of overkill point to the close timing with human expansion and to similar extinctions after human arrival on other continents and islands."},{"label":"Climate change","text":"Donald Grayson, David Meltzer and others stress the small number of kill sites and argue that rapid swings between the Bølling–Allerød and the Younger Dryas reshaped habitats."},{"label":"Both together","text":"Many recent studies combine the two, arguing that hunting pressure tipped populations already stressed by changing vegetation and climate."}],"status":"The combined view is the most widely held today. Better dating of the last appearance of each species, including from sediment DNA, is steadily refining the picture."},{"question":"Did a cosmic impact trigger the Younger Dryas?","views":[{"label":"Impact hypothesis","text":"Richard Firestone, Allen West, James Kennett and members of the Comet Research Group argue that a layer about 12,800 years old contains magnetic spherules, nanodiamonds, melt glass and platinum anomalies from a fragmented comet."},{"label":"Rejection","text":"Vance Holliday, David Meltzer, Mark Boslough and others counter that the markers are not unique, not reproducible and not of the same age everywhere, that no crater exists, that many extinctions were not simultaneous, that Clovis gave way to Folsom without a collapse, and that the Younger Dryas is well explained by meltwater weakening Atlantic circulation."}],"status":"A comprehensive 2023 review led by Holliday concluded that the hypothesis is not supported, and most geologists and archaeologists reject it, though its proponents continue to publish."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"White Sands footprints","value":"About 23,000–21,000 years ago (reported 2021; supported by new dates in 2023)"},{"label":"Monte Verde, Chile","value":"About 14,500 years ago; accepted in 1997"},{"label":"Clovis complex","value":"About 13,050–12,750 years ago"},{"label":"Ice-free corridor","value":"Viable for travellers only after about 13,000–12,600 years ago"},{"label":"Ancient Beringians","value":"Split from other Native American ancestors about 22,000–18,000 years ago"}],"siteIds":["white-sands","chiquihuite-cave","bluefish-caves","upward-sun-river","coopers-ferry","paisley-caves","page-ladson","friedkin-site","monte-verde","santa-elina","blackwater-draw","folsom-site"],"routes":[{"id":"beringian-crossing","label":"Across Beringia","start":25000,"end":15000,"certainty":"established","path":[[150,68],[165,67],[177,65.5],[-178,65.3],[-170,65.3],[-162,65.5],[-153,65.5],[-146,64.3]]},{"id":"pacific-coast-route","label":"Pacific coastal route (favoured for first arrivals)","start":17000,"end":14500,"certainty":"debated","path":[[-165,55],[-155,58],[-146,60],[-137,58],[-132,54],[-127,49.5],[-124.5,43],[-122,37],[-117.5,32.5],[-110,23.5],[-96,15.5],[-84,8],[-80,1],[-80.5,-6],[-76.5,-14],[-71.5,-30],[-73.2,-41.5]]},{"id":"ice-free-corridor","label":"Ice-free corridor (viable only after about 13,000 years ago)","start":13000,"end":11000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[-146,64.3],[-135,63],[-125,60],[-119,56],[-114,51.5],[-110,47.5],[-106,43]]}],"themes":["migration","genetics","technology","animals","climate","seafaring"],"sources":[{"cite":"Moreno-Mayar, J.V. et al. (2018). Terminal Pleistocene Alaskan genome reveals first founding population of Native Americans. Nature 553."},{"cite":"Skoglund, P. et al. (2015). Genetic evidence for two founding populations of the Americas. Nature 525."},{"cite":"Bennett, M.R. et al. (2021). Evidence of humans in North America during the Last Glacial Maximum. Science 373."},{"cite":"Pigati, J.S. et al. (2023). Independent age estimates resolve the controversy of ancient human footprints at White Sands. Science 382."},{"cite":"Ardelean, C.F. et al. (2020). Evidence of human occupation in Mexico around the Last Glacial Maximum. Nature 584."},{"cite":"Dillehay, T.D. et al. (2008). Monte Verde: seaweed, food, medicine, and the peopling of South America. Science 320."},{"cite":"Davis, L.G. et al. (2019). Late Upper Paleolithic occupation at Cooper’s Ferry, Idaho, USA, ~16,000 years ago. Science 365."},{"cite":"Waters, M.R., Stafford, T.W., Carlson, D.L. (2020). The age of Clovis: 13,050 to 12,750 cal yr B.P. Science Advances 6."},{"cite":"Pedersen, M.W. et al. (2016). Postglacial viability and colonization in North America’s ice-free corridor. Nature 537."},{"cite":"Holliday, V.T. et al. (2023). Comprehensive refutation of the Younger Dryas Impact Hypothesis (YDIH). Earth-Science Reviews 247."}]},{"id":"warming-world","order":28,"act":"warming","title":"The Great Thaw","subtitle":"Deglaciation, drowned lands and a green Sahara","era":"Late Glacial and Early Holocene","start":20000,"end":8000,"dateLabel":"20,000–8,000 years ago","regions":["europe","asia","africa","americas","oceania"],"focus":[-180,-50,180,78],"emblem":"ice","summary":"The end of the Ice Age came in lurches: sudden warmings, a return to cold in the Younger Dryas, sea level rising some 120 metres and drowning Doggerland, Sundaland and Beringia. The Sahara turned green, and people everywhere adapted how they lived.","paragraphs":[["Twenty thousand years ago, you could have walked from England to Denmark, from Siberia to Alaska, and from New Guinea to Tasmania.","So much water was locked up in ice sheets, some of them three kilometres thick, that the sea stood about 120 metres lower than it does today."],["Then the ice began to melt, but not smoothly.","About 14,700 years ago, Greenland warmed by around ten degrees within a few decades.","Soon afterwards the sea rose by some 15 metres in about three and a half centuries, far faster than anything people have experienced since.","Around 12,900 years ago, the North Atlantic world plunged back into near-glacial cold for twelve centuries, a relapse called the Younger Dryas.","When it ended, 11,700 years ago, the warmer and steadier Holocene began, the epoch in which every farming society has lived."],["The rising water drowned whole landscapes.","The plain joining Britain to the continent broke into islands, and around 8,150 years ago a vast undersea landslide off Norway sent a tsunami across the North Sea.","Sundaland, the great lowland of Southeast Asia, went under, and Tasmania became an island.","Some Aboriginal Australian stories may still recall coastlines that disappeared."],["Meanwhile the Sahara turned green, with lakes, hippos and grasslands.","People everywhere adapted: fishing, gathering seeds and nuts, and staying longer in one place.","And on Wrangel Island in the Arctic, a last population of woolly mammoths survived until about 4,000 years ago, when the pyramids of Egypt were already standing."]],"sentences":["Twenty thousand years ago, you could have walked from England to Denmark, from Siberia to Alaska, and from New Guinea to Tasmania.","So much water was locked up in ice sheets, some of them three kilometres thick, that the sea stood about 120 metres lower than it does today.","Then the ice began to melt, but not smoothly.","About 14,700 years ago, Greenland warmed by around ten degrees within a few decades.","Soon afterwards the sea rose by some 15 metres in about three and a half centuries, far faster than anything people have experienced since.","Around 12,900 years ago, the North Atlantic world plunged back into near-glacial cold for twelve centuries, a relapse called the Younger Dryas.","When it ended, 11,700 years ago, the warmer and steadier Holocene began, the epoch in which every farming society has lived.","The rising water drowned whole landscapes.","The plain joining Britain to the continent broke into islands, and around 8,150 years ago a vast undersea landslide off Norway sent a tsunami across the North Sea.","Sundaland, the great lowland of Southeast Asia, went under, and Tasmania became an island.","Some Aboriginal Australian stories may still recall coastlines that disappeared.","Meanwhile the Sahara turned green, with lakes, hippos and grasslands.","People everywhere adapted: fishing, gathering seeds and nuts, and staying longer in one place.","And on Wrangel Island in the Arctic, a last population of woolly mammoths survived until about 4,000 years ago, when the pyramids of Egypt were already standing."],"pronounce":{"Dryas":"Dry-us","Sundaland":"Soon-da-land","Wrangel":"Rang-gul"},"body":[{"heading":"The world at the glacial maximum","paragraphs":["At the Last Glacial Maximum, roughly 26,000–19,000 years ago, ice sheets covered most of Canada and the northern United States, Scandinavia, much of the British Isles, the Alps and Patagonia; the Laurentide sheet over North America was up to about 3 kilometres thick. Global sea level stood roughly 120–130 metres below today’s, and Kurt Lambeck’s 2014 reconstruction puts the ice-volume equivalent at about 134 metres. Vast lowlands emerged: Beringia between Siberia and Alaska (chapter 27); Sundaland, joining the Malay Peninsula, Sumatra, Java and Borneo to mainland Asia; Sahul, uniting Australia, New Guinea and Tasmania (chapter 19); Doggerland, joining Britain to the continent; and the dry floor of the Persian Gulf.","A 2020 study led by Jessica Tierney estimated that global mean temperature was about 6 °C lower than before industrialisation, and atmospheric carbon dioxide stood near 190 parts per million. The world was colder, drier and dustier, with tropical forests shrunk and deserts expanded."]},{"heading":"A thaw in fits and starts","paragraphs":["Deglaciation was paced by changes in Earth’s orbit that increased northern summer sunshine from about 20,000 years ago, amplified by carbon dioxide, which rose to about 265 parts per million by the early Holocene. The path was jagged because meltwater disturbed the Atlantic overturning circulation, which carries heat northwards. During Heinrich Stadial 1, about 18,000–14,700 years ago, armadas of icebergs from the Laurentide ice sheet freshened the North Atlantic; the circulation weakened, Europe stayed bitterly cold and the Southern Hemisphere warmed.","About 14,700 years ago the circulation strengthened and the Bølling–Allerød interstadial began: Greenland ice cores show a jump of around 10 °C within decades. Sea level surged. Corals drilled off Tahiti, analysed by Pierre Deschamps and colleagues in 2012, record Meltwater Pulse 1A, a rise of roughly 14–18 metres between about 14,650 and 14,310 years ago, some 4 centimetres a year; whether the water came mainly from North America or Antarctica is still debated.","Around 12,900 years ago the North Atlantic region fell back into near-glacial conditions for about 1,200 years. The Younger Dryas is named after an Arctic flower, the mountain avens (Dryas octopetala), whose remains reappear in Scandinavian lake sediments. Its most likely cause is meltwater, perhaps routed from glacial Lake Agassiz down the Mackenzie River into the Arctic or through the St Lawrence, weakening the overturning circulation again; the impact hypothesis is discussed in chapter 27. The cold ended abruptly: in 2008 a team led by Jørgen Peder Steffensen showed that dust and snow-source indicators in Greenland ice shifted within a few years.","The base of the Holocene is formally defined in the NorthGRIP ice core from central Greenland, at a depth of 1,492.45 metres, and dated to 11,700 years before 2000 CE (Mike Walker and colleagues, 2009). In 2018 the Holocene was divided into three ages: the Greenlandian, the Northgrippian from 8,236 years before 2000 CE and the Meghalayan from 4,250."]},{"heading":"Lake Agassiz and the cold event of 8,200 years ago","paragraphs":["The last big shock came from North America. As the Laurentide ice retreated, meltwater pooled into glacial Lakes Agassiz and Ojibway, which together covered a larger area than any lake today. When the ice over Hudson Bay finally gave way, shortly before 8,200 years ago, the lakes drained into the Labrador Sea, as David Barber and colleagues proposed in 1999. Greenland then cooled by several degrees for roughly 150 years, and records from Europe to Africa and Asia show colder or drier conditions, including a weaker monsoon. Its effect on people is debated: some archaeologists link it to changes in settlement in Southwest Asia and Europe, others find little effect."]},{"heading":"Drowned lands","paragraphs":["Rising seas drowned the coastal plains where many people had lived. Doggerland, the lowland between Britain, the Netherlands and Denmark, has been mapped since the early 2000s by Vince Gaffney’s team using seismic surveys made for the oil and gas industry, revealing river valleys, lakes and hills. Trawlers have long dredged up mammoth bones and tools; in 1931 the trawler Colinda netted a barbed antler point between the Leman and Ower banks.","Around 8,150 years ago part of the Norwegian continental slope collapsed in the Storegga slide, moving about 3,000 cubic kilometres of sediment. The tsunami left sand layers several metres above the shorelines of the time in Norway and mainland Scotland, and up to about 20 metres in Shetland. A 2020 study led by Gaffney found tsunami deposits in cores from the southern North Sea and suggested that the wave struck a Doggerland already breaking into islands, parts of which may have survived for some centuries more before the rising sea took them.","Much larger lands disappeared elsewhere. Sundaland, a lowland of well over a million square kilometres, was largely drowned by about 7,000 years ago, and the Bering land bridge was cut by about 11,000 years ago. Tasmania became an island when Bass Strait flooded, at a date estimated at between about 14,000 and 10,000 years ago; New Guinea separated from Australia around 8,000–7,000 years ago, as the Torres Strait filled. In 2016 the geographer Patrick Nunn and the linguist Nicholas Reid gathered Aboriginal stories from 21 places around Australia’s coast that describe land lost to the sea, and argued that some preserve observations more than 7,000 years old.","The best-known flood claim concerns the Black Sea. In 1997 the marine geologists William Ryan and Walter Pitman argued that it had been a freshwater lake far below the Mediterranean until rising seas broke through the Bosporus, raising its level by tens of metres within a few years; they originally dated the event to about 7,600 years ago and linked it to flood myths, including Noah’s. Later work by Ali Aksu, Richard Hiscott, Valentina Yanko-Hombach and others points to a more gradual reconnection and a smaller rise."]},{"heading":"A green Sahara","paragraphs":["In Africa, the orbital changes that melted northern ice also strengthened the summer monsoon. From about 14,500 years ago, and fully after the Younger Dryas, the African Humid Period turned much of the Sahara into grassland and savanna dotted with lakes; Lake Mega-Chad grew to rival the Caspian Sea in area. Hippos, crocodiles, elephants and giraffes lived where there is desert today, and people followed (chapter 36). How the humid period ended, around 5,500 years ago, is debated. Peter deMenocal’s 2000 study of dust in marine cores off Mauritania suggested an abrupt switch within a few centuries, whereas Stefan Kröpelin’s record from Lake Yoa in Chad (2008) shows gradual drying over millennia; a 2015 study led by Timothy Shanahan concluded that the end came earlier in the north than in the south."]},{"heading":"Living in a changing world","paragraphs":["People adapted in many ways. Across Eurasia and Africa, diets broadened to include small game, birds, fish, shellfish, seeds and nuts, which Kent Flannery in 1969 called the broad spectrum revolution. Microliths, small standardised stone inserts, made it easy to build and repair arrows, harpoons and sickles. Fishing grew in importance: at Franchthi Cave in southern Greece, Mesolithic layers contain the bones of large tuna, and obsidian from the island of Melos shows that people were crossing open sea by about 11,000 years ago. In the Levant, Natufian foragers built stone houses and stayed in one place for much of the year (chapter 29); in East Asia, hunter-gatherers were already making pottery (chapter 32).","Animals had to adapt too. Many Ice Age giants disappeared, but some survived in refuges. Woolly mammoths lived on St Paul Island in the Bering Sea until about 5,600 years ago, when, according to Russell Graham and colleagues (2016), the shrinking island ran short of fresh water. On Wrangel Island, cut off by the sea around 10,000 years ago, a population founded by a handful of animals persisted until about 4,000 years ago. A 2024 genomic study led by Marianne Dehasque found that it recovered from its founding bottleneck and stayed stable for some six thousand years, so its end was probably sudden rather than a slow genetic decline."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Did a catastrophic flood fill the Black Sea?","views":[{"label":"Sudden flood","text":"William Ryan and Walter Pitman (1997, and their 1998 book Noah’s Flood) argued from drowned shorelines, a sharp change from freshwater to marine shells and cores from the shelf that the Mediterranean poured through the Bosporus and raised the Black Sea by tens of metres within a few years."},{"label":"Gradual reconnection","text":"Ali Aksu, Richard Hiscott, Valentina Yanko-Hombach and others argue from sediments in the Sea of Marmara and the Black Sea shelf that water had long been flowing out of the Black Sea and that the reconnection and rise were gradual and more modest."}],"status":"Most researchers accept that the Mediterranean reconnected with the Black Sea roughly 9,000–8,000 years ago, but the size and speed of the level rise remain disputed, and the link to flood myths is widely regarded as speculative."},{"question":"Did the Green Sahara end suddenly or slowly?","views":[{"label":"Abrupt","text":"Peter deMenocal and colleagues (2000) found a sharp increase in wind-blown dust in marine sediments off West Africa around 5,500 years ago, suggesting a switch within decades to centuries, possibly amplified by vegetation feedbacks."},{"label":"Gradual","text":"Stefan Kröpelin and colleagues (2008) found in Lake Yoa, Chad, a continuous record of slowly changing pollen and lake chemistry over several thousand years."}],"status":"Both can be true in different places: Timothy Shanahan and colleagues (2015) showed that drying began earlier in the north and spread south. The details matter for understanding when herders moved (chapter 36)."},{"question":"Can oral traditions remember rising seas from 7,000 years ago?","views":[{"label":"Deep-time memory","text":"Patrick Nunn and Nicholas Reid (2016) argue that Aboriginal stories from 21 coastal places, such as Spencer Gulf and Port Phillip Bay, describe land that was drowned when the sea rose, and that matching them with sea-level curves implies transmission over more than 7,000 years, supported by strict cultural rules for passing on knowledge."},{"label":"Caution","text":"Sceptics note that many stories were recorded by colonial-era observers who may have shaped them, that people can infer past landscapes from what they see today, and that the dating depends on matching stories to sea-level reconstructions that are themselves uncertain."}],"status":"Many researchers accept that such long transmission is possible and that Aboriginal knowledge-keepers are authorities on their own traditions, but proving the age of any particular story remains difficult."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Sea level at the glacial maximum","value":"About 120–130 metres below today"},{"label":"Bølling–Allerød warming","value":"About 14,700–12,900 years ago"},{"label":"Younger Dryas cold relapse","value":"About 12,900–11,700 years ago"},{"label":"Start of the Holocene","value":"11,700 years before 2000 CE, defined in the NorthGRIP ice core"},{"label":"Storegga tsunami","value":"About 8,150 years ago"},{"label":"Last mammoths","value":"Wrangel Island, about 4,000 years ago"}],"siteIds":["northgrip","lake-agassiz","dogger-bank","shetland-storegga","bosporus","lake-yoa","bering-land-bridge","sunda-shelf","bass-strait","franchthi-cave","st-paul-island","wrangel-island"],"routes":[],"themes":["climate","migration","food","animals","seafaring","settlement"],"sources":[{"cite":"Lambeck, K. et al. (2014). Sea level and global ice volumes from the Last Glacial Maximum to the Holocene. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 111."},{"cite":"Tierney, J.E. et al. (2020). Glacial cooling and climate sensitivity revisited. Nature 584."},{"cite":"Deschamps, P. et al. (2012). Ice-sheet collapse and sea-level rise at the Bølling warming 14,600 years ago. Nature 483."},{"cite":"Walker, M. et al. (2009). Formal definition and dating of the GSSP (Global Stratotype Section and Point) for the base of the Holocene using the Greenland NorthGRIP ice core, and selected auxiliary records. Journal of Quaternary Science 24."},{"cite":"Barber, D.C. et al. (1999). Forcing of the cold event of 8,200 years ago by catastrophic drainage of Laurentide lakes. Nature 400."},{"cite":"Gaffney, V. et al. (2020). A great wave: the Storegga tsunami and the end of Doggerland? Antiquity 94."},{"cite":"Ryan, W.B.F. et al. (1997). An abrupt drowning of the Black Sea shelf. Marine Geology 138."},{"cite":"Shanahan, T.M. et al. (2015). The time-transgressive termination of the African Humid Period. Nature Geoscience 8."},{"cite":"Nunn, P.D., Reid, N.J. (2016). Aboriginal memories of inundation of the Australian coast dating from more than 7000 years ago. Australian Geographer 47."},{"cite":"Dehasque, M. et al. (2024). Temporal dynamics of woolly mammoth genome erosion prior to extinction. Cell 187."}]},{"id":"natufians","order":29,"act":"warming","title":"The First Villagers","subtitle":"Ohalo II, the Natufians and the first bread","era":"Epipalaeolithic (Kebaran and Natufian)","start":23000,"end":11600,"dateLabel":"23,000–11,600 years ago","regions":["asia"],"focus":[33.5,29,40,37.5],"emblem":"sickle","summary":"Long before farming, foragers in the Levant ground wild cereals at Ohalo II, and the Natufians built stone houses, baked bread, feasted and buried their dead beneath their floors. Whether the Younger Dryas pushed them towards cultivation is still debated.","paragraphs":[["In 1989 a drought lowered the Sea of Galilee, and on its exposed shore archaeologists found the charred remains of six brush huts.","The camp, called Ohalo II, had burned and then been drowned about 23,000 years ago, and the water had preserved almost everything: grass bedding on a hut floor, a grinding stone with traces of wild barley and wheat, flint blades polished by cutting cereal stalks, and seeds and fruits from about 140 kinds of plant."],["Eight thousand years later, in the same region, people did something new.","The Natufians, who lived in the Levant between about 15,000 and 11,600 years ago, built round stone houses and stayed in them for much of the year.","They harvested wild cereals with bone-handled sickles, pounded food in mortars cut into bedrock, and buried their dead beneath their floors, sometimes with dogs.","At Shubayqa in the Jordanian desert, charred crumbs show that people were baking a kind of flatbread 14,400 years ago, some 4,000 years before farming."],["They held feasts and honoured unusual people.","In a small cave in Galilee, a woman with a twisted spine was buried about 12,000 years ago with 50 tortoise shells, the wing of an eagle and the foot of another person."],["Then came the cold, dry centuries of the Younger Dryas.","Some archaeologists think hardship pushed these foragers to start cultivating; others think they simply moved around more.","Either way, their genomes show that they were ancestors of the region’s first farmers."]],"sentences":["In 1989 a drought lowered the Sea of Galilee, and on its exposed shore archaeologists found the charred remains of six brush huts.","The camp, called Ohalo II, had burned and then been drowned about 23,000 years ago, and the water had preserved almost everything: grass bedding on a hut floor, a grinding stone with traces of wild barley and wheat, flint blades polished by cutting cereal stalks, and seeds and fruits from about 140 kinds of plant.","Eight thousand years later, in the same region, people did something new.","The Natufians, who lived in the Levant between about 15,000 and 11,600 years ago, built round stone houses and stayed in them for much of the year.","They harvested wild cereals with bone-handled sickles, pounded food in mortars cut into bedrock, and buried their dead beneath their floors, sometimes with dogs.","At Shubayqa in the Jordanian desert, charred crumbs show that people were baking a kind of flatbread 14,400 years ago, some 4,000 years before farming.","They held feasts and honoured unusual people.","In a small cave in Galilee, a woman with a twisted spine was buried about 12,000 years ago with 50 tortoise shells, the wing of an eagle and the foot of another person.","Then came the cold, dry centuries of the Younger Dryas.","Some archaeologists think hardship pushed these foragers to start cultivating; others think they simply moved around more.","Either way, their genomes show that they were ancestors of the region’s first farmers."],"pronounce":{"Ohalo":"Oh-hah-lo","Natufians":"Nah-too-fee-ans","Shubayqa":"Shoo-bay-ka"},"body":[{"heading":"Ohalo II: a camp by the lake","paragraphs":["In 1989 a drought exposed the remains of a camp on the southwestern shore of the Sea of Galilee. Excavated by Dani Nadel of the University of Haifa in 1989–91 and again when the water fell in 1999–2001, Ohalo II had burned and then been sealed by rising water and silt around 23,000 years ago, preserving plant remains that rarely survive. There were six oval huts of brushwood; the floor of one was lined with bundles of grass, the oldest known bedding. A man was buried nearby.","Ehud Weiss and colleagues identified tens of thousands of seeds and fruits from about 140 plant species, including wild barley, wild emmer wheat, wild oats, figs, grapes, olives and many small-seeded grasses. Starch grains of barley and wheat on a flat basalt stone showed that grain was being ground (Dolores Piperno and colleagues, 2004), and a 2016 study led by Iris Groman-Yaroslavski found flint blades with the gloss typical of harvesting cereal stems. In 2015 Ainit Snir, Weiss and colleagues argued that 13 species of plants typical of arable weeds, found among the cereals, point to small-scale trial cultivation some 11,000 years before farming took hold. Critics reply that such plants also grow on naturally disturbed ground and could have been gathered with the grain. The inhabitants also fished in the lake and hunted gazelle, deer and birds."]},{"heading":"Kebaran foragers","paragraphs":["Ohalo II belongs to the early Epipalaeolithic of the Levant. The Kebaran culture, named after Kebara Cave on Mount Carmel, lasted from about 23,000 to 17,500 years ago and is known for tiny bladelets; the Geometric Kebaran, from about 17,500 to 15,000 years ago, added trapezes and rectangles, standardised inserts for composite tools. Most groups moved seasonally between the lowlands and the hills. Some places drew larger gatherings: at Kharaneh IV in the Jordanian steppe, Lisa Maher’s team found hut floors, burials and thousands of shell beads, some from the Mediterranean and the Red Sea, at a site occupied around 19,800–18,600 years ago, suggesting that people from wide areas came together there."]},{"heading":"The Natufians settle down","paragraphs":["The Natufian culture was named by Dorothy Garrod after Wadi en-Natuf, where she excavated Shuqba Cave in 1928. It spans about 15,000–11,600 years ago and is usually divided into Early, Late and Final phases. Early Natufian sites in the Mediterranean woodland of the southern Levant show a new way of life: semi-subterranean round houses with stone walls, hearths and storage, used for much of the year. At ʿAin Mallaha (Eynan) in the Hula Valley, excavated by Jean Perrot from 1955 and later by François Valla, houses up to about 9 metres across cluster on a slope, and the dead were buried among and beneath them, including a woman with a puppy (chapter 24). At el-Wad on Mount Carmel, Garrod found burials with elaborate headdresses of dentalium shells, and at Hayonim Cave in Galilee, Ofer Bar-Yosef’s team found round rooms and a grave in which people had been buried with two dogs.","Staying put changed the neighbours. A 2017 study led by Lior Weissbrod showed that house mice outcompeted wild mice at Natufian sites, the first known commensal house mice. Natufians hunted gazelle intensively, along with deer, wild boar, hares and birds, and processed plant foods on a scale not seen before."]},{"heading":"Sickles, mortars and the first bread","paragraphs":["The Natufian toolkit was geared to plants. Flint blades set in straight bone handles, some carved at the end with animal heads, served as sickles, and their edges carry the gloss of cutting stems. Mortars were carved into bedrock or boulders, some deep and narrow, alongside pestles, basalt bowls and grinding slabs. At Shubayqa 1 in the basalt desert of northeastern Jordan, Amaia Arranz-Otaegui, Tobias Richter and colleagues reported in 2018 charred fragments of bread-like food, about 14,400 years old, made from ground wild cereals such as einkorn, barley or oats mixed with tubers of club-rush. It is the oldest known bread, about 4,000 years older than farming. The authors suggest that bread, laborious to make, may have been a special food whose appeal later encouraged cultivation.","At Raqefet Cave on Mount Carmel, where Dani Nadel’s team found graves lined with flowering plants such as sage and mint, a 2018 study led by Li Liu analysed residues in bedrock mortars about 13,000 years old. Starch grains with damage typical of malting and mashing led the team to propose that Natufians brewed a thin, porridge-like beer from wheat or barley, perhaps for funeral feasts. Other researchers questioned whether the starch damage demonstrates fermentation and noted that open mortars are easily contaminated."]},{"heading":"Feasts, a shaman and lovers","paragraphs":["Natufian ritual left striking traces. At Hilazon Tachtit, a small cave in western Galilee, Leore Grosman and colleagues found the grave of a woman aged about 45, buried about 12,000 years ago. A deformed pelvis and spine would have given her an uneven gait. The grave was lined and covered with stones and held 50 complete tortoise shells, the wing of an eagle, the pelvis of a leopard, the forearm of a wild boar, the tail of an aurochs, the skulls of two martens and a human foot. The excavators interpret her as a shaman, one of the earliest known. A nearby pit held remains of at least 71 tortoises and three aurochs, the leftovers of what Natalie Munro and Grosman (2010) describe as a funeral feast.","The ʿAin Sakhri lovers, a calcite pebble about 10 centimetres tall carved with a couple embracing, was found in a cave near Bethlehem and brought to scholarly attention by René Neuville in 1933. About 11,000 years old and now in the British Museum, it is often called the oldest known depiction of people making love."]},{"heading":"The Younger Dryas question","paragraphs":["The Late Natufian, which overlaps with the Younger Dryas (about 12,900–11,700 years ago), looks different. Sites became smaller and more ephemeral, burials were more often secondary, and people ranged farther into the steppe and desert. Ofer Bar-Yosef and Anna Belfer-Cohen argued that the cold, dry Younger Dryas thinned the wild cereal stands on which Natufians had come to depend and pushed them to begin cultivating; Andrew Moore and Gordon Hillman made a similar case from Abu Hureyra. Critics, among them Lisa Maher, Ted Banning and Michael Chazan (2011), argue that local climate records do not show a simple crisis, that the Natufian response was to move more rather than to farm, and that domesticated cereals appear only much later, in the Pre-Pottery Neolithic (chapter 33). Other explanations stress feasting, social competition and gradual niche construction.","Abu Hureyra on the Euphrates in Syria was excavated by Moore in 1972–73, before the Tabqa Dam flooded it beneath Lake Assad. Its Epipalaeolithic village preserved abundant plant remains. Hillman reported rye grains of domestic type about 13,000 years old, a claim many doubt because the grains are few and could be intrusive. In 2020 Moore, James Kennett and colleagues argued that melted glass and other markers show that the village was destroyed by a cosmic airburst about 12,800 years ago; critics argue that such glass forms when buildings and vegetation burn, and they reject the wider impact hypothesis (chapter 27)."]},{"heading":"Natufian genomes","paragraphs":["Hot climates destroy DNA, but in 2016 Iosif Lazaridis, David Reich and colleagues obtained genomes from Natufian burials at Raqefet Cave. The Natufians carried a large share of Basal Eurasian ancestry, a lineage that branched off early from other non-Africans and carries little Neanderthal DNA, and they were genetically close to the later Pre-Pottery Neolithic farmers of the southern Levant, supporting continuity from foragers to farmers. In 2018 genomes from Taforalt in Morocco, about 15,000 years old (chapter 26), turned out to share much of their ancestry with the Natufians, evidence of ancient connections between North Africa and the Levant whose direction is still debated."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Did the people of Ohalo II cultivate cereals?","views":[{"label":"Trial cultivation","text":"Ainit Snir, Ehud Weiss and colleagues (2015) argue that the mix of wild cereals with plants that today thrive as arable weeds, together with sickle blades and grinding stones, shows small-scale sowing some 23,000 years ago."},{"label":"Intensive gathering","text":"Sceptics note that the weed species also grow in natural disturbed habitats, such as lake shores, and that none of the cereals shows domestic traits, so intensive gathering explains the evidence equally well."}],"status":"Most researchers accept that Ohalo II shows sophisticated use of wild cereals; whether anything was sown remains unproven, and in any case the practice did not lead directly to farming."},{"question":"Did the Natufians brew beer?","views":[{"label":"Ritual brewing","text":"Li Liu, Dani Nadel and colleagues (2018) argue that starch damage in Raqefet mortars matches malting, mashing and fermentation, and that beer was brewed for funerary feasts."},{"label":"Not demonstrated","text":"Critics argue that similar starch damage can result from other kinds of processing or from weathering, and that open bedrock mortars exposed for millennia are prone to contamination."}],"status":"The claim is plausible but not settled. Chemical markers of fermentation from securely sealed contexts would strengthen it."},{"question":"Did the Younger Dryas push people towards farming?","views":[{"label":"Climate as trigger","text":"Ofer Bar-Yosef, Anna Belfer-Cohen, Andrew Moore and Gordon Hillman argued that cold and drought reduced wild cereals, prompting Natufians who had grown dependent on them to begin cultivating."},{"label":"Other drivers","text":"Lisa Maher, Ted Banning and Michael Chazan (2011), among others, find no simple crisis in local records and note that Late Natufians became more mobile; they and others emphasise social factors such as feasting and competition, and gradual niche construction."}],"status":"Most researchers now see the transition to farming as a long, regionally varied process in which climate mattered but did not act alone. Domesticated cereals appear only after the Younger Dryas (chapter 33)."},{"question":"Was Abu Hureyra destroyed by a cosmic airburst?","views":[{"label":"Airburst","text":"Andrew Moore, James Kennett and colleagues (2020) reported melted glass, nanodiamonds and microspherules in the layer dated about 12,800 years ago and attributed them to a comet airburst."},{"label":"Ordinary fire","text":"Critics argue that the glass formed when reed and mud-brick structures and vegetation burned, and that the other markers are not diagnostic of impacts."}],"status":"Few researchers outside the impact group accept the claim, which is tied to the widely rejected Younger Dryas impact hypothesis."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Ohalo II","value":"About 23,000 years ago; about 140 plant species; the oldest known bedding"},{"label":"Natufian culture","value":"About 15,000–11,600 years ago, southern and northern Levant"},{"label":"Oldest known bread","value":"Shubayqa 1, Jordan, about 14,400 years ago"},{"label":"Hilazon Tachtit burial","value":"About 12,000 years ago, with 50 tortoise shells"},{"label":"First house mice","value":"Natufian settlements, about 15,000 years ago"}],"siteIds":["ohalo-ii","kebara-cave","kharaneh-iv","ain-mallaha","el-wad","hayonim-cave","raqefet-cave","shubayqa-1","hilazon-tachtit","ain-sakhri","abu-hureyra"],"routes":[],"themes":["settlement","food","farming","burial","art","animals","climate","genetics"],"sources":[{"cite":"Weiss, E. et al. (2004). The broad spectrum revisited: evidence from plant remains. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 101."},{"cite":"Snir, A. et al. (2015). The origin of cultivation: proto-weeds, long before Neolithic farming. PLoS ONE 10(7)."},{"cite":"Maher, L.A. et al. (2012). Twenty thousand-year-old huts at a hunter-gatherer settlement in eastern Jordan. PLoS ONE 7(2)."},{"cite":"Arranz-Otaegui, A. et al. (2018). Archaeobotanical evidence reveals the origins of bread 14,400 years ago in northeastern Jordan. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 115."},{"cite":"Liu, L. et al. (2018). Fermented beverage and food storage in 13,000 y-old stone mortars at Raqefet Cave, Israel: investigating Natufian ritual feasting. Journal of Archaeological Science: Reports 21."},{"cite":"Grosman, L., Munro, N.D., Belfer-Cohen, A. (2008). A 12,000-year-old shaman burial from the southern Levant (Israel). Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 105."},{"cite":"Weissbrod, L. et al. (2017). Origins of house mice in ecological niches created by settled hunter-gatherers in the Levant 15,000 y ago. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 114."},{"cite":"Maher, L.A., Banning, E.B., Chazan, M. (2011). Oasis or mirage? Assessing the role of abrupt climate change in the prehistory of the southern Levant. Cambridge Archaeological Journal 21."},{"cite":"Moore, A.M.T., Hillman, G.C., Legge, A.J. (2000). Village on the Euphrates: From Foraging to Farming at Abu Hureyra. Oxford University Press."},{"cite":"Lazaridis, I. et al. (2016). Genomic insights into the origin of farming in the ancient Near East. Nature 536."}]},{"id":"gobekli-tepe","order":30,"act":"warming","title":"The Stone Hills","subtitle":"Göbekli Tepe, Karahan Tepe and the Taş Tepeler","era":"Pre-Pottery Neolithic A and early B","start":11600,"end":10000,"dateLabel":"c. 9600–8000 BCE","regions":["asia"],"focus":[37.8,36.6,41.4,38.2],"emblem":"pillar","summary":"On limestone ridges around Şanlıurfa, people who still hunted and gathered carved and raised stone pillars more than 11,000 years ago. Göbekli Tepe and its newly excavated neighbours have overturned old ideas about what came first, monuments or farms, and the answer is still being rewritten.","paragraphs":[["Some 600 kilometres north of the Natufian hamlets, on a bare limestone ridge above the plain of Şanlıurfa in southeastern Türkiye, farmers had long been dragging broken slabs of stone out of their fields.","Surveyors who visited in 1963 took them for medieval gravestones.","In 1994 the German archaeologist Klaus Schmidt saw something else: the heads of carved pillars, each shaped like a giant letter T."],["Under the hill lay round enclosures ringed with pillars, the tallest about five and a half metres high, raised around 11,500 years ago.","Foxes snarl across their faces, snakes stream down their sides, vultures spread their wings.","The two central pillars of one enclosure have arms carved along their flanks, hands meeting above a belt, and a fox pelt hanging as a loincloth.","They are not just stones.","They are beings."],["Schmidt believed he had found a mountain sanctuary built by hunter-gatherers, a temple that came before the town and perhaps nudged people toward farming.","The researchers who followed him have found rain cisterns, houses and thousands of grinding stones, and a dozen neighbouring hills, the Taş Tepeler, are now yielding carved heads, leopards and whole rooms cut from the living rock.","The builders still hunted gazelle and harvested wild grain.","What they made has changed how we picture the people who lived on the eve of farming."]],"sentences":["Some 600 kilometres north of the Natufian hamlets, on a bare limestone ridge above the plain of Şanlıurfa in southeastern Türkiye, farmers had long been dragging broken slabs of stone out of their fields.","Surveyors who visited in 1963 took them for medieval gravestones.","In 1994 the German archaeologist Klaus Schmidt saw something else: the heads of carved pillars, each shaped like a giant letter T.","Under the hill lay round enclosures ringed with pillars, the tallest about five and a half metres high, raised around 11,500 years ago.","Foxes snarl across their faces, snakes stream down their sides, vultures spread their wings.","The two central pillars of one enclosure have arms carved along their flanks, hands meeting above a belt, and a fox pelt hanging as a loincloth.","They are not just stones.","They are beings.","Schmidt believed he had found a mountain sanctuary built by hunter-gatherers, a temple that came before the town and perhaps nudged people toward farming.","The researchers who followed him have found rain cisterns, houses and thousands of grinding stones, and a dozen neighbouring hills, the Taş Tepeler, are now yielding carved heads, leopards and whole rooms cut from the living rock.","The builders still hunted gazelle and harvested wild grain.","What they made has changed how we picture the people who lived on the eve of farming."],"pronounce":{"Göbekli Tepe":"Guh-beck-lee Teh-peh","Şanlıurfa":"Shahn-luh-oor-fah","Türkiye":"Tur-kee-yeh","Taş Tepeler":"Tahsh Teh-peh-lair"},"body":[{"heading":"A hill of pillars","paragraphs":["Göbekli Tepe (“potbelly hill” in Turkish) is an artificial mound about 15 m high and some 300 m across on a limestone ridge about 15 km northeast of Şanlıurfa. A joint survey by the universities of Istanbul and Chicago recorded it in 1963 as a Neolithic site littered with flint, but took the broken limestone slabs on its surface for medieval graves. In 1994 Klaus Schmidt of the German Archaeological Institute (DAI), who had worked at the nearby site of Nevalı Çori, recognised the slabs as the tops of T-shaped pillars. Excavations with the Şanlıurfa Museum began in 1995.","The older level, Layer III, holds large round or oval enclosures. Four of them, labelled A to D, have been excavated, and geophysical surveys suggest more beneath the mound. Each has ten or more pillars set into a stone wall with benches, facing two taller central pillars. In Enclosure D, about 20 m across, the central pillars stand some 5.5 m high and weigh several tonnes each. A quarry on the plateau still holds an unfinished pillar nearly 7 m long. The younger Layer II consists of smaller rectangular rooms with smaller pillars, often only one or two."]},{"heading":"Beasts and beings in stone","paragraphs":["Carved in relief on the pillars are foxes, wild boars, snakes, aurochs, wild asses, cranes, vultures, scorpions and spiders. Gazelle, the animal most often eaten at the site, barely appears, so the imagery is not a menu but a selection, perhaps of dangerous or symbolically charged creatures. Individual enclosures seem to favour particular animals: snakes in A, foxes in B, boars in C. On Pillar 43 in Enclosure D, the so-called Vulture Stone, a vulture spreads its wings beside a disc, above a scorpion and a headless figure.","The central pillars of Enclosure D are more than posts. Arms run down their sides, long-fingered hands meet above a belt, and a fox pelt hangs from the belt like a loincloth; the T-shaped top is a stylised head. Schmidt read them as supernatural beings or ancestors. Free-standing sculptures add human heads, animals and a pole of stacked figures. In 2023 the excavators announced a life-size limestone boar from Enclosure D, 1.35 m long, which still carried red, black and white pigment, the first painted sculpture known from the period. It stood on a bench carved with an H-shaped sign, a crescent, snakes and three human faces. In 2025 a statue of a human with a clearly shaped head and torso turned up, reused and laid horizontally, in a wall between Enclosures B and D."]},{"heading":"Dating and filling","paragraphs":["Radiocarbon dates place the Layer III enclosures in the Pre-Pottery Neolithic A (PPNA), roughly 9500 to 8800 BCE, and the Layer II buildings in the early and middle Pre-Pottery Neolithic B (PPNB), down to about 8000 BCE. The dates are harder to pin down than they sound. The enclosures were filled with rubble, flint, bone and soil, so charcoal and bone inside them can be older or younger than the walls, and the team has also dated organic material trapped in wall plaster.","Schmidt believed the enclosures had been deliberately buried when their use ended, which would explain their fine preservation. Research since his death in 2014, led by Lee Clare of the DAI with Oliver Dietrich, Jens Notroff, Moritz Kinzel and Turkish colleagues, suggests a messier history: enclosures were rebuilt and modified over centuries, and slope slides from higher parts of the mound helped fill them. Some team members think the enclosures may have been roofed, a question that remains open."]},{"heading":"From sanctuary to settlement","paragraphs":["Schmidt’s interpretation was bold. Göbekli Tepe, he argued, was a hilltop sanctuary without houses or a spring, where scattered bands of hunter-gatherers came together to build and worship. “First came the temple, then the city,” he said. Feeding the workforce might have encouraged intensive harvesting of wild cereals, and a 1997 genetic study by Manfred Heun and colleagues had traced domestic einkorn wheat to the nearby Karacadağ hills.","The team that followed him has reshaped that picture. Excavations since about 2014 have found rock-cut cisterns and channels that collected rainwater, domestic buildings, hearths and more than 7,000 grinding slabs, handstones, mortars and pestles. A 2019 study led by Laura Dietrich combined use-wear, plant phytoliths and animal bones: wild cereals were ground on a large scale, and gazelle were hunted in bulk between midsummer and autumn. Because no large storage facilities have been found, the authors proposed seasonal work feasts. Big limestone vessels, some holding up to about 160 litres, may have held fermented drinks, though that remains speculative.","Every plant and animal identified at Göbekli Tepe so far is wild. Lee Clare has said he does not see the site as the “smoking gun” of the Neolithic, but rather as a monumental effort by hunter-gatherers facing a changing world. The Canadian archaeologist Edward Banning had argued in 2011 that the sharp line between temple and house is a modern one, and in 2023 he reviewed a decade of reinterpretation under the title “Paradise found or common sense lost?”"]},{"heading":"Carved skulls","paragraphs":["No graves have been found, but hundreds of fragments of human bone have, most of them from skulls. In 2017 Julia Gresky, Juliane Haelm and Lee Clare described three skull pieces with deep grooves cut along the top with flint tools, one also drilled with a hole, all bearing cut marks from defleshing. The grooves may have held cords so the skulls could be hung up or decorated. It was a form of skull cult not seen before, a local variant of the Neolithic attention to heads that appears in the plastered skulls of the Levant (chapter 33)."]},{"heading":"A landscape of stone hills","paragraphs":["Göbekli Tepe was never alone. At Nevalı Çori, excavated by Harald Hauptmann from 1983 to 1991 before the Atatürk Dam reservoir flooded it in 1992, T-shaped pillars and sculptures stood in a special building inside an ordinary village of rectangular houses. At Körtik Tepe on the upper Tigris, hundreds of PPNA burials were furnished with finely carved stone bowls. Since 2021 the Turkish Ministry of Culture and Tourism has run the Taş Tepeler (“stone hills”) project, with excavations at a dozen sites in Şanlıurfa province.","The most spectacular is Karahan Tepe, about 46 km east of Göbekli Tepe, excavated by Necmi Karul of Istanbul University. Its builders carved rooms out of the bedrock. In one, Structure AB, ten pillars shaped as phalluses rise from the rock floor while a human head on a long neck projects from the wall above them. Finds announced in recent seasons include a man carrying a leopard on his back, a 2.3 m statue of a seated man holding his phallus and, in August 2026, a 70 cm sculpture of a person sitting on a leopard’s back.","At Sayburç, near Şanlıurfa, Eylem Özdoğan excavated a rock-cut communal building whose bench carries a relief of two scenes: a man holding his phallus between two leopards, and a man with a rattle or snake facing a bull. Published in 2022, it is among the oldest narrative scenes in art. Harbetsuvan Tepesi has produced a seated male statue, and at Sefer Tepe, where the tops of about 16 T-shaped pillars show through the soil, two carved human faces were announced in 2025. The emerging picture is of a whole region of villages with special buildings, sharing a symbolic world of men, predators and the dead."]},{"heading":"Comets, calendars and lost civilisations","paragraphs":["Göbekli Tepe’s fame has invited bolder readings. In 2017 the chemical engineer Martin Sweatman and Dimitrios Tsikritsis argued that the animals on the Vulture Stone are constellations recording a comet impact around 10,950 BCE, part of the contested Younger Dryas impact hypothesis. In 2024, in the journal Time and Mind, Sweatman read V-shaped marks on the same pillar as a calendar of 365 days: twelve lunar months plus eleven extra days. The excavators and most archaeologists reject both claims (see Debates).","The site is also a favourite of pseudo-archaeology. Graham Hancock’s books and his 2022 Netflix series Ancient Apocalypse present it as the legacy of a lost, advanced Ice Age civilisation. Nothing found there supports that: the tools are flint and bone, the food is wild, and earlier villages such as Hallan Çemi and Körtik Tepe show the culture’s local roots. What the evidence does show is remarkable enough: people who lived by hunting and gathering could plan, quarry, carve and gather in numbers on a scale once thought to require farming."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Was Göbekli Tepe a sanctuary or a settlement?","views":[{"label":"A ritual centre","text":"Klaus Schmidt (1953–2014) argued that Göbekli Tepe was a mountain sanctuary without permanent houses, where dispersed hunter-gatherer groups met to build, feast and worship. He pointed to the lack of obvious dwellings in the early excavations, the absence of a spring, the figurative pillars as supernatural beings, and what he saw as the deliberate burial of the enclosures."},{"label":"A settlement with special buildings","text":"Since 2014 Lee Clare, Moritz Kinzel, Oliver Dietrich and colleagues have reported domestic buildings, rock-cut cisterns and water channels, more than 7,000 grinding tools and abundant flint-knapping waste, all signs of people living on the site. Edward Banning argued as early as 2011 that separating ‘temples’ from ‘houses’ imposes a modern distinction on Neolithic buildings that could be both."}],"status":"Most specialists now accept that people lived at Göbekli Tepe for long periods and that the enclosures were special communal buildings within a settlement, much like those at Nevalı Çori and Karahan Tepe. How many people lived there, whether the enclosures were roofed, and whether they were buried deliberately or filled by slope slides and rebuilding are still argued."},{"question":"Did monument building drive the invention of farming?","views":[{"label":"Temple first","text":"Schmidt suggested that the need to feed large work parties and gatherings encouraged intensive harvesting and eventually cultivation of wild cereals, noting that einkorn wheat was domesticated in the nearby Karacadağ hills. The idea echoes Jacques Cauvin’s argument that a change in beliefs preceded farming."},{"label":"Feasting hunter-gatherers","text":"The current team stresses that every plant and animal identified at Göbekli Tepe is wild, that gazelle hunting dominated, and that grinding and feasting were part of a hunter-gatherer economy. Lee Clare has described the monuments as a response by hunter-gatherers to social and environmental change rather than the trigger of farming, which emerged slowly across the whole Fertile Crescent (chapter 33)."}],"status":"No domesticated plants or animals have been identified at Göbekli Tepe. Most researchers now treat monument building, feasting and the slow shift to cultivation as parts of one long transformation, in which communal work and ritual helped create the social ties that settled life needed, rather than one simply causing the other."},{"question":"Do the carvings record a comet and a calendar?","views":[{"label":"An astronomical code","text":"Martin Sweatman of the University of Edinburgh, with Dimitrios Tsikritsis (2017), matched animals on Pillar 43 to constellations and derived a date of about 10,950 BCE, which he links to a proposed comet impact at the start of the Younger Dryas. In a 2024 paper in Time and Mind he interpreted V-shaped marks as a 365-day lunisolar calendar and extended the reading to Karahan Tepe."},{"label":"Local symbolism","text":"The excavators, in a 2017 reply by Jens Notroff, Oliver Dietrich, Lee Clare and colleagues, pointed out that Enclosure D was built more than a thousand years after the supposed event, that the same animals recur across the region’s iconography, that linking them to much later Greek or Mesopotamian constellations is arbitrary, and that the Younger Dryas impact hypothesis itself is rejected by most specialists. Counting V-signs as days, critics add, depends on selective choices."}],"status":"The astronomical readings have little support among archaeologists who work on the site or the period, although they circulate widely in popular books and online. A convincing case would need predictions tested against the whole corpus of carvings rather than one selected pillar."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Tallest pillars","value":"About 5.5 m, the central pillars of Enclosure D"},{"label":"Oldest enclosures","value":"c. 9500 BCE (Layer III, radiocarbon)"},{"label":"Grinding tools","value":"More than 7,000 recovered"},{"label":"Taş Tepeler project","value":"About 12 sites in Şanlıurfa province, since 2021"},{"label":"Painted boar","value":"1.35 m long, red, black and white pigment (announced 2023)"},{"label":"World Heritage","value":"Göbekli Tepe, inscribed by UNESCO in 2018"}],"siteIds":["gobekli-tepe","karahan-tepe","nevali-cori","sayburc","kortik-tepe"],"routes":[],"themes":["monuments","art","society","food","settlement","burial"],"sources":[{"cite":"Schmidt, K. (2012). Göbekli Tepe: A Stone Age Sanctuary in South-Eastern Anatolia. Berlin: ex oriente / ArchaeNova."},{"cite":"Dietrich, O., Köksal-Schmidt, Ç., Notroff, J. & Schmidt, K. (2013). Establishing a radiocarbon sequence for Göbekli Tepe: state of research and new data. Neo-Lithics 1/13."},{"cite":"Gresky, J., Haelm, J. & Clare, L. (2017). Modified human crania from Göbekli Tepe provide evidence for a new form of Neolithic skull cult. Science Advances 3, e1700564.","doi":"10.1126/sciadv.1700564"},{"cite":"Notroff, J. et al. (2017). More than a vulture: a response to Sweatman and Tsikritsis. Mediterranean Archaeology and Archaeometry 17(2), 57–63."},{"cite":"Dietrich, L. et al. (2019). Cereal processing at Early Neolithic Göbekli Tepe, southeastern Turkey. PLOS ONE 14, e0215214.","doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0215214"},{"cite":"Clare, L. (2020). Göbekli Tepe, Turkey. A brief summary of research at a new World Heritage Site (2015–2019). e-Forschungsberichte des Deutschen Archäologischen Instituts 2020/2."},{"cite":"Karul, N. (2021). Buried buildings at Pre-Pottery Neolithic Karahantepe. Türk Arkeoloji ve Etnografya Dergisi 82."},{"cite":"Özdoğan, E. (2022). The Sayburç reliefs: a narrative scene from the Neolithic. Antiquity 96."},{"cite":"Banning, E. B. (2023). Paradise found or common sense lost? Göbekli Tepe’s last decade as a pre-farming cult centre. Open Archaeology 9.","doi":"10.1515/opar-2022-0317"},{"cite":"Sweatman, M. B. (2024). Representations of calendars and time at Göbekli Tepe and Karahan Tepe support an astronomical interpretation of their symbolism. Time and Mind 17.","doi":"10.1080/1751696X.2024.2373876"}]},{"id":"mesolithic-europe","order":31,"act":"warming","title":"Europe’s Last Hunter-Gatherers","subtitle":"Star Carr, Doggerland, Lepenski Vir and the Mesolithic world","era":"Mesolithic","start":12100,"end":6000,"dateLabel":"c. 10,000–4000 BCE","regions":["europe"],"focus":[-10,42,62,65],"emblem":"harpoon","summary":"As forests spread over a warming Europe, hunter-gatherers built timber platforms at Star Carr, carved a towering wooden idol in the Urals, paddled dugouts across Doggerland and raised houses beside the Danube at Lepenski Vir. Ancient genomes now show who they were, what some of them looked like, and how they met the first farmers.","paragraphs":[["While pillars were rising at Göbekli Tepe, on the shore of a vanished lake in North Yorkshire, someone took the skull of a red deer, cut away most of the bone, thinned the antlers and bored holes so the whole thing could be tied on.","Thirty-three of these antler headdresses have come out of the peat at Star Carr.","The ice sheets had gone.","Birch and pine were closing in, and the people who wore them lived in a new world of forests."],["Europe in these millennia was a continent of woods, rivers and restless coasts.","Between Britain and Denmark lay Doggerland, a low country of marshes and hills that the rising North Sea swallowed piece by piece.","People paddled dugout canoes, set fish traps, gathered hazelnuts by the basketful and buried their dead with care.","In one Danish grave, a newborn baby was laid on the wing of a swan."],["Their genomes now tell us what some of them looked like.","A man buried in Cheddar Gorge about 10,000 years ago most likely had dark skin and blue or green eyes, a combination rare in Europe today.","And their story did not end the moment farmers arrived from the southeast.","Beside the whirlpools of the Danube at Lepenski Vir, and in a cave in western Germany, hunters and farmers lived as neighbours for centuries, trading, marrying, and sometimes keeping to themselves."]],"sentences":["While pillars were rising at Göbekli Tepe, on the shore of a vanished lake in North Yorkshire, someone took the skull of a red deer, cut away most of the bone, thinned the antlers and bored holes so the whole thing could be tied on.","Thirty-three of these antler headdresses have come out of the peat at Star Carr.","The ice sheets had gone.","Birch and pine were closing in, and the people who wore them lived in a new world of forests.","Europe in these millennia was a continent of woods, rivers and restless coasts.","Between Britain and Denmark lay Doggerland, a low country of marshes and hills that the rising North Sea swallowed piece by piece.","People paddled dugout canoes, set fish traps, gathered hazelnuts by the basketful and buried their dead with care.","In one Danish grave, a newborn baby was laid on the wing of a swan.","Their genomes now tell us what some of them looked like.","A man buried in Cheddar Gorge about 10,000 years ago most likely had dark skin and blue or green eyes, a combination rare in Europe today.","And their story did not end the moment farmers arrived from the southeast.","Beside the whirlpools of the Danube at Lepenski Vir, and in a cave in western Germany, hunters and farmers lived as neighbours for centuries, trading, marrying, and sometimes keeping to themselves."],"pronounce":{"Star Carr":"Star Car","Doggerland":"Dogger-land","Lepenski Vir":"Leh-pen-skee Veer"},"body":[{"heading":"A continent of forests","paragraphs":["The Mesolithic, or Middle Stone Age, is the name European archaeologists give to the hunter-gatherers who lived between the end of the Ice Age, about 9700 BCE, and the arrival of farming, which reached Greece before 6500 BCE but Britain and Scandinavia only around 4000 BCE. As the climate warmed, birch and pine spread north, followed by hazel and then oak, elm and lime. Reindeer and wild horse gave way to red deer, roe deer, elk, wild boar and aurochs. Rising seas drowned old coastlines and created new ones rich in fish, seals and shellfish.","Mesolithic toolkits are known for microliths, tiny flint pieces set into arrows and knives, but waterlogged sites reveal a world made largely of wood, bark, antler and fibre: bows, fish traps, nets, dugout canoes and paddles. Dogs were already companions and hunting partners."]},{"heading":"Star Carr","paragraphs":["Star Carr, on the edge of the former Lake Flixton in North Yorkshire, is Britain’s best-known Mesolithic site. Grahame Clark excavated it from 1949 to 1951, and Nicky Milner, Chantal Conneller and Barry Taylor returned between 2004 and 2015. People came back to the lakeshore over several centuries around 9000 BCE. They laid platforms of split timbers along the water’s edge, among the earliest carpentry of this kind in Europe, and built at least one circular post-built structure about 3.5 m across, claimed as Britain’s oldest known house.","The most striking finds are 33 frontlets: red deer and a few roe deer skull caps with the antlers left attached and thinned, pierced so they could be worn. Barbed antler points, shale beads and a small shale pendant engraved with lines, found in 2015, fill out a rich material world. Star Carr belongs to a broader tradition of lakeside sites across northern Europe often called the Maglemosian, after a bog on the Danish island of Zealand."]},{"heading":"Doggerland and the first boats","paragraphs":["Around 10,000 years ago Britain was a peninsula of the continent. Between it and Denmark lay Doggerland, a lowland of rivers, marshes and hills that the rising North Sea drowned in stages. Trawlers and sand dredgers have brought up its traces: a barbed antler point hauled up in 1931 by the trawler Colinda between the Leman and Ower banks, and, from the Brown Bank area off the Netherlands, Mesolithic bone and antler tools and human bones. Vince Gaffney’s teams have mapped its drowned valleys and lakes from seismic survey data and cored their sediments. Around 6150 BCE the Storegga landslide off Norway sent a tsunami across the North Sea, and soon afterwards Britain was an island.","Boats made this watery world work. The dugout from Pesse in the Netherlands, found in 1955 during road building, is a roughly 3 m long trunk of Scots pine radiocarbon dated to around 8000 BCE, the oldest known boat anywhere. At Tybrind Vig, an Ertebølle settlement now under the sea off the Danish island of Funen, divers found dugouts nearly 10 m long, decorated ash paddles, fish weirs and fragments of textile.","The Ertebølle culture of southern Scandinavia (about 5400 to 3950 BCE) left great shell middens, the “kitchen middens” that nineteenth-century Danish scientists first recognised as the refuse of human meals. From around 4700 BCE Ertebølle people made pottery, pointed-based jars and small oval bowls usually interpreted as lamps, though they did not farm (chapter 32 follows hunter-gatherer pottery across Eurasia)."]},{"heading":"Faces from the genome","paragraphs":["Ancient DNA divides Mesolithic Europeans into distinct groups. Western Hunter-Gatherers (WHG), descended largely from people who spread across Europe from the south and southeast around 14,000 years ago, lived from Iberia to Hungary and Britain. Eastern Hunter-Gatherers (EHG), in Russia and Karelia, carried much ancestry from Ice Age Siberians like the boy from Mal’ta. At Yuzhny Oleni Ostrov, an island cemetery in Lake Onega with more than 170 graves roughly 8,000 years old, many people were buried with elk-tooth pendants and carved elk heads. Scandinavian Hunter-Gatherers were a mixture of the two. A 2024 study led by Morten Allentoft, drawing on more than 1,600 ancient genomes, found a sharp genetic boundary running from the Black Sea to the Baltic.","Cheddar Man, a young man buried in Gough’s Cave in Somerset about 10,000 years ago and found in 1903, had his genome sequenced in 2018 by the Natural History Museum and University College London; it was published in 2019 in a study led by Selina Brace. His DNA lacked the variants that lighten skin in most Europeans today, so he most likely had dark or dark-to-black skin, while variants linked to eye colour point to blue or green eyes. Other Western Hunter-Gatherers, from Loschbour in Luxembourg to La Braña in Spain, show the same combination. Light skin became common in Europe only later, carried by farmers and steppe migrants and favoured by natural selection."]},{"heading":"Idols, graves and violence","paragraphs":["Hunter-gatherers also made monumental art. The Shigir idol was dug out of a peat bog near Kirovgrad in the Middle Urals by gold miners in 1890. Carved from a larch trunk with faces and geometric signs, it stood about 5.3 m tall before parts were lost; about 2.8 m survive on display. Dating by Mikhail Zhilin, Thomas Terberger and colleagues put it at about 11,600 years old in 2018, and a later tree-ring study suggested it was carved about 12,000 years ago. It is the oldest known wooden sculpture in the world.","Burials show tenderness and ritual. At Vedbæk-Bøgebakken north of Copenhagen, around 5000 BCE, a young woman was buried with a newborn laid on a swan’s wing beside her, a flint blade at the baby’s waist. At Skateholm in southern Sweden, dogs were given their own graves, some with red ochre, antlers or flint blades. In 1908, at Ofnet cave in Bavaria, excavators found two pits holding more than 30 human heads, arranged facing west and adorned with shell and deer-tooth ornaments. Many skulls bear blows and cut marks from decapitation, which some read as the aftermath of a massacre roughly 8,500 years ago and others as a funerary rite for people who died at different times."]},{"heading":"Lepenski Vir and the Iron Gates","paragraphs":["Where the Danube cuts through the Carpathians in the Iron Gates gorge, hunter-fishers lived for millennia beside whirlpools rich in sturgeon and carp. At Lepenski Vir in Serbia, whose name refers to one of those whirlpools, Dragoslav Srejović excavated from 1965 to 1970 before a dam flooded the valley. The settlement’s trapezoidal houses, dated mostly to about 6200 to 5900 BCE, had hard lime-plaster floors and stone-lined hearths and were set in a fan facing the river. Around the hearths stood boulder sculptures: river stones carved into faces with fish-like mouths and staring eyes. Isotopes in the bones show a diet rich in fish.","Lepenski Vir flourished just as farmers reached the central Balkans, and the two worlds met there. Early Neolithic pottery turns up at Iron Gates sites. A 2013 strontium isotope study by Dušan Borić and T. Douglas Price found more incomers, many of them women, buried in this period, and genomes published in 2018 by Iain Mathieson and colleagues show individuals with Anatolian farmer ancestry alongside local hunter-gatherers. Whether the sculptures and houses were a flowering of local tradition or a response to the newcomers is still debated."]},{"heading":"Neighbours of the first farmers","paragraphs":["Farmers did not simply sweep Europe’s hunter-gatherers aside. At the Blätterhöhle cave in Hagen, western Germany, Ruth Bollongino and colleagues showed in 2013 that people of hunter-gatherer ancestry, eating a diet rich in freshwater fish, were buried in the same cave as farmers until about 3000 BCE, some 2,000 years after farming reached central Europe. On the Danish island of Lolland, a lump of birch pitch chewed about 5,700 years ago at Syltholm preserved the complete genome of a woman with Western Hunter-Gatherer ancestry, dark skin, dark hair and blue eyes, along with DNA from the hazelnuts and mallard duck she had eaten and from her mouth bacteria. The study, led by Theis Jensen and Hannes Schroeder, appeared in 2019.","When the end came, it could be abrupt. In Britain, genomes show that farmers arriving around 4000 BCE largely replaced the local population, and the 2024 study led by Allentoft found a similar near-complete turnover in Denmark around 3900 BCE. Further east, in the Baltic and the Russian forests, hunter-gatherers kept their ancestry and way of life far longer, adopting pottery and some domestic animals on their own terms."]}],"debates":[{"question":"What did Cheddar Man look like?","views":[{"label":"Dark skin, light eyes","text":"The team led by Selina Brace and Ian Barnes used variants in pigmentation genes such as SLC24A5 and SLC45A2 to predict that Cheddar Man most likely had dark to black skin and blue or green eyes. Other Western Hunter-Gatherer genomes, such as Loschbour and La Braña, show the same pattern, and the widely publicised 2018 model by the Kennis brothers was based on it."},{"label":"A probabilistic guess","text":"Other geneticists, and members of the team themselves, stressed that pigmentation prediction relies on variants studied mainly in living Europeans. Unknown variants may have affected his skin, so the prediction gives probabilities, not a portrait, and the exact shade cannot be known."}],"status":"Most geneticists accept that Western Hunter-Gatherers lacked the main light-skin variants common in Europe today, so their skin was probably considerably darker than that of modern Britons. Light skin spread later through farmer and steppe ancestry and natural selection. Precise appearance remains an informed reconstruction."},{"question":"How did Europe’s hunter-gatherers meet the first farmers?","views":[{"label":"Replacement","text":"Ancient DNA shows that the first farmers in most of Europe descended from Anatolian populations. In Britain around 4000 BCE (Brace et al. 2019) and Denmark around 3900 BCE (Allentoft et al. 2024), incoming farmers replaced the local hunter-gatherer population almost completely within a few centuries."},{"label":"Coexistence and mixing","text":"Other evidence shows long contact. At the Blätterhöhle, hunter-fisher-gatherers lived alongside farmers for two millennia; at Lepenski Vir, farmers and foragers intermarried; and hunter-gatherer ancestry rose steadily within farming populations. Marek Zvelebil and Peter Rowley-Conwy’s ‘availability model’ described long frontier zones in which foragers adopted farming gradually."}],"status":"Both happened. The outcome varied by region: rapid turnover in Britain and southern Scandinavia, centuries of coexistence and gradual mixing in central and southeastern Europe, and long hunter-gatherer continuity in the eastern Baltic and Russian forest zone."},{"question":"Were the Star Carr headdresses hunting disguises or ritual regalia?","views":[{"label":"Practical disguise","text":"Grahame Clark, who found the first 21 frontlets, suggested they might have been worn while stalking deer, as some hunters have done in historic times, or in dances that re-enacted the hunt."},{"label":"Ritual transformation","text":"Chantal Conneller, Nicky Milner and colleagues argue that the careful thinning of the antlers, the fragility of the finished objects and their deliberate deposition at the lake edge point to ritual use, perhaps performances in which wearers took on the identity of deer, a practice known among some Siberian peoples."}],"status":"Most researchers now favour a ritual role, supported by the way the frontlets were placed in the lake, but practical and ritual uses need not exclude each other, and no direct evidence shows how they were worn."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Oldest wooden sculpture","value":"Shigir idol, Urals, carved about 12,000 years ago"},{"label":"Star Carr headdresses","value":"33 deer frontlets, c. 9000 BCE"},{"label":"Oldest known boat","value":"Pesse canoe, Netherlands, c. 8000 BCE"},{"label":"Cheddar Man","value":"About 10,000 years old; genome published 2019"},{"label":"Genome from ‘chewing gum’","value":"Syltholm birch pitch, Denmark, 5,700 years old (2019)"},{"label":"Parallel societies","value":"Hunter-gatherers beside farmers at the Blätterhöhle until c. 3000 BCE"}],"siteIds":["star-carr","goughs-cave","pesse","vedbaek-bogebakken","syltholm","lepenski-vir","shigir","ofnet","blatterhohle","ertebolle"],"routes":[],"themes":["technology","art","burial","genetics","seafaring","food","climate","violence"],"sources":[{"cite":"Milner, N., Conneller, C. & Taylor, B. (2018). Star Carr, Volume 1: A Persistent Place in a Changing World. York: White Rose University Press."},{"cite":"Gaffney, V., Fitch, S. & Smith, D. (2009). Europe’s Lost World: The Rediscovery of Doggerland. York: Council for British Archaeology."},{"cite":"Zhilin, M., Savchenko, S., Hansen, S., Heussner, K.-U. & Terberger, T. (2018). Early art in the Urals: new research on the wooden sculpture from Shigir. Antiquity 92."},{"cite":"Brace, S. et al. (2019). Ancient genomes indicate population replacement in Early Neolithic Britain. Nature Ecology & Evolution 3, 765–771."},{"cite":"Jensen, T. Z. T. et al. (2019). A 5700 year-old human genome and oral microbiome from chewed birch pitch. Nature Communications 10, 5520."},{"cite":"Bollongino, R. et al. (2013). 2000 years of parallel societies in Stone Age Central Europe. Science 342, 479–481."},{"cite":"Borić, D. & Price, T. D. (2013). Strontium isotopes document greater human mobility at the start of the Balkan Neolithic. PNAS 110, 3298–3303."},{"cite":"Mathieson, I. et al. (2018). The genomic history of southeastern Europe. Nature 555, 197–203."},{"cite":"Allentoft, M. E. et al. (2024). Population genomics of post-glacial western Eurasia. Nature 625, 301–311.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-023-06865-0"}]},{"id":"pottery-east-asia","order":32,"act":"warming","title":"Pots Before Farms","subtitle":"Hunter-gatherer pottery and the Jōmon world","era":"Late Upper Palaeolithic to Jōmon","start":20000,"end":3000,"dateLabel":"c. 18,000–1000 BCE","regions":["asia"],"focus":[108,22,146,50],"emblem":"pottery","summary":"In East Asia people made pottery long before anyone farmed: at Xianrendong in China some 20,000 years ago, and in Japan and the Amur basin soon after. In Japan, Jōmon foragers went on making pots, figurines and long-lived villages for more than ten thousand years, while managing forests and seas.","paragraphs":[["At the far end of Eurasia from Star Carr, people had been cooking in clay for thousands of years before anyone there planted a crop.","In Xianrendong Cave in southern China, fragments of pots come from layers dated to about 20,000 years ago, in the depths of the last Ice Age.","Pottery, it turns out, was an invention of hunter-gatherers."],["In Japan the oldest pots are a few thousand years younger, and chemists have scraped their blackened crusts to learn what was cooked in them: fish and shellfish, again and again.","From these beginnings grew the Jōmon, named for the cord patterns pressed into its clay.","For more than ten thousand years Jōmon people fished, gathered chestnuts and walnuts, hunted deer and boar, and built villages that lasted for generations.","At Sannai-Maruyama, in the far north of Honshu, a settlement lived in for some 1,700 years grew up around a structure raised on six chestnut posts, each about a metre thick."],["They fired some of the most extravagant pottery ever made, vessels crowned with swirling flames of clay, and thousands of figurines with masks and goggle eyes.","They were not farmers in any ordinary sense, yet they tended chestnut groves and domesticated beans.","Their skeletons show remarkably few signs of violence, and their genes still run in people across the Japanese islands today."]],"sentences":["At the far end of Eurasia from Star Carr, people had been cooking in clay for thousands of years before anyone there planted a crop.","In Xianrendong Cave in southern China, fragments of pots come from layers dated to about 20,000 years ago, in the depths of the last Ice Age.","Pottery, it turns out, was an invention of hunter-gatherers.","In Japan the oldest pots are a few thousand years younger, and chemists have scraped their blackened crusts to learn what was cooked in them: fish and shellfish, again and again.","From these beginnings grew the Jōmon, named for the cord patterns pressed into its clay.","For more than ten thousand years Jōmon people fished, gathered chestnuts and walnuts, hunted deer and boar, and built villages that lasted for generations.","At Sannai-Maruyama, in the far north of Honshu, a settlement lived in for some 1,700 years grew up around a structure raised on six chestnut posts, each about a metre thick.","They fired some of the most extravagant pottery ever made, vessels crowned with swirling flames of clay, and thousands of figurines with masks and goggle eyes.","They were not farmers in any ordinary sense, yet they tended chestnut groves and domesticated beans.","Their skeletons show remarkably few signs of violence, and their genes still run in people across the Japanese islands today."],"pronounce":{"Xianrendong":"Shyen-ren-dong","Jōmon":"Joh-mon","Sannai-Maruyama":"San-nigh Mah-roo-yah-mah","Honshu":"Hon-shoo"},"body":[{"heading":"Pottery before farming","paragraphs":["For a century archaeologists treated pottery as part of a “Neolithic package” that arrived together with farming, villages and polished stone axes. East Asia broke that package apart. In 2012 a team led by Wu Xiaohong and Ofer Bar-Yosef reported radiocarbon dates from Xianrendong Cave in Jiangxi province, southern China: the layers holding the oldest sherds date to between about 20,000 and 19,000 years ago, near the coldest part of the last Ice Age. Some sherds are scorched on the outside, as if from cooking. At Yuchanyan Cave in Hunan, Elisabetta Boaretto and colleagues dated pottery to roughly 18,000 to 15,000 years ago.","In northern Japan, charred residue on plain sherds from Odai Yamamoto I has been dated to about 16,500 years ago, and in the Amur River basin of the Russian Far East, at sites such as Gasya and Khummi, pottery appears from about 16,000 years ago. The people who made these vessels were mobile or semi-settled hunter-gatherers and fishers. Whether pottery was invented once and spread, or several times in different parts of East Asia, is unresolved; differences in shape, clay and timing make several separate beginnings plausible."]},{"heading":"What the first pots cooked","paragraphs":["Chemistry can reveal what went into a pot. Fats and oils soak into porous clay and survive for millennia, and their molecular makeup and carbon isotopes point to their source. In 2013 Oliver Craig and colleagues analysed charred deposits on 101 sherds from Incipient Jōmon sites across Japan, roughly 15,000 to 11,800 years old. Most held fats from fish and shellfish, marine or freshwater. A 2016 study led by Alexandre Lucquin found the same aquatic signal continuing through some 9,000 years of Jōmon history. Studies of early pots from the Russian Far East have found aquatic signals too, although some groups also cooked land animals and plants, so the new containers served different needs in different places.","Why would hunter-gatherers bother with heavy, breakable containers? Boiling makes it possible to render oil from fish, open shellfish, soften tough plants, leach bitter acorns and make soups for infants and the old. Brian Hayden has argued that the earliest pots, which are rare, were prestige vessels for feasts. The residue evidence so far favours everyday processing of aquatic foods, at least in Japan."]},{"heading":"Across Eurasia","paragraphs":["From the Pacific, hunter-gatherer pottery spread west across Siberia and the steppe over the following millennia, reaching the Volga region by around 7000 BCE and the Baltic and the Ertebølle foragers of Denmark by about 4700 BCE (chapter 31). Modelling by Peter Jordan and colleagues in 2016 traced this as a slow dispersal among hunter-gatherers across northern Eurasia, separate from the pottery that farmers in Southwest Asia adopted around 7000 BCE and from the independent invention of pottery in the Sahara (chapter 36)."]},{"heading":"Ten thousand years of Jōmon","paragraphs":["In 1877 the American zoologist Edward S. Morse, digging the Ōmori shell mound in Tokyo, described its pottery as “cord marked”. Translated into Japanese as Jōmon, the phrase became the name of a culture that lasted from about 14,500 BCE until rice farming arrived in the first millennium BCE. Archaeologists divide it into six periods: Incipient, Initial, Early, Middle, Late and Final. Populations peaked in the Middle Jōmon, roughly 3500 to 2500 BCE, especially in the chestnut and walnut forests of central and northeastern Honshu.","Jōmon potters made some of the most extravagant vessels ever fired. Middle Jōmon “flame-style” pots from the Shinano River basin in Niigata have rims that erupt into swirling crests; a group from the Sasayama site is a National Treasure. They also made thousands of clay figurines called dogū: pregnant women, masked figures, and the goggle-eyed dogū of the Final Jōmon, named for eyes that resemble snow goggles. Many were deliberately broken. Lacquer from the urushi tree was used early: red lacquered threads from the Kakinoshima site in Hokkaido are about 9,000 years old. Around the coasts, thousands of shell middens record meals of clams, oysters, fish, seals and deer."]},{"heading":"Sannai-Maruyama","paragraphs":["Sannai-Maruyama, in Aomori at the northern tip of Honshu, came to light in 1992 during work on a baseball stadium; the project was cancelled and the site preserved. It was lived in from about 3900 to 2200 BCE. Excavators have found hundreds of pit dwellings, long communal buildings, rows of adult graves along a road, infants buried in pots, and mounds of discarded pottery and figurines. Its best-known feature is a rectangle of six post holes, 4.2 m apart, that held chestnut trunks about a metre across. How tall the structure stood, and whether it had floors or a roof, is unknown; the full-size reconstruction on the site is one guess.","Pollen from the site is dominated by chestnut, and studies of chestnut wood and DNA suggest that the villagers managed groves of the trees. Jadeite from Itoigawa, more than 400 km to the southwest, and obsidian from Hokkaido show exchange networks reaching across seas and mountains."]},{"heading":"Foragers who cultivated","paragraphs":["The Jōmon were long described as “affluent foragers” with no need to farm. That picture has blurred. Impressions of seeds in pottery, cast in silicone and studied by Hiroki Obata and others, show soybeans and azuki beans growing larger over the Middle Jōmon, a sign of cultivation and early domestication. In 2011 Gyoung-Ah Lee, Gary Crawford and colleagues argued that soybean was domesticated separately in Japan, Korea and China. Jōmon people also grew bottle gourd, managed chestnut and lacquer trees, and may have domesticated barnyard millet in the north. None of this made them dependent on crops. Wet rice farming arrived with the Yayoi culture, in northern Kyushu from around 1000 to 800 BCE according to radiocarbon dates, and spread north over the following centuries."]},{"heading":"Genes, peace and neighbours","paragraphs":["Jōmon genomes, such as those from Funadomari on Rebun Island and the Ikawazu shell mound in Aichi, belong to a deeply divergent East Asian lineage that became isolated on the archipelago early, perhaps during or before the Last Glacial Maximum, although estimates vary widely. In 2021 Niall Cooke, Shigeki Nakagome and colleagues showed that modern Japanese carry three layers of ancestry: Jōmon, a northeast Asian component that came with Yayoi rice farmers, and an East Asian component that arrived in the Kofun period. The Ainu of Hokkaido retain the most Jōmon ancestry, Okinawans the next most, and mainland Japanese a minority share.","A 2016 survey by Hisashi Nakao and colleagues found signs of violent injury on only about 1.8 percent of Jōmon skeletons, low compared with many prehistoric societies. Across the Korea Strait, the Chulmun (or Jeulmun) comb-pattern pottery culture flourished from around 8000 BCE, with still older sherds at Gosan-ri on Jeju Island, and its foragers took up millet cultivation from around 3500 BCE. In 2021 seventeen Jōmon sites in Hokkaido and northern Tohoku, including Sannai-Maruyama, became a UNESCO World Heritage Site."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Why did hunter-gatherers invent pottery?","views":[{"label":"Processing aquatic foods","text":"Oliver Craig, Alexandre Lucquin, Peter Jordan and colleagues point to lipid residues showing that early Jōmon and Russian Far Eastern pots were mostly used for fish and shellfish, perhaps to render oil, cook seasonal gluts and store food. On this view pottery was a practical tool for intensive use of rivers and coasts."},{"label":"Feasting and prestige","text":"Brian Hayden has argued that the first pots were costly, scarce objects used in competitive feasts, which would explain why so few vessels appear at the earliest sites."},{"label":"Cooking in the cold","text":"Researchers working in southern China, including the Xianrendong team, have suggested that boiling helped people extract more energy from limited foods, including starchy plants and bone grease, during the harsh conditions of the Last Glacial Maximum."}],"status":"Residue evidence supports aquatic processing in Japan and the Russian Far East, but the oldest Chinese pots have been less studied chemically. Most researchers now think pottery was invented more than once in East Asia and served different purposes in different places."},{"question":"Were the Jōmon farmers?","views":[{"label":"Affluent foragers","text":"The classic view holds that abundant nuts, fish, shellfish and game allowed the Jōmon to live in large, long-lasting villages without farming, making them a textbook case of complex hunter-gatherers."},{"label":"Low-level food producers","text":"Gary Crawford, Hiroki Obata, Junko Habu and others emphasise managed chestnut and lacquer groves, domesticated soybean and azuki beans, and possible barnyard millet domestication. They see the Jōmon as practising food production that never became dependence on staple crops, what Bruce Smith called low-level food production."}],"status":"Most specialists now accept that Jōmon people cultivated and domesticated some plants, but that wild foods remained the backbone of their economy until rice farming arrived with Yayoi migrants. The case shows that the line between foraging and farming is a continuum."},{"question":"Was Jōmon society unusually peaceful?","views":[{"label":"Low violence","text":"Hisashi Nakao and colleagues (2016) surveyed thousands of Jōmon skeletons and found violent injuries on about 1.8 percent, a rate lower than many estimates for prehistoric foragers, suggesting that warfare was not a constant feature of hunter-gatherer life."},{"label":"Caution","text":"Critics note that many injuries leave no mark on bone, that samples come mostly from shell middens in certain regions and periods where bones survive well, and that low rates may reflect low population density rather than a peaceful culture. Comparisons across studies use different methods."}],"status":"The low rate is widely accepted as real for the available samples, and it contrasts with the fortified villages and higher injury rates of the Yayoi period. What it says about hunter-gatherer violence in general remains contested."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Oldest pottery","value":"Xianrendong, China, about 20,000–19,000 years ago"},{"label":"Oldest pottery in Japan","value":"Odai Yamamoto I, about 16,500 years ago"},{"label":"First contents identified","value":"Fish and shellfish in Incipient Jōmon pots (2013)"},{"label":"Sannai-Maruyama","value":"c. 3900–2200 BCE, Aomori"},{"label":"Violent injuries","value":"About 1.8 percent of Jōmon skeletons (2016 study)"},{"label":"World Heritage","value":"17 Jōmon sites in northern Japan, 2021"}],"siteIds":["xianrendong","yuchanyan","odai-yamamoto-i","gasya","omori-shell-mound","sannai-maruyama","gosan-ri"],"routes":[],"themes":["technology","food","art","settlement","farming","genetics","violence"],"sources":[{"cite":"Boaretto, E. et al. (2009). Radiocarbon dating of charcoal and bone collagen associated with early pottery at Yuchanyan Cave, Hunan Province, China. PNAS 106, 9595–9600."},{"cite":"Wu, X. et al. (2012). Early pottery at 20,000 years ago in Xianrendong Cave, China. Science 336, 1696–1700."},{"cite":"Craig, O. E. et al. (2013). Earliest evidence for the use of pottery. Nature 496, 351–354."},{"cite":"Lucquin, A. et al. (2016). Ancient lipids document continuity in the use of early hunter–gatherer pottery through 9,000 years of Japanese prehistory. PNAS 113, 3991–3996."},{"cite":"Jordan, P. et al. (2016). Modelling the diffusion of pottery technologies across Afro-Eurasia: emerging insights and future research. Antiquity 90."},{"cite":"Habu, J. (2004). Ancient Jomon of Japan. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press."},{"cite":"Lee, G.-A., Crawford, G. W., Liu, L., Sasaki, Y. & Chen, X. (2011). Archaeological soybean (Glycine max) in East Asia: does size matter? PLOS ONE 6, e26720."},{"cite":"Nakao, H. et al. (2016). Violence in the prehistoric period of Japan: the spatio-temporal pattern of skeletal evidence for violence in the Jomon period. Biology Letters 12, 20160028."},{"cite":"Cooke, N. P. et al. (2021). Ancient genomics reveals tripartite origins of Japanese populations. Science Advances 7, eabh2419."}]},{"id":"first-farmers","order":33,"act":"transition","title":"The First Farmers","subtitle":"Domestication and villages in Southwest Asia, from Jericho to Çatalhöyük","era":"Pre-Pottery Neolithic A and B, early Pottery Neolithic","start":11700,"end":7950,"dateLabel":"c. 9700–5950 BCE","regions":["asia"],"focus":[30,29,50,40],"emblem":"wheat","summary":"Between about 9700 and 6000 BCE, people across the Fertile Crescent turned wild wheat, barley, lentils, goats, sheep, cattle and pigs into domesticates and built towns such as Jericho, ʿAin Ghazal and Çatalhöyük. It happened slowly, in many places and among distinct peoples, and it came at a real cost to health.","paragraphs":[["Jōmon villagers tended chestnut groves for thousands of years without ever coming to depend on crops.","In the hills and valleys of Southwest Asia, people took the other road.","Around 11,000 years ago, beside a spring in the Jordan Valley at Jericho, villagers built a round stone tower more than eight metres tall with a staircase inside.","No one is sure what it was for.","It is one of the oldest monuments raised by a community on its way to farming."],["Domestication was not a single invention.","Wild wheat and barley scatter their seeds when ripe; over centuries of harvesting and sowing, plants whose ears held on slowly became the norm, a change that took perhaps two thousand years.","Goats were herded in the Zagros mountains of Iran, cattle were tamed from the enormous wild aurochs, and settlers carried pigs, cattle and deer across the sea to Cyprus.","Ancient DNA shows that the first farmers of Iran, the Levant and Anatolia were distinct peoples, each making the change in its own way."],["The results cut both ways.","Villages grew into towns like Çatalhöyük, where people climbed in through their roofs and buried their dead beneath the floors.","Births rose, and so did tooth decay, disease and back-breaking work at the grinding stone.","Why people chose this path, and whether they ever really chose it, is one of the great arguments in archaeology."]],"sentences":["Jōmon villagers tended chestnut groves for thousands of years without ever coming to depend on crops.","In the hills and valleys of Southwest Asia, people took the other road.","Around 11,000 years ago, beside a spring in the Jordan Valley at Jericho, villagers built a round stone tower more than eight metres tall with a staircase inside.","No one is sure what it was for.","It is one of the oldest monuments raised by a community on its way to farming.","Domestication was not a single invention.","Wild wheat and barley scatter their seeds when ripe; over centuries of harvesting and sowing, plants whose ears held on slowly became the norm, a change that took perhaps two thousand years.","Goats were herded in the Zagros mountains of Iran, cattle were tamed from the enormous wild aurochs, and settlers carried pigs, cattle and deer across the sea to Cyprus.","Ancient DNA shows that the first farmers of Iran, the Levant and Anatolia were distinct peoples, each making the change in its own way.","The results cut both ways.","Villages grew into towns like Çatalhöyük, where people climbed in through their roofs and buried their dead beneath the floors.","Births rose, and so did tooth decay, disease and back-breaking work at the grinding stone.","Why people chose this path, and whether they ever really chose it, is one of the great arguments in archaeology."],"pronounce":{"Zagros":"Zag-ross","Çatalhöyük":"Chah-tahl-huh-yook"},"body":[{"heading":"Villages on the brink","paragraphs":["Archaeologists divide the early Neolithic of Southwest Asia using terms Kathleen Kenyon devised at Jericho: the Pre-Pottery Neolithic A (PPNA, about 9700 to 8800 BCE), with round houses and cultivated but still wild-type cereals, and the Pre-Pottery Neolithic B (PPNB, about 8800 to 7000 BCE), with rectangular houses, plastered floors and fully domestic plants and animals. Pottery came into use around 7000 BCE. The setting was the Fertile Crescent, the arc of hills and valleys running from the southern Levant through southeastern Anatolia to the Zagros mountains of Iran.","At Tell es-Sultan, ancient Jericho, Kenyon’s trenches in the 1950s exposed a PPNA stone wall and a solid round tower about 8.5 m high and 9 m across at the base, with an internal staircase of 22 steps, built roughly 11,000 years ago. Kenyon saw a fortification. Ofer Bar-Yosef argued in 1986 that the wall held back floods and debris flows and that the tower had a communal or ritual role. In 2008 Ran Barkai and Roy Liran showed that at sunset on the summer solstice the shadow of a nearby mountain falls across the tower, and proposed that it was a monument meant to bind the community together. Tell es-Sultan became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2023.","Storage came before domestication. At Dhraʿ, near the Dead Sea, Ian Kuijt and Bill Finlayson excavated granaries of about 9300 BCE with suspended floors on stone supports, built to keep wild barley dry and away from rodents. At Gilgal I in the Jordan Valley, Mordechai Kislev and colleagues found carbonised figs of a seedless kind that must be propagated from cuttings, and proposed in 2006 that they were the earliest domesticated crop. Critics replied that such figs also occur on wild trees and could simply have been gathered."]},{"heading":"Crops: a slow revolution","paragraphs":["The founder crops of Southwest Asia were emmer and einkorn wheat, barley, lentil, pea, chickpea, bitter vetch and flax. Domestication shows in the plants themselves: ears that no longer shatter when ripe, so that the seed depends on people to harvest and sow it, and larger grains. Measuring these traits in charred remains from many sites, Ken-ichi Tanno and George Willcox showed in 2006 that non-shattering wheat became dominant only gradually, over roughly two to three thousand years. Dorian Fuller, Robin Allaby and Eleni Asouti describe a protracted process spread across many places, with long periods of cultivating wild plants. Shahal Abbo, Avi Gopher and Simcha Lev-Yadun argue instead for faster, deliberate domestication within a core area of southeastern Turkey, where genetic studies had placed the wild ancestors of einkorn."]},{"heading":"Animals and islands","paragraphs":["Goats were among the first herd animals. At Ganj Dareh in the Zagros, Melinda Zeder and Brian Hesse showed in 2000 that around 8000 BCE people were killing mostly young males while keeping females to breed, a herding signature that appears before goats became smaller. Ancient goat genomes show that domestication drew on several wild populations across the region. Sheep were domesticated from wild mouflon in eastern Anatolia and the Zagros; at Aşıklı Höyük in Cappadocia, dung layers and urine salts inside the village track sheep and goats kept in pens during the ninth millennium BCE. Cattle were tamed from wild aurochs in the upper Euphrates and Tigris valleys around 8500 BCE; a 2012 study by Ruth Bollongino and colleagues estimated from ancient mitochondrial DNA that only about 80 wild females founded the domestic herds. Pigs were domesticated at sites such as Çayönü.","Farmers took their animals to sea. Cyprus, never joined to the mainland, was visited by hunter-gatherers by around 10,000 BCE, and by about 8600 BCE villagers at Klimonas were growing cereals. At Shillourokambos, from about 8400 BCE, settlers brought cattle, goats, sheep, pigs and fallow deer across at least 60 km of open water. There, around 7500 BCE, a cat was buried beside a person, the oldest known burial of a human with a cat, although it was probably a tamed wildcat rather than a domestic pet."]},{"heading":"Many peoples, many centres","paragraphs":["Ancient DNA has shown that farming was not the invention of a single people. In 2016 teams led by Iosif Lazaridis and by Farnaz Broushaki published genomes of early farmers from the Zagros, the Levant and Anatolia. The groups were as genetically different from one another as Europeans and East Asians are today, each descended largely from local hunter-gatherers. Crops, animals and ideas moved between them; the people mostly did not, until later mixing. Anatolian farmers went on to settle Europe (chapter 34), while ancestry related to the Zagros farmers spread east toward South Asia."]},{"heading":"Houses of the living and the dead","paragraphs":["PPNB villages grew large. ʿAin Ghazal, on the edge of modern Amman, eventually covered more than 10 hectares of rectangular houses whose lime-plaster floors consumed great quantities of fuel. The dead were often buried beneath floors and their skulls later removed. At Jericho, ʿAin Ghazal, Tell Aswad and Kfar HaHoresh, some skulls were remodelled with plaster into lifelike faces, sometimes with shells for eyes, and kept among the living. In 1983 and 1985 two pits at ʿAin Ghazal yielded about 30 statues and busts of lime plaster modelled over bundles of reeds, some nearly a metre tall, with eyes outlined in bitumen, buried roughly 9,000 years ago. At Çayönü in southeastern Anatolia, a “skull building” held the remains of hundreds of people."]},{"heading":"Çatalhöyük","paragraphs":["On the Konya plain of central Anatolia, Çatalhöyük grew between about 7100 and 5950 BCE into a town of several thousand people. Houses were packed wall to wall without streets; people entered by ladders through the roof, cooked, slept and worked in rooms whose white walls were replastered again and again, and buried their dead beneath the platforms. Walls bore paintings of vultures beside headless human bodies and crowds teasing huge wild bulls, reliefs of paired leopards, and aurochs horns set in plaster.","James Mellaart, who excavated there from 1961 to 1965, called many rooms shrines and made the site famous for its “Mother Goddess”, a clay figurine of a heavy seated woman flanked by two felines, found in a grain bin. Ian Hodder’s project from 1993 to 2017 found ritual woven through ordinary houses rather than confined to temples, that most figurines depict animals or are ambiguous, and that bone chemistry and skeletal wear show men and women eating similar diets and doing similar work. A 2025 genetic study in Science led by Eren Yüncü and Mehmet Somel of Middle East Technical University in Ankara reported that people buried in the same house were more often related through their mothers than their fathers, and that girls received grave goods more often than boys, hinting at households organised around women."]},{"heading":"The price of grain","paragraphs":["Farming fed more people, but not always better. Skeletons from early farming communities show more tooth decay from starchy diets, more growth defects in tooth enamel from childhood stress, and more infectious disease spread by crowding, waste and animals. At Abu Hureyra in Syria, Theya Molleson traced deformed toes, knees and lower backs to long hours kneeling at grinding stones. Yet births increased. Jean-Pierre Bocquet-Appel identified a Neolithic Demographic Transition: a rise in the share of children and adolescents in cemeteries that signals higher fertility, probably due to settled life and soft weaning foods. Populations grew even as individual health often declined.","Why did people do it? In the 1930s V. Gordon Childe called it the Neolithic Revolution and tied it to a drying climate. Later explanations have pointed to the stress of the Younger Dryas cold spell, the stable warm climate and higher carbon dioxide of the Holocene that made cultivation reliable, population pressure, competitive feasting (Brian Hayden), a “revolution of symbols” in which new beliefs came first (Jacques Cauvin, 1994), and niche construction, the gradual reshaping of environments by people, plants and animals together (Bruce Smith, Melinda Zeder). James C. Scott’s Against the Grain (2017) stressed the drudgery and disease of grain farming and its later role in making states possible, since grain can be seen, measured and taxed. David Graeber and David Wengrow’s The Dawn of Everything (2021) described three thousand years of “play farming”, in which people experimented with cultivation and could, and sometimes did, walk away from it."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Why did people start farming?","views":[{"label":"Climate","text":"Ofer Bar-Yosef and others linked the shift to the Younger Dryas cold spell, which stressed wild food supplies, while Peter Richerson, Robert Boyd and Robert Bettinger argued that farming was nearly impossible in the unstable Pleistocene and almost inevitable in the stable, carbon dioxide rich Holocene."},{"label":"Population pressure","text":"Lewis Binford and Mark Nathan Cohen proposed that growing populations, especially in marginal zones, forced people to intensify food production even at the cost of more work."},{"label":"Social and symbolic drivers","text":"Jacques Cauvin argued that a ‘revolution of symbols’ preceded farming, and Brian Hayden that ambitious individuals staged competitive feasts that demanded surplus food. Monuments such as Göbekli Tepe (chapter 30) fit these ideas."},{"label":"Niche construction","text":"Bruce Smith and Melinda Zeder see farming as the outcome of long, gradual relationships between people, plants and animals in rich environments, not a response to crisis."}],"status":"Few researchers now defend a single cause. A stable Holocene climate seems to have been a precondition, and most see social life, long-term niche construction and local ecological conditions interacting differently in each region of the Fertile Crescent."},{"question":"Was domestication fast or slow, in one centre or many?","views":[{"label":"Rapid, core area","text":"Shahal Abbo, Avi Gopher and Simcha Lev-Yadun argue that the founder crops were domesticated quickly and consciously in a core area of southeastern Turkey, pointing to genetic studies that locate wild einkorn and chickpea ancestors there."},{"label":"Protracted, dispersed","text":"Dorian Fuller, Robin Allaby, George Willcox and Eleni Asouti point to archaeobotanical sequences in which domestic traits spread slowly over two to three millennia at many sites across the Levant, Anatolia and the Zagros, with long phases of cultivating wild-type plants."}],"status":"The protracted, multi-centred model is now more widely accepted, strengthened by genetic evidence that the Zagros, Levantine and Anatolian farmers were distinct populations. Whether early farmers selected plants deliberately or domestication traits spread as unconscious side-effects of harvesting remains debated."},{"question":"Was there a Mother Goddess at Çatalhöyük?","views":[{"label":"A goddess-centred religion","text":"James Mellaart interpreted seated female figurines, bull horns and wall reliefs as evidence of a mother goddess cult, an idea taken up by Marija Gimbutas and by the modern goddess movement, which saw Neolithic Anatolia as woman-centred."},{"label":"Ritual in everyday life","text":"Ian Hodder and his team found that most figurines are animals or of uncertain sex, that ritual was part of ordinary houses, and that men and women had similar diets and workloads. On this view the seated woman may represent a powerful older woman rather than a deity."}],"status":"Few archaeologists now accept a single Mother Goddess, but the 2025 genetic study reporting female-line kinship in households and more grave goods for girls suggests that women held a central social position. How that translated into belief is still open."},{"question":"Was farming a trap or a triumph?","views":[{"label":"A costly trap","text":"Mark Nathan Cohen and George Armelagos compiled skeletal evidence for declining health with farming, Jared Diamond called it the ‘worst mistake in the history of the human race’, and James C. Scott argued that grain agriculture tied people to drudgery and, later, to taxing states."},{"label":"A choice with benefits","text":"Jean-Pierre Bocquet-Appel emphasised that farming allowed more children to be born and populations to boom, and David Graeber and David Wengrow argue that early cultivation was flexible and reversible, not a trap, with people moving in and out of farming for millennia."}],"status":"Both effects are real: farming populations grew fast while many individuals were less healthy. Research now focuses on regional differences, since health declines were marked in some regions and mild in others."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Tower of Jericho","value":"About 8.5 m high, built roughly 11,000 years ago"},{"label":"Early granaries","value":"Dhraʿ, Jordan, c. 9300 BCE, before domestic cereals"},{"label":"Founder crops","value":"Emmer, einkorn, barley, lentil, pea, chickpea, bitter vetch, flax"},{"label":"Early goat herding","value":"Ganj Dareh, Iran, c. 8000 BCE"},{"label":"Cattle founders","value":"About 80 wild females (2012 ancient DNA estimate)"},{"label":"Çatalhöyük","value":"c. 7100–5950 BCE, several thousand residents"}],"siteIds":["tell-es-sultan","dhra","gilgal-i","ain-ghazal","asikli-hoyuk","catalhoyuk","shillourokambos"],"routes":[],"themes":["farming","animals","food","settlement","society","genetics","burial","art"],"sources":[{"cite":"Zeder, M. A. & Hesse, B. (2000). The initial domestication of goats (Capra hircus) in the Zagros Mountains 10,000 years ago. Science 287, 2254–2257."},{"cite":"Kislev, M. E., Hartmann, A. & Bar-Yosef, O. (2006). Early domesticated fig in the Jordan Valley. Science 312, 1372–1374."},{"cite":"Tanno, K. & Willcox, G. (2006). How fast was wild wheat domesticated? Science 311, 1886."},{"cite":"Kuijt, I. & Finlayson, B. (2009). Evidence for food storage and predomestication granaries 11,000 years ago in the Jordan Valley. PNAS 106, 10966–10970."},{"cite":"Bocquet-Appel, J.-P. (2011). When the world’s population took off: the springboard of the Neolithic Demographic Transition. Science 333, 560–561."},{"cite":"Bollongino, R. et al. (2012). Modern taurine cattle descended from small number of Near-Eastern founders. Molecular Biology and Evolution 29, 2101–2104."},{"cite":"Lazaridis, I. et al. (2016). Genomic insights into the origin of farming in the ancient Near East. Nature 536, 419–424."},{"cite":"Hodder, I. (2006). The Leopard’s Tale: Revealing the Mysteries of Çatalhöyük. London: Thames & Hudson."},{"cite":"Yüncü, E. et al. (2025). Female lineages and changing kinship patterns in Neolithic Çatalhöyük. Science."},{"cite":"Scott, J. C. (2017). Against the Grain: A Deep History of the Earliest States. New Haven: Yale University Press."},{"cite":"Graeber, D. & Wengrow, D. (2021). The Dawn of Everything: A New History of Humanity. London: Allen Lane."}]},{"id":"neolithic-europe","order":34,"act":"transition","title":"Farmers Come to Europe","subtitle":"From Knossos to longhouses, pile dwellings and the Trypillia megasites","era":"Neolithic","start":9000,"end":5000,"dateLabel":"c. 7000–3000 BCE","regions":["europe"],"focus":[-10,34,36,57],"emblem":"house","summary":"From about 7000 BCE, farmers descended from Anatolian populations carried crops and herds into Europe along two great routes, up the Danube and around the Mediterranean shore. They built longhouses and timber wells, mixed with local hunter-gatherers, fought, boomed and crashed, and in Ukraine raised settlements of more than a thousand houses.","paragraphs":[["The farmers of Anatolia did not stay at home.","Beneath the Bronze Age palace at Knossos, on Crete, lies something far older: a farming village founded about 9,000 years ago by people who had crossed the sea with wheat, barley, sheep, goats, pigs and cattle in their boats.","None of those animals or crops was native to the island.","They were the vanguard of a movement that would transform Europe."],["Ancient DNA has settled an old argument.","Farming did not spread across Europe mainly as an idea passed from neighbour to neighbour.","It travelled with people, descendants of Anatolian farmers, who moved up the Danube and along the Mediterranean shore, meeting and slowly mixing with the hunter-gatherers already there.","Within two thousand years their longhouses stood from Hungary to the Paris Basin, and the timber linings of their wells, dated to the year by tree rings, are among the oldest surviving examples of carpentry."],["It was not a gentle story.","At Talheim in Germany, 34 men, women and children were killed with stone adzes and arrows and thrown into a pit about 7,000 years ago.","Populations boomed and then crashed.","Yet far to the east, in what is now Ukraine, farmers built settlements of more than a thousand houses laid out in great rings, and some archaeologists think they ran them without kings."]],"sentences":["The farmers of Anatolia did not stay at home.","Beneath the Bronze Age palace at Knossos, on Crete, lies something far older: a farming village founded about 9,000 years ago by people who had crossed the sea with wheat, barley, sheep, goats, pigs and cattle in their boats.","None of those animals or crops was native to the island.","They were the vanguard of a movement that would transform Europe.","Ancient DNA has settled an old argument.","Farming did not spread across Europe mainly as an idea passed from neighbour to neighbour.","It travelled with people, descendants of Anatolian farmers, who moved up the Danube and along the Mediterranean shore, meeting and slowly mixing with the hunter-gatherers already there.","Within two thousand years their longhouses stood from Hungary to the Paris Basin, and the timber linings of their wells, dated to the year by tree rings, are among the oldest surviving examples of carpentry.","It was not a gentle story.","At Talheim in Germany, 34 men, women and children were killed with stone adzes and arrows and thrown into a pit about 7,000 years ago.","Populations boomed and then crashed.","Yet far to the east, in what is now Ukraine, farmers built settlements of more than a thousand houses laid out in great rings, and some archaeologists think they ran them without kings."],"pronounce":{"Knossos":"Knoss-oss","Talheim":"Tahl-hime"},"body":[{"heading":"Across the Aegean","paragraphs":["Farming entered Europe by sea as well as by land. At Knossos on Crete, the lowest layer beneath the later palace is an aceramic village founded around 7000 BCE by settlers who brought cereals, sheep, goats, pigs and cattle, none of them native to the island. On the Greek mainland, Franchthi Cave in the Argolid had been home to hunter-gatherers who already sailed to Melos for obsidian; domestic plants and animals appear there around 6800 BCE. The plain of Thessaly soon filled with farming villages that grew into mounds, among them Sesklo and Argissa.","Genomes from early farmers in Greece and northwest Anatolia, published by Zuzana Hofmanová and colleagues in 2016, show that they descended from Anatolian farmers and were close relatives of the ancestors of most of Europe’s first farmers."]},{"heading":"Up the Danube","paragraphs":["From the southern Balkans, the Starčevo–Körös–Criș cultures spread farming villages across Serbia, Hungary and Romania between about 6200 and 5500 BCE. Around 5500 BCE, in western Hungary, a new way of life took shape: the Linearbandkeramik, or Linear Pottery culture (LBK), named after the incised bands on its pots. Within a few centuries LBK farmers had settled the fertile loess soils from Hungary to the Paris Basin and from the Rhine to Ukraine.","Their signature was the longhouse, a timber building 5 to 7 m wide and often 20 to 40 m long, with five rows of posts, walls of wattle and daub, and pits alongside from which clay was dug. Hamlets of a few houses grew emmer, einkorn, peas, lentils and flax and kept cattle, sheep, goats and pigs. They also dug wells. Waterlogged oak linings from wells at Erkelenz-Kückhoven in the Rhineland and Altscherbitz near Leipzig have been dated by tree rings to around 5100 BCE, and some wells in Saxony are older still. A 2012 study of four Saxon wells, including Altscherbitz, led by Willy Tegel showed that their corner joints were cut with stone adzes as skilfully as later log cabins, making them among the oldest surviving examples of timber joinery in the world."]},{"heading":"Along the Mediterranean shore","paragraphs":["A second stream moved west by boat. Farmers making Impressa pottery settled the Adriatic coasts and southern Italy from around 6100 BCE. The Cardial culture, named for pottery decorated with the edge of cockle shells, then leapfrogged along the coasts of Liguria, southern France and Iberia, reaching Portugal by around 5400 BCE. João Zilhão argued in 2001 that such speed implies small groups of seafaring pioneers founding enclaves among hunter-gatherers. At La Draga, beside Lake Banyoles in Catalonia, a lakeside village of about 5300 BCE preserved in waterlogged mud has yielded wooden posts, digging sticks, sickle handles, baskets and yew bows, the kind of equipment that almost never survives."]},{"heading":"People, not just ideas","paragraphs":["In the 1970s Albert Ammerman and Luca Cavalli-Sforza modelled the spread of farming as a “wave of advance” of about a kilometre a year, driven by population growth and short moves by farmers’ children. Many archaeologists preferred cultural diffusion, in which local hunter-gatherers adopted crops and herds. Ancient DNA has largely settled the question for central, southern and western Europe: the first farmers there were overwhelmingly of Anatolian descent. A 2017 study led by Mark Lipson then showed that hunter-gatherer ancestry rose within farming populations over the following two thousand years, by different amounts in different regions, as the two groups mixed.","Britain and Ireland, reached around 4000 BCE, show the same pattern: a 2019 study led by Selina Brace found that British Neolithic farmers descended mainly from continental farmers related to those of Iberia and France, with little local hunter-gatherer ancestry. In southern Scandinavia the Funnel Beaker culture brought farming around 4000 BCE with a similar turnover. In the eastern Baltic and the forests further east, by contrast, hunter-gatherers adopted pottery and some farming practices while keeping their own ancestry."]},{"heading":"Milk and cheese","paragraphs":["Chemistry shows what the new farmers did with their herds. Richard Evershed’s team found milk fats in pottery from northwestern Anatolia dating to the seventh millennium BCE. In 2013 Mélanie Salque and colleagues identified milk residues in perforated sherds, probably cheese strainers, from sites in the Kuyavia region of Poland dated between about 5200 and 4800 BCE, the earliest direct evidence of cheese making. Turning milk into cheese or yoghurt lowers its lactose, which mattered because almost no adults could then digest fresh milk: the genetic variant for lactase persistence became common only thousands of years later."]},{"heading":"Massacres and crashes","paragraphs":["The end of the LBK, around 5000 BCE, was violent in places. At Talheim in southwestern Germany, a pit held 34 people, men, women and children, killed by blows from stone adzes and by arrows. At Schöneck-Kilianstädten near Frankfurt, a mass grave of 26 people, published by Christian Meyer and colleagues in 2015, showed that many victims’ lower legs had been deliberately broken, perhaps as torture or mutilation. At Asparn-Schletz in Austria, the ditch around a settlement held dozens of bodies with head wounds, with young women notably under-represented, perhaps because they were taken captive.","Herxheim in the Palatinate is stranger still. Its ditches contained the broken, scattered remains of at least 500 people, with cut marks, smashed long bones and carefully trimmed skull caps, deposited with fine pottery, some of it in styles from regions hundreds of kilometres away. Whether this was cannibalism, sacrifice or an unusual funerary rite is one of the sharpest debates of European prehistory (see Debates).","In 2013 Stephen Shennan and colleagues used thousands of radiocarbon dates as a proxy for population and found that, region after region, numbers rose steeply after farming arrived and then fell back sharply within a few centuries. The LBK crisis fits this boom-and-bust pattern."]},{"heading":"Lakes and megasites","paragraphs":["Around the Alps, from about 5000 BCE, farmers built villages on the shores of lakes and bogs, often on raised floors or piles. Waterlogged sediments preserved wooden houses, textiles, bread, tools and seeds, and tree rings date many buildings to the exact year of felling. In 2011 UNESCO listed 111 of these pile-dwelling sites in six countries: Switzerland, Austria, France, Germany, Italy and Slovenia.","In the forest-steppe of Ukraine, the Cucuteni–Trypillia culture produced something without parallel in Neolithic Europe. Between about 4100 and 3400 BCE, settlements such as Nebelivka, Maidanetske and Talianki covered 200 to more than 300 hectares, with one to two thousand or more houses arranged in concentric oval rings around an open centre, larger assembly buildings at intervals, and houses deliberately burned at the end of their use. Geomagnetic surveys by German, British and Ukrainian teams have mapped them in detail. Population estimates range from a few thousand to tens of thousands, and how such places were organised without palaces or obvious rulers is fiercely argued."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Did farming spread by migration or by imitation?","views":[{"label":"Demic diffusion","text":"Albert Ammerman and Luca Cavalli-Sforza proposed that farming spread with farmers themselves in a slow wave of advance. Ancient DNA studies since 2015, by Wolfgang Haak, Iain Mathieson, Zuzana Hofmanová, Mark Lipson, Selina Brace and others, show that early farmers across most of Europe descended overwhelmingly from Anatolian populations."},{"label":"Cultural diffusion","text":"Marek Zvelebil, Julian Thomas and others argued that local hunter-gatherers adopted farming, pottery and monuments, pointing to continuity in stone tools and settlement in some regions. Hunter-gatherer ancestry did return within farming populations, and in the eastern Baltic and the forest zone foragers adopted pottery and herding largely as ideas."}],"status":"Migration was the main engine in southern, central and western Europe, including Britain and Ireland, often by leaps rather than a smooth wave. Local hunter-gatherers were absorbed gradually, and in the northeast adoption without large migration mattered more."},{"question":"What happened at Herxheim?","views":[{"label":"Cannibalism","text":"Bruno Boulestin, Andrea Zeeb-Lanz and colleagues (2009) argued that cut marks, marrow-cracking of long bones and processing patterns resembling those on butchered animals show that hundreds of people were eaten, perhaps as part of a ritual at the end of the LBK."},{"label":"Ritual destruction or secondary burial","text":"Jörg Orschiedt, Miriam Haidle and others see an elaborate mortuary rite in which bodies were dismembered and destroyed, arguing that the marks do not prove consumption."},{"label":"Sacrifice of outsiders","text":"Isotope studies indicate that many individuals were not local and pottery came from distant regions, leading some to suggest the killing or sacrifice of captives or pilgrims."}],"status":"Herxheim is unique and remains unresolved. Most researchers agree that the remains were deliberately processed at a time of social crisis; whether people were eaten is disputed."},{"question":"Were the Trypillia megasites egalitarian cities?","views":[{"label":"Large, permanent and self-governing","text":"Johannes Müller, Robert Hofmann, René Ohlrau and the Kiel team argue from dating and survey that most houses were occupied at the same time, implying populations in the thousands to tens of thousands, and interpret the assembly buildings as places of collective decision-making without rulers. David Graeber and David Wengrow popularised this as early urbanism without kings."},{"label":"Assembly places","text":"Bisserka Gaydarska, John Chapman and colleagues working at Nebelivka propose that megasites grew as periodic gathering places for dispersed communities, with far fewer permanent residents at any one time."}],"status":"Both camps agree the megasites lacked palaces or clear elites. Their size at any moment, and so whether they count as cities, depends on dating that is still being refined."},{"question":"Why did early farming populations boom and bust?","views":[{"label":"Internal dynamics","text":"Stephen Shennan and colleagues (2013) found that population collapses followed initial booms in most regions without matching climate events, suggesting overshoot, conflict or disease."},{"label":"Climate and environment","text":"Other researchers link some declines to cool, wet or unstable phases and soil exhaustion."},{"label":"Methodological caution","text":"Critics such as Daniel Contreras and John Meadows (2014) showed that summed radiocarbon dates can create spurious peaks and troughs, so the method needs careful testing."}],"status":"Improved statistical methods still show booms and busts in many regions, but their causes probably differed from place to place."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"First farmers in Europe","value":"Knossos and Thessaly, c. 7000–6500 BCE"},{"label":"LBK","value":"c. 5500–4900 BCE, from Hungary to the Paris Basin"},{"label":"Oldest timber wells","value":"Around 5100 BCE, dated by tree rings"},{"label":"Talheim massacre","value":"34 people killed, c. 5000 BCE"},{"label":"Pile dwellings","value":"111 UNESCO sites in six Alpine countries (2011)"},{"label":"Trypillia megasites","value":"200 to over 300 hectares, c. 4100–3400 BCE"}],"siteIds":["knossos","franchthi-cave","sesklo","erkelenz-kuckhoven","talheim","herxheim","la-draga","unteruhldingen","talianki"],"routes":[{"id":"danubian-farming","label":"Danubian spread of farming","start":8500,"end":6900,"certainty":"established","path":[[29.5,40.3],[26.5,41],[22.4,39.6],[21.9,41.9],[21.2,43.6],[20.5,44.8],[19.5,46.6],[17.6,47.3],[16.4,48.2],[12.1,48.9],[8.3,49.9],[6.3,51],[3,49.3]]},{"id":"mediterranean-farming","label":"Mediterranean spread of farming","start":8700,"end":7400,"certainty":"established","path":[[26.5,38.6],[23.1,37.4],[19.5,39.6],[16,41.4],[13,42.6],[10.3,43.6],[8.4,44.2],[4.5,43.5],[2.8,42.1],[-0.4,39.4],[-5,36.6],[-8.9,38.6]]}],"themes":["farming","migration","genetics","settlement","violence","food","animals","seafaring"],"sources":[{"cite":"Ammerman, A. J. & Cavalli-Sforza, L. L. (1984). The Neolithic Transition and the Genetics of Populations in Europe. Princeton: Princeton University Press."},{"cite":"Boulestin, B. et al. (2009). Mass cannibalism in the Linear Pottery Culture at Herxheim (Palatinate, Germany). Antiquity 83, 968–982."},{"cite":"Tegel, W. et al. (2012). Early Neolithic water wells reveal the world’s oldest wood architecture. PLOS ONE 7, e51374."},{"cite":"Salque, M. et al. (2013). Earliest evidence for cheese making in the sixth millennium BC in northern Europe. Nature 493, 522–525."},{"cite":"Shennan, S. et al. (2013). Regional population collapse followed initial agriculture booms in mid-Holocene Europe. Nature Communications 4, 2486."},{"cite":"Meyer, C. et al. (2015). The massacre mass grave of Schöneck-Kilianstädten reveals new insights into collective violence in Early Neolithic Central Europe. PNAS 112, 11217–11222."},{"cite":"Hofmanová, Z. et al. (2016). Early farmers from across Europe directly descended from Neolithic Aegeans. PNAS 113, 6886–6891."},{"cite":"Müller, J., Rassmann, K. & Videiko, M. (eds) (2016). Trypillia Mega-Sites and European Prehistory, 4100–3400 BCE. London: Routledge."},{"cite":"Lipson, M. et al. (2017). Parallel palaeogenomic transects reveal complex genetic history of early European farmers. Nature 551, 368–372."},{"cite":"Gaydarska, B. (ed.) (2020). Early Urbanism in Europe: The Trypillia Megasites of the Ukrainian Forest-Steppe. Warsaw and Berlin: De Gruyter Open."}]},{"id":"megaliths","order":35,"act":"transition","title":"The Age of Great Stones","subtitle":"Megaliths from Brittany to Malta, Newgrange, Orkney and Stonehenge","era":"Neolithic","start":6800,"end":4200,"dateLabel":"c. 4800–2200 BCE","regions":["europe"],"focus":[-10,35,16,60],"emblem":"megalith","summary":"From around 4700 BCE, farming communities along Europe’s Atlantic and Mediterranean coasts began to raise great stones as tombs, rows, circles and temples. Brittany, Iberia, Malta, Ireland, Orkney and Wessex each made the tradition their own, and new science is tracing where the stones, the animals and the people came from.","paragraphs":[["On the mornings around the winter solstice, a thin blade of light slips through a stone slot above the entrance of Newgrange in Ireland, creeps 19 metres up the passage and lights the chamber floor for about 17 minutes.","The builders arranged this some 5,200 years ago, centuries before the pyramids of Giza."],["Newgrange belongs to a tradition that the farmers of Atlantic Europe began in Brittany about fifteen hundred years earlier, and that travelled by sea along the Atlantic and Mediterranean coasts: chambered tombs, lines of standing stones, stone circles and, on Malta, temples of limestone.","With only stone tools, timber, rope and muscle, people moved blocks weighing tens of tonnes, and at Locmariaquer in Brittany they raised a single standing stone some 20 metres long."],["At Stonehenge the question is not only how, but from where.","The giant sarsens came from about 25 kilometres to the north.","The smaller bluestones came from the hills of west Wales, more than 200 kilometres away.","And the six-tonne Altar Stone, scientists revealed in 2024, came from the far northeast of Scotland, more than 700 kilometres away.","Nearby feasts drew pigs from all over Britain.","Whatever else it was, Stonehenge was a monument that a whole island helped to build."]],"sentences":["On the mornings around the winter solstice, a thin blade of light slips through a stone slot above the entrance of Newgrange in Ireland, creeps 19 metres up the passage and lights the chamber floor for about 17 minutes.","The builders arranged this some 5,200 years ago, centuries before the pyramids of Giza.","Newgrange belongs to a tradition that the farmers of Atlantic Europe began in Brittany about fifteen hundred years earlier, and that travelled by sea along the Atlantic and Mediterranean coasts: chambered tombs, lines of standing stones, stone circles and, on Malta, temples of limestone.","With only stone tools, timber, rope and muscle, people moved blocks weighing tens of tonnes, and at Locmariaquer in Brittany they raised a single standing stone some 20 metres long.","At Stonehenge the question is not only how, but from where.","The giant sarsens came from about 25 kilometres to the north.","The smaller bluestones came from the hills of west Wales, more than 200 kilometres away.","And the six-tonne Altar Stone, scientists revealed in 2024, came from the far northeast of Scotland, more than 700 kilometres away.","Nearby feasts drew pigs from all over Britain.","Whatever else it was, Stonehenge was a monument that a whole island helped to build."],"pronounce":{"Locmariaquer":"Lok-mar-ya-kair","sarsens":"sar-sens"},"body":[{"heading":"A tradition born in Brittany","paragraphs":["Megalith simply means “great stone”. Between about 4700 and 2000 BCE, communities from Portugal to Sweden and from Ireland to Malta built tens of thousands of monuments of large stones: chambered tombs (dolmens and passage graves), single standing stones (menhirs), rows and circles. In 2019 Bettina Schulz Paulsson modelled 2,410 radiocarbon dates from across Europe and concluded that the earliest megaliths appeared in northwest France around 4700 BCE and spread by sea in several waves along the Atlantic and Mediterranean coasts over the following centuries.","Brittany has some of the oldest and largest. The cairn of Barnenez on the north coast of Finistère, about 75 m long, covers eleven passage graves; its first phase dates to around 4800 to 4500 BCE. Near Carnac, some 3,000 standing stones stand in parallel rows at Le Ménec, Kermario and Kerlescan, stretching for kilometres; they are hard to date but belong mostly to the fifth and fourth millennia BCE. At Locmariaquer, the Grand Menhir, now lying in four pieces, was about 20 m long and weighed an estimated 280 tonnes or more. It was raised around 4500 BCE from stone brought several kilometres, and later fell or was toppled.","Nearby, the passage grave of Gavrinis, on an island in the Gulf of Morbihan, is lined with slabs covered in swirling engraved lines. Its capstone is part of an older decorated menhir; another part of the same stone roofs the Table des Marchand tomb at Locmariaquer, 4 km away, and the carved images join across the break. By around 4000 BCE great standing stones were being broken up and recycled into tombs."]},{"heading":"Iberia and the Mediterranean","paragraphs":["In southern Spain, the Dolmen de Menga at Antequera, built around 3800 to 3600 BCE, is a stone gallery about 27 m long roofed by capstones, the heaviest estimated at about 150 tonnes. A 2024 study in Science Advances by José Antonio Lozano Rodríguez, Leonardo García Sanjuán and colleagues examined how its stones were quarried, moved and set, and concluded that the builders applied practical knowledge of geology, friction, angles and loads amounting to an early form of engineering. Unusually, Menga’s axis points not to a sunrise but to a distinctive mountain, the Peña de los Enamorados.","In Portugal, the Almendres cromlech near Évora, with about 95 standing stones in two enclosures, may have begun as early as the sixth millennium BCE, though its dating is uncertain. On Malta and Gozo, communities built limestone “temples” from about 3600 BCE, among the oldest free-standing stone buildings anywhere. Ġgantija on Gozo has two lobed temples within one outer wall of huge blocks. Beneath Paola on Malta, the Ħal Saflieni Hypogeum was cut into the rock on three levels between about 4000 and 2500 BCE; it held the remains of perhaps 7,000 people and still bears spirals painted in red ochre. The Maltese temple culture came to an end around 2400 BCE."]},{"heading":"Brú na Bóinne","paragraphs":["In a bend of the River Boyne in Ireland, three great passage tombs, Newgrange, Knowth and Dowth, were built around 3200 BCE. Newgrange is a mound about 85 m across, ringed by 97 kerbstones, with a 19 m passage leading to a cross-shaped chamber under a corbelled roof that has kept it dry for five thousand years. Above the entrance is a “roof box”: at sunrise around the winter solstice, light enters it, travels up the rising passage and lights the chamber floor for about 17 minutes. Michael O’Kelly, who excavated and restored the site from 1962 to 1975, witnessed the effect in 1967. Knowth holds more than 200 decorated stones, the largest collection of megalithic art in western Europe.","In 2020 Lara Cassidy, Daniel Bradley and colleagues published dozens of genomes from Irish Neolithic tombs. A man buried in the chamber at Newgrange was the child of first-degree relatives, a brother and sister or a parent and child. Because such unions are almost universally taboo except among some ruling elites, such as the royal families of ancient Egypt and the Inca, the team proposed that Newgrange was built for a dynastic elite. They also found distant relatives of the man in passage tombs at Carrowmore and Carrowkeel in County Sligo, more than 150 km away."]},{"heading":"Orkney","paragraphs":["The Orkney islands off northern Scotland hold one of the densest Neolithic landscapes in Europe. Skara Brae, uncovered by a storm in 1850, is a village of stone houses lived in from about 3100 to 2500 BCE, with stone dressers, box beds, hearths and drains, linked by roofed passages. On a narrow strip of land between two lochs, the Stones of Stenness (around 3100 BCE) are among the earliest stone circles in Britain, and the Ring of Brodgar, about 104 m across, once had around 60 stones. Maeshowe, a chambered tomb of about 2800 BCE, is aligned so that the setting sun around the winter solstice shines down its passage.","Between the two circles lies the Ness of Brodgar, found in 2002 and excavated from 2004 to 2024 by a team led by Nick Card. It is a complex of large stone buildings with slate roofs and painted walls, used from about 3300 to 2400 BCE and enclosed by massive walls. Its use seems to have ended with a great feast that left the bones of hundreds of cattle around one building. After the final season the site was covered over to protect it. Grooved Ware, a flat-based pottery decorated with grooves, seems to have originated in Orkney around 3200 BCE and spread south as far as Wessex, a sign of long-distance ties across Britain."]},{"heading":"Stonehenge and its stones","paragraphs":["Stonehenge on Salisbury Plain was built and rebuilt over some 1,500 years. Around 3000 BCE it began as a circular ditch and bank about 110 m across, with a ring of pits, the Aubrey Holes, that probably held bluestones and received cremated human remains. Around 2500 BCE the great sarsen circle and five trilithons were raised, stones weighing up to about 25 to 30 tonnes; the bluestones were rearranged several times in later centuries. In 2020 David Nash and colleagues analysed a core drilled from one sarsen in 1958 and returned in 2019, and compared it with sarsen boulders across southern England: 50 of the 52 surviving sarsens match West Woods, about 25 km to the north.","The bluestones came from the Preseli Hills of west Wales, more than 200 km away. Mike Parker Pearson, the geologists Richard Bevins and Rob Ixer and colleagues identified outcrops at Carn Goedog (spotted dolerite) and Craig Rhos-y-felin (rhyolite) with stone wedges and hearths that point to quarrying around 3400 to 3000 BCE. In 2021 Parker Pearson proposed that a stone circle at Waun Mawn, of the same diameter as Stonehenge’s ditch, was dismantled and carried east, a hypothesis others dispute. In 2024 a team led by Anthony Clarke at Curtin University dated tiny zircon and rutile crystals in the six-tonne Altar Stone and traced it to the Old Red Sandstone of northeast Scotland, more than 700 km away. Follow-up work pointed to the Scottish mainland rather than Orkney as the source. How it travelled, by sea or overland, is unknown.","About 3 km to the northeast, the huge henge of Durrington Walls was the scene of great winter feasts around 2500 BCE, probably by the people who raised the sarsens. Isotopes in pig bones studied by Richard Madgwick and colleagues in 2019 show that animals were brought from as far as Scotland, northeast England and west Wales."]},{"heading":"What were they for?","paragraphs":["Megaliths were not one thing. Many were tombs holding the bones of many people over generations, often disarticulated and mixed, suggesting collective ancestors rather than individual heroes. Many were aligned on the sun: Newgrange and Maeshowe on midwinter, Stonehenge’s main axis on the midsummer sunrise and midwinter sunset.","For Stonehenge, Mike Parker Pearson and the Malagasy archaeologist Ramilisonina proposed in 1998 that stone stood for the permanent dead and timber, at Durrington Walls, for the living. Timothy Darvill and Geoffrey Wainwright argued that the bluestones were prized for healing. In 2022 Darvill proposed that the sarsens encode a solar calendar of 365 days, a claim the archaeoastronomers Giulio Magli and Juan Antonio Belmonte rejected in 2023. Parker Pearson also sees Stonehenge as a project that united the peoples of Britain, a reading the Welsh and Scottish stones make more compelling. The monument’s later history, and the arrival of the Beaker people, belong to chapter 42."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Did megaliths spread from one origin or arise many times?","views":[{"label":"A maritime spread from Brittany","text":"Bettina Schulz Paulsson’s 2019 Bayesian analysis of 2,410 radiocarbon dates placed the earliest megaliths in northwest France around 4700 BCE and traced three waves of spread along the coasts, implying skilled seafaring and long-distance contacts."},{"label":"Several independent beginnings","text":"Other researchers stress local traditions of monumental burial, uneven radiocarbon sampling and possibly early dates from Iberia, such as the first phase of the Almendres cromlech, arguing that similar solutions to building with large stones could have arisen more than once."}],"status":"Maritime connections along the Atlantic facade are broadly accepted, and Brittany’s early date is well supported. Whether every regional tradition derives from it, or some began independently and were later connected, is still argued, and better dating of Iberian monuments could decide it."},{"question":"How did the bluestones get to Stonehenge?","views":[{"label":"Moved by people","text":"Mike Parker Pearson, Richard Bevins, Rob Ixer and colleagues matched bluestones to specific Preseli outcrops, where they found quarry features, wedges and hearths dated to around 3400 to 3000 BCE, implying that people moved the stones some 200 km or more."},{"label":"Moved by ice","text":"The geomorphologist Brian John and a few others argue that glaciers carried the bluestones east during an earlier ice age, so that the builders collected them nearer Salisbury Plain."},{"label":"Doubts about Waun Mawn","text":"Some archaeologists who accept human transport question Parker Pearson’s 2021 proposal that a complete circle at Waun Mawn was dismantled and moved, arguing that the ring may never have been finished and that its dating is loose."}],"status":"Most archaeologists and geologists accept human transport, supported by the quarry evidence and now by the Scottish origin of the Altar Stone, which ice could not have carried south to Wiltshire. The Waun Mawn hypothesis remains contested."},{"question":"What was Stonehenge for?","views":[{"label":"A place of the ancestors","text":"Parker Pearson and Ramilisonina (1998) saw the stone monument as a domain of the dead, linked by the River Avon to the timber circles of the living at Durrington Walls, and early Stonehenge was indeed a cemetery for cremations."},{"label":"A place of healing","text":"Timothy Darvill and Geoffrey Wainwright suggested that the bluestones were believed to heal, drawing sick and injured people from afar."},{"label":"A calendar","text":"The solstice axis is accepted, but Darvill’s 2022 claim that the sarsens encode a 365-day solar calendar was rejected by Giulio Magli and Juan Antonio Belmonte, who found the numerology and alignments unsupported."},{"label":"A monument of unification","text":"Parker Pearson argues that bringing stones from Wales, and now apparently Scotland, and animals from across Britain made Stonehenge a shared project that bound distant communities together."}],"status":"These ideas are not exclusive. Stonehenge was probably a solar-aligned monument to ancestors that changed meaning over 1,500 years, and the new provenance studies strongly support its role as a focus for people from across Britain."},{"question":"Was Newgrange built for a dynastic elite?","views":[{"label":"Yes, an elite lineage","text":"Lara Cassidy, Daniel Bradley and colleagues (2020) argued that the incestuous parentage of the man buried in Newgrange’s chamber, together with his distant kin in other major passage tombs, points to a powerful lineage that, like Egyptian or Inca royalty, broke normal marriage rules to keep status within the family."},{"label":"Not proven","text":"Critics note that the argument rests on a single individual, that incest can have other explanations including abuse, that burial in a tomb does not prove rule, and that the sample of genomes is small. Some also question whether Neolithic Ireland had hereditary rulers at all."}],"status":"The genetic result itself is robust; its social meaning remains debated. More genomes from Brú na Bóinne and other passage tombs could show whether a distinct elite lineage existed."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Earliest megaliths","value":"Northwest France, c. 4700 BCE (Schulz Paulsson 2019)"},{"label":"Grand Menhir, Locmariaquer","value":"About 20 m long, an estimated 280 tonnes or more"},{"label":"Newgrange","value":"c. 3200 BCE, winter-solstice roof box"},{"label":"Stonehenge sarsens","value":"50 of 52 from West Woods, about 25 km away (2020)"},{"label":"Altar Stone","value":"From northeast Scotland, over 700 km away (2024)"},{"label":"Ħal Saflieni Hypogeum","value":"Remains of perhaps 7,000 people"}],"siteIds":["barnenez","carnac","locmariaquer","gavrinis","dolmen-de-menga","almendres","ggantija","hal-saflieni","newgrange","skara-brae","ness-of-brodgar","stonehenge"],"routes":[],"themes":["monuments","burial","society","genetics","seafaring","food"],"sources":[{"cite":"O’Kelly, M. J. (1982). Newgrange: Archaeology, Art and Legend. London: Thames & Hudson."},{"cite":"Parker Pearson, M. & Ramilisonina (1998). Stonehenge for the ancestors: the stones pass on the message. Antiquity 72, 308–326."},{"cite":"Schulz Paulsson, B. (2019). Radiocarbon dates and Bayesian modeling support maritime diffusion model for megaliths in Europe. PNAS 116, 3460–3465."},{"cite":"Madgwick, R. et al. (2019). Multi-isotope analysis reveals that feasts in the Stonehenge environs and across Wessex drew people and animals from throughout Britain. Science Advances 5, eaau6078."},{"cite":"Cassidy, L. M. et al. (2020). A dynastic elite in monumental Neolithic society. Nature 582, 384–388."},{"cite":"Nash, D. J. et al. (2020). Origins of the sarsen megaliths at Stonehenge. Science Advances 6, eabc0133."},{"cite":"Parker Pearson, M. et al. (2021). The original Stonehenge? A dismantled stone circle in the Preseli Hills of west Wales. Antiquity 95, 85–103."},{"cite":"Darvill, T. (2022). Keeping time at Stonehenge. Antiquity 96, 319–335."},{"cite":"Magli, G. & Belmonte, J. A. (2023). Archaeoastronomy and the alleged ‘Stonehenge calendar’. Antiquity 97."},{"cite":"Clarke, A. J. I. et al. (2024). A Scottish provenance for the Altar Stone of Stonehenge. Nature 632, 570–575."}]},{"id":"green-sahara","order":36,"act":"transition","title":"When the Sahara Was Green","subtitle":"Lakes, herders, rock art and the roots of African farming","era":"African Humid Period; Later Stone Age and Neolithic","start":11500,"end":4000,"dateLabel":"c. 9500–2000 BCE","regions":["africa"],"focus":[-12,0,38,32],"emblem":"cattle","summary":"For thousands of years after the Ice Age, monsoon rains turned the Sahara into grassland dotted with lakes. Fisher-hunters invented pottery there independently, herders spread cattle, sheep and goats and milked them, and artists covered rock shelters with paintings. When the rains retreated, people moved to the Nile and the Sahel, where Egypt’s first farming villages grew and African crops were domesticated.","paragraphs":[["South of the Mediterranean shores where Malta’s temple builders worked lies the Ténéré desert of Niger.","Stand there today and the only water is what you carry.","Ten thousand years ago the same spot was the shore of a lake, where people speared huge Nile perch with bone harpoons.","Thousands of years later, herders buried their dead in the dunes beside it, and in one grave a woman and two children lie with their arms around each other."],["This was the Green Sahara.","Between about 11,000 and 5,000 years ago, a slow wobble in Earth’s orbit pushed the African monsoon far to the north.","Grass, trees, lakes and rivers spread across what is now desert, and with them elephants, giraffes, hippos and people.","Here, hunters and fishers began making pottery more than 11,000 years ago, quite independently of anyone in Asia, and later herders milked their cattle, as traces of fat in their cooking pots reveal."],["Then the rains weakened, gradually in some places and quickly in others.","People followed the water east to the Nile and south into the Sahel.","Beside the Nile, their descendants built the villages from which pharaonic Egypt would grow, and in the Sahel they turned wild sorghum and pearl millet into crops that still feed hundreds of millions.","The desert kept their paintings: herds of cattle, dancers, and in a cave in Egypt, tiny figures that seem to be swimming."]],"sentences":["South of the Mediterranean shores where Malta’s temple builders worked lies the Ténéré desert of Niger.","Stand there today and the only water is what you carry.","Ten thousand years ago the same spot was the shore of a lake, where people speared huge Nile perch with bone harpoons.","Thousands of years later, herders buried their dead in the dunes beside it, and in one grave a woman and two children lie with their arms around each other.","This was the Green Sahara.","Between about 11,000 and 5,000 years ago, a slow wobble in Earth’s orbit pushed the African monsoon far to the north.","Grass, trees, lakes and rivers spread across what is now desert, and with them elephants, giraffes, hippos and people.","Here, hunters and fishers began making pottery more than 11,000 years ago, quite independently of anyone in Asia, and later herders milked their cattle, as traces of fat in their cooking pots reveal.","Then the rains weakened, gradually in some places and quickly in others.","People followed the water east to the Nile and south into the Sahel.","Beside the Nile, their descendants built the villages from which pharaonic Egypt would grow, and in the Sahel they turned wild sorghum and pearl millet into crops that still feed hundreds of millions.","The desert kept their paintings: herds of cattle, dancers, and in a cave in Egypt, tiny figures that seem to be swimming."],"pronounce":{"Ténéré":"Teh-neh-ray","Sahel":"Sah-hel"},"body":[{"heading":"A monsoon moves north","paragraphs":["Between roughly 11,000 and 5,000 years ago, and in places earlier and later, much of the Sahara was savanna. Changes in Earth’s orbit increased summer sunshine in the Northern Hemisphere, strengthening the West African monsoon and pushing its rains hundreds of kilometres north. Climate scientists call this the African Humid Period (chapter 28 describes the climate). Lake Chad swelled into “Mega-Chad”, an inland sea covering hundreds of thousands of square kilometres; rivers ran from the Hoggar and Tibesti mountains; elephants, giraffes, hippos and crocodiles lived where there is now sand.","People followed the rains. In 2006 Rudolph Kuper and Stefan Kröpelin compiled some 500 radiocarbon dates from the eastern Sahara: people moved in around 8500 BCE, settled widely, withdrew toward highlands and the south after about 5300 BCE as the rains weakened, and by about 3500 BCE had largely left for the Nile Valley and the Sahel. The rhythm of rain and drought, they argued, was a motor of African history."]},{"heading":"Harpoons, wavy lines and the first African pottery","paragraphs":["In the 1970s the archaeologist John Sutton described an “Aqualithic” way of life stretching from the Nile to the Niger: communities beside lakes and rivers who fished with barbed bone harpoons, hunted hippo and crocodile, and made pottery decorated with wavy lines combed into the clay. The label is now thought too sweeping, since these groups differed a great deal, but it captured something real: a waterside life that let people stay in one place for long periods without farming.","Pottery was invented in Africa independently of East Asia. At Ounjougou in the Dogon country of Mali, a team led by Eric Huysecom found sherds dated to around 9400 BCE, together with grinding stones and small arrowheads, suggesting pots for cooking wild grasses and seeds. Pottery appears soon afterwards in the Aïr mountains and across the central Sahara.","At Gobero in Niger, found in 2000 by a dinosaur-hunting expedition led by Paul Sereno, some 200 burials lie in dunes beside an ancient lake. The earlier Kiffian people (about 7700 to 6200 BCE) were tall fisher-hunters who caught huge Nile perch. After a dry interval, the more lightly built Tenerian people (about 5200 to 2500 BCE) hunted, fished and herded cattle. One Tenerian grave holds a woman and two children with their arms intertwined, laid on what pollen suggests was a bed of flowers."]},{"heading":"Cattle, sheep and milk","paragraphs":["In Egypt’s Western Desert, at Bir Kiseiba and Nabta Playa, Fred Wendorf, Romuald Schild and Angela Close found cattle bones dated as early as 9000 to 8000 BCE. Because wild cattle could hardly survive in so dry a land without people digging wells, they argued, these must have been domestic, perhaps an independent African domestication. Sceptics note that the bones are few and fragmentary, and genetic studies indicate that African cattle descend mainly from animals domesticated in Southwest Asia, with some genes from local wild aurochs. Sheep and goats, which have no wild ancestors in Africa, certainly came from Southwest Asia, arriving by around 6000 BCE.","By the sixth millennium BCE herders were spreading across the Sahara. In 2012 Julie Dunne, Richard Evershed and colleagues found milk fats in pottery from the Takarkori and Uan Muhuggiag rock shelters in the Tadrart Acacus of Libya, dated to the fifth millennium BCE, the earliest evidence of dairying in Africa. At Takarkori, two women buried about 7,000 years ago were naturally mummified. In 2025 a team led by Nada Salem and Johannes Krause, with Savino di Lernia, published their genomes in Nature. Most of their ancestry came from a previously unknown North African lineage that split from sub-Saharan populations around the time modern humans spread out of Africa and then stayed isolated for tens of thousands of years. Their closest known relatives were the 15,000-year-old people of Taforalt in Morocco, and they carried far less Neanderthal DNA than people living outside Africa. Herding, the authors concluded, spread into the central Sahara mainly as an idea adopted by local people rather than through large migrations."]},{"heading":"Stones and sky at Nabta Playa","paragraphs":["Nabta Playa, about 100 km west of Abu Simbel, is a basin that filled with water after summer rains. From about 9000 BCE people came to it seasonally, and by the sixth and fifth millennia BCE cattle herders had made it a ceremonial centre: cattle burials under stone tumuli, including a young cow in a roofed chamber, lines of large stones, and a small circle of upright slabs about 4 m across. Fred Wendorf and the astronomer J. McKim Malville argued in 1998 that gaps in the circle were aligned north to south and toward the midsummer sunrise. The circle has since been reassembled in the Nubian Museum in Aswan. More ambitious claims that Nabta’s stones map the stars of Orion have found little support (see Debates)."]},{"heading":"Painted herds and swimming figures","paragraphs":["The rock art of the Tassili n’Ajjer in Algeria and the Tadrart Acacus in Libya, both World Heritage Sites, runs to tens of thousands of images. Scholars broadly recognise a sequence: engravings of large wild animals such as elephants, rhinos and an extinct giant buffalo; “Round Head” paintings of floating, masked or horned human figures; a Pastoral phase full of cattle herds, camps and daily life; and later Horse and Camel phases, the last within the past 2,000 years. Few images have been dated directly, so absolute ages are disputed: a “long chronology” associated with Fabrizio Mori puts the oldest phases in the early Holocene, a “short chronology” associated with Alfred Muzzolini several thousand years later.","On Egypt’s remote Gilf Kebir plateau, the Hungarian explorer László Almásy found the Cave of Swimmers at Wadi Sura in 1933, its walls painted with small figures that seem to swim. In 2002 Massimo Foggini and Ahmed Mestekawi found a nearby shelter, the Cave of Beasts, with thousands of painted figures, handprints and strange headless animals. Almásy took the swimmers as proof of ancient lakes; others read them as figures floating in a spirit world. The paintings probably belong to the humid period, but direct dates are lacking."]},{"heading":"Retreat to the Nile","paragraphs":["As the rains retreated, the Nile Valley became a refuge. On the shores of the Faiyum lake, Gertrude Caton-Thompson and Elinor Gardner found in the 1920s basket-lined storage pits holding emmer wheat and barley, among the earliest domestic crops in Egypt, from roughly the fifth millennium BCE, alongside fishing and herding. At Merimde Beni Salama on the western edge of the Delta, villagers lived in small oval houses in the same millennium. In Middle Egypt the Badarian culture (about 4400 to 4000 BCE) buried its dead with fine rippled pottery and stone cosmetic palettes, and the Naqada culture that followed grew into the chiefdoms from which the Egyptian state emerged around 3100 BCE."]},{"heading":"Lake Turkana: violence and monuments","paragraphs":["At Nataruk, west of Lake Turkana in Kenya, Marta Mirazón Lahr and colleagues reported in 2016 the remains of 27 people, about 10,000 years old, at what was then the edge of a lagoon. At least ten showed lethal wounds from clubs and arrows; one skull still held a fragment of an obsidian blade, and a woman in late pregnancy may have had her hands bound. The team interpreted it as a massacre of one hunter-gatherer group by another; critics argue the bodies may have accumulated over time.","Some 5,000 years ago, the first herders to reach the lake built the Lothagam North Pillar Site: a platform about 30 m across, set with stone pillars and circles, over a cavity into which the dead of a whole community were placed, men, women and children, several hundred in all, many with beads and ornaments. A 2018 study led by Elisabeth Hildebrand found no signs of rank among them. Monumental building, it shows, did not require chiefs."]},{"heading":"African crops and the first Bantu steps","paragraphs":["In the Sahel and savanna, people domesticated their own crops. Sorghum was cultivated in the Butana region of eastern Sudan by around 3500 to 3000 BCE, shown by chaff impressions in pottery. Pearl millet was domesticated in the West African Sahel; domestic grains from the Tilemsi valley in Mali date to about 2500 BCE, and the crop reached India by about 2000 BCE. African rice was domesticated in the inland Niger delta, and cowpea and yams in the forest-savanna belt of West Africa, though their exact timing is still being worked out.","Genomes from Shum Laka, a rock shelter in the Grassfields of western Cameroon, published by Mark Lipson and colleagues in 2020, show that four children buried there about 8,000 and 3,000 years ago were related mainly to Central African rainforest hunter-gatherers, not to the Bantu-speaking peoples who live in the region today. The Grassfields lie close to the probable homeland of the Bantu languages, whose speakers, from around 5,000 to 4,000 years ago, began a slow expansion that eventually carried farming, and later iron, across much of central, eastern and southern Africa."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Were cattle domesticated independently in Africa?","views":[{"label":"Yes, early and local","text":"Fred Wendorf, Romuald Schild, Angela Close and the zooarchaeologist Achilles Gautier argued that cattle bones at Bir Kiseiba and Nabta Playa from around 9000 to 8000 BCE must be domestic, because wild aurochs could not have lived in such arid terrain without water supplied by people."},{"label":"No, imported and mixed","text":"Caroline Grigson, Diane Gifford-Gonzalez, Olivier Hanotte and others point out that the early bones are few and hard to identify, and that the DNA of African cattle derives mainly from Southwest Asian domestic stock, with later introgression from wild African aurochs. Secure domestic cattle appear in Egypt and the Sahara from around 6000 BCE, alongside sheep and goats that were certainly introduced."}],"status":"Most researchers now favour introduction from Southwest Asia with local wild admixture, while acknowledging that African herders developed their own distinctive cattle cultures. The early Nabta and Bir Kiseiba bones remain disputed, and ancient DNA from them could settle the question."},{"question":"Is Nabta Playa an astronomical monument?","views":[{"label":"Solar and cardinal alignments","text":"Fred Wendorf and J. McKim Malville (1998 and later) argued that the small stone circle marked the midsummer sunrise and the north–south line, and that lines of megaliths pointed to bright stars, making Nabta one of the oldest astronomical sites known."},{"label":"Elaborate star maps","text":"Thomas Brophy and others went further, claiming that stones map Orion’s belt and encode dates many thousands of years older than the site."},{"label":"Scepticism","text":"Critics among archaeologists and archaeoastronomers note that many stones were displaced by erosion and later disturbance, that the circle has been moved to a museum, and that with enough stones some alignments arise by chance. They find the stellar claims unsupported."}],"status":"The ceremonial role of Nabta Playa for cattle herders is well established. A rough solar and north–south orientation of the circle is plausible, but detailed stellar interpretations are not accepted by most specialists."},{"question":"Was Nataruk a massacre?","views":[{"label":"Intergroup violence","text":"Marta Mirazón Lahr, Robert Foley and colleagues (2016) argued that the injuries, the positions of the bodies and their shared context show one violent event, evidence that warfare between groups occurred among hunter-gatherers long before farming."},{"label":"Not a single event","text":"Christopher Stojanowski and colleagues replied in Nature that the remains could have been deposited at different times in a lagoon setting, and that some of the injuries were not clearly the cause of death, so a single massacre is not demonstrated."}],"status":"The original team defended its dating and interpretation. Most researchers accept that several individuals died violently; whether all died together remains contested, and better dating of individual skeletons would help."},{"question":"Did the Green Sahara end abruptly or gradually?","views":[{"label":"Abrupt collapse","text":"Peter deMenocal and colleagues (2000) found a sharp jump in wind-blown dust in marine cores off West Africa around 5,500 years ago, suggesting that vegetation feedbacks made the Sahara flip from green to desert within decades to centuries."},{"label":"Gradual and patchy","text":"Stefan Kröpelin and colleagues (2008), studying pollen in the sediments of Lake Yoa in northern Chad, found a slow shift in vegetation over several thousand years, and archaeological dates show regions emptying at different times."}],"status":"Both records are real. The end of the humid period was time-transgressive, earlier in the north and east and later in the south, and fast or slow depending on local conditions."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"African Humid Period","value":"Roughly 11,000–5,000 years ago"},{"label":"Early African pottery","value":"Ounjougou, Mali, c. 9400 BCE"},{"label":"Earliest dairying in Africa","value":"Milk fats in Libyan pots, 5th millennium BCE (2012)"},{"label":"Green Sahara genomes","value":"Two Takarkori women, about 7,000 years old (2025)"},{"label":"Lothagam North","value":"Communal cemetery of early herders, about 5,000 years ago"},{"label":"Pearl millet","value":"Domesticated in the West African Sahel by c. 2500 BCE"}],"siteIds":["ounjougou","gobero","takarkori","nabta-playa","wadi-sura","faiyum","nataruk","lothagam-north","shum-laka"],"routes":[],"themes":["climate","technology","animals","farming","art","genetics","burial","violence","migration"],"sources":[{"cite":"Wendorf, F., Schild, R. et al. (2001). Holocene Settlement of the Egyptian Sahara, Volume 1: The Archaeology of Nabta Playa. New York: Kluwer Academic/Plenum."},{"cite":"Kuper, R. & Kröpelin, S. (2006). Climate-controlled Holocene occupation in the Sahara: motor of Africa’s evolution. Science 313, 803–807."},{"cite":"Sereno, P. C. et al. (2008). Lakeside cemeteries in the Sahara: 5000 years of Holocene population and environmental change. PLOS ONE 3, e2995."},{"cite":"Huysecom, E. et al. (2009). The emergence of pottery in Africa during the tenth millennium cal BC: new evidence from Ounjougou (Mali). Antiquity 83."},{"cite":"Dunne, J. et al. (2012). First dairying in green Saharan Africa in the fifth millennium BC. Nature 486, 390–394."},{"cite":"Mirazón Lahr, M. et al. (2016). Inter-group violence among early Holocene hunter-gatherers of West Turkana, Kenya. Nature 529, 394–398."},{"cite":"Hildebrand, E. A. et al. (2018). A monumental cemetery built by eastern Africa’s first herders near Lake Turkana, Kenya. PNAS 115."},{"cite":"Lipson, M. et al. (2020). Ancient West African foragers in the context of African population history. Nature 577, 665–670."},{"cite":"Salem, N. et al. (2025). Ancient DNA from the Green Sahara reveals ancestral North African lineage. Nature."}]},{"id":"china-neolithic","order":37,"act":"transition","title":"Millet, Rice and Jade","subtitle":"Neolithic China from Cishan to Shimao, and the crossing to Taiwan","era":"Neolithic China","start":10000,"end":3800,"dateLabel":"c. 8000–1800 BCE","regions":["asia"],"focus":[100,18,126,44],"emblem":"rice","summary":"Two separate farming traditions, millet on the northern loess and rice in the Yangtze wetlands, grew over thousands of years into jade-rich, walled and water-engineered societies such as Liangzhu and Shimao, while people from the southern coast settled Taiwan and began the Austronesian expansion.","paragraphs":[["Around 10,000 years ago, at a place called Cishan on the edge of the North China Plain, villagers dug storage pits into the ground and filled them with the tiny seeds of broomcorn millet.","Far to the south, in the wetlands of the lower Yangtze, their contemporaries at Shangshan were mixing rice husks into their pottery clay and, according to a 2024 study, brewing rice into a kind of beer."],["These were two separate inventions of farming, one built on dry loess and millet, the other on marshes and rice, and neither happened quickly.","For thousands of years people went on hunting, fishing and gathering acorns and wild grain while their crops slowly changed.","At Jiahu, about 9,000 years ago, villagers played flutes cut from the wing bones of cranes and scratched signs onto tortoise shells."],["By 5,000 years ago the scale had changed.","At Liangzhu, near modern Hangzhou, a walled city of about 300 hectares sat behind a system of earthen dams, and its leaders were buried with carved jade tubes, discs and axes.","Far to the north, on the Loess Plateau, the stone walls of Shimao rose above the valleys, with carved faces set into its central platform and dozens of skulls, most of them from men, buried by its gate."],["Meanwhile, people from the southern coast crossed a narrow strait to Taiwan.","Their descendants would carry the Austronesian languages across more than half the globe, from Madagascar to Rapa Nui."]],"sentences":["Around 10,000 years ago, at a place called Cishan on the edge of the North China Plain, villagers dug storage pits into the ground and filled them with the tiny seeds of broomcorn millet.","Far to the south, in the wetlands of the lower Yangtze, their contemporaries at Shangshan were mixing rice husks into their pottery clay and, according to a 2024 study, brewing rice into a kind of beer.","These were two separate inventions of farming, one built on dry loess and millet, the other on marshes and rice, and neither happened quickly.","For thousands of years people went on hunting, fishing and gathering acorns and wild grain while their crops slowly changed.","At Jiahu, about 9,000 years ago, villagers played flutes cut from the wing bones of cranes and scratched signs onto tortoise shells.","By 5,000 years ago the scale had changed.","At Liangzhu, near modern Hangzhou, a walled city of about 300 hectares sat behind a system of earthen dams, and its leaders were buried with carved jade tubes, discs and axes.","Far to the north, on the Loess Plateau, the stone walls of Shimao rose above the valleys, with carved faces set into its central platform and dozens of skulls, most of them from men, buried by its gate.","Meanwhile, people from the southern coast crossed a narrow strait to Taiwan.","Their descendants would carry the Austronesian languages across more than half the globe, from Madagascar to Rapa Nui."],"pronounce":{"Cishan":"Tsuh-shahn","Shangshan":"Shahng-shahn","Jiahu":"Jyah-hoo","Liangzhu":"Lyahng-joo","Hangzhou":"Hahng-joe","Shimao":"Shr-mao"},"body":[{"heading":"Two cradles, two crops","paragraphs":["China did not have one agricultural revolution but at least two. In the north, on the wind-blown loess of the Yellow River basin, the crops were broomcorn millet (Panicum miliaceum) and foxtail millet (Setaria italica): drought-tolerant grasses with seeds the size of a pinhead. Starch grains on grinding stones and on an early potsherd from Nanzhuangtou (about 11,500–11,000 years ago) and Donghulin (about 11,000–9,500 years ago) show that people near modern Beijing were already processing millet-like grasses at the end of the Ice Age.","The clearest early evidence comes from Cishan in Hebei, where excavators in the 1970s found dozens of storage pits with decayed grain. In 2009 Lu Houyuan and colleagues re-examined new pits using husk phytoliths (microscopic silica bodies that differ between the two millets) and biomolecular markers. The stored grain, dated to about 10,300–8,700 years ago, was almost entirely broomcorn millet; foxtail millet appeared only after about 8,700 years ago and later became the staple of the Yangshao villages. Pigs and dogs completed the northern farmyard.","In the south the crop was rice. At Shangshan in Zhejiang, occupied from about 11,000 years ago, potters tempered their clay with rice husks and chaff. A 2024 study in PNAS analysed residues inside Shangshan vessels from about 10,000–9,000 years ago and found rice phytoliths, starch damaged by fermentation and the remains of Monascus mould and yeast: evidence, the authors argue, of a rice beer made with a qu-type starter, one of the ways a new crop could become socially important long before it fed people year round."]},{"heading":"Rice in slow motion","paragraphs":["Wild rice sheds its grains as soon as they ripen; domesticated rice holds them until harvest. That single trait, visible in the scar on the spikelet base, lets archaeobotanists watch domestication happen. At Tianluoshan, a waterlogged village near Hemudu dated to about 7,000 years ago, Dorian Fuller and colleagues found in 2009 that the share of non-shattering spikelets rose only from 27 to 39 percent between about 6,900 and 6,600 years ago. The villagers were still eating large amounts of acorns, water chestnuts and wild rice.","Hemudu and Tianluoshan also preserve an astonishing wooden world: houses raised on piles and joined with mortise-and-tenon joints, paddles, bone spades made from water-buffalo shoulder blades, and lacquered wood. At Kuahuqiao, near Hangzhou, excavators in 2002 uncovered a pine dugout canoe about 5.6 metres long, dated to about 8,000 years ago, beside a mix of wild-type and domestic-type rice.","In 2024 a team including Lu Houyuan used a different marker, the fish-scale pattern on rice leaf phytoliths, in long sediment sequences from Shangshan and Hehuashan. They proposed a trajectory stretching over 100,000 years: wild rice growing locally, gathering by about 24,000 years ago, cultivation of still-wild plants by about 13,000 years ago and domestication by about 11,000 years ago."]},{"heading":"Jiahu: flutes, drink and signs","paragraphs":["Jiahu, on the Huai River plain in Henan, was occupied between about 7000 and 5700 BCE. Its cemetery yielded more than thirty flutes made from the wing bones of red-crowned cranes, with five to eight finger holes; announced in Nature in 1999 by Zhang Juzhong and colleagues, some can still be played. In 2004 Patrick McGovern’s team analysed residues in Jiahu jars dated to about 7000–6600 BCE and identified a fermented drink of rice, honey and fruit, probably hawthorn or grape.","Signs incised on tortoise shells, most dated to about 6600–6200 BCE, include shapes that resemble later Chinese characters for “eye” and some numerals. The 2003 report by Li Xueqin and colleagues was careful: this was sign use, not writing, part of a long tradition that eventually led to the oracle-bone script some 5,000 years later. Jiahu also grew rice, showing how early the crop moved north of the Yangtze."]},{"heading":"Painted pots, jade dragons and graded graves","paragraphs":["Between about 5000 and 3000 BCE the Yangshao culture spread across the middle Yellow River. At Banpo, near Xi’an, excavated in the 1950s, a ditch enclosed round and square semi-subterranean houses, with kilns and a cemetery outside; children were buried in pottery urns among the houses. Yangshao potters painted fish, human faces and spirals in red and black. At Xishuipo in Puyang, grave M45, dated to roughly 6,500 years ago, held a tall man flanked by a dragon and a tiger laid out in white clamshells, often called China’s first dragon.","In the north-east the Hongshan culture (about 4500–3000 BCE) built a ritual landscape at Niuheliang in Liaoning between about 3500 and 3000 BCE: stone cairn tombs, round and square altars and a so-called Goddess Temple where a clay female head with inlaid jade eyes was found. Hongshan carvers made coiled jade “pig dragons”. In Shandong, Dawenkou cemeteries (about 4100–2600 BCE) show graves grading from bare pits to burials with ivory, jade and dozens of fine pots, plus signs incised on large jars."]},{"heading":"Liangzhu: a city built on water","paragraphs":["The Liangzhu culture of the lower Yangtze (about 3300–2300 BCE) produced the most ambitious Neolithic landscape in East Asia. Its capital, north-west of Hangzhou, covered about 300 hectares inside walls laid on stone foundations, with a raised palace platform at Mojiaoshan and elite cemeteries at Fanshan and Yaoshan full of jade cong tubes, bi discs and ceremonial axes carved with a recurring god-and-beast face.","Beyond the city lay eleven earthen dams, high dams closing valley mouths and low dams linking hills on the plain. Radiocarbon dates published by Liu Bin and colleagues in 2017 place their construction around 5,100–4,900 years ago; the team estimated that building the water-control system took thousands of workers several years. UNESCO inscribed the site in 2019 as evidence of an early rice-based urban society. Stalagmites from Shennong and Jiulong caves, studied by Zhang Haiwei and colleagues in 2021, record exceptionally heavy monsoon rains around 4,345–4,324 years ago, when flood silts buried much of the Liangzhu heartland."]},{"heading":"Walls, sacrifice and the first metal","paragraphs":["During the Longshan period (about 3000–1900 BCE) walled towns multiplied along the Yellow River. At Taosi in Shanxi, the middle-phase enclosure of about 2.8 square kilometres was among the largest in prehistoric China. The excavators interpreted a semicircular rammed-earth structure with gaps between pillars as a solar observatory marking solstices, though a 2025 paper argues that the surrounding walls would have blocked the sunrise.","Shimao, in northern Shaanxi (about 2300–1800 BCE), was a stone-walled enclosure of more than 400 hectares centred on Huangchengtai, a terraced platform with about 70 stone carvings of faces, animals and geometric designs. Pits by the east gate held around 80 skulls. A 2025 Nature study led by Fu Qiaomei, based on genomes from 169 people, found that Shimao’s population descended mainly from local Yangshao-period farmers, reconstructed patrilineal families over four generations, and showed that the skull-pit victims were mostly men while the attendants killed for elite tombs were almost all women.","Many of these centres declined around 2200–1900 BCE, during the drought known as the 4.2-thousand-year event. Metal arrived from the north-west: a bronze knife from Linjia in Gansu, dated to about 2900–2740 BCE, may itself be an import, and copper working spread through Qijia communities before the bronze vessels of Erlitou."]},{"heading":"Genes, languages and the crossing to Taiwan","paragraphs":["Ancient DNA shows two distinct Neolithic populations. A 2020 Science study led by Melinda Yang and Qiaomei Fu, with genomes from 26 people spanning 9,500 to 300 years ago, found that northern farmers along the Yellow River were closest to most present-day East Asians, while early people from Fujian and the Taiwan Strait islands were closest to Austronesian speakers. Northern ancestry later spread south. Two 2019 language studies, Laurent Sagart’s team in PNAS and Zhang Menghan’s in Nature, placed the origin of the Sino-Tibetan family among northern millet farmers about 7,200 or 5,900 years ago.","On Liang Island, off Fujian, a man buried in a shell midden about 8,300 years ago carries an ancestral form of mitochondrial lineage E, today found almost only among Austronesian speakers. Around 6,000 years ago the Dapenkeng culture, with cord-marked pottery, appeared in Taiwan, and by about 5,000 years ago rice and millet were grown at Nanguanli. Linguists led by Robert Blust note that nine of the ten primary branches of Austronesian are spoken only on Taiwan. By about 2000 BCE red-slipped pottery like Taiwan’s appeared at Nagsabaran in northern Luzon, and nephrite from Fengtian in eastern Taiwan later travelled across the South China Sea. The Pacific voyages that followed belong to a later chapter."]}],"debates":[{"question":"When was rice domesticated?","views":[{"label":"Early domestication","text":"Lu Houyuan, Zuo Xinxin, Jiang Leping and colleagues argue from phytoliths at Shangshan and Hehuashan, including the number of fish-scale decorations on leaf phytoliths and direct radiocarbon dates on phytolith carbon, that domesticated rice was present by about 11,000–9,000 years ago, making the lower Yangtze one of the earliest centres of plant domestication anywhere (Science, 2024)."},{"label":"Protracted domestication","text":"Dorian Fuller, Qin Ling and colleagues rely on spikelet bases, which record the loss of shattering directly. At Tianluoshan only 27–39 percent of spikelets were non-shattering around 6,900–6,600 years ago, so they see millennia of cultivation of largely wild rice and full domestication only around 6,000 years ago. They question whether phytolith patterns track domestication rather than growing conditions."}],"status":"Both sides now agree that rice was being cultivated in the lower Yangtze by about 10,000 years ago and that change was slow; the dispute is over when fully domestic rice dominated. Well-dated spikelet bases from Shangshan-period sites would settle much of it."},{"question":"Was Liangzhu a state?","views":[{"label":"An early state","text":"Colin Renfrew and Liu Bin (Antiquity, 2018), and most Chinese archaeologists, argue that a planned capital, dams needing thousands of workers, a standardised jade iconography across the region and a steep burial hierarchy show a state-level society around 3000 BCE, without writing or metal."},{"label":"A complex chiefdom or theocratic polity","text":"More cautious scholars, applying definitions that stress bureaucracy, record keeping and firm control over a large territory, note that the evidence for administration beyond the Liangzhu basin is thin and that shared jade symbols could reflect religion rather than government."}],"status":"The dispute is partly about definitions. UNESCO’s 2019 inscription and ongoing survey of the wider water system have strengthened the case that Liangzhu was an exceptionally centralised society; whether to call it a state still depends on the criteria used."},{"question":"Where did the Austronesians begin?","views":[{"label":"Out of Taiwan","text":"Peter Bellwood, Robert Blust and colleagues argue that rice and millet farmers from coastal south China settled Taiwan about 6,000 years ago and that their descendants moved to the Philippines about 4,000 years ago and onward. Evidence: Taiwan’s deep linguistic diversity, red-slipped pottery and Taiwan jade in Luzon, and ancient genomes from Liangdao and Fujian closely related to Austronesian speakers."},{"label":"Island Southeast Asian origins","text":"Wilhelm Solheim’s Nusantao model, and later genetic arguments by Stephen Oppenheimer and others, placed the key developments in island Southeast Asia, with maritime trade networks spreading languages and goods in several directions rather than a single migration."}],"status":"In 2026 the Out of Taiwan model is the majority view for the language family, reinforced by ancient DNA. Debate continues over how much of the spread was migration versus mixing with local peoples, and over the role of farming as opposed to seafaring and trade."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Earliest millet store","value":"Broomcorn millet in pits at Cishan, about 10,300–8,700 years ago"},{"label":"Rice cultivation","value":"Lower Yangtze, by about 10,000 years ago; domestication timing debated"},{"label":"Oldest playable flutes in China","value":"Crane-bone flutes from Jiahu, about 7000–5700 BCE"},{"label":"Liangzhu","value":"Walled city of about 300 hectares with eleven dams, about 3300–2300 BCE; UNESCO 2019"},{"label":"Shimao","value":"Stone-walled centre of more than 400 hectares, about 2300–1800 BCE"},{"label":"Taiwan settled","value":"About 6,000 years ago; departures to Luzon by about 2000 BCE"}],"siteIds":["cishan","shangshan","jiahu","kuahuqiao","tianluoshan","banpo","xishuipo","niuheliang","liangzhu","taosi","shimao","liangdao"],"routes":[{"id":"out-of-taiwan","label":"Out of Taiwan","start":6000,"end":3800,"certainty":"established","path":[[119.6,26.1],[121.4,25.15],[121.1,22.75],[121.95,20.45],[121.65,18.2]]}],"themes":["farming","food","settlement","monuments","society","genetics","language","seafaring","music","migration"],"sources":[{"cite":"Lu, H. et al. (2009). Earliest domestication of common millet (Panicum miliaceum) in East Asia extended to 10,000 years ago. PNAS 106, 7367–7372.","doi":"10.1073/pnas.0900158106"},{"cite":"Fuller, D. Q. et al. (2009). The domestication process and domestication rate in rice: spikelet bases from the Lower Yangtze. Science 323, 1607–1610.","doi":"10.1126/science.1166605"},{"cite":"Zhang, J. et al. (2024). Rice’s trajectory from wild to domesticated in East Asia. Science 384, 901–906.","doi":"10.1126/science.ade4487"},{"cite":"McGovern, P. E. et al. (2004). Fermented beverages of pre- and proto-historic China. PNAS 101, 17593–17598.","doi":"10.1073/pnas.0407921102"},{"cite":"Liu, B. et al. (2017). Earliest hydraulic enterprise in China, 5,100 years ago. PNAS 114, 13637–13642.","doi":"10.1073/pnas.1710516114"},{"cite":"Zhang, H. et al. (2021). Collapse of the Liangzhu and other Neolithic cultures in the lower Yangtze region in response to climate change. Science Advances 7, eabi9275.","doi":"10.1126/sciadv.abi9275"},{"cite":"Renfrew, C. and Liu, B. (2018). The emergence of complex society in China: the case of Liangzhu. Antiquity 92, 975–990."},{"cite":"Yang, M. A. et al. (2020). Ancient DNA indicates human population shifts and admixture in northern and southern China. Science 369, 282–288.","doi":"10.1126/science.aba0909"},{"cite":"Fu, Q. and colleagues (2025). Ancient DNA from Shimao city records kinship practices in Neolithic China. Nature.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-025-09799-x"},{"cite":"Hung, H.-C. et al. (2007). Ancient jades map 3,000 years of prehistoric exchange in Southeast Asia. PNAS 104, 19745–19750.","doi":"10.1073/pnas.0707304104"}]},{"id":"south-asia","order":38,"act":"transition","title":"From Microliths to Mehrgarh","subtitle":"South Asia’s hunters, herders and first farmers on the eve of the Indus civilisation","era":"Late Palaeolithic, Mesolithic, Neolithic and Early Harappan","start":48000,"end":5300,"dateLabel":"48,000 years ago to 3300 BCE","regions":["asia"],"focus":[60,4,92,37],"emblem":"cattle","summary":"From bow hunters in the Sri Lankan rainforest 48,000 years ago to the barley fields, zebu herds and drilled teeth of Mehrgarh, South Asia followed its own path to farming, carried mostly by local people rather than incoming migrants.","paragraphs":[["About 9,000 years ago, at the foot of the Bolan Pass in what is now Pakistan, a villager at Mehrgarh sat still while someone bored into a living molar with a tiny flint drill.","Archaeologists have found eleven drilled teeth in the cemetery there, and the worn edges of the holes show that their owners went on chewing for years afterwards."],["South Asia’s story is far older than its first farms.","In a cave in the Sri Lankan rainforest called Fa Hien Lena, people were making tiny stone blades and bone arrowheads about 48,000 years ago, and hunting monkeys and squirrels high in the canopy.","Across India, painted shelters like Bhimbetka and cemeteries beside the lakes of the Ganga plain show hunter-gatherers who thrived long after the Ice Age ended."],["At Mehrgarh, by around 7000 BCE, people were growing barley and wheat, crops with roots in Southwest Asia, but their cattle were local: humped zebu, tamed from the wild oxen of India.","Ancient DNA suggests that the farmers themselves were largely local too, descended from people who had lived in the region for thousands of years rather than from a wave of newcomers."],["By 3300 BCE villages and small towns stretched from the hills of Balochistan to the plains of the Indus and the seasonal Ghaggar Hakra river.","Their potters were scratching signs onto jars.","Within a few centuries those towns would grow into the planned cities of the Indus civilisation."]],"sentences":["About 9,000 years ago, at the foot of the Bolan Pass in what is now Pakistan, a villager at Mehrgarh sat still while someone bored into a living molar with a tiny flint drill.","Archaeologists have found eleven drilled teeth in the cemetery there, and the worn edges of the holes show that their owners went on chewing for years afterwards.","South Asia’s story is far older than its first farms.","In a cave in the Sri Lankan rainforest called Fa Hien Lena, people were making tiny stone blades and bone arrowheads about 48,000 years ago, and hunting monkeys and squirrels high in the canopy.","Across India, painted shelters like Bhimbetka and cemeteries beside the lakes of the Ganga plain show hunter-gatherers who thrived long after the Ice Age ended.","At Mehrgarh, by around 7000 BCE, people were growing barley and wheat, crops with roots in Southwest Asia, but their cattle were local: humped zebu, tamed from the wild oxen of India.","Ancient DNA suggests that the farmers themselves were largely local too, descended from people who had lived in the region for thousands of years rather than from a wave of newcomers.","By 3300 BCE villages and small towns stretched from the hills of Balochistan to the plains of the Indus and the seasonal Ghaggar Hakra river.","Their potters were scratching signs onto jars.","Within a few centuries those towns would grow into the planned cities of the Indus civilisation."],"pronounce":{"Mehrgarh":"Mair-garh","Bolan":"Bo-lahn","Fa Hien Lena":"Fah Hyen Leh-nah","Bhimbetka":"Bheem-bet-kah","Ghaggar Hakra":"Gug-gur Huk-rah"},"body":[{"heading":"Rainforest hunters of Sri Lanka","paragraphs":["The deepest part of this story lies in the wet zone of south-western Sri Lanka. Fa-Hien Lena, a large cave named after the Chinese pilgrim Faxian, was first excavated by Siran Deraniyagala in the 1980s and again from 2009 by a Sri Lankan and international team including Oshan Wedage, Patrick Roberts and Michelle Langley. Its radiocarbon-dated layers run from about 48,000 to 4,000 years ago and contain some of the oldest microliths in South Asia: small, standardised stone pieces made to be set into hafted, composite tools.","In 2020 Langley and colleagues described small bone points with impact fractures, dated to about 48,000 years ago, as the earliest clear evidence of bow-and-arrow hunting outside Africa, along with bone tools for working fibre and hide and shell beads and ochre. The animal bones tell a matching story: a 2019 study led by Wedage showed that people here specialised in hunting monkeys and squirrels in the forest canopy. Rainforests, once thought to be barriers to early Homo sapiens, were home."]},{"heading":"Painted shelters and a fossil that was not","paragraphs":["By the start of the Holocene, microlith-using hunter-gatherers lived across most of the subcontinent. Their best-known gallery is Bhimbetka, in the sandstone hills south of Bhopal, brought to archaeological attention by V. S. Wakankar in 1957 and inscribed by UNESCO in 2003. Hundreds of shelters carry paintings in red and white: deer, bison, boar and rhinoceros, hunters with bows, dancing lines of people, and much later riders on horses and elephants. The images are dated mainly by style and by which layer of paint lies over which, so the claim that the earliest belong to the Mesolithic is reasonable but not firmly measured.","Bhimbetka also gave science a cautionary tale. In 2021 a team led by geologist Gregory Retallack reported that an oval impression on the roof of the Auditorium Cave was Dickinsonia, one of the strange animals of the Ediacaran seas some 550 million years ago. Follow-up fieldwork concluded that the shape was the remains of a decayed beehive: even natural features on a famous rock surface need checking."]},{"heading":"Lakeside cemeteries of the Ganga plain","paragraphs":["On the middle Ganga plain, in Pratapgarh district of Uttar Pradesh, hunter-gatherers camped beside horseshoe lakes left by wandering rivers. Excavations since the 1970s at Sarai Nahar Rai, Mahadaha and Damdama, led by teams from the University of Allahabad, uncovered dozens of graves: tall, robust men and women laid in shallow pits near hearths, sometimes in double graves, with ornaments of bone and antler. One skeleton at Sarai Nahar Rai was reported to have a microlith lodged among its ribs. Radiocarbon dates spread across roughly 10,000 to 7,000 years ago, although some are disputed. People ate wild cattle, deer, pig, turtle and fish, and probably wild grass seeds.","In Rajasthan, V. N. Misra’s excavations at Bagor revealed microlith makers whose animal bones, from about 5000 BCE, include sheep and goats that many specialists regard as domestic, a sign that herding could be adopted by foragers without a full farming package. Copper arrowheads appear in the later levels."]},{"heading":"Mehrgarh: a village at the foot of the Bolan Pass","paragraphs":["Mehrgarh lies on the Kachi plain in Balochistan, where the Bolan Pass opens from the Iranian plateau onto the Indus lowlands. A French and Pakistani team led by Jean-François Jarrige excavated it from 1974, exposing a sequence from about 7000 BCE to about 2600 BCE. In the earliest, pre-pottery phase people built rectangular houses of mud brick and blocks of small compartments that the excavators interpret as granaries. The main crop was six-row naked barley, with einkorn, emmer and free-threshing wheat, and fruit such as dates and jujube.","The animal bones record a shift from hunting to herding. Early levels are rich in gazelle, wild sheep, nilgai and swamp deer; later ones are dominated by goats (some buried with the dead), sheep and cattle. Richard Meadow showed that the cattle became smaller and more numerous over time, the signature of the local domestication of humped zebu (Bos indicus) from the Indian aurochs, a separate event from the taming of humpless cattle in Southwest Asia, as genetic studies confirm.","Graves held beads of turquoise, lapis lazuli from the mountains of Afghanistan and shells from the Arabian Sea coast. In 2006 Andrea Coppa and colleagues reported eleven drilled molar crowns from nine adults, dated to about 9,000–7,500 years ago. In 2002 Christophe Moulherat and colleagues identified mineralised cotton fibres inside a copper bead from a burial of the sixth millennium BCE, among the oldest traces of cotton anywhere."]},{"heading":"Many roads to farming","paragraphs":["Mehrgarh was not the only experiment. At Lahuradewa on the middle Ganga plain, Rakesh Tewari and colleagues reported rice from levels they date to the seventh millennium BCE or earlier. Dorian Fuller and others accept early use of rice but see gathering or cultivation of wild rice, with clearly domesticated rice appearing thousands of years later. At Bhirrana in Haryana, excavations by the Archaeological Survey of India produced radiocarbon dates as early as about 7500 BCE for the lowest levels, which would make it older than Mehrgarh; critics argue that the dated charcoal may be older than the deposits it came from.","In the Kashmir valley, Burzahom was settled from about 3000 BCE by people who lived in deep pit dwellings, made bone tools and used perforated rectangular stone harvesting knives like those of northern China; some were buried with dogs. In the southern Deccan, from about 3000 BCE, cattle herders built great ashmounds by repeatedly burning heaps of dung in their pens, at sites such as Utnur, Budihal, Kupgal and Sanganakallu. They grew mung bean, horsegram and two small millets that are native to India: a separate centre of domestication."]},{"heading":"On the eve of the Indus cities","paragraphs":["In the fourth millennium BCE villages spread from the Balochistan uplands onto the Indus plain and along the Ghaggar Hakra, a river system that was more active then than today. Rafique Mughal’s surveys in Cholistan in the 1970s found dozens of sites with Hakra-ware pottery. At Harappa, the Harappa Archaeological Research Project led by Richard Meadow and Mark Kenoyer uncovered a Ravi-phase village (about 3700–2800 BCE) of craft workers making beads of stone and shell; some potsherds from about 3300 BCE carry incised marks resembling later Indus signs.","The following Kot Diji phase (about 3300–2600 BCE) saw walled settlements and increasingly standard pottery over a wide area. At Kalibangan in Rajasthan an Early Harappan field preserved criss-cross plough furrows. Around 2600 BCE these communities were transformed into the Mature Harappan civilisation, with its brick cities, weights, seals and script."]},{"heading":"What ancient DNA says","paragraphs":["Most people in South Asia today carry varying mixtures of three ancestral streams, with East Asian-related ancestry added in some regions. The oldest, called Ancient Ancestral South Indian (AASI), is distantly related to the Andaman Islanders and goes back to the first modern humans in the region. The second is related to ancient people of the Iranian plateau. The third, from the Eurasian steppe, arrived later.","In 2019 Vasant Shinde, David Reich and colleagues published the first genome from an Indus city, a woman buried at Rakhigarhi between about 2800 and 2300 BCE. She had a mix of AASI and Iranian-related ancestry and no steppe ancestry, and her Iranian-related component had separated from that of the Iranian plateau’s farmers before farming began there. A companion study led by Vagheesh Narasimhan, with genomes from more than 500 ancient people, found the same profile in migrants from the Indus world buried in Turkmenistan and eastern Iran, and showed steppe ancestry entering South Asia after about 2000 BCE. The implication is that South Asian farming spread mainly through crops, animals and ideas taken up by local people."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Was farming in South Asia imported or home-grown?","views":[{"label":"Imported from Southwest Asia","text":"Many archaeobotanists, including Dorian Fuller, stress that einkorn and emmer wheat, and probably sheep and goats, were first domesticated in the Fertile Crescent and reached Mehrgarh as a ready-made package, much as farming reached Europe."},{"label":"A largely local process","text":"Jean-François Jarrige argued that Mehrgarh was an independent early centre where barley and zebu were domesticated from local wild ancestors. Genetics supports continuity: the Rakhigarhi genome and the Narasimhan study found no major influx of Iranian farmers."}],"status":"Most researchers now see a mixture: some founder crops came from the west, while zebu, and perhaps barley, were tamed locally by people with deep regional roots. Better-dated plant remains from other early sites in Balochistan and eastern Iran are needed."},{"question":"How old is rice farming in the Ganga plains?","views":[{"label":"Very early cultivation","text":"Rakesh Tewari, K. S. Saraswat and colleagues argue that rice at Lahuradewa, dated to the seventh millennium BCE or earlier, shows cultivation on the Ganga plain almost as early as in China."},{"label":"Wild rice first, domestication later","text":"Dorian Fuller, Eleanor Kingwell-Banham and colleagues argue that rice grains or husks alone cannot show domestication. They suggest long management of wild rice, with non-shattering domesticated rice appearing only in the third or second millennium BCE, partly through crossing with japonica rice from China."}],"status":"Early use of rice is accepted; the date of true domestication is not. Systematic study of spikelet bases from early Ganga sites, as in the Yangtze, would decide it."},{"question":"What does the genetic history mean for language?","views":[{"label":"Indo-Aryan speech arrived with steppe ancestry","text":"Narasimhan, Reich and many historical linguists link the arrival of steppe ancestry after 2000 BCE with the spread of Indo-Aryan languages, which are closely related to Iranian languages and to the Indo-European family of Europe."},{"label":"Longer local continuity","text":"Some Indian scholars, pointing to the absence of steppe ancestry at Rakhigarhi and to continuities between Harappan and later Indian culture, argue that Indo-European languages or at least much of Vedic culture developed within South Asia."},{"label":"The Dravidian question","text":"Asko Parpola and others read the Indus script as Dravidian; Fuller links Proto-Dravidian with the Southern Neolithic herders. Both remain hypotheses, because the Indus script is undeciphered."}],"status":"Most geneticists and linguists favour the arrival of Indo-Aryan languages in the second millennium BCE. The language of the Indus cities remains unknown, and genes alone cannot prove which language anyone spoke."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Earliest bow hunting outside Africa","value":"Fa-Hien Lena, Sri Lanka, about 48,000 years ago"},{"label":"First farming village","value":"Mehrgarh, Balochistan, from about 7000 BCE"},{"label":"Locally domesticated animal","value":"Humped zebu cattle, from the Indian aurochs"},{"label":"Early dentistry","value":"Eleven drilled molars from nine adults at Mehrgarh, about 9,000–7,500 years ago"},{"label":"Native crops of the south","value":"Mung bean, horsegram and small millets, from about 3000 BCE"},{"label":"Genetic streams","value":"Ancient Ancestral South Indian, Iranian-related, and steppe ancestry after 2000 BCE"}],"siteIds":["fa-hien-lena","bhimbetka","sarai-nahar-rai","bagor","mehrgarh","burzahom","sanganakallu","harappa","kalibangan","rakhigarhi"],"routes":[],"themes":["technology","art","burial","farming","animals","food","settlement","genetics","language"],"sources":[{"cite":"Langley, M. C. et al. (2020). Bows and arrows and complex symbolic displays 48,000 years ago in the South Asian tropics. Science Advances 6, eaba3831."},{"cite":"Wedage, O. et al. (2019). Specialized rainforest hunting by Homo sapiens ~45,000 years ago. Nature Communications 10, 739."},{"cite":"Jarrige, J.-F. (2008). Mehrgarh Neolithic. Pragdhara 18."},{"cite":"Coppa, A. et al. (2006). Early Neolithic tradition of dentistry. Nature 440, 755–756."},{"cite":"Moulherat, C. et al. (2002). First evidence of cotton at Neolithic Mehrgarh, Pakistan: analysis of mineralized fibres from a copper bead. Journal of Archaeological Science 29, 1393–1401."},{"cite":"Fuller, D. Q. (2006). Agricultural origins and frontiers in South Asia: a working synthesis. Journal of World Prehistory 20, 1–86."},{"cite":"Shinde, V. et al. (2019). An ancient Harappan genome lacks ancestry from steppe pastoralists or Iranian farmers. Cell 179, 729–735."},{"cite":"Narasimhan, V. M. et al. (2019). The formation of human populations in South and Central Asia. Science 365, eaat7487."}]},{"id":"sahul-holocene","order":39,"act":"transition","title":"Gardens, Eels and Fire","subtitle":"New Guinea’s first farmers, Australia’s managed Country and the dingo’s arrival","era":"Holocene Sahul","start":12000,"end":3000,"dateLabel":"c. 10,000–1000 BCE","regions":["oceania"],"focus":[112,-44,180,2],"emblem":"fire","summary":"As rising seas split Sahul into New Guinea, Australia and Tasmania, highland New Guineans invented farming at Kuk Swamp, Aboriginal Australians shaped their Country with fire, stone channels and seed grinding, and the dingo and the Lapita voyagers arrived from the islands to the north.","paragraphs":[["In south-western Victoria, a lava flow from the volcano Budj Bim runs down towards the sea.","For at least 6,600 years the Gunditjmara people have shaped it, cutting channels through the basalt, building stone weirs and ponds, and guiding migrating eels into woven traps.","Gunditjmara Traditional Owners still care for the system, and in 2019 it became a World Heritage Site."],["As the Ice Age ended, the sea rose over the plains that had joined Australia to New Guinea and Tasmania, and people who had shared one continent found themselves on separate lands.","In the New Guinea highlands, at Kuk Swamp, people dug drains and planted bananas, taro and yams on mounds: one of the few places on Earth where farming was invented independently.","In Tasmania, cut off from the mainland for some twelve thousand years, people went their own way, and archaeologists still argue over what their choices mean."],["Across Australia, people managed Country with fire, painted and engraved rock surfaces from the Kimberley to the Pilbara coast, and spread one family of related languages over most of the continent.","By about 3,300 years ago the dingo had arrived, probably carried by boat from the islands to the north-west."],["At about the same time, far to the east, people making pottery stamped with fine toothed patterns sailed beyond New Guinea into the open Pacific.","They were the Lapita voyagers, and they were heading for islands no human had ever seen."]],"sentences":["In south-western Victoria, a lava flow from the volcano Budj Bim runs down towards the sea.","For at least 6,600 years the Gunditjmara people have shaped it, cutting channels through the basalt, building stone weirs and ponds, and guiding migrating eels into woven traps.","Gunditjmara Traditional Owners still care for the system, and in 2019 it became a World Heritage Site.","As the Ice Age ended, the sea rose over the plains that had joined Australia to New Guinea and Tasmania, and people who had shared one continent found themselves on separate lands.","In the New Guinea highlands, at Kuk Swamp, people dug drains and planted bananas, taro and yams on mounds: one of the few places on Earth where farming was invented independently.","In Tasmania, cut off from the mainland for some twelve thousand years, people went their own way, and archaeologists still argue over what their choices mean.","Across Australia, people managed Country with fire, painted and engraved rock surfaces from the Kimberley to the Pilbara coast, and spread one family of related languages over most of the continent.","By about 3,300 years ago the dingo had arrived, probably carried by boat from the islands to the north-west.","At about the same time, far to the east, people making pottery stamped with fine toothed patterns sailed beyond New Guinea into the open Pacific.","They were the Lapita voyagers, and they were heading for islands no human had ever seen."],"pronounce":{"Budj Bim":"Budge Bim","Gunditjmara":"Gun-ditch-mah-rah","Kuk":"Kook","Lapita":"Lah-pee-tah"},"body":[{"heading":"Seas rise, lands divide","paragraphs":["At the height of the last Ice Age, sea level stood some 120 metres lower than today, and New Guinea, Australia and Tasmania formed one landmass, Sahul. As the ice sheets melted, the sea advanced across the Bassian plain, and Tasmania was cut off from the mainland roughly 12,000 years ago. The Torres Strait land bridge went under later, by roughly 8,000 years ago, separating New Guinea from Australia. Coastal people lost great stretches of Country; sea level stabilised near its present height around 7,000 years ago.","Patrick Nunn and Nicholas Reid have argued that some Aboriginal narratives about drowning coastlines may preserve memories of this flooding over hundreds of generations. The idea is taken seriously but remains debated, because stories change as they are retold and are hard to date."]},{"heading":"Kuk: gardens in the highlands","paragraphs":["Kuk Swamp lies at about 1,560 metres in the upper Wahgi Valley near Mount Hagen, Papua New Guinea. Jack Golson led excavations in its wetland from the early 1970s, and in 2003 Tim Denham and colleagues published a new sequence in Science. About 10,000 years ago people dug pits, set stakes and cut small channels at the swamp margin, and starch grains of taro survive on their stone tools. Between about 6,950 and 6,440 years ago they built mounds for planting crops that do not tolerate waterlogged soil, and phytoliths show that bananas of the Musa section were abundant. From about 4,400 years ago networks of ditches drained the swamp for cultivation.","Kuk shows that farming was invented independently in highland New Guinea, based on vegetatively propagated crops rather than cereals: bananas, taro, yams and probably sugarcane, which was domesticated on the island. UNESCO inscribed the Kuk Early Agricultural Site in 2008. New Guinea bananas later spread westward across Asia and eventually to Africa. Farming here never produced cities, but it supported some of the densest rural populations in the world, and the pigs that became central to highland life arrived later from Asia."]},{"heading":"Tasmania alone","paragraphs":["For about 12,000 years the ancestors of today’s Palawa people lived without contact with the mainland. Excavations by Rhys Jones at Rocky Cape in the north-west in the 1960s showed that bone points and scale fish disappear from the record around 3,500 years ago. In 1977 Jones called this the “Tasmanian paradox” and wrote of “a slow strangulation of the mind”, a phrase that has been sharply criticised ever since.","Later archaeologists pointed out that Tasmanians hunted seals, wallabies and birds, harvested shellfish at great scale, built bark canoes to reach offshore islands, and made the shell necklaces still crafted by Palawa women today. Colonial claims that they could not make fire rest on weak evidence. The debate below asks what the losses mean."]},{"heading":"Caring for Country: fire, eels and seeds","paragraphs":["Rhys Jones coined the term “fire-stick farming” in 1969 for the deliberate burning with which Aboriginal people managed Country. Small, patchy, cool fires, lit in the right season, created mosaics of vegetation that favoured game and plant foods and reduced the risk of catastrophic wildfire. Long-term studies by Rebecca Bliege Bird and Douglas Bird with Martu people in the Western Desert show that such burning increases the variety of habitats and the numbers of some small animals. Historian Bill Gammage argued in 2011 that the whole continent was managed as “the biggest estate on earth”, and Aboriginal fire practitioners such as Victor Steffensen now lead a revival of cultural burning.","At Budj Bim, the Gunditjmara built channels, weirs and holding ponds in the Tyrendarra lava flow to trap and store short-finned eels, or kooyang; a channel dated by Ian McNiven, Gunditjmara Traditional Owners and colleagues is at least 6,600 years old. Remains of stone huts nearby suggest that people stayed at the site for long seasons, at least in recent centuries. At Brewarrina on the Barwon River, the stone fish traps of Baiame’s Ngunnhu are of unknown but considerable age. In the arid interior, grindstones became common in the later Holocene as people harvested native millet and other grass seeds, ground them into flour and baked them as damper."]},{"heading":"Painted and engraved histories","paragraphs":["Australia’s rock art is among the largest and longest records of human imagery on Earth, and dating it has become possible only recently. In the Kimberley, Damien Finch and colleagues radiocarbon-dated fossil mud-wasp nests lying under and over paintings. In 2020 they showed that the elegant Gwion Gwion figures, with tassels, sashes and headdresses, were being painted about 12,000 years ago. In western Arnhem Land, Paul Taçon and colleagues, working with Traditional Owners, described the Maliwawa figures in 2020: large, naturalistic figures of people and animals, including bilbies that no longer live in the region, painted roughly 9,400–6,000 years ago.","On the Pilbara coast, Murujuga (the Burrup Peninsula and Dampier Archipelago) holds one of the world’s densest concentrations of petroglyphs, perhaps more than a million, including images of thylacines and faces. It was inscribed on the World Heritage List in 2025 after long campaigning by its Traditional Custodians, amid continuing concern about emissions from nearby industry."]},{"heading":"New tools, new words, a new animal","paragraphs":["In the mid to late Holocene, small backed artefacts, stone pieces blunted along one edge and set into spears and knives, spread across southern and eastern Australia; they are most common between about 4,000 and 1,500 years ago. Peter Hiscock has linked them to reliable, easily repaired toolkits in a period of more variable El Niño-driven climate.","Pama-Nyungan languages cover about seven-eighths of the continent. A 2018 study by Remco Bouckaert, Claire Bowern and Quentin Atkinson traced their origin to the Gulf Plains region about 6,000 years ago, a rare example of a great language expansion without farming. Genetic studies so far suggest that the languages spread largely through contact and intermarriage rather than by replacing the people who adopted them.","The dingo came last. The oldest firmly dated dingo bones, from Madura Cave on the Nullarbor Plain, are about 3,350–3,100 years old (Jane Balme and colleagues, 2018). Dingoes never reached Tasmania, and on the mainland the thylacine and Tasmanian devil disappeared around 3,000 years ago, possibly through competition with dingoes, a drier and more variable climate and growing human populations."]},{"heading":"Into the Pacific","paragraphs":["Around 3,300 years ago, or roughly 1350 BCE, a new culture appeared in the Bismarck Archipelago north-east of New Guinea. Its people made pottery stamped with toothed tools in intricate patterns, named Lapita after a site in New Caledonia, and moved obsidian from New Britain over long distances. Their ancestors spoke Austronesian languages and had come ultimately from Taiwan through the Philippines and eastern Indonesia.","Within a few centuries Lapita voyagers crossed the gap beyond the main Solomon Islands into the remote Pacific, which no humans had reached before. At Teouma in Vanuatu, a cemetery about 3,000 years old held burials whose skulls had often been removed. Genomes from Teouma, published by Pontus Skoglund and colleagues in 2016, show that these first settlers had almost entirely Asian-related ancestry; Papuan ancestry arrived in Vanuatu later. By about 2,800 years ago Lapita people had reached Fiji, Tonga and Samoa."]}],"debates":[{"question":"What happened to technology in isolated Tasmania?","views":[{"label":"Loss through isolation","text":"Rhys Jones (1977) saw the abandonment of bone tools and fishing as a decline caused by isolation. The anthropologist Joseph Henrich (2004) recast the idea in cultural-evolution terms: in a small, isolated population fewer skilled experts are available to copy, so complex skills can be lost by chance."},{"label":"Population size is not enough","text":"Mathematical and cross-cultural studies, such as a 2016 analysis by Krist Vaesen and colleagues, found that population size does not reliably predict toolkit complexity, and critics note that Tasmania’s population was not tiny and that its toolkit remained effective."},{"label":"Adaptive choice","text":"Many archaeologists and Palawa people argue that dropping fish and bone tools reflected changing environments and choices, such as a shift to seals, shellfish and birds, or a cultural prohibition, while other technologies such as bark canoes and shell necklaces flourished."}],"status":"Jones’s language of mental decline is now rejected. Whether demography alone can erase skills is still debated in cultural evolution; for Tasmania most researchers see a mix of adaptation and some loss, and emphasise the continuity of Palawa culture."},{"question":"Were Aboriginal Australians farmers? The Dark Emu debate","views":[{"label":"Agriculture and villages","text":"Bruce Pascoe’s Dark Emu (2014), drawing on colonial explorers’ journals describing harvested grain stooks, yam fields, stone houses and fish traps, argued that many Aboriginal societies practised agriculture and aquaculture and that colonists hid this to justify dispossession. The book was hugely influential and valued by many readers, Indigenous and not, for challenging the image of aimless nomads."},{"label":"Sophisticated hunter-gatherers","text":"Anthropologist Peter Sutton and archaeologist Keryn Walshe, in Farmers or Hunter-Gatherers? (2021), argued that the evidence shows skilled hunter-gatherers whose economies were bound up with law, kinship and spiritual obligations to Country, and that calling them farmers measures them against a European yardstick."},{"label":"Beyond the labels","text":"Many Aboriginal scholars and knowledge holders stress that caring for Country through fire, water and ceremony was its own system, which needs no validation as farming, and that its knowledge is alive today in ranger and cultural burning programmes."}],"status":"Specialists broadly agree that pre-colonial Aboriginal economies involved deliberate, large-scale management of plants, animals, fire and water, and broadly disagree with calling them agricultural. The lasting result of the debate has been wider public recognition of that management."},{"question":"Did outsiders bring the dingo, backed artefacts and new languages?","views":[{"label":"An outside impulse","text":"A 2013 genetic study led by Irina Pugach reported gene flow from India into Australia about 4,200 years ago and suggested it might be linked to the dingo and changes in stone tools."},{"label":"Mostly internal change","text":"The larger 2016 genome study led by Anna-Sapfo Malaspinas found no such Indian signal. Backed artefacts appear in small numbers long before the dingo, and Pama-Nyungan began in northern Australia, so most researchers see these as local developments, with the dingo brought by seafarers from island Southeast Asia who left little genetic trace."}],"status":"The internal view is now favoured. Who brought the dingo, and from exactly where, remains open; ancient dingo genomes and more directly dated bones may narrow it down."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Independent farming","value":"Kuk Swamp, New Guinea: mounds about 6,950–6,440 years ago, drains from about 4,400 years ago"},{"label":"Aquaculture","value":"Budj Bim eel channels, at least 6,600 years old; UNESCO 2019"},{"label":"Tasmania isolated","value":"About 12,000 years ago, by the flooding of Bass Strait"},{"label":"Oldest dingo bones","value":"Madura Cave, about 3,350–3,100 years ago"},{"label":"Pama-Nyungan","value":"About seven-eighths of Australia; origin about 6,000 years ago"},{"label":"Lapita","value":"Bismarck Archipelago from about 1350 BCE; Fiji, Tonga and Samoa by about 800 BCE"}],"siteIds":["kuk-swamp","budj-bim","rocky-cape","brewarrina-fish-traps","gwion-gwion-kimberley","murujuga","madura-cave","teouma"],"routes":[{"id":"dingo-arrival","label":"Dingo arrives with seafarers","start":3600,"end":3300,"certainty":"debated","path":[[119.9,-4],[123.6,-10.2],[126.6,-14.3]]},{"id":"lapita-voyages","label":"Lapita voyages","start":3350,"end":2800,"certainty":"established","path":[[149.6,-1.5],[150.1,-5.5],[166.1,-10.2],[168.3,-17.7],[178,-17.8]]}],"themes":["farming","food","climate","fire","art","animals","language","technology","seafaring","migration","society"],"sources":[{"cite":"Denham, T. P. et al. (2003). Origins of agriculture at Kuk Swamp in the highlands of New Guinea. Science 301, 189–193."},{"cite":"McNiven, I. J. et al. (2012). Dating Aboriginal stone-walled fishtraps at Lake Condah, southeast Australia. Journal of Archaeological Science 39."},{"cite":"Jones, R. (1977). The Tasmanian paradox. In R. V. S. Wright (ed.), Stone Tools as Cultural Markers. Canberra: Australian Institute of Aboriginal Studies."},{"cite":"Henrich, J. (2004). Demography and cultural evolution: how adaptive cultural processes can produce maladaptive losses. The Tasmanian case. American Antiquity 69, 197–214."},{"cite":"Pascoe, B. (2014). Dark Emu: Black Seeds, Agriculture or Accident? Broome: Magabala Books."},{"cite":"Sutton, P. and Walshe, K. (2021). Farmers or Hunter-Gatherers? The Dark Emu Debate. Melbourne University Press."},{"cite":"Finch, D. et al. (2020). 12,000-year-old Aboriginal rock art from the Kimberley region, Western Australia. Science Advances 6."},{"cite":"Balme, J., O’Connor, S. and Fallon, S. (2018). New dates on dingo bones from Madura Cave provide oldest firm evidence for arrival of the species in Australia. Scientific Reports 8."},{"cite":"Bouckaert, R. R., Bowern, C. and Atkinson, Q. D. (2018). The origin and expansion of Pama-Nyungan languages across Australia. Nature Ecology & Evolution 2, 741–749."},{"cite":"Skoglund, P. et al. (2016). Genomic insights into the peopling of the Southwest Pacific. Nature 538, 510–513."}]},{"id":"americas-holocene","order":40,"act":"transition","title":"Maize, Mounds and Mummies","subtitle":"The Archaic Americas from the Great Lakes to the Andes","era":"Archaic and Preceramic periods","start":11000,"end":3500,"dateLabel":"c. 9000–1500 BCE","regions":["americas"],"focus":[-170,-45,-35,75],"emblem":"maize","summary":"In the millennia after the Ice Age, peoples of the Americas domesticated squash, maize, potatoes, manioc and llamas in several independent centres, hammered native copper, raised earthen mounds without farming, invented deliberate mummification and built the monumental centre of Caral without pottery.","paragraphs":[["On the desert coast of northern Chile, around 7,000 years ago, fishing families of the Chinchorro culture began to take their dead apart.","They removed the skin and organs, strengthened the skeleton with sticks, packed the body with plant fibre and clay, and rebuilt the person, finishing with a painted mask.","These are the oldest deliberately made mummies known anywhere."],["Across the Americas, the thousands of years after the Ice Age were a time of patient experiment.","In the dry hills of south-western Mexico people gathered a wild grass called teosinte, whose few hard seeds were hardly worth eating.","Over millennia of planting and selecting, it became maize.","Squash, beans, chilli and avocado joined it.","In the Andes people tamed the potato, quinoa, the llama and the guinea pig, and on forest islands in the Bolivian Amazon they were growing manioc and squash more than 10,000 years ago."],["Not every great work waited for farming.","In Louisiana, around 3500 BCE, hunter-gatherers raised eleven earthen mounds at Watson Brake.","Around the Great Lakes, people hammered lumps of pure copper into spear points and knives, among the earliest metal tools anywhere."],["And on the coast of Peru, at Caral, people who made no pottery built stepped platforms and sunken circular plazas, some of the oldest monumental architecture in the Americas, at the same time as the pyramids were rising in Egypt."]],"sentences":["On the desert coast of northern Chile, around 7,000 years ago, fishing families of the Chinchorro culture began to take their dead apart.","They removed the skin and organs, strengthened the skeleton with sticks, packed the body with plant fibre and clay, and rebuilt the person, finishing with a painted mask.","These are the oldest deliberately made mummies known anywhere.","Across the Americas, the thousands of years after the Ice Age were a time of patient experiment.","In the dry hills of south-western Mexico people gathered a wild grass called teosinte, whose few hard seeds were hardly worth eating.","Over millennia of planting and selecting, it became maize.","Squash, beans, chilli and avocado joined it.","In the Andes people tamed the potato, quinoa, the llama and the guinea pig, and on forest islands in the Bolivian Amazon they were growing manioc and squash more than 10,000 years ago.","Not every great work waited for farming.","In Louisiana, around 3500 BCE, hunter-gatherers raised eleven earthen mounds at Watson Brake.","Around the Great Lakes, people hammered lumps of pure copper into spear points and knives, among the earliest metal tools anywhere.","And on the coast of Peru, at Caral, people who made no pottery built stepped platforms and sunken circular plazas, some of the oldest monumental architecture in the Americas, at the same time as the pyramids were rising in Egypt."],"pronounce":{"Chinchorro":"Chin-chor-oh","teosinte":"tay-oh-sin-tay","quinoa":"keen-wah","Caral":"Kah-rahl"},"body":[{"heading":"The Archaic: many ways to live","paragraphs":["Archaeologists call the long period after the Ice Age hunters of the Paleoindian era the Archaic: in North America roughly 8000–1000 BCE. With the mammoths gone, people hunted deer and smaller game with spear-throwers, fished, collected shellfish and gathered nuts and seeds, which they ground on stone slabs. Many groups became more settled, returning to the same river valleys for generations.","At Koster in the lower Illinois Valley, excavated by Stuart Struever from 1969, more than a dozen occupation layers stack up over thousands of years, including houses from the Middle Archaic. Dogs buried there have been directly dated to about 10,000 years ago, among the oldest dog burials in the Americas. At Windover in Florida, people buried their dead in a peat pond between about 8,000 and 7,000 years ago. The waterlogged peat preserved twined fabrics of plant fibre and even brain tissue in many of the roughly 170 individuals recovered."]},{"heading":"Copper, mounds and seed crops","paragraphs":["Around Lake Superior, glaciers and bedrock expose nuggets of almost pure native copper. The people of the Old Copper Complex cold-hammered and annealed it into spear points, knives, awls and fishhooks. A 2021 radiocarbon study led by David Pompeani pushed the start of this metalworking back to about 9,500 years ago, with a peak between about 7,000 and 5,000 years ago; later, copper was used mainly for ornaments. It was metalworking without smelting or casting.","At Watson Brake in Louisiana, Joe Saunders and colleagues showed in 1997 that eleven mounds joined by ridges in an oval about 280 metres across were begun around 3500 BCE. The builders ate fish, deer, nuts and wild seeds; they did not farm or make pottery. Watson Brake overturned the assumption that monuments require agriculture. The much larger Poverty Point, with its concentric ridges, followed around 1700–1100 BCE.","In the same region people domesticated their own crops, the Eastern Agricultural Complex studied by Bruce Smith: a native squash, sunflower, marsh elder and goosefoot, with domestic forms appearing between about 5,000 and 3,800 years ago. Eastern North America was one of the world’s independent centres of plant domestication."]},{"heading":"From teosinte to maize","paragraphs":["At Guilá Naquitz, a small cave in the Oaxaca Valley excavated by Kent Flannery in the 1960s, Bruce Smith directly dated seeds of domesticated squash (Cucurbita pepo) to about 10,000 years ago, making squash one of the oldest crops in the Americas. The same cave yielded some of the earliest known maize cobs, about 6,250 years old: tiny, only a few centimetres long.","Maize comes from a lowland teosinte (Zea mays ssp. parviglumis). In 2002 Yoshihiro Matsuoka, John Doebley and colleagues used genetic markers to trace a single domestication to the Balsas River basin of south-western Mexico about 9,000 years ago. In 2009 Dolores Piperno and Anthony Ranere found maize starch and phytoliths on grinding tools at Xihuatoxtla rock shelter in the central Balsas Valley, dated to about 8,700 years ago.","Domestication was slow and scattered. Logan Kistler and colleagues showed in 2018 that maize reached South America only partly domesticated and continued to evolve there, with south-western Amazonia a secondary centre of improvement. Beans were domesticated separately in Mesoamerica and the Andes; chilli peppers and avocados were also first brought into cultivation in Mexico."]},{"heading":"The Andes: tubers, camelids and cotton","paragraphs":["In the Ñanchoc Valley of northern Peru, Tom Dillehay’s team reported in 2007 remains of squash, peanuts and cotton from small Preceramic farming communities, the squash nearly 10,000 years old and the cotton several thousand years younger. Higher up, the potato was domesticated from wild relatives in the region of Lake Titicaca and southern Peru, along with quinoa and other hardy crops. On the high grasslands of the puna, work by Jane Wheeler at sites such as Telarmachay traces the domestication of llamas from wild guanacos and alpacas from vicuñas, by roughly 6,000–5,000 years ago; guinea pigs were also domesticated in the Andes for meat.","At Huaca Prieta, a great mound on the north coast of Peru excavated by Dillehay, Jeffrey Splitstoser and colleagues identified in 2016 a cotton fabric dyed with indigo about 6,200 years ago, the oldest known use of indigo anywhere."]},{"heading":"Chinchorro: the first mummies","paragraphs":["The Chinchorro were fishers and shellfish gatherers on the Atacama coast of northern Chile and southern Peru. From about 5000 BCE they began to prepare some of their dead with extraordinary care: in so-called black mummies the body was taken apart, reinforced with sticks and reeds, filled with plant fibre, ash and clay, covered with skin and painted with manganese; later red mummies were painted with ochre. Infants, children and adults of both sexes were treated this way, so the practice was not reserved for a ruling class.","Hundreds of Chinchorro bodies have been found, many around Arica, and UNESCO inscribed the settlements and mummification sites in 2021. Some mummies seem to have been repaired and repainted, suggesting they were kept among the living for some time."]},{"heading":"Caral and the Norte Chico","paragraphs":["In the valleys north of Lima, the region known as the Norte Chico, some thirty sites have monumental architecture from the Late Preceramic. The best studied is Caral in the Supe Valley, excavated by Ruth Shady Solís since 1994. In 2001 Shady, Jonathan Haas and Winifred Creamer published radiocarbon dates showing that its platform mounds and sunken circular plazas were in use around 2600 BCE; the occupation spans roughly 3000–1800 BCE. Caral had no pottery, and its people grew cotton, squash, beans, gourds and fruit with irrigation, while coastal sites such as Áspero harvested anchovies and sardines. Caral was inscribed by UNESCO in 2009."]},{"heading":"Amazonia and the Arctic","paragraphs":["In the seasonally flooded savannas of the Llanos de Mojos in Bolivia, thousands of small forest islands rise above the grassland. In 2020 Umberto Lombardo and colleagues showed that many are human-made, built up from refuse, and that people grew manioc there about 10,350 years ago, squash about 10,250 years ago and maize by about 6,850 years ago, making south-western Amazonia an early centre of cultivation.","At the other end of the hemisphere, a new population crossed from Siberia about 5,000 years ago and spread across Arctic Alaska, Canada and Greenland: the Paleo-Inuit. In 2010 Morten Rasmussen and Eske Willerslev’s team sequenced a genome from a tuft of hair about 4,000 years old from a Saqqaq-culture site at Qeqertasussuk in Greenland. It was the first ancient human genome, and it showed that this man’s closest living relatives were the Chukchi and Koryak of north-eastern Siberia, not Native Americans or later Inuit. Later studies found that the Paleo-Inuit persisted for about 4,000 years before the ancestors of today’s Inuit spread from Alaska."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Were the Norte Chico monuments built on fish or on farming?","views":[{"label":"Maritime foundations","text":"Michael Moseley proposed in 1975 that the rich anchovy fishery of the Peruvian coast, not farming, supported the first large, complex societies of the Andes. Fish bones dominate food remains at Caral and other sites, and cotton was grown largely for nets."},{"label":"Irrigation agriculture","text":"Jonathan Haas, Winifred Creamer and colleagues argue that the largest centres lay inland in the valleys, where irrigation farming made them possible. In 2013 they reported pollen, starch and phytoliths suggesting that maize was a significant food, a claim others consider weakly supported."},{"label":"Exchange between coast and valley","text":"Ruth Shady emphasises a mixed economy in which inland farmers traded cotton and plant foods for fish from coastal settlements such as Áspero."}],"status":"Most specialists now see a combination: marine protein and irrigated plants, including industrial cotton, supported by exchange. The importance of maize at this early date remains disputed."},{"question":"Why did people start growing teosinte?","views":[{"label":"For its grain","text":"The conventional view, supported by maize starch grains on grinding tools at Xihuatoxtla by about 8,700 years ago, is that people selected teosinte for its seeds, gradually turning a hard-cased grass into an exposed-kernel cereal."},{"label":"For its sweet stalks","text":"Botanist Hugh Iltis, and later John Smalley and Michael Blake, argued that early teosinte was valued for sugary stalks that could be chewed or fermented into drinks, and that larger ears came later as a by-product."}],"status":"Both uses probably mattered. Genetic studies agree on a single origin in the Balsas region about 9,000 years ago followed by a long, geographically spread process of improvement, including later mixing with a highland teosinte in Mexico."},{"question":"Why did the Chinchorro mummify their dead?","views":[{"label":"A response to loss","text":"Bernardo Arriaza has suggested that high infant and foetal mortality, possibly linked to arsenic in the region’s rivers, encouraged elaborate treatment of the dead, beginning with children."},{"label":"Ancestors and identity","text":"Many specialists stress social and religious motives: preserved and repainted bodies kept ancestors present in the community and may have strengthened claims to territory and resources."}],"status":"The two ideas are compatible. Elevated arsenic has been measured in some Chinchorro remains, but it cannot explain on its own why a whole society adopted complex mummification for about 3,000 years."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Oldest domesticated crop in the Americas","value":"Squash at Guilá Naquitz, about 10,000 years ago"},{"label":"Maize","value":"Domesticated from Balsas teosinte about 9,000 years ago"},{"label":"First artificial mummies","value":"Chinchorro, from about 5000 BCE; UNESCO 2021"},{"label":"Mounds before farming","value":"Watson Brake, Louisiana, begun about 3500 BCE"},{"label":"Native copper tools","value":"Great Lakes, from about 9,500 years ago"},{"label":"Caral","value":"Monumental centre without pottery, about 3000–1800 BCE"}],"siteIds":["koster","windover","keweenaw-copper","watson-brake","guila-naquitz","xihuatoxtla","llanos-de-mojos","huaca-prieta","chinchorro-arica","caral","qeqertasussuk"],"routes":[{"id":"maize-south","label":"Maize spreads south","start":8700,"end":6800,"certainty":"established","path":[[-99.5,18.3],[-90.5,15.5],[-80,8.5],[-74.5,4.5],[-65.2,-14.2]]},{"id":"paleo-inuit","label":"Paleo-Inuit spread from Siberia","start":5500,"end":4500,"certainty":"established","path":[[-173,65],[-165,67.5],[-140,69.5],[-110,68.5],[-85,69.5],[-72,77],[-51.25,68.85]]}],"themes":["farming","food","animals","monuments","settlement","burial","metal","technology","genetics","migration","society"],"sources":[{"cite":"Smith, B. D. (1997). The initial domestication of Cucurbita pepo in the Americas 10,000 years ago. Science 276, 932–934."},{"cite":"Matsuoka, Y. et al. (2002). A single domestication for maize shown by multilocus microsatellite genotyping. PNAS 99, 6080–6084."},{"cite":"Piperno, D. R. et al. (2009). Starch grain and phytolith evidence for early ninth millennium B.P. maize from the Central Balsas River Valley, Mexico. PNAS 106, 5019–5024."},{"cite":"Kistler, L. et al. (2018). Multiproxy evidence highlights a complex evolutionary legacy of maize in South America. Science 362, 1309–1313."},{"cite":"Saunders, J. W. et al. (1997). A mound complex in Louisiana at 5400–5000 years before the present. Science 277, 1796–1799."},{"cite":"Shady Solís, R., Haas, J. and Creamer, W. (2001). Dating Caral, a preceramic site in the Supe Valley on the central coast of Peru. Science 292, 723–726."},{"cite":"Moseley, M. E. (1975). The Maritime Foundations of Andean Civilization. Menlo Park: Cummings."},{"cite":"Lombardo, U. et al. (2020). Early Holocene crop cultivation and landscape modification in Amazonia. Nature 581, 190–193."},{"cite":"Splitstoser, J. C. et al. (2016). Early pre-Hispanic use of indigo blue in Peru. Science Advances 2."},{"cite":"Rasmussen, M. et al. (2010). Ancient human genome sequence of an extinct Palaeo-Eskimo. Nature 463, 757–762."}]},{"id":"copper-age","order":41,"act":"metal","title":"The Copper Age","subtitle":"Smelters, gold, wheels, writing and the herders of the steppe","era":"Chalcolithic (Copper Age, Eneolithic)","start":10500,"end":4500,"dateLabel":"c. 8500–2500 BCE","regions":["europe","asia","africa"],"focus":[-10,25,88,60],"emblem":"copperaxe","summary":"From hammered nuggets of native copper to smelting furnaces, Varna’s gold, Ötzi’s axe, the first wheels, the first writing at Uruk and the Yamnaya herders of the steppe, the Copper Age remade the societies of western Eurasia.","paragraphs":[["In September 1991 two hikers high in the Ötztal Alps, on the border between Austria and Italy, found a body melting out of the ice.","The man had died about 5,200 years ago, shot in the back with a stone-tipped arrow.","Beside him lay a bow, a quiver, a flint dagger and an axe with a blade of almost pure copper, made from ore that probably came from Tuscany, hundreds of kilometres to the south."],["Ötzi, as he is now known, lived late in the Copper Age, a period that began when people hammered cold nuggets of native copper into beads.","Around 5000 BCE, at Belovode in Serbia, someone heated green malachite in a fire hot enough to release liquid metal.","Within a few centuries people at Varna, on the Black Sea coast, were being buried with more worked gold than has been found at any other site of that age."],["The next fifteen hundred years brought changes that still shape our lives.","Wheels and wagons appeared across a wide area around 3500 BCE.","In the city of Uruk, clerks began pressing accounts of grain and beer into clay, the first steps towards writing."],["And on the grasslands north of the Black Sea and the Caspian, herders called the Yamnaya raised great burial mounds and moved east and west with their wagons and cattle, spreading their ancestry, and very probably their languages, across a continent."]],"sentences":["In September 1991 two hikers high in the Ötztal Alps, on the border between Austria and Italy, found a body melting out of the ice.","The man had died about 5,200 years ago, shot in the back with a stone-tipped arrow.","Beside him lay a bow, a quiver, a flint dagger and an axe with a blade of almost pure copper, made from ore that probably came from Tuscany, hundreds of kilometres to the south.","Ötzi, as he is now known, lived late in the Copper Age, a period that began when people hammered cold nuggets of native copper into beads.","Around 5000 BCE, at Belovode in Serbia, someone heated green malachite in a fire hot enough to release liquid metal.","Within a few centuries people at Varna, on the Black Sea coast, were being buried with more worked gold than has been found at any other site of that age.","The next fifteen hundred years brought changes that still shape our lives.","Wheels and wagons appeared across a wide area around 3500 BCE.","In the city of Uruk, clerks began pressing accounts of grain and beer into clay, the first steps towards writing.","And on the grasslands north of the Black Sea and the Caspian, herders called the Yamnaya raised great burial mounds and moved east and west with their wagons and cattle, spreading their ancestry, and very probably their languages, across a continent."],"pronounce":{"Ötztal":"Urts-tahl","Ötzi":"Urt-see","Belovode":"Beh-lo-vo-deh","Varna":"Var-nah","Uruk":"Oo-rook","Yamnaya":"Yahm-nah-yah"},"body":[{"heading":"Before smelting: cold-hammered copper","paragraphs":["Metal began as a kind of stone. Nuggets of native copper, which occur naturally in almost pure form, can be hammered like a soft mineral. An oval copper pendant from the Shanidar area in Iraq is usually dated to about the tenth millennium BCE, and at Çayönü in south-eastern Turkey, from about 8200 BCE, villagers hammered native copper and the green copper mineral malachite into beads, hooks and awls. They also learned to anneal the metal, heating it so that it could be worked further without cracking. For some three thousand years copper remained a rare ornament.","Archaeologists call the age that followed the Chalcolithic, from the Greek for copper and stone, or the Eneolithic. The names are a reminder that stone tools remained in daily use throughout; metal was added, not substituted."]},{"heading":"Fire and ore: the first smelting","paragraphs":["Smelting, extracting metal from ore in a charcoal fire of well over 1,000 degrees, was a real invention. At Belovode in Serbia, a Vinča-culture settlement, Miljana Radivojević and colleagues reported in 2010 copper slag and processed malachite dated to about 5000 BCE, the oldest securely dated smelting yet known. Crucible smelting in the fifth millennium BCE is also known from Tal-i Iblis in south-eastern Iran, so the technique may have been invented more than once.","Balkan miners followed malachite veins underground at Rudna Glava in Serbia and cut long open trenches at Ai Bunar in Bulgaria, and heavy cast copper hammer-axes circulated widely. At Pločnik in Serbia a sheet of tin bronze, dated to about 4650 BCE, shows that metalworkers smelted mixed copper and tin ores long before bronze was made deliberately. In the southern Levant, copper was mined and smelted at Faynan in Jordan and Timna in Israel. In 1961 Pessah Bar-Adon found the Nahal Mishmar hoard of more than 400 objects in a Judean Desert cave, among them crowns, maceheads and standards made in the late fifth or early fourth millennium BCE by lost-wax casting in arsenic-rich copper. In Egypt, Naqada-period communities of the fourth millennium BCE worked copper from Sinai and the Eastern Desert."]},{"heading":"Varna: gold and inequality","paragraphs":["In 1972 a digger driver cutting a cable trench near Varna, on the Bulgarian Black Sea coast, turned up gold. Excavations led by Ivan Ivanov uncovered about 300 graves dated to roughly 4600–4300 BCE, with some 3,000 gold objects weighing about 6 kilograms in total, alongside copper axes, Spondylus shell ornaments from the Aegean and long flint blades. Grave 43 held a middle-aged man with gold bracelets, a gold-covered sceptre-like axe and a gold penis sheath. Some of the richest graves held no body at all, only objects and, in several, clay masks decorated with gold.","Nearby, at Provadia-Solnitsata, Vassil Nikolov’s team excavated a walled settlement of about 4700–4200 BCE where brine from salt springs was boiled in ovens to make salt blocks, a valuable trade good that may help explain the wealth of the region."]},{"heading":"The man in the ice","paragraphs":["The Iceman found on the Tisenjoch pass at about 3,210 metres in 1991 has been radiocarbon dated to about 3350–3120 BCE. His copper axe, with a yew handle, used metal that a 2017 lead-isotope study traced to southern Tuscany. He carried a longbow, arrows, a backpack, a birch-bark container with embers and a first-aid kit of birch fungus. His skin bears 61 tattoos, mostly groups of lines over joints and the lower back, probably a form of treatment for pain. His last meal, eaten a few hours before death, included very fatty ibex meat, red deer and einkorn wheat. An arrowhead lodged in his left shoulder, found by X-ray in 2001, severed an artery.","In 2023 a team led by Ke Wang and Johannes Krause published a high-coverage genome. It showed that Ötzi had darker skin than earlier reconstructions assumed, was probably balding, and carried about 90 percent Anatolian farmer ancestry, with only a little from European hunter-gatherers and none from the steppe."]},{"heading":"Uruk: the first city and the first signs","paragraphs":["In southern Mesopotamia the fourth millennium BCE is named after Uruk, which grew into one of the largest settlements of its time, with monumental temple complexes in the Eanna precinct. Mass-produced bevelled-rim bowls, probably used to issue rations, point to large-scale administration of labour. Uruk-style settlements and outposts appeared as far away as Syria and south-eastern Turkey.","Around 3300 BCE, scribes at Uruk began to impress signs into clay tablets. Several thousand of these proto-cuneiform tablets survive; most record quantities of barley, beer, livestock, textiles and workers, and a minority are standard lists of words used to train scribes. This was not yet a way of writing down speech, but it grew into the cuneiform script over the next few centuries, at almost the same time as writing emerged in Egypt."]},{"heading":"Wheels, ploughs and wool","paragraphs":["Wheeled vehicles appear almost simultaneously across a vast area. A pot from Bronocice in Poland, dated to about 3500–3350 BCE, carries what looks like a schematic wagon; wheel tracks preserved under a long barrow at Flintbek in northern Germany date to about 3400 BCE; the oldest surviving wooden wheel, found in the Ljubljana Marshes in Slovenia in 2002, dates to about 3150 BCE. Pictographs of wagons appear in the Uruk tablets, and clay wagon models and wagon burials are known from the steppe soon afterwards.","In 1981 Andrew Sherratt proposed a “secondary products revolution”: in the fourth millennium BCE farmers began to exploit animals for milk, wool and traction as well as meat, with the plough and the wagon transforming farming and transport. Ard marks under burial mounds confirm early ploughing. Later research, including milk residues in Anatolian pots of the seventh millennium BCE published by Richard Evershed and colleagues in 2008, shows that dairying began much earlier, but the combination of wool sheep, draught oxen, ploughs and wheels did come together in the fourth millennium."]},{"heading":"Horses, herders and plague","paragraphs":["At Botai in northern Kazakhstan, around 3500 BCE, people lived largely on horses, and Alan Outram and colleagues reported possible bit wear and mare’s milk residues. But in 2018 a genetic study led by Ludovic Orlando showed that the Botai horses were ancestors of Przewalski’s horses, not of domestic horses. In 2021 Pablo Librado, Orlando and colleagues traced all modern domestic horses (the lineage called DOM2) to the lower Volga-Don region, where breeding selected for docility and a stronger back, and whence they spread across Eurasia after about 2200 BCE; a 2024 study confirmed that controlled horse breeding and widespread horse-based mobility began around that time.","The Yamnaya (about 3300–2600 BCE) were mobile herders of cattle and sheep who buried their dead under kurgan mounds, sometimes with wagons. Genomes published in 2015 by Wolfgang Haak, David Reich and colleagues showed that people with largely Yamnaya-related ancestry moved into central Europe, where the Corded Ware culture of about 2900–2350 BCE carried around three-quarters of such ancestry. In 2025 Iosif Lazaridis and colleagues traced Yamnaya origins to a genetic cline of the fifth millennium BCE stretching from the Caucasus to the Lower Volga, whose people mixed with hunter-gatherers of what is now Ukraine. To the south, the Maykop culture of the North Caucasus, with its lavish kurgan burials, had links with the Near East.","Plague accompanied these changes. In 2019 Nicolás Rascovan and colleagues found Yersinia pestis in a woman buried about 4,900 years ago at Frälsegården in Sweden, and a 2024 Nature study led by Frederik Seersholm detected plague in about one in six of 108 individuals of the same farming community, in at least three waves, supporting the idea that epidemics contributed to the late Neolithic decline of northern European farmers."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Does Varna show the first hereditary elite?","views":[{"label":"An early ranked society","text":"Colin Renfrew and others read the concentration of gold, copper and exotic shell in a few graves as evidence of a hierarchical society in which a small group controlled prestige goods and perhaps inherited their status, a precocious step towards chiefdoms."},{"label":"Ritual display and social roles","text":"Other archaeologists point out that many rich graves were symbolic, with no body, and that most objects may mark ritual offices or community ceremonies rather than personal wealth. Refined radiocarbon dates suggest the cemetery was used over a relatively short period, and the Balkan Copper Age societies collapsed soon after."}],"status":"Varna clearly shows marked differences in access to precious goods by about 4500 BCE. Whether these were inherited, and whether they reflect political power or religious roles, is still open; ancient DNA and kinship analysis of the burials may help."},{"question":"Where was the wheel invented?","views":[{"label":"Mesopotamia","text":"The traditional view holds that the wheel spread from the urban societies of Mesopotamia, which had the potter’s wheel and early pictographs of wagons."},{"label":"The steppe","text":"David Anthony and others emphasise how quickly heavy wagons were adopted by herders of the Pontic-Caspian steppe and its western fringes, where wagon burials and wheeled clay models appear early, making the region a candidate and certainly a key route of spread."},{"label":"Central Europe","text":"The Bronocice pot, the Flintbek tracks and the Ljubljana wheel are among the oldest direct evidence. Historian Richard Bulliet suggested that the wheel and axle began as small carts in Carpathian copper mines."}],"status":"Wheeled vehicles appear within a few centuries of 3500 BCE across a band from Mesopotamia to northern Europe, faster than radiocarbon can resolve. The place of invention is unknown, and some researchers suspect the idea spread so quickly that it will never be pinned down."},{"question":"When were horses domesticated and ridden?","views":[{"label":"Early, at Botai","text":"Alan Outram and colleagues (2009) argued that Botai people bridled and milked horses around 3500 BCE, making them the first horse domesticators."},{"label":"Yamnaya riders","text":"Martin Trautmann, Volker Heyd and colleagues (2023) identified skeletal changes typical of riding in some Yamnaya individuals of about 3000 BCE and argued that riding helped drive the steppe expansion."},{"label":"Late domestication of today’s horses","text":"Genomic studies led by Ludovic Orlando and Pablo Librado (2018, 2021, 2024) show that Botai horses did not give rise to domestic horses and that the modern lineage expanded only after about 2200 BCE, too late for the Yamnaya migrations."}],"status":"Today’s horses come from the lower Volga-Don after about 2200 BCE. Earlier people may have managed or even occasionally ridden other horse populations, but the Yamnaya expansion did not depend on domestic horses; the riding evidence from skeletons is contested."},{"question":"Where was the Indo-European homeland?","views":[{"label":"The steppe","text":"Marija Gimbutas and later David Anthony located it among steppe herders of the fourth millennium BCE. Ancient DNA showing steppe ancestry spreading into Europe and South Asia after 3000 BCE strongly supports this for most branches."},{"label":"Anatolia","text":"Colin Renfrew (1987) proposed that the languages spread with the first farmers from Anatolia after about 7000 BCE; a 2012 language phylogeny by Remco Bouckaert, Quentin Atkinson and colleagues gave dates consistent with this."},{"label":"An Indo-Anatolian synthesis","text":"Early speakers of Hittite and the other Anatolian languages show little or no steppe ancestry. In 2023 Paul Heggarty and colleagues proposed a hybrid model with an older root south of the Caucasus; in 2025 Lazaridis and colleagues placed the Indo-Anatolian ancestors in the Caucasus to Lower Volga cline, with the Yamnaya carrying the later branches."}],"status":"By 2026 most researchers accept that the Yamnaya spread most Indo-European branches. Where and when the deeper Indo-Anatolian ancestor was spoken, and how the Anatolian branch reached Anatolia, remain the live questions."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Earliest securely dated smelting","value":"Belovode, Serbia, about 5000 BCE"},{"label":"Earliest tin bronze","value":"Foil from Pločnik, Serbia, about 4650 BCE"},{"label":"Varna gold","value":"About 3,000 objects, roughly 4600–4300 BCE"},{"label":"Ötzi","value":"Died about 3350–3120 BCE; copper axe, 61 tattoos"},{"label":"Proto-cuneiform","value":"Uruk, about 3300 BCE"},{"label":"Oldest wooden wheel","value":"Ljubljana Marshes, about 3150 BCE"}],"siteIds":["cayonu","belovode","plocnik","varna-necropolis","provadia-solnitsata","nahal-mishmar","timna","uruk","maykop-kurgan","khvalynsk","otzi","ljubljana-marshes-wheel"],"routes":[{"id":"steppe-west","label":"Steppe ancestry moves west","start":5100,"end":4700,"certainty":"established","path":[[46,48],[37,47],[30,46.5],[28,45.2],[21,47.2],[15,51]]},{"id":"steppe-east","label":"Yamnaya-related herders move east to the Altai","start":5300,"end":5000,"certainty":"established","path":[[50,51],[62,51.5],[75,52],[86,51]]}],"themes":["metal","technology","burial","society","settlement","animals","genetics","migration","language","farming"],"sources":[{"cite":"Radivojević, M. et al. (2010). On the origins of extractive metallurgy: new evidence from Europe. Journal of Archaeological Science 37, 2775–2787."},{"cite":"Sherratt, A. (1981). Plough and pastoralism: aspects of the secondary products revolution. In I. Hodder, G. Isaac and N. Hammond (eds), Pattern of the Past. Cambridge University Press."},{"cite":"Wang, K. et al. (2023). High-coverage genome of the Tyrolean Iceman reveals unusually high Anatolian farmer ancestry. Cell Genomics 3."},{"cite":"Gaunitz, C. et al. (2018). Ancient genomes revisit the ancestry of domestic and Przewalski’s horses. Science 360, 111–114."},{"cite":"Librado, P. et al. (2021). The origins and spread of domestic horses from the Western Eurasian steppes. Nature 598, 634–640."},{"cite":"Librado, P. et al. (2024). Widespread horse-based mobility arose around 2200 BCE in Eurasia. Nature 631."},{"cite":"Haak, W. et al. (2015). Massive migration from the steppe was a source for Indo-European languages in Europe. Nature 522, 207–211."},{"cite":"Heggarty, P. et al. (2023). Language trees with sampled ancestors support a hybrid model for the origin of Indo-European languages. Science 381."},{"cite":"Lazaridis, I. et al. (2025). The genetic origin of the Indo-Anatolians. Nature."},{"cite":"Seersholm, F. V. et al. (2024). Repeated plague infections across six generations of Neolithic Farmers. Nature."}]},{"id":"dawn-of-bronze","order":42,"act":"metal","title":"A Patchwork Ending","subtitle":"How bronze, iron or no metal at all closed the Stone Age, region by region","era":"Bronze Age and Early Iron Age transitions","start":5300,"end":2500,"dateLabel":"c. 3300–500 BCE","regions":["europe","asia","africa","americas","oceania"],"focus":[-120,-40,150,62],"emblem":"globe","summary":"Bronze reached the Near East around 3000 BCE, Britain and central Europe around 2200 BCE, China around 1900 BCE and Southeast Asia later still, while much of Africa went straight from stone to iron and the Americas and Oceania followed their own paths: the Stone Age ended as a patchwork, not a single event.","paragraphs":[["In 2002, archaeologists working ahead of new housing near Amesbury, about five kilometres from Stonehenge, uncovered the grave of a man who had died around 2300 BCE.","He was buried with three copper knives, a pair of gold hair ornaments, flint arrowheads and a small stone anvil for working metal.","Chemical traces in his teeth showed that he had grown up far away, probably in the Alps.","Within a few centuries, newcomers from the continent had replaced about nine tenths of the ancestry of Britain’s earlier farmers."],["Bronze, copper hardened with a little tin, sounds like a single invention, but it arrived like a slow tide with many inlets.","Tin is rare, found in a handful of places such as Cornwall, the Ore Mountains of central Europe and Central Asia, so bronze tied distant regions together through trade.","The Near East and the Aegean were making copper alloys by around 3000 BCE.","Central Europe and Britain followed around 2200 BCE, China around 1900 BCE, and Southeast Asia perhaps a thousand years later."],["Much of Africa south of the Sahara never had a Bronze Age and moved from stone to iron.","In the Andes, people hammered gold foil four thousand years ago but made bronze much later.","Australia, New Guinea and the Pacific islands had no metalworking of their own at all."],["So the Stone Age did not end on one day.","It faded region by region, and in many places stone tools stayed in daily use for thousands of years more."]],"sentences":["In 2002, archaeologists working ahead of new housing near Amesbury, about five kilometres from Stonehenge, uncovered the grave of a man who had died around 2300 BCE.","He was buried with three copper knives, a pair of gold hair ornaments, flint arrowheads and a small stone anvil for working metal.","Chemical traces in his teeth showed that he had grown up far away, probably in the Alps.","Within a few centuries, newcomers from the continent had replaced about nine tenths of the ancestry of Britain’s earlier farmers.","Bronze, copper hardened with a little tin, sounds like a single invention, but it arrived like a slow tide with many inlets.","Tin is rare, found in a handful of places such as Cornwall, the Ore Mountains of central Europe and Central Asia, so bronze tied distant regions together through trade.","The Near East and the Aegean were making copper alloys by around 3000 BCE.","Central Europe and Britain followed around 2200 BCE, China around 1900 BCE, and Southeast Asia perhaps a thousand years later.","Much of Africa south of the Sahara never had a Bronze Age and moved from stone to iron.","In the Andes, people hammered gold foil four thousand years ago but made bronze much later.","Australia, New Guinea and the Pacific islands had no metalworking of their own at all.","So the Stone Age did not end on one day.","It faded region by region, and in many places stone tools stayed in daily use for thousands of years more."],"pronounce":{"Amesbury":"Aimz-bree"},"body":[{"heading":"What bronze is, and why tin mattered","paragraphs":["Bronze is copper alloyed with roughly a tenth of tin. It is harder than copper, melts at a lower temperature and flows better into moulds. The first alloys, however, were mostly arsenical copper, made from ores rich in arsenic across the Near East and the Caucasus from the fifth and fourth millennia BCE; they were nearly as hard, but the fumes were poisonous and the arsenic content was hard to control.","Copper is widespread, but workable tin is found in only a few regions: Cornwall and Devon, Brittany, north-western Iberia, the Erzgebirge (Ore Mountains) on the Czech-German border, the mountains of Central Asia, and the tin belt of Southeast Asia. Bronze therefore required long-distance trade. Around 1900 BCE, Old Assyrian merchants recorded in thousands of clay letters how they carried tin and textiles by donkey caravan from Assur to Kanesh (Kültepe) in Anatolia, the tin itself coming from further east. At Kestel in the Taurus Mountains, Aslıhan Yener argued for tin mining in the third millennium BCE, a claim still debated. A 2022 isotope study suggested that tin ingots from the Uluburun shipwreck of about 1300 BCE came partly from Anatolia and partly from Central Asia."]},{"heading":"The Near East, Egypt, the Indus and the Aegean","paragraphs":["Archaeologists conventionally start the Early Bronze Age of Mesopotamia and the Levant around 3300–3000 BCE, of the Aegean around 3200 BCE and of the Indus region around 3300 BCE, with Egypt’s Early Dynastic period from about 3100 BCE. These dates mark broad changes, cities, writing and states, more than the first tin bronze. Arsenical copper dominated for centuries; tin bronze became common in Mesopotamia only in the mid third millennium BCE, as in the royal tombs of Ur around 2600–2500 BCE, and in Egypt copper remained the main working metal until the second millennium BCE. Indus metalworkers used copper, arsenical copper and some tin bronze alongside a large stone-blade industry."]},{"heading":"Europe: Beakers, daggers and Únětice","paragraphs":["In western and central Europe the centuries after 2800 BCE belong to the Bell Beaker phenomenon, named after its bell-shaped drinking cups, with archers’ wristguards, copper daggers and early gold. The Amesbury Archer, found by Wessex Archaeology in 2002 and dated to about 2380–2290 BCE, is the richest Beaker burial in Britain. In 2018 a study led by Iñigo Olalde and David Reich, analysing about 400 ancient genomes, found that people with Beaker-associated, largely steppe-related ancestry replaced about 90 percent of Britain’s gene pool within a few hundred years. Why remains debated; disease, including plague, is one suspect.","Tin bronze became normal in central Europe around 2200 BCE with the Únětice culture, known for its hoards and princely burial mounds such as Leubingen; the Nebra sky disc is usually attributed to its later phase, although a minority of researchers argue for a later date. In Scandinavia, which lacks copper and tin, Late Neolithic craftsmen made superb pressure-flaked flint daggers imitating metal ones, until imported bronze took over in the Nordic Bronze Age from about 1700 BCE."]},{"heading":"East and Southeast Asia","paragraphs":["In north-western China, copper and bronze knives, mirrors and ornaments appear in Qijia-culture sites of the late third and early second millennia BCE, alongside links with the steppe. Around 1900–1500 BCE, at Erlitou in the middle Yellow River valley, craftsmen began casting the first bronze ritual vessels in assembled clay piece-moulds, a technique quite different from Western lost-wax casting, near a palace compound that many see as the seat of China’s first state.","In Southeast Asia the date is disputed. Excavations at Ban Chiang in north-eastern Thailand in the 1970s produced early dates that suggested bronze by the fourth millennium BCE. Later work, especially Charles Higham’s radiocarbon dating of human bone at Ban Non Wat and Ban Chiang, places the start of bronze around 1000 BCE, while Joyce White and colleagues argue for about 2000 BCE. In Japan, bronze and iron arrived almost together in the Yayoi period, a few centuries after wet-rice farming reached the islands in the first millennium BCE."]},{"heading":"Africa: from stone to iron","paragraphs":["Egypt and Nubia, connected to the Mediterranean and the Near East, used copper and bronze. South of the Sahara, however, most regions moved directly from stone tools to iron in the first millennium BCE, without a Bronze Age. In central Nigeria, the Nok culture, famous for its terracotta sculptures, was smelting iron by the middle of the first millennium BCE, and iron working appears around the same time in the Great Lakes region of East Africa.","Some sites claim much earlier dates: Egaro and other sites in the Termit massif of Niger have produced radiocarbon dates of the second millennium BCE for iron working, and Oboui in the Central African Republic dates close to 2000 BCE. Most specialists treat these with caution because charcoal can be older than the furnace it lies in. Copper was mined and worked at Akjoujt in Mauritania in the first millennium BCE, and in the Aïr region of Niger. Across much of southern Africa, hunter-gatherers kept making Later Stone Age tools into recent centuries, alongside iron-using farmers."]},{"heading":"The Americas and Oceania","paragraphs":["In the Americas metallurgy developed independently and late. After the cold-hammered copper of the Great Lakes, the next steps came in the Andes: gold beads from Jiskairumoko in the Lake Titicaca basin, published by Mark Aldenderfer and colleagues in 2008, date to about 2000 BCE, and at Waywaka near Andahuaylas, Joel Grossman found gold foil and a goldworker’s toolkit dated to roughly the middle of the second millennium BCE. Arsenical bronze spread on the north coast of Peru in the first millennium CE, tin bronze in the southern Andes later, and metalworking reached West Mexico only around 600–800 CE. Everyday tools across the Americas stayed mostly stone, bone and wood until European contact.","Australia, New Guinea and the islands of the Pacific never developed metallurgy. Makassan sailors from Sulawesi brought iron to northern Australia from at least the 1700s, and Pacific Islanders eagerly traded for European iron in the eighteenth century. The absence of metal says nothing about intelligence: these societies built ocean-going canoes, complex farming systems and sophisticated technologies of stone, shell, fibre and fire."]},{"heading":"Stone that stayed, and a European yardstick","paragraphs":["Even in bronze-using Europe, stone kept working. At Grimes Graves in Norfolk, Neolithic miners sank more than 400 shafts, mostly around 2600–2300 BCE, using red-deer antler picks to reach the best flint, and the area was reused in the Bronze Age. At Krzemionki in Poland, inscribed by UNESCO in 2019, striped flint was mined from about 3900 to 1600 BCE. Flint sickles, arrowheads and scrapers remained common well into the second millennium BCE.","The three-age system of stone, bronze and iron was devised in the 1810s to 1830s by Christian Jürgensen Thomsen to order the Danish national collections, and it works well for Europe and the Near East. Critics have long noted that it does not fit most of Africa, the Americas or Oceania, and that it encouraged nineteenth-century writers to rank living peoples as “Stone Age” and therefore behind. Many regions now use their own sequences instead, and archaeologists treat “Stone Age” as a label for a technology, not for people."]}],"debates":[{"question":"Was Anatolia a source of Bronze Age tin?","views":[{"label":"Yes: Kestel and Göltepe","text":"Aslıhan Yener excavated mine galleries at Kestel and a processing settlement at Göltepe in the Taurus Mountains, dated to the third millennium BCE, and argued that they produced tin for Near Eastern bronze."},{"label":"Too little to matter","text":"Archaeometallurgists such as James Muhly countered that the ores are low in tin and that Old Assyrian texts show tin arriving from the east, so Kestel could not have been a major source."}],"status":"Mining at Kestel is accepted, but what was extracted and on what scale remains disputed. Tin isotope studies of ingots, including those from the Uluburun wreck, are now testing individual sources directly and suggest a mix of Anatolian and Central Asian tin in the Late Bronze Age."},{"question":"When did bronze reach Southeast Asia?","views":[{"label":"Early, around 2000 BCE or before","text":"Joyce White and colleagues, re-analysing Ban Chiang, argue for bronze by about 2000 BCE, which would allow a local or steppe-linked origin independent of China’s Shang dynasty."},{"label":"Late, around 1000 BCE","text":"Charles Higham, Thomas Higham and Amphan Kijngam, using radiocarbon dates on human bone from Ban Non Wat and Ban Chiang, place the first bronze around 1000 BCE and link it to exchange with southern China after bronze working was established there."}],"status":"The later chronology is favoured by most researchers, but the debate continues because the two teams dated different materials. More dates from well-stratified burials and lead-isotope sourcing of the metal are the way forward."},{"question":"Did Africans invent iron smelting independently?","views":[{"label":"Independent invention","text":"Researchers including Augustin Holl and contributors to the 2004 UNESCO volume edited by Hamady Bocoum point to early dates from Termit, Oboui and Nigeria, and to distinctive African furnace designs, as evidence that iron smelting was invented in sub-Saharan Africa."},{"label":"Spread from the north","text":"Sceptics such as Stanley Alpern argue that the earliest reliable dates are close to or after the spread of iron in the Mediterranean and Nubia, and that the oldest dates are on charcoal that may predate the furnaces."}],"status":"Open. Most agree that sub-Saharan Africa went straight from stone to iron and that African smelters developed a remarkable variety of furnaces. Deciding priority needs dates on short-lived material directly associated with smelting."},{"question":"Is the three-age system still useful?","views":[{"label":"A workable framework","text":"For Europe and western Asia, the sequence of stone, copper, bronze and iron still captures real changes in technology, trade and society, and it remains the shared vocabulary of museums and textbooks."},{"label":"A regional yardstick","text":"Critics note that it was built on Danish collections, implies a single ladder of progress and fits much of the world badly. Africa uses Earlier, Middle and Later Stone Age; the Americas use Paleoindian, Archaic and Formative; Japan uses Jōmon and Yayoi."}],"status":"Most archaeologists use the terms as convenient period labels for Europe and the Near East and prefer regional sequences elsewhere, while stressing that the labels describe tools, not people."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Bronze","value":"Copper with roughly 10 percent tin; arsenical copper came first"},{"label":"Near East and Aegean","value":"Early Bronze Age from about 3300–3000 BCE"},{"label":"Britain","value":"Beaker newcomers from about 2450 BCE; about 90 percent genetic turnover"},{"label":"China","value":"Bronze vessels at Erlitou, about 1900–1500 BCE"},{"label":"Sub-Saharan Africa","value":"Mostly from stone directly to iron, in the first millennium BCE"},{"label":"No metallurgy","value":"Australia, New Guinea and the Pacific islands before European contact"}],"siteIds":["amesbury-archer","grimes-graves","krzemionki","unetice","ur","kultepe-kanesh","erlitou","ban-chiang","nok","termit-egaro","akjoujt","waywaka"],"routes":[{"id":"assyrian-tin-route","label":"Old Assyrian tin caravans","start":3950,"end":3750,"certainty":"established","path":[[43.26,35.46],[40.2,36.9],[37.4,37.6],[35.63,38.85]]},{"id":"beakers-to-britain","label":"Beaker-associated migration to Britain","start":4500,"end":4300,"certainty":"established","path":[[6,51.5],[3.6,51.4],[1.4,51.3],[-1.76,51.17]]}],"themes":["metal","technology","migration","genetics","society","burial","settlement"],"sources":[{"cite":"Roberts, B. W., Thornton, C. P. and Pigott, V. C. (2009). Development of metallurgy in Eurasia. Antiquity 83, 1012–1022."},{"cite":"Yener, K. A. (2000). The Domestication of Metals: The Rise of Complex Metal Industries in Anatolia. Leiden: Brill."},{"cite":"Olalde, I. et al. (2018). The Beaker phenomenon and the genomic transformation of northwest Europe. Nature 555, 190–196."},{"cite":"Fitzpatrick, A. P. (2011). The Amesbury Archer and the Boscombe Bowmen: Bell Beaker Burials at Boscombe Down, Amesbury, Wiltshire. Salisbury: Wessex Archaeology."},{"cite":"Liu, L. and Chen, X. (2012). The Archaeology of China: From the Late Paleolithic to the Early Bronze Age. Cambridge University Press."},{"cite":"Higham, C., Higham, T. and Kijngam, A. (2011). Cutting a Gordian knot: the Bronze Age of Southeast Asia: origins, timing and impact. Antiquity 85, 583–598."},{"cite":"Bocoum, H. (ed.) (2004). The Origins of Iron Metallurgy in Africa: New Light on Its Antiquity. Paris: UNESCO Publishing."},{"cite":"Alpern, S. B. (2005). Did they or didn’t they invent it? Iron in sub-Saharan Africa. History in Africa 32, 41–94."},{"cite":"Aldenderfer, M. et al. (2008). Four-thousand-year-old gold artifacts from the Lake Titicaca basin, southern Peru. PNAS 105, 5002–5005."},{"cite":"Rowley-Conwy, P. (2007). From Genesis to Prehistory: The Archaeological Three Age System and its Contested Reception in Denmark, Britain, and Ireland. Oxford University Press."}]},{"id":"epilogue","order":43,"act":"metal","title":"What the Stone Age Left Us","subtitle":"Genes, crops, languages, inequality, a myth to unlearn and the questions still open","era":"The Stone Age and its legacy","start":3300000,"end":4000,"dateLabel":"3.3 million years ago to the present","regions":["africa","europe","asia","oceania","americas"],"focus":[-170,-50,180,72],"emblem":"hand","summary":"The Stone Age is not behind us: it lives on in our genomes, our crops and animals, the languages we speak, our diseases and our arguments about inequality, while new methods keep rewriting its story.","paragraphs":[["Strike a flake from a lump of volcanic glass and its edge is only a few millionths of a millimetre across, finer than a surgeon’s steel.","The skill that made it is at least 3.3 million years old, and people still practise it today.","For more than 99 percent of the time since the first stone tools, every human story was a Stone Age story."],["That long past is not behind us.","It is inside us.","Most people alive today carry a little Neanderthal DNA, and many in Asia and Oceania carry Denisovan DNA too, including a gene variant that helps Tibetans thrive in thin mountain air.","Our grain fields and herds, many of the languages we speak and some of the diseases we fear took shape in the last few thousand years of the Stone Age, as people settled, farmed and herded.","So did one of our oldest arguments: whether inequality grew step by step with farming, or whether our ancestors tried many ways of living together and sometimes kept would-be rulers in check."],["The hunched, grunting caveman of cartoons was invented in the nineteenth century, partly to rank living peoples as primitive.","The real people of the Stone Age crossed seas, painted caves, cared for the sick and injured, and shaped whole landscapes with fire.","Their descendants include everyone alive today."],["The story is far from finished.","A skull found in Harbin nearly a century ago has only just been identified, through its proteins and DNA, as a Denisovan.","Cave floors yield DNA without bones, lasers see through forest canopies, and ancient proteins reach back millions of years.","The next discovery may already be waiting in a museum drawer, or a few centimetres under someone’s feet."]],"sentences":["Strike a flake from a lump of volcanic glass and its edge is only a few millionths of a millimetre across, finer than a surgeon’s steel.","The skill that made it is at least 3.3 million years old, and people still practise it today.","For more than 99 percent of the time since the first stone tools, every human story was a Stone Age story.","That long past is not behind us.","It is inside us.","Most people alive today carry a little Neanderthal DNA, and many in Asia and Oceania carry Denisovan DNA too, including a gene variant that helps Tibetans thrive in thin mountain air.","Our grain fields and herds, many of the languages we speak and some of the diseases we fear took shape in the last few thousand years of the Stone Age, as people settled, farmed and herded.","So did one of our oldest arguments: whether inequality grew step by step with farming, or whether our ancestors tried many ways of living together and sometimes kept would-be rulers in check.","The hunched, grunting caveman of cartoons was invented in the nineteenth century, partly to rank living peoples as primitive.","The real people of the Stone Age crossed seas, painted caves, cared for the sick and injured, and shaped whole landscapes with fire.","Their descendants include everyone alive today.","The story is far from finished.","A skull found in Harbin nearly a century ago has only just been identified, through its proteins and DNA, as a Denisovan.","Cave floors yield DNA without bones, lasers see through forest canopies, and ancient proteins reach back millions of years.","The next discovery may already be waiting in a museum drawer, or a few centimetres under someone’s feet."],"pronounce":{"Denisovan":"Deh-nee-so-van","Harbin":"Har-bin"},"body":[{"heading":"The longest chapter","paragraphs":["The oldest known stone tools, from Lomekwi 3 in Kenya, are about 3.3 million years old. Metal came into everyday use in parts of western Eurasia a little over 5,000 years ago, and in many regions much later or not at all before recent centuries. Measured against the whole span, the age of metals is a thin top layer: more than 99 percent of the time since the first known tools falls within the Stone Age, and it includes the evolution of our genus, the origin of our species, the settlement of every continent except Antarctica, the first art and music, and the independent invention of farming in several parts of the world.","As the previous chapter showed, the Stone Age did not end at one moment. That is its first lesson: human history is regional and plural, and the three-age labels describe tools, not the worth of the people who used them."]},{"heading":"Ancient relatives in our genomes","paragraphs":["Since the first Neanderthal genome was published by Richard Green, Svante Pääbo and colleagues in 2010, we have known that people outside Africa carry roughly 1–2 percent Neanderthal DNA, and later work found traces in African populations too, brought back by later migrations. Two 2024 studies placed the main period of mixing at around 47,000 years ago. Denisovan ancestry reaches several percent in Papuans, Aboriginal Australians and some Philippine groups, the highest among the Ayta Magbukon.","Some of these inherited variants were useful. A Denisovan version of the EPAS1 gene helps Tibetans live at high altitude (Emilia Huerta-Sánchez and colleagues, 2014), and Neanderthal variants influence immunity, skin and hair. Others carry risks: in 2020 Hugo Zeberg and Svante Pääbo showed that the main genetic risk factor for severe Covid-19 on chromosome 3 was inherited from Neanderthals, while another Neanderthal segment offers some protection. In 2025 proteins and DNA finally tied the Harbin skull and the Penghu jaw from Taiwan to the Denisovans, giving our long-lost relatives a face."]},{"heading":"Bodies reshaped by farming and herding","paragraphs":["Farming changed not only diets but human bodies. Most adults in the world cannot digest the milk sugar lactose; the variants that keep the lactase enzyme working evolved independently in Europe, East Africa and Arabia. In 2022 Richard Evershed, Mark Thomas and colleagues combined animal-fat residues from nearly 7,000 pottery sherds with the ancient DNA of more than 1,700 people and found that Europeans used milk for thousands of years before lactase persistence was common. The variant rose mainly in the Bronze and Iron Ages, and the best explanation was not steady nutritional benefit but famine and disease, when diarrhoea from undigested milk could kill.","Pigmentation changed too. European hunter-gatherers such as Cheddar Man probably had dark skin and, often, light eyes; genetic variants for lighter skin arrived with Anatolian farmers and steppe herders and kept increasing into historical times. A 2024 Nature study led by William Barrie found that gene variants raising the risk of multiple sclerosis today rose in frequency among steppe herders, probably because they helped fight infections from livestock. Two 2024 studies of the salivary amylase genes showed that copies multiplied long ago and became more common after farming spread."]},{"heading":"Crops, animals, languages and germs","paragraphs":["Most of the world’s staple foods were domesticated by Stone Age people: wheat, barley, rice, millets, maize, potatoes, sorghum, manioc, beans and bananas, and cattle, sheep, goats, pigs and llamas. Rice, wheat and maize alone supply about half of humanity’s food energy. The dog, our oldest companion, joined us earlier still, before farming.","Many of the world’s largest language families spread with farmers or herders: Austronesian from Taiwan, Bantu from west-central Africa, Sino-Tibetan with northern Chinese millet farmers, and Indo-European, most branches of which spread with steppe herders. Crowded settlements and close contact with animals also created crowd diseases. Ancient pathogen DNA has found plague in Neolithic Sweden and on the steppe, hepatitis B in Bronze Age people, and a split between measles and the cattle disease rinderpest dated by Ariane Düx and colleagues (2020) to around the sixth century BCE."]},{"heading":"Who gets to rule? Inequality before history","paragraphs":["Stone Age societies were not all equal. At Sungir in Russia, about 34,000 years ago, two children were buried with thousands of ivory beads; at Varna, around 4500 BCE, a few graves held most of the gold. But large settlements such as Çatalhöyük and the Trypillia megasites show little sign of rulers. In The Creation of Inequality (2012) Kent Flannery and Joyce Marcus traced how particular social logics, such as ranking kin groups by descent, allowed hereditary inequality to take hold. In The Dawn of Everything (2021) David Graeber and David Wengrow argued that prehistoric people consciously experimented with many political arrangements, including seasonal ones, and that there was no single path from bands to states.","Numbers help. In 2017 Timothy Kohler and colleagues measured house sizes across the world as a proxy for wealth and found that inequality rose after farming, especially in the Old World, where draught animals and ploughs let some households farm far more land than others."]},{"heading":"The caveman myth","paragraphs":["The caveman, hunched, hairy and dim, is a Victorian invention. In Pre-historic Times (1865), John Lubbock, who coined the words Palaeolithic and Neolithic, explained ancient Europeans through comparisons with “modern savages”. Between 1911 and 1913 Marcellin Boule reconstructed the Neanderthal from La Chapelle-aux-Saints as a stooped, bent-kneed brute; in 1957 William Straus and A. J. E. Cave showed that the man had severe arthritis and that Neanderthals stood as upright as we do.","The same thinking justified colonial rule. Aboriginal Australians, Tasmanians, the San and others were labelled “Stone Age peoples” or “living fossils”, as if they had no history. In fact, living foragers such as the Hadza, the Ju/’hoansi and the BaYaka have long histories of change, trade and contact with farmers and states, as the “Kalahari debate” sparked by Edwin Wilmsen in 1989 made clear. They are contemporary peoples, not relics."]},{"heading":"Skills that survive","paragraphs":["Stone Age skills never vanished. Twentieth-century researchers such as François Bordes in France and Don Crabtree in the United States taught themselves to knap flint and obsidian, founding experimental archaeology and a worldwide hobby. An obsidian edge can be far finer than surgical steel, and Crabtree reportedly had obsidian blades he made used in his own heart surgery in the 1970s. Cordage, basketry, tanning, fermentation, bread and fire-making are all Stone Age inventions still in daily use. In Australia, Aboriginal cultural burning, practised for thousands of years, is now part of modern fire management."]},{"heading":"What may change next","paragraphs":["The next decade will bring surprises. Sediment DNA can now reveal who lived in a cave without a single bone, as at Denisova and Baishiya Karst Cave, and environmental DNA from Greenland has reached back two million years. Ancient proteins survive far longer than DNA: in 2025 enamel proteins were read from Paranthropus teeth about two million years old. Lidar has revealed a network of settlements in Ecuador’s Upano Valley, published in 2024, and hidden earthworks across Amazonia.","Big questions remain open: when people first reached the Americas (the White Sands footprints suggest 23,000–21,000 years ago, and the debate continues), when they reached Sahul (about 65,000 or about 50,000 years ago), what Denisovans looked like from head to toe, and what happened in the parts of Africa and Asia where ancient DNA is still scarce. The Stone Age is still being excavated, one layer at a time."]}],"debates":[{"question":"How did social inequality arise?","views":[{"label":"A path with identifiable steps","text":"Kent Flannery and Joyce Marcus (2012) argue that inequality emerged through specific, repeated social processes, such as the rise of ranked clans and hereditary leadership, which archaeology can trace from village societies to chiefdoms and kingdoms."},{"label":"Many experiments, no single path","text":"David Graeber and David Wengrow (2021) argue that prehistoric people consciously tried egalitarian, seasonal and hierarchical arrangements, that large settlements could exist without rulers, and that the question is how we got stuck in one form."},{"label":"Material drivers","text":"Timothy Kohler, Michael Smith and colleagues use measures such as house-size inequality to show that wealth gaps grew with farming and especially with ploughs and draught animals, which made land and livestock inheritable wealth."}],"status":"Critics of Graeber and Wengrow say they overstate the evidence for deliberate choice; critics of evolutionary models say they flatten variety. Most archaeologists now accept both regularities and real diversity, and are testing them with larger datasets."},{"question":"Did farmers spread the world’s great language families?","views":[{"label":"Farming and language dispersal","text":"Peter Bellwood and Colin Renfrew argue that growing farming populations carried their languages with them, explaining Austronesian, Bantu, Sino-Tibetan and, in Renfrew’s version, Indo-European."},{"label":"Many routes to spread","text":"Others stress that languages also spread by shift, with people adopting a new language without being replaced, and through herders or elites. Indo-European spread largely with steppe herders, and Pama-Nyungan spread across Australia without farming."}],"status":"Ancient DNA confirms that several great families spread with moving populations, but not all, and the correlation between genes and language is loose. Each family has to be judged on its own evidence."},{"question":"Why did lactase persistence spread?","views":[{"label":"Steady nutritional advantage","text":"The classic view holds that milk drinkers gained calories, protein, calcium and, in northern latitudes, help with vitamin D, so the variant spread wherever dairying was practised."},{"label":"Selection in crises","text":"Richard Evershed, Mark Thomas and colleagues (2022) found that the amount of milk use did not predict when the variant rose. They argue that it spread mainly during famines and epidemics, when lactose intolerance could turn deadly."}],"status":"The crisis model is now widely discussed but still being tested, including with ancient DNA from Africa and Asia, where lactase persistence evolved separately."},{"question":"Can living foragers tell us about the Stone Age?","views":[{"label":"Careful analogy","text":"Since the 1966 Man the Hunter conference, researchers have studied peoples such as the Hadza and Ju/’hoansi to understand how foragers share food, move, raise children and use energy, and use those findings to frame hypotheses about the past."},{"label":"Contemporary peoples with their own histories","text":"Critics, including Edwin Wilmsen in the Kalahari debate, show that today’s foragers have been shaped by centuries of contact with herders, farmers, states and markets, and warn that treating them as windows on the past repeats colonial ideas."}],"status":"Ethnography remains a valuable source of possibilities, never a direct picture of prehistory. Research is increasingly carried out in partnership with the communities concerned."}],"keyFacts":[{"label":"Length of the Stone Age","value":"About 3.3 million years, more than 99 percent of the time since the first known stone tools"},{"label":"Neanderthal DNA","value":"Roughly 1–2 percent in people outside Africa"},{"label":"Denisovan DNA","value":"Several percent in Papuans, Aboriginal Australians and some Philippine groups"},{"label":"Staple crops","value":"Rice, wheat and maize, all domesticated in the Stone Age, supply about half of human food energy"},{"label":"Lactase persistence","value":"Rose mainly in the Bronze and Iron Ages in Europe"},{"label":"Denisovans identified","value":"Harbin skull and Penghu jaw tied to Denisovans in 2025"}],"siteIds":["lomekwi-3","denisova-cave","baishiya-karst-cave","harbin","la-chapelle-aux-saints","sungir","madjedbebe","white-sands","gobekli-tepe","varna-necropolis"],"routes":[],"themes":["genetics","farming","food","animals","language","society","technology","species","migration"],"sources":[{"cite":"Green, R. E. et al. (2010). A draft sequence of the Neandertal genome. Science 328, 710–722."},{"cite":"Huerta-Sánchez, E. et al. (2014). Altitude adaptation in Tibetans caused by introgression of Denisovan-like DNA. Nature 512, 194–197."},{"cite":"Zeberg, H. and Pääbo, S. (2020). The major genetic risk factor for severe Covid-19 is inherited from Neanderthals. Nature 587, 610–612."},{"cite":"Evershed, R. P. et al. (2022). Dairying, diseases and the evolution of lactase persistence in Europe. Nature 608, 336–345."},{"cite":"Barrie, W. et al. (2024). Elevated genetic risk for multiple sclerosis emerged in steppe pastoralist populations. Nature 625, 321–328."},{"cite":"Bellwood, P. and Renfrew, C. (eds) (2002). Examining the Farming/Language Dispersal Hypothesis. Cambridge: McDonald Institute for Archaeological Research."},{"cite":"Flannery, K. and Marcus, J. (2012). The Creation of Inequality: How Our Prehistoric Ancestors Set the Stage for Monarchy, Slavery, and Empire. Harvard University Press."},{"cite":"Graeber, D. and Wengrow, D. (2021). The Dawn of Everything: A New History of Humanity. London: Allen Lane."},{"cite":"Kohler, T. A. et al. (2017). Greater post-Neolithic wealth disparities in Eurasia than in North America and Mesoamerica. Nature 551, 619–622."},{"cite":"Lubbock, J. (1865). Pre-historic Times, as Illustrated by Ancient Remains, and the Manners and Customs of Modern Savages. London: Williams and Norgate."}]}],"sites":[{"id":"hammerschmiede","name":"Hammerschmiede","country":"Germany","region":"europe","lat":47.92,"lon":10.59,"start":11620000,"end":11620000,"dateLabel":"about 11.6 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Bavarian clay pit that yielded Danuvius guggenmosi, an ape proposed in 2019 to show a way of moving that could have preceded bipedalism. The interpretation is contested.","chapters":["first-hominins"]},{"id":"toros-menalla","name":"Toros-Menalla (TM 266)","country":"Chad","region":"africa","lat":16.25,"lon":17.5,"start":7200000,"end":6800000,"dateLabel":"about 7.2–6.8 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Desert locality where the cranium of Sahelanthropus tchadensis (Toumaï) was found in 2001, together with a disputed femur and two ulnae. It lies about 2,500 kilometres west of the Rift Valley.","chapters":["first-hominins"]},{"id":"pyrgos-vassilissis","name":"Pyrgos Vassilissis","country":"Greece","region":"europe","lat":38.04,"lon":23.7,"start":7200000,"end":7200000,"dateLabel":"about 7.2 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Find spot, near Athens, of the Graecopithecus lower jaw, which a 2017 study argued was a hominin. Most specialists consider the evidence too thin.","chapters":["first-hominins"]},{"id":"kapsomin","name":"Kapsomin, Lukeino Formation","country":"Kenya","region":"africa","lat":0.8,"lon":35.8,"start":6000000,"end":5700000,"dateLabel":"about 6 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Tugen Hills locality that yielded Orrorin tugenensis in 2000, including three partial femora that are the oldest proposed evidence of hominin bipedalism from the leg.","chapters":["first-hominins"]},{"id":"middle-awash","name":"Middle Awash (western margin)","country":"Ethiopia","region":"africa","lat":10.3,"lon":40.4,"start":5800000,"end":5200000,"dateLabel":"5.8–5.2 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Localities that produced Ardipithecus kadabba, including a toe bone whose upward-angled joint suggests toe-off during upright walking.","chapters":["first-hominins"]},{"id":"gona","name":"Gona","country":"Ethiopia","region":"africa","lat":11.1,"lon":40.35,"start":4500000,"end":2500000,"dateLabel":"4.5–4.3 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Afar research area that produced further Ardipithecus ramidus fossils, and later some of the oldest Oldowan stone tools.","chapters":["first-hominins","first-tools"]},{"id":"aramis","name":"Aramis","country":"Ethiopia","region":"africa","lat":10.5,"lon":40.5,"start":4400000,"end":4400000,"dateLabel":"about 4.4 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Middle Awash site of Ardipithecus ramidus and the partial skeleton Ardi, sandwiched between volcanic ashes dated to 4.4 million years. Its habitat, woodland or tree savanna, is debated.","chapters":["first-hominins"]},{"id":"kanapoi","name":"Kanapoi","country":"Kenya","region":"africa","lat":2.32,"lon":36.07,"start":4200000,"end":4100000,"dateLabel":"4.2–4.1 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Type site of Australopithecus anamensis, the oldest australopith, whose shin bone shows a knee adapted to upright walking.","chapters":["australopiths"]},{"id":"woranso-mille","name":"Woranso-Mille","country":"Ethiopia","region":"africa","lat":11.45,"lon":40.5,"start":3800000,"end":3300000,"dateLabel":"3.8–3.3 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Afar research area that produced the 3.8-million-year-old MRD cranium of A. anamensis, the Burtele foot with its grasping big toe, and A. deyiremeda.","chapters":["australopiths"]},{"id":"sterkfontein","name":"Sterkfontein","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-26.02,"lon":27.73,"start":3700000,"end":2000000,"dateLabel":"3.7–2 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Cave system that yielded Mrs Ples, hundreds of A. africanus fossils and the Little Foot skeleton, whose ages are disputed between about 3.7 and 2 million years.","chapters":["australopiths"]},{"id":"laetoli","name":"Laetoli","country":"Tanzania","region":"africa","lat":-3.22,"lon":35.19,"start":3660000,"end":3660000,"dateLabel":"about 3.66 million years ago","type":"footprints","summary":"Volcanic ash that preserved the oldest hominin footprints: the Site G trail of A. afarensis and the Site A prints of a second, cross-stepping biped.","chapters":["australopiths"]},{"id":"koro-toro","name":"Koro Toro","country":"Chad","region":"africa","lat":16.08,"lon":18.5,"start":3500000,"end":3500000,"dateLabel":"about 3.5 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Find spot of the jaw of Australopithecus bahrelghazali (Abel), the first australopith found west of the Rift Valley.","chapters":["australopiths"]},{"id":"hadar","name":"Hadar","country":"Ethiopia","region":"africa","lat":11.1,"lon":40.58,"start":3400000,"end":2330000,"dateLabel":"3.4–2.9 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Where Lucy (AL 288-1) was found in 1974 and the ‘First Family’ of at least 13 A. afarensis individuals at AL 333 in 1975.","chapters":["australopiths","genus-homo"]},{"id":"dikika","name":"Dikika","country":"Ethiopia","region":"africa","lat":11.05,"lon":40.63,"start":3390000,"end":3300000,"dateLabel":"about 3.3 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Site of Selam, the skeleton of an A. afarensis child found in 2000, whose shoulder blades and braincase illuminate climbing and brain growth.","chapters":["australopiths","first-tools"]},{"id":"lomekwi-3","name":"Lomekwi 3","country":"Kenya","region":"africa","lat":3.91,"lon":35.87,"start":3300000,"end":3300000,"dateLabel":"3.3 million years ago","type":"tools","summary":"The oldest known knapped stone tools, found in 2011 west of Lake Turkana and dated by argon–argon and palaeomagnetism. They mark the conventional beginning of the Stone Age.","chapters":["the-longest-age","first-tools","epilogue"]},{"id":"nyayanga","name":"Nyayanga","country":"Kenya","region":"africa","lat":-0.4,"lon":34.4,"start":3000000,"end":2600000,"dateLabel":"3.0–2.6 million years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Homa Peninsula site with 330 Oldowan artefacts, butchered hippopotamus bones and two Paranthropus molars, published in 2023.","chapters":["first-tools"]},{"id":"taung","name":"Taung (Buxton Limeworks)","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-27.62,"lon":24.64,"start":2800000,"end":2600000,"dateLabel":"roughly 2.8–2.6 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Quarry where the Taung Child was found in 1924, the type specimen of Australopithecus africanus described by Raymond Dart. Its exact age is poorly constrained.","chapters":["australopiths"]},{"id":"ledi-geraru","name":"Ledi-Geraru (Bokol Dora 1)","country":"Ethiopia","region":"africa","lat":11.35,"lon":40.8,"start":2800000,"end":2580000,"dateLabel":"2.8–2.6 million years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Afar research area that produced LD 350-1, the oldest jaw of Homo, and in 2025 teeth of both early Homo and an unnamed Australopithecus.","chapters":["first-tools","genus-homo"]},{"id":"bouri","name":"Bouri","country":"Ethiopia","region":"africa","lat":10.3,"lon":40.53,"start":2500000,"end":1000000,"dateLabel":"about 2.5 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Middle Awash site of Australopithecus garhi, found near antelope bones with cut marks and marrow-extraction fractures.","chapters":["first-tools","erectus-acheulean"]},{"id":"uraha","name":"Uraha","country":"Malawi","region":"africa","lat":-10.02,"lon":33.92,"start":2500000,"end":2300000,"dateLabel":"roughly 2.5–2.3 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Find spot of a lower jaw attributed to Homo rudolfensis, extending early Homo to the southern end of the East African Rift.","chapters":["genus-homo"]},{"id":"ain-boucherit","name":"Ain Boucherit","country":"Algeria","region":"africa","lat":36.24,"lon":5.62,"start":2400000,"end":1900000,"dateLabel":"about 2.4–1.9 million years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Oldowan tools and cut-marked bones in northeastern Algeria, reported in 2018; the older date would make it one of the earliest toolmaking sites anywhere.","chapters":["first-tools"]},{"id":"shangchen","name":"Shangchen","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":34.22,"lon":109.49,"start":2120000,"end":1260000,"dateLabel":"2.12–1.26 million years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Ninety-six stone artefacts in 17 loess and soil layers on the Chinese Loess Plateau, dated by palaeomagnetism. The oldest are the earliest evidence of hominins outside Africa, older than any Homo erectus fossil in Eurasia.","chapters":["out-of-africa-first"]},{"id":"drimolen","name":"Drimolen","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-25.97,"lon":27.76,"start":2040000,"end":1950000,"dateLabel":"2.04–1.95 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Cave that yielded DNH 134, the oldest securely dated Homo erectus, alongside Paranthropus robustus, showing both lived together in southern Africa.","chapters":["erectus-acheulean"]},{"id":"swartkrans","name":"Swartkrans","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-26.01,"lon":27.72,"start":2000000,"end":1000000,"dateLabel":"about 2 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Cave in the Cradle of Humankind. In 2025 enamel proteins from four Paranthropus robustus teeth about 2 million years old revealed each individual’s sex, the oldest hominin protein sequences so far.","chapters":["the-longest-age","australopiths","genus-homo","erectus-acheulean","fire-and-wood"]},{"id":"kanjera-south","name":"Kanjera South","country":"Kenya","region":"africa","lat":-0.34,"lon":34.53,"start":2000000,"end":2000000,"dateLabel":"about 2 million years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Oldowan site where hominins carried stone from more than 10 kilometres away and repeatedly butchered antelopes, early evidence of regular meat eating.","chapters":["first-tools"]},{"id":"melka-kunture","name":"Melka Kunture","country":"Ethiopia","region":"africa","lat":8.7,"lon":38.59,"start":2000000,"end":1200000,"dateLabel":"about 2–1.2 million years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Highland site complex at about 2,000 metres where Homo erectus made Oldowan and early Acheulean tools, and later obsidian handaxes at Simbiro III.","chapters":["erectus-acheulean"]},{"id":"malapa","name":"Malapa","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-25.89,"lon":27.8,"start":1980000,"end":1980000,"dateLabel":"about 1.98 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Collapsed cave where Australopithecus sediba was discovered in 2008: two partial skeletons with a mix of australopith and Homo-like traits.","chapters":["australopiths"]},{"id":"koobi-fora","name":"Koobi Fora","country":"Kenya","region":"africa","lat":3.95,"lon":36.25,"start":1950000,"end":1500000,"dateLabel":"about 1.5 million years ago","type":"footprints","summary":"East Turkana area where footprints of Paranthropus and Homo erectus share one lakeshore surface, and where the first definite Paranthropus boisei hand bones were found.","chapters":["australopiths","genus-homo","erectus-acheulean"]},{"id":"olduvai-gorge","name":"Olduvai Gorge","country":"Tanzania","region":"africa","lat":-2.99,"lon":35.35,"start":1900000,"end":1000000,"dateLabel":"about 1.8 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Where Mary Leakey found the Paranthropus boisei cranium OH 5, ‘Nutcracker Man’, in 1959; its potassium–argon date transformed the timescale of human evolution.","chapters":["australopiths","first-tools","genus-homo","erectus-acheulean"]},{"id":"ubeidiya","name":"ʿUbeidiya","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":32.69,"lon":35.56,"start":1900000,"end":1200000,"dateLabel":"at least 1.9 million years (2026 study); earlier estimate 1.6–1.2 million","type":"tools","summary":"Acheulean handaxes, picks and choppers with African and Eurasian fauna in the Jordan Valley. A 2026 study using three dating methods argued it is at least 1.9 million years old, which would make it one of the oldest sites outside Africa.","chapters":["out-of-africa-first"]},{"id":"dmanisi","name":"Dmanisi","country":"Georgia","region":"asia","lat":41.34,"lon":44.34,"start":1850000,"end":1770000,"dateLabel":"1.85–1.77 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Five skulls and many other bones of small-brained early Homo, with Oldowan tools, beneath a medieval town. They include Skull 5, the most complete early Homo skull, and a nearly toothless elderly individual.","chapters":["out-of-africa-first"]},{"id":"flk-zinj","name":"FLK Zinj, Olduvai Gorge","country":"Tanzania","region":"africa","lat":-2.99,"lon":35.34,"start":1840000,"end":1840000,"dateLabel":"about 1.84 million years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Dense concentration of stone tools and butchered antelope bones, the classic test case in the debate over early hunting versus scavenging.","chapters":["genus-homo"]},{"id":"kokiselei","name":"Kokiselei 4","country":"Kenya","region":"africa","lat":4.18,"lon":35.87,"start":1760000,"end":1760000,"dateLabel":"about 1.76 million years ago","type":"tools","summary":"West Turkana site with the oldest securely dated Acheulean handaxes and picks, found alongside Oldowan tools.","chapters":["erectus-acheulean"]},{"id":"konso","name":"Konso","country":"Ethiopia","region":"africa","lat":5.35,"lon":37.4,"start":1750000,"end":850000,"dateLabel":"1.75 million–850,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Sequence spanning the earliest Acheulean at 1.75 million years to refined handaxes by 850,000 years, including a 1.4-million-year-old handaxe made of hippo bone.","chapters":["erectus-acheulean"]},{"id":"majuangou","name":"Majuangou, Nihewan Basin","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":40.22,"lon":114.66,"start":1660000,"end":1320000,"dateLabel":"about 1.66–1.32 million years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Stone tools and butchered bones in lake-margin sediments at about 40 degrees north, showing that hominins coped with cold northern winters by about 1.66 million years ago. A 2024 burial-dating study broadly confirmed the age.","chapters":["out-of-africa-first"]},{"id":"gongwangling","name":"Gongwangling (Lantian)","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":34.18,"lon":109.49,"start":1630000,"end":1630000,"dateLabel":"about 1.63 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"A thick-walled, small-brained Homo erectus cranium found in 1964, redated in 2015 by palaeomagnetism to about 1.63 million years, the oldest well-dated hominin fossil in China.","chapters":["out-of-africa-first"]},{"id":"ileret","name":"Ileret","country":"Kenya","region":"africa","lat":4.3,"lon":36.27,"start":1550000,"end":1440000,"dateLabel":"1.55–1.44 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Fossils showing that Homo habilis (1.44 million years) and Homo erectus (1.55 million years) lived in the same basin for a long time.","chapters":["genus-homo","erectus-acheulean"]},{"id":"nariokotome","name":"Nariokotome III","country":"Kenya","region":"africa","lat":4.13,"lon":35.88,"start":1500000,"end":1500000,"dateLabel":"about 1.5 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Find spot of the Turkana Boy (KNM-WT 15000), the most complete early Homo erectus skeleton, discovered in 1984.","chapters":["erectus-acheulean"]},{"id":"attirampakkam","name":"Attirampakkam","country":"India","region":"asia","lat":13.23,"lon":79.89,"start":1500000,"end":172000,"dateLabel":"Acheulean levels from about 1.5 million years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Thousands of Acheulean handaxes and cleavers near Chennai, dated directly by cosmogenic nuclides to about 1.5 million years, the earliest known Acheulean in South Asia.","chapters":["out-of-africa-first","levallois-msa"]},{"id":"koobi-fora-fxjj20","name":"FxJj 20, Koobi Fora","country":"Kenya","region":"africa","lat":3.88,"lon":36.35,"start":1500000,"end":1500000,"dateLabel":"about 1.5 million years ago","type":"fire","summary":"Reddened, magnetically altered patches of sediment and burnt bone among stone tools, the oldest widely cited evidence of hominin fire use, though natural fire cannot be fully excluded.","chapters":["fire-and-wood"]},{"id":"calio","name":"Calio","country":"Indonesia","region":"oceania","lat":-4.4,"lon":119.95,"start":1480000,"end":1040000,"dateLabel":"about 1.48–1.04 million years ago","type":"tools","summary":"A handful of stone flakes in the Walanae Basin of southern Sulawesi, published in 2025 and at least 1.04 million years old, the oldest known evidence of hominins in Wallacea.","chapters":["island-hominins"]},{"id":"korolevo","name":"Korolevo","country":"Ukraine","region":"europe","lat":48.15,"lon":23.13,"start":1420000,"end":1420000,"dateLabel":"about 1.42 million years ago","type":"tools","summary":"A deep loess sequence in Transcarpathia whose lowest artefact layer was dated in 2024 by cosmogenic nuclides to about 1.42 million years, the oldest securely dated evidence of hominins in Europe.","chapters":["first-europeans"]},{"id":"barranco-leon","name":"Barranco León (Orce)","country":"Spain","region":"europe","lat":37.71,"lon":-2.49,"start":1400000,"end":1400000,"dateLabel":"about 1.4 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"A hominin milk molar with Oldowan tools in an ancient lake basin in Andalusia, among the oldest human fossils in western Europe.","chapters":["first-europeans"]},{"id":"sima-del-elefante","name":"Sima del Elefante (Atapuerca)","country":"Spain","region":"europe","lat":42.35,"lon":-3.52,"start":1400000,"end":1100000,"dateLabel":"1.4–1.1 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Cave filling with a hominin jaw fragment (ATE9-1) and, deeper down, the partial face ATE7-1 (‘Pink’), published in 2025 and assigned to Homo affinis erectus.","chapters":["first-europeans"]},{"id":"sangiran","name":"Sangiran","country":"Indonesia","region":"asia","lat":-7.46,"lon":110.84,"start":1300000,"end":700000,"dateLabel":"about 1.3 million–700,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"An eroded dome in Central Java and the richest Homo erectus locality in Asia. Zircon dating in 2020 placed the first hominins there at no more than 1.5 and probably about 1.3 million years ago.","chapters":["out-of-africa-first"]},{"id":"olorgesailie","name":"Olorgesailie","country":"Kenya","region":"africa","lat":-1.58,"lon":36.45,"start":1200000,"end":300000,"dateLabel":"about 320,000–305,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"A basin where Middle Stone Age tools, obsidian carried from far away and red pigments replace handaxes by about 320,000 years ago, one of the signs of a continent-wide change.","chapters":["erectus-acheulean","levallois-msa","origin-of-sapiens","catastrophes"]},{"id":"fuente-nueva-3","name":"Fuente Nueva 3 (Orce)","country":"Spain","region":"europe","lat":37.72,"lon":-2.47,"start":1200000,"end":1200000,"dateLabel":"about 1.2 million years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Stone tools around an extinct mammoth carcass alongside giant hyena dung, read as evidence of hominins competing with carnivores for meat.","chapters":["first-europeans"]},{"id":"wolo-sege","name":"Wolo Sege","country":"Indonesia","region":"oceania","lat":-8.66,"lon":121.08,"start":1020000,"end":1020000,"dateLabel":"about 1.02 million years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Stone tools sealed beneath volcanic ash in the Soa Basin of Flores, argon-dated to about 1.02 million years, the oldest evidence of hominins beyond the Wallace Line on Flores.","chapters":["island-hominins"]},{"id":"wonderwerk-cave","name":"Wonderwerk Cave","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-27.85,"lon":23.55,"start":1000000,"end":1000000,"dateLabel":"about 1 million years ago","type":"fire","summary":"Ashed plants and burnt bone burned in place about 30 metres inside the cave in Acheulean layers, the oldest secure evidence of fire inside a cave.","chapters":["erectus-acheulean","fire-and-wood"]},{"id":"barranc-de-la-boella","name":"Barranc de la Boella","country":"Spain","region":"europe","lat":41.12,"lon":1.17,"start":1000000,"end":780000,"dateLabel":"about 1 million–780,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Large cutting tools with butchered mammoth remains near Tarragona, among the earliest Acheulean evidence in Europe.","chapters":["first-europeans"]},{"id":"evron-quarry","name":"Evron Quarry","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":32.99,"lon":35.1,"start":1000000,"end":800000,"dateLabel":"about 1 million–800,000 years ago","type":"fire","summary":"Open-air site where an artificial-intelligence model trained on spectroscopic data detected flints and tusk heated to several hundred degrees, invisible to the eye.","chapters":["fire-and-wood"]},{"id":"yunxian","name":"Yunxian","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":32.82,"lon":110.62,"start":1000000,"end":1000000,"dateLabel":"about 1 million years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Two crushed crania on a Han River terrace; a 2025 reconstruction placed one near the root of the Asian lineage that includes Harbin and the Denisovans.","chapters":["middle-pleistocene"]},{"id":"canteen-kopje","name":"Canteen Kopje","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-28.53,"lon":24.53,"start":1000000,"end":1000000,"dateLabel":"about 1 million years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Acheulean site on the Vaal River with Victoria West cores, prepared to yield single large flakes, an early precursor of Levallois.","chapters":["levallois-msa"]},{"id":"gran-dolina","name":"Gran Dolina TD6 (Atapuerca)","country":"Spain","region":"europe","lat":42.35,"lon":-3.52,"start":950000,"end":780000,"dateLabel":"about 850,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Type site of Homo antecessor, with a modern-looking juvenile face and the oldest well-documented cannibalism. Enamel proteins placed the species as a sister lineage of later humans.","chapters":["first-europeans"]},{"id":"happisburgh","name":"Happisburgh","country":"United Kingdom","region":"europe","lat":52.82,"lon":1.53,"start":950000,"end":780000,"dateLabel":"more than 780,000 years ago, perhaps about 900,000","type":"footprints","summary":"Flint tools and, exposed briefly in 2013, footprints of at least five adults and children on an estuary, the oldest known hominin footprints outside Africa.","chapters":["first-europeans"]},{"id":"bose-basin","name":"Bose (Baise) Basin","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":23.9,"lon":106.62,"start":803000,"end":803000,"dateLabel":"about 803,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Acheulean-like handaxes found with meteorite-impact tektites dated by argon to about 803,000 years, a key challenge to the idea of a handaxe-free East Asia.","chapters":["out-of-africa-first"]},{"id":"zhoukoudian","name":"Zhoukoudian Locality 1","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":39.69,"lon":115.93,"start":780000,"end":400000,"dateLabel":"lower layers about 770,000 years ago; upper layers younger","type":"fossil","summary":"The cave of Peking Man, with remains of about forty Homo erectus individuals and tens of thousands of stone tools. The original fossils were lost in 1941; the lower layers were dated to about 770,000 years in 2009.","chapters":["out-of-africa-first"]},{"id":"gesher-benot-yaaqov","name":"Gesher Benot Yaʿaqov","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":33.01,"lon":35.63,"start":780000,"end":780000,"dateLabel":"about 780,000 years ago","type":"fire","summary":"Waterlogged Acheulean lakeshore site with ‘phantom hearths’ of burnt flint and fish teeth heated at cooking temperatures, the oldest evidence of cooking.","chapters":["fire-and-wood"]},{"id":"kalinga-luzon","name":"Kalinga site, Cagayan Valley","country":"Philippines","region":"asia","lat":17.52,"lon":121.6,"start":709000,"end":709000,"dateLabel":"about 709,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Fifty-seven stone tools with a butchered rhinoceros skeleton, the oldest evidence of hominins in the Philippines.","chapters":["island-hominins"]},{"id":"pakefield","name":"Pakefield","country":"United Kingdom","region":"europe","lat":52.45,"lon":1.72,"start":700000,"end":700000,"dateLabel":"about 700,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Thirty-two flint artefacts from river deposits of a warm interglacial, the first evidence, published in 2005, that people reached northern Europe before about 500,000 years ago.","chapters":["first-europeans"]},{"id":"la-noira","name":"La Noira","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":47.17,"lon":2.12,"start":700000,"end":700000,"dateLabel":"about 700,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Handaxes in central France dated by electron spin resonance to about 700,000 years, the earliest Acheulean in northwestern Europe.","chapters":["first-europeans"]},{"id":"mata-menge","name":"Mata Menge","country":"Indonesia","region":"oceania","lat":-8.69,"lon":121.1,"start":700000,"end":700000,"dateLabel":"about 700,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"A jaw fragment, teeth and, published in 2024, part of an upper arm bone from tiny hominins, showing that Flores hominins were already about a metre tall 700,000 years ago.","chapters":["island-hominins"]},{"id":"mauer","name":"Mauer","country":"Germany","region":"europe","lat":49.34,"lon":8.8,"start":609000,"end":609000,"dateLabel":"about 609,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"The massive, chinless jaw found in a sand pit in 1907, type specimen of Homo heidelbergensis, dated in 2010 to about 609,000 years.","chapters":["first-europeans"]},{"id":"bodo","name":"Bodo","country":"Ethiopia","region":"africa","lat":10.25,"lon":40.55,"start":600000,"end":600000,"dateLabel":"about 600,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"A large-faced cranium from the Middle Awash with cut marks from defleshing, and the type specimen of the proposed species Homo bodoensis.","chapters":["middle-pleistocene"]},{"id":"arago","name":"Caune de l’Arago (Tautavel)","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":42.84,"lon":2.75,"start":560000,"end":400000,"dateLabel":"about 560,000–400,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Cave in the eastern Pyrenees with more than a hundred human remains, including the Arago 21 face of about 450,000 years ago.","chapters":["first-europeans"]},{"id":"kapthurin","name":"Kapthurin Formation","country":"Kenya","region":"africa","lat":0.58,"lon":35.97,"start":545000,"end":285000,"dateLabel":"about 545,000–285,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Sediments near Lake Baringo with systematic blade production over half a million years ago and later Levallois flakes, grindstones and red ochre.","chapters":["levallois-msa"]},{"id":"trinil","name":"Trinil","country":"Indonesia","region":"asia","lat":-7.37,"lon":111.36,"start":540000,"end":430000,"dateLabel":"540,000–430,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Where Eugène Dubois found the first Homo erectus in 1891. A freshwater mussel shell from his collection carries a zigzag engraving, the oldest known geometric mark, made by Homo erectus.","chapters":["out-of-africa-first"]},{"id":"boxgrove","name":"Boxgrove","country":"United Kingdom","region":"europe","lat":50.87,"lon":-0.71,"start":500000,"end":480000,"dateLabel":"about 500,000–480,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"An ancient shoreline with hundreds of handaxes, a robust human shinbone and teeth, and knapping scatters around a butchered horse, a snapshot of a single afternoon’s work.","chapters":["first-europeans"]},{"id":"kathu-pan-1","name":"Kathu Pan 1","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-27.67,"lon":23.02,"start":500000,"end":500000,"dateLabel":"about 500,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Prepared-core points and blades with damage interpreted as evidence of hafting and use as spear tips, a claim still debated.","chapters":["levallois-msa"]},{"id":"kalambo-falls","name":"Kalambo Falls","country":"Zambia","region":"africa","lat":-8.6,"lon":31.24,"start":476000,"end":476000,"dateLabel":"at least 476,000 years ago","type":"other","summary":"Two logs joined by a deliberately cut notch, the oldest known wooden structure, with a wedge, digging stick and other worked wood preserved in waterlogged sediment.","chapters":["fire-and-wood"]},{"id":"sima-de-los-huesos","name":"Sima de los Huesos, Atapuerca","country":"Spain","region":"europe","lat":42.35,"lon":-3.52,"start":430000,"end":430000,"dateLabel":"about 430,000 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"A shaft deep in the Atapuerca hills holding remains of at least 28 early Neanderthal relatives. Their DNA, about 430,000 years old, is the oldest human genetic material sequenced to date.","chapters":["the-longest-age","middle-pleistocene"]},{"id":"qesem-cave","name":"Qesem Cave","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":32.11,"lon":34.98,"start":420000,"end":200000,"dateLabel":"about 420,000–200,000 years ago; central hearth about 300,000","type":"fire","summary":"Cave with a large central hearth reused around 300,000 years ago, surrounded by burnt bone and flint, a focus of repeated gatherings.","chapters":["fire-and-wood","levallois-msa"]},{"id":"barnham","name":"Barnham","country":"United Kingdom","region":"europe","lat":52.38,"lon":0.75,"start":400000,"end":400000,"dateLabel":"about 400,000 years ago","type":"fire","summary":"A repeatedly heated patch of sediment, heat-shattered handaxes and imported pyrite, published in 2025 as the earliest evidence of deliberate fire-making.","chapters":["fire-and-wood"]},{"id":"clacton-on-sea","name":"Clacton-on-Sea","country":"United Kingdom","region":"europe","lat":51.79,"lon":1.15,"start":400000,"end":400000,"dateLabel":"about 400,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"A sharpened yew point found in 1911, usually interpreted as the tip of a spear and long the oldest known wooden artefact.","chapters":["fire-and-wood"]},{"id":"twin-rivers","name":"Twin Rivers","country":"Zambia","region":"africa","lat":-15.55,"lon":28.13,"start":400000,"end":260000,"dateLabel":"roughly 400,000–260,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Hilltop cave with hundreds of pieces of ochre and other pigments in a range of colours, argued to reflect early symbolic use.","chapters":["levallois-msa"]},{"id":"rising-star-cave","name":"Rising Star Cave","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-26.02,"lon":27.71,"start":335000,"end":236000,"dateLabel":"335,000–236,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Deep chambers holding more than 1,500 bones of Homo naledi, a small-brained species; claims of burial and engraving remain contested.","chapters":["middle-pleistocene"]},{"id":"nor-geghi-1","name":"Nor Geghi 1","country":"Armenia","region":"asia","lat":40.32,"lon":44.58,"start":335000,"end":325000,"dateLabel":"about 335,000–325,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Levallois cores alongside bifaces between dated lava flows, used to argue that Levallois arose locally from Acheulean traditions.","chapters":["levallois-msa"]},{"id":"jebel-irhoud","name":"Jebel Irhoud","country":"Morocco","region":"africa","lat":31.86,"lon":-8.87,"start":315000,"end":286000,"dateLabel":"about 315,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"A former cave in a barite quarry with fossils of at least five individuals, modern-looking faces and elongated braincases, dated by thermoluminescence on burnt flints.","chapters":["origin-of-sapiens"]},{"id":"denisova-cave","name":"Denisova Cave","country":"Russia","region":"asia","lat":51.4,"lon":84.68,"start":300000,"end":20000,"dateLabel":"about 250,000–50,000 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"The cave that gave the Denisovans their name: genomes from a finger bone, teeth and the first-generation Neanderthal–Denisovan girl Denisova 11, plus DNA from the sediments of both groups.","chapters":["the-longest-age","denisovans","north-asia","epilogue"]},{"id":"schoningen","name":"Schöningen","country":"Germany","region":"europe","lat":52.13,"lon":10.97,"start":300000,"end":200000,"dateLabel":"about 300,000 or 200,000 years ago (debated)","type":"tools","summary":"About ten wooden spears and several throwing sticks among butchered horse remains in lakeshore sediments, the oldest complete hunting weapons known.","chapters":["fire-and-wood"]},{"id":"gantangqing","name":"Gantangqing","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":24.35,"lon":102.75,"start":300000,"end":250000,"dateLabel":"roughly 300,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Dozens of wooden tools, including digging sticks for roots and tubers, from an ancient lakeshore in Yunnan, reported in 2025 as the oldest wooden tools in East Asia.","chapters":["fire-and-wood"]},{"id":"hualongdong","name":"Hualongdong","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":30.1,"lon":117.02,"start":300000,"end":300000,"dateLabel":"about 300,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Cave in Anhui with the face and jaw of a teenager combining archaic and modern-looking features.","chapters":["middle-pleistocene"]},{"id":"hathnora","name":"Hathnora (Narmada)","country":"India","region":"asia","lat":22.87,"lon":77.97,"start":300000,"end":150000,"dateLabel":"Middle Pleistocene, age poorly fixed","type":"fossil","summary":"Partial cranium found in 1982 in Narmada river gravels, the best-known Middle Pleistocene hominin fossil from South Asia.","chapters":["middle-pleistocene"]},{"id":"kabwe","name":"Kabwe (Broken Hill)","country":"Zambia","region":"africa","lat":-14.45,"lon":28.43,"start":299000,"end":299000,"dateLabel":"about 299,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Mine where a heavily built archaic skull was found in 1921. Its 2020 dating shows that archaic humans lived in Africa alongside early Homo sapiens.","chapters":["middle-pleistocene","origin-of-sapiens"]},{"id":"cova-negra","name":"Cova Negra","country":"Spain","region":"europe","lat":38.97,"lon":-0.53,"start":273000,"end":146000,"dateLabel":"about 273,000–146,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"A cave near Xàtiva where an ear-bone fragment of a Neanderthal child nicknamed “Tina” shows an inner-ear malformation known only in Down syndrome; the child lived to at least six.","chapters":["neanderthals"]},{"id":"dali","name":"Dali","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":34.85,"lon":109.85,"start":260000,"end":260000,"dateLabel":"about 260,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"A nearly complete, large-brained cranium found in 1978, one of the key Middle Pleistocene fossils of China.","chapters":["middle-pleistocene"]},{"id":"jinniushan","name":"Jinniushan","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":40.58,"lon":122.44,"start":260000,"end":260000,"dateLabel":"about 260,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"A cave that yielded a partial skeleton with a large skull, one of several Middle Pleistocene Chinese fossils suggested as possible Denisovans since the Harbin identification.","chapters":["middle-pleistocene","denisovans"]},{"id":"florisbad","name":"Florisbad","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-28.77,"lon":26.07,"start":259000,"end":259000,"dateLabel":"about 259,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"A spring mound where a partial skull with a broad face was found in 1932, dated by electron spin resonance on a tooth.","chapters":["origin-of-sapiens"]},{"id":"omo-kibish","name":"Omo Kibish","country":"Ethiopia","region":"africa","lat":5.4,"lon":35.93,"start":233000,"end":195000,"dateLabel":"at least 233,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Site of the Omo I and Omo II skulls found in 1967. A volcanic ash from the Shala eruption above Omo I shows it is at least about 233,000 years old.","chapters":["origin-of-sapiens"]},{"id":"xujiayao","name":"Xujiayao","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":40.1,"lon":113.98,"start":220000,"end":160000,"dateLabel":"roughly 220,000–160,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"A site in the Nihewan basin with fragments of large-brained archaic humans, the basis of the proposed species Homo juluensis and possible Denisovans.","chapters":["middle-pleistocene","denisovans"]},{"id":"apidima-cave","name":"Apidima Cave","country":"Greece","region":"europe","lat":36.63,"lon":22.38,"start":210000,"end":170000,"dateLabel":"at least 210,000 and 170,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"A coastal cave in the Mani peninsula where two skulls were found in 1978; Apidima 1, at least 210,000 years old, has been claimed as the oldest Homo sapiens outside Africa.","chapters":["early-dispersals"]},{"id":"stelida","name":"Stelida, Naxos","country":"Greece","region":"europe","lat":37.08,"lon":25.36,"start":200000,"end":200000,"dateLabel":"at least 200,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"A chert source on Naxos exploited by pre-modern hominins at least 200,000 years ago; whether reaching it required a sea crossing depends on reconstructions of the ancient Cycladic landmass.","chapters":["island-hominins"]},{"id":"campitello","name":"Campitello Quarry","country":"Italy","region":"europe","lat":43.48,"lon":11.62,"start":200000,"end":200000,"dateLabel":"about 200,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Flint flakes partly coated with birch-bark tar, found with elephant remains, the oldest known adhesive for hafting.","chapters":["levallois-msa"]},{"id":"border-cave","name":"Border Cave","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-27.02,"lon":31.99,"start":200000,"end":24000,"dateLabel":"Later Stone Age layers from about 44,000 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"Cave in the Lebombo Mountains with some of the earliest Later Stone Age finds: beeswax glue, bone points, digging sticks, beads, a poison applicator and the notched Lebombo bone.","chapters":["msa-symbolism","african-lsa"]},{"id":"misliya-cave","name":"Misliya Cave","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":32.71,"lon":34.97,"start":194000,"end":177000,"dateLabel":"about 194,000–177,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"A collapsed cave on Mount Carmel whose upper jaw with teeth is the oldest widely accepted Homo sapiens fossil outside Africa.","chapters":["early-dispersals"]},{"id":"liang-bua","name":"Liang Bua","country":"Indonesia","region":"oceania","lat":-8.53,"lon":120.46,"start":190000,"end":50000,"dateLabel":"tools 190,000–50,000; skeletons 100,000–60,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Limestone cave where the skeleton LB1 of Homo floresiensis was found in 2003, with remains of dwarf Stegodon, giant rats, storks and Komodo dragons.","chapters":["island-hominins"]},{"id":"penghu-channel","name":"Penghu Channel","country":"Taiwan","region":"asia","lat":23.5,"lon":119.9,"start":190000,"end":10000,"dateLabel":"probably 190,000–130,000 or 70,000–10,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Seafloor, dry land in glacial periods, from which fishermen dredged the Penghu 1 jaw, identified in 2025 by ancient proteins as a male Denisovan.","chapters":["denisovans"]},{"id":"bruniquel-cave","name":"Bruniquel Cave","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":44.06,"lon":1.67,"start":176500,"end":176500,"dateLabel":"about 176,500 years ago","type":"monument","summary":"Some 400 broken stalagmite pieces arranged in two rings and several piles, with traces of fire, 336 metres from the entrance. Dated by uranium-series on calcite regrowth, they are among the oldest known human constructions.","chapters":["neanderthals"]},{"id":"poggetti-vecchi","name":"Poggetti Vecchi","country":"Italy","region":"europe","lat":42.82,"lon":11.15,"start":171000,"end":171000,"dateLabel":"about 171,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Boxwood sticks shaped with stone tools and fire, probably digging sticks, found with straight-tusked elephant remains near Grosseto.","chapters":["fire-and-wood"]},{"id":"guanyindong","name":"Guanyindong","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":27.03,"lon":106.03,"start":170000,"end":80000,"dateLabel":"about 170,000–80,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Cave in Guizhou with Levallois cores and flakes, showing prepared-core technology in East Asia before the accepted arrival of modern humans.","chapters":["levallois-msa"]},{"id":"tam-ngu-hao-2","name":"Tam Ngu Hao 2 (Cobra Cave)","country":"Laos","region":"asia","lat":20.21,"lon":103.41,"start":164000,"end":131000,"dateLabel":"about 164,000–131,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"A cave in the Annamite mountains that yielded a child’s molar resembling the Baishiya Denisovan teeth, the first evidence of Denisovans in tropical Southeast Asia.","chapters":["denisovans"]},{"id":"pinnacle-point","name":"Pinnacle Point","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-34.21,"lon":22.09,"start":164000,"end":50000,"dateLabel":"about 164,000–50,000 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"Coastal caves near Mossel Bay with shellfish, ground ochre and heat-treated silcrete from about 164,000 years ago (PP13B) and microliths from about 71,000 years ago (PP5-6).","chapters":["msa-symbolism","catastrophes"]},{"id":"baishiya-karst-cave","name":"Baishiya Karst Cave","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":35.45,"lon":102.57,"start":160000,"end":32000,"dateLabel":"at least 160,000 to about 32,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"A sacred cave at 3,280 metres on the Tibetan Plateau with the Xiahe mandible, Denisovan sediment DNA and a Denisovan rib about 48,000–32,000 years old among butchered animal bones.","chapters":["denisovans","epilogue"]},{"id":"herto","name":"Herto","country":"Ethiopia","region":"africa","lat":10.29,"lon":40.53,"start":160000,"end":154000,"dateLabel":"about 160,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Three skulls, two adults and a child, with cut marks and polish suggesting mortuary handling, found with Middle Stone Age tools and hippopotamus bones.","chapters":["origin-of-sapiens"]},{"id":"bizmoune-cave","name":"Bizmoune Cave","country":"Morocco","region":"africa","lat":31.43,"lon":-9.68,"start":150000,"end":142000,"dateLabel":"at least 142,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"A cave near Essaouira with 33 perforated Tritia gibbosula shells, the oldest known shell beads.","chapters":["msa-symbolism"]},{"id":"harbin","name":"Harbin","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":45.77,"lon":126.62,"start":146000,"end":146000,"dateLabel":"at least 146,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Findspot of the near-complete “Dragon Man” skull, named Homo longi in 2021 and identified as Denisovan in 2025 from proteins and mitochondrial DNA in dental calculus.","chapters":["middle-pleistocene","denisovans","epilogue"]},{"id":"nesher-ramla","name":"Nesher Ramla","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":31.91,"lon":34.9,"start":140000,"end":120000,"dateLabel":"about 140,000–120,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Part of a skull and a jaw with Levallois tools, combining archaic and Neanderthal-like features, published in 2021.","chapters":["middle-pleistocene"]},{"id":"plakias","name":"Plakias","country":"Greece","region":"europe","lat":35.19,"lon":24.39,"start":130000,"end":130000,"dateLabel":"at least 130,000 years ago (estimated from geological context)","type":"tools","summary":"Quartz handaxes and cleavers on the south coast of Crete, used to argue for Lower Palaeolithic sea crossings; the dating is indirect and debated.","chapters":["island-hominins"]},{"id":"krapina","name":"Krapina","country":"Croatia","region":"europe","lat":46.16,"lon":15.87,"start":130000,"end":130000,"dateLabel":"about 130,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"A rock shelter with remains of dozens of Neanderthals and eight white-tailed eagle talons bearing cut marks and polish, possibly worn as jewellery.","chapters":["neanderthals"]},{"id":"es-skhul","name":"es-Skhul","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":32.67,"lon":34.97,"start":130000,"end":100000,"dateLabel":"about 130,000–100,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"A rock shelter on Mount Carmel with burials of early modern humans, perforated shell beads and the child Skhul I, recently argued to show mixed ancestry.","chapters":["early-dispersals"]},{"id":"neumark-nord","name":"Neumark-Nord","country":"Germany","region":"europe","lat":51.32,"lon":11.88,"start":126000,"end":124000,"dateLabel":"about 125,000 years ago","type":"other","summary":"Last Interglacial lake basins where Neanderthals butchered more than 70 straight-tusked elephants, mostly large males, and at Neumark-Nord 2 rendered grease from the bones of at least 172 large mammals.","chapters":["neanderthals"]},{"id":"jebel-faya","name":"Jebel Faya","country":"United Arab Emirates","region":"asia","lat":25.12,"lon":55.85,"start":125000,"end":40000,"dateLabel":"from about 125,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"A rock shelter whose oldest tool assemblage, about 125,000 years old, has been linked to East African toolmakers crossing the southern Red Sea.","chapters":["early-dispersals"]},{"id":"cueva-de-los-aviones","name":"Cueva de los Aviones","country":"Spain","region":"europe","lat":37.58,"lon":-0.98,"start":120000,"end":115000,"dateLabel":"about 120,000–115,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"A sea cave near Cartagena with perforated shells stained with red and yellow pigments, dated by uranium-series to a time when only Neanderthals lived in Europe.","chapters":["neanderthals"]},{"id":"fuyan-cave","name":"Fuyan Cave","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":25.62,"lon":111.57,"start":120000,"end":80000,"dateLabel":"claimed 120,000–80,000 years ago (disputed)","type":"fossil","summary":"A cave in Daoxian, Hunan, with 47 modern human teeth first dated to 120,000–80,000 years ago; later redating suggests they are far younger.","chapters":["early-dispersals"]},{"id":"talepu","name":"Talepu","country":"Indonesia","region":"oceania","lat":-4.36,"lon":119.97,"start":118000,"end":118000,"dateLabel":"at least 118,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Stone artefacts in river deposits of southern Sulawesi, reported in 2016 as at least 118,000 years old, long before modern humans reached the island.","chapters":["island-hominins"]},{"id":"ngandong","name":"Ngandong","country":"Indonesia","region":"asia","lat":-7.18,"lon":111.43,"start":117000,"end":108000,"dateLabel":"117,000–108,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Twelve skullcaps and two shinbones of late Homo erectus from a Solo River terrace, dated in 2020 with 52 age estimates. They are the youngest known Homo erectus fossils.","chapters":["out-of-africa-first"]},{"id":"figueira-brava","name":"Gruta da Figueira Brava","country":"Portugal","region":"europe","lat":38.48,"lon":-8.98,"start":106000,"end":86000,"dateLabel":"about 106,000–86,000 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"A coastal cave in the Arrábida hills where Neanderthals ate mussels, limpets, crabs, fish, seabirds and seals.","chapters":["neanderthals"]},{"id":"blombos-cave","name":"Blombos Cave","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-34.41,"lon":21.22,"start":100000,"end":70000,"dateLabel":"about 100,000–70,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"A cave with a 100,000-year-old paint kit in abalone shells, engraved ochre, the 73,000-year-old crosshatched drawing, Nassarius shell beads and Still Bay points.","chapters":["msa-symbolism"]},{"id":"qafzeh-cave","name":"Qafzeh Cave","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":32.68,"lon":35.3,"start":100000,"end":90000,"dateLabel":"about 100,000–90,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"A cave near Nazareth with burials of early modern humans, including an adolescent with deer antlers, alongside red ochre and marine shells brought from the coast.","chapters":["early-dispersals"]},{"id":"tam-pa-ling","name":"Tam Pà Ling","country":"Laos","region":"asia","lat":20.21,"lon":103.41,"start":86000,"end":68000,"dateLabel":"about 86,000–68,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"A mountain cave that has yielded a modern human skull, jaws and other bones, the oldest Homo sapiens fossils in mainland Southeast Asia.","chapters":["early-dispersals"]},{"id":"grotte-des-pigeons","name":"Grotte des Pigeons (Taforalt)","country":"Morocco","region":"africa","lat":34.81,"lon":-2.41,"start":82000,"end":13700,"dateLabel":"about 15,000–13,700 years ago (Iberomaurusian cemetery)","type":"art","summary":"Iberomaurusian cemetery whose people had heavy tooth decay from starchy wild plants, and whose genomes link North Africa with the Levant.","chapters":["msa-symbolism","african-lsa"]},{"id":"shanidar-cave","name":"Shanidar Cave","country":"Iraq","region":"asia","lat":36.83,"lon":44.22,"start":80000,"end":45000,"dateLabel":"roughly 80,000–45,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"Remains of ten Neanderthals excavated by Ralph Solecki, including the injured but long-surviving Shanidar 1 and the “flower burial” Shanidar 4, plus Shanidar Z, found in 2018.","chapters":["neanderthals"]},{"id":"shinfa-metema-1","name":"Shinfa-Metema 1","country":"Ethiopia","region":"africa","lat":12.7,"lon":36.2,"start":80000,"end":70000,"dateLabel":"about 74,000 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"A riverside site in the northwestern Ethiopian lowlands with Toba cryptotephra, where people turned increasingly to fishing as dry seasons lengthened.","chapters":["catastrophes"]},{"id":"panga-ya-saidi","name":"Panga ya Saidi","country":"Kenya","region":"africa","lat":-3.67,"lon":39.72,"start":78000,"end":78000,"dateLabel":"about 78,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"A cave where a child of about three, nicknamed Mtoto, was buried in a pit, flexed and probably wrapped: the oldest known burial in Africa.","chapters":["msa-symbolism"]},{"id":"sibudu-cave","name":"Sibudu Cave","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-29.52,"lon":31.09,"start":77000,"end":58000,"dateLabel":"about 77,000–58,000 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"A rock shelter with insect-repellent bedding, compound adhesives of gum and ochre, and some of the earliest evidence for bow-and-arrow hunting.","chapters":["msa-symbolism"]},{"id":"jwalapuram","name":"Jwalapuram","country":"India","region":"asia","lat":15.32,"lon":78.13,"start":77000,"end":70000,"dateLabel":"about 77,000–70,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Sites in the Jurreru valley where Middle Palaeolithic tools lie immediately below and above the Toba ash, showing that toolmakers persisted through the eruption.","chapters":["catastrophes"]},{"id":"la-roche-cotard","name":"La Roche-Cotard","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":47.33,"lon":0.42,"start":75000,"end":57000,"dateLabel":"before about 57,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"A cave in the Loire valley whose soft chalk walls bear finger flutings. Luminescence dating shows the cave was sealed about 57,000 years ago, and it contained only Mousterian tools.","chapters":["neanderthals"]},{"id":"toba-caldera","name":"Toba caldera","country":"Indonesia","region":"asia","lat":2.6,"lon":98.8,"start":74000,"end":74000,"dateLabel":"about 74,000 years ago","type":"other","summary":"The collapsed volcano, now Lake Toba, whose super-eruption spread ash from the South China Sea to the Arabian Sea and microscopic glass as far as South Africa.","chapters":["catastrophes"]},{"id":"lida-ajer","name":"Lida Ajer","country":"Indonesia","region":"asia","lat":-0.3,"lon":100.65,"start":73000,"end":63000,"dateLabel":"about 73,000–63,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"A cave in the Padang Highlands of Sumatra whose modern human teeth, first collected in the 1880s, show people in tropical rainforest by about 70,000 years ago.","chapters":["early-dispersals"]},{"id":"liang-metanduno","name":"Liang Metanduno, Muna Island","country":"Indonesia","region":"oceania","lat":-4.92,"lon":122.7,"start":67800,"end":67800,"dateLabel":"at least 67,800 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Limestone cave off southeastern Sulawesi with hand stencils, one dated in 2026 to at least 67,800 years by uranium-series analysis of the overlying crust. One stencil’s fingers were narrowed into a claw-like shape, which the team reads as the work of Homo sapiens.","chapters":["sahul"]},{"id":"callao-cave","name":"Callao Cave","country":"Philippines","region":"asia","lat":17.7,"lon":121.82,"start":67000,"end":50000,"dateLabel":"at least 67,000–50,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Teeth and hand and foot bones of at least three individuals, named Homo luzonensis in 2019, with small teeth and curved finger and toe bones.","chapters":["island-hominins"]},{"id":"kebara-cave","name":"Kebara Cave","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":32.56,"lon":34.94,"start":65000,"end":12000,"dateLabel":"about 60,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"A Mount Carmel cave where the buried Kebara 2 Neanderthal preserves the only complete Neanderthal hyoid bone, closely matching our own.","chapters":["neanderthals","natufians"]},{"id":"diepkloof","name":"Diepkloof Rock Shelter","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-32.39,"lon":18.45,"start":65000,"end":55000,"dateLabel":"about 60,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"A rock shelter with 270 fragments of engraved ostrich eggshell containers whose motifs follow a shared graphic tradition.","chapters":["msa-symbolism"]},{"id":"madjedbebe","name":"Madjedbebe","country":"Australia","region":"oceania","lat":-12.5,"lon":132.88,"start":65000,"end":50000,"dateLabel":"about 65,000 years ago onward","type":"tools","summary":"Rock shelter in Mirarr country, Arnhem Land. Luminescence dates of about 65,000 years for the lowest artefacts, with grinding stones, ground ochre and the oldest edge-ground axes, anchor the long chronology for Sahul.","chapters":["sahul","epilogue"]},{"id":"umhlatuzana","name":"Umhlatuzana Rock Shelter","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-29.81,"lon":30.73,"start":60000,"end":60000,"dateLabel":"about 60,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"A rock shelter whose backed quartz microliths carry traces of plant poison according to a 2026 study, the oldest known evidence for poisoned arrows.","chapters":["msa-symbolism"]},{"id":"chagyrskaya-cave","name":"Chagyrskaya Cave","country":"Russia","region":"asia","lat":51.44,"lon":83.16,"start":59000,"end":51000,"dateLabel":"about 59,000–51,000 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"An Altai cave whose Neanderthal remains yielded a high-coverage genome (2020) and, with nearby Okladnikov Cave, 13 genomes including a father and his teenage daughter (2022).","chapters":["neanderthals"]},{"id":"grotte-mandrin","name":"Grotte Mandrin","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":44.49,"lon":4.75,"start":56800,"end":42000,"dateLabel":"about 54,000–42,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Rock shelter above the Rhône with alternating Neanderthal and early sapiens layers. It yielded ‘Thorin’, a late Neanderthal whose 2024 genome revealed a lineage isolated for some 50,000 years.","chapters":["early-dispersals","neanderthal-end"]},{"id":"la-chapelle-aux-saints","name":"La Chapelle-aux-Saints","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":44.99,"lon":1.72,"start":56000,"end":47000,"dateLabel":"about 50,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"The grave of an elderly Neanderthal man, found in 1908. Re-excavation in the 2010s showed that the pit had been dug deliberately.","chapters":["neanderthals","epilogue"]},{"id":"abri-du-maras","name":"Abri du Maras","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":44.31,"lon":4.55,"start":52000,"end":41000,"dateLabel":"about 52,000–41,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Rock shelter in the Ardèche where a fragment of three-ply cord twisted from inner bark was found on a Neanderthal stone tool, reported in 2020: rare direct evidence of fibre technology.","chapters":["the-longest-age","neanderthals"]},{"id":"maros-pangkep-karst","name":"Maros-Pangkep karst (Leang Karampuang, Leang Tedongnge)","country":"Indonesia","region":"oceania","lat":-4.93,"lon":119.66,"start":51200,"end":45500,"dateLabel":"at least 51,200–45,500 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Limestone caves in South Sulawesi. A painted warty pig at Leang Tedongnge is at least 45,500 years old, and a scene of a pig with three human-like figures at Leang Karampuang at least 51,200 years.","chapters":["sahul"]},{"id":"boodie-cave","name":"Boodie Cave, Barrow Island","country":"Australia","region":"oceania","lat":-20.78,"lon":115.38,"start":51000,"end":46000,"dateLabel":"51,000–46,000 years ago (first occupation)","type":"settlement","summary":"Limestone cave on what was then a hill on the coastal plain; its shell middens are Australia’s earliest direct evidence of marine foods.","chapters":["sahul"]},{"id":"boker-tachtit","name":"Boker Tachtit","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":30.87,"lon":34.78,"start":50000,"end":44000,"dateLabel":"50,000–44,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Open-air site in the Negev that defines the Levantine Initial Upper Palaeolithic. Radiocarbon and luminescence dates published in 2021 place its earliest blade industry at 50,000–49,000 years ago, a likely marker of Homo sapiens moving out of Africa.","chapters":["great-dispersal"]},{"id":"enkapune-ya-muto","name":"Enkapune ya Muto","country":"Kenya","region":"africa","lat":-0.83,"lon":36.15,"start":50000,"end":40000,"dateLabel":"about 50,000–40,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Rock shelter in the Central Rift with an early Later Stone Age sequence and some of the oldest ostrich eggshell beads.","chapters":["african-lsa"]},{"id":"mumba-rockshelter","name":"Mumba rock shelter","country":"Tanzania","region":"africa","lat":-3.54,"lon":35.3,"start":50000,"end":30000,"dateLabel":"about 50,000–30,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Shelter near Lake Eyasi whose layers record the transition from Middle to Later Stone Age technology and early eggshell beads.","chapters":["african-lsa"]},{"id":"ivane-valley","name":"Ivane Valley","country":"Papua New Guinea","region":"oceania","lat":-8.45,"lon":147.2,"start":49000,"end":44000,"dateLabel":"49,000–44,000 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"Highland valley at about 2,000 metres where people collected pandanus nuts, ate yams, cleared forest by burning and made waisted axes.","chapters":["sahul"]},{"id":"warratyi","name":"Warratyi rock shelter","country":"Australia","region":"oceania","lat":-30.3,"lon":138.9,"start":49000,"end":46000,"dateLabel":"49,000–46,000 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"Rock shelter in Adnyamathanha country in the Flinders Ranges with early ochre and gypsum pigment, a bone point, Diprotodon bone and giant-bird eggshell.","chapters":["sahul"]},{"id":"devils-lair","name":"Devil’s Lair","country":"Australia","region":"oceania","lat":-34.15,"lon":115.08,"start":48000,"end":12000,"dateLabel":"from about 48,000 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"Limestone cave near Cape Leeuwin in southwestern Australia, occupied from about 48,000 years ago, with processed ochre and bone tools.","chapters":["sahul"]},{"id":"fa-hien-lena","name":"Fa-Hien Lena","country":"Sri Lanka","region":"asia","lat":6.65,"lon":80.21,"start":48000,"end":4000,"dateLabel":"c. 48,000–4,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Rainforest cave with early microliths, bone arrowheads from about 48,000 years ago and evidence of specialised hunting of monkeys and squirrels.","chapters":["south-asia"]},{"id":"ranis-ilsenhohle","name":"Ilsenhöhle, Ranis","country":"Germany","region":"europe","lat":50.66,"lon":11.57,"start":47500,"end":43000,"dateLabel":"47,500–43,000 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"Cave beneath Ranis castle in Thuringia. Bone fragments identified in 2024 as Homo sapiens tie the LRJ leaf-point industry to our species, and their genomes are among the oldest ever sequenced.","chapters":["great-dispersal"]},{"id":"bacho-kiro-cave","name":"Bacho Kiro Cave","country":"Bulgaria","region":"europe","lat":42.95,"lon":25.43,"start":46000,"end":42000,"dateLabel":"46,000–42,000 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"Cave with some of Europe’s earliest Homo sapiens remains, found with Initial Upper Palaeolithic tools and cave-bear tooth pendants. Genomes show Neanderthal ancestors five to seven generations back and links to later East Asians.","chapters":["great-dispersal"]},{"id":"vindija-cave","name":"Vindija Cave","country":"Croatia","region":"europe","lat":46.3,"lon":16.08,"start":46000,"end":40000,"dateLabel":"older than about 40,000 years (redated 2017)","type":"dna","summary":"Source of a high-quality Neanderthal genome. Its Neanderthals were once dated to around 30,000 years; improved radiocarbon dating showed they are older than about 40,000.","chapters":["neanderthal-end"]},{"id":"zlaty-kun","name":"Zlatý kůň","country":"Czech Republic","region":"europe","lat":49.92,"lon":14.07,"start":45000,"end":45000,"dateLabel":"about 45,000 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"Cave in the Bohemian Karst where the skull of a woman was found. Her high-quality genome (2021) and her kinship with individuals from Ranis (2024) place her in the earliest known branch of non-Africans.","chapters":["great-dispersal"]},{"id":"ust-ishim","name":"Ust’-Ishim","country":"Russia","region":"asia","lat":57.69,"lon":71.17,"start":45000,"end":45000,"dateLabel":"about 45,000 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"A human femur eroding from the bank of the Irtysh River yielded a high-quality genome in 2014. Its long Neanderthal segments helped date the mixing, and the man belonged to a lineage close to the split of western and eastern Eurasians.","chapters":["great-dispersal"]},{"id":"grotte-du-renne","name":"Grotte du Renne, Arcy-sur-Cure","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":47.6,"lon":3.76,"start":45000,"end":41000,"dateLabel":"about 45,000–41,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Cave with Châtelperronian ornaments, bone tools and Neanderthal remains, confirmed by ancient proteins in 2016; the integrity of its layers is debated.","chapters":["neanderthal-end"]},{"id":"grotta-del-cavallo","name":"Grotta del Cavallo","country":"Italy","region":"europe","lat":40.16,"lon":17.96,"start":45000,"end":40000,"dateLabel":"about 45,000–40,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Coastal cave in Apulia, type site of the Uluzzian. Two milk teeth identified in 2011 as Homo sapiens make it one of Europe’s earliest sapiens sites, though their stratigraphy is disputed.","chapters":["neanderthal-end"]},{"id":"tolbor-16","name":"Tolbor-16","country":"Mongolia","region":"asia","lat":49.24,"lon":102.96,"start":45000,"end":45000,"dateLabel":"about 45,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Open-air site in northern Mongolia with an Initial Upper Palaeolithic blade industry, evidence for a northern route of Homo sapiens across the steppe.","chapters":["north-asia"]},{"id":"spy-cave","name":"Spy","country":"Belgium","region":"europe","lat":50.48,"lon":4.67,"start":44200,"end":40600,"dateLabel":"about 44,200–40,600 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Cave where two Neanderthal skeletons found in 1886 helped establish the species. Redated in 2021, they are among the last Neanderthals of northwestern Europe.","chapters":["neanderthal-end"]},{"id":"geissenklosterle","name":"Geißenklösterle","country":"Germany","region":"europe","lat":48.4,"lon":9.77,"start":43000,"end":35000,"dateLabel":"about 43,000–35,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Cave in the Ach valley with flutes of swan bone and mammoth ivory and some of the earliest radiocarbon dates for the Aurignacian.","chapters":["aurignacian-art"]},{"id":"hohle-fels","name":"Hohle Fels","country":"Germany","region":"europe","lat":48.38,"lon":9.75,"start":42000,"end":35000,"dateLabel":"about 37,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Swabian Jura cave that yielded a mammoth-ivory baton with four spirally grooved holes. Experiments published in 2024 showed it was most likely used to make rope.","chapters":["the-longest-age","aurignacian-art"]},{"id":"lake-mungo","name":"Lake Mungo","country":"Australia","region":"oceania","lat":-33.72,"lon":143.1,"start":42000,"end":19000,"dateLabel":"about 40,000 years ago (burials); footprints 23,000–19,000","type":"burial","summary":"Dry lake basin where Mungo Lady, one of the world’s oldest cremations, and Mungo Man, buried with red ochre, date to about 40,000 years ago. Later footprints record adults and children crossing a clay pan.","chapters":["the-longest-age","sahul"]},{"id":"ngawha","name":"Ngāwhā","country":"New Zealand","region":"oceania","lat":-35.4,"lon":173.86,"start":42000,"end":41000,"dateLabel":"about 42,000–41,000 years ago","type":"other","summary":"A swamp in Northland where an ancient kauri tree spanning the Laschamp excursion gave a precise radiocarbon record of the weakening magnetic field.","chapters":["catastrophes"]},{"id":"pestera-cu-oase","name":"Peștera cu Oase","country":"Romania","region":"europe","lat":45.02,"lon":21.83,"start":42000,"end":37000,"dateLabel":"42,000–37,000 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"The “Cave with Bones” in the southwestern Carpathians. The Oase 1 jaw carried 6–9% Neanderthal DNA and had a Neanderthal ancestor only four to six generations back.","chapters":["great-dispersal"]},{"id":"jerimalai","name":"Jerimalai","country":"Timor-Leste","region":"oceania","lat":-8.4,"lon":127.28,"start":42000,"end":16000,"dateLabel":"42,000–16,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Cave at the eastern tip of Timor with bones of tuna and other open-sea fish from about 42,000 years ago and a shell fishhook of 23,000–16,000 years ago.","chapters":["sahul"]},{"id":"saint-cesaire","name":"Saint-Césaire (La Roche-à-Pierrot)","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":45.76,"lon":-0.5,"start":42000,"end":40000,"dateLabel":"about 42,000–40,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Rock shelter where a partial Neanderthal skeleton was found in 1979 in a Châtelperronian layer; a 2018 study questioned the association.","chapters":["neanderthal-end"]},{"id":"laschamps","name":"Laschamps","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":45.74,"lon":2.96,"start":41000,"end":41000,"dateLabel":"about 41,000 years ago","type":"other","summary":"Lava flows in the Chaîne des Puys whose reversed magnetisation revealed the brief geomagnetic excursion that bears their name.","chapters":["catastrophes"]},{"id":"hohlenstein-stadel","name":"Hohlenstein-Stadel","country":"Germany","region":"europe","lat":48.55,"lon":10.17,"start":41000,"end":39000,"dateLabel":"about 40,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Cave in the Lone valley where the fragments of the Lion Man, a 31-centimetre ivory figure with a lion’s head, were found in 1939 and reassembled decades later.","chapters":["aurignacian-art"]},{"id":"grotta-di-fumane","name":"Grotta di Fumane","country":"Italy","region":"europe","lat":45.59,"lon":10.91,"start":41000,"end":35000,"dateLabel":"around 41,000–35,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Cave in the Lessini hills with painted Aurignacian stones, hundreds of shell beads and earlier Neanderthal layers with evidence of feather removal.","chapters":["aurignacian-art"]},{"id":"shuidonggou","name":"Shuidonggou","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":38.29,"lon":106.5,"start":41000,"end":28000,"dateLabel":"about 41,000 years ago onward","type":"tools","summary":"Group of sites on the Yellow River where Initial Upper Palaeolithic blades appear about 41,000 years ago, later followed by ostrich-eggshell beads.","chapters":["north-asia"]},{"id":"el-castillo","name":"El Castillo","country":"Spain","region":"europe","lat":43.29,"lon":-3.97,"start":40800,"end":37300,"dateLabel":"at least 40,800 years (red disk)","type":"art","summary":"Cantabrian cave whose red disk, dated by uranium-series to at least 40,800 years, is among the oldest cave paintings in Europe and a candidate for Neanderthal authorship.","chapters":["aurignacian-art"]},{"id":"vogelherd","name":"Vogelherd","country":"Germany","region":"europe","lat":48.56,"lon":10.19,"start":40000,"end":35000,"dateLabel":"about 40,000–35,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Small cave that has produced the largest group of Aurignacian ivory animal figurines, including a horse, mammoths and lions.","chapters":["aurignacian-art"]},{"id":"tianyuan-cave","name":"Tianyuan Cave","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":39.66,"lon":115.87,"start":40000,"end":40000,"dateLabel":"about 40,000 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"Cave near Beijing whose 40,000-year-old man is an early member of the East Asian lineage, with puzzling genetic links to Goyet in Belgium and to some Amazonian peoples.","chapters":["north-asia"]},{"id":"xiamabei","name":"Xiamabei","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":40.2,"lon":114.6,"start":40000,"end":40000,"dateLabel":"about 40,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Site in the Nihewan Basin with ochre processed on a limestone slab and hafted miniature tools, a distinctive early Upper Palaeolithic culture.","chapters":["north-asia"]},{"id":"campi-flegrei","name":"Phlegraean Fields (Campanian Ignimbrite)","country":"Italy","region":"europe","lat":40.83,"lon":14.14,"start":39850,"end":39850,"dateLabel":"about 39,850 years ago","type":"other","summary":"Volcanic field west of Naples whose Campanian Ignimbrite eruption was the largest in Europe for some 200,000 years. Its ash is a time marker across the continent.","chapters":["neanderthal-end"]},{"id":"kostenki","name":"Kostenki (Kostenki 14, Markina Gora)","country":"Russia","region":"europe","lat":51.39,"lon":39.04,"start":38000,"end":22000,"dateLabel":"about 37,000 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"Cluster of Upper Palaeolithic sites on the Don. The skeleton Kostenki 14 yielded one of the oldest European genomes, already on the western Eurasian branch.","chapters":["aurignacian-art","gravettian-lgm"]},{"id":"kozushima","name":"Kōzushima (obsidian source)","country":"Japan","region":"asia","lat":34.21,"lon":139.14,"start":38000,"end":20000,"dateLabel":"from about 38,000 years ago","type":"other","summary":"Izu island whose obsidian reached mainland Japan from about 38,000 years ago, requiring repeated sea crossings.","chapters":["north-asia"]},{"id":"cueva-anton","name":"Cueva Antón","country":"Spain","region":"europe","lat":38.06,"lon":-1.49,"start":37000,"end":37000,"dateLabel":"about 37,000 years ago (Mousterian, disputed)","type":"tools","summary":"Rock shelter in Murcia whose late Mousterian dates underpin the ‘Ebro frontier’ model of late Neanderthal survival south of the Ebro.","chapters":["neanderthal-end"]},{"id":"chauvet-cave","name":"Chauvet-Pont d’Arc Cave","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":44.39,"lon":4.42,"start":37000,"end":28000,"dateLabel":"about 37,000–33,500 and 31,000–28,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Cave discovered in 1994 with hundreds of animal images, including lions, rhinoceroses and mammoths, dated by more than 250 radiocarbon determinations.","chapters":["aurignacian-art"]},{"id":"goyet-caves","name":"Goyet Caves","country":"Belgium","region":"europe","lat":50.45,"lon":5.02,"start":36000,"end":35000,"dateLabel":"about 36,000 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"A short-snouted canid skull identified in 2009 as a “Palaeolithic dog”. Its mitochondrial DNA lies outside modern dog lineages, and many researchers see it as an unusual wolf.","chapters":["aurignacian-art","dogs"]},{"id":"altamira","name":"Altamira","country":"Spain","region":"europe","lat":43.38,"lon":-4.12,"start":36000,"end":15000,"dateLabel":"about 36,000–15,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"The first Palaeolithic cave paintings to be recognised (1879), long rejected as fakes. Its polychrome bison are Magdalenian, but some signs are over 35,600 years old.","chapters":["magdalenian"]},{"id":"pestera-muierii","name":"Peștera Muierii","country":"Romania","region":"europe","lat":45.17,"lon":23.77,"start":35000,"end":35000,"dateLabel":"about 35,000 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"“Cave of the Women” in the southern Carpathians; the genome of a woman found here shows high diversity in Europe before the Ice Age maximum.","chapters":["aurignacian-art"]},{"id":"zhoukoudian-upper-cave","name":"Zhoukoudian Upper Cave","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":39.69,"lon":115.93,"start":35000,"end":30000,"dateLabel":"roughly 35,000–30,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"Cave at the top of the Zhoukoudian hill with human remains, pierced-tooth and shell ornaments and a bone needle; the fossils were lost in 1941.","chapters":["north-asia"]},{"id":"sakitari-cave","name":"Sakitari Cave","country":"Japan","region":"asia","lat":26.14,"lon":127.75,"start":35000,"end":20000,"dateLabel":"about 35,000–20,000 years ago; fishhooks about 23,000","type":"tools","summary":"Cave in southern Okinawa with shell fishhooks about 23,000 years old and evidence of seasonal crab meals.","chapters":["north-asia"]},{"id":"sungir","name":"Sungir","country":"Russia","region":"europe","lat":56.17,"lon":40.5,"start":35000,"end":34000,"dateLabel":"about 34,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"Open-air site near Vladimir with spectacular burials of a man and two children, adorned with some 13,000 ivory beads.","chapters":["gravettian-lgm","epilogue"]},{"id":"gorhams-cave","name":"Gorham’s Cave","country":"Gibraltar","region":"europe","lat":36.12,"lon":-5.34,"start":33000,"end":28000,"dateLabel":"claimed Mousterian ages of about 28,000 radiocarbon years (disputed)","type":"tools","summary":"Sea cave on Gibraltar’s east face, long occupied by Neanderthals. Claims of Neanderthal survival here until about 28,000 radiocarbon years ago remain contested.","chapters":["neanderthal-end"]},{"id":"chiquihuite-cave","name":"Chiquihuite Cave","country":"Mexico","region":"americas","lat":24.64,"lon":-101.4,"start":33000,"end":13000,"dateLabel":"about 33,000–13,000 years ago (claimed)","type":"tools","summary":"High mountain cave with nearly 2,000 claimed stone artefacts in layers from about 33,000 years ago; many archaeologists doubt they are human-made.","chapters":["americas-peopling"]},{"id":"yana-rhs","name":"Yana RHS","country":"Russia","region":"asia","lat":70.72,"lon":135.42,"start":32000,"end":32000,"dateLabel":"about 32,000 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"Arctic site on the Yana River with rhinoceros-horn and ivory foreshafts; milk teeth from here define the Ancient North Siberians.","chapters":["north-asia"]},{"id":"dolni-vestonice","name":"Dolní Věstonice and Pavlov","country":"Czech Republic","region":"europe","lat":48.88,"lon":16.65,"start":32000,"end":29000,"dateLabel":"about 32,000–29,000 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"Gravettian camps below the Pavlov Hills with the fired-clay Věstonice Venus, textile impressions and a triple burial.","chapters":["gravettian-lgm"]},{"id":"predmosti","name":"Předmostí u Přerova","country":"Czech Republic","region":"europe","lat":49.47,"lon":17.44,"start":31000,"end":27000,"dateLabel":"about 31,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"Gravettian mammoth-hunters’ site with canid skulls proposed as early dogs, one with a bone fragment wedged between its teeth. The identification is widely disputed.","chapters":["gravettian-lgm","dogs"]},{"id":"krems-wachtberg","name":"Krems-Wachtberg","country":"Austria","region":"europe","lat":48.41,"lon":15.6,"start":31000,"end":30000,"dateLabel":"about 30,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"Gravettian camp with a double infant burial beneath a mammoth scapula; ancient DNA showed the babies were identical twins.","chapters":["gravettian-lgm"]},{"id":"willendorf","name":"Willendorf","country":"Austria","region":"europe","lat":48.32,"lon":15.4,"start":30000,"end":30000,"dateLabel":"about 30,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Wachau site of the Venus of Willendorf, carved from oolitic limestone whose closest match lies near Lake Garda in Italy.","chapters":["gravettian-lgm"]},{"id":"coa-valley","name":"Côa Valley and Siega Verde","country":"Portugal and Spain","region":"europe","lat":41.07,"lon":-7.11,"start":30000,"end":12000,"dateLabel":"about 30,000–12,000 years ago and later","type":"art","summary":"Thousands of animals engraved on open-air rock outcrops, saved from a dam in 1995 and listed as World Heritage in 1998 (Siega Verde added in 2010).","chapters":["magdalenian"]},{"id":"apollo-11-cave","name":"Apollo 11 Cave","country":"Namibia","region":"africa","lat":-27.75,"lon":17.1,"start":30000,"end":26000,"dateLabel":"around 30,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Rock shelter named after the 1969 Moon landing, with painted stone slabs that are the oldest dated figurative art in Africa.","chapters":["african-lsa"]},{"id":"arene-candide","name":"Arene Candide","country":"Italy","region":"europe","lat":44.16,"lon":8.33,"start":28000,"end":28000,"dateLabel":"about 28,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"Ligurian coastal cave with the richly equipped burial of ‘Il Principe’, a teenage youth with a shell cap and elk-antler batons.","chapters":["gravettian-lgm"]},{"id":"shiraho-saonetabaru","name":"Shiraho-Saonetabaru Cave","country":"Japan","region":"asia","lat":24.4,"lon":124.24,"start":27000,"end":27000,"dateLabel":"about 27,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"Cave on Ishigaki with remains of many Palaeolithic individuals, including a near-complete flexed skeleton about 27,000 years old.","chapters":["north-asia"]},{"id":"laussel","name":"Laussel","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":44.93,"lon":1.12,"start":27000,"end":25000,"dateLabel":"about 27,000–25,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Rock shelter in the Dordogne with a limestone relief of a woman holding a horn, originally coloured with red ochre.","chapters":["gravettian-lgm"]},{"id":"santa-elina","name":"Santa Elina","country":"Brazil","region":"americas","lat":-15.45,"lon":-56.77,"start":27000,"end":10000,"dateLabel":"about 27,000 years ago (claimed)","type":"tools","summary":"Rock shelter with giant ground sloth bone plates that appear to have been worked into ornaments about 27,000 years ago.","chapters":["americas-peopling"]},{"id":"brassempouy","name":"Brassempouy (Grotte du Pape)","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":43.63,"lon":-0.7,"start":26000,"end":26000,"dateLabel":"about 26,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Cave in the Landes that yielded the ‘Lady with the Hood’, a tiny ivory head with a grid-patterned headdress or hairstyle.","chapters":["gravettian-lgm"]},{"id":"lespugue","name":"Lespugue","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":43.23,"lon":0.66,"start":26000,"end":26000,"dateLabel":"about 26,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Pyrenean cave where one of the most stylised Gravettian ivory figures of a woman was found in 1922.","chapters":["gravettian-lgm"]},{"id":"solutre","name":"Roche de Solutré","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":46.3,"lon":4.72,"start":25000,"end":20000,"dateLabel":"Solutrean about 25,000–20,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Limestone escarpment in Burgundy whose foot holds vast deposits of horse bones and gave its name to the Solutrean.","chapters":["gravettian-lgm"]},{"id":"ishango","name":"Ishango","country":"Democratic Republic of the Congo","region":"africa","lat":-0.13,"lon":29.6,"start":25000,"end":20000,"dateLabel":"about 25,000–20,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Lakeside site where the notched Ishango bone was found in 1950, often claimed as an early mathematical or calendrical object.","chapters":["african-lsa"]},{"id":"malta","name":"Mal’ta","country":"Russia","region":"asia","lat":52.84,"lon":103.52,"start":24000,"end":24000,"dateLabel":"about 24,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"Settlement near Lake Baikal with ivory figurines of women and birds and the burial of a boy, MA-1, whose genome revealed the Ancient North Eurasians.","chapters":["north-asia"]},{"id":"bluefish-caves","name":"Bluefish Caves","country":"Canada (Yukon)","region":"americas","lat":67.15,"lon":-140.78,"start":24000,"end":12000,"dateLabel":"about 24,000 years ago (claimed)","type":"tools","summary":"Small caves in eastern Beringia with animal bones that may carry human cut marks about 24,000 years old.","chapters":["americas-peopling"]},{"id":"white-sands","name":"White Sands","country":"United States (New Mexico)","region":"americas","lat":32.88,"lon":-106.38,"start":23000,"end":21000,"dateLabel":"about 23,000–21,000 years ago","type":"footprints","summary":"Human footprints, mostly of teenagers and children, beside tracks of mammoths and ground sloths. Dated with seeds, pollen and luminescence; still debated.","chapters":["americas-peopling","epilogue"]},{"id":"ohalo-ii","name":"Ohalo II","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":32.72,"lon":35.56,"start":23000,"end":23000,"dateLabel":"about 23,000 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"Drowned brush-hut camp on the Sea of Galilee with grass bedding, ground wild cereals, sickle blades and about 140 plant species.","chapters":["natufians"]},{"id":"nelson-bay-cave","name":"Nelson Bay Cave","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-34.1,"lon":23.37,"start":22000,"end":12000,"dateLabel":"about 22,000–12,000 years ago (Robberg)","type":"tools","summary":"Coastal cave on the Robberg peninsula that gave its name to the Robberg bladelet industry of the late glacial period.","chapters":["african-lsa"]},{"id":"lascaux","name":"Lascaux","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":45.05,"lon":1.17,"start":21000,"end":17000,"dateLabel":"about 21,000–17,000 years ago (debated)","type":"art","summary":"Found in 1940, with some 600 paintings including the Hall of the Bulls and the enigmatic shaft scene. Closed in 1963 to protect it; visitors see replicas.","chapters":["magdalenian"]},{"id":"dogger-bank","name":"Dogger Bank (Doggerland)","country":"North Sea","region":"europe","lat":54.8,"lon":2.5,"start":20000,"end":7500,"dateLabel":"dry land until about 8,000–7,500 years ago","type":"other","summary":"Upland of the drowned landscape that joined Britain to the continent, probably among the last parts of Doggerland to disappear.","chapters":["warming-world"]},{"id":"bering-land-bridge","name":"Bering land bridge","country":"Bering Strait","region":"americas","lat":65.3,"lon":-169.5,"start":20000,"end":11000,"dateLabel":"flooded about 11,000 years ago","type":"other","summary":"The lowland that joined Asia and America until rising seas cut it about 11,000 years ago.","chapters":["warming-world"]},{"id":"sunda-shelf","name":"Sunda Shelf","country":"South China Sea","region":"asia","lat":4,"lon":107,"start":20000,"end":7000,"dateLabel":"largely drowned by about 7,000 years ago","type":"other","summary":"Heart of Sundaland, the lowland that joined mainland Southeast Asia to Sumatra, Java and Borneo.","chapters":["warming-world"]},{"id":"bass-strait","name":"Bassian land bridge","country":"Australia","region":"oceania","lat":-40,"lon":146,"start":20000,"end":12000,"dateLabel":"flooded roughly 14,000–10,000 years ago","type":"other","summary":"The land that joined Tasmania to mainland Australia before the rising sea turned Tasmania into an island.","chapters":["warming-world"]},{"id":"xianrendong","name":"Xianrendong Cave","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":28.72,"lon":117.18,"start":20000,"end":19000,"dateLabel":"c. 18,000–17,000 BCE","type":"tools","summary":"Cave in Jiangxi whose oldest pottery sherds, some scorched from cooking, come from layers dated to about 20,000 years ago, the earliest known pottery.","chapters":["pottery-east-asia"]},{"id":"murujuga","name":"Murujuga (Burrup Peninsula)","country":"Australia","region":"oceania","lat":-20.63,"lon":116.79,"start":20000,"end":200,"dateLabel":"possibly tens of thousands of years to recent","type":"art","summary":"One of the world’s densest concentrations of petroglyphs, including thylacines and faces; inscribed as a World Heritage Site in 2025.","chapters":["sahul-holocene"]},{"id":"kharaneh-iv","name":"Kharaneh IV","country":"Jordan","region":"asia","lat":31.73,"lon":36.45,"start":19800,"end":18600,"dateLabel":"about 19,800–18,600 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"Large Epipalaeolithic aggregation site in the Jordanian steppe with hut floors, burials and shell beads from two seas.","chapters":["natufians"]},{"id":"font-de-gaume","name":"Font-de-Gaume","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":44.94,"lon":1.03,"start":19000,"end":14000,"dateLabel":"Magdalenian","type":"art","summary":"Cave near Les Eyzies with dozens of polychrome bison, one of the few original painted caves still open to small groups of visitors.","chapters":["magdalenian"]},{"id":"wadi-kubbaniya","name":"Wadi Kubbaniya","country":"Egypt","region":"africa","lat":24.22,"lon":32.85,"start":19000,"end":17000,"dateLabel":"about 19,000–17,000 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"Nile-side camps with charred nut-grass tubers and fish bones; claimed early barley proved to be a later intrusion.","chapters":["african-lsa"]},{"id":"yuchanyan","name":"Yuchanyan Cave","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":25.5,"lon":111.5,"start":18300,"end":15500,"dateLabel":"c. 16,300–13,500 BCE","type":"tools","summary":"Cave in Hunan with some of the world’s oldest pottery, dated by radiocarbon on associated charcoal and bone.","chapters":["pottery-east-asia"]},{"id":"les-trois-freres","name":"Les Trois-Frères","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":43.03,"lon":1.21,"start":18000,"end":14000,"dateLabel":"Magdalenian","type":"art","summary":"Cave explored in 1914 by the three Bégouën brothers, famous for the antlered “Sorcerer” figure and hundreds of engraved animals.","chapters":["magdalenian"]},{"id":"tuc-daudoubert","name":"Le Tuc d’Audoubert","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":43.02,"lon":1.2,"start":18000,"end":14000,"dateLabel":"Magdalenian","type":"art","summary":"Cave in the same system as Les Trois-Frères, where two bison modelled in clay were found in 1912, deep underground.","chapters":["magdalenian"]},{"id":"jebel-sahaba","name":"Jebel Sahaba (Cemetery 117)","country":"Sudan","region":"africa","lat":21.98,"lon":31.33,"start":18000,"end":13400,"dateLabel":"more than 13,400 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"Cemetery of 61 people, many with projectile wounds. A 2021 re-analysis showed repeated violence rather than a single battle.","chapters":["african-lsa"]},{"id":"afontova-gora","name":"Afontova Gora","country":"Russia","region":"asia","lat":56.02,"lon":92.84,"start":17000,"end":17000,"dateLabel":"about 17,000 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"Sites on the Yenisei at Krasnoyarsk; the genome of an individual from here shows Ancient North Eurasian ancestry persisting after the glacial maximum.","chapters":["north-asia"]},{"id":"la-madeleine","name":"La Madeleine","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":44.97,"lon":1.03,"start":17000,"end":14000,"dateLabel":"about 17,000–14,000 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"Rock shelter in the Vézère valley that gave the Magdalenian its name in 1869, rich in carved antler, bone tools and harpoons.","chapters":["magdalenian"]},{"id":"niaux","name":"Niaux","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":42.82,"lon":1.59,"start":17000,"end":15000,"dateLabel":"about 17,000–15,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Pyrenean cave whose Salon Noir, deep inside the mountain, holds bison, horses and ibex drawn in black outline.","chapters":["magdalenian"]},{"id":"odai-yamamoto-i","name":"Odai Yamamoto I","country":"Japan","region":"asia","lat":41.05,"lon":140.63,"start":16500,"end":16000,"dateLabel":"c. 14,500 BCE","type":"tools","summary":"Site on the Tsugaru peninsula whose plain sherds carry charred residue dated to about 16,500 years ago, the oldest pottery in Japan.","chapters":["pottery-east-asia"]},{"id":"gonnersdorf","name":"Gönnersdorf","country":"Germany","region":"europe","lat":50.45,"lon":7.43,"start":16000,"end":15000,"dateLabel":"about 16,000–15,000 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"Late Magdalenian camp on the Rhine whose slate floor slabs are engraved with animals and stylised female figures.","chapters":["magdalenian"]},{"id":"mas-dazil","name":"Le Mas d’Azil","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":43.07,"lon":1.36,"start":16000,"end":12000,"dateLabel":"about 16,000–12,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Huge cave through which a river flows, source of a spear-thrower carved as a young ibex with birds, and namesake of the Azilian culture with its painted pebbles.","chapters":["magdalenian"]},{"id":"coopers-ferry","name":"Cooper’s Ferry","country":"United States (Idaho)","region":"americas","lat":45.85,"lon":-116.42,"start":16000,"end":15000,"dateLabel":"about 16,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Pits and stemmed points about 16,000 years old on the Salmon River, among the oldest accepted evidence of people in North America.","chapters":["americas-peopling"]},{"id":"gasya","name":"Gasya","country":"Russia","region":"asia","lat":48.74,"lon":135.63,"start":16000,"end":13000,"dateLabel":"c. 14,000–11,000 BCE","type":"tools","summary":"Amur River site with some of the earliest pottery of the Russian Far East, made by Late Glacial fisher-hunters.","chapters":["pottery-east-asia"]},{"id":"pinarbasi","name":"Pınarbaşı","country":"Türkiye","region":"asia","lat":37.49,"lon":33.04,"start":15800,"end":15800,"dateLabel":"about 15,800 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"Hunter-gatherer rock shelter in central Anatolia whose buried pup is the oldest genetically confirmed dog (2026). The dogs there seem to have been fed fish.","chapters":["dogs"]},{"id":"friedkin-site","name":"Friedkin site (Buttermilk Creek)","country":"United States (Texas)","region":"americas","lat":30.89,"lon":-97.73,"start":15500,"end":13200,"dateLabel":"about 15,500–13,200 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Thousands of stone artefacts in layers beneath Clovis, dated by luminescence.","chapters":["americas-peopling"]},{"id":"ain-mallaha","name":"ʿAin Mallaha (Eynan)","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":33.08,"lon":35.58,"start":15000,"end":11700,"dateLabel":"about 12,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"Natufian village where a woman was buried with her hand resting on a puppy, one of the earliest signs of an emotional bond between people and dogs.","chapters":["dogs","natufians"]},{"id":"creswell-crags","name":"Creswell Crags","country":"United Kingdom","region":"europe","lat":53.26,"lon":-1.19,"start":15000,"end":12800,"dateLabel":"at least 12,800 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Limestone gorge with Britain’s only known Ice Age cave art, engravings in Church Hole recognised in 2003.","chapters":["magdalenian"]},{"id":"el-wad","name":"el-Wad","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":32.67,"lon":34.97,"start":15000,"end":12000,"dateLabel":"about 15,000–12,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"Mount Carmel cave and terrace where Dorothy Garrod found Natufian burials with dentalium-shell headdresses.","chapters":["natufians"]},{"id":"hayonim-cave","name":"Hayonim Cave","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":32.93,"lon":35.22,"start":15000,"end":12000,"dateLabel":"about 15,000–12,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"Galilee cave with Natufian round rooms and a grave in which people were buried with two dogs.","chapters":["natufians"]},{"id":"goughs-cave","name":"Gough’s Cave","country":"United Kingdom","region":"europe","lat":51.28,"lon":-2.76,"start":14800,"end":10000,"dateLabel":"about 14,300 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"Magdalenian cave in Cheddar Gorge whose dog is genetically almost identical to the Anatolian dogs of Pınarbaşı, showing how quickly dogs spread across western Eurasia.","chapters":["dogs","magdalenian","mesolithic-europe"]},{"id":"shubayqa-1","name":"Shubayqa 1","country":"Jordan","region":"asia","lat":32.47,"lon":37.72,"start":14600,"end":14400,"dateLabel":"about 14,400 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"Natufian site in the basalt desert whose hearth held charred bread-like remains, the oldest known bread.","chapters":["natufians"]},{"id":"page-ladson","name":"Page-Ladson","country":"United States (Florida)","region":"americas","lat":30.18,"lon":-83.93,"start":14550,"end":14550,"dateLabel":"about 14,550 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Underwater sinkhole site with a stone knife and a cut-marked mastodon tusk.","chapters":["americas-peopling"]},{"id":"monte-verde","name":"Monte Verde","country":"Chile","region":"americas","lat":-41.5,"lon":-73.2,"start":14500,"end":14500,"dateLabel":"about 14,500 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"Waterlogged campsite in southern Chile whose radiocarbon dates showed that people had reached South America before the Clovis culture, preserving wood, plants and even a footprint.","chapters":["the-longest-age","americas-peopling"]},{"id":"paisley-caves","name":"Paisley Caves","country":"United States (Oregon)","region":"americas","lat":42.76,"lon":-120.54,"start":14300,"end":12000,"dateLabel":"about 14,300 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"Caves with human coprolites containing human DNA and stemmed points, older than Clovis.","chapters":["americas-peopling"]},{"id":"kesslerloch","name":"Kesslerloch","country":"Switzerland","region":"europe","lat":47.74,"lon":8.71,"start":14200,"end":14200,"dateLabel":"about 14,200 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"Magdalenian cave whose long-suspected dog was confirmed genetically in 2026. Its ancestry traces to the same eastern wolf source as all other dogs.","chapters":["dogs"]},{"id":"bonn-oberkassel","name":"Bonn-Oberkassel","country":"Germany","region":"europe","lat":50.72,"lon":7.16,"start":14200,"end":14200,"dateLabel":"about 14,200 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"Double human burial with two dogs, found in 1914. One puppy survived severe distemper for weeks, which implies that people nursed it.","chapters":["dogs"]},{"id":"raqefet-cave","name":"Raqefet Cave","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":32.64,"lon":35.07,"start":13700,"end":11700,"dateLabel":"about 13,700–11,700 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"Natufian cemetery with flower-lined graves, mortars proposed as beer-brewing vessels, and the source of the first Natufian genomes.","chapters":["natufians"]},{"id":"abu-hureyra","name":"Abu Hureyra","country":"Syria","region":"asia","lat":35.87,"lon":38.4,"start":13400,"end":12000,"dateLabel":"Epipalaeolithic village from about 13,400 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"Euphrates village, now under Lake Assad, with rich plant remains, a contested claim of early rye cultivation and a disputed airburst claim.","chapters":["natufians"]},{"id":"blackwater-draw","name":"Blackwater Draw","country":"United States (New Mexico)","region":"americas","lat":34.28,"lon":-103.33,"start":13050,"end":12750,"dateLabel":"about 13,050–12,750 years ago (Clovis)","type":"tools","summary":"Type site of the Clovis culture, where fluted points were found with mammoth bones in the 1930s.","chapters":["americas-peopling"]},{"id":"lake-agassiz","name":"Glacial Lake Agassiz","country":"Canada and United States","region":"americas","lat":49.9,"lon":-97.14,"start":13000,"end":8200,"dateLabel":"about 13,000–8,200 years ago","type":"other","summary":"Vast meltwater lake at the edge of the Laurentide ice sheet whose final drainage is linked to the cold event of 8,200 years ago.","chapters":["warming-world"]},{"id":"franchthi-cave","name":"Franchthi Cave","country":"Greece","region":"europe","lat":37.42,"lon":23.13,"start":13000,"end":5000,"dateLabel":"c. 6800–3000 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Cave with a long hunter-gatherer sequence, including Melian obsidian, where domestic plants and animals appear around 6800 BCE.","chapters":["warming-world","neolithic-europe"]},{"id":"swan-point","name":"Swan Point","country":"United States (Alaska)","region":"americas","lat":64.29,"lon":-146.1,"start":12900,"end":11300,"dateLabel":"about 12,900–11,300 years ago","type":"other","summary":"A canid that ate salmon, probably provided by people. It matches no known dog lineage and may have been a tamed wolf.","chapters":["dogs"]},{"id":"folsom-site","name":"Folsom site","country":"United States (New Mexico)","region":"americas","lat":36.86,"lon":-104.03,"start":12700,"end":12200,"dateLabel":"about 12,700–12,200 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"Bison kill site where a point found between the ribs of an extinct bison in 1927 proved that people lived in the Americas during the Ice Age.","chapters":["americas-peopling"]},{"id":"gwion-gwion-kimberley","name":"Gwion Gwion paintings, north Kimberley","country":"Australia","region":"oceania","lat":-15.1,"lon":126.7,"start":12500,"end":11500,"dateLabel":"about 12,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Elegant painted human figures dated by radiocarbon on mud-wasp nests lying over and under the paint.","chapters":["sahul-holocene"]},{"id":"kortik-tepe","name":"Körtik Tepe","country":"Türkiye","region":"asia","lat":37.81,"lon":40.98,"start":12400,"end":11500,"dateLabel":"c. 10,400–9500 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Early village on the upper Tigris with hundreds of burials accompanied by decorated stone vessels, showing the local hunter-gatherer roots of the symbolic world seen at Göbekli Tepe.","chapters":["gobekli-tepe"]},{"id":"shigir","name":"Shigir peat bog","country":"Russia","region":"europe","lat":57.4,"lon":60.1,"start":12100,"end":11600,"dateLabel":"c. 10,100–9600 BCE","type":"art","summary":"Findspot of the Shigir idol, a carved larch sculpture about 12,000 years old and the oldest known wooden sculpture.","chapters":["mesolithic-europe"]},{"id":"st-paul-island","name":"St Paul Island","country":"United States (Alaska)","region":"americas","lat":57.15,"lon":-170.25,"start":12000,"end":5600,"dateLabel":"mammoths until about 5,600 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Bering Sea island where mammoths survived until about 5,600 years ago, probably until fresh water ran short.","chapters":["warming-world"]},{"id":"hilazon-tachtit","name":"Hilazon Tachtit","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":32.92,"lon":35.26,"start":12000,"end":12000,"dateLabel":"about 12,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"Small cave with the burial of a woman interpreted as a shaman, accompanied by 50 tortoise shells and animal parts, and remains of a feast.","chapters":["natufians"]},{"id":"gosan-ri","name":"Gosan-ri","country":"South Korea","region":"asia","lat":33.3,"lon":126.17,"start":12000,"end":11000,"dateLabel":"c. 10,000 BCE","type":"tools","summary":"Site on Jeju Island with Korea’s oldest known pottery, a forerunner of the Chulmun comb-pattern tradition.","chapters":["pottery-east-asia"]},{"id":"northgrip","name":"NorthGRIP ice core","country":"Greenland (Denmark)","region":"americas","lat":75.1,"lon":-42.32,"start":11700,"end":11700,"dateLabel":"11,700 years ago","type":"other","summary":"Deep ice core from central Greenland in which the formal boundary between the Pleistocene and the Holocene is defined.","chapters":["warming-world"]},{"id":"gobekli-tepe","name":"Göbekli Tepe","country":"Türkiye","region":"asia","lat":37.22,"lon":38.92,"start":11600,"end":10000,"dateLabel":"c. 9600–8000 BCE","type":"monument","summary":"Mound with monumental enclosures of carved T-shaped pillars up to 5.5 m high, built by hunter-gatherers. Recent work has added cisterns, domestic buildings, thousands of grinding tools, grooved skulls and a painted boar statue.","chapters":["gobekli-tepe","epilogue"]},{"id":"tell-es-sultan","name":"Tell es-Sultan (Jericho)","country":"Palestine","region":"asia","lat":31.87,"lon":35.44,"start":11600,"end":9000,"dateLabel":"c. 9600–7000 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Spring-fed mound with a PPNA wall and an 8.5 m stone tower, followed by PPNB houses with plastered skulls; a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2023.","chapters":["first-farmers"]},{"id":"upward-sun-river","name":"Upward Sun River","country":"United States (Alaska)","region":"americas","lat":64.3,"lon":-146.55,"start":11500,"end":11500,"dateLabel":"about 11,500 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"Burial of an infant girl whose genome revealed the Ancient Beringians, a previously unknown population.","chapters":["americas-peopling"]},{"id":"lepenski-vir","name":"Lepenski Vir","country":"Serbia","region":"europe","lat":44.56,"lon":22.03,"start":11500,"end":7500,"dateLabel":"c. 9500–5500 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Danube settlement of trapezoidal houses and fish-faced boulder sculptures, where hunter-fishers met and mixed with the first Balkan farmers.","chapters":["mesolithic-europe"]},{"id":"dhra","name":"Dhraʿ","country":"Jordan","region":"asia","lat":31.25,"lon":35.55,"start":11500,"end":11100,"dateLabel":"c. 9500–9100 BCE","type":"farming","summary":"PPNA village near the Dead Sea with purpose-built granaries on raised floors, storing wild barley before domestication.","chapters":["first-farmers"]},{"id":"karahan-tepe","name":"Karahan Tepe","country":"Türkiye","region":"asia","lat":37.1,"lon":39.3,"start":11400,"end":10200,"dateLabel":"c. 9400–8200 BCE","type":"monument","summary":"Taş Tepeler site with rooms carved from bedrock, including a chamber of ten phallic pillars watched by a human head emerging from the wall. Statues include a seated man 2.3 m tall and, found in 2026, a person riding a leopard.","chapters":["gobekli-tepe"]},{"id":"gilgal-i","name":"Gilgal I","country":"Palestine","region":"asia","lat":31.99,"lon":35.46,"start":11400,"end":11200,"dateLabel":"c. 9400–9200 BCE","type":"farming","summary":"Jordan Valley village where carbonised seedless figs were proposed as the earliest domesticated plant, a claim still debated.","chapters":["first-farmers"]},{"id":"ounjougou","name":"Ounjougou","country":"Mali","region":"africa","lat":14.63,"lon":-3.43,"start":11400,"end":11400,"dateLabel":"c. 9400 BCE","type":"tools","summary":"Valley site in the Dogon country with some of the oldest pottery in Africa, made by hunter-gatherers alongside grinding stones and small points.","chapters":["green-sahara"]},{"id":"star-carr","name":"Star Carr","country":"United Kingdom","region":"europe","lat":54.21,"lon":-0.42,"start":11300,"end":10500,"dateLabel":"c. 9300–8500 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Lakeside site with timber platforms, a post-built structure claimed as Britain’s oldest house, barbed points, an engraved pendant and 33 antler headdresses.","chapters":["mesolithic-europe"]},{"id":"ain-sakhri","name":"ʿAin Sakhri","country":"Palestine (West Bank)","region":"asia","lat":31.68,"lon":35.25,"start":11000,"end":11000,"dateLabel":"about 11,000 years ago","type":"art","summary":"Cave near Bethlehem that yielded the ʿAin Sakhri lovers figurine, a carved pebble showing a couple embracing.","chapters":["natufians"]},{"id":"blatterhohle","name":"Blätterhöhle","country":"Germany","region":"europe","lat":51.36,"lon":7.5,"start":11000,"end":5000,"dateLabel":"c. 9000–3000 BCE","type":"dna","summary":"Cave where fish-eating hunter-gatherers and farmers were buried side by side for centuries, evidence of parallel societies.","chapters":["mesolithic-europe"]},{"id":"nabta-playa","name":"Nabta Playa","country":"Egypt","region":"africa","lat":22.51,"lon":30.73,"start":11000,"end":5500,"dateLabel":"c. 9000–3500 BCE","type":"monument","summary":"Seasonal lake basin that herders turned into a ceremonial centre with cattle burials, megalith lines and a small stone circle.","chapters":["green-sahara"]},{"id":"shangshan","name":"Shangshan","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":29.46,"lon":119.96,"start":11000,"end":8500,"dateLabel":"c. 9000–6500 BCE","type":"farming","summary":"Early pottery tempered with rice husks, rice phytoliths and residues of a fermented rice drink make Shangshan the key site in the debate over when rice cultivation began.","chapters":["china-neolithic"]},{"id":"nevali-cori","name":"Nevalı Çori","country":"Türkiye","region":"asia","lat":37.52,"lon":38.6,"start":10800,"end":10000,"dateLabel":"9th millennium BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Early PPNB village where T-shaped pillars and sculptures were first recognised, in a special building among rectangular houses. Excavated 1983–1991 by Harald Hauptmann, it now lies beneath the Atatürk Dam reservoir.","chapters":["gobekli-tepe"]},{"id":"sayburc","name":"Sayburç","country":"Türkiye","region":"asia","lat":37.11,"lon":38.62,"start":10700,"end":10300,"dateLabel":"mid-9th millennium BCE","type":"art","summary":"Village with a rock-cut communal building whose bench bears reliefs of a man between two leopards and a man facing a bull, published in 2022 as one of the oldest narrative scenes known.","chapters":["gobekli-tepe"]},{"id":"nataruk","name":"Nataruk","country":"Kenya","region":"africa","lat":3.35,"lon":35.75,"start":10500,"end":9500,"dateLabel":"c. 8500–7500 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Remains of 27 hunter-gatherers, many with lethal wounds, interpreted as a massacre, an interpretation some dispute.","chapters":["green-sahara"]},{"id":"shillourokambos","name":"Shillourokambos","country":"Cyprus","region":"asia","lat":34.73,"lon":33.17,"start":10400,"end":9000,"dateLabel":"c. 8400–7000 BCE","type":"farming","summary":"Early farming village on Cyprus to which settlers ferried cattle, sheep, goats, pigs and deer, and where a cat was buried beside a human.","chapters":["first-farmers"]},{"id":"llanos-de-mojos","name":"Llanos de Mojos forest islands","country":"Bolivia","region":"americas","lat":-14.2,"lon":-65.2,"start":10400,"end":6800,"dateLabel":"c. 8400–4800 BCE","type":"farming","summary":"Human-made forest islands in the Amazonian savanna with evidence of manioc and squash cultivation from about 10,350 years ago.","chapters":["americas-holocene"]},{"id":"asikli-hoyuk","name":"Aşıklı Höyük","country":"Türkiye","region":"asia","lat":38.35,"lon":34.23,"start":10350,"end":9300,"dateLabel":"c. 8350–7300 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Densely packed mudbrick village in Cappadocia whose dung and urine-salt layers record the early penning and management of sheep and goats.","chapters":["first-farmers"]},{"id":"cishan","name":"Cishan","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":36.57,"lon":114.11,"start":10300,"end":8700,"dateLabel":"c. 8300–6700 BCE","type":"farming","summary":"Storage pits held broomcorn millet, identified by husk phytoliths and biomolecular markers and dated to about 10,300–8,700 years ago. Foxtail millet appears only after about 8,700 years ago.","chapters":["china-neolithic"]},{"id":"cayonu","name":"Çayönü","country":"Türkiye","region":"asia","lat":38.22,"lon":39.73,"start":10200,"end":9000,"dateLabel":"c. 8200–7000 BCE","type":"metal","summary":"Early farming village where native copper and malachite were hammered and annealed into beads and tools.","chapters":["copper-age"]},{"id":"koster","name":"Koster and Stilwell II","country":"United States (Illinois)","region":"americas","lat":39.25,"lon":-90.55,"start":10000,"end":3000,"dateLabel":"about 10,000 years ago","type":"burial","summary":"Individually buried dogs, directly radiocarbon dated in 2019, which are the oldest confirmed dogs in the Americas.","chapters":["dogs","americas-holocene"]},{"id":"wrangel-island","name":"Wrangel Island","country":"Russia","region":"asia","lat":71.23,"lon":-179.42,"start":10000,"end":4000,"dateLabel":"about 10,000–4,000 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Arctic island where the last woolly mammoths lived until about 4,000 years ago.","chapters":["warming-world"]},{"id":"pesse","name":"Pesse","country":"Netherlands","region":"europe","lat":52.78,"lon":6.46,"start":10000,"end":9500,"dateLabel":"c. 8000–7500 BCE","type":"other","summary":"Findspot of the Pesse canoe, a Scots pine dugout about 3 m long and the oldest known boat in the world.","chapters":["mesolithic-europe"]},{"id":"takarkori","name":"Takarkori rock shelter","country":"Libya","region":"africa","lat":24.4,"lon":10.35,"start":10000,"end":5000,"dateLabel":"c. 8000–3000 BCE","type":"dna","summary":"Rock shelter in the Tadrart Acacus with early milk residues in pottery and two naturally mummified women whose genomes revealed an isolated North African lineage.","chapters":["green-sahara"]},{"id":"bhimbetka","name":"Bhimbetka rock shelters","country":"India","region":"asia","lat":22.94,"lon":77.61,"start":10000,"end":1000,"dateLabel":"paintings from perhaps 10,000 years ago into historical times","type":"art","summary":"Hundreds of sandstone shelters with paintings of animals, hunts and dances, dated mostly by style; a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2003.","chapters":["south-asia"]},{"id":"sarai-nahar-rai","name":"Sarai Nahar Rai","country":"India","region":"asia","lat":25.8,"lon":81.85,"start":10000,"end":7000,"dateLabel":"c. 8000–5000 BCE (dates debated)","type":"burial","summary":"Mesolithic lakeside camp and cemetery on the Ganga plain; one of several with robust hunter-gatherer burials, including one reported to have a microlith lodged among its ribs.","chapters":["south-asia"]},{"id":"kuk-swamp","name":"Kuk Swamp","country":"Papua New Guinea","region":"oceania","lat":-5.78,"lon":144.33,"start":10000,"end":4000,"dateLabel":"c. 8000–2000 BCE","type":"farming","summary":"Highland wetland with pits, planting mounds and drainage ditches showing independent invention of agriculture with bananas, taro and yams. UNESCO World Heritage since 2008.","chapters":["sahul-holocene"]},{"id":"guila-naquitz","name":"Guilá Naquitz","country":"Mexico","region":"americas","lat":16.94,"lon":-96.37,"start":10000,"end":6200,"dateLabel":"c. 8000–4200 BCE","type":"farming","summary":"Oaxaca cave with domesticated squash seeds about 10,000 years old and some of the earliest maize cobs, about 6,250 years old.","chapters":["americas-holocene"]},{"id":"gobero","name":"Gobero","country":"Niger","region":"africa","lat":16.94,"lon":9.38,"start":9700,"end":4500,"dateLabel":"c. 7700–2500 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Lakeside cemeteries of Kiffian fisher-hunters and later Tenerian herders, including a woman and two children buried in an embrace.","chapters":["green-sahara"]},{"id":"zhokhov-island","name":"Zhokhov Island","country":"Russia","region":"asia","lat":76.15,"lon":152.83,"start":9500,"end":9500,"dateLabel":"about 9,500 years ago","type":"settlement","summary":"Arctic hunting camp with sledge parts and dog bones. A Zhokhov dog genome is closely related to modern sled dogs.","chapters":["dogs"]},{"id":"keweenaw-copper","name":"Keweenaw Peninsula native copper","country":"United States","region":"americas","lat":47.25,"lon":-88.4,"start":9500,"end":3000,"dateLabel":"from about 9,500 years ago","type":"metal","summary":"Source area of the native copper hammered into tools by the Old Copper Complex of the Great Lakes, one of the oldest metalworking traditions in the world.","chapters":["americas-holocene"]},{"id":"ain-ghazal","name":"ʿAin Ghazal","country":"Jordan","region":"asia","lat":31.99,"lon":35.98,"start":9250,"end":7000,"dateLabel":"c. 7250–5000 BCE","type":"art","summary":"One of the largest Neolithic villages of the Levant, known for plastered skulls and two caches of lime-plaster statues and busts.","chapters":["first-farmers"]},{"id":"catalhoyuk","name":"Çatalhöyük","country":"Türkiye","region":"asia","lat":37.67,"lon":32.83,"start":9100,"end":7950,"dateLabel":"c. 7100–5950 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Neolithic town of houses entered through the roof, with burials under floors, wall paintings and reliefs, studied by James Mellaart and Ian Hodder.","chapters":["first-farmers"]},{"id":"bosporus","name":"Bosporus","country":"Türkiye","region":"europe","lat":41.08,"lon":29.02,"start":9000,"end":7500,"dateLabel":"reconnection about 9,000–8,000 years ago","type":"other","summary":"Strait through which the Mediterranean reconnected with the Black Sea; whether as a sudden flood or gradually is still debated.","chapters":["warming-world"]},{"id":"knossos","name":"Knossos","country":"Greece","region":"europe","lat":35.3,"lon":25.16,"start":9000,"end":8500,"dateLabel":"c. 7000–6500 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Aceramic Neolithic village beneath the later palace, founded by seafaring farmers with imported crops and livestock.","chapters":["neolithic-europe"]},{"id":"wadi-sura","name":"Wadi Sura (Cave of Swimmers)","country":"Egypt","region":"africa","lat":23.62,"lon":25.23,"start":9000,"end":6000,"dateLabel":"c. 7000–4000 BCE","type":"art","summary":"Painted shelters on the Gilf Kebir, including the Cave of Swimmers and the Cave of Beasts with thousands of figures and handprints.","chapters":["green-sahara"]},{"id":"jiahu","name":"Jiahu","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":33.61,"lon":113.67,"start":9000,"end":7700,"dateLabel":"c. 7000–5700 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Village with more than thirty crane-bone flutes, residues of a fermented rice, honey and fruit drink, early rice, and signs incised on tortoise shells.","chapters":["china-neolithic"]},{"id":"mehrgarh","name":"Mehrgarh","country":"Pakistan","region":"asia","lat":29.39,"lon":67.61,"start":9000,"end":4600,"dateLabel":"c. 7000–2600 BCE","type":"farming","summary":"South Asia’s best-known early farming village: barley and wheat, local zebu domestication, mud-brick storage buildings, drilled teeth and early cotton.","chapters":["south-asia"]},{"id":"sesklo","name":"Sesklo","country":"Greece","region":"europe","lat":39.36,"lon":22.84,"start":8700,"end":6500,"dateLabel":"c. 6700–4500 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Thessalian tell that gave its name to an Early and Middle Neolithic culture of painted pottery and mudbrick houses.","chapters":["neolithic-europe"]},{"id":"xihuatoxtla","name":"Xihuatoxtla rock shelter","country":"Mexico","region":"americas","lat":18.3,"lon":-99.5,"start":8700,"end":8700,"dateLabel":"about 8,700 years ago","type":"farming","summary":"Central Balsas Valley shelter where maize starch and phytoliths on grinding tools provide the earliest direct evidence of maize.","chapters":["americas-holocene"]},{"id":"ofnet","name":"Ofnet cave","country":"Germany","region":"europe","lat":48.82,"lon":10.46,"start":8500,"end":8400,"dateLabel":"c. 6500 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Two pits of ornamented human heads, many with fatal injuries, interpreted as a massacre or as a mortuary ritual.","chapters":["mesolithic-europe"]},{"id":"liangdao","name":"Liangdao (Liang Island)","country":"Taiwan (Matsu Islands)","region":"asia","lat":26.36,"lon":120.25,"start":8300,"end":7500,"dateLabel":"c. 6300–5600 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Burials in a shell midden, the oldest about 8,300 years old, whose DNA links early coastal people of the Taiwan Strait with present-day Austronesian speakers.","chapters":["china-neolithic"]},{"id":"kuahuqiao","name":"Kuahuqiao","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":30.15,"lon":120.22,"start":8200,"end":7000,"dateLabel":"c. 6200–5000 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Waterlogged lakeside village with a pine dugout canoe about 5.6 metres long, dated to about 8,000 years ago, and a mix of wild-type and domestic-type rice.","chapters":["china-neolithic"]},{"id":"shetland-storegga","name":"Shetland tsunami deposits","country":"United Kingdom","region":"europe","lat":60.16,"lon":-1.16,"start":8150,"end":8150,"dateLabel":"about 8,150 years ago","type":"other","summary":"Sand layers left by the Storegga tsunami, which reached up to about 20 metres above the sea level of the time in Shetland.","chapters":["warming-world"]},{"id":"shum-laka","name":"Shum Laka","country":"Cameroon","region":"africa","lat":5.86,"lon":10.08,"start":8000,"end":3000,"dateLabel":"c. 6000–1000 BCE","type":"dna","summary":"Rock shelter in the Grassfields whose ancient children’s genomes relate to Central African hunter-gatherers rather than to today’s Bantu speakers.","chapters":["green-sahara"]},{"id":"rocky-cape","name":"Rocky Cape","country":"Australia (Tasmania)","region":"oceania","lat":-40.86,"lon":145.52,"start":8000,"end":1000,"dateLabel":"c. 8,000 years ago onwards","type":"settlement","summary":"Coastal caves whose deposits showed the disappearance of bone points and scale fish around 3,500 years ago, the basis of the Tasmanian debate.","chapters":["sahul-holocene"]},{"id":"windover","name":"Windover","country":"United States","region":"americas","lat":28.55,"lon":-80.87,"start":8000,"end":7000,"dateLabel":"c. 6000–5000 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Pond cemetery whose peat preserved woven fabrics and brain tissue from Early Archaic people.","chapters":["americas-holocene"]},{"id":"huaca-prieta","name":"Huaca Prieta","country":"Peru","region":"americas","lat":-7.93,"lon":-79.3,"start":7800,"end":4000,"dateLabel":"c. 5800–2000 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Coastal mound with twined cotton textiles, including fabric dyed with indigo about 6,200 years ago.","chapters":["americas-holocene"]},{"id":"almendres","name":"Almendres cromlech","country":"Portugal","region":"europe","lat":38.56,"lon":-8.06,"start":7500,"end":5000,"dateLabel":"c. 5500–3000 BCE","type":"monument","summary":"Enclosures of about 95 standing stones near Évora, possibly begun in the sixth millennium BCE and among the oldest stone circles in Europe.","chapters":["megaliths"]},{"id":"ertebolle","name":"Ertebølle","country":"Denmark","region":"europe","lat":56.8,"lon":9.18,"start":7400,"end":5950,"dateLabel":"c. 5400–3950 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Type site of the Ertebølle culture, a large shell midden on the Limfjord left by coastal hunter-gatherers who also made pottery.","chapters":["mesolithic-europe"]},{"id":"faiyum","name":"Faiyum north shore","country":"Egypt","region":"africa","lat":29.53,"lon":30.65,"start":7400,"end":6400,"dateLabel":"c. 5400–4400 BCE","type":"farming","summary":"Neolithic sites with basket-lined grain pits holding emmer and barley, among the earliest farming evidence in Egypt.","chapters":["green-sahara"]},{"id":"la-draga","name":"La Draga","country":"Spain","region":"europe","lat":42.13,"lon":2.76,"start":7300,"end":7000,"dateLabel":"c. 5300–5000 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Waterlogged early farming village on Lake Banyoles with rare wooden tools, bows and baskets.","chapters":["neolithic-europe"]},{"id":"erkelenz-kuckhoven","name":"Erkelenz-Kückhoven","country":"Germany","region":"europe","lat":51.06,"lon":6.34,"start":7100,"end":7000,"dateLabel":"c. 5100 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"LBK settlement with an oak-lined well dated by tree rings, one of the oldest examples of timber joinery known.","chapters":["neolithic-europe"]},{"id":"herxheim","name":"Herxheim","country":"Germany","region":"europe","lat":49.15,"lon":8.22,"start":7050,"end":6950,"dateLabel":"c. 5050–4950 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"LBK enclosure whose ditches held the processed remains of at least 500 people, interpreted as cannibalism, sacrifice or mortuary ritual.","chapters":["neolithic-europe"]},{"id":"vedbaek-bogebakken","name":"Vedbæk-Bøgebakken","country":"Denmark","region":"europe","lat":55.84,"lon":12.55,"start":7000,"end":7000,"dateLabel":"c. 5000 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Mesolithic cemetery where a young woman was buried with a newborn laid on a swan’s wing, among other carefully furnished graves.","chapters":["mesolithic-europe"]},{"id":"talheim","name":"Talheim","country":"Germany","region":"europe","lat":49.08,"lon":9.19,"start":7000,"end":7000,"dateLabel":"c. 5000 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Mass grave of 34 men, women and children killed with adzes and arrows at the end of the LBK.","chapters":["neolithic-europe"]},{"id":"unteruhldingen","name":"Unteruhldingen, Lake Constance","country":"Germany","region":"europe","lat":47.73,"lon":9.25,"start":7000,"end":2500,"dateLabel":"c. 5000–500 BCE (Alpine series)","type":"settlement","summary":"Part of the UNESCO series of prehistoric pile dwellings around the Alps, where waterlogged lake-shore villages are dated to the year by tree rings.","chapters":["neolithic-europe"]},{"id":"tianluoshan","name":"Tianluoshan","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":30.01,"lon":121.38,"start":7000,"end":5500,"dateLabel":"c. 5000–3500 BCE","type":"farming","summary":"Hemudu-culture village where rice spikelet bases record slow domestication, with non-shattering types rising from 27 to 39 percent over about three centuries.","chapters":["china-neolithic"]},{"id":"bagor","name":"Bagor","country":"India","region":"asia","lat":25.36,"lon":74.38,"start":7000,"end":4800,"dateLabel":"c. 5000–2800 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Microlith-using community in Rajasthan whose sheep and goat bones suggest foragers adopting herding, although whether the animals were domestic is debated.","chapters":["south-asia"]},{"id":"chinchorro-arica","name":"Chinchorro sites, Arica","country":"Chile","region":"americas","lat":-18.48,"lon":-70.32,"start":7000,"end":3500,"dateLabel":"c. 5000–1500 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Cemeteries of the Chinchorro fishers with the world’s oldest artificially mummified bodies. UNESCO World Heritage since 2021.","chapters":["americas-holocene"]},{"id":"belovode","name":"Belovode","country":"Serbia","region":"europe","lat":44.33,"lon":21.42,"start":7000,"end":6650,"dateLabel":"c. 5000–4650 BCE","type":"metal","summary":"Vinča-culture settlement with copper slag from about 5000 BCE, the oldest securely dated copper smelting.","chapters":["copper-age"]},{"id":"barnenez","name":"Barnenez","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":48.67,"lon":-3.86,"start":6800,"end":6500,"dateLabel":"c. 4800–4500 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Long stone cairn covering eleven passage graves, one of the oldest megalithic monuments in Europe.","chapters":["megaliths"]},{"id":"banpo","name":"Banpo","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":34.27,"lon":109.05,"start":6800,"end":6300,"dateLabel":"c. 4800–4300 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Ditch-enclosed Yangshao village with semi-subterranean houses, kilns, painted pottery and a cemetery, excavated in the 1950s near Xi’an.","chapters":["china-neolithic"]},{"id":"plocnik","name":"Pločnik","country":"Serbia","region":"europe","lat":43.21,"lon":21.42,"start":6800,"end":6500,"dateLabel":"c. 4800–4500 BCE","type":"metal","summary":"Vinča settlement with copper tools and a tin-bronze foil of about 4650 BCE, made from mixed copper and tin ores.","chapters":["copper-age"]},{"id":"provadia-solnitsata","name":"Provadia-Solnitsata","country":"Bulgaria","region":"europe","lat":43.17,"lon":27.43,"start":6700,"end":6200,"dateLabel":"c. 4700–4200 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Walled Copper Age settlement where brine was boiled into salt, a trade good behind the region’s wealth.","chapters":["copper-age"]},{"id":"khvalynsk","name":"Khvalynsk","country":"Russia","region":"europe","lat":52.49,"lon":48.1,"start":6700,"end":6300,"dateLabel":"c. 4700–4300 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Volga cemetery with copper ornaments from Balkan ores and horse and sheep offerings; its people carried an early form of the steppe ancestry later found in the Yamnaya.","chapters":["copper-age"]},{"id":"xishuipo","name":"Xishuipo","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":35.71,"lon":115.01,"start":6600,"end":6300,"dateLabel":"c. 4600–4300 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Yangshao-period grave M45, a man flanked by a dragon and a tiger made from clamshells, dated to roughly 6,500 years ago, though the dating is not fully settled.","chapters":["china-neolithic"]},{"id":"budj-bim","name":"Budj Bim (Tae Rak, Lake Condah)","country":"Australia","region":"oceania","lat":-38.07,"lon":141.84,"start":6600,"end":150,"dateLabel":"at least 6,600 years ago to the 19th century","type":"other","summary":"Gunditjmara channels, weirs and ponds built in a lava flow to trap and hold eels, with remains of stone huts. UNESCO World Heritage since 2019.","chapters":["sahul-holocene"]},{"id":"varna-necropolis","name":"Varna necropolis","country":"Bulgaria","region":"europe","lat":43.21,"lon":27.87,"start":6600,"end":6300,"dateLabel":"c. 4600–4300 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Cemetery of about 300 graves with the world’s oldest large assemblage of worked gold, concentrated in a few graves and symbolic burials.","chapters":["copper-age","epilogue"]},{"id":"carnac","name":"Carnac alignments","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":47.59,"lon":-3.08,"start":6500,"end":5300,"dateLabel":"c. 4500–3300 BCE","type":"monument","summary":"Some 3,000 standing stones in parallel rows stretching for kilometres, the largest concentration of menhirs in the world.","chapters":["megaliths"]},{"id":"locmariaquer","name":"Locmariaquer","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":47.57,"lon":-2.95,"start":6500,"end":6000,"dateLabel":"c. 4500–4000 BCE","type":"monument","summary":"Site of the Grand Menhir, a broken standing stone about 20 m long, and the Table des Marchand tomb built from a recycled menhir.","chapters":["megaliths"]},{"id":"nahal-mishmar","name":"Nahal Mishmar (Cave of the Treasure)","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":31.37,"lon":35.36,"start":6300,"end":5700,"dateLabel":"late 5th–early 4th millennium BCE","type":"metal","summary":"Cave hoard of more than 400 objects, including crowns and standards cast by the lost-wax method in arsenic-rich copper.","chapters":["copper-age"]},{"id":"gavrinis","name":"Gavrinis","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":47.57,"lon":-2.9,"start":6200,"end":6000,"dateLabel":"c. 4200–4000 BCE","type":"art","summary":"Island passage grave lined with richly engraved slabs; its capstone joins a broken menhir reused at Locmariaquer.","chapters":["megaliths"]},{"id":"lake-yoa","name":"Lake Yoa","country":"Chad","region":"africa","lat":19.05,"lon":20.5,"start":6000,"end":0,"dateLabel":"record of the last 6,000 years","type":"other","summary":"Saharan lake whose sediments record a gradual drying at the end of the African Humid Period.","chapters":["warming-world"]},{"id":"talianki","name":"Talianki","country":"Ukraine","region":"europe","lat":48.79,"lon":30.47,"start":6000,"end":5600,"dateLabel":"c. 4000–3600 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"The largest Trypillia megasite, covering some 300 hectares, with houses in concentric rings around an open centre.","chapters":["neolithic-europe"]},{"id":"hal-saflieni","name":"Ħal Saflieni Hypogeum","country":"Malta","region":"europe","lat":35.87,"lon":14.51,"start":6000,"end":4500,"dateLabel":"c. 4000–2500 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Underground rock-cut burial complex on three levels, with red ochre spirals and the remains of perhaps 7,000 people.","chapters":["megaliths"]},{"id":"timna","name":"Timna Valley","country":"Israel","region":"asia","lat":29.78,"lon":34.99,"start":6000,"end":4500,"dateLabel":"from the 5th–4th millennium BCE","type":"metal","summary":"Copper mining and smelting landscape of the southern Arava, worked from the Chalcolithic and, on a large scale, much later.","chapters":["copper-age"]},{"id":"uruk","name":"Uruk","country":"Iraq","region":"asia","lat":31.32,"lon":45.64,"start":6000,"end":5000,"dateLabel":"c. 4000–3000 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"One of the first cities, with the Eanna temple precinct and the earliest proto-cuneiform tablets, from about 3300 BCE.","chapters":["copper-age"]},{"id":"sannai-maruyama","name":"Sannai-Maruyama","country":"Japan","region":"asia","lat":40.81,"lon":140.7,"start":5900,"end":4200,"dateLabel":"c. 3900–2200 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Large, long-lived Jōmon village with hundreds of pit houses, a six-post chestnut structure, cemeteries along a road and evidence of managed chestnut groves.","chapters":["pottery-east-asia"]},{"id":"krzemionki","name":"Krzemionki","country":"Poland","region":"europe","lat":50.97,"lon":21.49,"start":5900,"end":3600,"dateLabel":"c. 3900–1600 BCE","type":"tools","summary":"Striped-flint mining landscape used from the Neolithic well into the Bronze Age. UNESCO World Heritage since 2019.","chapters":["dawn-of-bronze"]},{"id":"dolmen-de-menga","name":"Dolmen de Menga","country":"Spain","region":"europe","lat":37.02,"lon":-4.55,"start":5800,"end":5600,"dateLabel":"c. 3800–3600 BCE","type":"monument","summary":"Massive gallery dolmen at Antequera roofed with capstones of up to about 150 tonnes, oriented toward a mountain rather than the sun.","chapters":["megaliths"]},{"id":"syltholm","name":"Syltholm, Lolland","country":"Denmark","region":"europe","lat":54.67,"lon":11.42,"start":5700,"end":5700,"dateLabel":"c. 3700 BCE","type":"dna","summary":"Site where chewed birch pitch yielded the complete genome of a hunter-gatherer woman, with traces of her last meals and her oral microbes.","chapters":["mesolithic-europe"]},{"id":"harappa","name":"Harappa","country":"Pakistan","region":"asia","lat":30.63,"lon":72.87,"start":5700,"end":4600,"dateLabel":"c. 3700–2600 BCE (early phases)","type":"settlement","summary":"Ravi and Kot Diji phase village beneath the later city, with bead workshops and potsherds from about 3300 BCE bearing marks like later Indus signs.","chapters":["south-asia"]},{"id":"maykop-kurgan","name":"Maykop kurgan","country":"Russia","region":"europe","lat":44.61,"lon":40.1,"start":5700,"end":5000,"dateLabel":"c. 3700–3000 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Rich North Caucasian burial mound, excavated in 1897, with gold and silver bull figures, precious vessels and arsenical copper.","chapters":["copper-age"]},{"id":"ggantija","name":"Ġgantija","country":"Malta","region":"europe","lat":36.05,"lon":14.27,"start":5600,"end":4500,"dateLabel":"c. 3600–2500 BCE","type":"monument","summary":"Two lobed limestone temples on Gozo inside a wall of huge blocks, among the oldest free-standing stone buildings in the world.","chapters":["megaliths"]},{"id":"niuheliang","name":"Niuheliang","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":41.33,"lon":119.51,"start":5500,"end":5000,"dateLabel":"c. 3500–3000 BCE","type":"monument","summary":"Hongshan ritual complex of stone cairn tombs, altars and the “Goddess Temple”, with a clay head with jade eyes and jade pig-dragons; no ordinary settlement has been found.","chapters":["china-neolithic"]},{"id":"watson-brake","name":"Watson Brake","country":"United States","region":"americas","lat":32.37,"lon":-92.13,"start":5500,"end":4800,"dateLabel":"c. 3500–2800 BCE","type":"monument","summary":"Eleven earthen mounds joined by ridges in an oval, built by hunter-gatherers; the oldest known mound complex in North America.","chapters":["americas-holocene"]},{"id":"otzi","name":"Tisenjoch (Ötzi)","country":"Italy","region":"europe","lat":46.78,"lon":10.84,"start":5350,"end":5120,"dateLabel":"c. 3350–3120 BCE","type":"other","summary":"Find-spot of the Iceman, a naturally mummified man with a copper axe, bow, tattoos and a genome dominated by Anatolian farmer ancestry.","chapters":["copper-age"]},{"id":"ness-of-brodgar","name":"Ness of Brodgar","country":"United Kingdom","region":"europe","lat":58.99,"lon":-3.21,"start":5300,"end":4400,"dateLabel":"c. 3300–2400 BCE","type":"monument","summary":"Complex of monumental stone buildings with painted walls and slate roofs between the Ring of Brodgar and the Stones of Stenness.","chapters":["megaliths"]},{"id":"liangzhu","name":"Liangzhu","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":30.4,"lon":119.99,"start":5300,"end":4300,"dateLabel":"c. 3300–2300 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Walled city of about 300 hectares with a palace platform, jade-rich elite cemeteries and eleven dams built about 5,100–4,900 years ago. UNESCO World Heritage since 2019.","chapters":["china-neolithic"]},{"id":"newgrange","name":"Newgrange","country":"Ireland","region":"europe","lat":53.69,"lon":-6.48,"start":5200,"end":5200,"dateLabel":"c. 3200 BCE","type":"monument","summary":"Great passage tomb whose roof box admits the winter-solstice sunrise; a genome from its chamber sparked debate about a Neolithic dynastic elite.","chapters":["megaliths"]},{"id":"ljubljana-marshes-wheel","name":"Ljubljana Marshes wheel (Stare gmajne)","country":"Slovenia","region":"europe","lat":45.97,"lon":14.33,"start":5150,"end":5150,"dateLabel":"c. 3150 BCE","type":"other","summary":"Pile-dwelling site where the oldest surviving wooden wheel, made of ash with an oak axle, was found in 2002.","chapters":["copper-age"]},{"id":"skara-brae","name":"Skara Brae","country":"United Kingdom","region":"europe","lat":59.05,"lon":-3.34,"start":5100,"end":4500,"dateLabel":"c. 3100–2500 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Neolithic village of stone houses with built-in dressers and beds, linked by covered passages, revealed by a storm in 1850.","chapters":["megaliths"]},{"id":"stonehenge","name":"Stonehenge","country":"United Kingdom","region":"europe","lat":51.18,"lon":-1.83,"start":5000,"end":3500,"dateLabel":"c. 3000–1500 BCE","type":"monument","summary":"Solstice-aligned monument of local sarsens, Welsh bluestones and a Scottish Altar Stone, built in stages over 1,500 years; nearby Durrington Walls hosted great feasts.","chapters":["megaliths"]},{"id":"lothagam-north","name":"Lothagam North Pillar Site","country":"Kenya","region":"africa","lat":2.93,"lon":36.05,"start":5000,"end":4300,"dateLabel":"c. 3000–2300 BCE","type":"monument","summary":"Monumental communal cemetery of eastern Africa’s first herders, with pillars, stone circles and several hundred burials without signs of rank.","chapters":["green-sahara"]},{"id":"burzahom","name":"Burzahom","country":"India (Kashmir)","region":"asia","lat":34.17,"lon":74.88,"start":5000,"end":3700,"dateLabel":"c. 3000–1700 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Neolithic pit dwellings, bone tools, perforated harvesting knives like those of northern China, and human burials with dogs.","chapters":["south-asia"]},{"id":"sanganakallu","name":"Sanganakallu and Kupgal","country":"India","region":"asia","lat":15.17,"lon":76.97,"start":5000,"end":3200,"dateLabel":"3rd–2nd millennium BCE","type":"farming","summary":"Southern Neolithic hill settlements and ashmounds of burnt cattle dung, with native pulses and millets from a separate Indian centre of domestication.","chapters":["south-asia"]},{"id":"kalibangan","name":"Kalibangan","country":"India","region":"asia","lat":29.47,"lon":74.13,"start":5000,"end":4600,"dateLabel":"c. 3000–2600 BCE (Early Harappan)","type":"farming","summary":"Early Harappan settlement on the Ghaggar with a buried field showing criss-cross plough furrows.","chapters":["south-asia"]},{"id":"brewarrina-fish-traps","name":"Brewarrina fish traps (Baiame’s Ngunnhu)","country":"Australia","region":"oceania","lat":-29.96,"lon":146.86,"start":5000,"end":150,"dateLabel":"age unknown, possibly thousands of years","type":"other","summary":"Stone walls and ponds across the Barwon River used to trap fish, shared by several Aboriginal nations; their age has not been measured.","chapters":["sahul-holocene"]},{"id":"caral","name":"Caral","country":"Peru","region":"americas","lat":-10.89,"lon":-77.52,"start":5000,"end":3800,"dateLabel":"c. 3000–1800 BCE","type":"monument","summary":"Preceramic centre with platform mounds and sunken circular plazas, excavated by Ruth Shady Solís. UNESCO World Heritage since 2009.","chapters":["americas-holocene"]},{"id":"rakhigarhi","name":"Rakhigarhi","country":"India","region":"asia","lat":29.29,"lon":76.11,"start":4800,"end":4300,"dateLabel":"c. 2800–2300 BCE","type":"dna","summary":"Indus city whose cemetery yielded the first published Harappan genome (2019), with local and Iranian-related ancestry and no steppe ancestry.","chapters":["south-asia"]},{"id":"grimes-graves","name":"Grimes Graves","country":"United Kingdom","region":"europe","lat":52.48,"lon":0.67,"start":4600,"end":3300,"dateLabel":"c. 2600–1300 BCE","type":"tools","summary":"Flint mine with more than 400 shafts dug with antler picks, mostly in the Late Neolithic and reused in the Bronze Age.","chapters":["dawn-of-bronze"]},{"id":"ur","name":"Ur","country":"Iraq","region":"asia","lat":30.96,"lon":46.1,"start":4600,"end":4500,"dateLabel":"c. 2600–2500 BCE (royal tombs)","type":"burial","summary":"Sumerian city whose royal tombs contain early tin-bronze alongside gold, silver and arsenical copper.","chapters":["dawn-of-bronze"]},{"id":"qeqertasussuk","name":"Qeqertasussuk","country":"Greenland (Denmark)","region":"americas","lat":68.85,"lon":-51.25,"start":4400,"end":3400,"dateLabel":"c. 2400–1400 BCE","type":"dna","summary":"Saqqaq-culture site whose frozen deposits yielded the hair used for the first sequenced ancient human genome (2010).","chapters":["americas-holocene"]},{"id":"amesbury-archer","name":"Amesbury Archer (Boscombe Down)","country":"United Kingdom","region":"europe","lat":51.17,"lon":-1.76,"start":4380,"end":4290,"dateLabel":"c. 2380–2290 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Richest Beaker burial in Britain: a man raised probably in the Alps, buried with copper knives, gold ornaments, arrowheads and a metalworking stone.","chapters":["dawn-of-bronze"]},{"id":"taosi","name":"Taosi","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":35.88,"lon":111.5,"start":4300,"end":3900,"dateLabel":"c. 2300–1900 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Longshan-period walled town of about 2.8 square kilometres with a highly unequal cemetery and a semicircular structure claimed as a solar observatory.","chapters":["china-neolithic"]},{"id":"shimao","name":"Shimao","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":38.57,"lon":110.29,"start":4300,"end":3800,"dateLabel":"c. 2300–1800 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Stone-walled centre of more than 400 hectares with the terraced Huangchengtai platform, about 70 stone carvings and skull pits by the east gate; ancient DNA published in 2025 reveals patrilineal kinship.","chapters":["china-neolithic"]},{"id":"unetice","name":"Únětice","country":"Czechia","region":"europe","lat":50.15,"lon":14.35,"start":4200,"end":3600,"dateLabel":"c. 2200–1600 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Type site of the Únětice culture, which established tin-bronze metallurgy in central Europe.","chapters":["dawn-of-bronze"]},{"id":"omori-shell-mound","name":"Ōmori shell mound","country":"Japan","region":"asia","lat":35.59,"lon":139.73,"start":4000,"end":3000,"dateLabel":"c. 2000–1000 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Late Jōmon shell midden in Tokyo excavated by Edward S. Morse in 1877, the birthplace of Japanese scientific archaeology and of the name “cord marked” pottery.","chapters":["pottery-east-asia"]},{"id":"ban-chiang","name":"Ban Chiang","country":"Thailand","region":"asia","lat":17.41,"lon":103.24,"start":4000,"end":2000,"dateLabel":"burials from c. 2000 BCE; bronze from c. 1000 BCE or earlier (debated)","type":"metal","summary":"Village cemetery with bronze and painted pottery, central to the debate over when metal reached Southeast Asia. UNESCO World Heritage since 1992.","chapters":["dawn-of-bronze"]},{"id":"kultepe-kanesh","name":"Kültepe (Kanesh)","country":"Türkiye","region":"asia","lat":38.85,"lon":35.63,"start":3950,"end":3750,"dateLabel":"c. 1950–1750 BCE","type":"settlement","summary":"Anatolian trading city where thousands of Old Assyrian letters record caravans bringing tin and textiles from Assur.","chapters":["dawn-of-bronze"]},{"id":"erlitou","name":"Erlitou","country":"China","region":"asia","lat":34.69,"lon":112.69,"start":3900,"end":3500,"dateLabel":"c. 1900–1500 BCE","type":"metal","summary":"Palace centre with bronze workshops producing the first piece-mould cast ritual vessels in China.","chapters":["dawn-of-bronze"]},{"id":"waywaka","name":"Waywaka","country":"Peru","region":"americas","lat":-13.65,"lon":-73.38,"start":3700,"end":3300,"dateLabel":"c. 1500 BCE","type":"metal","summary":"Andean site with hammered gold foil and a goldworker’s toolkit, among the earliest metalworking in South America.","chapters":["dawn-of-bronze"]},{"id":"nok","name":"Nok","country":"Nigeria","region":"africa","lat":9.5,"lon":8,"start":3500,"end":2000,"dateLabel":"c. 1500 BCE–1 CE; iron by mid-1st millennium BCE","type":"metal","summary":"Culture known for terracotta sculptures and early iron smelting in West Africa, without a preceding Bronze Age.","chapters":["dawn-of-bronze"]},{"id":"termit-egaro","name":"Termit massif (Egaro)","country":"Niger","region":"africa","lat":16,"lon":11.4,"start":3500,"end":2500,"dateLabel":"claimed iron working from the 2nd millennium BCE (contested)","type":"metal","summary":"Sites with some of the earliest claimed iron-working dates in Africa, still debated because of the dating of the charcoal.","chapters":["dawn-of-bronze"]},{"id":"madura-cave","name":"Madura Cave","country":"Australia","region":"oceania","lat":-31.9,"lon":127.02,"start":3350,"end":3100,"dateLabel":"c. 3,350–3,100 years ago","type":"fossil","summary":"Nullarbor cave with the oldest firmly dated dingo bones, directly radiocarbon dated in 2018.","chapters":["sahul-holocene"]},{"id":"teouma","name":"Teouma","country":"Vanuatu","region":"oceania","lat":-17.78,"lon":168.39,"start":3000,"end":2800,"dateLabel":"c. 1000–800 BCE","type":"burial","summary":"Lapita cemetery of the first settlers of Vanuatu, with decorated pots and skull-removal practices; genomes show almost entirely Asian-related ancestry.","chapters":["sahul-holocene"]},{"id":"akjoujt","name":"Akjoujt","country":"Mauritania","region":"africa","lat":19.75,"lon":-14.39,"start":3000,"end":2200,"dateLabel":"1st millennium BCE","type":"metal","summary":"Copper mining and working region in the western Sahara, one of the few centres of early copper metallurgy in West Africa.","chapters":["dawn-of-bronze"]},{"id":"ballito-bay","name":"Ballito Bay","country":"South Africa","region":"africa","lat":-29.54,"lon":31.21,"start":2000,"end":2000,"dateLabel":"about 2,000 years ago","type":"dna","summary":"Burial of a boy whose ancient genome, free of later admixture, was used in 2017 to date the deepest split among living human populations to 350,000–260,000 years ago.","chapters":["origin-of-sapiens"]}],"species":[{"id":"sahelanthropus-tchadensis","name":"Sahelanthropus tchadensis","nickname":"Toumaï","group":"early","start":7000000,"end":7000000,"startMax":7200000,"endMin":6800000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"about 360–370 cm³ (one reconstructed skull)","stature":"unknown","discovered":"2001, Toros-Menalla, Djurab Desert, Chad (Ahounta Djimdoumalbaye, in Michel Brunet’s team); named in 2002","keyFossils":"Toumaï cranium (TM 266-01-060-1), several jaws and teeth, a femur and two ulnae","summary":"Known from a crushed but nearly complete cranium and a handful of jaws, teeth and limb bones from the Djurab Desert of Chad, Sahelanthropus lived about 7 million years ago, close to the time when the human and chimpanzee lineages separated. Its braincase was chimpanzee-sized, but its canines were small and worn at the tip, and the opening for the spinal cord sits further forward than in apes, which its discoverers read as a sign of upright posture. It lived some 2,500 kilometres west of the East African Rift, showing that early hominins were not confined to East Africa. The age comes from beryllium isotopes formed in the atmosphere and trapped in the surrounding sediments, and from the animal fossils found with it.","debate":"Whether Sahelanthropus walked upright, and so whether it is a hominin at all, is contested. A femur found in 2001 was only described in 2020, when Roberto Macchiarelli and colleagues argued it came from a quadruped; in 2022 Guillaume Daver, Franck Guy and colleagues countered that the femur and two ulnae show habitual bipedalism combined with climbing. A 2026 study led by Scott Williams identified a femoral tubercle, a feature known only in bipeds, tipping the balance toward upright walking, although it cannot be proven that the limb bones belong to the same species as the skull. Some researchers still see it as an ape living near the split rather than a member of our lineage.","status":"debated","chapter":"first-hominins"},{"id":"orrorin-tugenensis","name":"Orrorin tugenensis","nickname":"Millennium Man","group":"early","start":6100000,"end":5700000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"unknown","stature":"unknown","discovered":"2000, Lukeino Formation, Tugen Hills, Kenya (Brigitte Senut and Martin Pickford); named in 2001","keyFossils":"Three partial femora (including BAR 1002’00), jaw fragments, teeth, part of a humerus and a finger bone","summary":"Found in the Tugen Hills of Kenya in 2000, and therefore nicknamed “Millennium Man”, Orrorin lived about 6 million years ago. It is known from a small collection of fragments, including three partial thigh bones, teeth with thick enamel and small canines, and arm and finger bones. The thigh bones have a long femoral neck and a distribution of bone inside the neck that is expected in an animal carrying its weight on two legs, while the curved finger bone suggests it also climbed. Its discoverers argued it was a direct human ancestor and that the australopiths were a side branch, a view few others share.","debate":"Most researchers accept that Orrorin walked upright to some degree, based on CT scans of the femoral neck published in 2004 and later shape analyses of the femur, but how human-like its gait was is disputed. Its place in the tree is uncertain: Senut and Pickford see it as ancestral to Homo, bypassing Australopithecus, while others treat it as an early hominin of unclear relationships or even an ape. With no skull and few teeth, it is hard to compare directly with Sahelanthropus.","status":"debated","chapter":"first-hominins"},{"id":"ardipithecus-kadabba","name":"Ardipithecus kadabba","nickname":"","group":"early","start":5800000,"end":5200000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"unknown","stature":"unknown","discovered":"1997, Middle Awash, Afar, Ethiopia (Yohannes Haile-Selassie); named as a subspecies in 2001 and as a species in 2004","keyFossils":"Jaw fragment ALA-VP-2/10 (holotype), isolated teeth, hand and arm bones, toe bone AME-VP-1/71","summary":"Ardipithecus kadabba is known from teeth and scattered bones found in 5.8- to 5.2-million-year-old deposits of the Middle Awash in Ethiopia; its name comes from the Afar word for a basal family ancestor. It was first described in 2001 as a subspecies of Ar. ramidus and raised to a separate species in 2004, when new teeth showed a more ape-like arrangement in which the upper canine was sharpened against the lower premolar. A toe bone about 5.2 million years old has a joint angled upwards in a way associated with pushing off the ground in upright walking. Its fossils come from wooded, well-watered environments.","debate":"The toe bone is several hundred thousand years younger than most other kadabba fossils and comes from a different locality, so its attribution and its evidence for bipedalism are questioned. Haile-Selassie, Gen Suwa and Tim White noted in 2004 that the teeth of Sahelanthropus, Orrorin and Ar. kadabba are so similar that they might belong to a single genus, while others think the differences are real.","status":"established","chapter":"first-hominins"},{"id":"ardipithecus-ramidus","name":"Ardipithecus ramidus","nickname":"Ardi","group":"early","start":4500000,"end":4300000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"about 300–350 cm³","stature":"about 1.2 m (Ardi)","discovered":"1992, Aramis, Middle Awash, Ethiopia (Gen Suwa, in a team led by Tim White and Berhane Asfaw); named in 1994; Ardi found in 1994","keyFossils":"Ardi partial skeleton (ARA-VP-6/500), more than 100 other specimens from Aramis, fossils from As Duma, Gona","summary":"Ardipithecus ramidus lived about 4.4 million years ago in the Afar region of Ethiopia. Its best-known fossil, the partial female skeleton nicknamed Ardi, was excavated from 1994 onwards but published only in 2009, after years of painstaking restoration of the crushed bones. Ardi combined a pelvis that the team interpreted as adapted for upright walking with a divergent, grasping big toe and long arms suited to climbing, a mix unlike any living ape. Small, blunt canines in males suggest less fighting between males than in chimpanzees.","debate":"The 2009 descriptions, led by Tim White and Owen Lovejoy, argued that Ardi shows the last common ancestor with chimpanzees was not chimpanzee-like and never knuckle-walked; others, including Thomas Prang and colleagues in a 2021 study of its hand, see ape-like traits consistent with a more chimpanzee-like ancestor. Its habitat is also disputed: the team reconstructed woodland, while Thure Cerling and colleagues argued for more open, tree-studded grassland. Whether it is a direct ancestor of Australopithecus remains open.","status":"established","chapter":"first-hominins"},{"id":"australopithecus-anamensis","name":"Australopithecus anamensis","nickname":"","group":"australopith","start":4200000,"end":3800000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"about 365–370 cm³ (MRD cranium)","stature":"unknown","discovered":"1965, Kanapoi, Kenya (a humerus found by Bryan Patterson’s team); named in 1995 by Meave Leakey and colleagues from fossils from Kanapoi and Allia Bay","keyFossils":"KNM-KP 29281 mandible (holotype), KNM-KP 29285 tibia, Asa Issie fossils, MRD-VP-1/1 cranium (Woranso-Mille)","summary":"The earliest undisputed species of Australopithecus lived between about 4.2 and 3.8 million years ago in Kenya and Ethiopia. A shin bone from Kanapoi shows a knee built to carry the body’s weight on two legs, while its jaws were long and parallel-sided with large canines, more ape-like than those of later australopiths. In 2019 Yohannes Haile-Selassie’s team described MRD, a nearly complete 3.8-million-year-old cranium from Woranso-Mille in Ethiopia and the first skull of the species, with a small brain and a projecting face.","debate":"Anamensis was long seen as the direct ancestor of Au. afarensis in a single evolving lineage. The MRD skull, together with a 3.9-million-year-old frontal bone from Belohdelie attributed to afarensis, suggests the two species overlapped for at least 100,000 years, so afarensis may have split off from part of the anamensis population rather than replacing it gradually. Others argue that the dating and attribution of the Belohdelie bone are too uncertain to prove overlap.","status":"established","chapter":"australopiths"},{"id":"australopithecus-afarensis","name":"Australopithecus afarensis","nickname":"Lucy","group":"australopith","start":3850000,"end":2950000,"startMax":3900000,"endMin":2900000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"about 380–550 cm³","stature":"about 1.05–1.5 m","discovered":"1973–1974, Hadar, Ethiopia (Donald Johanson’s team) and 1974–1975, Laetoli, Tanzania (Mary Leakey’s team); named in 1978","keyFossils":"Lucy (AL 288-1), the “First Family” (AL 333), the Dikika child Selam (DIK-1-1), Kadanuumuu (KSD-VP-1/1), Laetoli jaw LH 4 (holotype), Laetoli footprints","summary":"Au. afarensis is the best-known early hominin, represented by hundreds of fossils from Ethiopia, Kenya and Tanzania dated between about 3.9 and 2.9 million years ago. Lucy, found at Hadar in 1974 and about 3.2 million years old, preserved some 40 percent of a skeleton and showed that upright walking came long before big brains. The Laetoli footprints, pressed into wet volcanic ash about 3.66 million years ago and usually attributed to this species, record a human-like, heel-first stride. Curved fingers and upward-facing shoulder joints suggest it still climbed regularly, and the brain of the 3.3-million-year-old Dikika child indicates slow, human-like brain growth.","debate":"Researchers disagree about how much time it spent in trees: some read the curved fingers and shoulders as signs of active climbing, others as leftovers from its ancestors. Cut-marked animal bones from Dikika, about 3.4 million years old, were claimed in 2010 as evidence of butchery with stone tools, but critics argue the marks could come from trampling. The degree of size difference between males and females, and what it implies for social life, is also debated.","status":"established","chapter":"australopiths"},{"id":"australopithecus-deyiremeda","name":"Australopithecus deyiremeda","nickname":"","group":"australopith","start":3500000,"end":3300000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"unknown","stature":"unknown","discovered":"Named in 2015 by Yohannes Haile-Selassie and colleagues from jaws and teeth found at Burtele and Waytaleyta, Woranso-Mille, Afar, Ethiopia","keyFossils":"Maxilla BRT-VP-3/1 (holotype), mandibles and teeth, the Burtele foot (BRT-VP-2/73)","summary":"Named in 2015 from 3.5- to 3.3-million-year-old jaws and teeth found at Woranso-Mille in Ethiopia’s Afar region, Au. deyiremeda lived at the same time and in the same region as Lucy’s species. Its name means “close relative” in the Afar language. In November 2025 Haile-Selassie and colleagues assigned to it the Burtele foot, eight bones found in 2009 that have a grasping, opposable big toe: this species walked on two legs but in a different way from afarensis, and still climbed trees. It is among the clearest evidence that two australopith species shared the same landscape in the middle Pliocene.","debate":"When it was named, several researchers, including Fred Spoor and colleagues, questioned whether its jaws differ enough from Au. afarensis or Kenyanthropus platyops to justify a new species. The Burtele foot was published in 2012 without a species name because no skull or teeth were found with it; the 2025 study links it to deyiremeda through new fossils from the same deposits. How the coexisting Afar hominins divided their diets and habitats is now a central question.","status":"debated","chapter":"australopiths"},{"id":"kenyanthropus-platyops","name":"Kenyanthropus platyops","nickname":"Flat-faced man of Kenya","group":"australopith","start":3500000,"end":3300000,"endMin":3200000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"unknown","stature":"unknown","discovered":"1999, Lomekwi, West Turkana, Kenya (Justus Erus, in Meave Leakey’s team); named in 2001","keyFossils":"KNM-WT 40000 cranium (holotype), KNM-WT 38350 maxilla","summary":"Kenyanthropus was named in 2001 by Meave Leakey and colleagues from a distorted cranium about 3.5 million years old found west of Lake Turkana. Its face is unusually flat below the nose and its molars small for an australopith, a combination its describers saw as distinct from Au. afarensis, which lived at the same time. It comes from the same area as Lomekwi 3, where 3.3-million-year-old stone tools were found in 2011, and is one candidate for their maker. Its flat face has also been compared with that of Homo rudolfensis.","debate":"Tim White argued in 2003 that the flat face could be an artefact of distortion, because the cranium is broken into many small pieces shifted by expanding mudstone, and that it may be a variant of Au. afarensis. Others accept it as a separate genus or species. A well-preserved face from the same period would settle the matter.","status":"debated","chapter":"australopiths"},{"id":"australopithecus-bahrelghazali","name":"Australopithecus bahrelghazali","nickname":"Abel","group":"australopith","start":3600000,"end":3500000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"unknown","stature":"unknown","discovered":"1995, Koro Toro, Bahr el Ghazal, Chad (Michel Brunet’s team); named in 1996","keyFossils":"KT12/H1 mandible fragment “Abel” (holotype) and a few teeth","summary":"Found in 1995 in the Bahr el Ghazal region of Chad and nicknamed Abel in memory of the geologist Abel Brillanceau, this partial jaw is the only australopith known from west of the Rift Valley, some 2,500 kilometres from the East African sites. Beryllium isotope dating of the deposits gave about 3.6 million years. Its discoverers argued that the shape of its chin region and its three-rooted premolars set it apart from Au. afarensis. Its main importance is geographic: it shows australopiths ranged across a far wider part of Africa than the fossil-rich rift valleys suggest.","debate":"Many researchers regard it as a western population of Au. afarensis, since the claimed differences rest on a single partial jaw and a few teeth. Others accept it as a distinct species. Either way, it implies that the fossil record from eastern and southern Africa is a geographically biased sample of where hominins lived.","status":"debated","chapter":"australopiths"},{"id":"australopithecus-africanus","name":"Australopithecus africanus","nickname":"Taung Child, Mrs Ples","group":"australopith","start":3000000,"end":2100000,"startMax":3700000,"endMin":2000000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"about 400–560 cm³","stature":"about 1.1–1.4 m","discovered":"1924, Taung limeworks, South Africa (quarry workers; described by Raymond Dart in 1925)","keyFossils":"Taung Child (holotype), Mrs Ples (Sts 5), Sts 14 partial skeleton, StW 431, Makapansgat fossils; Little Foot (StW 573) according to some","summary":"Au. africanus was the first australopith ever named: in 1925 Raymond Dart described the skull of a young child from the Taung limeworks as a small-brained human ancestor, a claim most experts rejected for two decades. Hundreds of fossils from Sterkfontein and Makapansgat have since filled out the species, including the adult cranium Mrs Ples, found by Robert Broom and John Robinson in 1947. It walked upright but had relatively long arms and a brain of about 400 to 560 cm³. Damage in the Taung skull’s eye sockets, identified by Lee Berger in 2006, suggests the child was killed by a large bird of prey.","debate":"Its age range is uncertain because South African cave deposits are hard to date: cosmogenic nuclide dating led by Darryl Granger in 2022 placed the Sterkfontein Member 4 fossils at about 3.4 to 3.7 million years, much older than the 2.6 to 2.0 million years usually assumed, but others dispute this. Ron Clarke assigns Little Foot and some other Sterkfontein fossils to a second species, Au. prometheus, and a 2025 study led by Jesse Martin argued that Little Foot may represent a species of its own; many researchers still count these fossils within africanus. Whether africanus was ancestral to Homo, to Paranthropus robustus or to neither remains open.","status":"established","chapter":"australopiths"},{"id":"australopithecus-garhi","name":"Australopithecus garhi","nickname":"","group":"australopith","start":2500000,"end":2500000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"about 450 cm³ (one skull)","stature":"unknown","discovered":"1997, Bouri Peninsula, Middle Awash, Ethiopia; named in 1999 by Berhane Asfaw, Tim White and colleagues","keyFossils":"BOU-VP-12/130 cranium (holotype), a partial skeleton from the same horizon (attribution uncertain)","summary":"Au. garhi was named in 1999 from a 2.5-million-year-old skull from the Bouri Peninsula in Ethiopia’s Middle Awash; garhi means “surprise” in the Afar language. It had a small brain and a projecting face with very large premolars and molars. Antelope bones from the same layer nearby carry cut marks and hammerstone impact scars, which suggested to the team that garhi, or a contemporary, used stone tools to take meat and marrow. A partial skeleton from the same horizon has a long thigh bone relative to its arm, as in later Homo, but cannot be assigned to garhi with certainty.","debate":"Its discoverers suggested it might be ancestral to Homo, since it lived at the right time and place, but its huge teeth make many researchers doubtful. The 2025 report of Homo teeth 2.78 and 2.59 million years old at nearby Ledi-Geraru, and of a different, unnamed australopith there at 2.63 million years, shows that several lineages overlapped in the Afar at this time.","status":"established","chapter":"australopiths"},{"id":"australopithecus-sediba","name":"Australopithecus sediba","nickname":"Karabo (MH1)","group":"australopith","start":1980000,"end":1977000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"about 420 cm³ (MH1)","stature":"about 1.3 m (estimated)","discovered":"2008, Malapa, Cradle of Humankind, South Africa (Matthew Berger, aged nine, and Lee Berger); named in 2010","keyFossils":"MH1 “Karabo” (juvenile male, holotype), MH2 (adult female), remains of further individuals from Malapa","summary":"In August 2008 nine-year-old Matthew Berger spotted a fossil collarbone at Malapa, a collapsed cave north of Johannesburg, leading to two partial skeletons described by his father Lee Berger and colleagues in 2010. Uranium–lead dating of flowstone and palaeomagnetism place them at about 1.98 million years ago. Au. sediba combined a small brain and long, climbing arms with a hand that had a long, Homo-like thumb, human-like features of the pelvis, and an ankle and heel that suggest an unusual way of walking. Vertebrae described in 2021 show a lower back curved like that of a habitual upright walker.","debate":"Berger and colleagues proposed sediba as a possible ancestor of Homo, or close to it. The main objection is timing: fossils attributed to Homo, such as the 2.8-million-year-old jaw from Ledi-Geraru and a 2.33-million-year-old upper jaw from Hadar, are older than Malapa, so sediba could only be a late-surviving population of an ancestral species. Many researchers instead see it as a southern African australopith that evolved some Homo-like features independently.","status":"established","chapter":"australopiths"},{"id":"paranthropus-aethiopicus","name":"Paranthropus aethiopicus","nickname":"Black Skull","group":"paranthropus","start":2700000,"end":2300000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"about 410 cm³ (Black Skull)","stature":"unknown","discovered":"1967, Omo, Ethiopia (French team of Camille Arambourg and Yves Coppens); named in 1968","keyFossils":"Omo 18 mandible (holotype), KNM-WT 17000 “Black Skull”, teeth from the Omo and West Turkana","summary":"The oldest widely accepted Paranthropus lived in the Omo valley of Ethiopia and around Lake Turkana between about 2.7 and 2.3 million years ago. Its best fossil is the Black Skull, found by Alan Walker in 1985 west of Lake Turkana, stained dark by manganese and about 2.5 million years old. It had a huge sagittal crest, a strongly projecting, dish-shaped face and large chewing teeth, with a braincase of only about 410 cm³. Its mix of primitive and robust features makes it the likely ancestor of P. boisei.","debate":"Some researchers prefer to keep the robust species in Australopithecus, and it has long been argued whether southern African P. robustus descends from aethiopicus or evolved its massive jaws independently; most phylogenetic analyses favour a single Paranthropus lineage. A 2.6-million-year-old Paranthropus jaw reported from Ethiopia’s Afar region in 2026 shows the genus ranged more widely, and in more varied habitats, than its classic sites suggest.","status":"established","chapter":"australopiths"},{"id":"paranthropus-boisei","name":"Paranthropus boisei","nickname":"Nutcracker Man","group":"paranthropus","start":2300000,"end":1300000,"endMin":1200000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"about 475–545 cm³","stature":"roughly 1.2–1.4 m (estimated)","discovered":"1959, Olduvai Gorge, Tanzania (Mary Leakey); named Zinjanthropus boisei by Louis Leakey","keyFossils":"OH 5 “Zinj” (holotype), KNM-ER 406, KNM-ER 732, Konso KGA10-525, Peninj mandible, a partial skeleton with hand and foot bones from Koobi Fora (2025)","summary":"Mary Leakey’s discovery of the OH 5 skull at Olduvai Gorge in July 1959 made the Leakeys famous, and the potassium–argon date of about 1.75 million years obtained for its layer in 1961 revealed how deep human prehistory ran. P. boisei had the largest molars and thickest enamel of any hominin, a broad face and a sagittal crest anchoring massive chewing muscles. Carbon isotopes show that it ate mostly grasses and sedges rather than hard nuts, so its nickname misleads. In 2025 Carrie Mongle and colleagues described the first hand bones confidently linked to the species, from a partial skeleton more than 1.5 million years old at Koobi Fora, combining gorilla-like gripping strength with human-like dexterity.","debate":"Because its hand was unknown, stone tools found alongside it were usually credited to Homo; the 2025 hand fossils suggest it was physically capable of making tools, reopening the question of who made the Oldowan tools of this period. How it used its enormous teeth is also debated: isotopes point to grasses and sedges, and the fine wear on its teeth does not show the pitting expected from cracking hard objects, suggesting tough rather than hard foods.","status":"established","chapter":"australopiths"},{"id":"paranthropus-robustus","name":"Paranthropus robustus","nickname":"","group":"paranthropus","start":2000000,"end":1000000,"startMax":2040000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"about 450–530 cm³","stature":"about 1.0–1.2 m","discovered":"1938, Kromdraai, South Africa (schoolboy Gert Terblanche; described and named by Robert Broom)","keyFossils":"TM 1517 (holotype, Kromdraai), SK 48 (Swartkrans), DNH 7, DNH 152 and DNH 155 (Drimolen)","summary":"In 1938 a schoolboy, Gert Terblanche, gave Robert Broom teeth from Kromdraai near Johannesburg, leading to the first robust australopith, which Broom named Paranthropus robustus. Hundreds of teeth and bones, especially from Swartkrans and Drimolen, show a species with a crested skull, huge cheek teeth and a small brain, living between about 2 and 1 million years ago. At Drimolen it lived alongside early Homo erectus about 2 million years ago. Bone tools worn from digging into termite mounds, and burnt bones at Swartkrans, may be linked to it or to Homo.","debate":"In 2025 Clément Zanolli and colleagues argued that the Swartkrans jaw SK 15, about 1.4 million years old and long assigned to early Homo, belongs to a second, smaller southern Paranthropus species, P. capensis; others want more fossils before accepting it. A 2025 study led by Palesa Madupe used enamel proteins to identify the sex of individual Swartkrans teeth and found more variation than body size alone suggested. Whether robustus descends from East African Paranthropus or from a local australopith such as Au. africanus is unresolved.","status":"established","chapter":"australopiths"},{"id":"homo-ledi-geraru","name":"Homo sp. (Ledi-Geraru)","nickname":"","group":"early-homo","start":2800000,"end":2590000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"unknown","stature":"unknown","discovered":"2013, Ledi-Geraru, Afar, Ethiopia (Chalachew Seyoum, in a project co-directed by Kaye Reed); published in 2015","keyFossils":"LD 350-1 mandible; isolated teeth described in 2025","summary":"In 2013 the Ethiopian graduate student Chalachew Seyoum found half of a lower jaw, LD 350-1, in 2.8-million-year-old sediments at Ledi-Geraru in the Afar. Published in 2015, it combines a primitive, receding chin region with slender molars and other features seen in later Homo, making it the oldest fossil widely attributed to our genus. In 2025 Brian Villmoare and colleagues reported more Homo teeth from Ledi-Geraru dated to 2.78 and 2.59 million years, together with teeth of a previously unknown kind of Australopithecus about 2.63 million years old. The fossils are too incomplete to name a species, but they place the origin of Homo close to 3 million years ago, at a time when open habitats were spreading in the region.","debate":"Many researchers accept LD 350-1 as early Homo; others note that a single jaw and some teeth cannot define a genus and that its features might fall within a variable australopith. It may belong to the lineage that led to Homo habilis, but the link is unproven. The overlap with an unnamed australopith at the same site shows that the transition to Homo was not a simple succession.","status":"debated","chapter":"genus-homo"},{"id":"homo-habilis","name":"Homo habilis","nickname":"Handy man","group":"early-homo","start":2300000,"end":1440000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"about 500–800 cm³","stature":"about 1.0–1.35 m","discovered":"1960, Olduvai Gorge, Tanzania (Jonathan Leakey found OH 7); named in 1964 by Louis Leakey, Phillip Tobias and John Napier","keyFossils":"OH 7 (holotype), OH 13, OH 24 “Twiggy”, OH 62, KNM-ER 1813, KNM-ER 42703, AL 666-1 (Hadar)","summary":"Named in 1964 from fossils found at Olduvai Gorge, Homo habilis, “handy man”, was linked by its discoverers to the Oldowan stone tools found in the same beds. It lived in East Africa between about 2.3 and 1.4 million years ago, alongside Paranthropus boisei and, later, Homo erectus. Its brain was larger than an australopith’s, but its body remained small, and the partial skeleton OH 62 suggests relatively long arms. A 2015 virtual reconstruction of the OH 7 jaw by Fred Spoor and colleagues found it surprisingly primitive, while the braincase was larger than previously thought.","debate":"Its fossils vary so much in size and shape that some researchers divide them among two or more species, including H. rudolfensis, while others see one variable species. Bernard Wood and Mark Collard argued in 1999 that its body proportions and chewing apparatus are closer to australopiths and that it should be moved to Australopithecus. The 1.44-million-year-old upper jaw KNM-ER 42703, described in 2007, shows habilis and erectus overlapped for hundreds of thousands of years, challenging the idea that one simply turned into the other.","status":"established","chapter":"genus-homo"},{"id":"homo-rudolfensis","name":"Homo rudolfensis","nickname":"","group":"early-homo","start":2000000,"end":1780000,"startMax":2400000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"about 700–750 cm³","stature":"unknown","discovered":"1972, Koobi Fora, Kenya (Bernard Ngeneo, in Richard Leakey’s team); named in 1986 by Valery Alexeev","keyFossils":"KNM-ER 1470 cranium (holotype), KNM-ER 62000 face, KNM-ER 1802 mandible, Uraha mandible UR 501 (Malawi)","summary":"Discovered in 1972 east of Lake Turkana, the skull KNM-ER 1470 caused a sensation: it had a brain of about 750 cm³ and seemed, from the first dating of a volcanic ash above it, to be 2.6 million years old. After years of dispute the ash was redated to about 1.9 million years. Its long, flat face and large brain distinguish it from Homo habilis, and in 1986 it became the type of a separate species named after Lake Rudolf, the old name of Lake Turkana. In 2012 Meave Leakey’s team described a face and jaws from 1.95 to 1.78 million years ago that match 1470, strengthening the case for two contemporary kinds of early Homo.","debate":"The main question is whether rudolfensis is a separate species or the large end of a variable H. habilis. KNM-ER 1470 has no teeth or lower jaw, which makes matching it with other fossils difficult, and this is why the 2012 finds mattered. Some researchers have pointed to resemblances between its flat face and Kenyanthropus platyops, and a few suggest moving it to that genus.","status":"debated","chapter":"genus-homo"},{"id":"homo-erectus","name":"Homo erectus","nickname":"Upright human","group":"erectus","start":2000000,"end":110000,"startMax":2100000,"endMin":108000,"regions":["africa","asia"],"brain":"about 550–1,250 cm³","stature":"about 1.45–1.85 m","discovered":"1891, Trinil, Java (Eugène Dubois)","keyFossils":"Trinil 2 skullcap, Turkana Boy (KNM-WT 15000), KNM-ER 3733, DNH 134 (Drimolen), Dmanisi skulls, Sangiran 17, Zhoukoudian, Ngandong","summary":"Homo erectus was the longest-lived human species, known from about 2 million years ago in South Africa and Kenya to about 110,000 years ago at Ngandong on Java. Below the neck it was built much like us, with long legs, a narrow pelvis and relatively short arms suited to walking and running long distances, and its brain grew from about 550 to more than 1,200 cm³ over its long history. It made Acheulean handaxes in Africa and western Asia, may have used fire by about a million years ago, and was the first hominin known to spread widely across Asia. The 1.5-million-year-old Turkana Boy, found by Kamoya Kimeu in 1984, is one of the most complete early human skeletons.","debate":"Many researchers call the early African fossils Homo ergaster, a name given in 1975 to a jaw from Koobi Fora, and keep erectus for the thicker-skulled Asian populations; others see one widespread species. The skulls from Dmanisi in Georgia, about 1.8 million years old, were named Homo georgicus in 2002, but the variation among them led David Lordkipanidze and colleagues in 2013 to argue that many early Homo fossils may belong to a single variable lineage. Open questions include whether Asian erectus gave rise to island hominins such as H. floresiensis, and how the 1.4- to 1.1-million-year-old “Pink” face from Sima del Elefante in Spain, attributed in 2025 to Homo affinis erectus, fits into the story of early Europe.","status":"established","chapter":"erectus-acheulean"},{"id":"homo-antecessor","name":"Homo antecessor","nickname":"Pioneer man","group":"archaic","start":900000,"end":780000,"startMax":950000,"endMin":772000,"regions":["europe"],"brain":"probably about 1,000 cm³ (estimated from fragments)","stature":"roughly 1.6–1.8 m (estimated from limb bones)","discovered":"1994, Gran Dolina (level TD6), Sierra de Atapuerca, Spain (team of Eudald Carbonell, Juan Luis Arsuaga and José María Bermúdez de Castro); named in 1997","keyFossils":"ATD6-5 mandible (holotype), ATD6-69 juvenile face, ATD6-15 frontal bone","summary":"Homo antecessor was named in 1997 from fragments of several individuals found from 1994 onwards in level TD6 of Gran Dolina, a cave in the Sierra de Atapuerca of northern Spain. The fossils are older than about 780,000 years, because the last reversal of Earth’s magnetic field is recorded in the layer above them. The face of a boy, ATD6-69, looks surprisingly modern, with a hollowed cheek below the eye, while the teeth and brow are more primitive. Many of the bones carry cut marks from butchery, making TD6 one of the oldest known cases of cannibalism.","debate":"Its discoverers first proposed antecessor as the last common ancestor of Neanderthals and modern humans. In 2020 Frido Welker and colleagues sequenced enamel proteins from an antecessor tooth and found it to be a close sister lineage of that shared ancestor rather than the ancestor itself. Some researchers doubt that it is distinct from other early populations, and the 2025 description of the older “Pink” face from nearby Sima del Elefante, which lacks antecessor’s modern-looking midface, suggests that Europe saw more than one early human population.","status":"established","chapter":"first-europeans"},{"id":"homo-heidelbergensis","name":"Homo heidelbergensis","nickname":"Heidelberg man","group":"archaic","start":700000,"end":200000,"regions":["africa","europe"],"brain":"about 1,100–1,400 cm³","stature":"about 1.6–1.8 m","discovered":"1907, Mauer sand pit near Heidelberg, Germany (the worker Daniel Hartmann; named in 1908 by Otto Schoetensack)","keyFossils":"Mauer 1 mandible (holotype), Kabwe 1 (Broken Hill) skull, Bodo cranium, Petralona skull, Arago 21, Boxgrove tibia","summary":"The name Homo heidelbergensis comes from a massive, chinless jaw found in 1907 in a sand pit at Mauer, near Heidelberg, and now dated to about 609,000 years ago. It is used for large-brained Middle Pleistocene humans of Africa and Europe between roughly 700,000 and 200,000 years ago, such as the Bodo cranium from Ethiopia, the Kabwe skull from Zambia and the Petralona skull from Greece. They had brains of about 1,100 to 1,400 cm³, heavy brow ridges and tall, robust bodies. They made Acheulean handaxes and later prepared-core tools, and hunted large animals, as at Boxgrove in England about 500,000 years ago.","debate":"Many researchers now doubt the name works. The 430,000-year-old Sima de los Huesos fossils, once seen as heidelbergensis, have Neanderthal features and Neanderthal-lineage DNA, and some argue the Mauer jaw itself could be an early member of the Neanderthal line, which would leave African fossils such as Kabwe, redated in 2020 to about 299,000 years, under the older name Homo rhodesiensis. In 2021 Mirjana Roksandic, Predrag Radović, Xiujie Wu and Christopher Bae proposed Homo bodoensis for African Middle Pleistocene humans, arguing that both older names were problematic; critics, including Eric Delson and Chris Stringer in 2022, objected that the proposal conflicts with the rules of zoological naming, and it has not been widely adopted. How Chinese Middle Pleistocene fossils such as Dali and Jinniushan relate to this group is also disputed.","status":"debated","chapter":"middle-pleistocene"},{"id":"homo-naledi","name":"Homo naledi","nickname":"","group":"early-homo","start":335000,"end":236000,"regions":["africa"],"brain":"about 465–610 cm³","stature":"about 1.4–1.5 m","discovered":"2013, Rising Star Cave, Cradle of Humankind, South Africa (cavers Rick Hunter and Steven Tucker); named in 2015 by Lee Berger and colleagues","keyFossils":"More than 1,500 bones from the Dinaledi Chamber, the Neo skeleton (LES1) from the Lesedi Chamber, the child’s skull Leti","summary":"In 2013 two cavers squeezed through a chute about 18 centimetres wide in the Rising Star Cave near Johannesburg and found a chamber strewn with bones; the excavation, led by Lee Berger with a team of slim scientists able to fit through, recovered more than 1,500 fossils from at least 15 individuals. Homo naledi combined a brain of only about 465 to 610 cm³ and curved fingers with human-like wrists, feet and legs. Dating published in 2017 showed that the fossils are only about 335,000 to 236,000 years old, so this small-brained species lived at the same time as early Homo sapiens in Africa. How so many bodies came to lie in a dark chamber far from any entrance remains unexplained.","debate":"Berger and colleagues argued in 2023 that naledi deliberately buried its dead, engraved cross-hatched marks on the cave wall and used fire to light its way, which would be remarkable for a species with such a small brain. Reviewers for the journal eLife and many other researchers judged the evidence incomplete, pointing to the lack of detailed geological and dating analyses of the claimed graves and engravings. Its place in the family tree is also unclear: it may be a late survivor of an early Homo lineage or a small-brained offshoot of later Homo.","status":"established","chapter":"middle-pleistocene"},{"id":"homo-floresiensis","name":"Homo floresiensis","nickname":"Hobbit","group":"erectus","start":700000,"end":60000,"endMin":50000,"regions":["oceania"],"brain":"about 420 cm³ (LB1)","stature":"about 1.0–1.1 m","discovered":"2003, Liang Bua, Flores, Indonesia (Indonesian–Australian team led by R. P. Soejono and Mike Morwood); named in 2004","keyFossils":"LB1 partial skeleton (holotype), remains of other individuals from Liang Bua, jaw fragment, teeth and humerus from Mata Menge","summary":"Found in 2003 in Liang Bua cave on the Indonesian island of Flores, the partial skeleton LB1 belonged to an adult about 1.06 metres tall with a brain of about 420 cm³, smaller than that of most australopiths. Homo floresiensis had long feet, short legs and primitive wrist and shoulder bones. Revised dating in 2016 placed the Liang Bua skeletons between about 100,000 and 60,000 years ago, and stone tools attributed to the species between about 190,000 and 50,000 years ago. Fossils from Mata Menge in central Flores show that tiny hominins already lived on the island about 700,000 years ago, and a humerus described in 2024 suggests they were even slightly smaller than LB1.","debate":"Early critics argued that LB1 was a modern human with a disorder such as microcephaly, but further individuals and detailed studies of the wrist, feet and brain shape have persuaded most researchers that it is a distinct species. The main open question is its ancestry: the Mata Menge finds support island dwarfing of an Asian Homo erectus population that reached Flores by about 1 million years ago, while analyses by Debbie Argue and colleagues suggest descent from an earlier, habilis-like hominin. Why it disappeared, around the time modern humans reached the region, is unknown.","status":"established","chapter":"island-hominins"},{"id":"homo-luzonensis","name":"Homo luzonensis","nickname":"Callao Man","group":"erectus","start":67000,"end":50000,"regions":["asia"],"brain":"unknown","stature":"unknown","discovered":"2007, Callao Cave, northern Luzon, Philippines (Armand Mijares’s team); named in 2019 by Florent Détroit and colleagues","keyFossils":"CCH1 foot bone, teeth of the holotype CCH6, finger and toe bones, part of a juvenile femur","summary":"In 2007 archaeologists led by Armand Mijares found a foot bone in Callao Cave on Luzon, in the northern Philippines. Later excavations recovered teeth and finger and toe bones from at least three individuals, and in 2019 Florent Détroit and colleagues named a new species, Homo luzonensis. Uranium-series dating gave minimum ages of about 67,000 and 50,000 years for the fossils. Its cheek teeth are very small and simple, while its curved finger and toe bones resemble those of australopiths, suggesting it still climbed.","debate":"With so few fossils, some researchers question whether luzonensis is truly distinct or an unusual population of Homo erectus. How its ancestors reached Luzon, which was never connected to the mainland, is unknown; butchered rhinoceros bones and stone tools at Kalinga show that hominins were on the island about 709,000 years ago. As on Flores, isolation is thought to have shaped its unusual anatomy, but no reliable estimate of its body size is yet possible.","status":"established","chapter":"island-hominins"},{"id":"neanderthals","name":"Homo neanderthalensis","nickname":"Neanderthals","group":"archaic","start":400000,"end":40000,"startMax":430000,"endMin":37000,"regions":["europe","asia"],"brain":"about 1,200–1,750 cm³","stature":"about 1.5–1.75 m","discovered":"1856, Feldhofer Cave, Neander Valley, Germany (quarry workers; recognised by Johann Carl Fuhlrott; named in 1864 by William King); earlier finds from Engis (1829) and Gibraltar (1848) were recognised later","keyFossils":"Feldhofer 1, La Chapelle-aux-Saints 1, La Ferrassie 1, Shanidar 1 and Shanidar Z, Kebara 2, Krapina, Vindija 33.19, Altai Neanderthal (Denisova 5), Chagyrskaya 8","summary":"Neanderthals lived across Europe and western and central Asia, from Gibraltar to the Altai Mountains of Siberia, from roughly 400,000 to 40,000 years ago; the 430,000-year-old Sima de los Huesos people in Spain already belonged to their lineage. They were stocky and strong, with large brains, big noses and long, low skulls, suited to cold climates but living in warm ones too. They made Mousterian tools, often by the Levallois method, hunted large game, produced birch-tar glue, cared for injured people and buried at least some of their dead; eagle talons, pigments and engraved lines suggest symbolic behaviour. Genomes sequenced since 2010 show that they interbred with Homo sapiens, leaving about 2 percent Neanderthal DNA in people of non-African descent today.","debate":"Why Neanderthals disappeared is the central debate: explanations include competition with incoming Homo sapiens, small and inbred populations, climate swings such as Heinrich event 4, disease, and absorption through interbreeding, and many researchers now favour a combination. How far their symbolic life matched ours is also argued: uranium-series dates of more than 64,000 years for painted marks in three Spanish caves (2018) are disputed, while the rings of broken stalagmites built deep in Bruniquel Cave in France about 176,000 years ago are widely accepted. Genomes such as that of Thorin, a late Neanderthal from Grotte Mandrin described in 2024, show that late populations were small and long isolated from one another.","status":"established","chapter":"neanderthals"},{"id":"denisovans","name":"Denisovans","nickname":"Dragon Man (the Harbin skull)","group":"archaic","start":250000,"end":40000,"startMax":430000,"endMin":30000,"regions":["asia"],"brain":"about 1,420 cm³ (Harbin skull); otherwise unknown","stature":"unknown","discovered":"2008, Denisova Cave, Altai Mountains, Siberia (finger bone excavated by Russian archaeologists; identified from its DNA in 2010 by Svante Pääbo’s team)","keyFossils":"Denisova 3 finger bone, Denisova 4 and 8 molars, Denisova 11, Xiahe mandible, Tam Ngu Hao 2 molar (Laos), Harbin cranium, Penghu 1 mandible, Baishiya rib fragment","summary":"Denisovans were first identified in 2010 not from their bones but from DNA in a finger bone from Denisova Cave in Siberia, which revealed a population closely related to Neanderthals that had separated from them more than 400,000 years ago. For a decade they were known only from a few teeth and scraps, until ancient proteins identified a jaw from Xiahe on the Tibetan Plateau in 2019. In 2025 proteins showed that the Penghu 1 jaw, dredged from the seabed off Taiwan, belonged to a Denisovan man, and proteins plus mitochondrial DNA from dental calculus showed that the Harbin skull from northeast China, more than 146,000 years old, was Denisovan, giving the group a face: heavy brows, a broad face, very large molars and a brain of about 1,420 cm³. Their genes survive in living people, especially in New Guinea, Australia and the Philippines, and a Denisovan version of the EPAS1 gene helps Tibetans live at high altitude.","debate":"The Harbin skull had been named Homo longi, “dragon man”, in 2021 by Qiang Ji, Xijun Ni and colleagues, so many now argue that Denisovans should take that name, while others want nuclear DNA or more fossils first. In 2024 Xiujie Wu and Christopher Bae proposed Homo juluensis, based on the large-headed Xujiayao fossils, to include the Xuchang, Xiahe and Penghu remains and the Denisovans, a proposal that competes with Homo longi and has not been widely adopted. A 2025 reconstruction of the million-year-old Yunxian 2 skull by Xijun Ni, Chris Stringer and colleagues placed it on the Homo longi branch, implying that the lineages leading to Denisovans, Neanderthals and modern humans separated more than a million years ago, far earlier than most genetic estimates; this remains debated.","status":"established","chapter":"denisovans"},{"id":"homo-sapiens","name":"Homo sapiens","nickname":"Modern humans","group":"sapiens","start":315000,"end":0,"startMax":350000,"regions":["africa","europe","asia","oceania","americas"],"brain":"about 1,100–1,700 cm³ (living average about 1,350)","stature":"about 1.4–1.9 m, varying widely between populations","discovered":"Named by Carl Linnaeus in 1758; the oldest fossils assigned to the species, from Jebel Irhoud, Morocco, were found in 1961 and redated in 2017","keyFossils":"Jebel Irhoud 1 and 10, Florisbad, Omo Kibish 1, Herto (BOU-VP-16/1), Misliya 1, Skhul and Qafzeh, Apidima 1 (disputed)","summary":"Our species emerged in Africa: the oldest fossils with sapiens-like faces, from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco, are about 315,000 years old, and Omo Kibish 1 in Ethiopia is at least 233,000 years old. Early members of the species had modern faces but longer, lower braincases; the rounded skull of living people appeared gradually over the following 200,000 years. Small groups reached western Asia by about 190,000 years ago, as the Misliya jaw shows, but the ancestors of all living non-Africans left Africa roughly 60,000 to 50,000 years ago, mixing with Neanderthals and Denisovans as they spread. They reached Sahul by at least 50,000 years ago and the Americas by the end of the last Ice Age, perhaps as early as 23,000 years ago.","debate":"Most researchers now favour a pan-African origin, in which populations across the continent, linked by intermittent gene flow, gradually assembled modern traits, rather than a single cradle in East Africa. Genetic models differ: a 2025 study by Trevor Cousins, Aylwyn Scally and Richard Durbin inferred that all living humans descend from two populations that separated about 1.5 million years ago and merged about 300,000 years ago in proportions of roughly 80 and 20 percent, while Aaron Ragsdale and colleagues (2023) favour a weakly structured ancestral population with continuous mixing. Whether the Jebel Irhoud people belong in Homo sapiens or represent a close relative is also argued.","status":"established","chapter":"origin-of-sapiens"}],"geneFlow":[{"from":"neanderthals","to":"homo-sapiens","when":47000,"label":"Neanderthal ancestry in all non-Africans","amount":"roughly 2 percent of every non-African genome","confidence":"established","text":"Neanderthal DNA segments found in all non-African populations point to interbreeding soon after the main dispersal of modern humans from Africa. Two 2024 studies, one led by Arev Sümer and Johannes Krause using 45,000-year-old genomes from Ranis in Germany and Zlatý kůň in Czechia, the other led by Leonardo Iasi and Benjamin Peter, dated the main period of mixing to roughly 50,000 to 43,500 years ago, centred near 47,000 years ago."},{"from":"neanderthals","to":"homo-sapiens","when":42000,"label":"Local mixing with the first modern Europeans","amount":"about 3 to 9 percent in some individuals","confidence":"established","text":"Some of the earliest modern humans in Europe carried long Neanderthal segments from recent ancestors: the roughly 40,000-year-old Oase 1 jaw from Romania had a Neanderthal ancestor only four to six generations back, and people from Bacho Kiro Cave in Bulgaria, about 45,000 years old, had Neanderthal ancestors a few generations back. These early groups left little or no ancestry in later Europeans."},{"from":"denisovans","to":"homo-sapiens","when":45000,"label":"Denisovan ancestry in New Guinea, Australia and the Philippines","amount":"about 3–6 percent in Papuans and Aboriginal Australians; highest in the Ayta Magbukon of Luzon","confidence":"established","text":"Papuan and Aboriginal Australian genomes contain several percent Denisovan DNA, and a 2021 study led by Maximilian Larena found the highest levels in the Ayta Magbukon, a Philippine Negrito group. Guy Jacobs, Murray Cox and colleagues showed in 2019 that Papuans carry segments from two distinct Denisovan populations, implying separate pulses of mixing, perhaps in Wallacea or New Guinea; some estimates place one of them as late as roughly 30,000 years ago, which is debated."},{"from":"denisovans","to":"homo-sapiens","when":45000,"label":"Denisovan ancestry in mainland Asians","amount":"a few tenths of a percent or less in East and South Asians","confidence":"established","text":"Mainland East and South Asians carry a much smaller Denisovan share. Sharon Browning, Joshua Akey and colleagues reported in 2018 that East Asians carry segments from two different Denisovan groups, one closely related to the Altai Denisovans, implying more than one pulse. A Denisovan version of the EPAS1 gene, identified by Emilia Huerta-Sánchez and colleagues in 2014, helps Tibetans cope with high altitude."},{"from":"homo-sapiens","to":"neanderthals","when":250000,"label":"Early modern human DNA replaces Neanderthal mitochondria and Y chromosomes","amount":"a few percent of later Neanderthal genomes; all of their mitochondrial DNA and Y chromosomes","confidence":"established","text":"Neanderthal nuclear genomes are closest to Denisovans, yet their mitochondrial DNA and Y chromosomes are closer to those of modern humans. Cosimo Posth and colleagues (2017) and Martin Petr and colleagues (2020) explained this by early mixing, more than 200,000 years ago, with a population related to Homo sapiens whose maternal and paternal lineages then spread through the small Neanderthal population. A 2024 analysis led by Liming Li and Joshua Akey estimated the main early pulse at roughly 250,000 years ago."},{"from":"homo-sapiens","to":"neanderthals","when":100000,"label":"A later modern human pulse into eastern Neanderthals","amount":"small; detected in the Altai Neanderthal genome","confidence":"debated","text":"Martin Kuhlwilm, Sergi Castellano and colleagues reported in 2016 that the Altai Neanderthal from Siberia carries DNA from an early modern human population that separated from other Africans very early, suggesting mixing around 100,000 years ago, possibly in western Asia, where early Homo sapiens are known at Skhul and Qafzeh. The timing and size of this pulse are less certain than for the older one."},{"from":"neanderthals","to":"denisovans","when":90000,"label":"Denisova 11: a Neanderthal mother and a Denisovan father","amount":"a first-generation hybrid; Neanderthal segments also occur in other Denisovan genomes","confidence":"established","text":"A bone fragment from Denisova Cave, recognised as human by collagen fingerprinting, yielded a genome in 2018 showing that its owner, a girl at least 13 years old who lived about 90,000 years ago, had a Neanderthal mother and a Denisovan father (Viviane Slon, Svante Pääbo and colleagues). Her mother’s population was closer to later Neanderthals from Croatia than to earlier Altai Neanderthals, and her father himself had some Neanderthal ancestry, so the two groups met repeatedly."},{"from":"superarchaic","to":"denisovans","when":400000,"label":"A deeply divergent “superarchaic” lineage in Denisovan genomes","amount":"uncertain; estimates range from under 1 to several percent","confidence":"debated","text":"Denisovan genomes contain segments more distant from all other humans than Neanderthal DNA is, suggesting mixing with a population that split from the human line well over a million years ago; some researchers suggest a late form of Homo erectus in Asia (Kay Prüfer and colleagues, 2014). Alan Rogers and colleagues argued in 2020 that a similar event affected the shared ancestors of Neanderthals and Denisovans about 700,000 years ago, from a lineage that separated about 2 million years ago. The timing and identity of the source remain uncertain."},{"from":"ghost-archaic","to":"homo-sapiens","when":43000,"label":"Ghost archaic ancestry in West Africans","amount":"estimated at 2–19 percent in Yoruba and Mende genomes","confidence":"debated","text":"Arun Durvasula and Sriram Sankararaman reported in 2020 that genomes of Yoruba and Mende people from West Africa contain segments best explained by mixing, perhaps around 43,000 years ago, with an unknown archaic population that split from the ancestors of Neanderthals and modern humans between about 360,000 and 1 million years ago. No fossils or ancient genomes of this population are known, since DNA rarely survives in tropical Africa. Aaron Ragsdale and colleagues showed in 2023 that long-standing, weakly connected structure among ancestral African populations could produce similar signals without any separate archaic group."}],"glossary":[{"term":"Acheulean","definition":"A stone tool tradition defined by large bifacial tools, above all handaxes and cleavers, named after Saint-Acheul in northern France. It appeared about 1.76 million years ago in East Africa and lasted until roughly 200,000 years ago, made mainly by Homo erectus and later by Middle Pleistocene humans across Africa, Europe and much of Asia."},{"term":"Admixture","definition":"Interbreeding between populations that had been separated for some time, and the mixed ancestry that results. Geneticists express it as the share of a genome traced to each source population, for example the roughly 2 percent Neanderthal ancestry of non-Africans."},{"term":"Anatomically modern humans","definition":"An older term for Homo sapiens with a skeleton like that of living people. It is used less today, because the earliest members of our species, such as those from Jebel Irhoud, had modern faces but longer, lower braincases, so modern anatomy assembled gradually."},{"term":"Ancient DNA","definition":"Genetic material recovered from the remains of dead organisms, usually as short, chemically damaged fragments. Since the first Neanderthal genome in 2010 it has transformed the study of human origins and migrations; the oldest human DNA so far, from Sima de los Huesos in Spain, is about 430,000 years old."},{"term":"Archaic humans","definition":"A loose label for members of the genus Homo other than Homo sapiens, such as Neanderthals, Denisovans and Homo heidelbergensis. It implies no inferiority: these groups were relatives, and through interbreeding some are among the ancestors of living people."},{"term":"Arsenical copper","definition":"Copper containing a few percent of arsenic, from mixed ores or added deliberately, which makes it harder and easier to cast than pure copper. It was the main alloy of the Chalcolithic and Early Bronze Age from the Levant and the Caucasus to Iberia, before tin bronze took over."},{"term":"Assemblage","definition":"All the artefacts, or all the animal bones, found together in one layer or phase of a site, studied as a group. Comparing assemblages is how archaeologists define industries and cultures."},{"term":"Aterian","definition":"A Middle Stone Age industry of North Africa and the Sahara, named after Bir el-Ater in Algeria and recognised by tanged (stemmed) points and scrapers that were probably hafted. Made by Homo sapiens, it dates roughly from 150,000 to 30,000 years ago and often occurs with shell beads and ochre."},{"term":"Atlatl (spear-thrower)","definition":"A hooked stick or board that effectively lengthens the throwing arm, launching a light spear, or dart, much faster than a hand throw. Carved antler examples survive from the Upper Palaeolithic of France, especially the Magdalenian, and the device was also used in the Americas and in Australia, where it is called a woomera."},{"term":"Aurignacian","definition":"The first widespread Upper Palaeolithic culture of Europe, named after Aurignac in southwest France and dated roughly 43,000 to 33,000 years ago. Its makers, Homo sapiens, produced bone and antler points, beads, ivory figurines such as the Lion Man of Hohlenstein-Stadel, bone flutes and the paintings of Chauvet Cave."},{"term":"Backed tool","definition":"A blade, bladelet or flake with one edge deliberately blunted by steep retouch, usually so it could be glued into a handle or shaft or held safely. Backed pieces are typical of the Howiesons Poort in Africa and of the Gravettian and later cultures in Europe."},{"term":"Bell Beaker","definition":"A cultural phenomenon of western and central Europe, roughly 2800 to 2200 BCE, named after bell-shaped drinking cups placed in graves with archery equipment, copper daggers and gold ornaments. Ancient DNA shows that in Britain the Beaker period brought people with steppe-related ancestry who replaced about 90 percent of the local gene pool within a few centuries (Olalde et al. 2018)."},{"term":"Beringia","definition":"The land that connected northeast Siberia and Alaska when sea levels were low during glacial periods, now largely under the Bering and Chukchi seas. Much of it stayed free of ice, and the ancestors of Native Americans lived there before moving south; how long they stayed, the so-called Beringian standstill, is debated."},{"term":"Biface","definition":"A stone tool shaped by removing flakes from both faces to create an edge around most of its outline. Handaxes and cleavers are the classic early bifaces; later ones include the thin leaf-shaped points of the Still Bay and the Solutrean."},{"term":"Bipedalism","definition":"Habitual walking on two legs, the defining trait of hominins. It is recognised in fossils by a forward-placed opening for the spinal cord, a short, bowl-shaped pelvis, an angled thigh bone and an arched foot, and directly in the 3.66-million-year-old footprints at Laetoli, Tanzania."},{"term":"Blade","definition":"A flake at least twice as long as it is wide, with roughly parallel edges, struck from a specially prepared core. Blade making is a hallmark of the Upper Palaeolithic and Later Stone Age, but blades were also produced much earlier, for example in the Kapthurin Formation of Kenya about 500,000 years ago."},{"term":"Bladelet","definition":"A small, narrow blade, conventionally less than about 12 millimetres wide. Bladelets were often backed or shaped into microliths and set in rows into bone or wooden shafts as barbs and cutting edges."},{"term":"Bottleneck","definition":"A sharp fall in population size that reduces genetic diversity for many generations afterwards. A 2023 study led by Wangjie Hu proposed that human ancestors fell to about 1,280 breeding individuals between about 930,000 and 813,000 years ago, a claim many geneticists doubt."},{"term":"BP (before present)","definition":"A convention used mainly for radiocarbon dates, in which the present is fixed at 1950 CE. This app counts years ago from 2000 CE instead, so its figures are 50 years larger than BP figures for the same moment."},{"term":"Broad-spectrum revolution","definition":"Kent Flannery’s 1969 term for a shift to a much wider range of foods, including small game, birds, fish, shellfish and wild seeds, in the late Pleistocene. Once seen as a prelude to farming in Southwest Asia, it is now known to vary widely in timing; Ohalo II in Israel shows a very broad diet 23,000 years ago."},{"term":"Burin","definition":"A stone tool with a narrow chisel-like edge made by striking a small spall off the end or side of a blade or flake. Burins were used to engrave and to cut grooves in bone, antler and ivory, and are common in Upper Palaeolithic assemblages."},{"term":"Calibration","definition":"The conversion of a radiocarbon measurement into calendar years, needed because the amount of carbon-14 in the atmosphere has varied over time. The current international curves, published in 2020 (IntCal20 for the northern hemisphere, SHCal20 for the southern and Marine20 for the oceans), are built from tree rings, corals, cave formations and lake sediments."},{"term":"Cardial","definition":"An early Neolithic culture of the western Mediterranean, named after pottery decorated by pressing the edge of a cockle shell (Cerastoderma, formerly called Cardium) into the clay. Its farmers spread largely by sea along the coasts of Italy, southern France and Iberia from the middle of the sixth millennium BCE."},{"term":"Chaîne opératoire","definition":"French for “operational sequence”: the whole chain of choices and actions from obtaining raw material through shaping, use, repair and discard of an object. The concept, developed by André Leroi-Gourhan, lets archaeologists compare how things were made, not just what they look like."},{"term":"Chalcolithic","definition":"The Copper Age, when people smelted and used copper but tin bronze was not yet common and stone tools remained important. In southeastern Europe and Southwest Asia it spans roughly the fifth and fourth millennia BCE, with much regional variation."},{"term":"Châtelperronian","definition":"An industry of central and southwestern France and northern Spain, about 45,000 to 40,000 years ago, named after Châtelperron in central France and characterised by curved backed knives and blades. Most researchers attribute it to late Neanderthals, based on the Saint-Césaire skeleton and Neanderthal remains from the Grotte du Renne, although some argue that these associations result from mixed layers."},{"term":"Clade","definition":"A group that includes a common ancestor and all of its descendants: a natural branch of the family tree. Whether the robust australopiths form a clade, for example, decides whether they deserve their own genus, Paranthropus."},{"term":"Cleaver","definition":"A large biface with a broad, straight cutting edge at one end instead of a point. Cleavers, often made on big flakes, are especially common in the Acheulean of Africa, the Levant and India."},{"term":"Clovis","definition":"A North American culture named after Clovis, New Mexico, dated to a few centuries around 13,000 years ago and known for fluted spear points, some found with mammoth bones. For decades it was thought to represent the first Americans, but sites such as Monte Verde in Chile and the White Sands footprints in New Mexico show that people arrived earlier."},{"term":"Corded Ware","definition":"A culture of northern, central and eastern Europe, about 2900 to 2350 BCE, named after pottery decorated by pressing cord into the wet clay and known for single graves under mounds. Ancient DNA studies since 2015 show its people had roughly three quarters of their ancestry from steppe populations related to the Yamnaya, evidence of a large movement of people."},{"term":"Core","definition":"The block of stone from which flakes or blades are struck. How a core was prepared and worked, for instance by the Levallois method, is one of the main ways archaeologists distinguish stone tool traditions."},{"term":"Cryptotephra","definition":"Volcanic ash shards too small or too few to see with the naked eye, extracted from sediments in the laboratory. Because each eruption has a chemical fingerprint, cryptotephra can link sites thousands of kilometres apart; shards from the Toba eruption about 74,000 years ago have been identified at Pinnacle Point in South Africa, some 9,000 kilometres away."},{"term":"Dansgaard–Oeschger event","definition":"One of about 25 abrupt warmings recorded in Greenland ice cores during the last glacial period, when temperatures there rose by several degrees, sometimes more than 10 °C, within decades, followed by slower cooling. Named after Willi Dansgaard and Hans Oeschger, these swings affected climates far beyond the North Atlantic."},{"term":"Doggerland","definition":"The low-lying land that joined Britain to continental Europe across the southern North Sea during and after the last Ice Age, a name coined by the archaeologist Bryony Coles. Rising seas drowned it gradually; the Storegga tsunami of around 6200 BCE struck its remaining coasts, and the last islands disappeared within the following millennium or so."},{"term":"Dolmen","definition":"A megalithic tomb made of upright stones supporting one or more large capstones, originally often covered by a mound of earth or stones. Dolmens were built by Neolithic communities across Atlantic Europe and the Mediterranean, and similar monuments were raised elsewhere, notably in Korea."},{"term":"Domestication","definition":"A long process in which people and plants or animals became mutually dependent, with human selection changing the organisms’ genes, bodies and behaviour. Archaeological signs include cereals whose ripe grains no longer fall from the ear, larger seeds, and animals that are smaller or were culled at ages and sexes that point to herding."},{"term":"Early Stone Age","definition":"The first stage of African archaeology, from the oldest stone tools more than 3 million years ago to roughly 300,000 years ago. It includes the Oldowan and the Acheulean and corresponds broadly to the Lower Palaeolithic of Eurasia."},{"term":"Epigravettian","definition":"The late Upper Palaeolithic cultures of Italy, the Balkans and eastern Europe that followed the Gravettian, from around the Last Glacial Maximum to the end of the Pleistocene, with backed points and bladelets. Ancient DNA studies show that people associated with the Epigravettian spread their ancestry widely across Europe from about 14,000 years ago."},{"term":"Epipalaeolithic","definition":"In Southwest Asia and North Africa, the period between the Upper Palaeolithic and the Neolithic, about 24,000 to 11,600 years ago, marked by microliths and increasingly settled life. In the Levant it includes the Kebaran, Geometric Kebaran and Natufian cultures."},{"term":"Flake","definition":"A piece of stone struck from a core, with a striking platform, a bulb of percussion and naturally sharp edges. Flakes were used directly as knives or shaped further by retouch into scrapers, points and other tools."},{"term":"Founder crops","definition":"The eight plants usually counted as the first domesticated crops of Southwest Asia: emmer wheat, einkorn wheat, barley, lentil, pea, chickpea, bitter vetch and flax. The idea was popularised by the botanist Daniel Zohary, but other plants, such as faba beans, were also cultivated early, so the list is now seen as incomplete."},{"term":"Genome coverage","definition":"The average number of times each position in a genome is read during sequencing. The Altai Neanderthal genome published in 2014 had about 52-fold coverage, enough to determine each position reliably, while many ancient genomes have less than 1-fold coverage yet are still useful for studying populations."},{"term":"Ghost population","definition":"A population known only from traces of its DNA in the genomes of others, with no directly sequenced remains. The Ancient North Eurasians were such a ghost until 2014, when the genome of a 24,000-year-old boy from Mal’ta in Siberia matched the prediction; the archaic ghost in West African genomes has not been identified."},{"term":"Glacial and interglacial","definition":"Glacials are long cold periods when ice sheets grow and sea level falls; interglacials are the shorter warm periods between them, like the present Holocene and the Eemian, about 130,000 to 115,000 years ago. Over the last 800,000 years they have alternated roughly every 100,000 years."},{"term":"Gravettian","definition":"An Upper Palaeolithic culture that spread from Portugal to Russia roughly 33,000 to 25,000 years ago, named after La Gravette in the Dordogne. It is known for narrow backed points, female figurines such as those of Willendorf and Dolní Věstonice, figures of fired clay and richly furnished burials."},{"term":"Ground stone","definition":"Tools shaped by pecking and grinding rather than by flaking, including grinding slabs, querns, mortars, pestles and axes. Grinding stones for plant foods were used at Ohalo II 23,000 years ago, and edge-ground axes appear in northern Australia more than 40,000 years ago."},{"term":"Hafting","definition":"Fixing a stone point or blade to a handle or shaft with bindings, adhesive or both, creating a composite tool. Evidence may go back 500,000 years at Kathu Pan in South Africa and is widespread after about 300,000 years ago; Neanderthals made birch-tar glue about 200,000 years ago, and people at Sibudu in South Africa mixed plant gum with ochre into compound adhesives."},{"term":"Handaxe","definition":"A large, teardrop-shaped or oval stone tool flaked on both faces, the signature tool of the Acheulean. Handaxes served as all-purpose tools for butchery, woodworking and cutting plants, and were made for more than 1.5 million years."},{"term":"Haplotype","definition":"A set of DNA variants inherited together along one stretch of a chromosome. Families of related haplotypes in mitochondrial DNA or the Y chromosome are called haplogroups and are used to trace maternal and paternal lines."},{"term":"Harpoon","definition":"A barbed point designed to lodge in prey, often detaching from its shaft and tied to a line. Barbed bone points from Katanda in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, dated to about 90,000 years ago, are the oldest claimed; antler harpoons are typical of the European Magdalenian."},{"term":"Heat treatment","definition":"Controlled heating of stone such as silcrete or flint to make it more glassy and easier to flake finely. At Pinnacle Point in South Africa it is documented from about 164,000 years ago and was routine by about 72,000 years ago (Brown et al. 2009)."},{"term":"Heinrich event","definition":"An episode during the last glacial period when huge numbers of icebergs broke off the North American ice sheet into the North Atlantic, leaving layers of rock debris on the sea floor. Named after Hartmut Heinrich, who described the layers in 1988, these events brought cold, dry conditions to Europe and weakened monsoons in Africa and Asia."},{"term":"Henge","definition":"In British archaeology, a circular or oval enclosure with a bank outside its ditch, the reverse of a defensive layout, built from about 3000 BCE. Avebury and Durrington Walls are henges; Stonehenge, whose main bank lies inside its ditch, strictly is not."},{"term":"Holocene","definition":"The current geological epoch, which began 11,700 years ago with the end of the Younger Dryas cold phase, as defined in a Greenland ice core. Since 2018 it has been divided into the Greenlandian, Northgrippian and Meghalayan ages."},{"term":"Holotype","definition":"The single specimen chosen to anchor a species name. OH 7 is the holotype of Homo habilis and the Harbin skull that of Homo longi, so any fossil judged to belong with them must take their names."},{"term":"Hominin","definition":"Humans and all extinct species more closely related to us than to chimpanzees and bonobos, including Sahelanthropus, Ardipithecus, Australopithecus, Paranthropus and Homo. The older term hominid now usually covers all great apes and humans."},{"term":"Howiesons Poort","definition":"A Middle Stone Age industry of southern Africa, about 65,000 to 59,000 years ago, named after a site near Makhanda (Grahamstown). It is known for small backed segments, probably hafted as arrow tips or barbs, and for engraved ostrich eggshell containers at Diepkloof."},{"term":"Iberomaurusian","definition":"A Later Stone Age industry of northwest Africa, roughly 25,000 to 11,000 years ago, rich in small backed bladelets; the name reflects a mistaken early idea of links with Iberia. At Taforalt in Morocco its people buried their dead in a cave cemetery, and genomes about 15,000 years old show a mix of Near Eastern and sub-Saharan African ancestry."},{"term":"Industry","definition":"A set of stone tool types and making techniques that recurs across many sites and layers, such as the Oldowan or the Mousterian. Archaeologists usually speak of a culture when more than tools, such as art, burial customs or houses, is shared."},{"term":"Introgression","definition":"The transfer of genetic material from one population or species into another through hybridisation followed by back-crossing. A Denisovan version of the EPAS1 gene that helps Tibetans cope with high altitude is a well-known example of introgression from archaic humans."},{"term":"Isotope analysis","definition":"Measuring ratios of stable isotopes, such as those of carbon, nitrogen, oxygen and strontium, in bones, teeth or shells. Carbon and nitrogen reveal diet, oxygen reflects climate and water sources, and strontium, which mirrors local geology, shows where a person or animal grew up."},{"term":"IUP (Initial Upper Palaeolithic)","definition":"Early blade industries made with distinctive core techniques, found from the Levant and Bulgaria to Siberia, Mongolia and northern China roughly 50,000 to 40,000 years ago. Many researchers link them to early dispersals of Homo sapiens, which human remains from Bacho Kiro Cave in Bulgaria confirm for Europe."},{"term":"Jōmon","definition":"The pottery-making culture of hunter-gatherers and fishers in the Japanese archipelago, lasting from about 16,000 years ago until rice farming spread in the first millennium BCE. Named after the cord-marked decoration of its pots, it produced large villages such as Sannai-Maruyama and elaborate clay figurines called dogū."},{"term":"ka and Ma","definition":"Abbreviations for thousands of years (kilo-annum) and millions of years (mega-annum), usually meaning thousands or millions of years ago. So 40 ka means about 40,000 years ago and 1.8 Ma about 1.8 million years ago."},{"term":"Knapping","definition":"Shaping stone by striking or pressing off flakes. Knappers use hard hammers of stone, soft hammers of antler, bone or wood, or pressure tools, and choose fine-grained stones such as flint, obsidian or quartzite that break predictably."},{"term":"Kurgan","definition":"A burial mound raised over one or more graves, especially on the Eurasian steppe; the word entered Russian from Turkic languages. Yamnaya kurgans of about 3300 to 2600 BCE gave their name to Marija Gimbutas’s Kurgan hypothesis of 1956, which linked steppe herders to the spread of Indo-European languages."},{"term":"Last Glacial Maximum (LGM)","definition":"The time when the ice sheets of the last glacial period were at their largest, about 26,000 to 19,000 years ago, with global sea level some 120 to 130 metres lower than today. Northern Europe, much of North America and high mountains lay under ice, and people withdrew to refugia further south."},{"term":"Later Stone Age","definition":"The final stage of the Stone Age in sub-Saharan Africa, beginning roughly 50,000 to 40,000 years ago in some regions, with microliths, bone tools and ornaments such as ostrich eggshell beads. In parts of southern Africa, Later Stone Age technology continued into recent centuries."},{"term":"LBK (Linear Pottery culture)","definition":"The Linearbandkeramik, the first farming culture of central Europe, about 5500 to 4900 BCE, named after bands of incised lines on its pottery. Its farmers built timber longhouses, some more than 30 metres long, and spread from Hungary to the Paris Basin and Ukraine within a few centuries."},{"term":"Levallois","definition":"A prepared-core technique in which a knapper shapes a core so that a flake of predetermined size and shape can be struck off with a single blow. Named after Levallois-Perret near Paris, it spread in Africa and Eurasia from roughly 400,000 to 300,000 years ago and became the typical method of the Middle Stone Age and Middle Palaeolithic."},{"term":"Lomekwian","definition":"The name proposed for stone tools about 3.3 million years old found in 2011 at Lomekwi 3, west of Lake Turkana in Kenya: large, simply struck cores and flakes older than any known fossil of Homo. Some researchers question whether the tools really come from such old layers, but many accept them as the earliest known."},{"term":"Lost-wax casting","definition":"A method of casting metal in which a model is made in wax, encased in clay and heated so that the wax melts out, leaving a mould for molten metal. Among the earliest examples are the arsenical copper objects of the Nahal Mishmar hoard in the southern Levant, of the late fifth or early fourth millennium BCE, and a copper amulet from Mehrgarh in Pakistan about 6,000 years old."},{"term":"Lower Palaeolithic","definition":"The earliest part of the Palaeolithic, from the first stone tools to roughly 300,000 years ago, including the Oldowan, the Acheulean and simple core-and-flake industries."},{"term":"Magdalenian","definition":"The last major Upper Palaeolithic culture of western and central Europe, roughly 21,000 to 14,000 years ago, named after La Madeleine in the Dordogne. Its people made antler harpoons, decorated spear-throwers, eyed needles and much of the famous cave art, including that of Altamira and Niaux, and they resettled northern Europe as the ice retreated."},{"term":"Marine Isotope Stage (MIS)","definition":"A numbered stage in the climate history recorded by oxygen isotopes in deep-sea sediments: odd numbers are warm periods, even numbers cold ones. MIS 1 is the Holocene, MIS 2 includes the Last Glacial Maximum, and MIS 5e is the last interglacial, about 130,000 to 115,000 years ago."},{"term":"Megafauna","definition":"Large animals, usually defined as those weighing more than about 44 kilograms. Many, such as mammoths, giant ground sloths and Australia’s Diprotodon, died out in the late Pleistocene; how far humans and how far climate change were responsible is debated and differed between continents."},{"term":"Megalith","definition":"A large stone used, alone or with others, to build a monument. Megalithic monuments such as dolmens, passage tombs, standing stones and stone circles were built in Atlantic Europe from about 4500 BCE onwards, and in many other parts of the world at different times."},{"term":"Megasite","definition":"An exceptionally large settlement for its time. The term is used especially for Cucuteni–Trypillia sites in Ukraine such as Maidanetske, Nebelivka and Talianki, which around 4000 BCE covered up to about 320 hectares, with hundreds to thousands of houses arranged in concentric rings."},{"term":"Menhir","definition":"A single standing stone, from Breton words meaning “long stone”. The largest known, the Grand Menhir Brisé at Locmariaquer in Brittany, was more than 20 metres long before it fell and broke."},{"term":"Mesolithic","definition":"Literally the “middle stone age”, but used only for Europe and neighbouring regions: the period of post-glacial hunter-gatherers from about 11,700 years ago until farming arrived, some 8,500 years ago in the southeast and about 6,000 years ago in Britain and Scandinavia. It is known for microliths, canoes, fishing gear and settlements such as Star Carr and Lepenski Vir."},{"term":"Microlith","definition":"A very small stone tool, usually less than about 3 centimetres long, often made from a bladelet and shaped into a geometric form such as a triangle, crescent or trapeze. Microliths were set into shafts and handles as points and barbs; they appear at Pinnacle Point in South Africa about 71,000 years ago and dominate many Mesolithic and Epipalaeolithic industries."},{"term":"Middle Palaeolithic","definition":"The Eurasian period from roughly 300,000 to 40,000 years ago, characterised by prepared-core flake tools such as those of the Mousterian. In Europe it is associated with Neanderthals, in western Asia with both Neanderthals and early Homo sapiens."},{"term":"Middle Stone Age","definition":"The African stage from roughly 300,000 years ago until between 50,000 and 25,000 years ago, depending on the region, characterised by prepared cores, points and scrapers, often hafted. It spans the emergence of Homo sapiens and includes early pigments, beads and engravings, as at Blombos Cave."},{"term":"Mousterian","definition":"The main Middle Palaeolithic industry of Europe and western Asia, named after Le Moustier in the Dordogne, with scrapers, points and flakes often made by the Levallois method. It was made mostly by Neanderthals, but in the Levant also by early Homo sapiens, between roughly 300,000 and 40,000 years ago."},{"term":"Movius line","definition":"A line drawn by the archaeologist Hallam Movius in 1948 to separate a western region with Acheulean handaxes from an East Asian region supposedly without them. Handaxes found since, for example in the Bose Basin of southern China about 800,000 years ago, have blurred the line, although handaxes are indeed rarer in East Asia."},{"term":"mtDNA (mitochondrial DNA)","definition":"The small circular genome, 16,569 base pairs long, of the cell’s mitochondria, inherited only from the mother. Because each cell holds many copies, it is easier to recover from ancient remains than nuclear DNA, but it records only one line of ancestry."},{"term":"Natufian","definition":"A late Epipalaeolithic culture of the Levant, about 15,000 to 11,600 years ago, whose people lived in villages of round stone houses, harvested wild cereals with sickles and buried their dead in cemeteries. Charred bread-like remains from Shubayqa 1 in Jordan, about 14,400 years old, are thousands of years older than farming."},{"term":"Neolithic","definition":"The “New Stone Age”, a term coined by John Lubbock in 1865 for the period of polished stone tools, now defined mainly by farming and herding, often with villages, pottery and ground stone tools. It began about 11,600 years ago in Southwest Asia and at different times elsewhere, in several regions independently, as in China, New Guinea and the Americas."},{"term":"Obsidian","definition":"Natural volcanic glass that breaks with extremely sharp edges. Each source has a distinctive chemical signature, so obsidian artefacts show how far stone was carried or traded; at Olorgesailie in Kenya, people about 300,000 years ago used obsidian from sources 25 to 95 kilometres away."},{"term":"Ochre","definition":"Earth pigments rich in iron oxides, red (haematite) or yellow (goethite), ground into powder for paint, adhesives and hide working, among other uses. Hominins were using pigments by about 300,000 years ago in East Africa, and pieces of ochre engraved with cross-hatched lines from Blombos Cave in South Africa are about 75,000 to 100,000 years old."},{"term":"Oldowan","definition":"The earliest widespread stone tool industry, named after Olduvai Gorge in Tanzania: sharp flakes struck from cobbles, together with the cores and hammerstones used to make them. The oldest Oldowan tools, at Nyayanga in Kenya, date to between about 3.0 and 2.6 million years ago, and the tradition persisted until about 1.7 million years ago, and later in places."},{"term":"OSL (optically stimulated luminescence)","definition":"A dating method that measures when sediment grains of quartz or feldspar were last exposed to sunlight. Natural radiation gradually fills tiny traps in the crystals with electrons; in the laboratory, light releases them as a glow proportional to the time since burial."},{"term":"Palaeolithic","definition":"The “Old Stone Age”, a term coined by John Lubbock in 1865, from the first stone tools more than 3 million years ago to the end of the Pleistocene about 11,700 years ago. In Eurasia it is divided into Lower, Middle and Upper Palaeolithic; in sub-Saharan Africa the Early, Middle and Later Stone Age are used instead."},{"term":"Passage tomb","definition":"A Neolithic monument in which a long stone-lined passage leads to a burial chamber beneath a large mound or cairn. Famous examples include Newgrange in Ireland, built around 3200 BCE, whose passage is lit by the rising sun at the winter solstice, Maeshowe in Orkney and Gavrinis in Brittany."},{"term":"Pastoralism","definition":"A way of life based on herding domestic animals such as cattle, sheep, goats, camels or reindeer, often with seasonal moves between pastures. In northern Africa, cattle herding spread across the then-green Sahara long before crops were farmed there."},{"term":"Phytolith","definition":"A microscopic particle of silica formed inside plant cells, which survives long after the rest of the plant has decayed. Phytolith shapes are distinctive enough to identify many plant groups, such as grasses, palms and rice husks, in sediments and on tools."},{"term":"Pleistocene","definition":"The geological epoch from 2.58 million to 11,700 years ago, marked by repeated cycles of glacials and interglacials. Almost all of human evolution and the whole of the Palaeolithic took place within it."},{"term":"Polished axe","definition":"A stone axe or adze head shaped by grinding and polishing, which made it tougher and less likely to chip in heavy woodworking. Polished axes are typical of the Neolithic; axes of jadeitite quarried in the Italian Alps were carried as prestige objects as far as Britain and Ireland."},{"term":"PPNA (Pre-Pottery Neolithic A)","definition":"The first Neolithic phase in the Levant and upper Mesopotamia, roughly 9600 to 8800 BCE. Its people lived in round houses, cultivated cereals and legumes that were still wild in form, and built the tower of Jericho and the earliest monumental enclosures of Göbekli Tepe."},{"term":"PPNB (Pre-Pottery Neolithic B)","definition":"The phase roughly 8800 to 7000 BCE when rectangular houses, domesticated wheat and barley, sheep, goats, cattle and pigs became established across Southwest Asia. Plastered skulls and the lime plaster statues of ʿAin Ghazal belong to this phase."},{"term":"Pressure flaking","definition":"Removing small, thin flakes by pressing a pointed tool of antler, bone or wood against the edge of a stone instead of striking it. It gives fine control; the earliest known examples are Still Bay points from Blombos Cave about 75,000 years ago, and the technique later produced thin Solutrean points and countless arrowheads."},{"term":"Radiocarbon dating","definition":"A method that dates once-living material by measuring its remaining carbon-14, a radioactive form of carbon that decays with a half-life of about 5,730 years. It works back to about 50,000 years ago, and its results must be calibrated to give calendar ages."},{"term":"Refugium","definition":"An area where plants, animals or people survived a period of hostile climate and from which they later spread out again (plural refugia). During the Last Glacial Maximum, southwestern France, Iberia, Italy and the Balkans served as refugia for European populations."},{"term":"Retouch","definition":"Small secondary removals along the edge of a flake or blade to sharpen, shape, strengthen or blunt it. The pattern of retouch defines most formal tool types, such as scrapers, points and backed pieces."},{"term":"Sahul","definition":"The continent formed by Australia, New Guinea and Tasmania when sea levels were low, separated from mainland Asia by the islands of Wallacea. Reaching it always required sea crossings; people arrived by at least 50,000 years ago, and possibly 65,000 years ago according to dates from Madjedbebe in northern Australia."},{"term":"Secondary products","definition":"Products taken from living animals rather than their carcasses, such as milk, wool, traction and riding. Andrew Sherratt proposed in 1981 a “Secondary Products Revolution” in the fourth millennium BCE, but milk residues in pottery show that dairying had begun by the seventh millennium BCE in Anatolia."},{"term":"Sedentism","definition":"Living in one place for all or most of the year. Sedentism did not always follow farming: the Natufians of the Levant and the Jōmon of Japan were largely settled hunter-gatherers."},{"term":"Shell midden","definition":"A heap of discarded shells and other food waste, often built up over centuries at a coastal or riverside camp. Middens record diet, seasons and environments; the Danish kitchen middens of the Ertebølle culture, studied from the 1840s, were among the first to be investigated scientifically."},{"term":"Sickle gloss","definition":"A bright polish that forms on the edges of flint blades used to cut silica-rich plant stems such as cereals and reeds. It is one of the main archaeological signs of harvesting, from the Natufian onwards."},{"term":"Smelting","definition":"Extracting metal from ore by heating it with charcoal in a low-oxygen fire. The earliest secure evidence of copper smelting, from Belovode in Serbia, dates to about 5000 BCE, although claims from Iran and Anatolia may be as old or older."},{"term":"Solutrean","definition":"An Upper Palaeolithic culture of France and Iberia during the Last Glacial Maximum, roughly 25,000 to 20,000 years ago, named after Solutré in Burgundy. Its knappers made exceptionally thin laurel-leaf points using heat treatment and pressure flaking; the idea that Solutreans crossed the Atlantic to America is rejected by most researchers on genetic and archaeological grounds."},{"term":"Starch grain","definition":"A microscopic granule in which plants store energy, with shapes typical of particular species. Starch grains preserved on grinding stones, tools and dental calculus reveal plant foods; at Ohalo II in Israel they show that wild barley and wheat were ground about 23,000 years ago."},{"term":"Still Bay","definition":"A Middle Stone Age industry of southern Africa, roughly 77,000 to 70,000 years ago, named after a coastal town in the Western Cape. It is known for finely made, leaf-shaped bifacial points, some finished by pressure flaking, as at Blombos Cave."},{"term":"Stratigraphy","definition":"The study of layers (strata) in sediments and archaeological deposits. Under the law of superposition, lower layers are older unless disturbed, so careful recording of layers underpins relative dating and the association of finds."},{"term":"Sunda","definition":"The continental shelf joining the Malay Peninsula, Sumatra, Java and Borneo to mainland Southeast Asia when sea levels were low. It formed a large land area of forests and savanna corridors, much of it now under the South China Sea and the Java Sea."},{"term":"Taphonomy","definition":"The study of what happens to remains between death and discovery: decay, scavenging, trampling, transport, burial and fossilisation. Taphonomic analysis distinguishes butchery marks from carnivore bites or trampling, and natural bone accumulations from deliberate burial; the term was coined by Ivan Efremov in 1940."},{"term":"Tell","definition":"A mound built up from the debris of successive settlements on the same spot over centuries or millennia, typical of Southwest Asia and southeastern Europe. Local names include tall in Arabic, tepe in Persian and Turkish, and höyük in Turkish, as in Çatalhöyük."},{"term":"Tephra","definition":"Fragments of rock, glass and crystals thrown out by volcanic eruptions, from fine ash to pumice. Tephra layers make ideal time markers because each eruption is geologically instantaneous and has a distinctive chemistry, and they can often be dated directly."},{"term":"Tin bronze","definition":"An alloy of copper with around 10 percent tin, harder and easier to cast than copper. A few early pieces, such as a foil from Pločnik in Serbia of about 4650 BCE, were made from naturally mixed ores; deliberate tin bronze spread across Southwest Asia in the third millennium BCE and through Europe by about 2000 BCE, creating long-distance trade in scarce tin."},{"term":"U-series dating","definition":"Uranium-series dating measures the decay of uranium, dissolved in water, into thorium in materials such as cave calcite, corals and teeth. Because thorium does not dissolve in water, newly formed calcite contains almost none, and its gradual build-up gives ages up to about 500,000 years."},{"term":"Upper Palaeolithic","definition":"The last part of the Palaeolithic in Eurasia, from roughly 50,000 to about 12,000 years ago, associated with Homo sapiens. It is marked by blade and bladelet technology, tools of bone, antler and ivory, personal ornaments, figurative art and musical instruments."},{"term":"Use-wear","definition":"Microscopic damage, polish and striations on a tool’s edge caused by use, which reveal how it was used and on what material. The method was founded by Sergei Semenov in the Soviet Union and refined from the 1970s by Lawrence Keeley and others through experiments with replica tools."},{"term":"Wallacea","definition":"The islands between the Sunda and Sahul continental shelves, including Sulawesi, Lombok, Flores, Timor and the Moluccas, which were never joined to either by land. Named after the naturalist Alfred Russel Wallace, they could only be reached across the sea, which hominins had done on Flores by about 1 million years ago."},{"term":"Y chromosome","definition":"The sex chromosome passed from father to son, most of which does not recombine and so traces a single paternal line. Ancient Y chromosomes show, for instance, that the original Neanderthal Y was replaced by one from early relatives of Homo sapiens (Petr et al. 2020)."},{"term":"Yamnaya","definition":"Pastoralists of the Pontic–Caspian steppe, about 3300 to 2600 BCE, who used wagons, herded cattle and sheep and buried their dead under kurgans. Ancient DNA shows that people with Yamnaya-related ancestry spread west into Europe and east to the Altai within a few centuries, a movement many researchers link to the spread of Indo-European languages."},{"term":"Yangshao","definition":"A Neolithic culture of the middle Yellow River valley in China, about 5000 to 3000 BCE, based on millet farming and pig keeping and known for red pottery painted in black. Villages such as Banpo near Xi’an and Jiangzhai had houses arranged around central open spaces."},{"term":"Younger Dryas","definition":"A cold interval about 12,900 to 11,700 years ago, when much of the northern hemisphere returned abruptly to near-glacial conditions. It was most likely caused by a slowdown of the Atlantic Ocean circulation; a proposed comet impact trigger is rejected by most researchers. It is named after the tundra flower Dryas octopetala."}],"methods":[{"id":"radiocarbon","name":"Radiocarbon dating (with calibration and AMS)","range":"up to about 50,000 years","how":"Cosmic rays constantly create radioactive carbon-14 in the upper atmosphere, and every plant and animal takes it up while alive. After death the carbon-14 decays with a half-life of about 5,730 years, so the fraction left in charcoal, bone collagen, shell or seeds gives the time since death. Accelerator mass spectrometry (AMS), in use since the late 1970s, counts carbon-14 atoms directly and needs only about a milligram of carbon, so single seeds or small bone samples can be dated. Because atmospheric carbon-14 has varied, raw results are converted to calendar ages with calibration curves, currently IntCal20, SHCal20 and Marine20, and are often combined with the order of layers in Bayesian statistical models.","strengths":"It is the most widely used method for the last 50,000 years and dates the organic material of interest itself, such as a charred seed or a cut-marked bone. In the Holocene, calibrated and modelled dates can often be narrowed to a few decades.","caveats":"Contamination matters most for old samples: just 1 percent of modern carbon makes a sample of infinite age look about 37,000 years old, which is why stricter cleaning methods, such as ultrafiltration of bone collagen, have revised many Palaeolithic chronologies. People and animals that ate marine or freshwater food absorb ‘old’ carbon and can appear centuries too old (reservoir effects), and flat stretches of the calibration curve produce wide calendar ranges.","example":"Radiocarbon dates from about 40 sites across Europe, processed with improved pretreatment, led Tom Higham and colleagues to conclude in 2014 that Neanderthal-associated industries ended between about 41,000 and 39,000 years ago."},{"id":"luminescence","name":"Luminescence dating (OSL, TL and IRSL)","range":"from a few hundred years to about 200,000 years with quartz; up to about 500,000 years with feldspar or burnt flint","how":"Buried mineral grains absorb energy from natural radioactivity in the surrounding sediment and from cosmic rays, which traps electrons in defects of their crystal lattice. Sunlight, for sediments, or strong heat, for burnt flint, pottery or hearth stones, empties the traps and resets the clock. In the laboratory, stimulating the grains with light (optically stimulated luminescence, OSL, mostly for quartz; infrared stimulated luminescence, IRSL, for feldspar) or with heat (thermoluminescence, TL) releases the stored energy as light. Dividing the total radiation dose this represents by the yearly dose rate measured at the site gives the age.","strengths":"It dates the sediments that contain artefacts and fossils directly, even where there is no organic material or volcanic rock. Single-grain OSL can spot grains that have moved in from other layers.","caveats":"Grains not fully exposed to light before burial give ages that are too old, and burrowing animals or trampling can mix grains between layers. Dose rates depend on past water content and radioactivity, and some feldspar signals fade over time, so uncertainties of 5 to 10 percent are usual.","example":"Thermoluminescence dates on burnt flints from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco, published in 2017 by Daniel Richter and colleagues, placed the oldest known Homo sapiens fossils at about 315,000 years ago."},{"id":"uranium-series","name":"Uranium-series dating","range":"a few hundred to about 500,000 years (uranium–thorium); uranium–lead reaches millions of years","how":"Uranium dissolves in groundwater and is built into calcite as stalagmites, flowstones and thin crusts form, while thorium, which does not dissolve, is almost absent at first. Uranium-234 then decays into thorium-230 at a known rate, so the ratio between them in the calcite gives its age. Laser ablation now allows dating of very thin crusts that have grown over cave paintings, and the same principle is applied, with more difficulty, to teeth and bones. The related uranium–lead method dates older flowstones, for example in South African caves.","strengths":"It is precise, reaches far beyond radiocarbon and suits caves, where flowstones above and below fossils can bracket their age.","caveats":"Bones and teeth take up and lose uranium after burial, so their dates are often minimum ages or depend on models, and dirt containing thorium must be corrected for. Crusts over rock art give only a minimum age for the painting, and disputed sampling has produced controversial results, such as the claimed Neanderthal paintings in Spanish caves more than 64,000 years old.","example":"In 2024 Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Maxime Aubert and colleagues used laser-ablation uranium-series dating of calcite crusts to show that a painted scene of human-like figures and a pig in Leang Karampuang, Sulawesi, is at least 51,200 years old."},{"id":"argon-argon","name":"Argon–argon and potassium–argon dating","range":"from a few thousand years to billions of years; in human evolution mainly from about 100,000 to 7 million years","how":"Radioactive potassium-40 in volcanic minerals decays into argon-40, a gas, with a half-life of about 1.25 billion years. The heat of an eruption drives out existing argon, so the argon that accumulates afterwards measures the time since the lava cooled or the ash fell. The older potassium–argon method measures potassium and argon in separate portions of a sample; in argon–argon dating the sample is first irradiated in a nuclear reactor so that some potassium becomes argon-39, allowing both to be measured together in single crystals heated by a laser.","strengths":"It gives precise ages, often to within a few thousand years for layers millions of years old, and is the backbone of East African chronology, where fossils lie between datable volcanic ash layers.","caveats":"It dates the eruption, not the fossil, so the relationship between the ash and the find must be secure. Older crystals incorporated from earlier eruptions or reworked by rivers can give ages that are too old, and the method only works where volcanic material is present.","example":"In 1961 potassium–argon dating by Jack Evernden and Garniss Curtis showed that the layer at Olduvai Gorge containing the Paranthropus boisei skull OH 5 was about 1.75 million years old, far older than most researchers had expected."},{"id":"palaeomagnetism","name":"Palaeomagnetism","range":"tens of thousands to millions of years, by matching reversals and excursions of Earth’s magnetic field","how":"Earth’s magnetic field has flipped polarity many times at irregular intervals, with magnetic north and south swapping places. Magnetic minerals in cooling lava, or in fine sediment as it settles, align with the field of the time and preserve its direction. By sampling a sequence of layers and matching its pattern of normal and reversed polarity to the dated geomagnetic polarity time scale, researchers can tell which interval a deposit belongs to; the most recent full reversal, the Matuyama–Brunhes, happened about 773,000 years ago.","strengths":"Reversals are global and, in geological terms, almost instantaneous, so they provide fixed time lines that can be recognised in caves, lake beds and river deposits worldwide, including deposits that cannot be dated in any other way.","caveats":"A single polarity reading could fit many intervals, so the method needs independent age estimates to choose the right match. Sediments can be remagnetised later, and gaps in a sequence can make patterns ambiguous.","example":"The discovery in 1995 that the Matuyama–Brunhes reversal lies in level TD7 at Gran Dolina, Atapuerca, showed that the Homo antecessor fossils in the level below are older than about 780,000 years, and in 2026 a record of the same reversal was used to date hominin fossils from a cave at Thomas Quarry near Casablanca, Morocco, to about 773,000 years ago."},{"id":"electron-spin-resonance","name":"Electron spin resonance (ESR) dating","range":"roughly 20,000 to 2 million years","how":"Like luminescence, ESR measures electrons trapped in crystal defects by natural radiation, but it detects them directly by placing the sample in a strong magnetic field and measuring how it absorbs microwaves, without emptying the traps. It is applied mainly to tooth enamel and to quartz grains in sediments. For teeth it is usually combined with uranium-series measurements (US-ESR) to model how much uranium the tooth took up after burial.","strengths":"It can date fossil teeth themselves, including hominin teeth, over a range far beyond radiocarbon and many luminescence methods, and small enamel fragments can be measured with little damage.","caveats":"Ages depend on reconstructing the radiation dose from the surrounding sediment and the history of uranium uptake, so uncertainties of 10 to 20 percent are common. Results are most convincing when they agree with other methods.","example":"Combined uranium-series and ESR dating of Homo naledi teeth, together with dates on flowstones, placed the Rising Star Cave fossils between about 335,000 and 236,000 years ago (Paul Dirks and colleagues, 2017)."},{"id":"cosmogenic-burial","name":"Cosmogenic nuclide burial dating","range":"about 200,000 to 5 million years","how":"Near the ground surface, cosmic rays turn a few atoms in quartz grains into rare isotopes, aluminium-26 and beryllium-10, at a nearly fixed ratio. When the quartz is washed deep into a cave or buried under thick sediment, production stops and both isotopes decay, aluminium-26 about twice as fast as beryllium-10, so their falling ratio records the time since burial. The isochron version measures several samples from one layer to correct for isotopes inherited before burial. A related method uses beryllium isotopes formed in the atmosphere and trapped in lake and river sediments.","strengths":"It can date cave and river deposits in regions without volcanic rocks, such as the South African caves and parts of China, across the key period of human evolution.","caveats":"It requires quartz buried quickly and deeply, and older sediment reworked into a deposit can give misleading ages. Uncertainties are often 100,000 to 300,000 years, and in complex caves such as Sterkfontein the relationship between the dated sediment and the fossils is sometimes disputed.","example":"In 2015 Darryl Granger and colleagues used aluminium–beryllium isochron dating to place the Little Foot skeleton at Sterkfontein at about 3.67 million years, much older than an age of about 2.2 million years that others had derived from flowstones."},{"id":"tephrochronology","name":"Tephrochronology","range":"any age at which volcanic ash is preserved, from recent centuries to millions of years","how":"An eruption spreads ash (tephra) over a wide area within days or weeks, leaving a layer with a distinctive chemical fingerprint in its glass shards and crystals. Geochemists measure major and trace elements with electron microprobes and laser-ablation mass spectrometry, match layers between sites, and date them by argon–argon, radiocarbon or other methods. Invisible shards, called cryptotephra, can be extracted from cave and lake sediments far from the volcano.","strengths":"A tephra layer is an instantaneous time line: sites containing the same ash are contemporaneous at that level, which allows correlation across whole regions and between archaeological sites and climate records.","caveats":"Ash can be reworked by wind, water or burrowing animals, and different eruptions of the same volcano may be chemically similar. A tephra dates the sediment, so the finds must be securely related to it.","example":"In 2022 Céline Vidal and colleagues matched ash above the Omo Kibish 1 fossils in Ethiopia to a large eruption of the Shala volcano about 233,000 years ago, showing that these early Homo sapiens fossils are at least that old."},{"id":"dendrochronology","name":"Dendrochronology (tree-ring dating)","range":"back to about 12,000 years ago in central Europe; older ‘floating’ sequences exist","how":"Trees in seasonal climates add one ring a year, and ring widths vary with the weather, producing patterns shared by trees of the same region. Overlapping sequences from living trees, old buildings, archaeological timbers and subfossil trunks from rivers and bogs have been linked into master chronologies reaching back more than 12,000 years in central Europe. A timber’s ring pattern is matched against the master to give the calendar year of each ring and, if the outermost ring under the bark survives, the year the tree was felled.","strengths":"It gives exact calendar years, the most precise dates in archaeology, and tree rings also provide the backbone of the radiocarbon calibration curve.","caveats":"It needs wood with enough rings, usually 50 or more, from a species and region with a master chronology, and it dates the felling rather than the use, since timbers could be stored or reused. Few Stone Age sites preserve wood, except in waterlogged conditions.","example":"Tree rings in the timber piles of Arbon Bleiche 3, a Neolithic lakeside village on Lake Constance in Switzerland, show that it was built and lived in between 3384 and 3370 BCE, a span of only about 15 years."},{"id":"amino-acid-racemisation","name":"Amino acid racemisation","range":"thousands to a few million years, depending on temperature","how":"Living organisms build their proteins almost entirely from the ‘left-handed’ (L) forms of amino acids. After death these slowly convert into a mixture of left- and right-handed (D) forms, so the ratio of D to L rises with time. Modern methods analyse proteins sealed inside mineral crystals, such as the calcite lids (opercula) of freshwater snails or ostrich eggshell, which behave as closed systems.","strengths":"It is relatively cheap, needs little material and works on common fossils such as shells, beyond the range of radiocarbon, allowing sites to be placed in order of age and correlated with particular interglacials.","caveats":"The reaction speeds up strongly with temperature, so results depend on a site’s temperature history and must be calibrated against sites dated by other methods. It gives relative ages more reliably than absolute ones.","example":"Amino acid ratios in snail opercula, analysed by Kirsty Penkman and colleagues, supported an age of about 700,000 years for the flint tools at Pakefield in Suffolk, reported in 2005 by Simon Parfitt and colleagues as the oldest evidence of humans in northern Europe at the time."},{"id":"biostratigraphy","name":"Biostratigraphy (biochronology)","range":"any age with suitable fossils; resolution typically tens to hundreds of thousands of years","how":"Animal species evolve, appear and disappear at particular times. When those times are known from well-dated sequences, the combination of species in a layer, or the evolutionary stage of a fast-changing lineage such as pigs, elephants or voles, indicates its approximate age. In Europe, changes in the teeth of water voles, including the replacement of Mimomys by Arvicola, are key markers; in East Africa, pig teeth have long been used.","strengths":"It can date deposits that contain no volcanic rock or datable minerals and provides an independent check on numerical dates.","caveats":"It assumes that first and last appearances are known and were the same everywhere, but species may have appeared or survived at different times in different regions. It usually gives broad age ranges rather than precise dates.","example":"In the 1970s the evolutionary stage of fossil pig teeth at Koobi Fora, studied by Basil Cooke and by Tim White and John Harris, indicated that the KNM-ER 1470 skull was younger than the 2.6 million years first obtained for the overlying KBS Tuff, which was later redated to about 1.9 million years."},{"id":"ancient-dna","name":"Ancient DNA","range":"about 430,000 years for humans so far; more than 1 million years for mammoths preserved in permafrost","how":"DNA survives in bones and teeth as short fragments that become rarer with time and warmth; the dense petrous bone of the inner ear preserves it best. In clean laboratories, researchers extract the fragments, convert them into sequencing libraries and read millions of them, often enriching human DNA with capture probes. Characteristic chemical damage at the ends of ancient molecules helps separate genuine ancient DNA from modern contamination.","strengths":"It reveals relationships, population movements, interbreeding, sex and even some physical traits directly, and has discovered whole groups, such as the Denisovans, that are almost invisible in the fossil record.","caveats":"DNA preserves poorly in hot climates, so there are few ancient genomes older than about 20,000 years from tropical Africa or Southeast Asia, which biases the picture. Contamination from modern people and microbes must be controlled, and sampling destroys part of irreplaceable fossils, which raises ethical questions, including consultation with descendant communities.","example":"In 2010 DNA from a finger bone found in Denisova Cave, Siberia, revealed the Denisovans, a previously unknown human group, and in the same year the first Neanderthal genome showed that Neanderthals had interbred with the ancestors of non-Africans."},{"id":"sediment-dna","name":"Sediment DNA","range":"hundreds of thousands of years in caves; about 2 million years in permanently frozen ground","how":"Animals and people shed DNA into the ground through bones, faeces, hair and body fluids, and some of it binds to mineral particles in cave sediments. Researchers take small sediment samples, extract the DNA and use capture probes to fish out mitochondrial or nuclear sequences of mammals, including hominins, which are identified by comparison with reference genomes.","strengths":"It shows which humans and animals were present in a layer even when no bones survive, and can be sampled systematically across a whole excavation profile.","caveats":"DNA can move between layers with water or disturbance, so its link to the dated sediment must be checked, and the quantities are tiny and easily contaminated. It usually recovers short mitochondrial sequences, which give limited detail.","example":"In 2020 Dongju Zhang, Qiaomei Fu and colleagues found Denisovan mitochondrial DNA in sediment layers of Baishiya Karst Cave on the Tibetan Plateau dating to about 100,000 and 60,000 years ago, and possibly 45,000 years ago."},{"id":"palaeoproteomics","name":"Palaeoproteomics (ancient proteins)","range":"about 2 million years for hominin tooth enamel so far; older in cold settings and for other animals","how":"Proteins such as bone collagen and the proteins of tooth enamel are more durable than DNA. Mass spectrometers break them into peptides and read their amino acid sequences, which are encoded by genes and therefore carry evolutionary information. A quick screening version, ZooMS (zooarchaeology by mass spectrometry), uses collagen peptide fingerprints to identify which animal a scrap of bone came from.","strengths":"It works on fossils far too old or from climates too warm for DNA, identifies tiny bone fragments, and enamel proteins reveal sex, because one form of the protein amelogenin is encoded on the Y chromosome.","caveats":"Proteins carry far less information than genomes, so they can place a fossil among broad lineages but rarely resolve close relationships. Contamination and chemical changes after death must be carefully excluded.","example":"In 2025 a team led by Qiaomei Fu recovered dozens of ancient proteins from the Harbin skull and identified Denisovan-specific variants, while Takumi Tsutaya and colleagues used proteins to show that the Penghu 1 jaw from Taiwan belonged to a Denisovan man."},{"id":"stable-isotopes","name":"Stable isotope analysis","range":"tooth enamel for millions of years; bone collagen usually for tens of thousands of years","how":"Elements such as carbon, nitrogen, oxygen and strontium occur as stable isotopes whose ratios differ between foods, water sources and rocks, and these ratios are recorded in bones and teeth. Carbon distinguishes tropical grasses and sedges (C4 plants) from trees and shrubs (C3 plants), nitrogen rises with each step up the food chain, oxygen reflects temperature and water sources, and strontium mirrors the geology of the place where a person or animal lived while its teeth formed.","strengths":"It gives direct evidence of what individuals ate and where they grew up, and sampling in sequence along a tooth can reveal seasonal changes and movements.","caveats":"Bone collagen, needed for most nitrogen measurements, rarely survives beyond tens of thousands of years except in cold places, although newer methods can measure nitrogen in enamel: a 2025 study led by Tina Lüdecke used them on Australopithecus teeth from Sterkfontein and found little sign of meat eating. Isotope baselines vary between regions and periods, so values must be compared with those of local plants and animals.","example":"Carbon isotopes in the tooth enamel of Paranthropus boisei, measured by Thure Cerling and colleagues in 2011, showed that about three quarters of its diet came from C4 plants such as grasses and sedges rather than from hard nuts."},{"id":"residue-use-wear","name":"Residue and use-wear analysis","range":"any age at which tool surfaces are well preserved","how":"Use-wear analysts examine tool edges under low- and high-power microscopes, recognising the chipping, polish and scratches left by cutting meat, scraping hide, working wood or bone, or impact as a projectile, and compare them with traces on experimental replicas. Residue analysts identify material left on tools and pots, such as starch grains, phytoliths, blood, fats, resins and adhesives, using microscopy and chemical methods such as gas chromatography with mass spectrometry. Fats absorbed into pottery walls reveal what was cooked or stored, including milk.","strengths":"They show directly how tools and vessels were used, rather than inferring function from shape, and can detect hafting, plant processing and cooking that otherwise leave no trace.","caveats":"Different activities can produce similar traces, and trampling, soil chemistry or excavation damage can mimic wear. Residues may come from the burial environment or from handling, so control samples from the surrounding sediment are essential.","example":"Residues on stone tools from Sibudu Cave in South Africa, analysed by Lyn Wadley and colleagues in 2009, show that Middle Stone Age people more than 60,000 years ago hafted them with a compound glue of plant gum and ochre."},{"id":"climate-archives","name":"Climate archives (ice cores, benthic foraminifera and the LR04 stack)","range":"ice cores continuously to about 800,000 years; ocean sediment records over millions of years (LR04 covers 5.3 million years)","how":"Ice sheets preserve annual layers of snow with bubbles of ancient air, whose carbon dioxide and methane can be measured directly, while the oxygen isotope ratio of the ice reflects temperature. On the ocean floor, tiny foraminifera build calcite shells whose oxygen isotope ratio rises as ice sheets lock up isotopically light water and deep water cools. In 2005 Lorraine Lisiecki and Maureen Raymo combined 57 such seafloor records into the LR04 stack, a reference curve for the last 5.3 million years that defines the numbered Marine Isotope Stages.","strengths":"Together they provide a continuous, global framework of glacial and interglacial cycles against which fossils and archaeological sites can be placed, and Greenland ice records abrupt changes almost year by year.","caveats":"The LR04 time scale is tuned to astronomical cycles and is less precise than direct dating, and its global signal can hide regional differences, such as changes in African rainfall, which need local archives like lake cores. Continuous Antarctic ice cores reach about 800,000 years and Greenland cores only the last glacial cycle, although in 2025 a European team reported drilling ice more than 1.2 million years old near Dome C in Antarctica.","example":"Greenland ice cores show that the Younger Dryas cold spell ended about 11,700 years ago with a warming of several degrees within a few decades, the boundary now used to define the start of the Holocene."},{"id":"remote-sensing","name":"Remote sensing and lidar","range":"any age, for features visible at or just below the surface","how":"Aerial photographs, satellite images and drone surveys reveal buried ditches, walls and structures through differences in crop growth, soil colour or shadow, often best seen in low sunlight or during drought. Lidar (light detection and ranging) fires hundreds of thousands of laser pulses per second from an aircraft or drone and filters out those reflected by vegetation, producing a detailed model of the ground surface even beneath forest. Geophysical methods such as magnetometry and ground-penetrating radar extend the view below the surface.","strengths":"They map whole landscapes quickly and without digging, revealing settlement plans, field systems and monuments that ground survey would miss, and they help target excavation.","caveats":"Features seen from above cannot be dated or reliably interpreted without checks on the ground and excavation, and natural features can be mistaken for human ones. Deeply buried Stone Age sites and small hunter-gatherer camps are usually invisible to these methods.","example":"Satellite imagery has revealed thousands of ‘desert kites’, stone-walled funnels used to trap wild animals, across Southwest Asia, and magnetometry at Trypillia megasites in Ukraine such as Maidanetske has mapped the plans of thousands of burnt houses."}],"stats":{"sources":434}};
